(function () {
  if (typeof window === 'undefined') return;
  window.TIH_LESSON_NOTES = window.TIH_LESSON_NOTES || {};
  window.TIH_LESSON_NOTES['human-rights-ihl'] = {
  "Welcome to the Course": "<div class=\"study-note\"><div class=\"revision-banner\"><strong>Human Rights and International Humanitarian Law</strong><span>Module 1: Foundations of Human Rights</span></div><h3 class=\"lesson-note-title\">LESSON NOTE: WELCOME TO THE COURSE</h3><div class=\"table-wrap\"><table class=\"study-table\"><thead><tr><th>Subject</th><th>Topic</th><th>Level</th><th>Lesson duration</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Human Rights and International Humanitarian Law</td><td>Welcome to the Course</td><td>Beginner to Advanced</td><td>30 minutes</td></tr></tbody></table></div><h4>Learning objectives</h4><p>By the end of this lesson you should be able to:</p><ul><li>Explain what this course covers and why it combines human rights law with international humanitarian law</li><li>Describe the difference between a moral claim and a legal right</li><li>Set up a study method that lets you use the law rather than only recite it</li></ul><h4>1. Introduction</h4><p>This course covers two bodies of law that are often confused and are genuinely different: international human rights law, which governs how a state treats people at all times, and international humanitarian law, which governs how parties to an armed conflict may fight. They overlap, and knowing precisely where is one of the most useful skills in the field.</p><h4>2. What this course covers</h4><div class=\"table-wrap\"><table class=\"study-table\"><thead><tr><th>Stage</th><th>Modules</th><th>What you learn</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Foundations</td><td>1 \u2013 2</td><td>What rights are, where they come from, and how international law is made</td></tr><tr><td>The instruments</td><td>3 \u2013 4</td><td>The International Bill of Rights and the thematic treaties</td></tr><tr><td>The systems</td><td>5 \u2013 6</td><td>Regional human rights systems and the United Nations machinery</td></tr><tr><td>The law of war</td><td>7 \u2013 10</td><td>IHL: its sources, principles, protected persons and limits on weapons</td></tr><tr><td>Accountability</td><td>11</td><td>War crimes, genocide, crimes against humanity and the courts that try them</td></tr><tr><td>Practice</td><td>12 \u2013 14</td><td>Monitoring, documentation, advocacy, projects and assessments</td></tr></tbody></table></div><h4>3. Moral claim and legal right</h4><div class=\"study-defn\"><strong>Moral claim:</strong> An assertion that something ought to be so, based on ethics, religion or conscience.</div><div class=\"study-defn\"><strong>Legal right:</strong> An entitlement recognised by a legal system, with a corresponding duty on someone and, ideally, a mechanism to enforce it.</div><p>Both matter. Human rights began as moral claims and became legal rights through treaties and custom. But when you work in this field, the question that decides cases is the legal one: which instrument, binding on which state, creates which obligation, and who can be asked to enforce it.</p><div class=\"study-callout\"><strong>Note:</strong> A common beginner's mistake is to argue that something is a human rights violation because it is unjust. Injustice is the reason a rule exists; it is not itself the rule. Learn to name the article.</div><h4>4. How to study this subject</h4><ol><li>Watch the lesson video once without pausing.</li><li>Read the note, then state the rule out loud in one sentence, including which instrument it comes from.</li><li>Answer the practice quiz before moving on.</li><li>Apply the rule to one real situation reported in the news that week.</li></ol><div class=\"study-callout\"><strong>Note:</strong> Get into the habit of citing. 'Torture is prohibited' is a slogan. 'Torture is prohibited absolutely under Article 7 ICCPR and Article 2 CAT, and the prohibition is non-derogable' is knowledge you can use.</div><h4>5. A word about the subject matter</h4><p>This course deals with atrocity: torture, killing, disappearance, war. That is unavoidable, because the law exists in response to it. Two things are worth saying at the start.</p><ul><li>The law is not neutral about these things. It prohibits them, and studying the prohibition is not the same as being detached from what it prohibits.</li><li>If you have lived through conflict or serious abuse, some of this material may be difficult. Working at your own pace, and stopping when you need to, is entirely legitimate.</li></ul><h4>Summary</h4><ul><li>The course covers international human rights law and international humanitarian law, which overlap but are distinct.</li><li>A moral claim says what ought to be; a legal right names an instrument, an obligation and a mechanism.</li><li>Learn to cite the article, not just assert the principle.</li><li>Apply each rule to a real reported situation to make it usable.</li></ul><h4>Key Terms</h4><div class=\"table-wrap\"><table class=\"study-table\"><thead><tr><th>Term</th><th>Meaning</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>International human rights law</td><td>The body of law governing how states must treat people at all times.</td></tr><tr><td>International humanitarian law</td><td>The body of law regulating the conduct of armed conflict.</td></tr><tr><td>Instrument</td><td>A treaty, declaration or other formal legal text.</td></tr><tr><td>Obligation</td><td>A binding duty imposed on a state or other actor by law.</td></tr></tbody></table></div><h4>Review Questions</h4><h5>Multiple choice</h5><div class=\"study-mcq\"><p class=\"mcq-q\">What is the difference between a moral claim and a legal right?</p><ol type=\"A\"><li>A legal right names an instrument, an obligation and a mechanism; a moral claim asserts what ought to be</li><li>They are the same thing</li><li>Moral claims are enforceable and legal rights are not</li><li>Legal rights apply only in wartime</li></ol><details class=\"mcq-a\"><summary>Show answer</summary><p><strong>Answer: A.</strong> Injustice is the reason a rule exists; it is not itself the rule, which is why you must be able to name the article.</p></details></div><div class=\"study-mcq\"><p class=\"mcq-q\">What distinguishes international humanitarian law from human rights law?</p><ol type=\"A\"><li>IHL regulates the conduct of armed conflict; human rights law governs treatment of people at all times</li><li>IHL applies only to civilians</li><li>Human rights law applies only during war</li><li>They are two names for the same body of law</li></ol><details class=\"mcq-a\"><summary>Show answer</summary><p><strong>Answer: A.</strong> They overlap, and knowing precisely where is one of the most useful skills in the field.</p></details></div><div class=\"study-mcq\"><p class=\"mcq-q\">Which statement shows usable legal knowledge?</p><ol type=\"A\"><li>&#x27;Torture is prohibited absolutely under Article 7 ICCPR and is non-derogable&#x27;</li><li>&#x27;Torture is wrong&#x27;</li><li>&#x27;Torture should never happen anywhere&#x27;</li><li>&#x27;Everyone agrees torture is unacceptable&#x27;</li></ol><details class=\"mcq-a\"><summary>Show answer</summary><p><strong>Answer: A.</strong> Citing the instrument and the nature of the obligation is what allows the rule to be applied or invoked.</p></details></div><div class=\"study-mcq\"><p class=\"mcq-q\">What should you do immediately after learning a rule in this course?</p><ol type=\"A\"><li>Apply it to one real reported situation</li><li>Memorise the video</li><li>Move to the next lesson</li><li>Read the treaty text in full</li></ol><details class=\"mcq-a\"><summary>Show answer</summary><p><strong>Answer: A.</strong> Applying a rule to a concrete case is what converts recognition into usable knowledge.</p></details></div><h5>Short answer</h5><div class=\"study-sa\"><p class=\"sa-q\">Name the two bodies of law this course covers and state the difference.</p><details class=\"sa-a\"><summary>Model answer</summary><p>International human rights law, which governs how a state must treat people at all times, and international humanitarian law, which regulates the conduct of parties to an armed conflict. They overlap during conflict, but they arise from different instruments and apply in different circumstances.</p></details></div><div class=\"study-sa\"><p class=\"sa-q\">Why is it insufficient to argue that something is a human rights violation because it is unjust?</p><details class=\"sa-a\"><summary>Model answer</summary><p>Because injustice is the reason rules exist, not the rule itself. A legal argument requires identifying the instrument that binds the state, the specific obligation it creates, and the mechanism through which it can be raised.</p></details></div><h4>Class Activity</h4><ol><li>Choose one human rights situation currently in the news. Write down what you think the violation is, then try to name the treaty and article. Note where you could not.</li><li>Start a citation notebook. Every time you learn a rule in this course, write the rule in one sentence with its instrument and article.</li></ol><h4>Assignment</h4><p>In half a page, explain why this course teaches human rights law and international humanitarian law together. Give one example of a situation in which both would apply, and say what each would contribute to the analysis.</p><p class=\"study-print\"><strong>Printable:</strong> use your browser\u2019s Print \u2192 Save as PDF to keep an offline copy.</p></div>",
  "What Are Human Rights?": "<div class=\"study-note\"><div class=\"revision-banner\"><strong>Human Rights and International Humanitarian Law</strong><span>Module 1: Foundations of Human Rights</span></div><h3 class=\"lesson-note-title\">LESSON NOTE: WHAT ARE HUMAN RIGHTS?</h3><div class=\"table-wrap\"><table class=\"study-table\"><thead><tr><th>Subject</th><th>Topic</th><th>Level</th><th>Lesson duration</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Human Rights and International Humanitarian Law</td><td>What Are Human Rights?</td><td>Beginner to Advanced</td><td>35 minutes</td></tr></tbody></table></div><h4>Learning objectives</h4><p>By the end of this lesson you should be able to:</p><ul><li>Define human rights precisely</li><li>Explain the tripartite typology of state obligations</li><li>Distinguish a human rights violation from a general wrong</li><li>Identify who holds human rights and who bears the corresponding duties</li></ul><h4>1. Introduction</h4><p>Almost everyone can say that human rights are the rights everyone has because they are human. That is correct and almost useless in practice, because it does not tell you who owes what to whom.</p><h4>2. Meaning</h4><div class=\"study-defn\"><strong>Human rights:</strong> Universal entitlements belonging to every person by virtue of being human, which impose binding obligations on states and are recognised in international and national law.</div><p>Three parts of that definition do the work. <em>Universal</em> means they are not earned, granted or conditional on behaviour. <em>Binding obligations on states</em> identifies who owes the duty. And <em>recognised in law</em> is what separates a human right from an aspiration.</p><h4>3. Who holds rights and who owes duties</h4><div class=\"table-wrap\"><table class=\"study-table\"><thead><tr><th></th><th>Rights holder</th><th>Duty bearer</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Who</td><td>Every individual; sometimes groups such as peoples or indigenous communities</td><td>Primarily the state, through all its organs</td></tr><tr><td>Basis</td><td>Being human</td><td>Having ratified treaties, or being bound by custom</td></tr><tr><td>Example</td><td>A detained person has the right not to be tortured</td><td>The state must not torture, must prevent torture by others, and must investigate allegations</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"study-callout\"><strong>Note:</strong> A frequent confusion: a crime committed by one private individual against another is not automatically a human rights violation. It becomes a state responsibility question when the state fails in its own duty \u2014 to protect, to investigate, to prosecute. Learn to identify what the state did or failed to do.</div><h4>4. The tripartite typology of obligations</h4><p>Every human right imposes three levels of obligation on the state. This framework applies to civil, political, economic, social and cultural rights alike, and it is the single most useful analytical tool in the subject.</p><div class=\"table-wrap\"><table class=\"study-table\"><thead><tr><th>Obligation</th><th>What it requires</th><th>Example: the right to health</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Respect</td><td>The state must not itself interfere with the right</td><td>Do not deny treatment to detainees or a disfavoured group</td></tr><tr><td>Protect</td><td>The state must prevent third parties from interfering</td><td>Regulate private clinics; prevent a company polluting a water source</td></tr><tr><td>Fulfil</td><td>The state must take positive steps to realise the right</td><td>Build clinics, train staff, run vaccination programmes</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"study-callout\"><strong>Example:</strong> A woman is denied emergency care at a private hospital because she cannot pay, and dies. The state did not itself refuse care, so the duty to respect was not breached directly. But if the state has failed to regulate private providers or to guarantee emergency care regardless of ability to pay, it has breached the duty to protect. Identifying the correct limb is what turns an outrage into a legal argument.</div><h4>5. Violation and general wrong</h4><div class=\"table-wrap\"><table class=\"study-table\"><thead><tr><th></th><th>A human rights violation</th><th>A wrong that is not a violation</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Actor</td><td>The state, or a private actor the state failed to control</td><td>A purely private wrong the state properly addressed</td></tr><tr><td>Example</td><td>Police beat a detainee</td><td>One neighbour assaults another and the state investigates and prosecutes properly</td></tr><tr><td>Test</td><td>Did the state breach respect, protect or fulfil?</td><td>No state failure identified</td></tr></tbody></table></div><h4>6. How the examiner&#x27;s question should be answered</h4><p>For <em>'Define human rights and explain state obligations'</em>, give the definition, then spend most of your answer on the tripartite typology with a worked example at each level. Answers that list rights instead of obligations score poorly, because the whole legal content of a right lies in the duty it imposes.</p><h4>Summary</h4><ul><li>Human rights are universal entitlements imposing binding obligations on states, recognised in law.</li><li>Individuals hold rights; states are the primary duty bearers.</li><li>Every right imposes duties to respect, to protect and to fulfil.</li><li>A private wrong becomes a human rights matter when the state fails its own duty to protect, investigate or remedy.</li></ul><h4>Key Terms</h4><div class=\"table-wrap\"><table class=\"study-table\"><thead><tr><th>Term</th><th>Meaning</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Rights holder</td><td>The person or group entitled to a right.</td></tr><tr><td>Duty bearer</td><td>The actor, normally the state, obliged to give effect to a right.</td></tr><tr><td>Obligation to respect</td><td>The duty of the state not to interfere with a right itself.</td></tr><tr><td>Obligation to fulfil</td><td>The duty of the state to take positive steps to realise a right.</td></tr></tbody></table></div><h4>Review Questions</h4><h5>Multiple choice</h5><div class=\"study-mcq\"><p class=\"mcq-q\">What are the three levels of state obligation for every human right?</p><ol type=\"A\"><li>Respect, protect and fulfil</li><li>Ratify, report and remedy</li><li>Legislate, adjudicate and enforce</li><li>Prevent, punish and compensate</li></ol><details class=\"mcq-a\"><summary>Show answer</summary><p><strong>Answer: A.</strong> The typology applies equally to civil, political, economic, social and cultural rights and is the core analytical tool of the subject.</p></details></div><div class=\"study-mcq\"><p class=\"mcq-q\">A company pollutes a village water source and the state has no regulation. Which obligation is breached?</p><ol type=\"A\"><li>The obligation to protect</li><li>The obligation to respect</li><li>The obligation to fulfil</li><li>None; the actor is private</li></ol><details class=\"mcq-a\"><summary>Show answer</summary><p><strong>Answer: A.</strong> The state did not itself pollute, but it failed to prevent a third party interfering with the right.</p></details></div><div class=\"study-mcq\"><p class=\"mcq-q\">Is every crime by one private person against another a human rights violation?</p><ol type=\"A\"><li>No; it becomes one where the state fails to protect, investigate or remedy</li><li>Yes; all crimes are human rights violations</li><li>Only if the victim is a citizen</li><li>Only if the perpetrator is a public official</li></ol><details class=\"mcq-a\"><summary>Show answer</summary><p><strong>Answer: A.</strong> The state&#x27;s own failure is what converts a private wrong into a question of state responsibility.</p></details></div><div class=\"study-mcq\"><p class=\"mcq-q\">What makes human rights &#x27;universal&#x27;?</p><ol type=\"A\"><li>They are not earned, granted or conditional on behaviour</li><li>Every country has ratified every treaty</li><li>They are identical in every legal system</li><li>They apply only to citizens of UN member states</li></ol><details class=\"mcq-a\"><summary>Show answer</summary><p><strong>Answer: A.</strong> Universality means they belong to every person by virtue of being human, not by grant or good conduct.</p></details></div><h5>Short answer</h5><div class=\"study-sa\"><p class=\"sa-q\">Explain the tripartite typology of obligations using the right to education.</p><details class=\"sa-a\"><summary>Model answer</summary><p>Respect: the state must not exclude children from school on discriminatory grounds. Protect: it must prevent third parties, such as employers using child labour, from interfering with access to education. Fulfil: it must take positive steps, building schools, training teachers and making primary education free and available.</p></details></div><div class=\"study-sa\"><p class=\"sa-q\">Explain why the definition of human rights must mention obligations on states.</p><details class=\"sa-a\"><summary>Model answer</summary><p>Because the legal content of a right lies entirely in the duty it imposes. Without identifying who owes what, a right is only an aspiration; naming the duty bearer and the obligation is what makes it enforceable and what allows a violation to be identified.</p></details></div><h4>Class Activity</h4><ol><li>Take three rights and, for each, write one concrete example of a state breaching the duty to respect, to protect and to fulfil.</li><li>Find one news report of a serious wrong. Decide whether it raises a human rights question, and if so, identify which limb of the state&#x27;s obligation is engaged.</li></ol><h4>Assignment</h4><p>Write one page defining human rights and explaining state obligations. Set out the tripartite typology with a worked example at each level for a single named right, and explain when a wrong committed by a private actor becomes a matter of state responsibility.</p><p class=\"study-print\"><strong>Printable:</strong> use your browser\u2019s Print \u2192 Save as PDF to keep an offline copy.</p></div>",
  "Philosophical Foundations of Rights": "<div class=\"study-note\"><div class=\"revision-banner\"><strong>Human Rights and International Humanitarian Law</strong><span>Module 1: Foundations of Human Rights</span></div><h3 class=\"lesson-note-title\">LESSON NOTE: PHILOSOPHICAL FOUNDATIONS OF RIGHTS</h3><div class=\"table-wrap\"><table class=\"study-table\"><thead><tr><th>Subject</th><th>Topic</th><th>Level</th><th>Lesson duration</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Human Rights and International Humanitarian Law</td><td>Philosophical Foundations of Rights</td><td>Beginner to Advanced</td><td>35 minutes</td></tr></tbody></table></div><h4>Learning objectives</h4><p>By the end of this lesson you should be able to:</p><ul><li>Describe the main philosophical justifications for human rights</li><li>Explain the natural law and positivist positions</li><li>Engage seriously with the cultural relativism critique</li><li>Explain why the foundations question matters in practice</li></ul><h4>1. Introduction</h4><p>Why does anyone have rights at all? States sign treaties, but treaties can be denounced, and a right that exists only because a government agreed to it is a weak thing. The foundations question is not academic decoration; it decides what you can say when a government withdraws from a treaty.</p><h4>2. The main justifications</h4><div class=\"table-wrap\"><table class=\"study-table\"><thead><tr><th>Theory</th><th>Core claim</th><th>Strength</th><th>Weakness</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Natural law</td><td>Rights derive from human nature or a moral order that exists independently of any state</td><td>Explains why rights bind even a state that never consented</td><td>Depends on a contested account of nature or the good</td></tr><tr><td>Human dignity</td><td>Rights follow from the inherent worth of every person</td><td>The basis actually used in the UDHR and most constitutions</td><td>'Dignity' is used to argue opposite conclusions</td></tr><tr><td>Social contract</td><td>Rights are what rational people would agree to as terms of living together</td><td>Fits democratic legitimacy well</td><td>Historically the contract was never made; who counts as a party?</td></tr><tr><td>Legal positivism</td><td>Rights exist because a legal system says they do, and only then</td><td>Clear and verifiable</td><td>Cannot criticise a legal system for having no rights</td></tr><tr><td>Interest theory</td><td>Rights protect interests important enough to justify imposing duties</td><td>Practical; explains why rights conflict</td><td>Requires a theory of which interests matter</td></tr><tr><td>Consequentialist</td><td>Recognising rights produces better outcomes overall</td><td>Empirically testable</td><td>Would permit violating a right where outcomes improved</td></tr></tbody></table></div><h4>3. Locke and the natural rights tradition</h4><p>John Locke's argument, set out in the Second Treatise of Government, runs roughly like this: people exist in a natural condition of freedom and equality; they possess rights to life, liberty and property prior to any government; government is created by consent to protect those rights; and a government that systematically violates them forfeits its authority.</p><p>This structure matters because it is the structure of the American and French revolutionary declarations, and through them of the modern human rights instruments. The key move is that rights come <em>before</em> the state, which is why the state cannot legitimately abolish them.</p><h4>4. The cultural relativism critique, taken seriously</h4><p>The objection is that human rights are a Western construction, drafted largely by Western states in 1948, and imposed on societies with different traditions. It deserves a real answer rather than dismissal.</p><div class=\"table-wrap\"><table class=\"study-table\"><thead><tr><th>The critique says</th><th>A serious response</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>The UDHR was drafted by Western powers</td><td>The drafting committee included Chang Peng-chun of China, Charles Malik of Lebanon and Hansa Mehta of India, whose contributions shaped the text substantially</td></tr><tr><td>Rights reflect individualism alien to communal cultures</td><td>The African Charter explicitly includes peoples' rights and duties to family and community, showing the framework can accommodate communal traditions</td></tr><tr><td>Universality is cultural imperialism</td><td>The strongest counter-argument is empirical: the people invoking human rights against a government are almost always that government's own citizens</td></tr><tr><td>Practices such as corporal punishment are traditional</td><td>Tradition explains a practice; it does not by itself justify it. Slavery was traditional in many societies</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"study-callout\"><strong>Note:</strong> Notice who usually makes the relativist argument. It is far more often a government resisting scrutiny than a community defending its own practice. That does not make the argument false, but it is relevant to how it is used.</div><h4>5. Why the foundations question matters practically</h4><ol><li>When a state denounces a treaty, whether the right survives depends on whether it exists independently of consent.</li><li>Where a right is not written down, courts reason from underlying principles such as dignity.</li><li>When two rights conflict, resolving the conflict requires knowing what rights are for.</li><li>When a government says a right is foreign to its culture, you need an answer that is not merely 'the treaty says so'.</li></ol><h4>Summary</h4><ul><li>The main justifications are natural law, dignity, social contract, positivism, interest theory and consequentialism.</li><li>The natural rights tradition places rights before the state, which is why the state cannot legitimately abolish them.</li><li>The cultural relativism critique deserves a serious answer, not dismissal.</li><li>The strongest response is that those invoking human rights against a government are usually its own citizens.</li><li>Foundations matter when treaties are denounced, rights conflict, or universality is challenged.</li></ul><h4>Key Terms</h4><div class=\"table-wrap\"><table class=\"study-table\"><thead><tr><th>Term</th><th>Meaning</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Natural law</td><td>The view that rights derive from human nature or a moral order independent of any state.</td></tr><tr><td>Legal positivism</td><td>The view that rights exist only because a legal system recognises them.</td></tr><tr><td>Cultural relativism</td><td>The claim that moral standards, including rights, are valid only within a particular culture.</td></tr><tr><td>Human dignity</td><td>The inherent worth of every person, used as the foundational basis of most rights instruments.</td></tr></tbody></table></div><h4>Review Questions</h4><h5>Multiple choice</h5><div class=\"study-mcq\"><p class=\"mcq-q\">What is the key structural claim of the natural rights tradition?</p><ol type=\"A\"><li>Rights exist before the state, so the state cannot legitimately abolish them</li><li>Rights are granted by governments to citizens</li><li>Rights depend on a written constitution</li><li>Rights exist only where courts enforce them</li></ol><details class=\"mcq-a\"><summary>Show answer</summary><p><strong>Answer: A.</strong> Because rights precede the state, a government that systematically violates them forfeits its authority.</p></details></div><div class=\"study-mcq\"><p class=\"mcq-q\">What is the main weakness of legal positivism as a foundation for human rights?</p><ol type=\"A\"><li>It cannot criticise a legal system that recognises no rights at all</li><li>It is too complicated to apply</li><li>It relies on contested claims about human nature</li><li>It has been rejected by all courts</li></ol><details class=\"mcq-a\"><summary>Show answer</summary><p><strong>Answer: A.</strong> If rights exist only because law says so, a legal system with no rights provisions cannot be faulted on rights grounds.</p></details></div><div class=\"study-mcq\"><p class=\"mcq-q\">Which is the strongest response to the claim that human rights are a Western imposition?</p><ol type=\"A\"><li>Those invoking human rights against a government are almost always its own citizens</li><li>The UDHR was adopted unanimously</li><li>Western states have the strongest legal systems</li><li>Cultural traditions are always outdated</li></ol><details class=\"mcq-a\"><summary>Show answer</summary><p><strong>Answer: A.</strong> The empirical pattern of who actually claims these rights undercuts the imperialism framing more effectively than any theoretical argument.</p></details></div><div class=\"study-mcq\"><p class=\"mcq-q\">Who among these helped draft the Universal Declaration of Human Rights?</p><ol type=\"A\"><li>Chang Peng-chun of China, Charles Malik of Lebanon and Hansa Mehta of India</li><li>Only representatives of the United States and United Kingdom</li><li>Only European legal scholars</li><li>Delegates appointed by colonial administrations</li></ol><details class=\"mcq-a\"><summary>Show answer</summary><p><strong>Answer: A.</strong> The drafting committee was genuinely international, which is relevant to the claim that the text is purely Western in origin.</p></details></div><h5>Short answer</h5><div class=\"study-sa\"><p class=\"sa-q\">Compare natural law and legal positivism as foundations for human rights.</p><details class=\"sa-a\"><summary>Model answer</summary><p>Natural law holds that rights derive from human nature or a moral order independent of any state, which explains why they bind even a government that never consented, though it rests on a contested account of nature. Legal positivism holds that rights exist only because a legal system recognises them, which is clear and verifiable but leaves no basis for criticising a system that recognises no rights.</p></details></div><div class=\"study-sa\"><p class=\"sa-q\">Give two serious responses to the cultural relativism critique of human rights.</p><details class=\"sa-a\"><summary>Model answer</summary><p>First, the drafting of the UDHR was genuinely international, with substantial contributions from Chinese, Lebanese and Indian delegates. Second, the framework can accommodate communal traditions, as the African Charter shows by including peoples&#x27; rights and duties to family and community alongside individual rights.</p></details></div><h4>Class Activity</h4><ol><li>Take one right and justify it from three different philosophical positions. Note where the justifications would disagree about its limits.</li><li>In pairs, one argues the cultural relativism position and the other the universalist position for eight minutes. Then each writes the strongest point the other made.</li></ol><h4>Assignment</h4><p>Write one page on the philosophical foundations of human rights. Compare at least three justifications with a strength and weakness of each, then set out the cultural relativism critique fairly and give a reasoned response to it.</p><p class=\"study-print\"><strong>Printable:</strong> use your browser\u2019s Print \u2192 Save as PDF to keep an offline copy.</p></div>",
  "Characteristics of Human Rights": "<div class=\"study-note\"><div class=\"revision-banner\"><strong>Human Rights and International Humanitarian Law</strong><span>Module 1: Foundations of Human Rights</span></div><h3 class=\"lesson-note-title\">LESSON NOTE: CHARACTERISTICS OF HUMAN RIGHTS</h3><div class=\"table-wrap\"><table class=\"study-table\"><thead><tr><th>Subject</th><th>Topic</th><th>Level</th><th>Lesson duration</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Human Rights and International Humanitarian Law</td><td>Characteristics of Human Rights</td><td>Beginner to Advanced</td><td>35 minutes</td></tr></tbody></table></div><h4>Learning objectives</h4><p>By the end of this lesson you should be able to:</p><ul><li>State and explain the core characteristics of human rights</li><li>Explain indivisibility and interdependence with examples</li><li>Distinguish absolute, derogable and qualified rights</li><li>Apply the characteristics to a concrete case</li></ul><h4>1. Introduction</h4><p>The standard list \u2014 universal, inalienable, indivisible, interdependent, equal and non-discriminatory \u2014 is easy to recite and frequently misunderstood. Two of these characteristics have important legal consequences and one of them is routinely overstated.</p><h4>2. The characteristics</h4><div class=\"table-wrap\"><table class=\"study-table\"><thead><tr><th>Characteristic</th><th>Meaning</th><th>Practical consequence</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Universal</td><td>Held by every person everywhere</td><td>A state cannot exclude a group by declaring them outside the framework</td></tr><tr><td>Inalienable</td><td>Cannot be given up or taken away</td><td>A person cannot validly consent to being enslaved or tortured</td></tr><tr><td>Indivisible</td><td>No category of rights is inherently superior to another</td><td>Civil and political rights cannot be indefinitely postponed pending development</td></tr><tr><td>Interdependent</td><td>The realisation of one right depends on others</td><td>Without education, the right to vote is much harder to exercise meaningfully</td></tr><tr><td>Equal and non-discriminatory</td><td>Held by all without distinction</td><td>Any differential treatment requires objective and reasonable justification</td></tr><tr><td>Inherent</td><td>Held by virtue of being human, not granted by any authority</td><td>Rights survive a change of government or withdrawal from a treaty</td></tr></tbody></table></div><h4>3. Indivisibility and interdependence, worked through</h4><p>These two are the most cited and least explained. Take one case and follow it through:</p><div class=\"study-callout\"><strong>Example:</strong> A girl in a rural community cannot attend school because the nearest school is far away and unsafe to reach. Her <strong>right to education</strong> is unrealised. Because she is uneducated, she is less able to obtain paid work, engaging the <strong>right to work</strong>. She is less able to read health information, engaging the <strong>right to health</strong>. She cannot easily read a ballot paper or a party manifesto, weakening the <strong>right to participate in public affairs</strong>. And her lack of independent income makes it harder to leave an abusive marriage, engaging the <strong>right to physical integrity</strong>. One unrealised right has degraded five others.</div><p>That is what interdependence means concretely: rights are not a shopping list from which a government can select. Indivisibility is the related claim that a state may not argue it will deliver economic rights first and political rights later, or the reverse.</p><h4>4. Absolute, derogable and qualified rights</h4><p>This distinction is where the characteristic of inalienability meets legal reality. Not all rights operate the same way.</p><div class=\"table-wrap\"><table class=\"study-table\"><thead><tr><th>Type</th><th>Meaning</th><th>Examples</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Absolute</td><td>Never limited, never suspended, in any circumstances</td><td>Prohibition of torture and inhuman treatment; prohibition of slavery; freedom from retroactive criminal law</td></tr><tr><td>Non-derogable but not absolute</td><td>Cannot be suspended in emergency, though the right itself has an internal scope</td><td>Right to life: cannot be derogated from, but the prohibition is of <em>arbitrary</em> deprivation</td></tr><tr><td>Derogable</td><td>May be suspended temporarily in a properly declared public emergency threatening the life of the nation</td><td>Freedom of movement; liberty; fair trial guarantees, subject to strict conditions</td></tr><tr><td>Qualified</td><td>May be limited where the limitation is lawful, pursues a legitimate aim, and is necessary and proportionate</td><td>Freedom of expression; assembly; religion in its manifestation; privacy</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"study-callout\"><strong>Note:</strong> Saying that all human rights are inalienable and absolute is a common and serious error. The prohibition of torture is absolute. Freedom of expression is not, and pretending it is makes any argument about it unpersuasive to a court.</div><h4>5. How the examiner&#x27;s question should be answered</h4><p>List the characteristics briefly, then take one \u2014 usually indivisibility or interdependence \u2014 and work a single concrete example all the way through, as above. Finish by distinguishing absolute from qualified rights, because that distinction shows you understand how the characteristics operate in law rather than in a slogan.</p><h4>Summary</h4><ul><li>Human rights are universal, inalienable, indivisible, interdependent, equal and inherent.</li><li>Interdependence means one unrealised right degrades others; indivisibility means no category may be postponed.</li><li>Absolute rights such as the prohibition of torture can never be limited or suspended.</li><li>Derogable rights may be suspended in a properly declared emergency; qualified rights may be limited if lawful, legitimate, necessary and proportionate.</li><li>Treating every right as absolute is a serious analytical error.</li></ul><h4>Key Terms</h4><div class=\"table-wrap\"><table class=\"study-table\"><thead><tr><th>Term</th><th>Meaning</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Indivisibility</td><td>The principle that no category of human rights is inherently superior to another.</td></tr><tr><td>Interdependence</td><td>The principle that the realisation of one right depends on the realisation of others.</td></tr><tr><td>Absolute right</td><td>A right that may never be limited or suspended in any circumstances.</td></tr><tr><td>Derogation</td><td>Temporary suspension of a right in a properly declared public emergency.</td></tr></tbody></table></div><h4>Review Questions</h4><h5>Multiple choice</h5><div class=\"study-mcq\"><p class=\"mcq-q\">Which of these is an absolute right?</p><ol type=\"A\"><li>The prohibition of torture</li><li>Freedom of expression</li><li>Freedom of movement</li><li>The right to privacy</li></ol><details class=\"mcq-a\"><summary>Show answer</summary><p><strong>Answer: A.</strong> Torture may never be justified in any circumstances, whereas expression, movement and privacy may all be lawfully limited.</p></details></div><div class=\"study-mcq\"><p class=\"mcq-q\">What does interdependence of rights mean?</p><ol type=\"A\"><li>The realisation of one right depends on the realisation of others</li><li>All rights must be enacted in a single statute</li><li>Rights conflict with one another</li><li>Every right has the same legal status</li></ol><details class=\"mcq-a\"><summary>Show answer</summary><p><strong>Answer: A.</strong> An unrealised right to education degrades the rights to work, health, participation and physical integrity in turn.</p></details></div><div class=\"study-mcq\"><p class=\"mcq-q\">What must a limitation on a qualified right satisfy?</p><ol type=\"A\"><li>Lawful, pursuing a legitimate aim, and necessary and proportionate</li><li>Approved by a majority of the population</li><li>Temporary and publicly announced</li><li>Applied equally to all citizens</li></ol><details class=\"mcq-a\"><summary>Show answer</summary><p><strong>Answer: A.</strong> All four elements must be satisfied; a limitation failing any of them is unlawful.</p></details></div><div class=\"study-mcq\"><p class=\"mcq-q\">Why is it an error to say all human rights are absolute?</p><ol type=\"A\"><li>Most rights are qualified or derogable, and claiming otherwise makes an argument unpersuasive</li><li>It is not an error; all rights are absolute</li><li>Only economic rights are absolute</li><li>Absoluteness depends on the state&#x27;s constitution</li></ol><details class=\"mcq-a\"><summary>Show answer</summary><p><strong>Answer: A.</strong> Precision about which rights are absolute is what allows a serious legal argument about the rest.</p></details></div><h5>Short answer</h5><div class=\"study-sa\"><p class=\"sa-q\">Explain interdependence of rights with a worked example.</p><details class=\"sa-a\"><summary>Model answer</summary><p>A girl unable to attend school has an unrealised right to education. Being uneducated reduces her access to paid work, her ability to read health information, her capacity to participate meaningfully in elections, and her independence from an abusive relationship. One unrealised right has degraded the rights to work, health, political participation and physical integrity.</p></details></div><div class=\"study-sa\"><p class=\"sa-q\">Distinguish absolute, derogable and qualified rights with an example of each.</p><details class=\"sa-a\"><summary>Model answer</summary><p>Absolute rights, such as the prohibition of torture, may never be limited or suspended. Derogable rights, such as freedom of movement, may be temporarily suspended in a properly declared emergency threatening the life of the nation. Qualified rights, such as freedom of expression, may be limited where the limitation is lawful, pursues a legitimate aim, and is necessary and proportionate.</p></details></div><h4>Class Activity</h4><ol><li>Take one right you care about and trace, in writing, three other rights whose realisation depends on it.</li><li>Classify ten rights as absolute, non-derogable, derogable or qualified. Compare your list with a partner&#x27;s and resolve any disagreement by reference to the treaty text.</li></ol><h4>Assignment</h4><p>Write one page on the characteristics of human rights. Explain each characteristic briefly, work one full example of interdependence, and set out the distinction between absolute, derogable and qualified rights with examples, explaining why the distinction matters.</p><p class=\"study-print\"><strong>Printable:</strong> use your browser\u2019s Print \u2192 Save as PDF to keep an offline copy.</p></div>",
  "Generations of Human Rights": "<div class=\"study-note\"><div class=\"revision-banner\"><strong>Human Rights and International Humanitarian Law</strong><span>Module 1: Foundations of Human Rights</span></div><h3 class=\"lesson-note-title\">LESSON NOTE: GENERATIONS OF HUMAN RIGHTS</h3><div class=\"table-wrap\"><table class=\"study-table\"><thead><tr><th>Subject</th><th>Topic</th><th>Level</th><th>Lesson duration</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Human Rights and International Humanitarian Law</td><td>Generations of Human Rights</td><td>Beginner to Advanced</td><td>35 minutes</td></tr></tbody></table></div><h4>Learning objectives</h4><p>By the end of this lesson you should be able to:</p><ul><li>Describe the three generations of human rights</li><li>Explain the historical and political context of the division</li><li>Set out the criticisms of the generational framework</li><li>Use the framework carefully without overstating it</li></ul><h4>1. Introduction</h4><p>The 'three generations' framework, proposed by Karel Vasak, is the most widely taught organising idea in human rights and one of the most criticised. It is genuinely useful for understanding history and genuinely misleading if taken as a hierarchy.</p><h4>2. The three generations</h4><div class=\"table-wrap\"><table class=\"study-table\"><thead><tr><th>Generation</th><th>Theme</th><th>Content</th><th>Principal instrument</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>First</td><td>Liberty</td><td>Civil and political rights: life, liberty, fair trial, expression, assembly, religion, participation, freedom from torture</td><td>ICCPR</td></tr><tr><td>Second</td><td>Equality</td><td>Economic, social and cultural rights: work, fair conditions, social security, health, education, adequate standard of living, cultural life</td><td>ICESCR</td></tr><tr><td>Third</td><td>Solidarity</td><td>Collective or group rights: self-determination, development, peace, a healthy environment, benefit from the common heritage of humanity</td><td>African Charter; various declarations</td></tr></tbody></table></div><p>Vasak mapped the three onto the French revolutionary slogan: libert\u00e9, \u00e9galit\u00e9, fraternit\u00e9. That is a memorable framing and also a clue that the division is a historical and rhetorical construct rather than a legal one.</p><h4>3. Why the division exists</h4><p>The split was not conceptual; it was political. When the UDHR was to be turned into a binding treaty, the Cold War divided states over which rights mattered.</p><div class=\"table-wrap\"><table class=\"study-table\"><thead><tr><th>Bloc</th><th>Emphasis</th><th>Argument</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Western states</td><td>Civil and political rights</td><td>These are immediately enforceable by courts and cost nothing but restraint</td></tr><tr><td>Socialist states</td><td>Economic and social rights</td><td>Formal liberty is empty without material security</td></tr><tr><td>Newly independent states</td><td>Self-determination and development</td><td>Neither set means much without ending colonial domination and poverty</td></tr></tbody></table></div><p>The compromise was two separate covenants, both adopted in 1966, with different implementation regimes: immediate obligation for the ICCPR, progressive realisation for the ICESCR. The generational framework is essentially a description of that compromise.</p><h4>4. The criticisms</h4><ol><li><strong>It implies a hierarchy.</strong> 'First' sounds prior and more important, which contradicts the principle of indivisibility affirmed at the 1993 Vienna World Conference.</li><li><strong>The categories leak.</strong> The right to form a trade union appears in both covenants. The right to education has obvious civil and political dimensions.</li><li><strong>The cost distinction is false.</strong> Civil and political rights are said to be cost-free, but running courts, training police and holding elections is expensive. Fair trial rights are among the most costly of all.</li><li><strong>It is chronologically inaccurate.</strong> Early labour conventions on working conditions predate several first-generation instruments.</li><li><strong>Third-generation rights are legally uncertain.</strong> Who holds the right to peace, and against whom is it claimed?</li></ol><div class=\"study-callout\"><strong>Note:</strong> The cost objection is the one to remember, because it undercuts the main practical argument used to postpone economic and social rights. A state that says it cannot afford the right to health while funding a court system has made a choice about priorities, not encountered a difference in kind.</div><h4>5. Using the framework carefully</h4><ul><li>Use it to explain the historical split into two covenants; that is what it describes accurately.</li><li>Do not use it to rank rights or to justify postponing one category.</li><li>Be aware that many scholars and practitioners now avoid it entirely, preferring to speak of the tripartite obligations, which apply identically to all rights.</li><li>In an examination, showing that you know the criticisms is worth more than reciting the three categories.</li></ul><h4>Summary</h4><ul><li>Vasak&#x27;s three generations are civil and political, economic and social, and solidarity rights.</li><li>The division reflects a Cold War political compromise, not a conceptual distinction, and produced two covenants in 1966.</li><li>The framework is criticised for implying a hierarchy, leaking categories, a false cost distinction and chronological inaccuracy.</li><li>The tripartite obligation framework applies identically to all rights and is now often preferred.</li></ul><h4>Key Terms</h4><div class=\"table-wrap\"><table class=\"study-table\"><thead><tr><th>Term</th><th>Meaning</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>First generation rights</td><td>Civil and political rights, associated with liberty.</td></tr><tr><td>Second generation rights</td><td>Economic, social and cultural rights, associated with equality.</td></tr><tr><td>Third generation rights</td><td>Collective or solidarity rights such as self-determination and development.</td></tr><tr><td>Vienna Declaration 1993</td><td>The instrument affirming that all human rights are universal, indivisible, interdependent and interrelated.</td></tr></tbody></table></div><h4>Review Questions</h4><h5>Multiple choice</h5><div class=\"study-mcq\"><p class=\"mcq-q\">Why were two separate covenants adopted in 1966 rather than one?</p><ol type=\"A\"><li>Cold War disagreement between states over which category of rights mattered</li><li>The text was too long for one treaty</li><li>Different UN bodies drafted them</li><li>Economic rights were discovered later</li></ol><details class=\"mcq-a\"><summary>Show answer</summary><p><strong>Answer: A.</strong> The split was a political compromise, and the generational framework essentially describes that compromise.</p></details></div><div class=\"study-mcq\"><p class=\"mcq-q\">What is the strongest criticism of the claim that civil and political rights are cost-free?</p><ol type=\"A\"><li>Courts, police training and elections are expensive, and fair trial rights are among the costliest</li><li>Civil rights are actually cheaper than claimed</li><li>The claim is correct</li><li>Costs are borne by individuals, not states</li></ol><details class=\"mcq-a\"><summary>Show answer</summary><p><strong>Answer: A.</strong> The cost objection undercuts the main practical argument used to postpone economic and social rights.</p></details></div><div class=\"study-mcq\"><p class=\"mcq-q\">Which instrument affirmed that all human rights are indivisible and interdependent?</p><ol type=\"A\"><li>The Vienna Declaration and Programme of Action 1993</li><li>The UDHR 1948</li><li>The ICCPR 1966</li><li>The African Charter 1981</li></ol><details class=\"mcq-a\"><summary>Show answer</summary><p><strong>Answer: A.</strong> Vienna explicitly rejected any hierarchy between categories of rights.</p></details></div><div class=\"study-mcq\"><p class=\"mcq-q\">What is the main danger of the generational framework?</p><ol type=\"A\"><li>It implies a hierarchy in which first-generation rights come first</li><li>It is too difficult for students</li><li>It omits civil rights</li><li>It applies only in Europe</li></ol><details class=\"mcq-a\"><summary>Show answer</summary><p><strong>Answer: A.</strong> &#x27;First&#x27; sounds prior and more important, which contradicts the principle of indivisibility.</p></details></div><h5>Short answer</h5><div class=\"study-sa\"><p class=\"sa-q\">Name the three generations of rights with two examples of each.</p><details class=\"sa-a\"><summary>Model answer</summary><p>First generation, civil and political: freedom from torture and the right to a fair trial. Second generation, economic, social and cultural: the rights to health and education. Third generation, solidarity rights: self-determination and the right to development.</p></details></div><div class=\"study-sa\"><p class=\"sa-q\">Give three criticisms of the generational framework.</p><details class=\"sa-a\"><summary>Model answer</summary><p>It implies a hierarchy that contradicts the principle of indivisibility. The categories leak, since rights such as trade union freedom appear in both covenants. And the claim that civil and political rights are cost-free is false, since courts, policing and elections are expensive.</p></details></div><h4>Class Activity</h4><ol><li>Take five rights and try to place each in a generation. Note every case where the classification is genuinely ambiguous and explain why.</li><li>In groups, argue for and against the proposition that the generational framework should be abandoned in teaching.</li></ol><h4>Assignment</h4><p>Write one page on the generations of human rights. Set out the three generations with examples, explain the historical reason for the division, and assess at least three criticisms of the framework, giving your own reasoned view on whether it remains useful.</p><p class=\"study-print\"><strong>Printable:</strong> use your browser\u2019s Print \u2192 Save as PDF to keep an offline copy.</p></div>",
  "Rights, Duties and Responsibilities": "<div class=\"study-note\"><div class=\"revision-banner\"><strong>Human Rights and International Humanitarian Law</strong><span>Module 1: Foundations of Human Rights</span></div><h3 class=\"lesson-note-title\">LESSON NOTE: RIGHTS, DUTIES AND RESPONSIBILITIES</h3><div class=\"table-wrap\"><table class=\"study-table\"><thead><tr><th>Subject</th><th>Topic</th><th>Level</th><th>Lesson duration</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Human Rights and International Humanitarian Law</td><td>Rights, Duties and Responsibilities</td><td>Beginner to Advanced</td><td>35 minutes</td></tr></tbody></table></div><h4>Learning objectives</h4><p>By the end of this lesson you should be able to:</p><ul><li>Explain the relationship between rights and duties</li><li>Distinguish correlative duties from individual responsibilities</li><li>Describe how the African Charter treats duties</li><li>Recognise when a duties argument is being misused</li></ul><h4>1. Introduction</h4><p>Every right implies a duty on someone. That much is uncontroversial. What is contested, and politically loaded, is whether the individual rights holder also owes duties as a condition of holding the right.</p><h4>2. Correlative duties</h4><div class=\"study-defn\"><strong>Correlative duty:</strong> The obligation that necessarily corresponds to a right, held by the duty bearer.</div><div class=\"table-wrap\"><table class=\"study-table\"><thead><tr><th>Right</th><th>Correlative duty</th><th>Held by</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Not to be tortured</td><td>Not to torture; to prevent and investigate torture</td><td>The state</td></tr><tr><td>To education</td><td>To provide accessible free primary education</td><td>The state</td></tr><tr><td>To a fair trial</td><td>To maintain independent courts and provide legal assistance</td><td>The state</td></tr></tbody></table></div><p>Note that the correlative duty falls on the state, not on other individuals. My right not to be tortured does not create a duty in you to do anything; it creates a duty in the state.</p><h4>3. Individual responsibilities</h4><p>Separately from correlative duties, most rights frameworks recognise that individuals have responsibilities. Article 29 of the UDHR states that everyone has duties to the community, and that rights may be limited to secure recognition of the rights of others and the requirements of morality, public order and general welfare in a democratic society.</p><ul><li>The clearest individual responsibility is to respect the rights of others: my freedom of expression does not extend to defaming you.</li><li>Duties to the community \u2014 such as paying tax, obeying the law, serving on a jury \u2014 arise under domestic law, not international human rights law.</li><li>Article 29 is a limitation clause as much as a duties clause, which is why its wording matters.</li></ul><h4>4. The African Charter and duties</h4><p>The African Charter on Human and Peoples' Rights is distinctive in giving duties substantial treatment. Articles 27 to 29 set out duties towards family, society, the state and the international community, including duties to preserve family harmony, to serve the national community, and to preserve and strengthen positive African cultural values.</p><div class=\"table-wrap\"><table class=\"study-table\"><thead><tr><th>Reading</th><th>Argument</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Positive</td><td>It reflects communal traditions in which the individual is situated in a family and community, correcting an over-individualistic framing, and it answers the cultural relativism critique from within Africa</td></tr><tr><td>Cautionary</td><td>Duties provisions can be invoked by governments to condition rights on good behaviour, which is exactly what inalienability forbids</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"study-callout\"><strong>Note:</strong> The African Commission has generally interpreted the duty provisions so that they do not undermine the rights provisions. That is the correct approach: duties inform how rights are exercised; they are not preconditions for holding them.</div><h4>5. When a duties argument is being misused</h4><div class=\"table-wrap\"><table class=\"study-table\"><thead><tr><th>Legitimate use</th><th>Misuse</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>'Your freedom of assembly does not include destroying property'</td><td>'Only law-abiding citizens have rights'</td></tr><tr><td>'Free expression carries responsibility not to incite violence'</td><td>'Critics of the government have failed their duty to the nation, so may be detained'</td></tr><tr><td>'Parents have responsibilities towards their children'</td><td>'People who do not pay tax forfeit the right to a fair trial'</td></tr></tbody></table></div><p>The test is straightforward. A duties argument is legitimate when it defines the boundary of a right against the rights of others. It is a misuse when it makes the right itself conditional on the holder's conduct, because a right that can be forfeited by behaviour is not a human right at all.</p><div class=\"study-callout\"><strong>Example:</strong> A government arrests journalists on the basis that they have breached their duty not to endanger national unity. Duties language is doing the work of a limitation without any of the safeguards a lawful limitation requires: legality, legitimate aim, necessity and proportionality. This is the characteristic pattern of misuse.</div><h4>Summary</h4><ul><li>Every right has a correlative duty, and for human rights that duty falls primarily on the state.</li><li>Individuals have responsibilities, chiefly to respect the rights of others; UDHR Article 29 states this and also functions as a limitation clause.</li><li>The African Charter treats duties substantially, reflecting communal traditions, and the African Commission reads them so as not to undermine rights.</li><li>A duties argument is legitimate when it marks the boundary of a right and a misuse when it makes the right conditional on conduct.</li></ul><h4>Key Terms</h4><div class=\"table-wrap\"><table class=\"study-table\"><thead><tr><th>Term</th><th>Meaning</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Correlative duty</td><td>The obligation corresponding to a right, held by the duty bearer.</td></tr><tr><td>Article 29 UDHR</td><td>The provision recognising duties to the community and permitting limitations on rights.</td></tr><tr><td>African Charter duties</td><td>Articles 27 to 29, setting out individual duties to family, society and the state.</td></tr><tr><td>Forfeiture</td><td>The mistaken idea that a person can lose human rights through their own conduct.</td></tr></tbody></table></div><h4>Review Questions</h4><h5>Multiple choice</h5><div class=\"study-mcq\"><p class=\"mcq-q\">On whom does the correlative duty of a human right primarily fall?</p><ol type=\"A\"><li>The state</li><li>Other individuals</li><li>The international community</li><li>The rights holder</li></ol><details class=\"mcq-a\"><summary>Show answer</summary><p><strong>Answer: A.</strong> My right not to be tortured creates a duty in the state, not in other private persons.</p></details></div><div class=\"study-mcq\"><p class=\"mcq-q\">What makes a duties argument a misuse?</p><ol type=\"A\"><li>When it makes the right itself conditional on the holder&#x27;s conduct</li><li>When it refers to the African Charter</li><li>When it mentions the community</li><li>When it limits any right at all</li></ol><details class=\"mcq-a\"><summary>Show answer</summary><p><strong>Answer: A.</strong> A right that can be forfeited through behaviour is not a human right, because human rights are inalienable.</p></details></div><div class=\"study-mcq\"><p class=\"mcq-q\">How has the African Commission generally interpreted the Charter&#x27;s duty provisions?</p><ol type=\"A\"><li>So that they do not undermine the rights provisions</li><li>As overriding the rights provisions</li><li>As applying only to states</li><li>As legally unenforceable and irrelevant</li></ol><details class=\"mcq-a\"><summary>Show answer</summary><p><strong>Answer: A.</strong> Duties inform how rights are exercised; they are not preconditions for holding them.</p></details></div><div class=\"study-mcq\"><p class=\"mcq-q\">Which is a legitimate use of a duties argument?</p><ol type=\"A\"><li>&#x27;Free expression carries a responsibility not to incite violence&#x27;</li><li>&#x27;Only law-abiding citizens have rights&#x27;</li><li>&#x27;Critics of the government have failed their duty to the nation&#x27;</li><li>&#x27;Non-taxpayers forfeit fair trial rights&#x27;</li></ol><details class=\"mcq-a\"><summary>Show answer</summary><p><strong>Answer: A.</strong> It defines the boundary of the right against the rights of others rather than making the right conditional.</p></details></div><h5>Short answer</h5><div class=\"study-sa\"><p class=\"sa-q\">Explain the difference between a correlative duty and an individual responsibility.</p><details class=\"sa-a\"><summary>Model answer</summary><p>A correlative duty is the obligation that necessarily corresponds to a right and, in human rights law, falls on the state: the right not to be tortured creates the state&#x27;s duty not to torture and to prevent and investigate torture. An individual responsibility is a duty owed by a person to others or the community, chiefly to respect the rights of others, which does not condition their own rights.</p></details></div><div class=\"study-sa\"><p class=\"sa-q\">Give one positive and one cautionary reading of the African Charter&#x27;s duty provisions.</p><details class=\"sa-a\"><summary>Model answer</summary><p>Positively, they reflect communal traditions in which the individual is situated within family and community, correcting an over-individualistic framing and answering the relativism critique from within Africa. Cautiously, duty provisions can be invoked by governments to condition rights on good behaviour, which inalienability forbids.</p></details></div><h4>Class Activity</h4><ol><li>Find three statements by public figures that use duties language about rights. Classify each as a legitimate boundary argument or a misuse, with your reasons.</li><li>Read Articles 27 to 29 of the African Charter and write one paragraph on whether you find the duties provisions valuable or risky.</li></ol><h4>Assignment</h4><p>Write one page on rights and duties. Explain correlative duties and individual responsibilities, describe how the African Charter treats duties with both a positive and a cautionary reading, and set out the test for distinguishing a legitimate duties argument from a misuse.</p><p class=\"study-print\"><strong>Printable:</strong> use your browser\u2019s Print \u2192 Save as PDF to keep an offline copy.</p></div>",
  "Course Roadmap": "<div class=\"study-note\"><div class=\"revision-banner\"><strong>Human Rights and International Humanitarian Law</strong><span>Module 1: Foundations of Human Rights</span></div><h3 class=\"lesson-note-title\">LESSON NOTE: COURSE ROADMAP</h3><div class=\"table-wrap\"><table class=\"study-table\"><thead><tr><th>Subject</th><th>Topic</th><th>Level</th><th>Lesson duration</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Human Rights and International Humanitarian Law</td><td>Course Roadmap</td><td>Beginner to Advanced</td><td>25 minutes</td></tr></tbody></table></div><h4>Learning objectives</h4><p>By the end of this lesson you should be able to:</p><ul><li>Describe how the fourteen modules fit together</li><li>Explain what you will produce at each stage</li><li>Plan your route through the course and its assessments</li></ul><h4>1. Introduction</h4><p>You now have the conceptual foundations: what rights are, where they are said to come from, how they behave, and how they relate to duties. From here the course moves to the actual instruments, the systems that enforce them, and the separate body of law that governs armed conflict.</p><h4>2. The full map</h4><div class=\"table-wrap\"><table class=\"study-table\"><thead><tr><th>Modules</th><th>Theme</th><th>What you will be able to do</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>1 \u2013 2</td><td>Foundations and sources</td><td>Define rights, apply the tripartite obligations, identify sources of international law, explain treaties, custom and derogation</td></tr><tr><td>3 \u2013 4</td><td>The instruments</td><td>Work with the ICCPR, ICESCR and the eight thematic treaties</td></tr><tr><td>5 \u2013 6</td><td>The systems</td><td>Use the African, European and Inter-American systems and the UN machinery, including the UPR and individual complaints</td></tr><tr><td>7 \u2013 8</td><td>IHL foundations and principles</td><td>Classify a conflict and apply distinction, proportionality, necessity and precautions</td></tr><tr><td>9 \u2013 10</td><td>Protection and weapons</td><td>Identify protected persons and objects and the limits on means and methods of warfare</td></tr><tr><td>11</td><td>Accountability</td><td>Distinguish war crimes, genocide and crimes against humanity, and explain the courts that try them</td></tr><tr><td>12</td><td>Practice</td><td>Monitor, document, interview safely, write reports and advocate</td></tr><tr><td>13</td><td>Projects</td><td>Produce six portfolio pieces</td></tr><tr><td>14</td><td>Assessments</td><td>Four subject quizzes, midterm, final, capstone and certificate</td></tr></tbody></table></div><h4>3. What you will produce</h4><ol><li>A human rights monitoring report.</li><li>A treaty analysis, tracing one right through an instrument and its interpretation.</li><li>An IHL case study applying the principles to a real or constructed scenario.</li><li>An advocacy campaign plan.</li><li>A documentation interview guide built on do-no-harm principles.</li><li>A transitional justice proposal.</li></ol><p>These are the documents human rights organisations actually produce. Treat them as work samples for a CV, not as homework.</p><h4>4. How the assessments work</h4><div class=\"table-wrap\"><table class=\"study-table\"><thead><tr><th>Assessment</th><th>Draws on</th><th>Purpose</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Practice quiz after each lesson</td><td>That lesson only</td><td>Immediate check of understanding</td></tr><tr><td>Four subject quizzes</td><td>Several modules at once</td><td>Test that you can connect topics</td></tr><tr><td>Midterm and final examinations</td><td>The course so far</td><td>Test retention across the programme</td></tr><tr><td>Capstone presentation</td><td>Everything</td><td>Analyse a real case as a practitioner would</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"study-callout\"><strong>Note:</strong> Every assessment question is new. Nothing met in a practice quiz reappears in an examination, so a good result reflects command of the subject rather than recall of a question bank.</div><h4>5. Planning your route</h4><ul><li>Do the modules in order; Module 7 onwards assumes you can already handle treaty sources.</li><li>Do not skip Module 2. Sources of law is the least glamorous module and the one that makes every later argument possible.</li><li>Read at least one primary text in full during the course: the UDHR is two pages and takes fifteen minutes.</li><li>Start the Module 13 projects early, choosing a country or situation you will follow throughout.</li></ul><h4>Summary</h4><ul><li>The course runs from foundations and sources, through instruments and systems, to IHL, accountability and practice.</li><li>Six portfolio projects mirror the documents human rights organisations actually produce.</li><li>Practice quizzes, subject quizzes, examinations and a capstone assess different depths of learning, with no question reused.</li><li>Module 2 on sources is essential groundwork for every later argument.</li></ul><h4>Key Terms</h4><div class=\"table-wrap\"><table class=\"study-table\"><thead><tr><th>Term</th><th>Meaning</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Capstone</td><td>The final project analysing a real human rights case.</td></tr><tr><td>Portfolio</td><td>The set of documents produced across the course as work samples.</td></tr><tr><td>Primary text</td><td>The original treaty or declaration, as opposed to a commentary on it.</td></tr></tbody></table></div><h4>Review Questions</h4><h5>Multiple choice</h5><div class=\"study-mcq\"><p class=\"mcq-q\">Which module is described as essential groundwork for every later argument?</p><ol type=\"A\"><li>Module 2, History and Sources of Human Rights Law</li><li>Module 13, Practical Projects</li><li>Module 1, Foundations</li><li>Module 12, Human Rights in Practice</li></ol><details class=\"mcq-a\"><summary>Show answer</summary><p><strong>Answer: A.</strong> Without knowing how treaties, custom and derogation work, no later argument about a specific right can be made properly.</p></details></div><div class=\"study-mcq\"><p class=\"mcq-q\">Why does no question appear in more than one assessment?</p><ol type=\"A\"><li>So results reflect command of the subject rather than recall of a question bank</li><li>To make the course harder</li><li>Because the law changes frequently</li><li>So quizzes can be reused next year</li></ol><details class=\"mcq-a\"><summary>Show answer</summary><p><strong>Answer: A.</strong> Fresh questions measure understanding of the topic rather than memory of previously seen items.</p></details></div><div class=\"study-mcq\"><p class=\"mcq-q\">What are the Module 13 projects intended to be?</p><ol type=\"A\"><li>Work samples of the documents human rights organisations actually produce</li><li>Written examinations</li><li>Summaries of the lesson notes</li><li>Group presentations only</li></ol><details class=\"mcq-a\"><summary>Show answer</summary><p><strong>Answer: A.</strong> They mirror real professional outputs and belong in a portfolio rather than a homework folder.</p></details></div><div class=\"study-mcq\"><p class=\"mcq-q\">Which primary text can be read in full in about fifteen minutes?</p><ol type=\"A\"><li>The Universal Declaration of Human Rights</li><li>The ICCPR</li><li>The Rome Statute</li><li>The Geneva Conventions</li></ol><details class=\"mcq-a\"><summary>Show answer</summary><p><strong>Answer: A.</strong> The UDHR runs to thirty short articles across roughly two pages.</p></details></div><h5>Short answer</h5><div class=\"study-sa\"><p class=\"sa-q\">Name the six portfolio pieces produced in this course.</p><details class=\"sa-a\"><summary>Model answer</summary><p>A human rights monitoring report; a treaty analysis; an IHL case study; an advocacy campaign plan; a documentation interview guide; and a transitional justice proposal.</p></details></div><div class=\"study-sa\"><p class=\"sa-q\">Explain the difference in purpose between a practice quiz and the capstone presentation.</p><details class=\"sa-a\"><summary>Model answer</summary><p>A practice quiz checks understanding of a single lesson immediately after study, so gaps can be closed at once. The capstone requires analysing a real case as a practitioner would, drawing on the whole course, and tests whether you can apply the law rather than recall it.</p></details></div><h4>Class Activity</h4><ol><li>Write a study plan with a target date for each block of modules and mark the six points at which a project is due.</li><li>Choose the country or situation you will use for your projects. Write three sentences on why you chose it and what sources you will be able to access.</li></ol><h4>Assignment</h4><p>Produce a one-page personal learning plan. State your target dates, name the country or situation you will follow through the projects, identify the two modules you expect to find hardest and why, and read the Universal Declaration of Human Rights in full and note the three articles that most surprised you.</p><p class=\"study-print\"><strong>Printable:</strong> use your browser\u2019s Print \u2192 Save as PDF to keep an offline copy.</p></div>",
  "The Origins of Human Rights": "<div class=\"study-note\"><div class=\"revision-banner\"><strong>Human Rights and International Humanitarian Law</strong><span>Module 2: History and Sources of Human Rights Law</span></div><h3 class=\"lesson-note-title\">LESSON NOTE: THE ORIGINS OF HUMAN RIGHTS</h3><div class=\"table-wrap\"><table class=\"study-table\"><thead><tr><th>Subject</th><th>Topic</th><th>Level</th><th>Lesson duration</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Human Rights and International Humanitarian Law</td><td>The Origins of Human Rights</td><td>Beginner to Advanced</td><td>40 minutes</td></tr></tbody></table></div><h4>Learning objectives</h4><p>By the end of this lesson you should be able to:</p><ul><li>Trace the development of rights thinking from early charters to 1945</li><li>Explain what each milestone actually achieved and what it did not</li><li>Describe why the Second World War transformed rights into international law</li><li>Avoid the common exaggerations in this history</li></ul><h4>1. Introduction</h4><p>The history of human rights is routinely told as a smooth ascent from Magna Carta to the UDHR. The real story is more interesting and more useful: a series of documents that protected some people against some abuses, gradually generalised, and then internationalised only after a catastrophe made domestic protection look insufficient.</p><h4>2. The milestones, accurately described</h4><div class=\"table-wrap\"><table class=\"study-table\"><thead><tr><th>Date</th><th>Instrument</th><th>What it actually did</th><th>What it did not do</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>1215</td><td>Magna Carta</td><td>Bound the English king to legal limits, notably that free men could not be imprisoned except by lawful judgment of peers or the law of the land</td><td>Apply to most of the population; 'free men' excluded the unfree majority</td></tr><tr><td>1689</td><td>English Bill of Rights</td><td>Limited royal power, established parliamentary privileges, prohibited cruel and unusual punishment</td><td>Create universal rights; it protected Parliament and Protestant subjects</td></tr><tr><td>1776</td><td>US Declaration of Independence</td><td>Declared that all men are created equal with unalienable rights</td><td>Extend to enslaved people, indigenous peoples, or women</td></tr><tr><td>1789</td><td>French Declaration of the Rights of Man and of the Citizen</td><td>Stated rights as universal and inherent in a general text</td><td>Extend to women, as Olympe de Gouges pointed out at the time, or to the colonies</td></tr><tr><td>1863</td><td>Lieber Code</td><td>Codified rules for the conduct of Union forces in the US Civil War</td><td>Bind other states</td></tr><tr><td>1864</td><td>First Geneva Convention</td><td>Created binding international obligations to care for wounded soldiers</td><td>Protect civilians</td></tr><tr><td>1919</td><td>ILO Constitution</td><td>Created an international organisation with binding labour conventions</td><td>Address rights generally</td></tr><tr><td>1926</td><td>Slavery Convention</td><td>Bound states to suppress slavery</td><td>Establish enforcement machinery</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"study-callout\"><strong>Note:</strong> The honest version of this history includes the exclusions. Every one of these documents protected some people and not others, and saying so is not cynicism: it is the point. The movement from 'the rights of Englishmen' to 'the rights of man' to 'human rights' is precisely the movement of expanding who counts.</div><h4>3. Non-Western antecedents</h4><p>Rights thinking is not exclusively European in origin, and this matters for the relativism debate covered earlier.</p><ul><li>The Cyrus Cylinder from ancient Persia, often described as an early charter of rights, records the restoration of displaced peoples and their sanctuaries, though describing it as a human rights document is an overstatement.</li><li>The Charter of Mande, associated with the Mali Empire of Sundiata Keita in the thirteenth century, includes provisions on the sanctity of life, personal liberty and the prohibition of ill-treatment.</li><li>Islamic, Confucian and Buddhist traditions each contain substantial teaching on obligations towards the vulnerable, though framed as duties rather than as individual claim-rights.</li></ul><p>The careful position is that many traditions contain deep resources on human dignity and obligation, while the specific legal form of an individual right claimable against the state has a more particular history. Both halves of that sentence matter.</p><h4>4. Why 1945 changed everything</h4><p>Before the Second World War, how a state treated its own nationals was regarded as a domestic matter protected by sovereignty. Three things broke that.</p><ol><li><strong>The scale of the Holocaust.</strong> The atrocities were largely committed by a state against its own citizens, lawfully under its own domestic law. Domestic legality was shown to be no protection at all.</li><li><strong>Nuremberg.</strong> The trials established that individuals could be criminally responsible under international law for acts their own state had authorised, and that 'I followed orders' was not a complete defence.</li><li><strong>The UN Charter.</strong> Its preamble reaffirms faith in fundamental human rights, and Articles 1 and 55 make promoting respect for human rights a purpose of the organisation, putting the subject on the international agenda permanently.</li></ol><div class=\"study-callout\"><strong>Example:</strong> The significance of the domestic-legality point is hard to overstate. The Nuremberg Laws were validly enacted German law. If law is only what a state enacts, no criticism of them is possible in legal terms. This is exactly why the natural-law tradition regained influence after 1945, and why the UDHR speaks of rights as inherent rather than granted.</div><h4>5. Common exaggerations to avoid</h4><div class=\"table-wrap\"><table class=\"study-table\"><thead><tr><th>Exaggeration</th><th>Accurate version</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Magna Carta created human rights</td><td>It bound a king to law, for a limited class, in a feudal dispute</td></tr><tr><td>The UDHR is legally binding</td><td>It is a declaration; much of it has since become binding through custom and treaty</td></tr><tr><td>Human rights are entirely a Western invention</td><td>Many traditions contain relevant resources; the specific legal form has a particular history</td></tr><tr><td>Nuremberg created international criminal law from nothing</td><td>It built on existing laws of war, though the crimes against humanity charge was substantially novel</td></tr></tbody></table></div><h4>Summary</h4><ul><li>Early charters protected some people against some abuses; the history is one of expanding who counts.</li><li>Non-Western traditions, including the Charter of Mande, contain substantial resources on dignity and obligation.</li><li>The Holocaust showed domestic legality is no protection, Nuremberg established individual responsibility, and the UN Charter internationalised the subject.</li><li>Avoid the standard exaggerations, especially treating the UDHR as binding in itself.</li></ul><h4>Key Terms</h4><div class=\"table-wrap\"><table class=\"study-table\"><thead><tr><th>Term</th><th>Meaning</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Magna Carta</td><td>The 1215 charter binding the English king to legal limits for free men.</td></tr><tr><td>Lieber Code</td><td>The 1863 codification of rules for Union forces in the US Civil War.</td></tr><tr><td>Charter of Mande</td><td>A thirteenth-century West African charter associated with the Mali Empire, containing provisions on life and liberty.</td></tr><tr><td>Nuremberg</td><td>The post-war trials establishing individual criminal responsibility under international law.</td></tr></tbody></table></div><h4>Review Questions</h4><h5>Multiple choice</h5><div class=\"study-mcq\"><p class=\"mcq-q\">What did Magna Carta actually achieve?</p><ol type=\"A\"><li>It bound the English king to legal limits for free men, who were a minority</li><li>It created universal human rights</li><li>It abolished slavery in England</li><li>It established a parliament elected by all</li></ol><details class=\"mcq-a\"><summary>Show answer</summary><p><strong>Answer: A.</strong> Describing it as the origin of human rights overstates a feudal settlement that excluded most of the population.</p></details></div><div class=\"study-mcq\"><p class=\"mcq-q\">Why did the Holocaust transform the international approach to rights?</p><ol type=\"A\"><li>The atrocities were lawful under the state&#x27;s own domestic law, showing domestic legality is no protection</li><li>Because the UN did not exist yet</li><li>Because other states intervened successfully</li><li>Because it involved more than one country</li></ol><details class=\"mcq-a\"><summary>Show answer</summary><p><strong>Answer: A.</strong> The Nuremberg Laws were validly enacted; if law is only what a state enacts, no legal criticism of them is possible.</p></details></div><div class=\"study-mcq\"><p class=\"mcq-q\">What did the Nuremberg trials establish?</p><ol type=\"A\"><li>Individuals can be criminally responsible under international law for acts their state authorised</li><li>States can be fined for human rights violations</li><li>The UDHR is legally binding</li><li>Sovereignty is abolished</li></ol><details class=\"mcq-a\"><summary>Show answer</summary><p><strong>Answer: A.</strong> They also established that following superior orders is not a complete defence.</p></details></div><div class=\"study-mcq\"><p class=\"mcq-q\">Which statement about the UDHR is accurate?</p><ol type=\"A\"><li>It is a declaration, though much of it has since become binding through custom and treaty</li><li>It was a binding treaty from adoption</li><li>It has no legal significance whatever</li><li>It applies only to UN member states that voted for it</li></ol><details class=\"mcq-a\"><summary>Show answer</summary><p><strong>Answer: A.</strong> Its legal force today comes from subsequent customary status and treaty codification, not from the instrument&#x27;s original form.</p></details></div><h5>Short answer</h5><div class=\"study-sa\"><p class=\"sa-q\">Explain why the Second World War transformed how human rights were treated in international law.</p><details class=\"sa-a\"><summary>Model answer</summary><p>Before 1945 a state&#x27;s treatment of its own nationals was regarded as a domestic matter shielded by sovereignty. The Holocaust demonstrated that domestic legality offered no protection, since the atrocities were lawful under German law. Nuremberg established individual criminal responsibility under international law, and the UN Charter made promoting respect for human rights a purpose of the organisation.</p></details></div><div class=\"study-sa\"><p class=\"sa-q\">Give two non-Western antecedents of rights thinking and state the careful position on them.</p><details class=\"sa-a\"><summary>Model answer</summary><p>The Charter of Mande, associated with the thirteenth-century Mali Empire, includes provisions on the sanctity of life and personal liberty; and Islamic, Confucian and Buddhist traditions contain substantial teaching on obligations to the vulnerable. The careful position is that many traditions contain deep resources on dignity and obligation, while the specific legal form of an individual right claimable against the state has a more particular history.</p></details></div><h4>Class Activity</h4><ol><li>Build a timeline of eight milestones. For each, write one sentence on what it achieved and one on whom it excluded.</li><li>Read the first three articles of the UDHR alongside the first three of the 1789 French Declaration. Note every difference in scope and wording.</li></ol><h4>Assignment</h4><p>Write one page tracing the origins of human rights. Cover at least five milestones with an accurate account of what each achieved and did not achieve, include at least one non-Western antecedent, and explain why 1945 marks the decisive turning point.</p><p class=\"study-print\"><strong>Printable:</strong> use your browser\u2019s Print \u2192 Save as PDF to keep an offline copy.</p></div>",
  "The Universal Declaration of Human Rights": "<div class=\"study-note\"><div class=\"revision-banner\"><strong>Human Rights and International Humanitarian Law</strong><span>Module 2: History and Sources of Human Rights Law</span></div><h3 class=\"lesson-note-title\">LESSON NOTE: THE UNIVERSAL DECLARATION OF HUMAN RIGHTS</h3><div class=\"table-wrap\"><table class=\"study-table\"><thead><tr><th>Subject</th><th>Topic</th><th>Level</th><th>Lesson duration</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Human Rights and International Humanitarian Law</td><td>The Universal Declaration of Human Rights</td><td>Beginner to Advanced</td><td>40 minutes</td></tr></tbody></table></div><h4>Learning objectives</h4><p>By the end of this lesson you should be able to:</p><ul><li>Describe the drafting, adoption and structure of the UDHR</li><li>Explain its legal status accurately</li><li>Summarise the rights it contains</li><li>Explain its influence on later law</li></ul><h4>1. Introduction</h4><p>The Universal Declaration of Human Rights, adopted by the UN General Assembly on 10 December 1948, is the founding text of the modern system. It is thirty short articles, it is not a treaty, and it is nonetheless the most influential human rights document ever written.</p><h4>2. Drafting and adoption</h4><div class=\"table-wrap\"><table class=\"study-table\"><thead><tr><th>Fact</th><th>Detail</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Body</td><td>Drafted by the Commission on Human Rights, chaired by Eleanor Roosevelt</td></tr><tr><td>Key drafters</td><td>Ren\u00e9 Cassin of France; Chang Peng-chun of China; Charles Malik of Lebanon; John Humphrey of Canada; Hansa Mehta of India</td></tr><tr><td>Adopted</td><td>10 December 1948, Paris, by the UN General Assembly</td></tr><tr><td>Vote</td><td>48 in favour, none against, 8 abstentions</td></tr><tr><td>Abstaining states</td><td>The Soviet bloc, Saudi Arabia and South Africa</td></tr></tbody></table></div><p>The abstentions are instructive. The Soviet bloc objected that the text underweighted economic and social rights and interfered in domestic affairs. Saudi Arabia objected to the provisions on freedom to change religion and on equal marriage rights. South Africa, then constructing apartheid, could not accept the equality provisions. Nobody voted against.</p><div class=\"study-callout\"><strong>Note:</strong> Hansa Mehta of India is credited with changing the opening line from 'all men are born free and equal' to 'all human beings', which is a small edit with substantial consequences and a useful answer to the claim that the drafting was purely Western and male.</div><h4>3. Structure and content</h4><div class=\"table-wrap\"><table class=\"study-table\"><thead><tr><th>Articles</th><th>Content</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Preamble</td><td>Recognises inherent dignity and equal inalienable rights as the foundation of freedom, justice and peace</td></tr><tr><td>1 \u2013 2</td><td>Foundational: all born free and equal in dignity and rights; entitlement without distinction of any kind</td></tr><tr><td>3 \u2013 21</td><td>Civil and political rights: life, liberty and security; no slavery; no torture; recognition before the law; equality before the law; effective remedy; no arbitrary arrest; fair trial; presumption of innocence; privacy; movement; asylum; nationality; marriage; property; thought, conscience and religion; opinion and expression; assembly and association; participation in government</td></tr><tr><td>22 \u2013 27</td><td>Economic, social and cultural rights: social security; work and fair pay; rest and leisure; adequate standard of living; education; participation in cultural life</td></tr><tr><td>28 \u2013 30</td><td>Framework: entitlement to a social and international order in which rights can be realised; duties to the community; and a prohibition on reading the Declaration as permitting the destruction of any of its rights</td></tr></tbody></table></div><p>Article 30 is worth noting. It prevents the Declaration being used against itself \u2014 a state cannot invoke a right in the Declaration as a licence to destroy other rights in it.</p><h4>4. Legal status, stated precisely</h4><p>This is the single question most often answered badly.</p><ol><li>The UDHR is a resolution of the General Assembly. As such it was <strong>not binding</strong> as a treaty when adopted, and it created no direct legal obligations.</li><li>Many of its provisions have since become binding as <strong>customary international law</strong> through consistent state practice and acceptance, notably the prohibitions of torture, slavery, racial discrimination and arbitrary detention.</li><li>Its content was codified in binding form in the <strong>ICCPR and ICESCR</strong>, both adopted in 1966 and in force from 1976.</li><li>It is widely cited in national constitutions and by domestic and international courts, giving it substantial practical authority.</li><li>Some argue the whole Declaration now reflects customary law; the more defensible position is that a substantial part of it does.</li></ol><div class=\"study-callout\"><strong>Note:</strong> The correct formulation in an exam or a legal argument is: 'The UDHR is not itself a treaty and was not binding on adoption, but many of its provisions now bind states as customary international law, and its content was given treaty form in the two 1966 Covenants.' Saying simply that it is binding, or simply that it is not, will both lose marks.</div><h4>5. Influence</h4><ul><li>It provided the template for the ICCPR and ICESCR, which together with the UDHR form the International Bill of Human Rights.</li><li>It shaped the regional instruments: the European Convention (1950), the American Convention (1969) and the African Charter (1981).</li><li>Its language appears in a great many national constitutions written since 1948.</li><li>It supplies the vocabulary in which claims are made worldwide, which is a form of influence not captured by asking whether it is binding.</li><li>It has been translated into more languages than any other document, which is itself part of how it became universal in practice.</li></ul><h4>Summary</h4><ul><li>The UDHR was adopted on 10 December 1948 with 48 votes in favour, none against and 8 abstentions.</li><li>Drafters included Roosevelt, Cassin, Chang, Malik, Humphrey and Mehta, making the drafting genuinely international.</li><li>It contains thirty articles covering civil, political, economic, social and cultural rights, plus framework provisions.</li><li>It was not binding on adoption, but many provisions now bind as customary law and its content was codified in the 1966 Covenants.</li></ul><h4>Key Terms</h4><div class=\"table-wrap\"><table class=\"study-table\"><thead><tr><th>Term</th><th>Meaning</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Universal Declaration of Human Rights</td><td>The 1948 General Assembly resolution setting out thirty articles of fundamental rights.</td></tr><tr><td>General Assembly resolution</td><td>A UN instrument that is not in itself legally binding on states.</td></tr><tr><td>Customary international law</td><td>Binding law formed by consistent state practice accepted as law.</td></tr><tr><td>International Bill of Human Rights</td><td>The UDHR together with the ICCPR and ICESCR and their protocols.</td></tr></tbody></table></div><h4>Review Questions</h4><h5>Multiple choice</h5><div class=\"study-mcq\"><p class=\"mcq-q\">How many states voted against the UDHR in 1948?</p><ol type=\"A\"><li>None; there were 48 in favour and 8 abstentions</li><li>Eight</li><li>Twelve</li><li>Two</li></ol><details class=\"mcq-a\"><summary>Show answer</summary><p><strong>Answer: A.</strong> The abstentions came from the Soviet bloc, Saudi Arabia and South Africa, but no state voted against.</p></details></div><div class=\"study-mcq\"><p class=\"mcq-q\">What is the most accurate statement of the UDHR&#x27;s legal status?</p><ol type=\"A\"><li>Not binding as a treaty on adoption, but many provisions now bind as customary law and were codified in the 1966 Covenants</li><li>A binding treaty from the moment of adoption</li><li>Entirely without legal effect</li><li>Binding only on states that voted for it</li></ol><details class=\"mcq-a\"><summary>Show answer</summary><p><strong>Answer: A.</strong> Saying simply that it is binding, or simply that it is not, are both wrong; the position is layered.</p></details></div><div class=\"study-mcq\"><p class=\"mcq-q\">What change is Hansa Mehta credited with making to the UDHR text?</p><ol type=\"A\"><li>Changing &#x27;all men are born free and equal&#x27; to &#x27;all human beings&#x27;</li><li>Adding the economic and social rights articles</li><li>Drafting the preamble</li><li>Inserting the limitation clause</li></ol><details class=\"mcq-a\"><summary>Show answer</summary><p><strong>Answer: A.</strong> A small edit with substantial consequences, and a useful answer to the claim the drafting was purely Western and male.</p></details></div><div class=\"study-mcq\"><p class=\"mcq-q\">What does Article 30 of the UDHR provide?</p><ol type=\"A\"><li>That nothing in the Declaration may be read as permitting the destruction of any of its rights</li><li>That everyone has duties to the community</li><li>That rights may be limited in emergencies</li><li>That the Declaration is binding on all states</li></ol><details class=\"mcq-a\"><summary>Show answer</summary><p><strong>Answer: A.</strong> It prevents the Declaration being used against itself by invoking one right as a licence to destroy others.</p></details></div><h5>Short answer</h5><div class=\"study-sa\"><p class=\"sa-q\">State the legal status of the UDHR precisely.</p><details class=\"sa-a\"><summary>Model answer</summary><p>It is a General Assembly resolution and was therefore not binding as a treaty when adopted. Many of its provisions have since become binding as customary international law, notably the prohibitions of torture, slavery, racial discrimination and arbitrary detention, and its content was codified in treaty form in the ICCPR and ICESCR of 1966.</p></details></div><div class=\"study-sa\"><p class=\"sa-q\">Why are the eight abstentions on the UDHR vote instructive?</p><details class=\"sa-a\"><summary>Model answer</summary><p>Because each reveals a distinct objection: the Soviet bloc considered economic and social rights underweighted and objected to interference in domestic affairs; Saudi Arabia objected to freedom to change religion and equal marriage rights; and South Africa, then building apartheid, could not accept the equality provisions. The pattern shows exactly which provisions were contested and why.</p></details></div><h4>Class Activity</h4><ol><li>Read the UDHR in full, which takes about fifteen minutes, and list the three articles you did not expect to find in it.</li><li>Take the constitution of one country and identify three provisions that clearly track UDHR language.</li></ol><h4>Assignment</h4><p>Write one page on the Universal Declaration of Human Rights. Cover its drafting and adoption including the abstentions, summarise its structure, state its legal status precisely, and explain its influence on later law with at least three examples.</p><p class=\"study-print\"><strong>Printable:</strong> use your browser\u2019s Print \u2192 Save as PDF to keep an offline copy.</p></div>",
  "Sources of International Law": "<div class=\"study-note\"><div class=\"revision-banner\"><strong>Human Rights and International Humanitarian Law</strong><span>Module 2: History and Sources of Human Rights Law</span></div><h3 class=\"lesson-note-title\">LESSON NOTE: SOURCES OF INTERNATIONAL LAW</h3><div class=\"table-wrap\"><table class=\"study-table\"><thead><tr><th>Subject</th><th>Topic</th><th>Level</th><th>Lesson duration</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Human Rights and International Humanitarian Law</td><td>Sources of International Law</td><td>Beginner to Advanced</td><td>40 minutes</td></tr></tbody></table></div><h4>Learning objectives</h4><p>By the end of this lesson you should be able to:</p><ul><li>List the sources of international law under Article 38 of the ICJ Statute</li><li>Explain the relative weight of each source</li><li>Distinguish hard law from soft law</li><li>Identify the source of a given rule</li></ul><h4>1. Introduction</h4><p>Where does a rule of international law come from? Unlike a domestic system there is no legislature, so the answer is not obvious. Article 38(1) of the Statute of the International Court of Justice is the conventional starting point.</p><h4>2. Article 38(1) ICJ Statute</h4><p>The Court, whose function is to decide disputes in accordance with international law, applies:</p><div class=\"table-wrap\"><table class=\"study-table\"><thead><tr><th>Sub-paragraph</th><th>Source</th><th>Nature</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>(a)</td><td>International conventions, general or particular, establishing rules expressly recognised by the contesting states</td><td>Treaties: binding on parties</td></tr><tr><td>(b)</td><td>International custom, as evidence of a general practice accepted as law</td><td>Custom: binding on all states except persistent objectors</td></tr><tr><td>(c)</td><td>The general principles of law recognised by civilised nations</td><td>General principles: gap-fillers drawn from domestic systems</td></tr><tr><td>(d)</td><td>Judicial decisions and the teachings of the most highly qualified publicists, as subsidiary means for the determination of rules of law</td><td>Subsidiary: evidence of the law, not sources of it</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"study-callout\"><strong>Note:</strong> Sub-paragraph (d) is <strong>subsidiary</strong>. Court judgments and academic writing help identify what the law is; they do not create it. Candidates who list four equal sources have missed the most examinable distinction in the article. The phrase 'civilised nations' in (c) is a colonial-era formulation now generally read as meaning all legal systems.</div><h4>3. The relative weight of the sources</h4><ol><li>A treaty binds its parties and, as between them, generally prevails over inconsistent custom.</li><li>Custom binds all states, including those that never signed anything, which is why it matters enormously for states outside a treaty regime.</li><li>A rule of jus cogens overrides both treaty and ordinary custom.</li><li>General principles fill gaps where neither treaty nor custom answers the question.</li><li>Judicial decisions and scholarship are evidence of what the rules are.</li></ol><p>Note that Article 38 contains no formal hierarchy between treaty and custom. In practice the later and more specific rule tends to prevail, subject always to jus cogens.</p><h4>4. Hard law and soft law</h4><div class=\"study-defn\"><strong>Hard law:</strong> Legally binding rules creating obligations that can be enforced.</div><div class=\"study-defn\"><strong>Soft law:</strong> Instruments that are not binding but carry normative weight and influence conduct and the development of law.</div><div class=\"table-wrap\"><table class=\"study-table\"><thead><tr><th>Instrument</th><th>Type</th><th>Effect</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>ICCPR</td><td>Hard law</td><td>Binding on states parties</td></tr><tr><td>UDHR</td><td>Soft law originally</td><td>Not binding as such, though much is now custom</td></tr><tr><td>General Assembly resolutions</td><td>Soft law</td><td>Evidence of opinio juris; can contribute to custom forming</td></tr><tr><td>Treaty body General Comments</td><td>Soft law</td><td>Authoritative interpretation, widely relied on, not formally binding</td></tr><tr><td>UN Guiding Principles on Business and Human Rights</td><td>Soft law</td><td>Widely adopted standard, not a treaty</td></tr><tr><td>Codes of conduct and declarations</td><td>Soft law</td><td>Set expectations; may harden over time</td></tr></tbody></table></div><p>Soft law is not weak law. Much of the most influential material in this field \u2014 General Comments, the Guiding Principles, the Standard Minimum Rules for the Treatment of Prisoners \u2014 is formally non-binding and shapes practice more than many ratified treaties do.</p><h4>5. Identifying the source of a rule</h4><div class=\"study-callout\"><strong>Example:</strong> Question: what is the source of the rule that a state may not return a person to a country where they face torture? Answer: it is <em>both</em> treaty and custom. It appears expressly in Article 3 of the Convention Against Torture, binding on states parties. It is also widely regarded as customary international law, and arguably as jus cogens, so it binds states that have not ratified CAT. Being able to give both limbs is what a good answer looks like.</div><ol><li>Ask first whether a treaty covers it and whether the state is a party.</li><li>If not, or in addition, ask whether there is general practice accepted as law.</li><li>Check whether the rule is peremptory, which would override any contrary agreement.</li><li>Look for General Comments, judgments and resolutions as evidence of content.</li></ol><h4>Summary</h4><ul><li>Article 38(1) ICJ Statute lists treaties, custom, general principles, and judicial decisions and scholarship as subsidiary means.</li><li>Judicial decisions and writings are evidence of law, not sources of it.</li><li>Treaties bind parties; custom binds all states except persistent objectors; jus cogens overrides both.</li><li>Soft law is formally non-binding but often more influential in practice than many treaties.</li></ul><h4>Key Terms</h4><div class=\"table-wrap\"><table class=\"study-table\"><thead><tr><th>Term</th><th>Meaning</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Article 38(1)</td><td>The ICJ Statute provision listing the sources of international law.</td></tr><tr><td>Hard law</td><td>Legally binding rules creating enforceable obligations.</td></tr><tr><td>Soft law</td><td>Non-binding instruments carrying normative weight.</td></tr><tr><td>General principles of law</td><td>Principles common to domestic legal systems, used to fill gaps in international law.</td></tr></tbody></table></div><h4>Review Questions</h4><h5>Multiple choice</h5><div class=\"study-mcq\"><p class=\"mcq-q\">Which of the Article 38 sources is described as subsidiary?</p><ol type=\"A\"><li>Judicial decisions and the teachings of publicists</li><li>International custom</li><li>General principles of law</li><li>International conventions</li></ol><details class=\"mcq-a\"><summary>Show answer</summary><p><strong>Answer: A.</strong> Judgments and scholarship help identify what the law is; they do not create it, which is the most examinable point in the article.</p></details></div><div class=\"study-mcq\"><p class=\"mcq-q\">Why does custom matter enormously for states outside a treaty regime?</p><ol type=\"A\"><li>Custom binds all states, including those that never signed anything</li><li>Custom is easier to prove than treaty obligations</li><li>Custom applies only outside treaties</li><li>Non-parties may opt into treaties through custom</li></ol><details class=\"mcq-a\"><summary>Show answer</summary><p><strong>Answer: A.</strong> A state that has not ratified a convention may still be bound by the equivalent customary rule.</p></details></div><div class=\"study-mcq\"><p class=\"mcq-q\">Which is an example of soft law?</p><ol type=\"A\"><li>Treaty body General Comments</li><li>The ICCPR</li><li>The Convention Against Torture</li><li>The Geneva Conventions</li></ol><details class=\"mcq-a\"><summary>Show answer</summary><p><strong>Answer: A.</strong> General Comments are authoritative interpretation that is widely relied on but not formally binding.</p></details></div><div class=\"study-mcq\"><p class=\"mcq-q\">What is the source of the rule prohibiting return of a person to face torture?</p><ol type=\"A\"><li>Both treaty, in Article 3 CAT, and customary international law</li><li>Treaty only</li><li>Custom only</li><li>General principles of law only</li></ol><details class=\"mcq-a\"><summary>Show answer</summary><p><strong>Answer: A.</strong> Giving both limbs is what distinguishes a good answer, since the customary status binds states that have not ratified CAT.</p></details></div><h5>Short answer</h5><div class=\"study-sa\"><p class=\"sa-q\">List the sources in Article 38(1) of the ICJ Statute and identify which is subsidiary.</p><details class=\"sa-a\"><summary>Model answer</summary><p>International conventions; international custom as evidence of general practice accepted as law; the general principles of law recognised by legal systems; and, as subsidiary means for determining rules of law, judicial decisions and the teachings of the most highly qualified publicists.</p></details></div><div class=\"study-sa\"><p class=\"sa-q\">Explain why soft law is not weak law, with two examples.</p><details class=\"sa-a\"><summary>Model answer</summary><p>Formally non-binding instruments often shape practice more than ratified treaties. Treaty body General Comments provide authoritative interpretation relied on by courts and states, and the UN Guiding Principles on Business and Human Rights have been widely adopted as the operative standard for corporate conduct despite not being a treaty.</p></details></div><h4>Class Activity</h4><ol><li>Take five rules you already know from this course and identify the source of each, distinguishing treaty from custom and noting any that are both.</li><li>Find one General Comment from a treaty body and one General Assembly resolution. Write one paragraph on the legal weight of each.</li></ol><h4>Assignment</h4><p>Write one page on the sources of international law. Set out Article 38(1) with the subsidiary distinction explained, describe the relative weight of the sources, distinguish hard from soft law with examples, and identify the source of one specific human rights rule showing both treaty and customary limbs.</p><p class=\"study-print\"><strong>Printable:</strong> use your browser\u2019s Print \u2192 Save as PDF to keep an offline copy.</p></div>",
  "Treaties and How They Work": "<div class=\"study-note\"><div class=\"revision-banner\"><strong>Human Rights and International Humanitarian Law</strong><span>Module 2: History and Sources of Human Rights Law</span></div><h3 class=\"lesson-note-title\">LESSON NOTE: TREATIES AND HOW THEY WORK</h3><div class=\"table-wrap\"><table class=\"study-table\"><thead><tr><th>Subject</th><th>Topic</th><th>Level</th><th>Lesson duration</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Human Rights and International Humanitarian Law</td><td>Treaties and How They Work</td><td>Beginner to Advanced</td><td>40 minutes</td></tr></tbody></table></div><h4>Learning objectives</h4><p>By the end of this lesson you should be able to:</p><ul><li>Describe the stages by which a state becomes bound by a treaty</li><li>Distinguish signature, ratification, accession and succession</li><li>Explain entry into force and the Vienna Convention framework</li><li>Explain the obligation not to defeat a treaty&#x27;s object and purpose</li></ul><h4>1. Introduction</h4><p>A treaty binds only those states that have consented to be bound, and consent is expressed through a defined sequence of acts. Confusing signature with ratification is the most common error in this area and it changes the answer completely.</p><h4>2. The governing framework</h4><p>The law of treaties is largely codified in the Vienna Convention on the Law of Treaties 1969, which entered into force in 1980 and much of which reflects customary law, so it applies in substance even to states that have not ratified it.</p><div class=\"study-defn\"><strong>Treaty:</strong> An international agreement concluded between states in written form and governed by international law, whatever its particular designation.</div><h4>3. The stages</h4><div class=\"table-wrap\"><table class=\"study-table\"><thead><tr><th>Stage</th><th>What it is</th><th>Legal effect</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Negotiation and adoption</td><td>States agree the text</td><td>No obligation yet</td></tr><tr><td>Signature</td><td>A state signs the text</td><td>Does not bind the state to perform, but creates the obligation not to defeat the treaty's object and purpose</td></tr><tr><td>Ratification</td><td>The state formally consents to be bound, usually after domestic approval</td><td>The state becomes a party once the treaty is in force for it</td></tr><tr><td>Accession</td><td>Consent to be bound by a state that did not sign, usually after the treaty closed for signature</td><td>Same effect as ratification</td></tr><tr><td>Entry into force</td><td>The treaty becomes operative, usually once a stated number of ratifications is deposited</td><td>Obligations begin</td></tr><tr><td>Succession</td><td>A newly independent state continues the treaty obligations of its predecessor</td><td>Continuity of obligation</td></tr><tr><td>Reservation</td><td>A statement excluding or modifying the effect of certain provisions for that state</td><td>Modifies obligations, if compatible with object and purpose</td></tr><tr><td>Denunciation or withdrawal</td><td>Leaving the treaty, where the treaty permits</td><td>Ends obligations prospectively, subject to the treaty's terms</td></tr></tbody></table></div><h4>4. Signature is not ratification</h4><p>This distinction decides real cases. A state that has signed but not ratified is not bound to perform the treaty. It is, however, under an obligation of good faith not to act so as to defeat the treaty's object and purpose before it decides whether to ratify.</p><div class=\"study-callout\"><strong>Example:</strong> A state signs a treaty prohibiting a category of weapon but has not ratified. It is not yet bound by the prohibition. But if it were to rapidly stockpile that weapon precisely in order to be in a stronger position before ratification, it would arguably be defeating the object and purpose of the instrument it signed, contrary to Article 18 of the Vienna Convention.</div><div class=\"study-callout\"><strong>Note:</strong> In examinations the standard trap is a question stating that a state 'signed' a treaty and asking whether it is bound. The correct answer is that signature alone does not create an obligation to perform; ratification or accession is required.</div><h4>5. Entry into force and its two senses</h4><div class=\"table-wrap\"><table class=\"study-table\"><thead><tr><th>Sense</th><th>Meaning</th><th>Example</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Entry into force generally</td><td>The treaty becomes operative once the required number of ratifications is reached</td><td>The ICCPR required 35 ratifications and entered into force in 1976</td></tr><tr><td>Entry into force for a particular state</td><td>The treaty becomes binding on that state, usually a set period after it deposits its instrument</td><td>A state ratifying today typically becomes bound three months later</td></tr></tbody></table></div><p>A state is not bound before the treaty enters into force for it, which means obligations generally do not apply retrospectively to events before that date.</p><h4>6. Depositary and registration</h4><ul><li>Treaties designate a <strong>depositary</strong>, often the UN Secretary-General, who receives instruments of ratification, keeps the authoritative list of parties and circulates reservations and objections.</li><li>Article 102 of the UN Charter requires treaties to be registered with the Secretariat; an unregistered treaty may not be invoked before any UN organ, including the ICJ.</li><li>The depositary's published status list is the practical place to check whether a state is actually a party, and it is where you should look before asserting that it is.</li></ul><h4>Summary</h4><ul><li>The law of treaties is codified in the Vienna Convention 1969, much of which reflects custom.</li><li>Signature does not bind a state to perform; it creates only the obligation not to defeat the object and purpose.</li><li>Ratification or accession is what makes a state a party, and the treaty must also be in force for it.</li><li>Succession, reservation and denunciation each modify the position in defined ways.</li><li>Check the depositary&#x27;s status list before asserting that a state is a party.</li></ul><h4>Key Terms</h4><div class=\"table-wrap\"><table class=\"study-table\"><thead><tr><th>Term</th><th>Meaning</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Ratification</td><td>The formal act by which a state consents to be bound by a treaty it has signed.</td></tr><tr><td>Accession</td><td>Consent to be bound by a state that did not sign the treaty.</td></tr><tr><td>Entry into force</td><td>The point at which a treaty becomes operative, generally or for a particular state.</td></tr><tr><td>Depositary</td><td>The body that receives instruments of ratification and maintains the list of parties.</td></tr></tbody></table></div><h4>Review Questions</h4><h5>Multiple choice</h5><div class=\"study-mcq\"><p class=\"mcq-q\">A state has signed but not ratified a treaty. What is its position?</p><ol type=\"A\"><li>Not bound to perform, but obliged not to defeat the treaty&#x27;s object and purpose</li><li>Fully bound by all provisions</li><li>Under no obligation whatever</li><li>Bound only to the preamble</li></ol><details class=\"mcq-a\"><summary>Show answer</summary><p><strong>Answer: A.</strong> This is the standard examination trap; signature alone does not create an obligation to perform.</p></details></div><div class=\"study-mcq\"><p class=\"mcq-q\">What is accession?</p><ol type=\"A\"><li>Consent to be bound by a state that did not sign the treaty</li><li>Withdrawal from a treaty</li><li>A reservation to a specific article</li><li>The point a treaty enters into force</li></ol><details class=\"mcq-a\"><summary>Show answer</summary><p><strong>Answer: A.</strong> It has the same effect as ratification and is used where a state did not sign, often because the treaty has closed for signature.</p></details></div><div class=\"study-mcq\"><p class=\"mcq-q\">Which convention codifies the law of treaties?</p><ol type=\"A\"><li>The Vienna Convention on the Law of Treaties 1969</li><li>The UN Charter 1945</li><li>The ICJ Statute 1945</li><li>The Geneva Conventions 1949</li></ol><details class=\"mcq-a\"><summary>Show answer</summary><p><strong>Answer: A.</strong> Much of it reflects customary law, so it applies in substance even to states that have not ratified it.</p></details></div><div class=\"study-mcq\"><p class=\"mcq-q\">Where should you check whether a state is actually a party to a treaty?</p><ol type=\"A\"><li>The depositary&#x27;s published status list</li><li>The state&#x27;s own foreign ministry website</li><li>A press report of the signing ceremony</li><li>The treaty&#x27;s preamble</li></ol><details class=\"mcq-a\"><summary>Show answer</summary><p><strong>Answer: A.</strong> The depositary maintains the authoritative record of ratifications, reservations and objections.</p></details></div><h5>Short answer</h5><div class=\"study-sa\"><p class=\"sa-q\">Distinguish signature, ratification and accession.</p><details class=\"sa-a\"><summary>Model answer</summary><p>Signature indicates a state&#x27;s agreement with the text and creates an obligation not to defeat the treaty&#x27;s object and purpose, but not to perform it. Ratification is the formal consent to be bound, normally following domestic approval, after which the state becomes a party once the treaty is in force for it. Accession is consent to be bound by a state that did not sign, and has the same effect as ratification.</p></details></div><div class=\"study-sa\"><p class=\"sa-q\">Explain the two senses of entry into force.</p><details class=\"sa-a\"><summary>Model answer</summary><p>Generally, a treaty enters into force once the number of ratifications it specifies has been deposited: the ICCPR required 35 and entered into force in 1976. For a particular state, it enters into force when that state&#x27;s own instrument takes effect, usually a set period after deposit, and the state is not bound before that date.</p></details></div><h4>Class Activity</h4><ol><li>Choose one human rights treaty and use the UN Treaty Collection to establish its date of entry into force, its number of parties, and whether your own country is a party and with what reservations.</li><li>In pairs, take three scenarios in which a state has signed, ratified, or acceded, and state precisely what obligation each carries.</li></ol><h4>Assignment</h4><p>Write one page on how treaties work. Set out the stages from negotiation to entry into force, explain precisely why signature is not ratification with an example, and describe the role of the depositary and the practical importance of checking the status list.</p><p class=\"study-print\"><strong>Printable:</strong> use your browser\u2019s Print \u2192 Save as PDF to keep an offline copy.</p></div>",
  "Customary International Law and Jus Cogens": "<div class=\"study-note\"><div class=\"revision-banner\"><strong>Human Rights and International Humanitarian Law</strong><span>Module 2: History and Sources of Human Rights Law</span></div><h3 class=\"lesson-note-title\">LESSON NOTE: CUSTOMARY INTERNATIONAL LAW AND JUS COGENS</h3><div class=\"table-wrap\"><table class=\"study-table\"><thead><tr><th>Subject</th><th>Topic</th><th>Level</th><th>Lesson duration</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Human Rights and International Humanitarian Law</td><td>Customary International Law and Jus Cogens</td><td>Beginner to Advanced</td><td>40 minutes</td></tr></tbody></table></div><h4>Learning objectives</h4><p>By the end of this lesson you should be able to:</p><ul><li>Define customary international law and state its two elements</li><li>Explain how custom is proved</li><li>Define jus cogens and give examples</li><li>Explain the persistent objector doctrine and its limits</li></ul><h4>1. Introduction</h4><p>Custom is what binds states that never signed anything. For human rights work this matters enormously: a state can refuse to ratify every treaty and still be bound by the prohibition of torture, because that prohibition is customary and indeed peremptory.</p><h4>2. The two elements of custom</h4><div class=\"study-defn\"><strong>Customary international law:</strong> A general practice of states accepted as law.</div><div class=\"table-wrap\"><table class=\"study-table\"><thead><tr><th>Element</th><th>Name</th><th>What it requires</th><th>How proved</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>1</td><td>State practice</td><td>Consistent, general and sufficiently widespread conduct by states</td><td>Legislation, court decisions, diplomatic correspondence, military manuals, official statements, voting patterns, actual conduct</td></tr><tr><td>2</td><td>Opinio juris</td><td>Belief that the practice is required by law, not merely convenient or courteous</td><td>Statements accepting a legal obligation, treaty ratifications, GA resolutions, official explanations of conduct</td></tr></tbody></table></div><p>Both elements are required. Practice without opinio juris is mere habit: states have long extended diplomatic courtesies without regarding themselves as legally bound. Opinio juris without practice is aspiration.</p><h4>3. Proving custom</h4><ol><li>Practice need not be universal, but it must be general and include states whose interests are specially affected.</li><li>Consistency matters more than duration; a customary rule can form relatively quickly if practice is dense and uniform.</li><li>Contrary practice does not necessarily defeat a rule if the state concerned defends its conduct by reference to the rule rather than denying it.</li><li>Treaty provisions can crystallise or generate customary rules binding non-parties, particularly where widely ratified.</li></ol><div class=\"study-callout\"><strong>Example:</strong> A state accused of torturing detainees does not say 'torture is lawful'. It says the allegations are false, or that what occurred was not torture. That response is itself evidence of opinio juris: the state accepts the rule and disputes the facts. The International Court of Justice made essentially this point in the <em>Nicaragua</em> case, holding that conduct inconsistent with a rule, defended as an exception rather than a denial, confirms rather than weakens the rule.</div><h4>4. Jus cogens</h4><div class=\"study-defn\"><strong>Jus cogens:</strong> A peremptory norm of general international law, accepted and recognised by the international community of states as a whole as a norm from which no derogation is permitted and which can be modified only by a subsequent norm of the same character.</div><p>The definition comes from Article 53 of the Vienna Convention on the Law of Treaties. Its consequences are severe: a treaty conflicting with a peremptory norm is void, and no state may contract out of it.</p><div class=\"table-wrap\"><table class=\"study-table\"><thead><tr><th>Widely accepted as jus cogens</th><th>Contested or emerging</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Prohibition of genocide</td><td>Right to self-determination beyond the colonial context</td></tr><tr><td>Prohibition of torture</td><td>Prohibition of enforced disappearance</td></tr><tr><td>Prohibition of slavery and the slave trade</td><td>Prohibition of arbitrary deprivation of life</td></tr><tr><td>Prohibition of aggression</td><td>Core environmental protections</td></tr><tr><td>Prohibition of racial discrimination and apartheid</td><td>Right to a fair trial in all its aspects</td></tr><tr><td>Basic rules of international humanitarian law</td><td></td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"study-callout\"><strong>Note:</strong> Be careful. Students frequently assert that a favoured rule is jus cogens. The category is narrow by design; the whole point of a peremptory norm is that it is exceptional. Claiming too much for it weakens the argument.</div><h4>5. The persistent objector</h4><div class=\"study-defn\"><strong>Persistent objector:</strong> A state that has clearly and consistently objected to an emerging customary rule from the outset, and is therefore not bound by it.</div><ol><li>The objection must be made while the rule is emerging, not after it has crystallised.</li><li>It must be clear, consistent and maintained.</li><li>It is exceptional and difficult to establish in practice.</li><li>Crucially, the doctrine does <strong>not</strong> apply to jus cogens. No state can persistently object its way out of the prohibition of torture or genocide.</li></ol><p>That last point is the one that matters most for human rights. A state cannot escape a peremptory norm by any means: not by refusing to ratify, not by reservation, not by persistent objection, and not by agreement with another state.</p><h4>Summary</h4><ul><li>Customary law requires state practice and opinio juris, the belief that the practice is legally required.</li><li>Practice need not be universal but must be general and include specially affected states.</li><li>A state that denies the facts rather than the rule confirms the rule, as the Nicaragua case established.</li><li>Jus cogens norms permit no derogation and void conflicting treaties; the category is deliberately narrow.</li><li>The persistent objector doctrine is exceptional and does not apply to jus cogens.</li></ul><h4>Key Terms</h4><div class=\"table-wrap\"><table class=\"study-table\"><thead><tr><th>Term</th><th>Meaning</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>State practice</td><td>Consistent and general conduct by states, evidencing a customary rule.</td></tr><tr><td>Opinio juris</td><td>The belief that a practice is followed out of legal obligation.</td></tr><tr><td>Jus cogens</td><td>A peremptory norm permitting no derogation, from which no state may contract out.</td></tr><tr><td>Persistent objector</td><td>A state that consistently objected to a customary rule as it emerged and is not bound by it.</td></tr></tbody></table></div><h4>Review Questions</h4><h5>Multiple choice</h5><div class=\"study-mcq\"><p class=\"mcq-q\">What are the two elements of customary international law?</p><ol type=\"A\"><li>State practice and opinio juris</li><li>Treaty and ratification</li><li>Practice and duration</li><li>Consent and enforcement</li></ol><details class=\"mcq-a\"><summary>Show answer</summary><p><strong>Answer: A.</strong> Practice without opinio juris is mere habit, and opinio juris without practice is aspiration.</p></details></div><div class=\"study-mcq\"><p class=\"mcq-q\">A state accused of torture denies the allegations rather than claiming torture is lawful. What does this show?</p><ol type=\"A\"><li>Opinio juris: the state accepts the rule and disputes the facts</li><li>That the rule does not exist</li><li>That custom cannot be proved</li><li>That the state is a persistent objector</li></ol><details class=\"mcq-a\"><summary>Show answer</summary><p><strong>Answer: A.</strong> The Nicaragua case established that conduct defended as an exception, rather than by denying the rule, confirms the rule.</p></details></div><div class=\"study-mcq\"><p class=\"mcq-q\">Can a state avoid a jus cogens norm by persistent objection?</p><ol type=\"A\"><li>No; the doctrine does not apply to peremptory norms</li><li>Yes, if the objection is consistent</li><li>Yes, if it also refuses to ratify the relevant treaty</li><li>Only if another state agrees</li></ol><details class=\"mcq-a\"><summary>Show answer</summary><p><strong>Answer: A.</strong> No state can escape a peremptory norm by any means, which is what makes the category peremptory.</p></details></div><div class=\"study-mcq\"><p class=\"mcq-q\">What happens to a treaty that conflicts with a jus cogens norm?</p><ol type=\"A\"><li>It is void</li><li>It is valid but unenforceable</li><li>It binds only the states that ratified it</li><li>It must be renegotiated within a year</li></ol><details class=\"mcq-a\"><summary>Show answer</summary><p><strong>Answer: A.</strong> Article 53 of the Vienna Convention provides that such a treaty is void.</p></details></div><h5>Short answer</h5><div class=\"study-sa\"><p class=\"sa-q\">Define jus cogens and give three examples.</p><details class=\"sa-a\"><summary>Model answer</summary><p>A peremptory norm of general international law, accepted and recognised by the international community of states as a whole as one from which no derogation is permitted and which can be modified only by a subsequent norm of the same character. Examples include the prohibitions of genocide, torture, and slavery and the slave trade.</p></details></div><div class=\"study-sa\"><p class=\"sa-q\">Explain how a widely ratified treaty can bind states that are not parties to it.</p><details class=\"sa-a\"><summary>Model answer</summary><p>Treaty provisions can crystallise an emerging customary rule or generate a new one, where the treaty is widely ratified and its provisions are accompanied by general practice and opinio juris. Once the rule exists as custom it binds all states, including those that never became parties to the treaty.</p></details></div><h4>Class Activity</h4><ol><li>Take the prohibition of arbitrary detention and assemble the evidence you would use to establish it as customary: practice from at least three categories of source, and evidence of opinio juris.</li><li>In groups, argue whether the prohibition of enforced disappearance has attained jus cogens status. Identify what evidence would settle the question.</li></ol><h4>Assignment</h4><p>Write one page on customary international law and jus cogens. Explain the two elements of custom and how each is proved, define jus cogens with examples and its consequences, and explain the persistent objector doctrine and why it does not apply to peremptory norms.</p><p class=\"study-print\"><strong>Printable:</strong> use your browser\u2019s Print \u2192 Save as PDF to keep an offline copy.</p></div>",
  "Reservations, Derogations and Limitations": "<div class=\"study-note\"><div class=\"revision-banner\"><strong>Human Rights and International Humanitarian Law</strong><span>Module 2: History and Sources of Human Rights Law</span></div><h3 class=\"lesson-note-title\">LESSON NOTE: RESERVATIONS, DEROGATIONS AND LIMITATIONS</h3><div class=\"table-wrap\"><table class=\"study-table\"><thead><tr><th>Subject</th><th>Topic</th><th>Level</th><th>Lesson duration</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Human Rights and International Humanitarian Law</td><td>Reservations, Derogations and Limitations</td><td>Beginner to Advanced</td><td>45 minutes</td></tr></tbody></table></div><h4>Learning objectives</h4><p>By the end of this lesson you should be able to:</p><ul><li>Distinguish reservations, derogations and limitations</li><li>Apply the object and purpose test to a reservation</li><li>State the conditions for a valid derogation</li><li>Apply the limitation test to a qualified right</li></ul><h4>1. Introduction</h4><p>Three different mechanisms allow a state to hold back from the full force of a human rights obligation. They operate at different points and under different conditions, and confusing them is the most common analytical error in this part of the subject.</p><h4>2. The three mechanisms compared</h4><div class=\"table-wrap\"><table class=\"study-table\"><thead><tr><th></th><th>Reservation</th><th>Derogation</th><th>Limitation</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>When</td><td>At the point of joining the treaty</td><td>During a declared public emergency</td><td>At any time, in ordinary circumstances</td></tr><tr><td>Effect</td><td>Excludes or modifies a provision for that state permanently</td><td>Suspends a provision temporarily</td><td>Restricts the scope of a right in a particular case</td></tr><tr><td>Governed by</td><td>Vienna Convention Articles 19\u201323</td><td>The treaty's derogation clause, e.g. Article 4 ICCPR</td><td>The right's own limitation clause</td></tr><tr><td>Test</td><td>Compatible with the object and purpose of the treaty</td><td>Strictly required by the exigencies of the situation</td><td>Lawful, legitimate aim, necessary and proportionate</td></tr><tr><td>Applies to</td><td>Any provision, subject to the test</td><td>Derogable rights only</td><td>Qualified rights only</td></tr></tbody></table></div><h4>3. Reservations and the object and purpose test</h4><div class=\"study-defn\"><strong>Reservation:</strong> A unilateral statement made by a state when joining a treaty, purporting to exclude or modify the legal effect of certain provisions in their application to that state.</div><ol><li>A reservation is impermissible if the treaty prohibits it, if the treaty permits only specified reservations and this is not one, or if it is incompatible with the object and purpose of the treaty.</li><li>Other states may object to a reservation. In classical treaty law an objection may prevent the treaty entering into force between the two states.</li><li>Human rights treaties raise a particular problem: they are not bargains between states but obligations owed to individuals, so the reciprocity logic of objection fits poorly.</li><li>The Human Rights Committee's General Comment 24 took the position that an incompatible reservation is severable, so the state remains a party without the benefit of the reservation. Several states objected strongly to that view.</li></ol><div class=\"study-callout\"><strong>Example:</strong> A state ratifies CEDAW with a reservation that it will comply only so far as consistent with its domestic family law. Since CEDAW's central purpose is to eliminate discrimination against women, much of which historically resides in family law, such a reservation arguably empties the treaty of content for that state and is therefore incompatible with its object and purpose. Reservations of this general kind have attracted numerous formal objections.</div><h4>4. Derogation</h4><p>Article 4 ICCPR sets the standard conditions, and the regional instruments are similar.</p><ol><li>There must be a <strong>public emergency threatening the life of the nation</strong>. Serious disturbance is not enough; the threshold is high.</li><li>The emergency must be <strong>officially proclaimed</strong>.</li><li>Measures must be <strong>strictly required by the exigencies of the situation</strong>, which imports a proportionality test.</li><li>Measures must not be <strong>inconsistent with the state's other international obligations</strong>.</li><li>Measures must not involve <strong>discrimination</strong> solely on grounds of race, colour, sex, language, religion or social origin.</li><li>Other states parties must be <strong>notified</strong> through the UN Secretary-General, stating the provisions derogated from and the reasons.</li></ol><div class=\"table-wrap\"><table class=\"study-table\"><thead><tr><th>Non-derogable under Article 4(2) ICCPR</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Article 6: right to life</td></tr><tr><td>Article 7: torture and cruel, inhuman or degrading treatment</td></tr><tr><td>Article 8(1) and (2): slavery and servitude</td></tr><tr><td>Article 11: imprisonment for inability to fulfil a contract</td></tr><tr><td>Article 15: no retroactive criminal law</td></tr><tr><td>Article 16: recognition as a person before the law</td></tr><tr><td>Article 18: freedom of thought, conscience and religion</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"study-callout\"><strong>Note:</strong> Learn this list. A question describing an emergency measure that involves torture or retroactive punishment is testing whether you know that no emergency, however genuine, can justify it.</div><h4>5. Limitation</h4><p>Most qualified rights carry a limitation clause. The standard test has four parts, all of which must be satisfied.</p><ol><li><strong>Prescribed by law:</strong> the restriction must have a legal basis that is accessible and sufficiently precise for a person to foresee its consequences.</li><li><strong>Legitimate aim:</strong> it must pursue one of the aims listed in the clause, typically national security, public order, public health or morals, or the rights and freedoms of others.</li><li><strong>Necessary in a democratic society:</strong> it must respond to a pressing social need, not merely be useful or convenient.</li><li><strong>Proportionate:</strong> it must be the least restrictive means capable of achieving the aim, and its benefit must outweigh the harm to the right.</li></ol><div class=\"study-callout\"><strong>Example:</strong> A government bans all public assemblies for six months, citing public order. Prescribed by law: possibly, if enacted properly. Legitimate aim: yes, public order qualifies. Necessary: questionable, since a blanket ban is not obviously responsive to a specific pressing need. Proportionate: almost certainly not, because less restrictive measures such as route conditions, notification requirements or targeted bans were available. The measure fails the test at the last two stages, which is where most limitation arguments are decided.</div><h4>Summary</h4><ul><li>Reservations operate at joining, derogations during declared emergencies, and limitations in ordinary circumstances.</li><li>A reservation must be compatible with the treaty&#x27;s object and purpose.</li><li>Derogation requires a proclaimed emergency threatening the life of the nation, strict necessity, non-discrimination and notification.</li><li>Certain rights, including the prohibitions of torture and retroactive punishment, are non-derogable in any emergency.</li><li>A limitation must be prescribed by law, pursue a legitimate aim, be necessary and be proportionate.</li></ul><h4>Key Terms</h4><div class=\"table-wrap\"><table class=\"study-table\"><thead><tr><th>Term</th><th>Meaning</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Reservation</td><td>A unilateral statement excluding or modifying a treaty provision for the reserving state.</td></tr><tr><td>Derogation</td><td>Temporary suspension of a right during a proclaimed public emergency.</td></tr><tr><td>Limitation</td><td>A restriction on a qualified right in ordinary circumstances.</td></tr><tr><td>Non-derogable right</td><td>A right that may not be suspended even in a public emergency.</td></tr></tbody></table></div><h4>Review Questions</h4><h5>Multiple choice</h5><div class=\"study-mcq\"><p class=\"mcq-q\">Which of these may never be derogated from under Article 4(2) ICCPR?</p><ol type=\"A\"><li>The prohibition of torture</li><li>Freedom of movement</li><li>The right to liberty</li><li>Freedom of assembly</li></ol><details class=\"mcq-a\"><summary>Show answer</summary><p><strong>Answer: A.</strong> No emergency, however genuine, can justify torture; the list of non-derogable rights should be learned.</p></details></div><div class=\"study-mcq\"><p class=\"mcq-q\">What test applies to a reservation?</p><ol type=\"A\"><li>Whether it is compatible with the object and purpose of the treaty</li><li>Whether other states consent to it</li><li>Whether it is temporary</li><li>Whether the emergency is proclaimed</li></ol><details class=\"mcq-a\"><summary>Show answer</summary><p><strong>Answer: A.</strong> A reservation that empties the treaty of content for the reserving state fails this test.</p></details></div><div class=\"study-mcq\"><p class=\"mcq-q\">A government bans all public assemblies for six months citing public order. Where is this most likely to fail?</p><ol type=\"A\"><li>Necessity and proportionality, since less restrictive measures were available</li><li>Legitimate aim, since public order does not qualify</li><li>Prescribed by law, automatically</li><li>It would not fail; public order justifies any measure</li></ol><details class=\"mcq-a\"><summary>Show answer</summary><p><strong>Answer: A.</strong> Most limitation arguments are decided at the necessity and proportionality stages, not the first two.</p></details></div><div class=\"study-mcq\"><p class=\"mcq-q\">What must a state do to derogate validly under Article 4 ICCPR?</p><ol type=\"A\"><li>Proclaim an emergency threatening the life of the nation and notify other parties through the Secretary-General</li><li>Simply announce the measures domestically</li><li>Obtain approval from the Human Rights Committee</li><li>Amend its constitution first</li></ol><details class=\"mcq-a\"><summary>Show answer</summary><p><strong>Answer: A.</strong> Proclamation, strict necessity, non-discrimination and international notification are all required.</p></details></div><h5>Short answer</h5><div class=\"study-sa\"><p class=\"sa-q\">Distinguish a reservation, a derogation and a limitation.</p><details class=\"sa-a\"><summary>Model answer</summary><p>A reservation is made when joining a treaty and permanently excludes or modifies a provision for that state, subject to compatibility with the treaty&#x27;s object and purpose. A derogation temporarily suspends a derogable right during a proclaimed public emergency threatening the life of the nation. A limitation restricts a qualified right in ordinary circumstances, and must be prescribed by law, pursue a legitimate aim, and be necessary and proportionate.</p></details></div><div class=\"study-sa\"><p class=\"sa-q\">Set out the four-part test for a valid limitation on a qualified right.</p><details class=\"sa-a\"><summary>Model answer</summary><p>It must be prescribed by law, meaning an accessible and sufficiently precise legal basis; pursue a legitimate aim listed in the limitation clause; be necessary in a democratic society, responding to a pressing social need rather than mere convenience; and be proportionate, using the least restrictive means capable of achieving the aim.</p></details></div><h4>Class Activity</h4><ol><li>Find one reservation entered to a human rights treaty and assess whether it is compatible with the object and purpose. Note whether any state objected.</li><li>Take one emergency measure taken by any government in recent years and run it through the Article 4 conditions and, separately, the four-part limitation test.</li></ol><h4>Assignment</h4><p>Write one page distinguishing reservations, derogations and limitations. Apply the object and purpose test to a real reservation, set out the Article 4 conditions with the non-derogable rights, and work one limitation example through all four parts of the test.</p><p class=\"study-print\"><strong>Printable:</strong> use your browser\u2019s Print \u2192 Save as PDF to keep an offline copy.</p></div>",
  "Ratification and Domestication": "<div class=\"study-note\"><div class=\"revision-banner\"><strong>Human Rights and International Humanitarian Law</strong><span>Module 2: History and Sources of Human Rights Law</span></div><h3 class=\"lesson-note-title\">LESSON NOTE: RATIFICATION AND DOMESTICATION</h3><div class=\"table-wrap\"><table class=\"study-table\"><thead><tr><th>Subject</th><th>Topic</th><th>Level</th><th>Lesson duration</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Human Rights and International Humanitarian Law</td><td>Ratification and Domestication</td><td>Beginner to Advanced</td><td>35 minutes</td></tr></tbody></table></div><h4>Learning objectives</h4><p>By the end of this lesson you should be able to:</p><ul><li>Explain the difference between monism and dualism</li><li>Describe how a treaty takes effect in domestic law</li><li>Explain why ratification alone often changes nothing</li><li>Identify the steps needed to make a treaty usable domestically</li></ul><h4>1. Introduction</h4><p>A state ratifies a treaty and the human rights community celebrates. Then a lawyer tries to rely on it in a national court and discovers it has no domestic effect at all. Understanding why is essential to doing anything useful with a ratification.</p><h4>2. Monism and dualism</h4><div class=\"study-defn\"><strong>Monism:</strong> The view that international and domestic law form a single legal order, so a ratified treaty automatically becomes part of domestic law.</div><div class=\"study-defn\"><strong>Dualism:</strong> The view that international and domestic law are separate orders, so a treaty takes effect domestically only when incorporated by legislation.</div><div class=\"table-wrap\"><table class=\"study-table\"><thead><tr><th></th><th>Monist system</th><th>Dualist system</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>On ratification</td><td>The treaty generally becomes part of domestic law automatically</td><td>The treaty binds the state internationally but has no domestic effect</td></tr><tr><td>To be used in court</td><td>May be invoked directly, subject to whether the provision is self-executing</td><td>Requires an implementing statute</td></tr><tr><td>Typical examples</td><td>Many civil law states, including the Netherlands and France</td><td>Most common law states, including the United Kingdom and many Commonwealth countries</td></tr><tr><td>Risk</td><td>Courts may be reluctant to apply provisions that are not self-executing</td><td>Ratification becomes symbolic if no legislation follows</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"study-callout\"><strong>Note:</strong> Very few systems are purely one or the other, and many constitutions take an intermediate position, for example giving treaties a status above ordinary statute but below the constitution, or requiring incorporation for some categories only. Always check the constitution rather than assuming.</div><h4>3. Self-executing and non-self-executing provisions</h4><p>Even in a monist system, not every provision can be applied directly by a court.</p><div class=\"table-wrap\"><table class=\"study-table\"><thead><tr><th>Type</th><th>Character</th><th>Example</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Self-executing</td><td>Sufficiently precise to be applied by a court without further legislation</td><td>'No one shall be subjected to torture'</td></tr><tr><td>Non-self-executing</td><td>Requires legislative or administrative action to give it content</td><td>'States shall take steps to achieve progressively the full realisation of the right to health'</td></tr></tbody></table></div><p>The distinction is not the same as the civil-political versus economic-social divide, though it correlates with it. Some economic and social provisions are precise enough to apply directly, and some civil and political provisions require implementing machinery.</p><h4>4. Why ratification alone often changes nothing</h4><ol><li>In a dualist state, no incorporating legislation means no domestic remedy.</li><li>Even where incorporated, existing inconsistent legislation may remain on the books until repealed.</li><li>Judges may be unfamiliar with the instrument and reluctant to apply it.</li><li>Lawyers may not plead it, so the courts never get the chance.</li><li>Administrative practice, police training and prison regulations may be unchanged.</li><li>There may be no accessible remedy even if the right is recognised.</li></ol><div class=\"study-callout\"><strong>Example:</strong> A state ratifies the Convention Against Torture. Torture continues in police stations. The reason is not usually that officials have read the treaty and decided to breach it. It is that no domestic offence of torture was created, so prosecutors charge assault where they charge anything at all; police training was never revised; there is no independent complaints body; and detainees have no practical route to a court. Ratification without any of these is a diplomatic act, not a legal change.</div><h4>5. Making a treaty usable domestically</h4><ol><li><strong>Incorporate:</strong> pass legislation giving the treaty domestic force, or enact its substance.</li><li><strong>Harmonise:</strong> audit existing law for inconsistency and repeal or amend what conflicts.</li><li><strong>Criminalise:</strong> where the treaty requires it, create specific offences with adequate penalties.</li><li><strong>Institutionalise:</strong> establish or empower the bodies the treaty requires, such as a national preventive mechanism or a human rights commission.</li><li><strong>Train:</strong> revise the training of police, prison staff, prosecutors and judges.</li><li><strong>Provide a remedy:</strong> ensure an accessible route to challenge violations and obtain redress.</li><li><strong>Report:</strong> meet the treaty's reporting obligations, which is also the discipline that surfaces gaps.</li></ol><p>An advocate's most useful question after any ratification is not 'has it been ratified?' but 'which of these seven steps has actually been taken?' The answer is usually one or two, and identifying the missing five is where the advocacy work lies.</p><h4>Summary</h4><ul><li>Monist systems generally give ratified treaties automatic domestic effect; dualist systems require incorporating legislation.</li><li>Even in monist systems, only self-executing provisions can be applied directly by courts.</li><li>Ratification alone often changes nothing without incorporation, harmonisation, criminalisation, institutions, training and remedies.</li><li>The useful advocacy question is which implementation steps have actually been taken.</li></ul><h4>Key Terms</h4><div class=\"table-wrap\"><table class=\"study-table\"><thead><tr><th>Term</th><th>Meaning</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Monism</td><td>The view that international and domestic law form a single legal order.</td></tr><tr><td>Dualism</td><td>The view that international and domestic law are separate, requiring incorporation.</td></tr><tr><td>Self-executing provision</td><td>A treaty provision precise enough to be applied by a court without further legislation.</td></tr><tr><td>Incorporation</td><td>Legislation giving a treaty effect in domestic law.</td></tr></tbody></table></div><h4>Review Questions</h4><h5>Multiple choice</h5><div class=\"study-mcq\"><p class=\"mcq-q\">In a dualist state, what is the effect of ratifying a treaty without incorporating legislation?</p><ol type=\"A\"><li>The state is bound internationally but the treaty has no domestic effect</li><li>The treaty becomes automatically enforceable in courts</li><li>The treaty is void</li><li>Domestic law is automatically repealed where inconsistent</li></ol><details class=\"mcq-a\"><summary>Show answer</summary><p><strong>Answer: A.</strong> This is why ratification alone frequently produces no change in practice.</p></details></div><div class=\"study-mcq\"><p class=\"mcq-q\">Which is a self-executing provision?</p><ol type=\"A\"><li>&#x27;No one shall be subjected to torture&#x27;</li><li>&#x27;States shall take steps to achieve progressively the full realisation of the right to health&#x27;</li><li>&#x27;States Parties undertake to adopt appropriate measures&#x27;</li><li>&#x27;States shall cooperate to promote development&#x27;</li></ol><details class=\"mcq-a\"><summary>Show answer</summary><p><strong>Answer: A.</strong> It is precise enough for a court to apply without further legislative action.</p></details></div><div class=\"study-mcq\"><p class=\"mcq-q\">A state ratifies CAT but torture continues. What is the most likely explanation?</p><ol type=\"A\"><li>No domestic offence was created, training was unchanged and detainees have no practical remedy</li><li>Officials read the treaty and decided to breach it</li><li>The treaty does not apply to police</li><li>Ratification was invalid</li></ol><details class=\"mcq-a\"><summary>Show answer</summary><p><strong>Answer: A.</strong> Implementation failure, not deliberate defiance of a known text, is the usual mechanism.</p></details></div><div class=\"study-mcq\"><p class=\"mcq-q\">What is the most useful question to ask after a ratification?</p><ol type=\"A\"><li>Which implementation steps have actually been taken?</li><li>When was it signed?</li><li>How many other states have ratified?</li><li>Was there a reservation?</li></ol><details class=\"mcq-a\"><summary>Show answer</summary><p><strong>Answer: A.</strong> Identifying which of incorporation, harmonisation, criminalisation, institutions, training and remedies are missing is where the advocacy work lies.</p></details></div><h5>Short answer</h5><div class=\"study-sa\"><p class=\"sa-q\">Explain the difference between monism and dualism with an example of each.</p><details class=\"sa-a\"><summary>Model answer</summary><p>In a monist system international and domestic law form a single order, so a ratified treaty generally becomes part of domestic law automatically and may be invoked in court if the provision is self-executing; many civil law states operate this way. In a dualist system the two orders are separate, so a treaty binds the state internationally but has no domestic effect until incorporated by legislation; most common law states, including the United Kingdom, follow this approach.</p></details></div><div class=\"study-sa\"><p class=\"sa-q\">List five steps needed to make a ratified treaty usable domestically.</p><details class=\"sa-a\"><summary>Model answer</summary><p>Incorporate it by legislation; harmonise existing law by repealing or amending inconsistent provisions; criminalise conduct where the treaty requires specific offences; establish or empower the institutions the treaty requires; revise the training of police, prosecutors and judges; and provide an accessible remedy for violations.</p></details></div><h4>Class Activity</h4><ol><li>Determine whether your own country is monist or dualist by reading the relevant constitutional provision. Note the exact wording.</li><li>Choose one treaty your country has ratified and assess which of the seven implementation steps have been taken. Write one paragraph on the largest gap.</li></ol><h4>Assignment</h4><p>Write one page on ratification and domestication. Explain monism and dualism with examples, distinguish self-executing from non-self-executing provisions, and set out the steps required to make a ratified treaty usable domestically, applying them to one real treaty and country.</p><p class=\"study-print\"><strong>Printable:</strong> use your browser\u2019s Print \u2192 Save as PDF to keep an offline copy.</p></div>",
  "Introduction to the International Bill of Rights": "<div class=\"study-note\"><div class=\"revision-banner\"><strong>Human Rights and International Humanitarian Law</strong><span>Module 3: The International Bill of Human Rights</span></div><h3 class=\"lesson-note-title\">LESSON NOTE: INTRODUCTION TO THE INTERNATIONAL BILL OF RIGHTS</h3><div class=\"table-wrap\"><table class=\"study-table\"><thead><tr><th>Subject</th><th>Topic</th><th>Level</th><th>Lesson duration</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Human Rights and International Humanitarian Law</td><td>Introduction to the International Bill of Rights</td><td>Beginner to Advanced</td><td>40 minutes</td></tr></tbody></table></div><h4>Learning objectives</h4><p>By the end of this lesson you should be able to:</p><ul><li>Name the instruments that make up the International Bill of Human Rights</li><li>Explain why the single declaration became three separate documents</li><li>Describe the legal status of each component</li><li>Answer the examiner&#x27;s question on the structure of the Bill accurately</li></ul><h4>1. Introduction</h4><p>The International Bill of Human Rights is not one document. It is a name given to a group of instruments that together were meant to do what a national bill of rights does: state the rights, make them binding, and provide a way of enforcing them. Understanding why it took three documents and eighteen years explains most of what students find confusing about the system.</p><h4>2. Meaning: what the Bill consists of</h4><div class=\"study-defn\"><strong>International Bill of Human Rights:</strong> The collective name for the Universal Declaration of Human Rights (1948), the International Covenant on Civil and Political Rights (1966), the International Covenant on Economic, Social and Cultural Rights (1966), and the Optional Protocols to those Covenants.</div><div class=\"table-wrap\"><table class=\"study-table\"><thead><tr><th>Instrument</th><th>Adopted</th><th>In force</th><th>Legal form</th><th>Function</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>UDHR</td><td>1948</td><td>n/a</td><td>Resolution of the General Assembly; much now customary</td><td>States the rights</td></tr><tr><td>ICESCR</td><td>1966</td><td>1976</td><td>Treaty</td><td>Binds parties on economic, social and cultural rights</td></tr><tr><td>ICCPR</td><td>1966</td><td>1976</td><td>Treaty</td><td>Binds parties on civil and political rights</td></tr><tr><td>ICCPR First Optional Protocol</td><td>1966</td><td>1976</td><td>Treaty</td><td>Allows individual complaints to the Human Rights Committee</td></tr><tr><td>ICCPR Second Optional Protocol</td><td>1989</td><td>1991</td><td>Treaty</td><td>Abolition of the death penalty</td></tr><tr><td>ICESCR Optional Protocol</td><td>2008</td><td>2013</td><td>Treaty</td><td>Allows individual complaints to the CESCR Committee</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"study-callout\"><strong>Note:</strong> A very common error is to call the UDHR a treaty. It is not, and never was. It is a General Assembly resolution. Its enormous authority comes from near-universal acceptance over seventy-five years, which has turned much of its content into customary international law, not from treaty status.</div><h4>3. Why one declaration became three documents</h4><p>The Commission on Human Rights was asked in 1947 to produce a bill of rights. It decided the work had three parts: a declaration, a convention, and measures of implementation. The declaration came quickly. The rest took eighteen years, for reasons that were political rather than technical.</p><ol><li><strong>The Cold War split the content.</strong> Western states prioritised civil and political rights and argued these were immediately enforceable. Socialist states prioritised economic and social rights. Neither would accept a single treaty on the other's terms.</li><li><strong>The two categories were argued to need different machinery.</strong> Western states claimed civil and political rights could be judicially enforced immediately, while economic rights required resources and could only be achieved over time. This claim shaped the drafting even though, as later modules show, it is only partly true.</li><li><strong>Decolonisation changed the drafting body.</strong> Between 1948 and 1966 dozens of newly independent states joined the United Nations. Their priorities, particularly self-determination and the prohibition of racial discrimination, are visible in the Covenants and absent from the UDHR.</li><li><strong>The compromise was two treaties adopted the same day.</strong> The General Assembly resolved in 1952 to draft two covenants rather than one, insisting they be adopted and opened for signature simultaneously to emphasise their equal standing. Both were adopted on 16 December 1966.</li></ol><p>Note the consequence: the split is a product of Cold War politics, not of any principled difference in the nature of the rights. The 1993 Vienna Declaration explicitly reaffirmed that all human rights are universal, indivisible, interdependent and interrelated, which is the international community formally disowning the logic of the 1952 split while keeping the treaties it produced.</p><h4>4. What each Covenant contains</h4><div class=\"table-wrap\"><table class=\"study-table\"><thead><tr><th>ICCPR (53 articles)</th><th>ICESCR (31 articles)</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Self-determination (Art 1)</td><td>Self-determination (Art 1, identical text)</td></tr><tr><td>Life, freedom from torture, slavery, arbitrary detention</td><td>Work, just conditions of work, trade unions</td></tr><tr><td>Fair trial, no retroactive criminal law</td><td>Social security, protection of the family</td></tr><tr><td>Privacy, freedom of thought, conscience and religion</td><td>Adequate standard of living, food, housing</td></tr><tr><td>Expression, assembly, association</td><td>Highest attainable standard of health</td></tr><tr><td>Family, rights of the child, participation in public affairs</td><td>Education, including free compulsory primary education</td></tr><tr><td>Equality before the law, minority rights (Art 27)</td><td>Cultural life and benefits of scientific progress</td></tr></tbody></table></div><p>Article 1 of both Covenants is the same text on self-determination. This is deliberate: it was placed first in both to signal that the newly independent states regarded it as the precondition for all other rights.</p><h4>5. The obligation clauses differ, and this is the key comparison</h4><div class=\"table-wrap\"><table class=\"study-table\"><thead><tr><th>Feature</th><th>ICCPR Art 2</th><th>ICESCR Art 2</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Timing</td><td>'respect and ensure' immediately</td><td>'achieve progressively'</td></tr><tr><td>Resources</td><td>No resource qualifier</td><td>'to the maximum of its available resources'</td></tr><tr><td>Remedy</td><td>Art 2(3) requires an effective remedy</td><td>No equivalent clause</td></tr><tr><td>Derogation</td><td>Art 4 allows derogation in emergency, with non-derogable core</td><td>No derogation clause; Art 4 allows limitations only</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"study-callout\"><strong>Note:</strong> Examiners love this comparison. The honest answer says the difference is real but smaller than it looks: the ICCPR contains rights requiring resources (courts, prisons, elections), and the ICESCR contains immediate obligations (non-discrimination, the minimum core, the duty to take deliberate steps at once). Reciting 'civil rights are immediate, social rights are progressive' without that qualification loses marks.</div><h4>6. How the examiner&#x27;s question should be answered</h4><p>A question asking you to describe the International Bill of Human Rights is testing four things. Deliver them in this order.</p><ol><li><strong>Name the components with dates and legal form.</strong> UDHR 1948, a General Assembly resolution; ICCPR and ICESCR both 1966, in force 1976, both treaties; the three Optional Protocols with their functions.</li><li><strong>Explain the split.</strong> Cold War politics, the argued difference in machinery, and the 1952 decision to draft two covenants adopted simultaneously.</li><li><strong>Compare the obligation clauses.</strong> Use the table above, then qualify it as the note explains.</li><li><strong>State the current position.</strong> Vienna 1993: universal, indivisible, interdependent and interrelated. The 2008 ICESCR Optional Protocol gave economic rights an individual complaints procedure, closing much of the machinery gap.</li></ol><div class=\"study-callout\"><strong>Example:</strong> Weak answer: 'The International Bill of Human Rights is the UDHR and two treaties. One covers civil rights and one covers social rights.' This is not wrong but earns few marks: no dates, no legal form, no explanation of the split, no comparison of obligations, no current position. Strong answers do the four steps above in about two pages.</div><h4>Summary</h4><ul><li>The International Bill of Human Rights comprises the UDHR, the ICCPR, the ICESCR and their Optional Protocols.</li><li>The UDHR is a General Assembly resolution, not a treaty; its authority comes from acceptance and much of it is now customary law.</li><li>The split into two Covenants was a Cold War compromise reached in 1952, not a principled distinction.</li><li>The obligation clauses differ on timing, resources, remedy and derogation, but the difference is smaller than the slogan suggests.</li><li>Vienna 1993 reaffirmed that all rights are universal, indivisible, interdependent and interrelated.</li></ul><h4>Key Terms</h4><div class=\"table-wrap\"><table class=\"study-table\"><thead><tr><th>Term</th><th>Meaning</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>International Bill of Human Rights</td><td>The UDHR, the two 1966 Covenants and their Optional Protocols taken together.</td></tr><tr><td>Optional Protocol</td><td>A separate treaty adding obligations or procedures for states that choose to join it.</td></tr><tr><td>Progressive realisation</td><td>The ICESCR standard requiring steps towards full realisation to the maximum of available resources.</td></tr><tr><td>Indivisibility</td><td>The principle that civil, political, economic, social and cultural rights cannot be ranked or separated.</td></tr></tbody></table></div><h4>Review Questions</h4><h5>Multiple choice</h5><div class=\"study-mcq\"><p class=\"mcq-q\">Which instrument in the International Bill of Human Rights is not a treaty?</p><ol type=\"A\"><li>The Universal Declaration of Human Rights</li><li>The ICCPR</li><li>The ICESCR</li><li>The First Optional Protocol to the ICCPR</li></ol><details class=\"mcq-a\"><summary>Show answer</summary><p><strong>Answer: A.</strong> The UDHR is a General Assembly resolution; its force comes from acceptance and customary status, not treaty ratification.</p></details></div><div class=\"study-mcq\"><p class=\"mcq-q\">Why were there two Covenants rather than one?</p><ol type=\"A\"><li>Cold War disagreement over which rights mattered and how they should be enforced</li><li>The text was too long for one document</li><li>The UDHR required it</li><li>Regional bodies demanded separate treaties</li></ol><details class=\"mcq-a\"><summary>Show answer</summary><p><strong>Answer: A.</strong> The 1952 General Assembly decision split the drafting; both were nonetheless adopted the same day to signal equal standing.</p></details></div><div class=\"study-mcq\"><p class=\"mcq-q\">Which article is identical in both Covenants?</p><ol type=\"A\"><li>Article 1 on self-determination</li><li>Article 2 on obligations</li><li>Article 4 on derogation</li><li>Article 6 on the right to life</li></ol><details class=\"mcq-a\"><summary>Show answer</summary><p><strong>Answer: A.</strong> Self-determination was placed first in both at the insistence of newly independent states.</p></details></div><div class=\"study-mcq\"><p class=\"mcq-q\">What did the 2008 Optional Protocol to the ICESCR add?</p><ol type=\"A\"><li>An individual complaints procedure for economic, social and cultural rights</li><li>A derogation clause</li><li>A new list of rights</li><li>Compulsory jurisdiction of the International Court of Justice</li></ol><details class=\"mcq-a\"><summary>Show answer</summary><p><strong>Answer: A.</strong> It closed much of the machinery gap between the two Covenants, undermining the old claim that social rights are inherently non-justiciable.</p></details></div><h5>Short answer</h5><div class=\"study-sa\"><p class=\"sa-q\">List the instruments of the International Bill of Human Rights with their dates and legal form.</p><details class=\"sa-a\"><summary>Model answer</summary><p>The Universal Declaration of Human Rights, 1948, a resolution of the General Assembly; the International Covenant on Civil and Political Rights, adopted 1966 and in force 1976, a treaty; the International Covenant on Economic, Social and Cultural Rights, adopted 1966 and in force 1976, a treaty; the First Optional Protocol to the ICCPR, 1966, allowing individual complaints; the Second Optional Protocol to the ICCPR, 1989, on abolition of the death penalty; and the Optional Protocol to the ICESCR, 2008, allowing individual complaints.</p></details></div><div class=\"study-sa\"><p class=\"sa-q\">Compare the obligation clauses of the two Covenants and state one qualification.</p><details class=\"sa-a\"><summary>Model answer</summary><p>ICCPR Article 2 requires states to respect and ensure the rights immediately, with no resource qualifier and an express duty to provide an effective remedy. ICESCR Article 2 requires steps towards progressive realisation to the maximum of available resources, with no remedy clause. The qualification is that the difference is smaller than it appears: the ICCPR contains rights that cost money, such as courts and elections, and the ICESCR imposes immediate obligations of non-discrimination and a minimum core.</p></details></div><h4>Class Activity</h4><ol><li>Draw a one-page diagram of the International Bill of Human Rights showing each instrument, its date, its legal form and its function. Display it in class.</li><li>In pairs, argue the 1952 debate: one takes the Western position that the rights need different treaties, the other the socialist position that they belong together. Then write jointly which side history has vindicated.</li></ol><h4>Assignment</h4><p>Write one and a half pages describing the International Bill of Human Rights. Name every component with its date and legal form, explain why the drafting split into two Covenants, compare the obligation clauses of Article 2 in each, and state the current position on indivisibility with reference to Vienna 1993 and the 2008 Optional Protocol.</p><p class=\"study-print\"><strong>Printable:</strong> use your browser\u2019s Print \u2192 Save as PDF to keep an offline copy.</p></div>",
  "The ICCPR: Civil and Political Rights": "<div class=\"study-note\"><div class=\"revision-banner\"><strong>Human Rights and International Humanitarian Law</strong><span>Module 3: The International Bill of Human Rights</span></div><h3 class=\"lesson-note-title\">LESSON NOTE: THE ICCPR: CIVIL AND POLITICAL RIGHTS</h3><div class=\"table-wrap\"><table class=\"study-table\"><thead><tr><th>Subject</th><th>Topic</th><th>Level</th><th>Lesson duration</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Human Rights and International Humanitarian Law</td><td>The ICCPR: Civil and Political Rights</td><td>Beginner to Advanced</td><td>45 minutes</td></tr></tbody></table></div><h4>Learning objectives</h4><p>By the end of this lesson you should be able to:</p><ul><li>Set out the structure and main rights of the ICCPR</li><li>Explain the tripartite obligation to respect, protect and fulfil</li><li>Identify the non-derogable rights and explain why they are listed</li><li>Describe the ICCPR&#x27;s supervisory machinery</li></ul><h4>1. Introduction</h4><p>The ICCPR is the central treaty of the international human rights system. It has 174 states parties, a functioning complaints procedure, and a body of interpretation built over five decades in the General Comments and views of the Human Rights Committee. If you learn one treaty thoroughly, learn this one.</p><h4>2. Meaning and structure</h4><div class=\"study-defn\"><strong>ICCPR:</strong> The International Covenant on Civil and Political Rights, adopted 16 December 1966, in force 23 March 1976, containing 53 articles in six parts.</div><div class=\"table-wrap\"><table class=\"study-table\"><thead><tr><th>Part</th><th>Articles</th><th>Content</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>I</td><td>1</td><td>Self-determination</td></tr><tr><td>II</td><td>2-5</td><td>General obligations, non-discrimination, equality of men and women, derogation, prohibition of abuse of rights</td></tr><tr><td>III</td><td>6-27</td><td>The substantive rights</td></tr><tr><td>IV</td><td>28-45</td><td>The Human Rights Committee and reporting</td></tr><tr><td>V</td><td>46-47</td><td>Interpretive savings clauses</td></tr><tr><td>VI</td><td>48-53</td><td>Ratification, amendment, final clauses</td></tr></tbody></table></div><h4>3. The substantive rights in Part III</h4><div class=\"table-wrap\"><table class=\"study-table\"><thead><tr><th>Article</th><th>Right</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>6</td><td>Life</td></tr><tr><td>7</td><td>Freedom from torture and cruel, inhuman or degrading treatment; no medical experimentation without consent</td></tr><tr><td>8</td><td>Freedom from slavery, servitude and forced labour</td></tr><tr><td>9</td><td>Liberty and security of person; freedom from arbitrary arrest</td></tr><tr><td>10</td><td>Humane treatment in detention</td></tr><tr><td>11</td><td>No imprisonment for inability to fulfil a contractual obligation</td></tr><tr><td>12</td><td>Freedom of movement and residence</td></tr><tr><td>13</td><td>Procedural protection against expulsion of aliens</td></tr><tr><td>14</td><td>Fair trial and equality before the courts</td></tr><tr><td>15</td><td>No retroactive criminal law</td></tr><tr><td>16</td><td>Recognition as a person before the law</td></tr><tr><td>17</td><td>Privacy, family, home and correspondence; honour and reputation</td></tr><tr><td>18</td><td>Freedom of thought, conscience and religion</td></tr><tr><td>19</td><td>Freedom of opinion and expression</td></tr><tr><td>20</td><td>Prohibition of war propaganda and incitement to discrimination, hostility or violence</td></tr><tr><td>21</td><td>Freedom of peaceful assembly</td></tr><tr><td>22</td><td>Freedom of association, including trade unions</td></tr><tr><td>23</td><td>Protection of the family and the right to marry</td></tr><tr><td>24</td><td>Rights of the child</td></tr><tr><td>25</td><td>Participation in public affairs, voting, access to public service</td></tr><tr><td>26</td><td>Equality before the law and equal protection without discrimination</td></tr><tr><td>27</td><td>Rights of ethnic, religious and linguistic minorities</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"study-callout\"><strong>Note:</strong> Article 26 is free-standing. Unlike Article 2(1), which prohibits discrimination in the enjoyment of Covenant rights, Article 26 guarantees equality before the law generally. That means a discriminatory social security scheme can be challenged under Article 26 even though social security is an ICESCR right. The Human Rights Committee established this in Broeks v Netherlands (1987), and it is the single most useful doctrinal point in the whole Covenant.</div><h4>4. The tripartite obligation</h4><p>Article 2(1) requires states to 'respect and ensure' the rights. Modern interpretation reads all human rights obligations as having three layers.</p><div class=\"table-wrap\"><table class=\"study-table\"><thead><tr><th>Layer</th><th>What it requires</th><th>ICCPR illustration</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Respect</td><td>The state must not itself violate the right</td><td>Police must not torture a detainee</td></tr><tr><td>Protect</td><td>The state must prevent third parties from violating it</td><td>The state must investigate and prosecute a private killing</td></tr><tr><td>Fulfil</td><td>The state must take positive steps to make the right real</td><td>The state must fund legal aid so a fair trial is possible</td></tr></tbody></table></div><p>The old claim that civil and political rights are merely negative, requiring only that the state leave people alone, is false. Article 2(3) alone requires a functioning system of remedies, which is expensive. A fair trial requires courts, judges, interpreters and defence counsel. This is why the sharp ICCPR/ICESCR distinction breaks down under examination.</p><h4>5. Derogation and the non-derogable core</h4><div class=\"study-defn\"><strong>Derogation:</strong> A lawful temporary suspension of certain treaty obligations during a public emergency threatening the life of the nation.</div><p>Article 4 permits derogation only where four conditions are met.</p><ol><li>A public emergency threatening the life of the nation, officially proclaimed.</li><li>Measures strictly required by the exigencies of the situation, which is a proportionality test.</li><li>No inconsistency with other international obligations, including IHL.</li><li>No discrimination solely on grounds of race, colour, sex, language, religion or social origin.</li></ol><p>Even then, Article 4(2) lists rights from which no derogation is ever permitted.</p><ul><li>Article 6, the right to life.</li><li>Article 7, freedom from torture and cruel, inhuman or degrading treatment.</li><li>Article 8(1) and (2), freedom from slavery and servitude.</li><li>Article 11, no imprisonment for contractual debt.</li><li>Article 15, no retroactive criminal law.</li><li>Article 16, recognition as a person before the law.</li><li>Article 18, freedom of thought, conscience and religion.</li></ul><div class=\"study-callout\"><strong>Note:</strong> General Comment 29 adds that certain guarantees not listed in Article 4(2) are nonetheless non-derogable because they are necessary to protect those that are. The clearest example is habeas corpus: if a detainee cannot challenge detention before a court, the prohibition of torture becomes unenforceable in practice. Mentioning General Comment 29 in an answer on derogation is a reliable way to show reading beyond the treaty text.</div><h4>6. Supervisory machinery</h4><div class=\"table-wrap\"><table class=\"study-table\"><thead><tr><th>Mechanism</th><th>Basis</th><th>What it does</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>State reporting</td><td>ICCPR Art 40</td><td>Every party reports periodically; the Committee issues concluding observations</td></tr><tr><td>General Comments</td><td>Practice under Art 40</td><td>Authoritative interpretation of Covenant articles</td></tr><tr><td>Inter-state complaints</td><td>ICCPR Art 41</td><td>Optional; almost never used</td></tr><tr><td>Individual communications</td><td>First Optional Protocol</td><td>Individuals may complain after exhausting domestic remedies; the Committee issues views</td></tr></tbody></table></div><p>The Committee's views under the Optional Protocol are not binding judgments in the way a court judgment is, but General Comment 33 states that they represent an authoritative determination and that a state party acts in bad faith by ignoring them. Compliance is partial, which is the standard criticism of the system.</p><h4>7. How the examiner&#x27;s question should be answered</h4><p>For 'describe the ICCPR', structure the answer as: adoption and entry into force with the number of parties; the six-part structure; the main rights grouped rather than listed one by one, which wastes space; the tripartite obligation; derogation with the non-derogable list and General Comment 29; and the machinery. Group the rights as liberty and integrity (6-11), due process (14-16), private life and belief (17-18), participation (19-22, 25), and equality and minorities (26-27).</p><div class=\"study-callout\"><strong>Example:</strong> Do not list all twenty-two rights individually in an essay. An examiner marking 'main rights protected' wants to see that you can organise them. Grouping shows understanding; a list shows memorisation.</div><h4>Summary</h4><ul><li>The ICCPR was adopted in 1966, entered into force in 1976, and contains 53 articles in six parts.</li><li>Article 26 is a free-standing equality guarantee not limited to Covenant rights.</li><li>All rights carry obligations to respect, protect and fulfil, so the claim that civil rights are purely negative is false.</li><li>Article 4 permits derogation on four conditions but Article 4(2) lists seven non-derogable rights.</li><li>General Comment 29 treats habeas corpus and other guarantees protecting non-derogable rights as themselves non-derogable.</li><li>Supervision runs through state reporting, General Comments, and individual communications under the First Optional Protocol.</li></ul><h4>Key Terms</h4><div class=\"table-wrap\"><table class=\"study-table\"><thead><tr><th>Term</th><th>Meaning</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>ICCPR</td><td>The International Covenant on Civil and Political Rights, 1966.</td></tr><tr><td>Non-derogable right</td><td>A right that may not be suspended even during a public emergency.</td></tr><tr><td>General Comment</td><td>An authoritative interpretation of a treaty provision issued by its supervisory committee.</td></tr><tr><td>Views</td><td>The Human Rights Committee&#x27;s findings on an individual communication under the First Optional Protocol.</td></tr></tbody></table></div><h4>Review Questions</h4><h5>Multiple choice</h5><div class=\"study-mcq\"><p class=\"mcq-q\">Which right is NOT listed as non-derogable in ICCPR Article 4(2)?</p><ol type=\"A\"><li>Freedom of expression</li><li>Freedom from torture</li><li>Freedom of thought, conscience and religion</li><li>Recognition as a person before the law</li></ol><details class=\"mcq-a\"><summary>Show answer</summary><p><strong>Answer: A.</strong> Expression under Article 19 may be restricted and derogated from; torture, thought and legal personality may not.</p></details></div><div class=\"study-mcq\"><p class=\"mcq-q\">What makes ICCPR Article 26 distinctive?</p><ol type=\"A\"><li>It guarantees equality before the law generally, not only in relation to Covenant rights</li><li>It is non-derogable</li><li>It applies only to citizens</li><li>It creates the Human Rights Committee</li></ol><details class=\"mcq-a\"><summary>Show answer</summary><p><strong>Answer: A.</strong> Broeks v Netherlands used it to challenge discrimination in social security, an ICESCR subject matter.</p></details></div><div class=\"study-mcq\"><p class=\"mcq-q\">Under General Comment 29, why is habeas corpus treated as effectively non-derogable?</p><ol type=\"A\"><li>Without it, the non-derogable prohibition of torture cannot be enforced</li><li>It is listed in Article 4(2)</li><li>It is a peremptory norm of general international law</li><li>The Optional Protocol requires it</li></ol><details class=\"mcq-a\"><summary>Show answer</summary><p><strong>Answer: A.</strong> Guarantees necessary to protect non-derogable rights are themselves protected, even though not listed.</p></details></div><div class=\"study-mcq\"><p class=\"mcq-q\">What is the status of the Human Rights Committee&#x27;s views on an individual communication?</p><ol type=\"A\"><li>An authoritative determination that a state cannot in good faith ignore, though not a binding court judgment</li><li>A binding judgment enforceable by the Security Council</li><li>A non-binding recommendation of no legal weight</li><li>A referral to the International Criminal Court</li></ol><details class=\"mcq-a\"><summary>Show answer</summary><p><strong>Answer: A.</strong> General Comment 33 sets out this position; compliance in practice is partial.</p></details></div><h5>Short answer</h5><div class=\"study-sa\"><p class=\"sa-q\">State the four conditions for a lawful derogation under ICCPR Article 4.</p><details class=\"sa-a\"><summary>Model answer</summary><p>There must be a public emergency threatening the life of the nation which has been officially proclaimed; the measures must be strictly required by the exigencies of the situation, a proportionality test; the measures must not be inconsistent with the state&#x27;s other international obligations, including international humanitarian law; and they must not discriminate solely on grounds of race, colour, sex, language, religion or social origin. In addition, other states parties must be notified through the Secretary-General.</p></details></div><div class=\"study-sa\"><p class=\"sa-q\">Explain the tripartite obligation using one ICCPR right.</p><details class=\"sa-a\"><summary>Model answer</summary><p>Take the right to life under Article 6. The duty to respect requires the state&#x27;s own agents not to kill unlawfully. The duty to protect requires the state to prevent killings by private persons, which includes an effective criminal law, policing and a duty to investigate deaths. The duty to fulfil requires positive steps such as regulating dangerous industries and, on the Human Rights Committee&#x27;s General Comment 36, addressing conditions like malnutrition that threaten life.</p></details></div><h4>Class Activity</h4><ol><li>Take any three ICCPR articles and write out what the duties to respect, protect and fulfil each require. Compare with a classmate.</li><li>Find one real derogation notification lodged with the UN Secretary-General. Assess it against the four Article 4 conditions and write a short verdict.</li></ol><h4>Assignment</h4><p>Write two pages on the ICCPR. Cover its adoption and structure, group and describe its main rights, explain the obligations to respect, protect and fulfil, set out the derogation regime including the non-derogable rights and the effect of General Comment 29, and describe its supervisory machinery, ending with an assessment of how effective that machinery is.</p><p class=\"study-print\"><strong>Printable:</strong> use your browser\u2019s Print \u2192 Save as PDF to keep an offline copy.</p></div>",
  "The ICESCR: Economic, Social and Cultural Rights": "<div class=\"study-note\"><div class=\"revision-banner\"><strong>Human Rights and International Humanitarian Law</strong><span>Module 3: The International Bill of Human Rights</span></div><h3 class=\"lesson-note-title\">LESSON NOTE: THE ICESCR: ECONOMIC, SOCIAL AND CULTURAL RIGHTS</h3><div class=\"table-wrap\"><table class=\"study-table\"><thead><tr><th>Subject</th><th>Topic</th><th>Level</th><th>Lesson duration</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Human Rights and International Humanitarian Law</td><td>The ICESCR: Economic, Social and Cultural Rights</td><td>Beginner to Advanced</td><td>45 minutes</td></tr></tbody></table></div><h4>Learning objectives</h4><p>By the end of this lesson you should be able to:</p><ul><li>Set out the structure and main rights of the ICESCR</li><li>Explain the standard objections to these rights and answer them</li><li>Describe how the Covenant is supervised</li><li>Show that economic and social rights are justiciable</li></ul><h4>1. Introduction</h4><p>Economic, social and cultural rights are the half of the human rights system that is most often dismissed and least often taught properly. The dismissal usually takes the form: these are aspirations, not rights; they cost money; courts cannot enforce them. Each objection has an answer, and this lesson gives it.</p><h4>2. Meaning and structure</h4><div class=\"study-defn\"><strong>ICESCR:</strong> The International Covenant on Economic, Social and Cultural Rights, adopted 16 December 1966, in force 3 January 1976, with 172 states parties.</div><div class=\"table-wrap\"><table class=\"study-table\"><thead><tr><th>Article</th><th>Right</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>1</td><td>Self-determination, including over natural wealth and resources</td></tr><tr><td>2</td><td>General obligations: progressive realisation, maximum available resources, non-discrimination</td></tr><tr><td>3</td><td>Equal rights of men and women</td></tr><tr><td>6</td><td>Work, freely chosen or accepted</td></tr><tr><td>7</td><td>Just and favourable conditions of work, including fair wages and safe conditions</td></tr><tr><td>8</td><td>Trade union rights, including the right to strike</td></tr><tr><td>9</td><td>Social security, including social insurance</td></tr><tr><td>10</td><td>Protection of the family, mothers and children</td></tr><tr><td>11</td><td>Adequate standard of living: food, clothing, housing, and continuous improvement</td></tr><tr><td>12</td><td>Highest attainable standard of physical and mental health</td></tr><tr><td>13-14</td><td>Education, with free compulsory primary education and a plan of action where it is not yet provided</td></tr><tr><td>15</td><td>Cultural life, benefits of scientific progress, and authors' interests</td></tr></tbody></table></div><h4>3. Article 2(1): the obligation clause parsed</h4><p>Article 2(1) requires each state party 'to take steps ... to the maximum of its available resources, with a view to achieving progressively the full realization of the rights ... by all appropriate means, including particularly the adoption of legislative measures.'</p><div class=\"table-wrap\"><table class=\"study-table\"><thead><tr><th>Phrase</th><th>What it means</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>'undertakes to take steps'</td><td>An immediate obligation. The steps must begin at once; only the outcome is progressive.</td></tr><tr><td>'maximum of its available resources'</td><td>Measured against what the state actually has, so a poor state is judged differently from a rich one, but a state that spends on prestige projects while primary schools have no roofs is in breach.</td></tr><tr><td>'achieving progressively'</td><td>Continuous improvement, which also implies a strong presumption against retrogression.</td></tr><tr><td>'all appropriate means'</td><td>Legislation is specifically named; judicial remedies, policy, budget allocation and administrative measures are all included.</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"study-callout\"><strong>Note:</strong> General Comment 3 of the CESCR Committee is the essential reading here. It establishes that despite the progressive language, the Covenant imposes obligations of immediate effect: the duty to take steps at once, the duty of non-discrimination, and the minimum core obligation.</div><h4>4. The three standard objections and their answers</h4><div class=\"table-wrap\"><table class=\"study-table\"><thead><tr><th>Objection</th><th>Answer</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>They are aspirations, not rights</td><td>They are in a ratified treaty with 172 parties, a supervisory committee, General Comments and, since 2013, an individual complaints procedure. That is the same architecture as the ICCPR.</td></tr><tr><td>They cost money, so they cannot be legal obligations</td><td>So do civil and political rights. Courts, prisons meeting Article 10 standards, elections and legal aid are expensive. Cost distinguishes no category of right.</td></tr><tr><td>Courts cannot enforce them without usurping budget decisions</td><td>Courts across the world do enforce them, using reasonableness review rather than substituting their own budget. The South African, Indian, Colombian and Kenyan courts all have substantial jurisprudence.</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"study-callout\"><strong>Example:</strong> Government of South Africa v Grootboom (2000) is the standard illustration. The Constitutional Court held that the state's housing programme was unreasonable because it made no provision at all for people in desperate need. The court did not order houses built or set a budget. It reviewed the programme for reasonableness and found it failed because a whole category of people was excluded. This is the model answer to the objection that courts cannot handle these rights.</div><h4>5. Supervision</h4><div class=\"table-wrap\"><table class=\"study-table\"><thead><tr><th>Mechanism</th><th>Basis</th><th>Note</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>State reporting</td><td>ECOSOC practice, now the Committee</td><td>Periodic reports and concluding observations</td></tr><tr><td>General Comments</td><td>Committee practice</td><td>GC 3 on obligations, GC 12 food, GC 13 education, GC 14 health, GC 15 water, GC 4 and 7 housing</td></tr><tr><td>Individual communications</td><td>Optional Protocol 2008, in force 2013</td><td>Uses a reasonableness standard of review</td></tr><tr><td>Inquiry and inter-state</td><td>Optional Protocol, opt-in</td><td>Rarely used</td></tr></tbody></table></div><p>The Committee on Economic, Social and Cultural Rights is unusual in that it was created by an ECOSOC resolution in 1985 rather than by the Covenant itself, which had left supervision to ECOSOC directly. That arrangement had proved ineffective.</p><h4>6. How the examiner&#x27;s question should be answered</h4><p>Questions on this Covenant almost always ask, in some form, whether economic and social rights are 'real' rights. The structure that scores highest is: state the objection fairly; answer each limb; give a case; then concede the genuine difficulty.</p><ol><li>State the three objections as their proponents would state them, not as straw men.</li><li>Answer each: treaty status and machinery; cost is common to all rights; reasonableness review is a workable judicial technique.</li><li>Give one case with its reasoning, not just its name. Grootboom is the safest.</li><li>Concede the real difficulty: enforcement outcomes are mixed, judgments are often not implemented, and the resource qualifier makes breach genuinely harder to establish than under the ICCPR. A student who concedes nothing looks partisan; a student who concedes precisely looks informed.</li></ol><div class=\"study-callout\"><strong>Note:</strong> The single most common error is writing that the ICESCR contains no immediate obligations. It contains three: take steps now, do not discriminate, and satisfy the minimum core. Stating this correctly separates strong answers from average ones.</div><h4>Summary</h4><ul><li>The ICESCR contains rights to work, social security, an adequate standard of living, health, education and cultural life.</li><li>Article 2(1) requires immediate steps towards progressive realisation to the maximum of available resources.</li><li>General Comment 3 identifies three obligations of immediate effect: take steps, non-discrimination, and the minimum core.</li><li>The objections that these rights are aspirational, costly and non-justiciable each have clear answers.</li><li>Grootboom shows courts reviewing social rights programmes for reasonableness without setting budgets.</li><li>The 2008 Optional Protocol, in force 2013, added an individual complaints procedure.</li></ul><h4>Key Terms</h4><div class=\"table-wrap\"><table class=\"study-table\"><thead><tr><th>Term</th><th>Meaning</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>ICESCR</td><td>The International Covenant on Economic, Social and Cultural Rights, 1966.</td></tr><tr><td>Justiciability</td><td>The capacity of a right to be adjudicated by a court.</td></tr><tr><td>Reasonableness review</td><td>Judicial assessment of whether a state&#x27;s programme is a reasonable means of realising a right, without the court setting the policy.</td></tr><tr><td>Non-retrogression</td><td>The presumption that deliberate backward steps in the enjoyment of a right are impermissible.</td></tr></tbody></table></div><h4>Review Questions</h4><h5>Multiple choice</h5><div class=\"study-mcq\"><p class=\"mcq-q\">Which obligations under the ICESCR take immediate effect?</p><ol type=\"A\"><li>Taking steps, non-discrimination, and the minimum core</li><li>None; all obligations are progressive</li><li>Only the right to education</li><li>Only obligations accepted under the Optional Protocol</li></ol><details class=\"mcq-a\"><summary>Show answer</summary><p><strong>Answer: A.</strong> General Comment 3 establishes all three despite the progressive language of Article 2(1).</p></details></div><div class=\"study-mcq\"><p class=\"mcq-q\">What did the South African Constitutional Court decide in Grootboom?</p><ol type=\"A\"><li>The housing programme was unreasonable because it made no provision for people in desperate need</li><li>Every claimant was entitled to a house immediately</li><li>Social rights are not justiciable</li><li>The court ordered a specific housing budget</li></ol><details class=\"mcq-a\"><summary>Show answer</summary><p><strong>Answer: A.</strong> It applied reasonableness review, leaving the policy choice to government but requiring the programme to cover the desperate.</p></details></div><div class=\"study-mcq\"><p class=\"mcq-q\">Why is the objection that social rights &#x27;cost money&#x27; weak?</p><ol type=\"A\"><li>Civil and political rights also cost money, so cost distinguishes no category</li><li>Social rights are in fact free to provide</li><li>States have unlimited resources</li><li>The Covenant excludes costly rights</li></ol><details class=\"mcq-a\"><summary>Show answer</summary><p><strong>Answer: A.</strong> Courts, prisons, elections and legal aid are expensive, yet nobody argues those rights are therefore aspirational.</p></details></div><div class=\"study-mcq\"><p class=\"mcq-q\">What standard of review does the ICESCR Optional Protocol procedure apply?</p><ol type=\"A\"><li>Reasonableness of the steps taken</li><li>Strict liability for any unmet need</li><li>No review; it is purely advisory</li><li>The margin of appreciation only</li></ol><details class=\"mcq-a\"><summary>Show answer</summary><p><strong>Answer: A.</strong> Article 8(4) directs the Committee to consider the reasonableness of the steps taken by the state party.</p></details></div><h5>Short answer</h5><div class=\"study-sa\"><p class=\"sa-q\">Parse the four key phrases of ICESCR Article 2(1).</p><details class=\"sa-a\"><summary>Model answer</summary><p>&#x27;Undertakes to take steps&#x27; is an immediate obligation: action must begin at once even though the outcome is progressive. &#x27;To the maximum of its available resources&#x27; measures the state against what it actually has, so poverty is a relevant but not unlimited defence and misallocation is a breach. &#x27;Achieving progressively&#x27; requires continuous improvement and creates a strong presumption against retrogression. &#x27;All appropriate means&#x27; expressly includes legislation and extends to judicial remedies, policy, administration and budgeting.</p></details></div><div class=\"study-sa\"><p class=\"sa-q\">Answer the objection that economic and social rights are not justiciable.</p><details class=\"sa-a\"><summary>Model answer</summary><p>Courts in South Africa, India, Colombia and Kenya adjudicate these rights regularly using reasonableness review, which asks whether the state&#x27;s programme is a reasonable means of realisation rather than substituting the court&#x27;s own policy. Grootboom illustrates this: the court found the housing programme unreasonable for excluding people in desperate need, but did not order houses built or fix a budget. The 2008 Optional Protocol adopts the same reasonableness standard at international level.</p></details></div><h4>Class Activity</h4><ol><li>Choose one ICESCR right and find your country&#x27;s most recent concluding observations from the CESCR Committee on it. Summarise the Committee&#x27;s main criticism in one paragraph.</li><li>Debate the motion &#x27;Economic and social rights should be enforceable in court.&#x27; Prepare both sides, then write which argument you found strongest and why.</li></ol><h4>Assignment</h4><p>Write two pages on the ICESCR. Set out its main rights and the obligation in Article 2(1), explain the three obligations of immediate effect identified in General Comment 3, state and answer the three standard objections to economic and social rights using at least one decided case, describe the supervisory machinery, and conclude with a fair statement of the genuine difficulties that remain.</p><p class=\"study-print\"><strong>Printable:</strong> use your browser\u2019s Print \u2192 Save as PDF to keep an offline copy.</p></div>",
  "Progressive Realisation and Minimum Core Obligations": "<div class=\"study-note\"><div class=\"revision-banner\"><strong>Human Rights and International Humanitarian Law</strong><span>Module 3: The International Bill of Human Rights</span></div><h3 class=\"lesson-note-title\">LESSON NOTE: PROGRESSIVE REALISATION AND MINIMUM CORE OBLIGATIONS</h3><div class=\"table-wrap\"><table class=\"study-table\"><thead><tr><th>Subject</th><th>Topic</th><th>Level</th><th>Lesson duration</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Human Rights and International Humanitarian Law</td><td>Progressive Realisation and Minimum Core Obligations</td><td>Beginner to Advanced</td><td>40 minutes</td></tr></tbody></table></div><h4>Learning objectives</h4><p>By the end of this lesson you should be able to:</p><ul><li>Define progressive realisation and explain what it does and does not permit</li><li>State the minimum core obligation and its content for named rights</li><li>Explain the presumption against retrogressive measures</li><li>Apply the framework to a real policy decision</li></ul><h4>1. Introduction</h4><p>Progressive realisation is the most misunderstood phrase in human rights law. Governments read it as 'when we can afford it'. Advocates sometimes read it as meaningless. Both are wrong. It is a legal standard with a determinate content, and this lesson sets out that content.</p><h4>2. Meaning</h4><div class=\"study-defn\"><strong>Progressive realisation:</strong> The obligation under ICESCR Article 2(1) to take steps, to the maximum of available resources, with a view to achieving the full realisation of Covenant rights over time.</div><p>Three things follow immediately from the wording.</p><ul><li>It recognises that full realisation of rights such as health and housing cannot be achieved overnight in any state.</li><li>It imposes a specific and continuing obligation to move as expeditiously and effectively as possible towards the goal.</li><li>It is not a licence to defer. General Comment 3 says the phrase 'must not be misinterpreted as depriving the obligation of all meaningful content'.</li></ul><h4>3. What progressive realisation is not</h4><div class=\"table-wrap\"><table class=\"study-table\"><thead><tr><th>Misreading</th><th>Correct position</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>'We will do it when we can afford it'</td><td>Steps must be taken now, using resources actually available; the obligation is continuous.</td></tr><tr><td>'Progress is whatever we choose to do'</td><td>Steps must be deliberate, concrete and targeted as clearly as possible towards realisation.</td></tr><tr><td>'Any progress satisfies the obligation'</td><td>Progress must be to the maximum of available resources, so token measures while resources are diverted elsewhere fail.</td></tr><tr><td>'There is no breach without total failure'</td><td>Failure to satisfy the minimum core is a breach regardless of overall progress.</td></tr><tr><td>'We may go backwards if finances tighten'</td><td>Retrogression is presumed impermissible and must be justified.</td></tr></tbody></table></div><h4>4. The minimum core obligation</h4><div class=\"study-defn\"><strong>Minimum core obligation:</strong> The obligation to ensure the satisfaction of, at the very least, minimum essential levels of each right, irrespective of the state's stage of development.</div><p>General Comment 3, paragraph 10, states that a state in which any significant number of individuals is deprived of essential foodstuffs, essential primary health care, basic shelter and housing, or the most basic forms of education is prima facie failing to discharge its obligations. Later General Comments give the content right by right.</p><div class=\"table-wrap\"><table class=\"study-table\"><thead><tr><th>Right</th><th>Minimum core content (illustrative)</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Food (GC 12)</td><td>Access to the minimum essential food that is nutritionally adequate and safe, ensuring freedom from hunger</td></tr><tr><td>Water (GC 15)</td><td>Access to the minimum essential amount of safe water for personal and domestic use; physical access without discrimination</td></tr><tr><td>Health (GC 14)</td><td>Essential primary health care, essential drugs, non-discriminatory access to health facilities, reproductive and child health care, a national public health strategy</td></tr><tr><td>Education (GC 13)</td><td>Free and compulsory primary education for all, without discrimination</td></tr><tr><td>Housing (GC 4)</td><td>Basic shelter, security of tenure, and protection from forced eviction</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"study-callout\"><strong>Note:</strong> The minimum core can be shifted only by proving that every available resource, including through international assistance, has been used and the core still cannot be met. General Comment 3 places that burden squarely on the state. In practice states almost never attempt this showing.</div><h4>5. Retrogressive measures</h4><div class=\"study-defn\"><strong>Retrogressive measure:</strong> A deliberate step backwards in the level of enjoyment of a Covenant right.</div><p>Retrogression is not automatically prohibited, but it is presumed impermissible. To justify one, a state must show the following.</p><ol><li>The measure was adopted after the most careful consideration of all alternatives.</li><li>It is justified by reference to the totality of the rights in the Covenant, in the context of full use of maximum available resources.</li><li>It is temporary and covers only the period of necessity.</li><li>It is non-discriminatory and mitigates inequalities that may grow in times of crisis.</li><li>It protects the minimum core content of the right absolutely.</li><li>It was adopted with genuine participation of affected groups and is subject to meaningful review.</li></ol><div class=\"study-callout\"><strong>Example:</strong> An austerity programme cuts the primary healthcare budget by forty per cent while leaving military procurement untouched and cutting the top rate of tax. Under the test above this fails at once: alternatives were plainly not exhausted, resources were not used to the maximum, the measure is not shown to be temporary, and if it takes essential primary care below the minimum core it is impermissible however the rest of the analysis goes.</div><h4>6. Applying the framework</h4><p>A useful four-question test for any policy decision affecting an ICESCR right.</p><ol><li><strong>Is the minimum core satisfied?</strong> If not, there is a prima facie breach regardless of everything else.</li><li><strong>Are deliberate, concrete, targeted steps being taken?</strong> Not intentions or plans without funding.</li><li><strong>Are resources being used to the maximum available?</strong> Compare the sector's budget with discretionary spending elsewhere.</li><li><strong>Is anything going backwards, and if so is it justified under the six-part test?</strong></li></ol><p>This test is the practical value of the lesson. It converts a vague standard into questions you can put to a ministry, a budget document or an examiner's problem question.</p><h4>7. How the examiner&#x27;s question should be answered</h4><p>A question on progressive realisation is usually a trap: it invites you to say the obligation is weak. Do the opposite. Define it precisely, list what it excludes, set out the minimum core with content from at least two General Comments, give the retrogression test, and then apply the four questions to whatever facts the question supplies. Naming General Comment 3 and one right-specific General Comment is the difference between a general and a specific answer.</p><h4>Summary</h4><ul><li>Progressive realisation requires immediate, deliberate, concrete steps towards full realisation, not deferral.</li><li>The minimum core obligation applies irrespective of a state&#x27;s stage of development and its breach is prima facie a violation.</li><li>General Comment 3 places on the state the burden of showing that maximum available resources were used before the core can be excused.</li><li>Retrogressive measures are presumed impermissible and must satisfy a six-part justification test.</li><li>A four-question test converts the standard into a usable tool: core satisfied, steps taken, resources maximised, retrogression justified.</li></ul><h4>Key Terms</h4><div class=\"table-wrap\"><table class=\"study-table\"><thead><tr><th>Term</th><th>Meaning</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Progressive realisation</td><td>The duty to take steps to the maximum of available resources towards the full realisation of ICESCR rights over time.</td></tr><tr><td>Minimum core obligation</td><td>The duty to ensure minimum essential levels of each right regardless of the state&#x27;s development level.</td></tr><tr><td>Retrogressive measure</td><td>A deliberate backward step in the enjoyment of a right, presumed impermissible.</td></tr><tr><td>Maximum available resources</td><td>The benchmark measuring state effort against what it actually has, including through international assistance.</td></tr></tbody></table></div><h4>Review Questions</h4><h5>Multiple choice</h5><div class=\"study-mcq\"><p class=\"mcq-q\">What does &#x27;progressive realisation&#x27; require immediately?</p><ol type=\"A\"><li>Deliberate, concrete and targeted steps, beginning at once</li><li>Nothing until resources permit</li><li>Full realisation of the right</li><li>Only the adoption of a plan</li></ol><details class=\"mcq-a\"><summary>Show answer</summary><p><strong>Answer: A.</strong> General Comment 3 says the phrase must not be read as depriving the obligation of meaningful content; the steps are immediate even though the outcome is progressive.</p></details></div><div class=\"study-mcq\"><p class=\"mcq-q\">A state fails to provide essential primary health care to a significant part of its population. What is the position?</p><ol type=\"A\"><li>A prima facie breach of the minimum core, regardless of overall progress</li><li>No breach if the state is developing</li><li>No breach unless the Optional Protocol applies</li><li>A breach only if discrimination is shown</li></ol><details class=\"mcq-a\"><summary>Show answer</summary><p><strong>Answer: A.</strong> General Comment 3 paragraph 10 makes the minimum core independent of the state&#x27;s stage of development.</p></details></div><div class=\"study-mcq\"><p class=\"mcq-q\">Which is required to justify a retrogressive measure?</p><ol type=\"A\"><li>That it protects the minimum core and is temporary, non-discriminatory and adopted after considering all alternatives</li><li>Nothing; states may reduce provision freely</li><li>Approval by the Human Rights Council</li><li>That another right is improved instead</li></ol><details class=\"mcq-a\"><summary>Show answer</summary><p><strong>Answer: A.</strong> The six-part test also requires full use of maximum available resources, participation of affected groups and meaningful review.</p></details></div><div class=\"study-mcq\"><p class=\"mcq-q\">Who bears the burden of showing that maximum available resources were used?</p><ol type=\"A\"><li>The state</li><li>The complainant</li><li>The CESCR Committee</li><li>The donor community</li></ol><details class=\"mcq-a\"><summary>Show answer</summary><p><strong>Answer: A.</strong> General Comment 3 places the burden on the state, which is why states almost never attempt the showing.</p></details></div><h5>Short answer</h5><div class=\"study-sa\"><p class=\"sa-q\">Distinguish progressive realisation from the minimum core obligation.</p><details class=\"sa-a\"><summary>Model answer</summary><p>Progressive realisation is the general standard in Article 2(1): the state must take deliberate, concrete steps to the maximum of its available resources towards full realisation over time, and its performance is judged against its actual capacity. The minimum core is a floor beneath that standard: minimum essential levels of each right, such as essential primary health care, basic shelter and free primary education, must be ensured irrespective of the state&#x27;s stage of development, and failure is prima facie a violation unless the state proves every available resource including international assistance was exhausted.</p></details></div><div class=\"study-sa\"><p class=\"sa-q\">Apply the four-question test to a decision to cut the education budget in a financial crisis.</p><details class=\"sa-a\"><summary>Model answer</summary><p>First, does the cut take provision below the minimum core of free and compulsory primary education for all? If so it is impermissible. Second, are deliberate targeted steps still being taken towards realisation, or has the programme stalled? Third, are resources being used to the maximum, which requires comparing the education cut with discretionary spending and revenue choices elsewhere? Fourth, since this is retrogressive, can the state show the six conditions: alternatives considered, justified against the totality of rights, temporary, non-discriminatory with mitigation, core protected, and adopted with participation and review?</p></details></div><h4>Class Activity</h4><ol><li>Obtain your national budget summary. Calculate the share allocated to health and education and compare with any regional commitment your state has accepted. Write a paragraph on whether resources appear to be used to the maximum.</li><li>In groups, take a real austerity measure from any country and test it against the six-part retrogression test. Present your verdict with reasons.</li></ol><h4>Assignment</h4><p>Write one and a half pages on progressive realisation. Define it, list four misreadings and correct each, explain the minimum core with content drawn from at least two General Comments, set out the retrogression test in full, and apply the four-question framework to one real policy decision of your choice.</p><p class=\"study-print\"><strong>Printable:</strong> use your browser\u2019s Print \u2192 Save as PDF to keep an offline copy.</p></div>",
  "Equality and Non-Discrimination": "<div class=\"study-note\"><div class=\"revision-banner\"><strong>Human Rights and International Humanitarian Law</strong><span>Module 3: The International Bill of Human Rights</span></div><h3 class=\"lesson-note-title\">LESSON NOTE: EQUALITY AND NON-DISCRIMINATION</h3><div class=\"table-wrap\"><table class=\"study-table\"><thead><tr><th>Subject</th><th>Topic</th><th>Level</th><th>Lesson duration</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Human Rights and International Humanitarian Law</td><td>Equality and Non-Discrimination</td><td>Beginner to Advanced</td><td>45 minutes</td></tr></tbody></table></div><h4>Learning objectives</h4><p>By the end of this lesson you should be able to:</p><ul><li>Distinguish formal from substantive equality</li><li>Distinguish direct from indirect discrimination</li><li>Explain the test for justified differential treatment</li><li>Explain special measures and intersectional discrimination</li></ul><h4>1. Introduction</h4><p>Non-discrimination is the one obligation that appears in every human rights treaty, applies immediately without any resource qualifier, and cannot be postponed under any progressive realisation argument. It is also the concept students most often handle loosely, because the everyday meaning of 'discrimination' is narrower than the legal one.</p><h4>2. Meaning</h4><div class=\"study-defn\"><strong>Discrimination:</strong> Any distinction, exclusion, restriction or preference based on a prohibited ground which has the purpose or effect of nullifying or impairing the recognition, enjoyment or exercise of rights on an equal footing.</div><p>Two features of that definition do heavy work. 'Purpose or effect' means intention is not required. 'Nullifying or impairing' means the harm need not be total.</p><div class=\"table-wrap\"><table class=\"study-table\"><thead><tr><th>Instrument</th><th>Provision</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>UDHR</td><td>Article 2 and Article 7</td></tr><tr><td>ICCPR</td><td>Article 2(1) in relation to Covenant rights; Article 26 free-standing</td></tr><tr><td>ICESCR</td><td>Article 2(2) and Article 3</td></tr><tr><td>ICERD</td><td>The whole treaty, on racial discrimination</td></tr><tr><td>CEDAW</td><td>The whole treaty, on discrimination against women</td></tr><tr><td>CRPD</td><td>The whole treaty, including denial of reasonable accommodation as discrimination</td></tr><tr><td>African Charter</td><td>Articles 2, 3, 18(3), 19 and 28</td></tr></tbody></table></div><h4>3. Formal and substantive equality</h4><div class=\"table-wrap\"><table class=\"study-table\"><thead><tr><th></th><th>Formal equality</th><th>Substantive equality</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Requires</td><td>Treating likes alike; identical rules for all</td><td>Equal enjoyment in fact, which may require different treatment</td></tr><tr><td>Asks</td><td>Is the rule neutral on its face?</td><td>What is the outcome for the disadvantaged group?</td></tr><tr><td>Blind spot</td><td>A neutral rule can entrench existing disadvantage</td><td>Requires judgement about which differences matter</td></tr><tr><td>Example</td><td>A single physical fitness standard applied to all applicants</td><td>Adjusting the standard where it is not job-related and excludes women disproportionately</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"study-callout\"><strong>Example:</strong> A city sets a minimum height requirement of 1.75 metres for police officers. It is formally equal: it applies to everyone. In practice it excludes the overwhelming majority of women and members of some ethnic groups. Unless the employer can show height is genuinely necessary for the job, this is indirect discrimination, and the answer 'but the rule is the same for everyone' is precisely the answer that substantive equality rejects.</div><h4>4. Direct and indirect discrimination</h4><div class=\"table-wrap\"><table class=\"study-table\"><thead><tr><th></th><th>Direct</th><th>Indirect</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Form</td><td>Explicitly or by design treats a protected group less favourably</td><td>Neutral rule with disproportionate adverse impact on a protected group</td></tr><tr><td>Intention</td><td>Usually apparent</td><td>Irrelevant</td></tr><tr><td>Proof</td><td>The distinction is on the face of the rule or decision</td><td>Requires evidence of impact, often statistical</td></tr><tr><td>Defence</td><td>Very narrow; some grounds admit almost none</td><td>The rule must pursue a legitimate aim by proportionate means</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"study-callout\"><strong>Note:</strong> A third form is increasingly recognised: denial of reasonable accommodation. CRPD Article 2 states expressly that discrimination on the basis of disability includes denial of reasonable accommodation. A rule can be neutral, have no disparate impact, and still discriminate if the state or employer refuses an adjustment that would remove a barrier at proportionate cost.</div><h4>5. When is differential treatment lawful?</h4><p>Not every distinction is discrimination. The Human Rights Committee's General Comment 18 states that differentiation is permissible if the criteria are reasonable and objective and the aim is legitimate under the Covenant. In practice a four-stage test is applied.</p><ol><li><strong>Is there a difference in treatment</strong> between people in analogous situations, or a failure to treat differently people in relevantly different situations?</li><li><strong>Is it based on a prohibited ground</strong>, listed or analogous? The lists are open-ended: 'or other status' has been read to include sexual orientation, nationality, disability, age and health status.</li><li><strong>Does it pursue a legitimate aim?</strong></li><li><strong>Is it a proportionate means of achieving that aim?</strong> This is where most cases are decided.</li></ol><p>The intensity of the proportionality review varies with the ground. Distinctions on race, sex, religion and birth are treated as suspect and require very weighty reasons. Distinctions on, say, length of residence for a benefit may need only a rational justification.</p><h4>6. Special measures</h4><div class=\"study-defn\"><strong>Special measures:</strong> Temporary preferential measures taken to accelerate de facto equality for a disadvantaged group, which do not constitute discrimination provided they cease once their objective is achieved.</div><ul><li>ICERD Article 1(4) and Article 2(2) provide for them expressly in the racial context.</li><li>CEDAW Article 4(1) provides for temporary special measures to accelerate de facto equality between men and women.</li><li>General Recommendation 25 of the CEDAW Committee explains that these are not an exception to non-discrimination but a means of achieving it.</li><li>They must be temporary, targeted, and discontinued when the objective is met; a permanent preference is not a special measure.</li></ul><div class=\"study-callout\"><strong>Note:</strong> Distinguish temporary special measures from permanent differential provision. Maternity protection is not a temporary special measure: it is permanent and responds to a biological difference. Reserved parliamentary seats for women are a temporary special measure. Examiners test this distinction.</div><h4>7. Intersectional discrimination</h4><div class=\"study-defn\"><strong>Intersectional discrimination:</strong> Discrimination arising from the interaction of two or more grounds, producing a distinct harm not captured by analysing each ground separately.</div><div class=\"study-callout\"><strong>Example:</strong> A rural woman with a disability may be excluded from a health programme in a way that is not explained by disability alone, since urban disabled men reach the service, nor by sex alone, since rural non-disabled women reach it, nor by location alone. The exclusion is produced by the combination. Analysing one ground at a time makes the harm invisible, which is why treaty bodies now require an intersectional analysis.</div><h4>8. How the examiner&#x27;s question should be answered</h4><p>Almost every problem question in this field is answered with the same sequence. Identify the treatment. Identify the ground and say whether it is listed or analogous. Say whether the discrimination is direct, indirect or a denial of reasonable accommodation, and give your evidence. Apply the four-stage test, spending most of your words on proportionality. Consider whether a special measure is in play. Finally ask whether more than one ground interacts.</p><div class=\"study-callout\"><strong>Note:</strong> The commonest failing is stopping at 'this is discrimination' without doing the proportionality analysis. The proportionality stage is where the marks are, because that is where a real tribunal would decide the case.</div><h4>Summary</h4><ul><li>Discrimination covers distinctions with discriminatory purpose or effect; intention is not required.</li><li>Formal equality treats everyone identically; substantive equality looks at actual enjoyment and may require different treatment.</li><li>Discrimination may be direct, indirect, or a denial of reasonable accommodation.</li><li>Differential treatment is lawful if based on reasonable and objective criteria pursuing a legitimate aim by proportionate means.</li><li>Special measures are temporary, targeted and permissible; they must end when their objective is achieved.</li><li>Intersectional discrimination requires analysing grounds in combination, not one at a time.</li></ul><h4>Key Terms</h4><div class=\"table-wrap\"><table class=\"study-table\"><thead><tr><th>Term</th><th>Meaning</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Formal equality</td><td>Identical treatment under identical rules.</td></tr><tr><td>Substantive equality</td><td>Equality in actual enjoyment, which may require differential treatment.</td></tr><tr><td>Indirect discrimination</td><td>A neutral rule with disproportionate adverse impact on a protected group.</td></tr><tr><td>Reasonable accommodation</td><td>A necessary and appropriate modification not imposing disproportionate burden, whose denial is discrimination.</td></tr><tr><td>Temporary special measures</td><td>Time-limited preferential measures to accelerate de facto equality.</td></tr></tbody></table></div><h4>Review Questions</h4><h5>Multiple choice</h5><div class=\"study-mcq\"><p class=\"mcq-q\">A neutral rule disproportionately excludes members of one ethnic group and the employer had no such intention. What is this?</p><ol type=\"A\"><li>Indirect discrimination, since intention is irrelevant</li><li>Not discrimination, because there was no intent</li><li>Direct discrimination</li><li>A lawful special measure</li></ol><details class=\"mcq-a\"><summary>Show answer</summary><p><strong>Answer: A.</strong> The definition covers measures with discriminatory purpose or effect; effect alone is sufficient.</p></details></div><div class=\"study-mcq\"><p class=\"mcq-q\">Which is a temporary special measure rather than a permanent differential provision?</p><ol type=\"A\"><li>Reserved parliamentary seats for women until parity is approached</li><li>Maternity leave</li><li>Separate sanitation facilities</li><li>A lower retirement age based on life expectancy</li></ol><details class=\"mcq-a\"><summary>Show answer</summary><p><strong>Answer: A.</strong> Temporary special measures accelerate de facto equality and must end once the objective is achieved; maternity protection is permanent and responds to a biological difference.</p></details></div><div class=\"study-mcq\"><p class=\"mcq-q\">At which stage are most discrimination cases actually decided?</p><ol type=\"A\"><li>Proportionality of the means to the legitimate aim</li><li>Whether a difference in treatment exists</li><li>Whether the ground is listed</li><li>Whether the complainant has standing</li></ol><details class=\"mcq-a\"><summary>Show answer</summary><p><strong>Answer: A.</strong> The first stages are usually straightforward; the contested question is nearly always whether the measure is proportionate.</p></details></div><div class=\"study-mcq\"><p class=\"mcq-q\">Under the CRPD, what additional conduct counts as discrimination?</p><ol type=\"A\"><li>Denial of reasonable accommodation</li><li>Any differential treatment whatsoever</li><li>Only direct discrimination</li><li>Only discrimination by public authorities</li></ol><details class=\"mcq-a\"><summary>Show answer</summary><p><strong>Answer: A.</strong> CRPD Article 2 states expressly that discrimination on the basis of disability includes denial of reasonable accommodation.</p></details></div><h5>Short answer</h5><div class=\"study-sa\"><p class=\"sa-q\">Distinguish direct from indirect discrimination with an example of each.</p><details class=\"sa-a\"><summary>Model answer</summary><p>Direct discrimination treats a protected group less favourably on the face of the rule or decision, for example a job advertisement stating that only men may apply. Indirect discrimination arises where a neutral rule produces disproportionate adverse impact on a protected group, for example a minimum height requirement that excludes most women, and it is unlawful unless the rule pursues a legitimate aim by proportionate means. Intention is required for neither, and indirect discrimination is usually proved by evidence of impact rather than by the wording of the rule.</p></details></div><div class=\"study-sa\"><p class=\"sa-q\">Set out the four-stage test for whether differential treatment is discriminatory.</p><details class=\"sa-a\"><summary>Model answer</summary><p>First, establish a difference in treatment between people in analogous situations, or a failure to treat differently people in relevantly different situations. Second, identify a prohibited ground, whether listed or analogous under an open-ended clause such as &#x27;other status&#x27;. Third, ask whether the measure pursues a legitimate aim. Fourth, ask whether it is a proportionate means of achieving that aim, with the intensity of review depending on how suspect the ground is.</p></details></div><h4>Class Activity</h4><ol><li>Find one rule in your school, workplace or local law that is formally neutral. Identify who it disadvantages in practice and apply the four-stage test to it.</li><li>In groups, draft a temporary special measure to address one real inequality in your community. Specify its objective, its duration, and the indicator that will tell you when to discontinue it.</li></ol><h4>Assignment</h4><p>Write one and a half pages on equality and non-discrimination. Define discrimination, distinguish formal from substantive equality and direct from indirect discrimination, set out the four-stage test for lawful differentiation, explain special measures and how they differ from permanent differential provision, and illustrate intersectional discrimination with a worked example.</p><p class=\"study-print\"><strong>Printable:</strong> use your browser\u2019s Print \u2192 Save as PDF to keep an offline copy.</p></div>",
  "The Right to Life": "<div class=\"study-note\"><div class=\"revision-banner\"><strong>Human Rights and International Humanitarian Law</strong><span>Module 3: The International Bill of Human Rights</span></div><h3 class=\"lesson-note-title\">LESSON NOTE: THE RIGHT TO LIFE</h3><div class=\"table-wrap\"><table class=\"study-table\"><thead><tr><th>Subject</th><th>Topic</th><th>Level</th><th>Lesson duration</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Human Rights and International Humanitarian Law</td><td>The Right to Life</td><td>Beginner to Advanced</td><td>45 minutes</td></tr></tbody></table></div><h4>Learning objectives</h4><p>By the end of this lesson you should be able to:</p><ul><li>Explain the scope of the right to life and the meaning of &#x27;arbitrary&#x27;</li><li>State the rules on use of force by state agents</li><li>Explain the procedural duty to investigate</li><li>Set out the international law position on the death penalty</li></ul><h4>1. Introduction</h4><p>The right to life is called the supreme right because without it no other right can be enjoyed. It is non-derogable under ICCPR Article 4(2), even in war. But it is not a right never to be killed: it is a right not to be arbitrarily deprived of life, and almost all the law lies in the word 'arbitrary'.</p><h4>2. Meaning and sources</h4><blockquote class=\"study-quote\">Every human being has the inherent right to life. This right shall be protected by law. No one shall be arbitrarily deprived of his life.</blockquote><p>That is ICCPR Article 6(1). The same right appears in UDHR Article 3, African Charter Article 4, ECHR Article 2 and ACHR Article 4.</p><div class=\"study-defn\"><strong>Arbitrary deprivation of life:</strong> A killing that is unlawful, or that although lawful in domestic terms is unjust, unreasonable, unnecessary or disproportionate, or lacks due process.</div><div class=\"study-callout\"><strong>Note:</strong> General Comment 36 of the Human Rights Committee, adopted in 2018, is the modern statement of this right and should be cited in any serious answer. It reads Article 6 broadly, extending it to conditions such as environmental degradation and lack of access to essential health services where these amount to a reasonably foreseeable threat to life.</div><h4>3. The three layers of obligation</h4><div class=\"table-wrap\"><table class=\"study-table\"><thead><tr><th>Layer</th><th>Content</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Respect</td><td>State agents must not kill unlawfully. This governs policing, military operations outside armed conflict, deaths in custody and executions.</td></tr><tr><td>Protect</td><td>The state must prevent killings by private persons: an effective criminal law, effective policing, protection for individuals at known real and immediate risk, and regulation of firearms and dangerous activities.</td></tr><tr><td>Fulfil</td><td>Positive measures addressing foreseeable threats to life: road safety, industrial safety, epidemic response, access to essential health care, and mitigation of environmental hazards.</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"study-callout\"><strong>Example:</strong> The duty to protect can be individual. Where authorities know or ought to know of a real and immediate risk to the life of an identified person from the criminal acts of a third party, they must take reasonable measures within their powers. A police force repeatedly told of escalating threats against a named woman by a former partner, taking no action, breaches the duty to protect even though the killer was a private individual.</div><h4>4. Use of force by state agents</h4><p>Lethal force by law enforcement is governed by necessity and proportionality, elaborated in the UN Basic Principles on the Use of Force and Firearms and in General Comment 36.</p><ol><li><strong>Legality:</strong> there must be a domestic legal framework meeting international standards, and force must comply with it.</li><li><strong>Necessity:</strong> force may be used only when strictly necessary and only to the minimum extent required; non-violent means must be tried first where feasible.</li><li><strong>Proportionality:</strong> the harm risked must be proportionate to the threat. Intentional lethal force is permissible only where strictly unavoidable to protect life.</li><li><strong>Precaution:</strong> operations must be planned and controlled so as to minimise the risk to life, including in the choice of equipment and tactics.</li><li><strong>Accountability:</strong> every use of lethal force must be reported and independently reviewed.</li></ol><div class=\"study-callout\"><strong>Note:</strong> Protecting property alone never justifies lethal force. Nor does preventing the escape of a suspect who poses no threat to life. These two propositions dispose of a large proportion of problem questions.</div><h4>5. The duty to investigate</h4><p>Where a death occurs at the hands of state agents, or in state custody, or in circumstances suggesting a violation, the state must investigate. The investigation must satisfy five criteria.</p><div class=\"table-wrap\"><table class=\"study-table\"><thead><tr><th>Criterion</th><th>Requirement</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Independence</td><td>Investigators must be institutionally and practically independent of those implicated</td></tr><tr><td>Effectiveness</td><td>Capable of establishing the facts and identifying and punishing those responsible; it is an obligation of means, not result</td></tr><tr><td>Promptness</td><td>Begun without delay and pursued with reasonable expedition</td></tr><tr><td>Transparency</td><td>Subject to public scrutiny sufficient to secure accountability</td></tr><tr><td>Victim participation</td><td>Next of kin must be involved to the extent necessary to safeguard their legitimate interests</td></tr></tbody></table></div><p>The procedural obligation is autonomous: a state can breach it even where the killing itself turns out to have been lawful, because the failure lies in the investigation. This point is worth a paragraph in any answer, since students often assume that a lawful killing ends the analysis.</p><h4>6. The death penalty</h4><p>International law does not yet prohibit the death penalty outright, but it constrains it heavily and the direction of travel is abolition.</p><ol><li>ICCPR Article 6(2) permits it in states that have not abolished it, but only for the most serious crimes, which General Comment 36 confines to crimes involving intentional killing.</li><li>It may not be imposed for drug offences, economic crimes, adultery, consensual sexual relations, apostasy or political offences.</li><li>It requires a final judgment of a competent court after a trial meeting every Article 14 fair trial guarantee; any fair trial breach makes the resulting execution arbitrary.</li><li>It may not be imposed on persons under eighteen at the time of the offence, or carried out on pregnant women; General Comment 36 extends the prohibition to persons with serious psychosocial or intellectual disabilities.</li><li>Anyone sentenced to death has the right to seek pardon or commutation.</li><li>Article 6(6) states that nothing in the article may be invoked to delay or prevent abolition. The Second Optional Protocol commits its parties to abolition.</li><li>Reintroduction by an abolitionist state is impermissible, since Article 6(2) speaks only of states that have not abolished it.</li></ol><div class=\"study-callout\"><strong>Note:</strong> Add the method and the wait. Execution methods causing unnecessary suffering breach Article 7, and prolonged detention under sentence of death in poor conditions has been found to constitute inhuman treatment in several jurisdictions, though the Human Rights Committee has been more cautious about the death row phenomenon than some regional courts.</div><h4>7. How the examiner&#x27;s question should be answered</h4><p>For a problem question about a killing, work through this sequence and you will not miss a limb.</p><ol><li>Was the death caused by a state agent, by a private person, or by conditions?</li><li>If by a state agent, apply legality, necessity, proportionality, precaution and accountability in that order.</li><li>If by a private person, ask whether the state knew or ought to have known of a real and immediate risk and failed to take reasonable measures.</li><li>In every case, ask separately whether the procedural duty to investigate was met against the five criteria.</li><li>If a death sentence is involved, run the seven death penalty constraints.</li><li>State whether the situation is one of armed conflict, in which case IHL applies as lex specialis in determining what is arbitrary, a point taken up in the IHL modules.</li></ol><h4>Summary</h4><ul><li>Article 6 prohibits arbitrary deprivation of life and is non-derogable even in emergency.</li><li>The right imposes duties to respect, protect and fulfil, extending to foreseeable threats from private actors and from conditions.</li><li>Lethal force must satisfy legality, necessity, proportionality, precaution and accountability; property alone never justifies it.</li><li>The procedural duty to investigate is autonomous and must be independent, effective, prompt, transparent and open to the next of kin.</li><li>The death penalty is heavily constrained: most serious crimes only, full fair trial, no juveniles, no reintroduction, and no invoking Article 6 to delay abolition.</li><li>General Comment 36 is the authoritative modern statement of the right.</li></ul><h4>Key Terms</h4><div class=\"table-wrap\"><table class=\"study-table\"><thead><tr><th>Term</th><th>Meaning</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Arbitrary deprivation of life</td><td>A killing that is unlawful, or unjust, unnecessary, disproportionate or lacking due process.</td></tr><tr><td>Procedural obligation</td><td>The autonomous duty to investigate deaths effectively, independently, promptly and transparently.</td></tr><tr><td>Most serious crimes</td><td>The category limiting the death penalty, confined by General Comment 36 to crimes involving intentional killing.</td></tr><tr><td>Real and immediate risk</td><td>The threshold at which the duty to protect an identified individual from private violence is engaged.</td></tr></tbody></table></div><h4>Review Questions</h4><h5>Multiple choice</h5><div class=\"study-mcq\"><p class=\"mcq-q\">Which of these can justify intentional lethal force by police?</p><ol type=\"A\"><li>An imminent threat to life that cannot be averted by any lesser means</li><li>Protection of valuable property</li><li>Preventing the escape of a non-violent suspect</li><li>Dispersing an unlawful but peaceful assembly</li></ol><details class=\"mcq-a\"><summary>Show answer</summary><p><strong>Answer: A.</strong> Intentional lethal force is permissible only where strictly unavoidable to protect life; property and non-violent escape never suffice.</p></details></div><div class=\"study-mcq\"><p class=\"mcq-q\">A killing by state agents turns out to have been lawful, but no independent investigation was held. What follows?</p><ol type=\"A\"><li>A separate breach of the procedural obligation to investigate</li><li>No breach, since the killing was lawful</li><li>A breach only if the family complains</li><li>A breach of the prohibition of torture</li></ol><details class=\"mcq-a\"><summary>Show answer</summary><p><strong>Answer: A.</strong> The procedural obligation is autonomous and can be violated even where the substantive killing was justified.</p></details></div><div class=\"study-mcq\"><p class=\"mcq-q\">Under General Comment 36, which crimes may attract the death penalty?</p><ol type=\"A\"><li>Only crimes involving intentional killing</li><li>Any crime the state classifies as serious</li><li>Drug trafficking and economic crimes</li><li>Any crime carrying a life sentence</li></ol><details class=\"mcq-a\"><summary>Show answer</summary><p><strong>Answer: A.</strong> &#x27;Most serious crimes&#x27; is read restrictively, excluding drug offences, economic crimes, adultery, consensual sexual relations and apostasy.</p></details></div><div class=\"study-mcq\"><p class=\"mcq-q\">May an abolitionist state reintroduce the death penalty consistently with the ICCPR?</p><ol type=\"A\"><li>No, because Article 6(2) applies only to states that have not abolished it</li><li>Yes, at any time</li><li>Yes, if approved by referendum</li><li>Yes, for terrorism offences</li></ol><details class=\"mcq-a\"><summary>Show answer</summary><p><strong>Answer: A.</strong> Article 6(6) also provides that nothing in the article may be invoked to delay or prevent abolition.</p></details></div><h5>Short answer</h5><div class=\"study-sa\"><p class=\"sa-q\">List the five criteria an investigation into a death must satisfy.</p><details class=\"sa-a\"><summary>Model answer</summary><p>It must be independent, meaning investigators are institutionally and practically separate from those implicated; effective, meaning capable of establishing the facts and identifying and punishing those responsible, though this is an obligation of means and not of result; prompt, meaning begun without delay and pursued expeditiously; transparent, meaning open to sufficient public scrutiny to secure accountability; and open to the participation of the next of kin to the extent necessary to safeguard their legitimate interests.</p></details></div><div class=\"study-sa\"><p class=\"sa-q\">Explain the duty to protect life from private actors.</p><details class=\"sa-a\"><summary>Model answer</summary><p>The state must maintain an effective criminal law backed by law enforcement machinery to deter and punish killings by private persons. Beyond that general duty, an individual duty arises where authorities know or ought to know of a real and immediate risk to the life of an identified person from the criminal acts of a third party; they must then take reasonable measures within their powers to avert it. Failure to act on repeated credible reports of escalating threats against a named individual therefore breaches Article 6 even though the state did not cause the death.</p></details></div><h4>Class Activity</h4><ol><li>Take one reported death in police custody from any country. Assess the official investigation against the five criteria and write a one-page verdict.</li><li>Research your country&#x27;s law on police use of firearms. Compare it with the necessity and proportionality standards in this lesson and identify the largest gap.</li></ol><h4>Assignment</h4><p>Write two pages on the right to life. Explain the meaning of arbitrary deprivation, set out the obligations to respect, protect and fulfil with examples, state the rules governing use of lethal force by state agents, explain the procedural duty to investigate and why it is autonomous, and summarise the international law constraints on the death penalty.</p><p class=\"study-print\"><strong>Printable:</strong> use your browser\u2019s Print \u2192 Save as PDF to keep an offline copy.</p></div>",
  "Freedom from Torture and Ill-Treatment": "<div class=\"study-note\"><div class=\"revision-banner\"><strong>Human Rights and International Humanitarian Law</strong><span>Module 3: The International Bill of Human Rights</span></div><h3 class=\"lesson-note-title\">LESSON NOTE: FREEDOM FROM TORTURE AND ILL-TREATMENT</h3><div class=\"table-wrap\"><table class=\"study-table\"><thead><tr><th>Subject</th><th>Topic</th><th>Level</th><th>Lesson duration</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Human Rights and International Humanitarian Law</td><td>Freedom from Torture and Ill-Treatment</td><td>Beginner to Advanced</td><td>45 minutes</td></tr></tbody></table></div><h4>Learning objectives</h4><p>By the end of this lesson you should be able to:</p><ul><li>Define torture and distinguish it from cruel, inhuman and degrading treatment</li><li>Explain the absolute and non-derogable nature of the prohibition</li><li>State the obligations of prevention, investigation and non-refoulement</li><li>Apply the exclusionary rule and answer the ticking bomb argument</li></ul><h4>1. Introduction</h4><p>The prohibition of torture is the clearest example in international law of an absolute rule. It admits no exception, no derogation, no balancing and no emergency. Students who understand why it is absolute, and can defend that against the standard counter-argument, understand something important about how human rights law works.</p><h4>2. Meaning: the CAT Article 1 definition</h4><div class=\"study-defn\"><strong>Torture:</strong> Any act by which severe pain or suffering, whether physical or mental, is intentionally inflicted on a person for such purposes as obtaining information or a confession, punishing, intimidating or coercing, or for any reason based on discrimination of any kind, when such pain or suffering is inflicted by or at the instigation of or with the consent or acquiescence of a public official or other person acting in an official capacity. It does not include pain or suffering arising only from, inherent in or incidental to lawful sanctions.</div><p>The definition has four elements. Take them one at a time in any answer.</p><div class=\"table-wrap\"><table class=\"study-table\"><thead><tr><th>Element</th><th>Requirement</th><th>Note</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Severity</td><td>Severe pain or suffering, physical or mental</td><td>No fixed threshold; assessed on all the circumstances including duration, effects, sex, age and health of the victim</td></tr><tr><td>Intention</td><td>The act must be intentional</td><td>Negligence causing severe suffering is not torture, though it may violate Article 7 ICCPR</td></tr><tr><td>Purpose</td><td>Information, confession, punishment, intimidation, coercion, or discrimination</td><td>The list is illustrative: 'such purposes as'</td></tr><tr><td>Official involvement</td><td>By, at the instigation of, or with the consent or acquiescence of a public official</td><td>Acquiescence covers a state that knowingly tolerates private torture</td></tr></tbody></table></div><h4>3. The gradation: torture, cruel, inhuman, degrading</h4><div class=\"table-wrap\"><table class=\"study-table\"><thead><tr><th>Category</th><th>Distinguishing feature</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Torture</td><td>All four elements present: severe suffering, intentional, purposive, official involvement</td></tr><tr><td>Cruel or inhuman treatment</td><td>Severe suffering but one element absent, typically the specific purpose</td></tr><tr><td>Degrading treatment</td><td>Humiliation or debasement grossly exceeding what is inherent in lawful treatment, arousing feelings of fear, anguish or inferiority</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"study-callout\"><strong>Note:</strong> The legal consequence of the gradation is smaller than students expect. All three are absolutely prohibited under ICCPR Article 7, all three are non-derogable, and CAT Article 16 requires prevention of the lesser forms too. The gradation matters mainly for the stigma of the label, for the specific CAT obligations of criminalisation and universal jurisdiction which attach to torture proper, and for reparation. It does not create a category of permitted ill-treatment.</div><h4>4. Absoluteness</h4><p>Four separate features make this prohibition exceptionally strong.</p><ol><li><strong>Non-derogable:</strong> ICCPR Article 4(2) excludes Article 7 from derogation. CAT Article 2(2) states that no exceptional circumstances whatsoever, including a state of war, internal political instability or any other public emergency, may be invoked as a justification.</li><li><strong>No superior orders defence:</strong> CAT Article 2(3) states that an order from a superior officer or a public authority may not be invoked as a justification.</li><li><strong>Jus cogens:</strong> the prohibition is a peremptory norm of general international law from which no derogation by treaty is permitted, affirmed by the ICTY in Furundzija.</li><li><strong>Erga omnes:</strong> the obligation is owed to the international community as a whole, so any state may invoke responsibility for its breach.</li></ol><h4>5. The obligations in the Convention Against Torture</h4><div class=\"table-wrap\"><table class=\"study-table\"><thead><tr><th>Article</th><th>Obligation</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>2</td><td>Take effective legislative, administrative, judicial and other measures to prevent torture; no exceptional circumstances; no superior orders</td></tr><tr><td>3</td><td>Non-refoulement: no expulsion, return or extradition where there are substantial grounds for believing the person would be in danger of torture</td></tr><tr><td>4</td><td>Make all acts of torture offences under criminal law, punishable by appropriate penalties</td></tr><tr><td>5-7</td><td>Establish jurisdiction, including universal jurisdiction; extradite or prosecute</td></tr><tr><td>10</td><td>Train law enforcement, medical, military and public officials</td></tr><tr><td>11</td><td>Systematically review interrogation rules, instructions, methods and detention arrangements</td></tr><tr><td>12-13</td><td>Prompt and impartial investigation ex officio, and a right to complain without reprisal</td></tr><tr><td>14</td><td>Redress and an enforceable right to fair and adequate compensation, including rehabilitation</td></tr><tr><td>15</td><td>Exclusionary rule: no statement established to have been made as a result of torture may be invoked as evidence, except against a person accused of torture as evidence that the statement was made</td></tr></tbody></table></div><p>The Optional Protocol to CAT adds a preventive system: the Subcommittee on Prevention of Torture, and an obligation on each state party to establish a National Preventive Mechanism with the power to visit all places of detention unannounced.</p><h4>6. Non-refoulement</h4><div class=\"study-defn\"><strong>Non-refoulement:</strong> The prohibition on returning a person to a place where they face a real risk of torture or other irreparable harm.</div><ul><li>Under CAT Article 3 the prohibition is absolute: unlike refugee law's Article 33(2), there is no exception for national security or serious criminality.</li><li>The assessment is prospective and individual: is there a real risk to this person on return now?</li><li>Diplomatic assurances from the receiving state are viewed with deep scepticism. The Committee's position is that they cannot substitute for an assessment of risk, particularly where torture is systematic, and that the very need for an assurance is itself evidence of risk.</li><li>The prohibition applies wherever the state exercises effective control, including at borders and in interception operations.</li></ul><h4>7. The exclusionary rule and the ticking bomb</h4><p>CAT Article 15 excludes torture-tainted statements from any proceedings. The rule is not merely evidential hygiene: if such evidence were admissible, the incentive to torture would survive every other prohibition.</p><p>The standard challenge to absoluteness is the ticking bomb hypothetical: a bomb is hidden in a city, the captured suspect knows where, and only torture will extract it in time. The answer has several limbs and a good answer gives all of them.</p><ol><li><strong>The premises are almost never true.</strong> The scenario assumes certainty that the person knows, certainty that torture will produce truth quickly, and certainty that there is no other means. Real interrogations have none of these.</li><li><strong>Torture is unreliable.</strong> People under severe pain say what they believe will stop it. Extensive review of interrogation practice has found coercive methods produce false information, and false leads consume the very time the scenario says is scarce.</li><li><strong>The exception cannot be confined.</strong> Once torture is lawful in the extreme case, institutions must train torturers, develop methods, and decide which cases qualify. The category expands, as every state that has tried it has discovered.</li><li><strong>It is a philosophical device, not a policy.</strong> A hypothetical constructed to defeat a rule proves only that a rule can be embarrassed by a sufficiently artificial fact pattern.</li><li><strong>The law has already answered.</strong> CAT Article 2(2) says no exceptional circumstances whatsoever. That wording was chosen precisely to foreclose this argument.</li></ol><h4>8. How the examiner&#x27;s question should be answered</h4><p>Take the four elements of the CAT definition in order and apply each to the facts before naming the conduct. Then say why the label matters and, more importantly, why it matters less than students think, since all forms are absolutely prohibited. Then set out the relevant obligations: prevention, investigation, criminalisation, non-refoulement, exclusion of evidence, redress. If the question raises an emergency, state the absoluteness rules with their sources. If it raises the ticking bomb, give all five limbs above rather than simply asserting that torture is wrong.</p><h4>Summary</h4><ul><li>Torture under CAT Article 1 requires severe suffering, intention, a prohibited purpose and official involvement or acquiescence.</li><li>Cruel, inhuman and degrading treatment lack one element but are equally absolutely prohibited.</li><li>The prohibition is non-derogable, admits no superior orders defence, and is a jus cogens norm owed erga omnes.</li><li>CAT imposes duties to prevent, criminalise, investigate, exercise universal jurisdiction, provide redress and exclude tainted evidence.</li><li>Non-refoulement under CAT Article 3 is absolute and diplomatic assurances are treated with deep scepticism.</li><li>The ticking bomb argument fails on its premises, on reliability, on institutional containment and on the express wording of CAT Article 2(2).</li></ul><h4>Key Terms</h4><div class=\"table-wrap\"><table class=\"study-table\"><thead><tr><th>Term</th><th>Meaning</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Torture</td><td>Intentional infliction of severe pain or suffering for a prohibited purpose with official involvement.</td></tr><tr><td>Non-refoulement</td><td>The prohibition on return to a real risk of torture, absolute under CAT Article 3.</td></tr><tr><td>Exclusionary rule</td><td>CAT Article 15&#x27;s bar on invoking torture-derived statements as evidence.</td></tr><tr><td>National Preventive Mechanism</td><td>The domestic body required by OPCAT to visit all places of detention.</td></tr><tr><td>Jus cogens</td><td>A peremptory norm of general international law from which no derogation is permitted.</td></tr></tbody></table></div><h4>Review Questions</h4><h5>Multiple choice</h5><div class=\"study-mcq\"><p class=\"mcq-q\">Which element distinguishes torture from cruel or inhuman treatment?</p><ol type=\"A\"><li>The presence of a specific prohibited purpose such as obtaining a confession</li><li>The severity of the suffering alone</li><li>Whether the victim was detained</li><li>Whether the state has ratified CAT</li></ol><details class=\"mcq-a\"><summary>Show answer</summary><p><strong>Answer: A.</strong> Both involve severe suffering; torture additionally requires intention and one of the listed purposes, with official involvement.</p></details></div><div class=\"study-mcq\"><p class=\"mcq-q\">A state faces a declared national emergency. What may it do about the prohibition of torture?</p><ol type=\"A\"><li>Nothing; CAT Article 2(2) excludes any exceptional circumstances whatsoever</li><li>Derogate for the duration of the emergency</li><li>Apply a proportionality test</li><li>Permit ill-treatment short of torture</li></ol><details class=\"mcq-a\"><summary>Show answer</summary><p><strong>Answer: A.</strong> The prohibition is non-derogable under ICCPR Article 4(2) and absolute under CAT, and all forms of ill-treatment are covered.</p></details></div><div class=\"study-mcq\"><p class=\"mcq-q\">What is the effect of CAT Article 15?</p><ol type=\"A\"><li>A statement made as a result of torture may not be invoked as evidence, except against the alleged torturer to prove it was made</li><li>Torture evidence is admissible if corroborated</li><li>Torture evidence may be used in emergencies</li><li>The rule applies only to criminal trials in the state where the torture occurred</li></ol><details class=\"mcq-a\"><summary>Show answer</summary><p><strong>Answer: A.</strong> Admitting such evidence would preserve the incentive to torture, which is why the rule is framed so broadly.</p></details></div><div class=\"study-mcq\"><p class=\"mcq-q\">Why does the Committee against Torture distrust diplomatic assurances?</p><ol type=\"A\"><li>They cannot substitute for an individual risk assessment and the need for one is itself evidence of risk</li><li>They are not written in treaty form</li><li>They bind only the sending state</li><li>They expire after twelve months</li></ol><details class=\"mcq-a\"><summary>Show answer</summary><p><strong>Answer: A.</strong> Where torture is systematic, an assurance from the same authorities is worth little and there is rarely any effective monitoring.</p></details></div><h5>Short answer</h5><div class=\"study-sa\"><p class=\"sa-q\">Set out the four elements of the CAT Article 1 definition of torture.</p><details class=\"sa-a\"><summary>Model answer</summary><p>First, severe pain or suffering, whether physical or mental, assessed on all the circumstances including duration, method, and the victim&#x27;s sex, age and health. Second, intentional infliction, so that suffering caused negligently is not torture though it may still breach ICCPR Article 7. Third, a prohibited purpose, the listed examples being obtaining information or a confession, punishment, intimidation, coercion, or any reason based on discrimination, the list being illustrative. Fourth, infliction by, at the instigation of, or with the consent or acquiescence of a public official, which covers a state that knowingly tolerates torture by private actors.</p></details></div><div class=\"study-sa\"><p class=\"sa-q\">Give three reasons why the ticking bomb argument does not justify torture.</p><details class=\"sa-a\"><summary>Model answer</summary><p>First, its premises are almost never satisfied in reality: it assumes certainty that the detainee knows, that torture will produce truth in time, and that no alternative exists. Second, torture is unreliable because people in severe pain say whatever they believe will stop it, and false leads consume the scarce time the scenario itself emphasises. Third, an exception cannot be institutionally confined: permitting torture in the extreme case requires trained torturers, developed methods and officials deciding which cases qualify, and the category has expanded wherever this has been attempted. In any event CAT Article 2(2) forecloses the argument by excluding any exceptional circumstances whatsoever.</p></details></div><h4>Class Activity</h4><ol><li>Obtain the standard minimum rules for the treatment of prisoners, known as the Mandela Rules, and identify five provisions designed to prevent ill-treatment. Explain how each works as a preventive measure.</li><li>Research whether your country has ratified OPCAT and established a National Preventive Mechanism. If it has, assess its powers and independence; if not, write a short case for establishing one.</li></ol><h4>Assignment</h4><p>Write two pages on freedom from torture and ill-treatment. Set out the CAT Article 1 definition element by element, distinguish torture from cruel, inhuman and degrading treatment and explain what turns on the distinction, establish the absoluteness of the prohibition with its four supporting features, describe the main obligations under the Convention including non-refoulement and the exclusionary rule, and answer the ticking bomb argument in full.</p><p class=\"study-print\"><strong>Printable:</strong> use your browser\u2019s Print \u2192 Save as PDF to keep an offline copy.</p></div>",
  "Eliminating Racial Discrimination (ICERD)": "<div class=\"study-note\"><div class=\"revision-banner\"><strong>Human Rights and International Humanitarian Law</strong><span>Module 4: Thematic Human Rights Treaties</span></div><h3 class=\"lesson-note-title\">LESSON NOTE: ELIMINATING RACIAL DISCRIMINATION (ICERD)</h3><div class=\"table-wrap\"><table class=\"study-table\"><thead><tr><th>Subject</th><th>Topic</th><th>Level</th><th>Lesson duration</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Human Rights and International Humanitarian Law</td><td>Eliminating Racial Discrimination (ICERD)</td><td>Beginner to Advanced</td><td>40 minutes</td></tr></tbody></table></div><h4>Learning objectives</h4><p>By the end of this lesson you should be able to:</p><ul><li>State the definition of racial discrimination in ICERD Article 1</li><li>Describe the main obligations the Convention imposes</li><li>Explain the work of the CERD Committee and its procedures</li><li>Distinguish special measures from prohibited discrimination</li></ul><h4>1. Introduction</h4><p>ICERD was the first of the modern thematic treaties, adopted in 1965, a year before the Covenants. It was driven by decolonisation, by a wave of antisemitic incidents in Europe at the end of the 1950s, and above all by the campaign against apartheid. It has 182 states parties, one of the highest of any human rights treaty.</p><h4>2. Meaning: the Article 1 definition</h4><div class=\"study-defn\"><strong>Racial discrimination:</strong> Any distinction, exclusion, restriction or preference based on race, colour, descent, or national or ethnic origin which has the purpose or effect of nullifying or impairing the recognition, enjoyment or exercise, on an equal footing, of human rights and fundamental freedoms in the political, economic, social, cultural or any other field of public life.</div><div class=\"table-wrap\"><table class=\"study-table\"><thead><tr><th>Ground</th><th>Covers</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Race</td><td>The socially constructed category; the Committee rejects biological race but applies the ground as used in practice</td></tr><tr><td>Colour</td><td>Skin colour, including colourism within a single ethnic group</td></tr><tr><td>Descent</td><td>Read by the Committee to include caste and analogous systems of inherited status, a reading India has contested</td></tr><tr><td>National origin</td><td>Ethnic or historical national background, distinct from present citizenship</td></tr><tr><td>Ethnic origin</td><td>Shared culture, language, religion or ancestry constituting a distinct group</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"study-callout\"><strong>Note:</strong> Article 1(2) excludes distinctions between citizens and non-citizens. This is narrower than it looks. General Recommendation 30 states that differential treatment based on citizenship constitutes discrimination if the criteria are not applied pursuant to a legitimate aim and are not proportional. So a state may reserve voting to citizens, but it may not deny emergency medical care or protection from violence on that basis.</div><h4>3. The obligations</h4><div class=\"table-wrap\"><table class=\"study-table\"><thead><tr><th>Article</th><th>Obligation</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>2</td><td>Condemn racial discrimination and pursue by all appropriate means a policy of eliminating it; do not sponsor, defend or support it; review and amend laws having that effect</td></tr><tr><td>3</td><td>Condemn apartheid and racial segregation and eradicate them in territories under jurisdiction</td></tr><tr><td>4</td><td>Declare punishable the dissemination of ideas based on racial superiority or hatred, incitement to racial discrimination, and acts of violence; declare illegal organisations promoting it</td></tr><tr><td>5</td><td>Guarantee equality before the law in the enjoyment of a long list of civil, political, economic, social and cultural rights</td></tr><tr><td>6</td><td>Provide effective protection and remedies, including just and adequate reparation</td></tr><tr><td>7</td><td>Adopt immediate and effective measures in teaching, education, culture and information to combat prejudice</td></tr></tbody></table></div><p>Article 5 is worth attention. It does not create the listed rights: it requires that whatever rights the state provides are provided without racial discrimination. That makes ICERD useful in areas the state might argue lie outside its other treaty commitments.</p><h4>4. Article 4 and freedom of expression</h4><p>Article 4 is the most contested provision. It requires states to criminalise racist speech, which sits uneasily with ICCPR Article 19. Several states, including the United States and some European states, entered reservations or interpretive declarations preserving freedom of expression.</p><p>The Committee's position, in General Recommendation 35, is that the two are compatible if criminalisation is confined to the most serious forms and applied with a proportionality analysis. It endorses the Rabat Plan of Action's six-factor test.</p><ol><li>The social and political context.</li><li>The speaker's position or status and their influence over the audience.</li><li>The intent of the speaker; negligence and recklessness are not sufficient for a criminal offence.</li><li>The content and form of the speech.</li><li>The extent of dissemination, including reach and audience size.</li><li>The likelihood of harm, including its imminence.</li></ol><div class=\"study-callout\"><strong>Note:</strong> Applied properly, this test criminalises very little. Most racist speech should be answered by counter-speech, civil remedies and platform measures rather than by prosecution, with criminal law reserved for incitement that is intentional and likely to produce discrimination, hostility or violence.</div><h4>5. The CERD Committee and its procedures</h4><div class=\"table-wrap\"><table class=\"study-table\"><thead><tr><th>Procedure</th><th>Basis</th><th>Note</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>State reporting</td><td>Art 9</td><td>Every two years in principle; concluding observations issued</td></tr><tr><td>Early warning and urgent action</td><td>Committee practice since 1993</td><td>Used where a situation risks escalating into conflict; distinctive to CERD</td></tr><tr><td>Inter-state communications</td><td>Arts 11-13</td><td>Dormant for decades, then used from 2018 in three cases, giving this long-dead procedure real content</td></tr><tr><td>Individual communications</td><td>Art 14</td><td>Optional; requires a declaration accepting the Committee's competence</td></tr><tr><td>General Recommendations</td><td>Practice</td><td>GR 30 on non-citizens, GR 32 on special measures, GR 34 on people of African descent, GR 35 on hate speech</td></tr></tbody></table></div><h4>6. Special measures under ICERD</h4><p>Article 1(4) provides that special measures taken for the sole purpose of securing adequate advancement of certain racial or ethnic groups shall not be deemed racial discrimination, provided they do not lead to the maintenance of separate rights for different racial groups and are not continued after their objectives have been achieved. Article 2(2) goes further and requires such measures where circumstances so warrant.</p><p>General Recommendation 32 sets out the conditions: they must be appropriate to the situation, necessary, legitimate in aim, respect fairness and proportionality, and be temporary. They must be designed and implemented with consultation of the affected communities.</p><div class=\"study-callout\"><strong>Example:</strong> A university reserves a proportion of places for students from a historically excluded ethnic group, sets a target for representation, and reviews the scheme every five years against that target with a plan to discontinue it. This satisfies Article 1(4). A permanent quota with no review and no target does not, because permanence converts a corrective measure into the maintenance of separate rights that Article 1(4) forbids.</div><h4>7. How the examiner&#x27;s question should be answered</h4><p>For an ICERD problem, apply the Article 1 definition first: is the ground within race, colour, descent, national or ethnic origin, and is there purpose or effect? Then locate the obligation breached by article number. If speech is involved, run the Rabat six factors rather than asserting a conclusion. If a preference is involved, run the Article 1(4) and General Recommendation 32 conditions. Mention that the non-citizen exclusion in Article 1(2) is limited by General Recommendation 30, since this is the most frequently missed point.</p><h4>Summary</h4><ul><li>ICERD, adopted 1965, defines racial discrimination by reference to race, colour, descent and national or ethnic origin, covering purpose or effect.</li><li>Descent has been read to include caste, and the citizenship exclusion in Article 1(2) is limited by a legitimate aim and proportionality test.</li><li>Article 4 requires criminalisation of racist incitement, reconciled with free expression through the Rabat six-factor test.</li><li>Article 5 requires equality in the enjoyment of listed rights rather than creating those rights.</li><li>CERD is distinctive for its early warning and urgent action procedure and, since 2018, active inter-state communications.</li><li>Special measures are permitted and sometimes required, but must be necessary, proportionate, consultative and temporary.</li></ul><h4>Key Terms</h4><div class=\"table-wrap\"><table class=\"study-table\"><thead><tr><th>Term</th><th>Meaning</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>ICERD</td><td>The International Convention on the Elimination of All Forms of Racial Discrimination, 1965.</td></tr><tr><td>Descent</td><td>An ICERD ground read to include caste and inherited status systems.</td></tr><tr><td>Rabat Plan of Action</td><td>A six-factor test for when speech should attract criminal sanction.</td></tr><tr><td>Early warning procedure</td><td>CERD&#x27;s mechanism for addressing situations at risk of escalating into conflict.</td></tr></tbody></table></div><h4>Review Questions</h4><h5>Multiple choice</h5><div class=\"study-mcq\"><p class=\"mcq-q\">Which ground under ICERD has been read to include caste?</p><ol type=\"A\"><li>Descent</li><li>Colour</li><li>National origin</li><li>Race</li></ol><details class=\"mcq-a\"><summary>Show answer</summary><p><strong>Answer: A.</strong> The CERD Committee&#x27;s interpretation covers caste and analogous systems of inherited status, a reading contested by India.</p></details></div><div class=\"study-mcq\"><p class=\"mcq-q\">Under General Recommendation 30, when does differential treatment of non-citizens amount to discrimination?</p><ol type=\"A\"><li>When it is not pursuant to a legitimate aim and not proportional to that aim</li><li>Never, because Article 1(2) excludes it entirely</li><li>Always, because citizenship is a prohibited ground</li><li>Only where violence is involved</li></ol><details class=\"mcq-a\"><summary>Show answer</summary><p><strong>Answer: A.</strong> The Article 1(2) exclusion is narrower than its wording suggests; states may restrict voting but not emergency care.</p></details></div><div class=\"study-mcq\"><p class=\"mcq-q\">What does the Rabat Plan of Action provide?</p><ol type=\"A\"><li>A six-factor test for when speech should be criminalised</li><li>A list of prohibited words</li><li>A ban on all racist expression</li><li>A reporting timetable for states</li></ol><details class=\"mcq-a\"><summary>Show answer</summary><p><strong>Answer: A.</strong> The factors are context, speaker, intent, content and form, extent of dissemination, and likelihood including imminence of harm.</p></details></div><div class=\"study-mcq\"><p class=\"mcq-q\">Which condition must a special measure under ICERD satisfy?</p><ol type=\"A\"><li>It must be temporary and discontinued once its objective is achieved</li><li>It must apply to all groups equally</li><li>It must be approved by the CERD Committee</li><li>It must be written into the constitution</li></ol><details class=\"mcq-a\"><summary>Show answer</summary><p><strong>Answer: A.</strong> Article 1(4) forbids measures leading to the maintenance of separate rights, and General Recommendation 32 adds necessity, proportionality and consultation.</p></details></div><h5>Short answer</h5><div class=\"study-sa\"><p class=\"sa-q\">Explain what Article 5 of ICERD does and does not require.</p><details class=\"sa-a\"><summary>Model answer</summary><p>Article 5 requires states to guarantee equality before the law in the enjoyment of a long list of civil, political, economic, social and cultural rights, without distinction as to race, colour, or national or ethnic origin. It does not itself create those rights or oblige a state to provide any particular level of them. Its effect is that whatever the state does provide must be provided without racial discrimination, which makes it useful for challenging unequal provision in fields the state might argue fall outside its other treaty commitments.</p></details></div><div class=\"study-sa\"><p class=\"sa-q\">Set out the conditions a special measure must satisfy under ICERD.</p><details class=\"sa-a\"><summary>Model answer</summary><p>It must be taken for the sole purpose of securing adequate advancement of a group requiring protection to ensure equal enjoyment of rights. It must be appropriate to the situation and necessary. It must pursue a legitimate aim and respect fairness and proportionality. It must not lead to the maintenance of separate rights for different racial groups. It must be temporary, with defined objectives and discontinuation once those are achieved. And under General Recommendation 32 it must be designed and implemented in consultation with the affected communities.</p></details></div><h4>Class Activity</h4><ol><li>Find your country&#x27;s most recent CERD concluding observations. List the three main concerns raised and check whether any earlier recommendation was repeated, which indicates non-implementation.</li><li>Take one real instance of hate speech from the media and apply the six Rabat factors to it. State whether criminal prosecution would be justified and why.</li></ol><h4>Assignment</h4><p>Write one and a half pages on ICERD. Give the Article 1 definition and explain each ground, summarise the main obligations by article, discuss the tension between Article 4 and freedom of expression and how the Rabat factors resolve it, describe the Committee&#x27;s procedures including early warning, and explain the conditions for lawful special measures.</p><p class=\"study-print\"><strong>Printable:</strong> use your browser\u2019s Print \u2192 Save as PDF to keep an offline copy.</p></div>",
  "Women's Rights (CEDAW)": "<div class=\"study-note\"><div class=\"revision-banner\"><strong>Human Rights and International Humanitarian Law</strong><span>Module 4: Thematic Human Rights Treaties</span></div><h3 class=\"lesson-note-title\">LESSON NOTE: WOMEN&#x27;S RIGHTS (CEDAW)</h3><div class=\"table-wrap\"><table class=\"study-table\"><thead><tr><th>Subject</th><th>Topic</th><th>Level</th><th>Lesson duration</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Human Rights and International Humanitarian Law</td><td>Women&#x27;s Rights (CEDAW)</td><td>Beginner to Advanced</td><td>45 minutes</td></tr></tbody></table></div><h4>Learning objectives</h4><p>By the end of this lesson you should be able to:</p><ul><li>State the CEDAW definition of discrimination against women</li><li>Describe the substantive obligations and the concept of substantive equality</li><li>Explain temporary special measures and General Recommendations 19 and 35</li><li>Assess the reservations problem</li></ul><h4>1. Introduction</h4><p>CEDAW is sometimes called the international bill of rights for women. It has 189 states parties, which is close to universal, and it also attracts more reservations than any other human rights treaty. Both facts matter and a good answer discusses both.</p><h4>2. Meaning: the Article 1 definition</h4><div class=\"study-defn\"><strong>Discrimination against women:</strong> Any distinction, exclusion or restriction made on the basis of sex which has the effect or purpose of impairing or nullifying the recognition, enjoyment or exercise by women, irrespective of their marital status, on a basis of equality of men and women, of human rights and fundamental freedoms in the political, economic, social, cultural, civil or any other field.</div><p>Three features distinguish this from a simple equal treatment guarantee.</p><ul><li>It covers 'effect or purpose', so a neutral rule with disparate impact is caught.</li><li>It reaches 'any other field', which the Committee reads as including the private sphere, so the state must address discrimination in the family and by private actors.</li><li>It refers to equality 'on a basis of equality of men and women', which the Committee reads as substantive rather than merely formal equality.</li></ul><h4>3. The three-part obligation the Committee reads into the Convention</h4><div class=\"table-wrap\"><table class=\"study-table\"><thead><tr><th>Obligation</th><th>Content</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Formal equality</td><td>Guarantee equality in law: constitutional and legislative provision, repeal of discriminatory laws</td></tr><tr><td>Substantive equality</td><td>Ensure equality of opportunity and of results in practice, which may require differential treatment</td></tr><tr><td>Transformative equality</td><td>Address the underlying social and cultural patterns, stereotypes and structures that produce inequality</td></tr></tbody></table></div><p>The third limb is what makes CEDAW unusual. Article 5(a) requires states to modify the social and cultural patterns of conduct of men and women with a view to eliminating prejudices and practices based on the idea of the inferiority or superiority of either sex or on stereotyped roles. No other core treaty asks a state to change a culture in quite these terms.</p><h4>4. Substantive obligations by article</h4><div class=\"table-wrap\"><table class=\"study-table\"><thead><tr><th>Article</th><th>Field</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>2</td><td>Policy measures: constitutional embodiment, legal protection, abolition of discriminatory laws and practices, including by private persons and organisations</td></tr><tr><td>4</td><td>Temporary special measures</td></tr><tr><td>5</td><td>Modification of social and cultural patterns; recognition of the common responsibility of men and women in child-rearing</td></tr><tr><td>6</td><td>Suppression of trafficking and exploitation of prostitution</td></tr><tr><td>7-8</td><td>Political and public life; representation at international level</td></tr><tr><td>9</td><td>Nationality, including the right to transmit nationality to children</td></tr><tr><td>10</td><td>Education, including elimination of stereotyped conceptions of roles</td></tr><tr><td>11</td><td>Employment, including maternity protection and prohibition of dismissal on grounds of pregnancy</td></tr><tr><td>12</td><td>Health care, including family planning and appropriate services in pregnancy</td></tr><tr><td>13</td><td>Economic and social life, including family benefits, credit and recreation</td></tr><tr><td>14</td><td>Rural women, a provision without parallel in other treaties</td></tr><tr><td>15</td><td>Equality before the law and legal capacity, including in contracts and property</td></tr><tr><td>16</td><td>Marriage and family relations, including free consent, equal rights in dissolution, and a minimum age of marriage</td></tr></tbody></table></div><h4>5. Temporary special measures under Article 4(1)</h4><p>Article 4(1) provides that temporary special measures aimed at accelerating de facto equality shall not be considered discrimination but must be discontinued when the objectives of equality of opportunity and treatment have been achieved.</p><p>General Recommendation 25 makes three points examiners look for.</p><ol><li>They are not an exception to the principle of non-discrimination but a necessary means of achieving substantive equality.</li><li>'Temporary' is defined by result, not by a calendar: the measure lasts until the desired result has been achieved and sustained, however long that takes.</li><li>Article 4(2) measures aimed at protecting maternity are not temporary and are not covered by Article 4(1). They are permanent and respond to a biological difference.</li></ol><div class=\"study-callout\"><strong>Note:</strong> The distinction between Article 4(1) and Article 4(2) is one of the most reliable short-answer questions in this field. Gender quotas in a legislature are Article 4(1). Paid maternity leave is Article 4(2).</div><h4>6. Violence against women: General Recommendations 19 and 35</h4><p>CEDAW contains no article on violence against women. The Committee supplied one by interpretation.</p><ul><li>General Recommendation 19 in 1992 held that gender-based violence is a form of discrimination within Article 1, because it impairs women's enjoyment of rights on a basis of equality with men.</li><li>It established that states are responsible for private acts where they fail to act with due diligence to prevent, investigate, punish and provide reparation.</li><li>General Recommendation 35 in 2017 updated this, using the term 'gender-based violence against women', treating the prohibition as having become a norm of customary international law, and extending the analysis to technology-facilitated violence and to reproductive rights violations.</li><li>It sets out obligations across four areas: prevention, protection, prosecution and punishment, and reparation.</li></ul><div class=\"study-callout\"><strong>Example:</strong> A state's law treats domestic assault as a private matter requiring the victim to bring a private prosecution, while assault by a stranger is prosecuted by the state. Under General Recommendation 19 and 35 this is discrimination: the difference in treatment tracks a gendered pattern of violence and the state has failed the due diligence standard by leaving enforcement to the victim.</div><h4>7. The reservations problem</h4><p>CEDAW attracts more reservations than any other core treaty, and many are to its central provisions, particularly Articles 2 and 16.</p><ol><li>A reservation incompatible with the object and purpose of a treaty is impermissible under the Vienna Convention Article 19(c).</li><li>The Committee has stated repeatedly that reservations to Article 2, the general policy obligation, and Article 16, on marriage and family, are incompatible with the object and purpose and therefore impermissible.</li><li>Several reservations are framed as subjecting the treaty to national or religious law, which the Committee treats as effectively reserving against the whole treaty.</li><li>The Convention itself, in Article 28(2), states that a reservation incompatible with the object and purpose shall not be permitted, but provides no body to determine the question, and objections by other states have limited practical effect.</li><li>The result is a treaty with near-universal ratification and uneven substantive commitment, which is the standard criticism of CEDAW and should appear in any evaluative answer.</li></ol><h4>8. How the examiner&#x27;s question should be answered</h4><p>For 'assess CEDAW's effectiveness', give both sides with evidence. On the positive side: near-universal ratification, the transformative equality obligation in Article 5, the interpretive achievement of General Recommendations 19 and 35, the Optional Protocol complaints and inquiry procedures, and demonstrable legislative change in many states following concluding observations. On the negative side: the reservations problem, the absence of an enforcement body able to rule on compatibility, weak implementation of views, and the Committee's limited resources. Conclude with a position rather than trailing off.</p><h4>Summary</h4><ul><li>CEDAW defines discrimination against women by effect or purpose, reaching the private sphere and requiring substantive not merely formal equality.</li><li>Article 5(a) adds a transformative obligation to modify social and cultural patterns and stereotyped roles.</li><li>Article 4(1) temporary special measures accelerate de facto equality and end when the objective is achieved; Article 4(2) maternity protection is permanent.</li><li>General Recommendations 19 and 35 brought gender-based violence within the Convention and set the due diligence standard for private acts.</li><li>CEDAW has near-universal ratification but attracts more reservations than any other core treaty, many to Articles 2 and 16.</li></ul><h4>Key Terms</h4><div class=\"table-wrap\"><table class=\"study-table\"><thead><tr><th>Term</th><th>Meaning</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>CEDAW</td><td>The Convention on the Elimination of All Forms of Discrimination against Women, 1979.</td></tr><tr><td>Transformative equality</td><td>The obligation to change the underlying structures and stereotypes producing inequality.</td></tr><tr><td>Due diligence</td><td>The standard by which a state is responsible for failing to prevent, investigate, punish and remedy private violence.</td></tr><tr><td>Temporary special measure</td><td>A time-limited measure under Article 4(1) accelerating de facto equality, ending when its objective is achieved.</td></tr></tbody></table></div><h4>Review Questions</h4><h5>Multiple choice</h5><div class=\"study-mcq\"><p class=\"mcq-q\">Which article of CEDAW requires states to modify social and cultural patterns and stereotyped roles?</p><ol type=\"A\"><li>Article 5</li><li>Article 2</li><li>Article 4</li><li>Article 16</li></ol><details class=\"mcq-a\"><summary>Show answer</summary><p><strong>Answer: A.</strong> This transformative obligation has no direct parallel in the other core treaties.</p></details></div><div class=\"study-mcq\"><p class=\"mcq-q\">Paid maternity leave falls under which provision?</p><ol type=\"A\"><li>Article 4(2), as a permanent measure protecting maternity</li><li>Article 4(1), as a temporary special measure</li><li>Article 11 only</li><li>It is outside the Convention</li></ol><details class=\"mcq-a\"><summary>Show answer</summary><p><strong>Answer: A.</strong> Article 4(1) measures are temporary and end when equality is achieved; maternity protection is permanent because it responds to a biological difference.</p></details></div><div class=\"study-mcq\"><p class=\"mcq-q\">How did the Committee bring violence against women within CEDAW?</p><ol type=\"A\"><li>General Recommendation 19 held that gender-based violence is a form of discrimination under Article 1</li><li>An amendment to the Convention added a new article</li><li>The Optional Protocol created the obligation</li><li>The General Assembly adopted a binding resolution</li></ol><details class=\"mcq-a\"><summary>Show answer</summary><p><strong>Answer: A.</strong> General Recommendation 35 later updated it, treating the prohibition as customary international law.</p></details></div><div class=\"study-mcq\"><p class=\"mcq-q\">What is the main criticism of CEDAW&#x27;s ratification record?</p><ol type=\"A\"><li>Near-universal ratification coexists with extensive reservations to core articles such as 2 and 16</li><li>Too few states have ratified</li><li>It has no reporting procedure</li><li>It contains no equality provision</li></ol><details class=\"mcq-a\"><summary>Show answer</summary><p><strong>Answer: A.</strong> Article 28(2) prohibits reservations incompatible with the object and purpose but provides no body to determine compatibility.</p></details></div><h5>Short answer</h5><div class=\"study-sa\"><p class=\"sa-q\">Explain the difference between formal, substantive and transformative equality under CEDAW.</p><details class=\"sa-a\"><summary>Model answer</summary><p>Formal equality requires equality in law: the constitution and statutes must not discriminate and discriminatory provisions must be repealed. Substantive equality requires equality of opportunity and of result in practice, which may require treating women differently, for example through temporary special measures, because identical treatment can perpetuate existing disadvantage. Transformative equality, drawn from Article 5(a), requires the state to address the underlying social and cultural patterns, prejudices and stereotyped roles that generate inequality in the first place, which is a demand no other core treaty makes in the same terms.</p></details></div><div class=\"study-sa\"><p class=\"sa-q\">State the due diligence standard for violence against women.</p><details class=\"sa-a\"><summary>Model answer</summary><p>Under General Recommendations 19 and 35, a state is responsible not only for gender-based violence committed by its own agents but also for private acts where it fails to act with due diligence. That standard requires the state to take reasonable measures to prevent violence, to protect those at risk, to investigate and prosecute allegations effectively and punish those responsible, and to provide reparation to victims. Failure in any of these limbs engages state responsibility even though the perpetrator was a private individual.</p></details></div><h4>Class Activity</h4><ol><li>List the reservations your country has entered to CEDAW, if any. For each, write whether you think it is compatible with the object and purpose and why.</li><li>In groups, design a temporary special measure to increase women&#x27;s representation in one named institution. Specify the objective, the mechanism, the review interval and the exit condition.</li></ol><h4>Assignment</h4><p>Write two pages on CEDAW. Give the Article 1 definition and explain its distinctive features, set out the three-part equality obligation with the role of Article 5, explain temporary special measures and distinguish them from maternity protection, describe how General Recommendations 19 and 35 brought violence against women within the Convention, and assess the treaty&#x27;s effectiveness in light of the reservations problem.</p><p class=\"study-print\"><strong>Printable:</strong> use your browser\u2019s Print \u2192 Save as PDF to keep an offline copy.</p></div>",
  "The Rights of the Child (CRC)": "<div class=\"study-note\"><div class=\"revision-banner\"><strong>Human Rights and International Humanitarian Law</strong><span>Module 4: Thematic Human Rights Treaties</span></div><h3 class=\"lesson-note-title\">LESSON NOTE: THE RIGHTS OF THE CHILD (CRC)</h3><div class=\"table-wrap\"><table class=\"study-table\"><thead><tr><th>Subject</th><th>Topic</th><th>Level</th><th>Lesson duration</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Human Rights and International Humanitarian Law</td><td>The Rights of the Child (CRC)</td><td>Beginner to Advanced</td><td>45 minutes</td></tr></tbody></table></div><h4>Learning objectives</h4><p>By the end of this lesson you should be able to:</p><ul><li>State the four general principles of the CRC</li><li>Explain the evolving capacities of the child and the right to be heard</li><li>Describe the Convention&#x27;s main protections and its Optional Protocols</li><li>Apply the best interests principle to a concrete decision</li></ul><h4>1. Introduction</h4><p>The CRC is the most widely ratified human rights treaty in history, with 196 states parties. It was the first treaty to treat children as rights holders rather than as objects of protection, which is a genuine conceptual shift and the thing most worth understanding about it.</p><h4>2. Meaning and scope</h4><div class=\"study-defn\"><strong>Child:</strong> Every human being below the age of eighteen years unless, under the law applicable to the child, majority is attained earlier (CRC Article 1).</div><div class=\"study-callout\"><strong>Note:</strong> The 'unless majority is attained earlier' clause allows a lower age of majority but does not permit a state to lower the age for protective purposes. The Committee reads the Convention as requiring eighteen for the most protective provisions, such as the minimum age of marriage and the prohibition of the death penalty and life imprisonment without release.</div><h4>3. The four general principles</h4><div class=\"table-wrap\"><table class=\"study-table\"><thead><tr><th>Principle</th><th>Article</th><th>Content</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Non-discrimination</td><td>2</td><td>All rights to all children within jurisdiction, without discrimination on any ground, including the status of the child's parents</td></tr><tr><td>Best interests of the child</td><td>3</td><td>A primary consideration in all actions concerning children by any public or private body</td></tr><tr><td>Right to life, survival and development</td><td>6</td><td>Not only protection from killing but the conditions for development to the maximum extent possible</td></tr><tr><td>Respect for the views of the child</td><td>12</td><td>The child capable of forming views has the right to express them freely and have them given due weight in accordance with age and maturity</td></tr></tbody></table></div><p>The Committee treats these four as lenses through which every other article must be read. An answer that applies all four to the facts is doing what the Committee does.</p><h4>4. The best interests principle</h4><p>General Comment 14 explains that Article 3(1) contains a threefold concept.</p><ol><li><strong>A substantive right:</strong> the child's best interests must be assessed and taken as a primary consideration when different interests are weighed.</li><li><strong>An interpretative principle:</strong> where a provision is open to more than one interpretation, the one most effectively serving the child's best interests is chosen.</li><li><strong>A rule of procedure:</strong> any decision concerning a child must include an evaluation of the possible impact on the child, and the reasoning must show that this was done.</li></ol><p>Note the wording: 'a primary consideration', not 'the paramount consideration'. This was deliberate. The child's interests carry high priority but do not automatically defeat every competing interest. Getting this wording right is a small point that separates careful answers from careless ones.</p><div class=\"study-callout\"><strong>Example:</strong> A court decides whether to deport a parent whose child is settled in the country. The best interests principle does not decide the case by itself, but it requires the court to identify the child's interests specifically, evaluate the impact of each option on this child, treat those interests as a primary consideration against immigration control, and set out in the judgment how the balance was struck. A judgment that mentions the child only in passing breaches the procedural limb even if the outcome might have been the same.</div><h4>5. Evolving capacities and the right to be heard</h4><div class=\"study-defn\"><strong>Evolving capacities:</strong> The principle in Article 5 that parental direction and guidance must be provided in a manner consistent with the child's developing abilities, so that the child exercises rights increasingly independently as capacity grows.</div><p>This resolves the apparent tension between protection and autonomy. The child is not either a passive object or a fully autonomous adult; the balance shifts with capacity, and the state's job is to track that shift rather than to apply a single age cut-off to everything.</p><p>Article 12 gives it procedural teeth. General Comment 12 sets out what a genuine hearing requires.</p><ul><li>No lower age limit may be imposed; the presumption is that a child is capable of forming views.</li><li>The child must be informed about the matter, the options and the consequences.</li><li>The hearing must be in a child-friendly setting, with support where needed.</li><li>The views must be given due weight, and the decision must explain what weight was given and why.</li><li>There must be a route to complain about a failure to hear the child.</li></ul><h4>6. Main protections</h4><div class=\"table-wrap\"><table class=\"study-table\"><thead><tr><th>Theme</th><th>Articles</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Identity, name, nationality, family relations</td><td>7, 8</td></tr><tr><td>Family environment, separation from parents, reunification</td><td>9, 10, 18</td></tr><tr><td>Alternative care and adoption</td><td>20, 21</td></tr><tr><td>Refugee and displaced children</td><td>22</td></tr><tr><td>Children with disabilities</td><td>23</td></tr><tr><td>Health and health services</td><td>24</td></tr><tr><td>Social security and standard of living</td><td>26, 27</td></tr><tr><td>Education and its aims</td><td>28, 29</td></tr><tr><td>Minority and indigenous children</td><td>30</td></tr><tr><td>Rest, play and cultural life</td><td>31</td></tr><tr><td>Economic exploitation and child labour</td><td>32</td></tr><tr><td>Drugs, sexual exploitation, trafficking, other exploitation</td><td>33, 34, 35, 36</td></tr><tr><td>Torture, deprivation of liberty, juvenile justice</td><td>37, 40</td></tr><tr><td>Children in armed conflict</td><td>38</td></tr><tr><td>Recovery and reintegration</td><td>39</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"study-callout\"><strong>Note:</strong> Article 37(a) prohibits capital punishment and life imprisonment without possibility of release for offences committed by persons under eighteen. Article 37(b) requires that detention be a measure of last resort and for the shortest appropriate period. Together with Article 40 these make the CRC the principal source of international juvenile justice standards.</div><h4>7. The Optional Protocols</h4><div class=\"table-wrap\"><table class=\"study-table\"><thead><tr><th>Protocol</th><th>Year</th><th>Content</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>On the involvement of children in armed conflict</td><td>2000</td><td>Raises the minimum age for direct participation in hostilities and compulsory recruitment to eighteen; requires safeguards for voluntary recruitment from sixteen; prohibits any recruitment under eighteen by non-state armed groups</td></tr><tr><td>On the sale of children, child prostitution and child pornography</td><td>2000</td><td>Requires criminalisation of these offences, with extraterritorial jurisdiction and provision for victim protection</td></tr><tr><td>On a communications procedure</td><td>2011</td><td>Allows individual communications, inter-state communications and an inquiry procedure</td></tr></tbody></table></div><h4>8. How the examiner&#x27;s question should be answered</h4><p>Any CRC problem should be run through the four general principles explicitly, in order, then through the specific articles engaged. On best interests, say 'a primary consideration' and give the threefold analysis from General Comment 14. On the child's views, apply the General Comment 12 requirements rather than simply noting that the child should be heard. If the question involves an age threshold, ask whether the state is using a lower age of majority to reduce protection, which the Committee does not accept.</p><h4>Summary</h4><ul><li>The CRC has 196 states parties and treats children as rights holders rather than objects of protection.</li><li>Its four general principles are non-discrimination, best interests, life, survival and development, and respect for the child&#x27;s views.</li><li>Best interests is a primary consideration, not the paramount one, and operates as a substantive right, an interpretive principle and a rule of procedure.</li><li>Evolving capacities under Article 5 shifts the balance from protection to autonomy as the child develops.</li><li>Article 37 prohibits capital punishment and life imprisonment without release for under-eighteens and makes detention a last resort.</li><li>Three Optional Protocols cover armed conflict, sale and sexual exploitation, and a communications procedure.</li></ul><h4>Key Terms</h4><div class=\"table-wrap\"><table class=\"study-table\"><thead><tr><th>Term</th><th>Meaning</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Best interests of the child</td><td>The principle in CRC Article 3 that a child&#x27;s interests are a primary consideration in all actions concerning them.</td></tr><tr><td>Evolving capacities</td><td>The developing ability of a child to exercise rights independently, guiding the degree of parental direction.</td></tr><tr><td>Right to be heard</td><td>CRC Article 12&#x27;s guarantee that a child&#x27;s views be expressed freely and given due weight.</td></tr><tr><td>Last resort principle</td><td>CRC Article 37(b)&#x27;s requirement that detention of a child be used only as a last resort and for the shortest appropriate period.</td></tr></tbody></table></div><h4>Review Questions</h4><h5>Multiple choice</h5><div class=\"study-mcq\"><p class=\"mcq-q\">How does CRC Article 3 describe the weight given to a child&#x27;s best interests?</p><ol type=\"A\"><li>A primary consideration</li><li>The paramount consideration</li><li>The only consideration</li><li>A relevant factor among many</li></ol><details class=\"mcq-a\"><summary>Show answer</summary><p><strong>Answer: A.</strong> The wording was deliberate: the child&#x27;s interests carry high priority but do not automatically override every competing interest.</p></details></div><div class=\"study-mcq\"><p class=\"mcq-q\">Under General Comment 12, may a state set a minimum age for a child&#x27;s views to be heard?</p><ol type=\"A\"><li>No; the presumption is that a child is capable of forming views</li><li>Yes, twelve years</li><li>Yes, if a court approves</li><li>Yes, in criminal matters only</li></ol><details class=\"mcq-a\"><summary>Show answer</summary><p><strong>Answer: A.</strong> Due weight is calibrated to age and maturity, but the right to be heard is not gated by an age threshold.</p></details></div><div class=\"study-mcq\"><p class=\"mcq-q\">What does CRC Article 37(a) prohibit for offences committed under eighteen?</p><ol type=\"A\"><li>Capital punishment and life imprisonment without possibility of release</li><li>All custodial sentences</li><li>Prosecution in adult courts</li><li>Fines exceeding a set amount</li></ol><details class=\"mcq-a\"><summary>Show answer</summary><p><strong>Answer: A.</strong> Article 37(b) separately requires detention to be a last resort for the shortest appropriate period.</p></details></div><div class=\"study-mcq\"><p class=\"mcq-q\">What does the principle of evolving capacities require?</p><ol type=\"A\"><li>Parental direction consistent with the child&#x27;s developing abilities, so the child exercises rights increasingly independently</li><li>A fixed age of majority for all purposes</li><li>That children have the same rights as adults from birth</li><li>That parents decide all matters until eighteen</li></ol><details class=\"mcq-a\"><summary>Show answer</summary><p><strong>Answer: A.</strong> Article 5 resolves the tension between protection and autonomy by making the balance shift with capacity.</p></details></div><h5>Short answer</h5><div class=\"study-sa\"><p class=\"sa-q\">Explain the threefold nature of the best interests principle under General Comment 14.</p><details class=\"sa-a\"><summary>Model answer</summary><p>It is first a substantive right: the child&#x27;s best interests must be assessed and taken as a primary consideration whenever different interests are weighed, and the child has a right to have this done. It is second an interpretative legal principle: where a provision admits more than one interpretation, the one most effectively serving the child&#x27;s best interests must be chosen. It is third a rule of procedure: any decision concerning a child must include an evaluation of the likely impact on that child, and the decision must show explicitly that this evaluation was carried out and how the interests were weighed.</p></details></div><div class=\"study-sa\"><p class=\"sa-q\">Name the four general principles of the CRC and state one practical consequence of each.</p><details class=\"sa-a\"><summary>Model answer</summary><p>Non-discrimination under Article 2 means all rights extend to every child in the jurisdiction regardless of the status of the child or the child&#x27;s parents, so undocumented children must have access to school. Best interests under Article 3 means every decision affecting a child must evaluate the impact on that child and record the reasoning. Life, survival and development under Article 6 means the state must provide the conditions for development, not merely refrain from killing. Respect for the child&#x27;s views under Article 12 means the child must be heard in any proceeding affecting them and told what weight the views were given.</p></details></div><h4>Class Activity</h4><ol><li>Take a real decision affecting children in your community, such as a school closure or a relocation. Run it through the four general principles and write a one-page assessment.</li><li>Design a child-friendly procedure for hearing children&#x27;s views on one issue in your school. Specify who hears them, how they are informed, and how the outcome is explained back to them.</li></ol><h4>Assignment</h4><p>Write two pages on the Convention on the Rights of the Child. Explain its conceptual shift from protection to rights-holding, set out and illustrate the four general principles, analyse the best interests principle using General Comment 14, explain evolving capacities and the requirements of a genuine Article 12 hearing, summarise the main protections and the three Optional Protocols, and apply the framework to one decision of your choice.</p><p class=\"study-print\"><strong>Printable:</strong> use your browser\u2019s Print \u2192 Save as PDF to keep an offline copy.</p></div>",
  "The Convention Against Torture (CAT)": "<div class=\"study-note\"><div class=\"revision-banner\"><strong>Human Rights and International Humanitarian Law</strong><span>Module 4: Thematic Human Rights Treaties</span></div><h3 class=\"lesson-note-title\">LESSON NOTE: THE CONVENTION AGAINST TORTURE (CAT)</h3><div class=\"table-wrap\"><table class=\"study-table\"><thead><tr><th>Subject</th><th>Topic</th><th>Level</th><th>Lesson duration</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Human Rights and International Humanitarian Law</td><td>The Convention Against Torture (CAT)</td><td>Beginner to Advanced</td><td>40 minutes</td></tr></tbody></table></div><h4>Learning objectives</h4><p>By the end of this lesson you should be able to:</p><ul><li>Describe the structure and main obligations of the Convention Against Torture</li><li>Explain the jurisdictional provisions and the aut dedere aut judicare rule</li><li>Describe the Committee&#x27;s procedures and the role of OPCAT</li><li>Assess the Convention&#x27;s practical effect</li></ul><h4>1. Introduction</h4><p>The prohibition of torture already existed in customary law and in the ICCPR when CAT was adopted in 1984. The Convention's contribution is not the prohibition but the machinery: a definition, a duty to criminalise, universal jurisdiction, an exclusionary rule, a specialised committee, and later a preventive visiting system. This lesson is about that machinery.</p><h4>2. Structure</h4><div class=\"table-wrap\"><table class=\"study-table\"><thead><tr><th>Part</th><th>Articles</th><th>Content</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>I</td><td>1-16</td><td>Substantive obligations</td></tr><tr><td>II</td><td>17-24</td><td>The Committee against Torture and its procedures</td></tr><tr><td>III</td><td>25-33</td><td>Final clauses</td></tr></tbody></table></div><p>Article 1 defines torture; Article 16 extends the preventive and investigative obligations to other cruel, inhuman or degrading treatment or punishment. That two-tier structure runs through the whole Convention: the strongest duties, criminalisation and universal jurisdiction, attach to torture proper.</p><h4>3. The core obligations</h4><div class=\"table-wrap\"><table class=\"study-table\"><thead><tr><th>Article</th><th>Obligation</th><th>Practical test</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>2(1)</td><td>Effective legislative, administrative, judicial and other measures to prevent torture</td><td>Is there a functioning prevention system, not merely a law?</td></tr><tr><td>2(2)</td><td>No exceptional circumstances whatsoever</td><td>Does emergency legislation carve out interrogation?</td></tr><tr><td>2(3)</td><td>No superior orders defence</td><td>Is this excluded in the criminal code and in military law?</td></tr><tr><td>3</td><td>Non-refoulement</td><td>Is there an individualised risk assessment with suspensive appeal?</td></tr><tr><td>4</td><td>Criminalise all acts of torture, attempts and complicity, with appropriate penalties</td><td>Is there a specific offence of torture, or only assault?</td></tr><tr><td>10</td><td>Training of law enforcement, medical, military and other personnel</td><td>Is the Istanbul Protocol taught to doctors examining detainees?</td></tr><tr><td>11</td><td>Systematic review of interrogation rules and detention arrangements</td><td>Is there periodic review, or only reaction to scandals?</td></tr><tr><td>12</td><td>Prompt, impartial investigation ex officio whenever there is reasonable ground to believe torture occurred</td><td>Does an investigation start without a complaint?</td></tr><tr><td>13</td><td>Right to complain, with protection against reprisals</td><td>Can a detainee complain to someone outside the detaining authority?</td></tr><tr><td>14</td><td>Redress, fair and adequate compensation, and the fullest possible rehabilitation</td><td>Do rehabilitation services exist and are they funded?</td></tr><tr><td>15</td><td>Exclusion of statements obtained by torture</td><td>Do courts inquire into how a confession was obtained?</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"study-callout\"><strong>Note:</strong> Article 4 matters more than students expect. Where there is no specific offence of torture, prosecutors charge assault or abuse of office, penalties are low, statutes of limitation apply, and the conduct is never recorded as torture in any official statistic. Creating the offence is the single most consequential piece of CAT implementation.</div><h4>4. Jurisdiction and the duty to extradite or prosecute</h4><p>Articles 5 to 9 build a jurisdictional net designed to leave no safe haven.</p><ol><li>Article 5(1) requires each state to establish jurisdiction over torture committed in its territory, by its nationals, and, at its option, against its nationals.</li><li>Article 5(2) requires jurisdiction where the alleged offender is present in its territory and it does not extradite. This is universal jurisdiction, triggered by presence.</li><li>Article 6 requires the state to take the person into custody and make a preliminary inquiry.</li><li>Article 7 states the obligation aut dedere aut judicare: if the state does not extradite, it must submit the case to its competent authorities for prosecution.</li><li>Article 8 deems torture an extraditable offence in every extradition treaty between parties.</li></ol><div class=\"study-defn\"><strong>Aut dedere aut judicare:</strong> The obligation either to extradite an alleged offender or to submit the case to the competent authorities for the purpose of prosecution.</div><div class=\"study-callout\"><strong>Example:</strong> Questions Relating to the Obligation to Prosecute or Extradite (Belgium v Senegal), decided by the International Court of Justice in 2012, is the leading authority. Senegal had Hissene Habre on its territory and neither prosecuted him nor extradited him for years. The Court held that Senegal had breached Articles 6(2) and 7(1), that the obligation to prosecute arises regardless of any extradition request, and that every state party has a legal interest in compliance, so Belgium had standing. Habre was eventually convicted in 2016 by the Extraordinary African Chambers in Dakar.</div><h4>5. The Committee against Torture</h4><div class=\"table-wrap\"><table class=\"study-table\"><thead><tr><th>Procedure</th><th>Article</th><th>Note</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>State reporting</td><td>19</td><td>Initial report within one year, then every four years</td></tr><tr><td>Confidential inquiry</td><td>20</td><td>Where the Committee receives reliable indications of systematic torture; may include a visit with consent; states may opt out under Article 28</td></tr><tr><td>Inter-state complaints</td><td>21</td><td>Requires declaration; rarely used</td></tr><tr><td>Individual communications</td><td>22</td><td>Requires declaration; a substantial body of Article 3 non-refoulement decisions</td></tr><tr><td>General Comments</td><td>Practice</td><td>GC 2 on implementation of Article 2, GC 3 on Article 14 redress, GC 4 on Article 3</td></tr></tbody></table></div><h4>6. OPCAT and the preventive model</h4><p>The Optional Protocol, adopted in 2002 and in force in 2006, added a fundamentally different approach: not responding to torture after the event but preventing it by making places of detention visible.</p><ul><li>The Subcommittee on Prevention of Torture may visit any place of detention in a state party without needing consent for each visit.</li><li>Each state party must designate or establish one or more National Preventive Mechanisms within a year.</li><li>An NPM must have functional independence, adequate resources, expert and gender-balanced membership, access to all places of detention including unannounced, access to all information and records, and the right to interview detainees in private.</li><li>The model rests on a simple insight: torture happens where there are no witnesses, so a credible prospect of unannounced independent inspection is the most effective single preventive measure.</li></ul><h4>7. How the examiner&#x27;s question should be answered</h4><p>For 'assess the effectiveness of the Convention Against Torture', give the achievements: a workable definition adopted by other bodies; the criminalisation duty; universal jurisdiction with Belgium v Senegal enforcing it; the exclusionary rule; and the preventive turn represented by OPCAT and National Preventive Mechanisms. Then give the limits: torture persists in a large number of states parties; Article 20 inquiry can be opted out of; Articles 21 and 22 require separate declarations, so many states are outside the complaints system; there is no enforcement body; and non-refoulement is undermined in practice by diplomatic assurances and by interception at sea. Conclude with a judgement about which reform would matter most, which shows evaluation rather than listing.</p><h4>Summary</h4><ul><li>CAT&#x27;s contribution is machinery: definition, criminalisation, jurisdiction, exclusion of evidence, a committee and a preventive system.</li><li>Article 4 requires a specific criminal offence of torture, without which cases are charged as ordinary assault.</li><li>Articles 5 to 8 create universal jurisdiction based on presence and the duty to extradite or prosecute.</li><li>Belgium v Senegal confirmed that the duty to prosecute arises independently of any extradition request and that all parties have standing.</li><li>OPCAT shifts to prevention through the Subcommittee and independent National Preventive Mechanisms with unannounced access.</li><li>The system&#x27;s main weaknesses are opt-outs, the absence of enforcement, and the practical erosion of non-refoulement.</li></ul><h4>Key Terms</h4><div class=\"table-wrap\"><table class=\"study-table\"><thead><tr><th>Term</th><th>Meaning</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Aut dedere aut judicare</td><td>The duty either to extradite an alleged offender or to submit the case for prosecution.</td></tr><tr><td>Universal jurisdiction</td><td>Jurisdiction over certain grave crimes irrespective of where committed or the nationality of those involved.</td></tr><tr><td>National Preventive Mechanism</td><td>The independent domestic body required by OPCAT to inspect all places of detention.</td></tr><tr><td>Istanbul Protocol</td><td>The manual on effective investigation and documentation of torture and ill-treatment.</td></tr></tbody></table></div><h4>Review Questions</h4><h5>Multiple choice</h5><div class=\"study-mcq\"><p class=\"mcq-q\">What did the International Court of Justice hold in Belgium v Senegal?</p><ol type=\"A\"><li>Senegal breached its duty to prosecute or extradite, and every state party has a legal interest in compliance</li><li>Senegal was entitled to await an extradition request</li><li>Universal jurisdiction does not exist under CAT</li><li>Only the victim&#x27;s state could bring the claim</li></ol><details class=\"mcq-a\"><summary>Show answer</summary><p><strong>Answer: A.</strong> The obligation to submit the case for prosecution arises independently of any extradition request.</p></details></div><div class=\"study-mcq\"><p class=\"mcq-q\">Why does CAT Article 4 matter in practice?</p><ol type=\"A\"><li>Without a specific offence of torture, cases are charged as ordinary assault with low penalties and limitation periods</li><li>It creates the Committee against Torture</li><li>It permits derogation in emergencies</li><li>It governs extradition procedure</li></ol><details class=\"mcq-a\"><summary>Show answer</summary><p><strong>Answer: A.</strong> Creating the offence is the single most consequential piece of CAT implementation.</p></details></div><div class=\"study-mcq\"><p class=\"mcq-q\">What power distinguishes a National Preventive Mechanism?</p><ol type=\"A\"><li>Unannounced access to all places of detention and private interviews with detainees</li><li>The power to prosecute torturers</li><li>The power to award compensation</li><li>The power to release detainees</li></ol><details class=\"mcq-a\"><summary>Show answer</summary><p><strong>Answer: A.</strong> Prevention works because torture occurs where there are no witnesses; credible unannounced inspection removes that condition.</p></details></div><div class=\"study-mcq\"><p class=\"mcq-q\">Which CAT procedure may a state opt out of on ratification?</p><ol type=\"A\"><li>The Article 20 confidential inquiry into systematic torture</li><li>The Article 19 reporting obligation</li><li>The Article 3 non-refoulement obligation</li><li>The Article 15 exclusionary rule</li></ol><details class=\"mcq-a\"><summary>Show answer</summary><p><strong>Answer: A.</strong> Article 28 permits a declaration excluding the Article 20 procedure; Articles 21 and 22 conversely require positive declarations to accept them.</p></details></div><h5>Short answer</h5><div class=\"study-sa\"><p class=\"sa-q\">Explain how Articles 5 to 8 of CAT are designed to leave no safe haven.</p><details class=\"sa-a\"><summary>Model answer</summary><p>Article 5(1) requires territorial and nationality jurisdiction. Article 5(2) requires each state to establish jurisdiction whenever an alleged torturer is present on its territory and it does not extradite, which is universal jurisdiction triggered by presence. Article 6 requires that state to take the person into custody and conduct a preliminary inquiry. Article 7 then obliges it either to extradite or to submit the case to its own prosecuting authorities. Article 8 deems torture an extraditable offence in all extradition treaties between parties, removing the argument that no treaty covers it. Together these mean that wherever an alleged torturer travels among states parties, some state has both jurisdiction and a duty to act.</p></details></div><div class=\"study-sa\"><p class=\"sa-q\">Give three achievements and three limitations of the Convention Against Torture.</p><details class=\"sa-a\"><summary>Model answer</summary><p>Achievements: it supplied a definition now used across international law; it requires a specific criminal offence with appropriate penalties; and it established universal jurisdiction, enforced by the International Court of Justice in Belgium v Senegal, alongside the exclusionary rule and the preventive OPCAT system. Limitations: the Article 20 inquiry can be opted out of and Articles 21 and 22 require positive declarations, so many states sit outside the complaints machinery; there is no body able to enforce the Committee&#x27;s findings; and non-refoulement is eroded in practice by reliance on diplomatic assurances and by interception operations that avoid formal removal procedures.</p></details></div><h4>Class Activity</h4><ol><li>Check whether your country has a specific criminal offence of torture, what penalty it carries, and whether any limitation period applies. Report your findings in one paragraph.</li><li>Draft a five-point inspection checklist an NPM could use on an unannounced visit to a police station. Explain what each point is designed to detect.</li></ol><h4>Assignment</h4><p>Write one and a half pages on the Convention Against Torture. Describe its structure and core obligations, explain the jurisdictional scheme and the duty to extradite or prosecute with reference to Belgium v Senegal, describe the Committee&#x27;s procedures and the preventive model introduced by OPCAT, and assess the Convention&#x27;s effectiveness with three achievements and three limitations.</p><p class=\"study-print\"><strong>Printable:</strong> use your browser\u2019s Print \u2192 Save as PDF to keep an offline copy.</p></div>",
  "The Rights of Persons with Disabilities (CRPD)": "<div class=\"study-note\"><div class=\"revision-banner\"><strong>Human Rights and International Humanitarian Law</strong><span>Module 4: Thematic Human Rights Treaties</span></div><h3 class=\"lesson-note-title\">LESSON NOTE: THE RIGHTS OF PERSONS WITH DISABILITIES (CRPD)</h3><div class=\"table-wrap\"><table class=\"study-table\"><thead><tr><th>Subject</th><th>Topic</th><th>Level</th><th>Lesson duration</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Human Rights and International Humanitarian Law</td><td>The Rights of Persons with Disabilities (CRPD)</td><td>Beginner to Advanced</td><td>45 minutes</td></tr></tbody></table></div><h4>Learning objectives</h4><p>By the end of this lesson you should be able to:</p><ul><li>Explain the shift from the medical to the social and human rights model of disability</li><li>Define reasonable accommodation and universal design and distinguish them</li><li>Explain legal capacity under Article 12 and supported decision-making</li><li>Describe the Convention&#x27;s main obligations and machinery</li></ul><h4>1. Introduction</h4><p>The CRPD, adopted in 2006, is the treaty that most clearly changed how a problem is conceived rather than merely adding rights. It relocates disability from the individual's body to the interaction between impairment and a disabling environment. Everything else in the Convention follows from that move.</p><h4>2. Models of disability</h4><div class=\"table-wrap\"><table class=\"study-table\"><thead><tr><th>Model</th><th>Locates the problem in</th><th>Response</th><th>Role of the person</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Charity model</td><td>The person's misfortune</td><td>Benevolence, alms, institutions</td><td>Recipient of pity</td></tr><tr><td>Medical model</td><td>The person's impairment</td><td>Cure, treatment, rehabilitation to normality</td><td>Patient</td></tr><tr><td>Social model</td><td>Barriers in society</td><td>Remove the barriers</td><td>Excluded citizen</td></tr><tr><td>Human rights model</td><td>Barriers, plus the denial of rights</td><td>Remove barriers and guarantee rights, including support</td><td>Rights holder</td></tr></tbody></table></div><p>The CRPD adopts the human rights model. Article 1 describes persons with disabilities as including those with long-term physical, mental, intellectual or sensory impairments which, 'in interaction with various barriers', may hinder full and effective participation in society on an equal basis with others. The words 'in interaction with various barriers' carry the whole theory.</p><div class=\"study-callout\"><strong>Example:</strong> A wheelchair user cannot enter a courthouse. The medical model asks what is wrong with the person's legs and whether they can be treated. The social model asks why the courthouse has steps and no ramp. The human rights model asks both about the ramp and about the denial of access to justice that the steps produce, and treats that denial as a violation of a right rather than as an unfortunate consequence.</div><h4>3. Accessibility, universal design and reasonable accommodation</h4><div class=\"table-wrap\"><table class=\"study-table\"><thead><tr><th>Concept</th><th>Definition</th><th>Nature of duty</th><th>Example</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Accessibility (Art 9)</td><td>Access to the physical environment, transport, information and communications on an equal basis with others</td><td>Owed to the group in advance; progressive but with immediate steps</td><td>Installing lifts and tactile signage in all public buildings</td></tr><tr><td>Universal design (Art 2)</td><td>Design of products, environments and services usable by all people to the greatest extent possible without adaptation</td><td>A design standard applied ex ante</td><td>Designing a new building step-free from the outset</td></tr><tr><td>Reasonable accommodation (Art 2)</td><td>Necessary and appropriate modification and adjustment, not imposing a disproportionate or undue burden, needed in a particular case</td><td>Owed to an individual, immediately, on request</td><td>Providing a sign language interpreter for one employee's meetings</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"study-callout\"><strong>Note:</strong> The distinction is examinable and frequently confused. Accessibility and universal design are anticipatory and owed to everyone; reasonable accommodation is reactive, individual and immediate. A state cannot answer a request for accommodation by saying accessibility is being achieved progressively. Article 2 states that denial of reasonable accommodation is itself discrimination.</div><h4>4. Article 12: legal capacity</h4><p>Article 12 is the Convention's most radical provision. It recognises that persons with disabilities enjoy legal capacity on an equal basis with others in all aspects of life, and requires states to provide access to the support they may require in exercising it.</p><div class=\"table-wrap\"><table class=\"study-table\"><thead><tr><th></th><th>Substituted decision-making</th><th>Supported decision-making</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Who decides</td><td>A guardian, on the person's behalf</td><td>The person, with support</td></tr><tr><td>Basis of decision</td><td>The guardian's view of the person's best interests</td><td>The person's will and preferences</td></tr><tr><td>Effect on capacity</td><td>Legal capacity removed or restricted</td><td>Legal capacity retained in full</td></tr><tr><td>CRPD position</td><td>General Comment 1 says it must be abolished</td><td>Required as the replacement</td></tr></tbody></table></div><p>General Comment 1 draws the essential distinction between mental capacity, which is a decision-making skill that varies between people and over time, and legal capacity, which is the recognition of a person as a holder of rights and an actor in law. The Committee's position is that impaired mental capacity may never be used as a ground for removing legal capacity, because that conflates the two.</p><div class=\"study-callout\"><strong>Note:</strong> This is genuinely contested. Several states entered declarations preserving substituted decision-making in limited cases, and academic critics argue that some situations, such as a person in a coma, cannot be handled by support alone. A strong answer states the Committee's position accurately, notes the objection, and explains the Committee's reply, which is that even then decisions must be based on the best interpretation of the person's will and preferences rather than on an objective best interests test.</div><h4>5. Other key articles</h4><div class=\"table-wrap\"><table class=\"study-table\"><thead><tr><th>Article</th><th>Content</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>5</td><td>Equality and non-discrimination, including reasonable accommodation</td></tr><tr><td>6, 7</td><td>Women and children with disabilities, recognising intersectional discrimination</td></tr><tr><td>13</td><td>Access to justice, including procedural and age-appropriate accommodations</td></tr><tr><td>14</td><td>Liberty and security; the Committee reads it as prohibiting detention based on impairment</td></tr><tr><td>19</td><td>Living independently and being included in the community, the basis of the deinstitutionalisation agenda</td></tr><tr><td>21, 24</td><td>Freedom of expression including sign languages; inclusive education at all levels</td></tr><tr><td>27</td><td>Work and employment in an open, inclusive and accessible labour market</td></tr><tr><td>29</td><td>Participation in political and public life, including accessible voting</td></tr><tr><td>33</td><td>National implementation: focal points, a coordination mechanism, and an independent monitoring framework</td></tr></tbody></table></div><p>Article 24 on inclusive education is the source of frequent disputes. General Comment 4 states that inclusive education means a systemic transformation of mainstream education, not the maintenance of parallel segregated systems, and that segregation is not compatible with the Convention.</p><h4>6. Machinery and the participation principle</h4><ul><li>The Committee on the Rights of Persons with Disabilities monitors implementation through state reporting.</li><li>The Optional Protocol provides for individual communications and an inquiry procedure.</li><li>Article 33(3) requires that civil society, and in particular persons with disabilities and their representative organisations, be involved and participate fully in monitoring.</li><li>Article 4(3) requires close consultation with and active involvement of persons with disabilities in developing legislation and policies affecting them.</li><li>The slogan of the drafting movement, 'nothing about us without us', became a legal obligation in these two provisions, which is unusual among human rights treaties.</li></ul><h4>7. How the examiner&#x27;s question should be answered</h4><p>Begin any CRPD answer with the models of disability, because the examiner is testing whether you understand the conceptual shift rather than whether you can list articles. Then take the specific issue: if it concerns access, distinguish accessibility, universal design and reasonable accommodation and say which is engaged. If it concerns decision-making, distinguish mental from legal capacity and set out the supported decision-making model with the objection and reply. Throughout, apply the participation principle: ask whether persons with disabilities were consulted, because the Convention treats that as a substantive requirement and not a courtesy.</p><h4>Summary</h4><ul><li>The CRPD adopts the human rights model, locating disability in the interaction between impairment and barriers.</li><li>Accessibility and universal design are anticipatory duties owed to all; reasonable accommodation is individual, immediate, and its denial is discrimination.</li><li>Article 12 distinguishes mental from legal capacity and requires supported decision-making in place of guardianship.</li><li>Article 19 grounds deinstitutionalisation and Article 24 requires inclusive rather than segregated education.</li><li>Articles 4(3) and 33(3) make participation of persons with disabilities a legal obligation in law-making and monitoring.</li></ul><h4>Key Terms</h4><div class=\"table-wrap\"><table class=\"study-table\"><thead><tr><th>Term</th><th>Meaning</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Human rights model of disability</td><td>The approach locating disability in barriers and rights denial, treating the person as a rights holder.</td></tr><tr><td>Reasonable accommodation</td><td>An individual modification not imposing disproportionate burden, whose denial is discrimination.</td></tr><tr><td>Universal design</td><td>Design usable by all to the greatest extent possible without adaptation.</td></tr><tr><td>Legal capacity</td><td>Recognition as a holder of rights and an actor in law, distinct from mental capacity.</td></tr><tr><td>Supported decision-making</td><td>Assistance enabling a person to exercise legal capacity according to their own will and preferences.</td></tr></tbody></table></div><h4>Review Questions</h4><h5>Multiple choice</h5><div class=\"study-mcq\"><p class=\"mcq-q\">A state says it will make all courts accessible over ten years, and on that basis refuses an interpreter to a deaf litigant next month. What is wrong?</p><ol type=\"A\"><li>Reasonable accommodation is an immediate individual duty and cannot be deferred by a progressive accessibility plan</li><li>Nothing; accessibility is progressive</li><li>The litigant must fund the interpreter</li><li>Article 13 does not apply to civil cases</li></ol><details class=\"mcq-a\"><summary>Show answer</summary><p><strong>Answer: A.</strong> Accessibility is anticipatory and owed to the group; reasonable accommodation is reactive, individual and immediate, and its denial is discrimination.</p></details></div><div class=\"study-mcq\"><p class=\"mcq-q\">What distinction does General Comment 1 draw?</p><ol type=\"A\"><li>Between mental capacity as a decision-making skill and legal capacity as recognition as a rights holder</li><li>Between physical and intellectual disability</li><li>Between public and private discrimination</li><li>Between accessibility and universal design</li></ol><details class=\"mcq-a\"><summary>Show answer</summary><p><strong>Answer: A.</strong> The Committee holds that impaired mental capacity may never justify removing legal capacity, because that conflates two different things.</p></details></div><div class=\"study-mcq\"><p class=\"mcq-q\">Which phrase in CRPD Article 1 carries the social and human rights model?</p><ol type=\"A\"><li>&#x27;in interaction with various barriers&#x27;</li><li>&#x27;long-term impairments&#x27;</li><li>&#x27;on an equal basis with others&#x27;</li><li>&#x27;full and effective participation&#x27;</li></ol><details class=\"mcq-a\"><summary>Show answer</summary><p><strong>Answer: A.</strong> It locates disability in the interaction between impairment and environment rather than in the person.</p></details></div><div class=\"study-mcq\"><p class=\"mcq-q\">What do Articles 4(3) and 33(3) require?</p><ol type=\"A\"><li>Close consultation with and active involvement of persons with disabilities in law-making and monitoring</li><li>Annual reporting to the General Assembly</li><li>Establishment of specialised disability courts</li><li>A quota of persons with disabilities in public employment</li></ol><details class=\"mcq-a\"><summary>Show answer</summary><p><strong>Answer: A.</strong> They convert the movement&#x27;s slogan &#x27;nothing about us without us&#x27; into a legal obligation, which is unusual among human rights treaties.</p></details></div><h5>Short answer</h5><div class=\"study-sa\"><p class=\"sa-q\">Distinguish accessibility, universal design and reasonable accommodation.</p><details class=\"sa-a\"><summary>Model answer</summary><p>Accessibility under Article 9 is an anticipatory duty owed to persons with disabilities as a group, requiring the state to make the physical environment, transport, information and communications usable on an equal basis with others; it is achieved over time but requires immediate steps. Universal design under Article 2 is the design standard applied in advance so that products, environments and services are usable by all to the greatest extent possible without adaptation. Reasonable accommodation under Article 2 is an individual, immediate duty to make a necessary modification in a particular case, limited only by disproportionate or undue burden, and its denial constitutes discrimination.</p></details></div><div class=\"study-sa\"><p class=\"sa-q\">Explain supported decision-making and one objection to it.</p><details class=\"sa-a\"><summary>Model answer</summary><p>Supported decision-making leaves legal capacity with the person and provides whatever assistance they need to exercise it, so decisions rest on the person&#x27;s own will and preferences rather than on someone else&#x27;s judgement of their best interests. It replaces guardianship, which the CRPD Committee holds must be abolished. The main objection is that some situations, such as a person who is unconscious or who cannot communicate any preference, appear to require a decision to be made for them. The Committee&#x27;s reply is that even in those cases the decision must rest on the best interpretation of the person&#x27;s will and preferences, drawn from their known values and history, rather than on an objective best interests test.</p></details></div><h4>Class Activity</h4><ol><li>Audit one public building for accessibility. List the barriers you find, classify each as a failure of accessibility or of universal design, and estimate what a reasonable accommodation would look like for a specific individual.</li><li>Research whether your country still permits guardianship. If so, describe the procedure and write a short proposal for replacing it with a supported decision-making scheme.</li></ol><h4>Assignment</h4><p>Write two pages on the CRPD. Explain the four models of disability and identify which the Convention adopts, distinguish accessibility, universal design and reasonable accommodation with examples, analyse Article 12 including the mental and legal capacity distinction and the objection to abolishing guardianship, summarise the other key obligations, and explain how the participation principle operates as a legal requirement.</p><p class=\"study-print\"><strong>Printable:</strong> use your browser\u2019s Print \u2192 Save as PDF to keep an offline copy.</p></div>",
  "Refugees, Asylum and Non-Refoulement": "<div class=\"study-note\"><div class=\"revision-banner\"><strong>Human Rights and International Humanitarian Law</strong><span>Module 4: Thematic Human Rights Treaties</span></div><h3 class=\"lesson-note-title\">LESSON NOTE: REFUGEES, ASYLUM AND NON-REFOULEMENT</h3><div class=\"table-wrap\"><table class=\"study-table\"><thead><tr><th>Subject</th><th>Topic</th><th>Level</th><th>Lesson duration</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Human Rights and International Humanitarian Law</td><td>Refugees, Asylum and Non-Refoulement</td><td>Beginner to Advanced</td><td>45 minutes</td></tr></tbody></table></div><h4>Learning objectives</h4><p>By the end of this lesson you should be able to:</p><ul><li>State the refugee definition and its five elements</li><li>Explain non-refoulement, its scope and its exceptions</li><li>Distinguish refugees from other categories of displaced person</li><li>Describe the regional extensions and the cessation and exclusion clauses</li></ul><h4>1. Introduction</h4><p>Refugee law is a distinct regime with its own treaty, its own agency and its own definition, but it interlocks with human rights law at every point. The most important interlock is non-refoulement, which exists in two forms with different scopes, and confusing them is the commonest error in this field.</p><h4>2. The refugee definition</h4><div class=\"study-defn\"><strong>Refugee:</strong> A person who, owing to well-founded fear of being persecuted for reasons of race, religion, nationality, membership of a particular social group or political opinion, is outside the country of their nationality and is unable or, owing to such fear, unwilling to avail themselves of the protection of that country (1951 Convention Article 1A(2), as amended by the 1967 Protocol).</div><div class=\"table-wrap\"><table class=\"study-table\"><thead><tr><th>Element</th><th>What must be shown</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Well-founded fear</td><td>Both a subjective fear and an objective basis; the standard is a reasonable possibility, not a probability</td></tr><tr><td>Persecution</td><td>Serious harm plus a failure of state protection; undefined in the Convention but read as including threats to life or freedom and other serious human rights violations</td></tr><tr><td>Convention ground</td><td>Race, religion, nationality, membership of a particular social group, or political opinion, with a causal nexus to the persecution</td></tr><tr><td>Outside the country of nationality</td><td>The alienage requirement; a person still inside their own country is not a Convention refugee</td></tr><tr><td>Unable or unwilling to avail of protection</td><td>State protection is unavailable or the fear makes seeking it unreasonable</td></tr></tbody></table></div><p>The 1967 Protocol removed the original temporal and geographic limits, which had restricted the Convention to events occurring in Europe before 1951.</p><div class=\"study-callout\"><strong>Note:</strong> 'Membership of a particular social group' is the ground that has developed most. It has been held to include women fearing gender-based persecution, people persecuted for sexual orientation or gender identity, families, and in some jurisdictions those fleeing forced recruitment. The standard test asks whether the group shares an innate or unchangeable characteristic, or one so fundamental that no one should be required to change it, and whether the group is perceived as distinct in the society.</div><h4>3. Non-refoulement: two regimes</h4><div class=\"table-wrap\"><table class=\"study-table\"><thead><tr><th></th><th>Refugee law (1951 Convention Art 33)</th><th>Human rights law (CAT Art 3, ICCPR Art 7)</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Protects against</td><td>Return to threats to life or freedom on a Convention ground</td><td>Return to a real risk of torture, cruel treatment or other irreparable harm</td></tr><tr><td>Who is covered</td><td>Refugees, including those not yet formally recognised</td><td>Anyone, regardless of status or conduct</td></tr><tr><td>Exceptions</td><td>Art 33(2): reasonable grounds for regarding the person as a danger to security, or convicted of a particularly serious crime and a danger to the community</td><td>None; absolute</td></tr><tr><td>Source</td><td>Treaty and customary law</td><td>Treaty, customary law, and jus cogens as regards torture</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"study-callout\"><strong>Note:</strong> This table answers most exam questions in this area. A person excluded from refugee status under Article 1F, or subject to the Article 33(2) exception, may still not be returned if they face a real risk of torture, because the human rights prohibition has no exceptions. The practical consequence is that such a person may be unremovable while also being denied refugee status, which is a real and unresolved problem in state practice.</div><h4>4. Exclusion and cessation</h4><p>Article 1F excludes from refugee status anyone for whom there are serious reasons for considering that they have committed a crime against peace, a war crime or a crime against humanity; a serious non-political crime outside the country of refuge prior to admission; or acts contrary to the purposes and principles of the United Nations. Exclusion is not punishment and does not require a conviction, but the standard of 'serious reasons for considering' requires clear and credible evidence.</p><p>Article 1C sets out cessation: refugee status ends where the person voluntarily re-avails of national protection, reacquires nationality, acquires a new nationality, voluntarily re-establishes in the country of feared persecution, or where the circumstances in connection with which they were recognised have ceased to exist. The last of these, the ceased circumstances clause, requires that the change be fundamental, stable and durable, and it admits an exception for compelling reasons arising out of previous persecution.</p><h4>5. Regional extensions</h4><div class=\"table-wrap\"><table class=\"study-table\"><thead><tr><th>Instrument</th><th>Additional coverage</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>OAU Convention 1969 (Africa)</td><td>Adds every person compelled to leave by external aggression, occupation, foreign domination or events seriously disturbing public order in part or the whole of the country of origin</td></tr><tr><td>Cartagena Declaration 1984 (Latin America)</td><td>Adds those fleeing generalised violence, foreign aggression, internal conflicts, massive violation of human rights or other circumstances seriously disturbing public order</td></tr><tr><td>EU Qualification Directive</td><td>Adds subsidiary protection for those facing a real risk of serious harm, including indiscriminate violence in armed conflict</td></tr></tbody></table></div><p>The African definition is significant for this course. It removes the need to prove individual targeting on a Convention ground: a person fleeing events seriously disturbing public order qualifies. That covers most conflict displacement in the region, which the 1951 definition alone does not.</p><h4>6. Related categories</h4><div class=\"table-wrap\"><table class=\"study-table\"><thead><tr><th>Category</th><th>Definition</th><th>Key instrument</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Asylum seeker</td><td>A person seeking recognition as a refugee whose claim has not yet been determined</td><td>Same protections as a refugee pending determination, since recognition is declaratory</td></tr><tr><td>Internally displaced person</td><td>Forced to flee but has not crossed an international border</td><td>Guiding Principles on Internal Displacement 1998; Kampala Convention 2009 in Africa, the first binding IDP treaty</td></tr><tr><td>Stateless person</td><td>Not considered as a national by any state under the operation of its law</td><td>1954 and 1961 Statelessness Conventions</td></tr><tr><td>Migrant</td><td>A broad term for a person moving; not a legal status conferring protection</td><td>Migrant Workers Convention 1990 for a subset</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"study-callout\"><strong>Note:</strong> Recognition of refugee status is declaratory, not constitutive. A person becomes a refugee when the criteria are met, not when a state says so. It follows that an asylum seeker whose claim is pending must be treated as a possible refugee and protected by non-refoulement in the meantime. This is a frequently examined point.</div><h4>7. Core rights of refugees</h4><ul><li>Article 3: non-discrimination as to race, religion or country of origin.</li><li>Article 16: access to courts.</li><li>Articles 17 to 19: wage-earning employment, self-employment and the liberal professions.</li><li>Articles 21 to 22: housing and public education, with elementary education on the same terms as nationals.</li><li>Article 26: freedom of movement within the territory.</li><li>Article 27 and 28: identity papers and Convention travel documents.</li><li>Article 31: no penalties for illegal entry where the refugee comes directly from a territory where their life or freedom was threatened, presents themselves without delay and shows good cause.</li><li>Article 34: states shall as far as possible facilitate naturalisation.</li></ul><h4>8. How the examiner&#x27;s question should be answered</h4><p>For a problem question, work through the five elements of the definition in order and reach a conclusion on each rather than treating them as a single impression. Then check the applicable regional definition, which in Africa may resolve a case the 1951 definition would not. Then check exclusion under Article 1F. Then, separately and always, ask whether human rights non-refoulement applies, because it survives both exclusion and the Article 33(2) exception. Finish with the practical consequence for the individual, which is the part weaker answers omit.</p><h4>Summary</h4><ul><li>The refugee definition has five elements: well-founded fear, persecution, a Convention ground, alienage, and lack of state protection.</li><li>Refugee status is declaratory, so asylum seekers are protected by non-refoulement pending determination.</li><li>Refugee law non-refoulement has exceptions under Article 33(2); human rights non-refoulement under CAT Article 3 has none.</li><li>Article 1F excludes those with serious reasons for considering they committed grave crimes, but exclusion does not permit return to torture.</li><li>The OAU Convention and Cartagena Declaration extend the definition to those fleeing conflict and events seriously disturbing public order.</li><li>Internally displaced persons are covered by the Guiding Principles and, in Africa, by the binding Kampala Convention.</li></ul><h4>Key Terms</h4><div class=\"table-wrap\"><table class=\"study-table\"><thead><tr><th>Term</th><th>Meaning</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Non-refoulement</td><td>The prohibition on returning a person to a place where they face persecution or a real risk of irreparable harm.</td></tr><tr><td>Particular social group</td><td>A Convention ground covering groups sharing an innate or fundamental characteristic and perceived as distinct.</td></tr><tr><td>Exclusion clause</td><td>Article 1F&#x27;s bar on refugee status for those with serious reasons for considering they committed grave crimes.</td></tr><tr><td>Declaratory recognition</td><td>The principle that a person is a refugee once the criteria are met, not when a state so decides.</td></tr><tr><td>Kampala Convention</td><td>The 2009 African Union treaty on protection and assistance of internally displaced persons.</td></tr></tbody></table></div><h4>Review Questions</h4><h5>Multiple choice</h5><div class=\"study-mcq\"><p class=\"mcq-q\">A person is excluded from refugee status under Article 1F but faces a real risk of torture on return. What follows?</p><ol type=\"A\"><li>They may not be returned, because human rights non-refoulement has no exceptions</li><li>They may be returned, since exclusion removes all protection</li><li>They must be prosecuted before removal</li><li>They automatically receive subsidiary protection</li></ol><details class=\"mcq-a\"><summary>Show answer</summary><p><strong>Answer: A.</strong> CAT Article 3 is absolute, so the person may be unremovable while also being denied refugee status.</p></details></div><div class=\"study-mcq\"><p class=\"mcq-q\">What does the OAU Convention add to the refugee definition?</p><ol type=\"A\"><li>Persons compelled to leave by aggression, occupation, foreign domination or events seriously disturbing public order</li><li>A requirement of individual targeting</li><li>A time limit on claims</li><li>An exclusion for economic hardship</li></ol><details class=\"mcq-a\"><summary>Show answer</summary><p><strong>Answer: A.</strong> It removes the need to prove individual persecution on a Convention ground, covering most conflict displacement in the region.</p></details></div><div class=\"study-mcq\"><p class=\"mcq-q\">Why must an asylum seeker be protected by non-refoulement before their claim is decided?</p><ol type=\"A\"><li>Because recognition of refugee status is declaratory, not constitutive</li><li>Because all migrants are protected</li><li>Because the Convention says so expressly for asylum seekers</li><li>Because states waive their rights during processing</li></ol><details class=\"mcq-a\"><summary>Show answer</summary><p><strong>Answer: A.</strong> A person becomes a refugee when the criteria are met; the determination merely confirms it, so returning them before determination risks refoulement.</p></details></div><div class=\"study-mcq\"><p class=\"mcq-q\">Under Article 31, when may a refugee not be penalised for illegal entry?</p><ol type=\"A\"><li>Where they come directly from a territory of threat, present themselves without delay and show good cause</li><li>In all circumstances</li><li>Only if they entered by air</li><li>Only after formal recognition</li></ol><details class=\"mcq-a\"><summary>Show answer</summary><p><strong>Answer: A.</strong> The provision recognises that a person fleeing persecution frequently cannot obtain a visa or travel documents.</p></details></div><h5>Short answer</h5><div class=\"study-sa\"><p class=\"sa-q\">Set out the five elements of the 1951 Convention refugee definition.</p><details class=\"sa-a\"><summary>Model answer</summary><p>First, a well-founded fear, requiring both a subjective fear and an objective basis, assessed on a reasonable possibility standard rather than a balance of probabilities. Second, persecution, meaning serious harm coupled with a failure of state protection. Third, a nexus to one of five Convention grounds: race, religion, nationality, membership of a particular social group, or political opinion. Fourth, alienage: the person must be outside their country of nationality. Fifth, being unable or, owing to that fear, unwilling to avail themselves of that country&#x27;s protection.</p></details></div><div class=\"study-sa\"><p class=\"sa-q\">Compare non-refoulement under refugee law and under human rights law.</p><details class=\"sa-a\"><summary>Model answer</summary><p>Under Article 33 of the 1951 Convention, a refugee may not be returned to a territory where their life or freedom would be threatened on a Convention ground, but Article 33(2) creates exceptions for a person who is a danger to national security or who, having been convicted of a particularly serious crime, is a danger to the community. Under CAT Article 3 and ICCPR Article 7 the prohibition covers anyone, regardless of status or conduct, whenever there is a real risk of torture or other irreparable harm, and it admits no exception at all. The practical consequence is that a person excluded or excepted under refugee law may still be unreturnable under human rights law.</p></details></div><h4>Class Activity</h4><ol><li>Take a real asylum case reported in the media. Apply the five elements of the definition in order and state your conclusion on each, then say whether the regional definition would change the result.</li><li>Compare the 1951 definition with the OAU Convention definition line by line. Write one paragraph on which cases the African definition covers that the 1951 definition does not.</li></ol><h4>Assignment</h4><p>Write two pages on refugees, asylum and non-refoulement. Set out the refugee definition element by element, explain the two non-refoulement regimes and the practical consequence where they diverge, describe exclusion and cessation, summarise the regional extensions with particular attention to the OAU Convention, distinguish refugees from internally displaced and stateless persons, and list the core rights the Convention confers.</p><p class=\"study-print\"><strong>Printable:</strong> use your browser\u2019s Print \u2192 Save as PDF to keep an offline copy.</p></div>",
  "Enforced Disappearance and Migrant Workers": "<div class=\"study-note\"><div class=\"revision-banner\"><strong>Human Rights and International Humanitarian Law</strong><span>Module 4: Thematic Human Rights Treaties</span></div><h3 class=\"lesson-note-title\">LESSON NOTE: ENFORCED DISAPPEARANCE AND MIGRANT WORKERS</h3><div class=\"table-wrap\"><table class=\"study-table\"><thead><tr><th>Subject</th><th>Topic</th><th>Level</th><th>Lesson duration</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Human Rights and International Humanitarian Law</td><td>Enforced Disappearance and Migrant Workers</td><td>Beginner to Advanced</td><td>40 minutes</td></tr></tbody></table></div><h4>Learning objectives</h4><p>By the end of this lesson you should be able to:</p><ul><li>Define enforced disappearance and identify its three elements</li><li>Explain why it is treated as a continuing violation and a multiple violation</li><li>Describe the obligations under the 2006 Convention</li><li>Set out the structure and main protections of the Migrant Workers Convention</li></ul><h4>1. Introduction</h4><p>This lesson covers two of the less studied core treaties. They are grouped here because both address groups whose defining problem is invisibility: the disappeared, whose fate the state denies, and migrant workers, whose irregular status is used to argue they are outside the protection of law. In both cases the treaty's central move is to insist that the person remains a rights holder.</p><h4>2. Enforced disappearance: the definition</h4><div class=\"study-defn\"><strong>Enforced disappearance:</strong> The arrest, detention, abduction or any other form of deprivation of liberty by agents of the State or by persons or groups acting with the authorisation, support or acquiescence of the State, followed by a refusal to acknowledge the deprivation of liberty or by concealment of the fate or whereabouts of the disappeared person, which places such a person outside the protection of the law (Convention Article 2).</div><div class=\"table-wrap\"><table class=\"study-table\"><thead><tr><th>Element</th><th>Requirement</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Deprivation of liberty</td><td>In any form: arrest, detention, abduction or otherwise</td></tr><tr><td>State involvement</td><td>By state agents, or by others acting with authorisation, support or acquiescence</td></tr><tr><td>Refusal to acknowledge or concealment</td><td>The distinguishing feature; a lawful but unacknowledged detention is a disappearance</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"study-callout\"><strong>Note:</strong> Article 3 requires states also to investigate disappearances committed by persons or groups acting without state authorisation. So conduct by a purely private armed group is not an enforced disappearance in the Article 2 sense, but it generates an investigation obligation. Distinguishing Article 2 from Article 3 is a reliable examination point.</div><h4>3. A continuing and multiple violation</h4><p>Two doctrinal features make enforced disappearance distinctive.</p><ol><li><strong>It is a continuing violation.</strong> The offence continues for as long as the fate and whereabouts remain concealed. This has two consequences: limitation periods do not begin to run, and a treaty body may have jurisdiction over a disappearance that began before the treaty entered into force for the state, because the violation continues into the period of jurisdiction.</li><li><strong>It violates multiple rights at once.</strong> Liberty and security, the prohibition of torture, recognition as a person before the law, the right to life where the person is killed, and the right to an effective remedy. It also violates the rights of the family: the Human Rights Committee and regional courts have repeatedly held that the anguish of relatives kept in ignorance is itself inhuman treatment.</li></ol><div class=\"study-defn\"><strong>Right to the truth:</strong> The right of victims and their families to know the truth about the circumstances of the disappearance, the progress and results of the investigation, and the fate of the disappeared person (Convention Article 24(2)).</div><p>Article 24(1) defines a victim as the disappeared person and any individual who has suffered harm as the direct result of an enforced disappearance. That formulation makes the family victims in their own right, not merely representatives of the disappeared person.</p><h4>4. Obligations under the 2006 Convention</h4><div class=\"table-wrap\"><table class=\"study-table\"><thead><tr><th>Article</th><th>Obligation</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>1</td><td>Absolute prohibition; no exceptional circumstances whatsoever may be invoked</td></tr><tr><td>4-7</td><td>Criminalise enforced disappearance; appropriate penalties; superior responsibility; no superior orders defence</td></tr><tr><td>5</td><td>Widespread or systematic practice constitutes a crime against humanity</td></tr><tr><td>8</td><td>If limitation applies at all, it must be lengthy and start only when the offence ceases</td></tr><tr><td>9-11</td><td>Jurisdiction including universal jurisdiction; extradite or prosecute</td></tr><tr><td>16</td><td>Non-refoulement where there is a danger of enforced disappearance</td></tr><tr><td>17</td><td>Prohibition of secret detention; official registers of all persons deprived of liberty with specified minimum information</td></tr><tr><td>18-19</td><td>Access to information for relatives and counsel; protection of personal data</td></tr><tr><td>24</td><td>Rights of victims: truth, reparation, and prompt, fair and adequate compensation</td></tr><tr><td>25</td><td>Protection of children subjected to disappearance or whose parents were disappeared, including annulment of wrongful adoptions</td></tr></tbody></table></div><p>The Committee on Enforced Disappearances has an unusual power. Article 30 allows an urgent action request: relatives may ask the Committee to seek and find a disappeared person, and the Committee transmits the request to the state with a deadline for a response. This operates outside the ordinary complaints procedure and is designed for speed.</p><h4>5. The Migrant Workers Convention</h4><div class=\"study-defn\"><strong>Migrant worker:</strong> A person who is to be engaged, is engaged or has been engaged in a remunerated activity in a State of which they are not a national (ICRMW Article 2).</div><p>The Convention was adopted in 1990 and entered into force in 2003. It has around sixty parties, the fewest of any core treaty, and almost all are states of origin rather than states of employment. That imbalance is the central fact about it and any answer should say so.</p><div class=\"table-wrap\"><table class=\"study-table\"><thead><tr><th>Part</th><th>Coverage</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>III (Arts 8-35)</td><td>Rights of all migrant workers and members of their families, whatever their status, including those in an irregular situation</td></tr><tr><td>IV (Arts 36-56)</td><td>Additional rights of migrant workers in a regular situation</td></tr><tr><td>V (Arts 57-63)</td><td>Provisions for particular categories: frontier, seasonal, itinerant, project-tied, specified-employment and self-employed workers</td></tr><tr><td>VI (Arts 64-71)</td><td>Promotion of sound, equitable and lawful conditions for international migration</td></tr></tbody></table></div><p>Part III is the heart of the Convention. It applies irrespective of immigration status and includes the following.</p><ul><li>Freedom from slavery, forced labour, torture and inhuman treatment.</li><li>Equality with nationals before courts and tribunals, and in respect of remuneration and conditions of work.</li><li>Prohibition of collective expulsion; each case must be examined and decided individually.</li><li>Prohibition of arbitrary deprivation of property and of unlawful confiscation of identity documents by anyone other than an authorised official.</li><li>The right to emergency medical care and access of children to education, both regardless of status.</li><li>The right to transfer earnings and savings on termination of stay.</li><li>Article 68 obliges states to act against irregular migration while Article 69 requires them to consider regularising the situation of those already present.</li></ul><div class=\"study-callout\"><strong>Note:</strong> Article 25(3) makes a subtle and important point: states must ensure that migrant workers are not deprived of rights derived from the equality principle by reason of irregularity in their stay, and in particular employers are not relieved of any legal or contractual obligations by that irregularity. In plain terms, an employer cannot use a worker's irregular status as a reason not to pay them.</div><h4>6. Why ratification is low</h4><ol><li>Destination states fear that the Convention limits their control over immigration policy, though it expressly preserves the right of states to determine admission.</li><li>Part III's protection of irregular migrants is politically difficult in states with large irregular populations.</li><li>Some destination states argue their existing labour law already covers migrant workers, which is true in law and frequently untrue in practice.</li><li>The Convention is long and technical, with 93 articles, which raises the cost of a ratification review.</li><li>The result is a treaty ratified mainly by countries whose nationals migrate, so its obligations bind those least able to protect them.</li></ol><h4>7. How the examiner&#x27;s question should be answered</h4><p>On enforced disappearance, always state the three elements, always say it is a continuing violation and explain both consequences, and always identify the family as victims in their own right. On migrant workers, the examiner is usually testing whether you know Part III applies regardless of status and whether you can explain the ratification imbalance. Do not simply list the rights: explain why a treaty with near-complete protection on paper has almost no purchase where the workers actually are.</p><h4>Summary</h4><ul><li>Enforced disappearance requires deprivation of liberty, state involvement, and refusal to acknowledge or concealment of fate.</li><li>It is a continuing violation, so limitation does not run and jurisdiction can extend to disappearances beginning before entry into force.</li><li>It violates multiple rights at once and the family are victims in their own right, with a right to the truth.</li><li>The 2006 Convention prohibits secret detention, requires registers of detainees, and provides an urgent action procedure.</li><li>The Migrant Workers Convention protects all migrant workers regardless of status in Part III and adds rights for those regular in Part IV.</li><li>It is the least ratified core treaty and its parties are overwhelmingly states of origin rather than employment.</li></ul><h4>Key Terms</h4><div class=\"table-wrap\"><table class=\"study-table\"><thead><tr><th>Term</th><th>Meaning</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Enforced disappearance</td><td>Deprivation of liberty by or with state involvement followed by refusal to acknowledge it or concealment of the person&#x27;s fate.</td></tr><tr><td>Continuing violation</td><td>A violation that persists until the concealed fate is clarified, so limitation does not begin to run.</td></tr><tr><td>Right to the truth</td><td>The right of victims and families to know the circumstances, the progress of the investigation and the fate of the disappeared.</td></tr><tr><td>Urgent action</td><td>The Committee&#x27;s Article 30 procedure requesting a state to seek and find a disappeared person.</td></tr><tr><td>Migrant worker</td><td>A person engaged in remunerated activity in a state of which they are not a national.</td></tr></tbody></table></div><h4>Review Questions</h4><h5>Multiple choice</h5><div class=\"study-mcq\"><p class=\"mcq-q\">Which element distinguishes enforced disappearance from ordinary unlawful detention?</p><ol type=\"A\"><li>Refusal to acknowledge the detention or concealment of the person&#x27;s fate</li><li>Use of force at the point of arrest</li><li>The length of the detention</li><li>The absence of a warrant</li></ol><details class=\"mcq-a\"><summary>Show answer</summary><p><strong>Answer: A.</strong> A detention that is lawful in form but unacknowledged is still a disappearance, because concealment places the person outside the protection of the law.</p></details></div><div class=\"study-mcq\"><p class=\"mcq-q\">Why does limitation not run in a case of enforced disappearance?</p><ol type=\"A\"><li>The violation continues until the fate and whereabouts are clarified</li><li>Disappearance is a crime against humanity in all cases</li><li>There is no limitation for any human rights violation</li><li>The treaty abolishes limitation entirely</li></ol><details class=\"mcq-a\"><summary>Show answer</summary><p><strong>Answer: A.</strong> Article 8 provides that where limitation applies at all, it must be lengthy and start only when the offence ceases.</p></details></div><div class=\"study-mcq\"><p class=\"mcq-q\">What is the status of the family of a disappeared person?</p><ol type=\"A\"><li>Victims in their own right, with a right to the truth and reparation</li><li>Witnesses only</li><li>Representatives of the disappeared person alone</li><li>They have standing only after a death certificate is issued</li></ol><details class=\"mcq-a\"><summary>Show answer</summary><p><strong>Answer: A.</strong> Article 24(1) defines a victim as the disappeared person and any individual who has suffered harm as a direct result.</p></details></div><div class=\"study-mcq\"><p class=\"mcq-q\">Which part of the Migrant Workers Convention applies regardless of immigration status?</p><ol type=\"A\"><li>Part III</li><li>Part IV</li><li>Part V</li><li>Part VI</li></ol><details class=\"mcq-a\"><summary>Show answer</summary><p><strong>Answer: A.</strong> Part III covers all migrant workers and their families including those in an irregular situation; Part IV adds rights for those in a regular situation.</p></details></div><h5>Short answer</h5><div class=\"study-sa\"><p class=\"sa-q\">Explain why enforced disappearance is described as a multiple violation.</p><details class=\"sa-a\"><summary>Model answer</summary><p>A single act of enforced disappearance breaches several rights simultaneously. It violates liberty and security of person, since the detention is unlawful and unacknowledged. It violates the prohibition of torture and inhuman treatment, both for the disappeared person held incommunicado and, as treaty bodies and regional courts have repeatedly held, for the relatives kept in ignorance of their fate. It violates recognition as a person before the law, which is the express language of the Article 2 definition. Where the person is killed it violates the right to life. And it violates the right to an effective remedy, since the state&#x27;s denial forecloses any legal challenge.</p></details></div><div class=\"study-sa\"><p class=\"sa-q\">Why has the Migrant Workers Convention attracted so few ratifications?</p><details class=\"sa-a\"><summary>Model answer</summary><p>Destination states fear it constrains immigration control, even though it expressly preserves their power to determine admission. Part III&#x27;s protection of migrants in an irregular situation is politically difficult where irregular populations are large. Some states argue their general labour law already covers migrant workers, which is formally true but frequently untrue in practice. The instrument is also long and technical at 93 articles, raising the cost of ratification review. The result is that the parties are overwhelmingly states of origin, so the obligations fall on countries whose nationals are abroad rather than on the states where the workers actually are.</p></details></div><h4>Class Activity</h4><ol><li>Find one documented case of enforced disappearance from any region. Identify each of the three elements in the facts and explain what the family&#x27;s remedy would be under the 2006 Convention.</li><li>Check whether your country has ratified the Migrant Workers Convention. Write a short brief either arguing for ratification or, if it has ratified, assessing one Part III right against the actual situation of migrant workers there.</li></ol><h4>Assignment</h4><p>Write one and a half pages covering both treaties. Define enforced disappearance with its three elements, explain why it is a continuing and multiple violation and what follows from each, summarise the obligations in the 2006 Convention including the urgent action procedure, then set out the structure of the Migrant Workers Convention, explain what Part III protects regardless of status, and account for the treaty&#x27;s low ratification.</p><p class=\"study-print\"><strong>Printable:</strong> use your browser\u2019s Print \u2192 Save as PDF to keep an offline copy.</p></div>",
  "The Rights of Indigenous Peoples": "<div class=\"study-note\"><div class=\"revision-banner\"><strong>Human Rights and International Humanitarian Law</strong><span>Module 4: Thematic Human Rights Treaties</span></div><h3 class=\"lesson-note-title\">LESSON NOTE: THE RIGHTS OF INDIGENOUS PEOPLES</h3><div class=\"table-wrap\"><table class=\"study-table\"><thead><tr><th>Subject</th><th>Topic</th><th>Level</th><th>Lesson duration</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Human Rights and International Humanitarian Law</td><td>The Rights of Indigenous Peoples</td><td>Beginner to Advanced</td><td>40 minutes</td></tr></tbody></table></div><h4>Learning objectives</h4><p>By the end of this lesson you should be able to:</p><ul><li>Explain why indigenous rights required a separate framework</li><li>Describe UNDRIP and ILO Convention 169 and their legal status</li><li>Explain free, prior and informed consent and its scope</li><li>Describe collective land rights and the leading jurisprudence</li></ul><h4>1. Introduction</h4><p>Indigenous peoples' rights are the clearest case in which the individual rights framework proved insufficient. The rights at stake are held collectively, attach to territory, and concern the survival of a people as a people. That required a distinct set of instruments, and it generated the sharpest disputes about self-determination in the whole system.</p><h4>2. Who are indigenous peoples?</h4><p>No instrument defines the term, and this is deliberate. The Martinez Cobo study and ILO Convention 169 instead identify criteria.</p><ul><li>Historical continuity with pre-invasion or pre-colonial societies on their territories.</li><li>Distinct social, economic, cultural or political institutions retained in whole or in part.</li><li>Non-dominance in the present society.</li><li>A determination to preserve, develop and transmit ancestral territories and identity to future generations.</li><li>Self-identification as indigenous, treated as a fundamental criterion, together with recognition and acceptance by the group.</li></ul><div class=\"study-callout\"><strong>Note:</strong> Self-identification is the governing criterion, which is why no definition was adopted: a definition drafted by states would have allowed states to define groups out of existence. In Africa the African Commission's Working Group has adapted the criteria, emphasising self-identification, a special attachment to and use of traditional land, and experience of subjugation, marginalisation or dispossession, because the historical-continuity criterion fits the African context imperfectly.</div><h4>3. The two principal instruments</h4><div class=\"table-wrap\"><table class=\"study-table\"><thead><tr><th></th><th>ILO Convention 169 (1989)</th><th>UNDRIP (2007)</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Form</td><td>Treaty</td><td>General Assembly declaration</td></tr><tr><td>Binding</td><td>Yes, on its 24 parties</td><td>Not as such; parts reflect or are crystallising into custom</td></tr><tr><td>Drafted by</td><td>States and social partners within the ILO</td><td>Extensive participation by indigenous peoples over more than twenty years</td></tr><tr><td>Consent standard</td><td>Consultation with the objective of achieving agreement or consent</td><td>Free, prior and informed consent</td></tr><tr><td>Self-determination</td><td>Avoided; uses 'peoples' with a disclaimer in Art 1(3)</td><td>Art 3 affirms the right of self-determination; Art 4 specifies autonomy in internal affairs; Art 46 preserves territorial integrity</td></tr><tr><td>Reach</td><td>Limited ratification, concentrated in Latin America</td><td>Adopted 143 to 4; the four opposing states have since endorsed it</td></tr></tbody></table></div><p>The relationship between UNDRIP Articles 3 and 46 is the compromise that made adoption possible. Article 3 grants self-determination in the same words as common Article 1 of the Covenants. Article 46 states that nothing may be construed as authorising or encouraging any action which would dismember or impair the territorial integrity or political unity of sovereign and independent states. The settlement is internal self-determination: autonomy and self-government within the state, not secession.</p><h4>4. Free, prior and informed consent</h4><div class=\"study-defn\"><strong>Free, prior and informed consent:</strong> The requirement that indigenous peoples give consent, freely, in advance of authorisation or commencement, and on the basis of full information, to measures affecting them, obtained through their own representative institutions.</div><div class=\"table-wrap\"><table class=\"study-table\"><thead><tr><th>Element</th><th>Requirement</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Free</td><td>No coercion, intimidation, manipulation or inducement; no pressure of deadlines set by the developer</td></tr><tr><td>Prior</td><td>Sought sufficiently in advance of authorisation or commencement, allowing time for the community's own decision process</td></tr><tr><td>Informed</td><td>Full disclosure of the nature, scope, duration, purpose, personnel, locality and likely impacts, in accessible language</td></tr><tr><td>Consent</td><td>A collective decision reached through the peoples' own representative institutions and customary decision-making, not a signature from selected individuals</td></tr></tbody></table></div><p>UNDRIP requires consent expressly in three situations: relocation from lands or territories (Article 10), storage or disposal of hazardous materials on their lands (Article 29(2)), and legislative or administrative measures affecting them (Article 19, which requires consultation to obtain consent). Article 32(2) requires consultation to obtain consent before approving projects affecting lands, territories and resources.</p><div class=\"study-callout\"><strong>Note:</strong> Whether consent means a veto is contested. The Inter-American Court's approach in Saramaka v Suriname is the most cited: consultation is required for all measures, but consent is required for large-scale development or investment projects having a major impact within the territory. That graduated approach, scaling the standard to the impact, is the position to state in an answer.</div><h4>5. Collective land rights</h4><p>Land is not one right among many in this field. It is the substrate of culture, subsistence, spirituality and identity, which is why the jurisprudence treats it as the central issue.</p><ol><li><strong>Awas Tingni v Nicaragua (2001).</strong> The Inter-American Court held that Article 21 of the American Convention, on property, protects communal property held according to indigenous customary tenure, and that the state must delimit, demarcate and title those lands. This was the first international judgment recognising indigenous communal land title.</li><li><strong>Saramaka v Suriname (2007).</strong> Established three safeguards for any concession affecting indigenous territory: effective participation with consultation and, for major projects, consent; a reasonable share of the benefits; and a prior independent environmental and social impact assessment.</li><li><strong>Endorois v Kenya (2009).</strong> The African Commission found that eviction of the Endorois from around Lake Bogoria for a game reserve violated the African Charter rights to property, health, culture, free disposition of natural resources and development. It recognised the Endorois as an indigenous people and required restitution and compensation.</li><li><strong>Ogiek v Kenya (2017 and 2022).</strong> The African Court found violations of the Charter arising from eviction from the Mau Forest and, in its 2022 reparations judgment, ordered restitution, compensation and formal recognition of the Ogiek as an indigenous people, with delimitation and titling of their land.</li></ol><div class=\"study-callout\"><strong>Example:</strong> The pattern across all four cases is the same. A state treats indigenous territory as vacant public land because it is not registered under the state's own tenure system. The tribunal holds that customary occupation itself generates a property right the state must recognise, demarcate and title, and that failing to do so is not a policy choice but a violation. This is the single most useful sentence to be able to write about indigenous land rights.</div><h4>6. Other core protections in UNDRIP</h4><ul><li>Article 8: protection from forced assimilation and destruction of culture, including any action depriving them of integrity as distinct peoples or dispossessing them of lands.</li><li>Article 11 to 13: culture, traditions, spiritual and religious traditions, languages, oral histories, and repatriation of ceremonial objects and human remains.</li><li>Article 14: the right to establish and control their own educational systems in their own languages.</li><li>Article 18 and 20: participation in decision-making through their own representatives, and the right to maintain their own political, economic and social systems.</li><li>Article 24: traditional medicines and health practices, and the right to conservation of vital medicinal plants and animals.</li><li>Article 31: control of cultural heritage, traditional knowledge and traditional cultural expressions.</li><li>Article 37: recognition and enforcement of treaties and agreements concluded with states.</li></ul><h4>7. How the examiner&#x27;s question should be answered</h4><p>Begin by explaining why individual rights were insufficient, because that framing earns the conceptual marks. State the legal status of each instrument precisely: UNDRIP is a declaration whose provisions are variably customary, ILO 169 is binding on its parties. On self-determination, always pair Article 3 with Article 46 and name the result as internal self-determination. On consent, set out the four elements and then give the graduated Saramaka position rather than asserting a flat veto. On land, name at least one African case, since a course in this region should not rely only on Inter-American jurisprudence.</p><h4>Summary</h4><ul><li>No instrument defines indigenous peoples; self-identification is the governing criterion, with criteria rather than a definition.</li><li>ILO Convention 169 is a binding treaty with limited ratification; UNDRIP is a declaration with much broader acceptance.</li><li>UNDRIP Article 3 affirms self-determination and Article 46 preserves territorial integrity, producing internal self-determination.</li><li>Free, prior and informed consent has four elements and is required outright for relocation, hazardous waste and legislative measures.</li><li>Saramaka established a graduated standard: consultation always, consent for large-scale projects with major impact.</li><li>Awas Tingni, Saramaka, Endorois and Ogiek establish that customary occupation generates property rights the state must demarcate and title.</li></ul><h4>Key Terms</h4><div class=\"table-wrap\"><table class=\"study-table\"><thead><tr><th>Term</th><th>Meaning</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Self-identification</td><td>The fundamental criterion for indigenous status, adopted because a state-drafted definition could exclude groups.</td></tr><tr><td>Free, prior and informed consent</td><td>Consent given without coercion, in advance, on full information, through the peoples&#x27; own institutions.</td></tr><tr><td>Internal self-determination</td><td>Autonomy and self-government within an existing state, without a right of secession.</td></tr><tr><td>Communal title</td><td>Property rights arising from customary collective occupation, which the state must delimit, demarcate and register.</td></tr></tbody></table></div><h4>Review Questions</h4><h5>Multiple choice</h5><div class=\"study-mcq\"><p class=\"mcq-q\">What is the fundamental criterion for identifying indigenous peoples?</p><ol type=\"A\"><li>Self-identification, together with acceptance by the group</li><li>A definition in ILO Convention 169</li><li>Registration by the state</li><li>Continuous occupation since before 1500</li></ol><details class=\"mcq-a\"><summary>Show answer</summary><p><strong>Answer: A.</strong> No definition was adopted precisely because a state-drafted one could define groups out of existence.</p></details></div><div class=\"study-mcq\"><p class=\"mcq-q\">How do UNDRIP Articles 3 and 46 fit together?</p><ol type=\"A\"><li>Article 3 affirms self-determination and Article 46 preserves territorial integrity, yielding internal self-determination</li><li>Article 46 repeals Article 3</li><li>Article 3 permits secession in all cases</li><li>They apply to different peoples</li></ol><details class=\"mcq-a\"><summary>Show answer</summary><p><strong>Answer: A.</strong> The compromise that made adoption possible was autonomy and self-government within the state, not a right to secede.</p></details></div><div class=\"study-mcq\"><p class=\"mcq-q\">Under the Saramaka approach, when is consent rather than consultation required?</p><ol type=\"A\"><li>For large-scale development or investment projects with a major impact within the territory</li><li>For every administrative measure</li><li>Never; consultation always suffices</li><li>Only where the state has ratified ILO Convention 169</li></ol><details class=\"mcq-a\"><summary>Show answer</summary><p><strong>Answer: A.</strong> The standard is graduated to the scale of impact rather than being a flat veto over all measures.</p></details></div><div class=\"study-mcq\"><p class=\"mcq-q\">What did the African Commission decide in the Endorois case?</p><ol type=\"A\"><li>Eviction from Lake Bogoria violated the Charter rights to property, health, culture, resources and development, requiring restitution</li><li>That the Endorois were not an indigenous people</li><li>That game reserves override communal title</li><li>That only individual compensation was available</li></ol><details class=\"mcq-a\"><summary>Show answer</summary><p><strong>Answer: A.</strong> It was the first African decision recognising an indigenous people&#x27;s communal land rights under the Charter.</p></details></div><h5>Short answer</h5><div class=\"study-sa\"><p class=\"sa-q\">Explain the four elements of free, prior and informed consent.</p><details class=\"sa-a\"><summary>Model answer</summary><p>Free means the process is without coercion, intimidation, manipulation or inducement, including pressure from deadlines set by a developer. Prior means consent is sought sufficiently in advance of any authorisation or commencement to allow the community to use its own decision-making process. Informed means full disclosure of the nature, scope, duration, purpose, personnel, location and likely impacts of the measure, communicated in accessible language. Consent means a collective decision reached through the peoples&#x27; own representative institutions and customary procedures, not signatures gathered from selected individuals.</p></details></div><div class=\"study-sa\"><p class=\"sa-q\">Why did indigenous peoples require a separate rights framework?</p><details class=\"sa-a\"><summary>Model answer</summary><p>The general framework protects individuals, but the rights at issue for indigenous peoples are held collectively: title to ancestral territory, the maintenance of distinct institutions, the transmission of language and culture, and the survival of the group as a group. An individual right to property does not capture customary communal tenure, and an individual right to culture does not prevent the destruction of a people&#x27;s institutions. The framework also had to address self-determination, which no general treaty applies to sub-state peoples, and to establish decision-making standards such as free, prior and informed consent that operate at the level of the community rather than the person.</p></details></div><h4>Class Activity</h4><ol><li>Identify one indigenous or minority community in your country or region. Research whether their land is formally titled and, if not, what the legal obstacle is. Write one page.</li><li>Take a real development project affecting a community. Assess the consultation that took place against the four elements of free, prior and informed consent and state whether consent, rather than consultation, was required under the Saramaka approach.</li></ol><h4>Assignment</h4><p>Write one and a half pages on the rights of indigenous peoples. Explain why a separate framework was needed and how indigenous peoples are identified, compare UNDRIP with ILO Convention 169 on form, status and consent standard, explain how Articles 3 and 46 of UNDRIP resolve the self-determination question, set out free, prior and informed consent with the graduated Saramaka standard, and discuss collective land rights using at least one African case.</p><p class=\"study-print\"><strong>Printable:</strong> use your browser\u2019s Print \u2192 Save as PDF to keep an offline copy.</p></div>",
  "Introduction to Regional Systems": "<div class=\"study-note\"><div class=\"revision-banner\"><strong>Human Rights and International Humanitarian Law</strong><span>Module 5: Regional Human Rights Systems</span></div><h3 class=\"lesson-note-title\">LESSON NOTE: INTRODUCTION TO REGIONAL SYSTEMS</h3><div class=\"table-wrap\"><table class=\"study-table\"><thead><tr><th>Subject</th><th>Topic</th><th>Level</th><th>Lesson duration</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Human Rights and International Humanitarian Law</td><td>Introduction to Regional Systems</td><td>Beginner to Advanced</td><td>40 minutes</td></tr></tbody></table></div><h4>Learning objectives</h4><p>By the end of this lesson you should be able to:</p><ul><li>Explain why regional human rights systems developed alongside the UN system</li><li>Identify the three established regional systems and their instruments</li><li>Describe the relationship between regional and universal protection</li><li>Explain the principle of subsidiarity and the exhaustion of domestic remedies</li></ul><h4>1. Introduction</h4><p>The United Nations system is universal but distant. Regional systems are closer to the people they protect, and in two regions they produce binding judgments that states generally obey. Understanding why regions built their own machinery, and how it fits with the universal system, explains a great deal about where human rights law actually bites.</p><h4>2. Why regional systems emerged</h4><ol><li><strong>Enforcement.</strong> The UN treaty bodies issue views and recommendations. Regional courts issue binding judgments with compliance mechanisms. That is the single largest difference.</li><li><strong>Cultural and legal proximity.</strong> States within a region share legal traditions and history, which makes it easier to agree on standards and to accept a supervisory body's authority.</li><li><strong>Shared problems.</strong> Europe after 1945 was preoccupied with preventing a return to totalitarianism. Latin America after the dictatorships was preoccupied with disappearances and amnesty laws. Africa was preoccupied with colonialism, self-determination and development. Each system's instrument shows its founding preoccupation.</li><li><strong>Peer pressure works better among neighbours.</strong> A state cares more about its standing among the states it trades with, borders and negotiates with daily.</li><li><strong>Access.</strong> A regional commission or court is physically and procedurally more reachable for a victim than a committee in Geneva.</li></ol><h4>3. The three established systems</h4><div class=\"table-wrap\"><table class=\"study-table\"><thead><tr><th></th><th>Europe</th><th>Americas</th><th>Africa</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Organisation</td><td>Council of Europe (1949)</td><td>Organization of American States (1948)</td><td>African Union, formerly OAU (1963/2002)</td></tr><tr><td>Principal treaty</td><td>European Convention on Human Rights (1950)</td><td>American Convention on Human Rights (1969)</td><td>African Charter on Human and Peoples' Rights (1981)</td></tr><tr><td>Court</td><td>European Court of Human Rights (1959; single permanent court from 1998)</td><td>Inter-American Court of Human Rights (1979)</td><td>African Court on Human and Peoples' Rights (2006)</td></tr><tr><td>Commission</td><td>Abolished 1998</td><td>Inter-American Commission (1959), still central</td><td>African Commission (1987), still central</td></tr><tr><td>Individual access to court</td><td>Direct, by right, for all parties</td><td>Only through the Commission</td><td>Only where the state has made an Article 34(6) declaration</td></tr><tr><td>Distinctive feature</td><td>Very high case volume and dense jurisprudence</td><td>Strong reparations orders and amnesty jurisprudence</td><td>Peoples' rights, duties, and economic and social rights in the same binding text</td></tr></tbody></table></div><h4>4. Emerging and partial systems</h4><ul><li>The Arab Charter on Human Rights, revised in 2004 and in force in 2008, with an Arab Human Rights Committee that reviews reports. An Arab Court of Human Rights was approved in 2014 but is not operational and its statute permits only state access.</li><li>The ASEAN Intergovernmental Commission on Human Rights, established 2009, and the ASEAN Human Rights Declaration of 2012. It has no complaints procedure and its declaration contains general limitation language widely criticised as undermining the rights it states.</li><li>There is no Asia-wide or Pacific system. Asia is the only large region without one, usually explained by its diversity of legal systems, the absence of a region-wide political organisation, and strong sovereignty doctrines.</li></ul><h4>5. Relationship with the universal system</h4><div class=\"table-wrap\"><table class=\"study-table\"><thead><tr><th>Question</th><th>Answer</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Do regional systems replace the UN system?</td><td>No. They coexist. A state may be bound by both and answer to both.</td></tr><tr><td>Can the same case go to both?</td><td>Generally not simultaneously. Most instruments have a provision barring examination of a matter already under another international procedure.</td></tr><tr><td>Which standard applies if they differ?</td><td>The more protective. ECHR Article 53, ACHR Article 29 and ICCPR Article 5(2) all preserve any higher protection available elsewhere.</td></tr><tr><td>Do regional bodies cite UN material?</td><td>Constantly. General Comments, treaty body views and special procedures reports are routinely relied on, and the traffic runs both ways.</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"study-defn\"><strong>Pro homine principle:</strong> The interpretive rule that where two provisions could apply, the one more favourable to the individual governs.</div><div class=\"study-callout\"><strong>Note:</strong> The 'more protective standard' point is worth stating in any comparative answer. Regional systems set a floor, not a ceiling, and a state cannot use a weaker regional standard to justify falling below a universal one it has accepted.</div><h4>6. Subsidiarity and exhaustion of domestic remedies</h4><div class=\"study-defn\"><strong>Subsidiarity:</strong> The principle that international protection is secondary to national protection; the primary responsibility for securing rights lies with the state.</div><p>Subsidiarity has a procedural expression and a substantive one.</p><ul><li>Procedurally, it produces the exhaustion rule: an applicant must first pursue available domestic remedies. All three systems require it.</li><li>Substantively, it produces deference doctrines, most famously the European margin of appreciation, discussed later in this module.</li></ul><p>The exhaustion rule is not absolute. Remedies need only be exhausted where they are available, effective and sufficient. Exceptions are recognised where:</p><ol><li>No remedy exists, or the only remedy is discretionary rather than a matter of right.</li><li>The remedy is unduly prolonged. The African Commission has found delays of several years sufficient.</li><li>The remedy is ineffective in practice, for example where the courts consistently refuse jurisdiction over the type of claim.</li><li>The applicant was prevented from access, for example by detention incommunicado, threats, or the cost of litigation with no legal aid.</li><li>The violation is massive and serious, affecting large numbers, so that individual remedies are impracticable. This exception has been developed particularly by the African Commission.</li></ol><div class=\"study-callout\"><strong>Example:</strong> An applicant complains of torture in police custody in a state whose courts have never once admitted such a claim, where no legal aid exists and where the applicant was released only after signing a waiver. The exhaustion objection should fail on at least three grounds: ineffectiveness in practice, obstruction of access, and the coerced waiver. Setting out each ground separately is what a tribunal expects.</div><h4>7. How the examiner&#x27;s question should be answered</h4><p>A comparative question is not answered by describing each system in turn. Choose axes and compare across them: access, bindingness, remedies, subject matter, and effectiveness. The table above gives five ready-made axes. Then say which system is strongest on which axis and why, and give a reason grounded in the region's history rather than a general assertion.</p><h4>Summary</h4><ul><li>Regional systems developed because they offer enforcement, proximity, shared problems, effective peer pressure and better access.</li><li>Europe, the Americas and Africa have established systems; the Arab and ASEAN arrangements are partial and Asia has none region-wide.</li><li>Regional and universal systems coexist, cannot normally examine the same matter simultaneously, and the more protective standard prevails.</li><li>Subsidiarity produces the exhaustion rule procedurally and deference doctrines substantively.</li><li>Exhaustion is required only of remedies that are available, effective and sufficient, with recognised exceptions.</li></ul><h4>Key Terms</h4><div class=\"table-wrap\"><table class=\"study-table\"><thead><tr><th>Term</th><th>Meaning</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Subsidiarity</td><td>The principle that international protection supplements rather than replaces national protection.</td></tr><tr><td>Exhaustion of domestic remedies</td><td>The requirement to pursue available and effective national remedies before an international claim.</td></tr><tr><td>Pro homine</td><td>The rule that the provision more favourable to the individual governs.</td></tr><tr><td>Regional system</td><td>A treaty-based human rights regime covering the states of a defined geographic region.</td></tr></tbody></table></div><h4>Review Questions</h4><h5>Multiple choice</h5><div class=\"study-mcq\"><p class=\"mcq-q\">What is the principal advantage of regional systems over the UN treaty bodies?</p><ol type=\"A\"><li>Two of the three produce binding judgments with compliance machinery</li><li>They cover more rights</li><li>They are cheaper to run</li><li>They admit complaints without exhaustion</li></ol><details class=\"mcq-a\"><summary>Show answer</summary><p><strong>Answer: A.</strong> UN treaty bodies issue views and recommendations; the European and Inter-American Courts issue binding judgments.</p></details></div><div class=\"study-mcq\"><p class=\"mcq-q\">Which region has no established region-wide human rights system?</p><ol type=\"A\"><li>Asia</li><li>Africa</li><li>The Americas</li><li>Europe</li></ol><details class=\"mcq-a\"><summary>Show answer</summary><p><strong>Answer: A.</strong> The ASEAN arrangement is sub-regional and has no complaints procedure; there is no Asia-wide organisation to host one.</p></details></div><div class=\"study-mcq\"><p class=\"mcq-q\">If a regional standard is lower than a universal one the state has accepted, which applies?</p><ol type=\"A\"><li>The more protective standard</li><li>The regional standard, as lex specialis</li><li>The later standard in time</li><li>Whichever the state elects</li></ol><details class=\"mcq-a\"><summary>Show answer</summary><p><strong>Answer: A.</strong> ECHR Article 53, ACHR Article 29 and ICCPR Article 5(2) all preserve higher protection available elsewhere.</p></details></div><div class=\"study-mcq\"><p class=\"mcq-q\">When may an applicant proceed without exhausting domestic remedies?</p><ol type=\"A\"><li>Where the remedy is unavailable, ineffective, unduly prolonged or access was obstructed</li><li>Never; exhaustion is absolute</li><li>Only with the state&#x27;s consent</li><li>Only in cases involving torture</li></ol><details class=\"mcq-a\"><summary>Show answer</summary><p><strong>Answer: A.</strong> The African Commission has also recognised an exception for massive and serious violations where individual remedies are impracticable.</p></details></div><h5>Short answer</h5><div class=\"study-sa\"><p class=\"sa-q\">Give four reasons why regional human rights systems developed.</p><details class=\"sa-a\"><summary>Model answer</summary><p>First, enforcement: regional courts issue binding judgments, while UN treaty bodies issue non-binding views. Second, proximity of legal tradition and history, which makes agreement on standards and acceptance of a supervisory body easier. Third, shared regional problems, so that each instrument reflects its region&#x27;s founding preoccupation, whether preventing totalitarianism, addressing disappearances and amnesties, or confronting colonialism and underdevelopment. Fourth, peer pressure operates more strongly among neighbours who trade and negotiate daily. A fifth reason is practical access for victims, who can reach a regional body more readily than a committee in Geneva.</p></details></div><div class=\"study-sa\"><p class=\"sa-q\">Explain the exhaustion of domestic remedies rule and three exceptions.</p><details class=\"sa-a\"><summary>Model answer</summary><p>An applicant must ordinarily pursue the remedies available in the national legal system before bringing an international claim, which follows from subsidiarity: the state has the primary responsibility and the first opportunity to put matters right. The rule applies only to remedies that are available, effective and sufficient. It is excused where no remedy exists or the only one is discretionary rather than of right; where the remedy is unduly prolonged; and where it is ineffective in practice because the courts consistently decline such claims. Access being obstructed by detention, threats or the absence of legal aid, and the massive and serious nature of the violations, are further recognised exceptions.</p></details></div><h4>Class Activity</h4><ol><li>Draw a comparison table of the three established systems across five axes: access, bindingness, remedies, subject matter and effectiveness. Fill it from this lesson and add one fact of your own research per cell.</li><li>Research why Asia has no regional human rights system. Write one page setting out three explanations and stating which you find most convincing.</li></ol><h4>Assignment</h4><p>Write one and a half pages introducing regional human rights systems. Explain why they developed, identify the three established systems with their instruments and organs, describe the partial arrangements in the Arab world and ASEAN, explain how regional and universal protection interact including the more protective standard rule, and set out subsidiarity, the exhaustion rule and its exceptions.</p><p class=\"study-print\"><strong>Printable:</strong> use your browser\u2019s Print \u2192 Save as PDF to keep an offline copy.</p></div>",
  "The African Charter on Human and Peoples' Rights": "<div class=\"study-note\"><div class=\"revision-banner\"><strong>Human Rights and International Humanitarian Law</strong><span>Module 5: Regional Human Rights Systems</span></div><h3 class=\"lesson-note-title\">LESSON NOTE: THE AFRICAN CHARTER ON HUMAN AND PEOPLES&#x27; RIGHTS</h3><div class=\"table-wrap\"><table class=\"study-table\"><thead><tr><th>Subject</th><th>Topic</th><th>Level</th><th>Lesson duration</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Human Rights and International Humanitarian Law</td><td>The African Charter on Human and Peoples&#x27; Rights</td><td>Beginner to Advanced</td><td>45 minutes</td></tr></tbody></table></div><h4>Learning objectives</h4><p>By the end of this lesson you should be able to:</p><ul><li>Describe the distinctive features of the African Charter</li><li>Explain peoples&#x27; rights and individual duties</li><li>Explain the clawback clauses and how they have been handled</li><li>Describe the Charter&#x27;s protocols</li></ul><h4>1. Introduction</h4><p>The African Charter, adopted in Nairobi in 1981 and in force in 1986, is ratified by every African Union member state. It is the most distinctive of the three regional instruments, and its distinctiveness is not accidental: it was drafted by states emerging from colonialism, in a period of one-party government, and it shows both influences.</p><h4>2. Four distinctive features</h4><div class=\"table-wrap\"><table class=\"study-table\"><thead><tr><th>Feature</th><th>What it means</th><th>Contrast</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Peoples' rights</td><td>Chapter I includes rights held by peoples, not individuals: existence and self-determination, free disposal of wealth and natural resources, development, peace and security, and a satisfactory environment</td><td>No equivalent in the ECHR or ACHR</td></tr><tr><td>Duties</td><td>Chapter II imposes duties on individuals towards family, society, the state and the international community</td><td>Unique among binding human rights treaties</td></tr><tr><td>Indivisibility in one text</td><td>Civil, political, economic, social and cultural rights all appear in a single binding instrument with the same enforcement machinery</td><td>Europe splits them between the ECHR and the European Social Charter</td></tr><tr><td>No general derogation clause</td><td>The Charter contains no provision permitting derogation in emergency</td><td>The ECHR, ACHR and ICCPR all have one</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"study-callout\"><strong>Note:</strong> The absence of a derogation clause is the most consequential of the four. In Commission Nationale des Droits de l'Homme et des Libertes v Chad the African Commission held that the Charter does not allow states to derogate, so even civil war does not suspend Charter obligations. That makes the Charter formally stricter in emergencies than any other regional instrument, though clawback clauses complicate the picture.</div><h4>3. Peoples&#x27; rights</h4><div class=\"table-wrap\"><table class=\"study-table\"><thead><tr><th>Article</th><th>Right</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>19</td><td>Equality of peoples; no domination of one people by another</td></tr><tr><td>20</td><td>Existence and self-determination, including the right of colonised or oppressed peoples to free themselves</td></tr><tr><td>21</td><td>Free disposal of wealth and natural resources, with a right to recovery and adequate compensation for spoliation</td></tr><tr><td>22</td><td>Economic, social and cultural development</td></tr><tr><td>23</td><td>National and international peace and security</td></tr><tr><td>24</td><td>A general satisfactory environment favourable to development</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"study-callout\"><strong>Example:</strong> SERAC v Nigeria (2001), the Ogoniland case, is the leading decision. The Commission found that oil operations conducted with the state's participation had contaminated Ogoni land and water, and that Nigeria had violated Articles 21 and 24 among others. Crucially it read the Charter as imposing duties to respect, protect, promote and fulfil across all rights including economic and social ones, and it found violations of an implied right to housing and an implied right to food derived from existing articles. The case is cited far beyond Africa as authority that social and environmental rights are justiciable.</div><h4>4. Duties</h4><p>Articles 27 to 29 impose duties on the individual. This is the Charter's most debated feature.</p><ul><li>Article 27: duties towards family and society, the state, other legally recognised communities, and the international community; rights are exercised with due regard to the rights of others, collective security, morality and common interest.</li><li>Article 28: to respect and consider fellow beings without discrimination and to maintain relations aimed at promoting, safeguarding and reinforcing mutual respect and tolerance.</li><li>Article 29: eight specific duties including preserving family harmony, serving the national community, not compromising the security of the state, preserving and strengthening social and national solidarity, preserving and strengthening national independence and territorial integrity, working and paying taxes, preserving positive African cultural values, and contributing to African unity.</li></ul><p>Two readings compete. The sympathetic reading is that duties express a communitarian conception of the person grounded in African philosophical traditions, and that all rights systems imply duties which other instruments leave implicit. The critical reading is that duty language can be used to condition rights on good citizenship, and that Article 29(3), on not compromising the security of the state, is capable of swallowing dissent.</p><div class=\"study-callout\"><strong>Note:</strong> The Commission's practice has largely defused this. It has never used the duties provisions to defeat a claim of violation, and has emphasised that duties do not create limitations on rights beyond those the Charter itself permits. State the debate and then state the practice: that combination is what a strong answer looks like.</div><h4>5. Clawback clauses</h4><div class=\"study-defn\"><strong>Clawback clause:</strong> A provision granting a right but subjecting it to limitation 'in accordance with the law' or 'within the law', apparently allowing domestic law to define the scope of the international obligation.</div><div class=\"table-wrap\"><table class=\"study-table\"><thead><tr><th>Article</th><th>Wording</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>6</td><td>Liberty and security, except for reasons and conditions previously laid down by law</td></tr><tr><td>8</td><td>Freedom of conscience, subject to law and order</td></tr><tr><td>9(2)</td><td>Right to express and disseminate opinions within the law</td></tr><tr><td>10(1)</td><td>Freedom of association provided that one abides by the law</td></tr><tr><td>11</td><td>Assembly, subject to necessary restrictions provided for by law</td></tr><tr><td>12</td><td>Movement and residence, subject to restrictions provided for by law</td></tr><tr><td>13</td><td>Participation in government in accordance with the provisions of the law</td></tr></tbody></table></div><p>Read literally these clauses would let a state legislate the right away. The Commission has refused that reading in a consistent line of decisions.</p><ol><li>In Media Rights Agenda v Nigeria and Constitutional Rights Project v Nigeria the Commission held that permitting national law to override an international obligation would defeat the purpose of the Charter and the international obligations undertaken.</li><li>In Amnesty International v Zambia and later cases it held that any limitation must be justified by reference to Article 27(2), which requires that rights be exercised with due regard to the rights of others, collective security, morality and common interest.</li><li>The resulting test is a proportionality test: the limitation must serve a legitimate purpose within Article 27(2), be necessary in a democratic society, and be proportionate to that purpose.</li><li>The Commission has also held that the limitation must be in a law that is accessible and precise, so an executive decree or a vaguely worded provision cannot satisfy it.</li></ol><div class=\"study-callout\"><strong>Note:</strong> This is the single most important interpretive development in the Charter's history. Without it the clawback clauses would have rendered the civil and political rights nugatory. Any answer discussing clawback clauses must give both the problem and the Commission's solution.</div><h4>6. The protocols</h4><div class=\"table-wrap\"><table class=\"study-table\"><thead><tr><th>Protocol</th><th>Year</th><th>Effect</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Protocol on the establishment of an African Court on Human and Peoples' Rights</td><td>1998, in force 2004</td><td>Created the African Court, which began work in 2006</td></tr><tr><td>Protocol on the Rights of Women in Africa (Maputo Protocol)</td><td>2003, in force 2005</td><td>Comprehensive women's rights instrument; first treaty to address HIV in a rights framework and to provide expressly for medical abortion in defined circumstances</td></tr><tr><td>Protocol on the Statute of the African Court of Justice and Human Rights</td><td>2008</td><td>Would merge the African Court with the Court of Justice; not in force</td></tr><tr><td>Malabo Protocol</td><td>2014</td><td>Would add international criminal jurisdiction to the merged court, including crimes such as unconstitutional change of government and corporate criminal liability; contains a controversial immunity for serving heads of state; not in force</td></tr><tr><td>Protocol on the Rights of Older Persons</td><td>2016</td><td>Not yet in force</td></tr><tr><td>Protocol on the Rights of Persons with Disabilities</td><td>2018</td><td>Not yet in force</td></tr></tbody></table></div><p>The African Charter on the Rights and Welfare of the Child, adopted in 1990 and in force in 1999, is a separate treaty rather than a protocol. It has its own committee of experts and differs from the CRC in several respects, notably by defining a child as every human being below eighteen without exception, and by expressly addressing harmful social and cultural practices, children of imprisoned mothers, and child soldiers.</p><h4>7. How the examiner&#x27;s question should be answered</h4><p>A question on the African Charter is almost always testing whether you can name and explain its distinctive features. Do that first: peoples' rights, duties, indivisibility, no derogation clause. Then take the clawback problem and the Commission's solution, because that is the analytical content. Cite SERAC for peoples' rights and social rights justiciability, and one Nigerian case for clawback. Finish with the protocols, mentioning that the Maputo Protocol is the most advanced women's rights treaty in any region.</p><h4>Summary</h4><ul><li>The African Charter is ratified by every AU member state and is distinctive for peoples&#x27; rights, individual duties, indivisibility in one text and the absence of a derogation clause.</li><li>Peoples&#x27; rights cover self-determination, natural resources, development, peace and the environment, and were applied in SERAC v Nigeria.</li><li>Duties in Articles 27 to 29 are debated but have never been used by the Commission to defeat a claim.</li><li>Clawback clauses appear to subject rights to national law, but the Commission requires limitations to satisfy Article 27(2) and a proportionality test.</li><li>The Maputo Protocol on women&#x27;s rights and a separate Children&#x27;s Charter supplement the main instrument.</li></ul><h4>Key Terms</h4><div class=\"table-wrap\"><table class=\"study-table\"><thead><tr><th>Term</th><th>Meaning</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Peoples&#x27; rights</td><td>Rights held collectively by peoples, including self-determination, resources, development, peace and environment.</td></tr><tr><td>Clawback clause</td><td>A provision limiting a right by reference to national law, read down by the Commission through Article 27(2).</td></tr><tr><td>Maputo Protocol</td><td>The 2003 Protocol on the Rights of Women in Africa.</td></tr><tr><td>SERAC v Nigeria</td><td>The Ogoniland decision applying peoples&#x27; rights and finding implied rights to housing and food.</td></tr></tbody></table></div><h4>Review Questions</h4><h5>Multiple choice</h5><div class=\"study-mcq\"><p class=\"mcq-q\">Which feature makes the African Charter formally stricter in emergencies than other regional instruments?</p><ol type=\"A\"><li>It contains no derogation clause</li><li>It has more non-derogable rights</li><li>It requires Security Council approval for emergencies</li><li>It suspends only economic rights</li></ol><details class=\"mcq-a\"><summary>Show answer</summary><p><strong>Answer: A.</strong> In the Chad case the Commission held that the Charter permits no derogation, so even civil war does not suspend obligations.</p></details></div><div class=\"study-mcq\"><p class=\"mcq-q\">How has the African Commission handled clawback clauses?</p><ol type=\"A\"><li>By requiring limitations to fall within Article 27(2) and to satisfy a proportionality test</li><li>By striking them out of the Charter</li><li>By applying them literally</li><li>By referring them to the African Court</li></ol><details class=\"mcq-a\"><summary>Show answer</summary><p><strong>Answer: A.</strong> A literal reading would let national law defeat the international obligation, which the Commission held would frustrate the Charter&#x27;s purpose.</p></details></div><div class=\"study-mcq\"><p class=\"mcq-q\">What did SERAC v Nigeria establish?</p><ol type=\"A\"><li>Duties to respect, protect, promote and fulfil apply across all Charter rights, including implied rights to housing and food</li><li>That peoples&#x27; rights are non-justiciable</li><li>That oil companies cannot be regulated</li><li>That Article 24 protects only state property</li></ol><details class=\"mcq-a\"><summary>Show answer</summary><p><strong>Answer: A.</strong> It is cited well beyond Africa as authority that social and environmental rights can be adjudicated.</p></details></div><div class=\"study-mcq\"><p class=\"mcq-q\">Which African instrument first addressed HIV within a rights framework and provided expressly for medical abortion in defined circumstances?</p><ol type=\"A\"><li>The Maputo Protocol</li><li>The African Charter itself</li><li>The Children&#x27;s Charter</li><li>The Malabo Protocol</li></ol><details class=\"mcq-a\"><summary>Show answer</summary><p><strong>Answer: A.</strong> The 2003 Protocol on the Rights of Women in Africa is the most advanced women&#x27;s rights treaty in any region on these questions.</p></details></div><h5>Short answer</h5><div class=\"study-sa\"><p class=\"sa-q\">Explain the debate about individual duties in the African Charter and how the Commission has resolved it in practice.</p><details class=\"sa-a\"><summary>Model answer</summary><p>Articles 27 to 29 impose duties on individuals towards family, society, the state and the international community, including duties not to compromise state security and to preserve national solidarity. The sympathetic reading is that this expresses a communitarian conception of the person rooted in African philosophical traditions, and that other instruments merely leave such duties implicit. The critical reading is that duty language can be used to condition rights on approved conduct, with Article 29(3) capable of suppressing dissent. In practice the Commission has never invoked the duties provisions to defeat a claim of violation and has held that they do not create limitations on rights beyond those the Charter itself permits.</p></details></div><div class=\"study-sa\"><p class=\"sa-q\">Set out the test the African Commission applies to a clawback clause.</p><details class=\"sa-a\"><summary>Model answer</summary><p>The limitation must fall within Article 27(2), which permits restrictions only with due regard to the rights of others, collective security, morality and common interest, so a purpose outside that list cannot justify it. It must be prescribed by a law that is accessible and sufficiently precise, which excludes vague provisions and bare executive decrees. It must be necessary in a democratic society. And it must be proportionate to the legitimate purpose pursued. The Commission has held that allowing national law to define the obligation would defeat the Charter&#x27;s purpose, so the words &#x27;within the law&#x27; are read as a reference to law meeting these requirements rather than to whatever a state chooses to enact.</p></details></div><h4>Class Activity</h4><ol><li>Read Article 29 of the Charter and list its eight duties. For each, write one sentence on whether it could be misused to restrict a right and how the Commission&#x27;s approach prevents that.</li><li>Find one African Commission decision on a clawback clause. Summarise the facts, the state&#x27;s argument and the Commission&#x27;s reasoning in half a page.</li></ol><h4>Assignment</h4><p>Write two pages on the African Charter on Human and Peoples&#x27; Rights. Describe its adoption and coverage, explain its four distinctive features, set out the peoples&#x27; rights with reference to SERAC v Nigeria, discuss the debate over individual duties and the Commission&#x27;s practice, explain the clawback clause problem and the test the Commission has developed, and list the main protocols including the Maputo Protocol.</p><p class=\"study-print\"><strong>Printable:</strong> use your browser\u2019s Print \u2192 Save as PDF to keep an offline copy.</p></div>",
  "The African Commission and African Court": "<div class=\"study-note\"><div class=\"revision-banner\"><strong>Human Rights and International Humanitarian Law</strong><span>Module 5: Regional Human Rights Systems</span></div><h3 class=\"lesson-note-title\">LESSON NOTE: THE AFRICAN COMMISSION AND AFRICAN COURT</h3><div class=\"table-wrap\"><table class=\"study-table\"><thead><tr><th>Subject</th><th>Topic</th><th>Level</th><th>Lesson duration</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Human Rights and International Humanitarian Law</td><td>The African Commission and African Court</td><td>Beginner to Advanced</td><td>45 minutes</td></tr></tbody></table></div><h4>Learning objectives</h4><p>By the end of this lesson you should be able to:</p><ul><li>Describe the composition, mandate and procedures of the African Commission</li><li>Describe the African Court and the Article 34(6) declaration problem</li><li>Explain the relationship between the Commission and the Court</li><li>Assess the effectiveness of the African system</li></ul><h4>1. Introduction</h4><p>The African system has two bodies with overlapping functions and very different powers. Understanding how a case moves through them, and why so few reach the Court, is the practical core of this lesson.</p><h4>2. The African Commission</h4><div class=\"table-wrap\"><table class=\"study-table\"><thead><tr><th>Feature</th><th>Detail</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Established</td><td>1987, under Charter Article 30</td></tr><tr><td>Composition</td><td>Eleven members serving in a personal capacity, elected by the AU Assembly for six-year renewable terms</td></tr><tr><td>Seat</td><td>Banjul, The Gambia</td></tr><tr><td>Sessions</td><td>Two ordinary sessions a year, plus extraordinary sessions</td></tr><tr><td>Mandate</td><td>Promotion of human and peoples' rights, protection, and interpretation of the Charter</td></tr></tbody></table></div><p>Its functions divide into four.</p><ol><li><strong>Promotional:</strong> studies, seminars, dissemination, and cooperation with national and international institutions.</li><li><strong>Protective:</strong> communications from states and, in practice, from individuals and NGOs.</li><li><strong>Interpretive:</strong> interpreting the Charter at the request of a state party, an AU organ or an African organisation recognised by the AU.</li><li><strong>Special mechanisms:</strong> special rapporteurs, working groups and committees on themes such as prisons and conditions of detention, freedom of expression, human rights defenders, indigenous populations, extractive industries, and the death penalty.</li></ol><h4>3. Communications before the Commission</h4><p>Charter Article 55 refers to 'other communications', which is the textual basis on which the Commission has accepted complaints from individuals and NGOs. This is worth noting: the Charter does not expressly create an individual complaints procedure, and the practice was built by interpretation.</p><p>Admissibility under Article 56 requires seven conditions.</p><ol><li>The author must be indicated, even if requesting anonymity.</li><li>It must be compatible with the AU Constitutive Act and the Charter.</li><li>It must not be written in disparaging or insulting language directed at the state or the AU.</li><li>It must not be based exclusively on news disseminated through the mass media.</li><li>Local remedies must be exhausted, unless the procedure is unduly prolonged.</li><li>It must be submitted within a reasonable period from exhaustion.</li><li>It must not deal with a case already settled by the states involved under the UN Charter, the AU Constitutive Act or the African Charter.</li></ol><div class=\"study-callout\"><strong>Note:</strong> Condition three, on insulting language, is unusual and has been used to reject complaints. The Commission has narrowed it, holding that the language must be aimed at undermining the integrity and standing of the institution rather than merely being strongly worded criticism. Condition four is also narrower than it reads: media reports may be used, but the complaint must not rest on them exclusively.</div><h4>4. The African Court</h4><div class=\"table-wrap\"><table class=\"study-table\"><thead><tr><th>Feature</th><th>Detail</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Established</td><td>Protocol of 1998, in force 2004; operational 2006</td></tr><tr><td>Seat</td><td>Arusha, Tanzania</td></tr><tr><td>Composition</td><td>Eleven judges elected for six-year terms, renewable once; only the President serves full time</td></tr><tr><td>Contentious jurisdiction</td><td>Cases concerning interpretation and application of the Charter, the Protocol and any other relevant human rights instrument ratified by the state concerned</td></tr><tr><td>Advisory jurisdiction</td><td>At the request of an AU member state, an AU organ, or an African organisation recognised by the AU</td></tr><tr><td>Judgments</td><td>Binding; states undertake to comply and to guarantee execution; the Executive Council monitors on behalf of the Assembly</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"study-callout\"><strong>Note:</strong> The jurisdiction clause is remarkably wide. Article 3(1) of the Protocol extends to 'any other relevant human rights instrument ratified by the States concerned'. That means the African Court can apply the ICCPR, CEDAW, the CRC and other UN treaties directly, which no other regional court can do. This is a genuine strength and should be mentioned in any comparative answer.</div><h4>5. Access to the Court and the Article 34(6) problem</h4><p>Article 5 of the Protocol lists who may bring a case.</p><ul><li>The Commission, a state party which has lodged a complaint with the Commission, a state party against which a complaint has been lodged, a state party whose citizen is a victim, and African intergovernmental organisations may all seise the Court directly.</li><li>Individuals and NGOs with observer status before the Commission may do so only if the state has made a declaration under Article 34(6) accepting the Court's competence to receive such cases.</li></ul><p>The declaration requirement is the central weakness of the system. Of the states party to the Protocol, only a small minority have ever made the declaration, and several that did have since withdrawn it, including Rwanda in 2016, Tanzania in 2019, Benin and Cote d'Ivoire in 2020. Withdrawals typically followed judgments the state disliked.</p><p>The Court held in cases arising from those withdrawals that a withdrawal takes effect only after one year and does not affect cases already pending. That preserves the docket in transition but does not solve the structural problem: a court whose individual access depends on a revocable declaration is a court states can exit when it becomes effective.</p><div class=\"study-callout\"><strong>Example:</strong> Tanzania withdrew its declaration in November 2019 after a series of adverse judgments, including on the fairness of criminal trials and mandatory death sentences. The Court held the withdrawal effective from November 2020 and continued to decide pending cases. The pattern of adverse judgment followed by withdrawal is the clearest evidence that the Court's decisions were having effect, and simultaneously the clearest evidence of the fragility of individual access.</div><h4>6. Relationship between the two bodies</h4><div class=\"table-wrap\"><table class=\"study-table\"><thead><tr><th></th><th>Commission</th><th>Court</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Nature of output</td><td>Recommendations to the AU Assembly</td><td>Binding judgments</td></tr><tr><td>Individual access</td><td>Open to individuals and NGOs without any declaration</td><td>Requires an Article 34(6) declaration</td></tr><tr><td>Referral</td><td>May refer a case to the Court, including for non-compliance with its own provisional measures or recommendations</td><td>Receives such referrals</td></tr><tr><td>Complementarity</td><td>The Protocol states the Court complements the protective mandate of the Commission</td><td>Rules of both bodies govern the interaction</td></tr></tbody></table></div><p>In practice the Commission remains the main route for individuals, because the Court's door is closed to them in most states. The Commission's power to refer is therefore important: it converts a non-binding recommendation into a potentially binding judgment. It has been used sparingly.</p><h4>7. Assessing the system</h4><div class=\"table-wrap\"><table class=\"study-table\"><thead><tr><th>Strength</th><th>Weakness</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Universal ratification of the Charter across the AU</td><td>Individual access to the Court gated by a revocable declaration</td></tr><tr><td>Economic, social and peoples' rights justiciable in the same instrument</td><td>Chronic under-resourcing of both bodies</td></tr><tr><td>The Court may apply any human rights treaty the state has ratified</td><td>Weak compliance with Commission recommendations and mixed compliance with Court judgments</td></tr><tr><td>Strong special mechanisms and NGO participation</td><td>Commission recommendations require Assembly adoption before publication, creating political filtering</td></tr><tr><td>Significant jurisprudence on land, resources and social rights</td><td>Backlog and long delays before both bodies</td></tr></tbody></table></div><p>Sub-regional courts add a further route. The ECOWAS Community Court of Justice has human rights jurisdiction, requires no exhaustion of domestic remedies, and is open to individuals directly, which in West Africa has made it in practice more accessible than the African Court. The East African Court of Justice and the SADC Tribunal, before its suspension in 2010, have played comparable roles.</p><h4>8. How the examiner&#x27;s question should be answered</h4><p>For 'assess the African human rights system', do not simply list institutions. Trace how a case actually moves: national courts, then the Commission under Article 56, then possibly referral to the Court, or the ECOWAS Court in West Africa. Identify the choke point, which is Article 34(6). Give the withdrawal pattern with named states and dates. Then balance strengths against weaknesses using the table above, and conclude with the reform you consider most important, which is normally making individual access automatic on ratification of the Protocol.</p><h4>Summary</h4><ul><li>The African Commission has eleven members, meets twice a year, and exercises promotional, protective, interpretive and special mechanism functions.</li><li>Individual communications rest on the phrase &#x27;other communications&#x27; in Article 55 and must satisfy seven Article 56 conditions.</li><li>The African Court was established by the 1998 Protocol and can apply any human rights treaty the respondent state has ratified.</li><li>Individual and NGO access requires an Article 34(6) declaration, which few states have made and several have withdrawn after adverse judgments.</li><li>The Commission may refer cases to the Court, converting recommendations into potentially binding judgments.</li><li>The ECOWAS Court offers direct individual access without exhaustion and is in practice more accessible in West Africa.</li></ul><h4>Key Terms</h4><div class=\"table-wrap\"><table class=\"study-table\"><thead><tr><th>Term</th><th>Meaning</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Article 34(6) declaration</td><td>The optional acceptance of the African Court&#x27;s competence to receive cases from individuals and NGOs.</td></tr><tr><td>Article 56</td><td>The African Charter provision setting out seven admissibility conditions for communications.</td></tr><tr><td>Complementarity</td><td>The relationship by which the African Court complements the protective mandate of the Commission.</td></tr><tr><td>ECOWAS Community Court of Justice</td><td>A sub-regional court with human rights jurisdiction, direct individual access and no exhaustion requirement.</td></tr></tbody></table></div><h4>Review Questions</h4><h5>Multiple choice</h5><div class=\"study-mcq\"><p class=\"mcq-q\">Why can individuals bring cases to the African Commission but usually not to the African Court?</p><ol type=\"A\"><li>The Court requires an Article 34(6) declaration that most states have not made</li><li>The Commission has wider subject-matter jurisdiction</li><li>The Court hears only inter-state cases</li><li>Individuals must be represented by their government</li></ol><details class=\"mcq-a\"><summary>Show answer</summary><p><strong>Answer: A.</strong> Several states that made the declaration later withdrew it following adverse judgments.</p></details></div><div class=\"study-mcq\"><p class=\"mcq-q\">What is unusual about the African Court&#x27;s jurisdiction under Article 3(1) of its Protocol?</p><ol type=\"A\"><li>It may apply any human rights instrument the respondent state has ratified, including UN treaties</li><li>It may hear criminal cases</li><li>It may review AU Assembly decisions</li><li>It requires no exhaustion of remedies</li></ol><details class=\"mcq-a\"><summary>Show answer</summary><p><strong>Answer: A.</strong> No other regional human rights court can apply the ICCPR or CEDAW directly in this way.</p></details></div><div class=\"study-mcq\"><p class=\"mcq-q\">Which Article 56 condition has the Commission had to narrow by interpretation?</p><ol type=\"A\"><li>The prohibition on disparaging or insulting language</li><li>The requirement to identify the author</li><li>The exhaustion requirement</li><li>The reasonable time requirement</li></ol><details class=\"mcq-a\"><summary>Show answer</summary><p><strong>Answer: A.</strong> It now applies only where the language aims to undermine the institution&#x27;s integrity, not to strongly worded criticism.</p></details></div><div class=\"study-mcq\"><p class=\"mcq-q\">Why is the ECOWAS Court often more accessible than the African Court in West Africa?</p><ol type=\"A\"><li>It permits direct individual access with no exhaustion of domestic remedies required</li><li>It sits more frequently</li><li>It awards higher damages</li><li>It has criminal jurisdiction</li></ol><details class=\"mcq-a\"><summary>Show answer</summary><p><strong>Answer: A.</strong> Its human rights jurisdiction combined with open standing makes it a practical first choice for many West African litigants.</p></details></div><h5>Short answer</h5><div class=\"study-sa\"><p class=\"sa-q\">Explain the Article 34(6) problem and its consequences.</p><details class=\"sa-a\"><summary>Model answer</summary><p>Article 34(6) of the Court Protocol provides that individuals and NGOs with observer status may bring cases only against states that have made a separate declaration accepting the Court&#x27;s competence to receive them. Most states party to the Protocol have never made it, so for their populations the Court is effectively closed. Of those that did, Rwanda, Tanzania, Benin and Cote d&#x27;Ivoire have withdrawn, in each case after judgments they disliked. The Court has held that withdrawal takes effect only after one year and does not affect pending cases, which preserves the immediate docket but leaves the structural problem intact: individual access depends on a consent a state can revoke precisely when the Court becomes effective.</p></details></div><div class=\"study-sa\"><p class=\"sa-q\">Describe the relationship between the African Commission and the African Court.</p><details class=\"sa-a\"><summary>Model answer</summary><p>The Protocol states that the Court complements the protective mandate of the Commission rather than replacing it. The Commission remains open to individuals and NGOs without any declaration and handles the great majority of complaints, but its output is recommendations that require adoption by the AU Assembly. The Court issues binding judgments but its door is closed to individuals in most states. The link between them is the Commission&#x27;s power to refer a case to the Court, including where a state has failed to comply with the Commission&#x27;s provisional measures or recommendations, which converts a non-binding recommendation into a potentially binding judgment. That power has been used sparingly.</p></details></div><h4>Class Activity</h4><ol><li>Find one decision of the African Commission and one judgment of the African Court. Compare the remedies each ordered and note whether the state complied.</li><li>Check whether your country has made an Article 34(6) declaration. If not, write a one-page brief to the ministry of justice arguing for or against making one.</li></ol><h4>Assignment</h4><p>Write two pages on the African Commission and African Court. Describe the composition, mandate and procedures of each, set out the Article 56 admissibility conditions, explain access to the Court and the Article 34(6) declaration problem with the withdrawal record, describe how the two bodies relate, and assess the system&#x27;s strengths and weaknesses including the role of sub-regional courts.</p><p class=\"study-print\"><strong>Printable:</strong> use your browser\u2019s Print \u2192 Save as PDF to keep an offline copy.</p></div>",
  "The European Human Rights System": "<div class=\"study-note\"><div class=\"revision-banner\"><strong>Human Rights and International Humanitarian Law</strong><span>Module 5: Regional Human Rights Systems</span></div><h3 class=\"lesson-note-title\">LESSON NOTE: THE EUROPEAN HUMAN RIGHTS SYSTEM</h3><div class=\"table-wrap\"><table class=\"study-table\"><thead><tr><th>Subject</th><th>Topic</th><th>Level</th><th>Lesson duration</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Human Rights and International Humanitarian Law</td><td>The European Human Rights System</td><td>Beginner to Advanced</td><td>45 minutes</td></tr></tbody></table></div><h4>Learning objectives</h4><p>By the end of this lesson you should be able to:</p><ul><li>Describe the European Convention and the Court&#x27;s structure</li><li>Explain the admissibility criteria and the case volume problem</li><li>Explain the margin of appreciation and living instrument doctrines</li><li>Describe execution of judgments and the system&#x27;s limits</li></ul><h4>1. Introduction</h4><p>The European system is the oldest, the busiest and by most measures the most effective regional human rights machinery in existence. It is also the system whose techniques the others borrowed. Two of its doctrines, the margin of appreciation and the living instrument, are cited worldwide.</p><h4>2. The Convention and its protocols</h4><div class=\"table-wrap\"><table class=\"study-table\"><thead><tr><th>Instrument</th><th>Year</th><th>Content</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>ECHR</td><td>1950, in force 1953</td><td>Civil and political rights; 46 states parties</td></tr><tr><td>Protocol 1</td><td>1952</td><td>Property, education, free elections</td></tr><tr><td>Protocol 4</td><td>1963</td><td>No imprisonment for debt, freedom of movement, no expulsion of nationals, no collective expulsion of aliens</td></tr><tr><td>Protocol 6</td><td>1983</td><td>Abolition of the death penalty in peacetime</td></tr><tr><td>Protocol 7</td><td>1984</td><td>Procedural safeguards on expulsion, appeal in criminal matters, compensation for wrongful conviction, ne bis in idem, equality between spouses</td></tr><tr><td>Protocol 12</td><td>2000</td><td>A free-standing prohibition of discrimination, comparable to ICCPR Article 26</td></tr><tr><td>Protocol 13</td><td>2002</td><td>Abolition of the death penalty in all circumstances, including war</td></tr><tr><td>Protocol 14</td><td>2004, in force 2010</td><td>Single-judge formations, committees of three, and a new admissibility criterion</td></tr><tr><td>Protocol 15</td><td>2013, in force 2021</td><td>Added subsidiarity and margin of appreciation to the preamble; cut the time limit from six to four months</td></tr><tr><td>Protocol 16</td><td>2013, in force 2018</td><td>Advisory opinions at the request of designated highest national courts</td></tr></tbody></table></div><p>The European Social Charter, a separate Council of Europe treaty, covers economic and social rights and is supervised by the European Committee of Social Rights through reporting and a collective complaints procedure. Keeping the two regimes distinct is a frequent examination point.</p><h4>3. The Court</h4><ul><li>A single permanent Court since Protocol 11 took effect in 1998, replacing the earlier Commission and part-time Court.</li><li>One judge per state party, elected by the Parliamentary Assembly from a list of three submitted by the state, for a single non-renewable nine-year term.</li><li>Formations: a single judge for clearly inadmissible cases; committees of three for well-established case law; chambers of seven for ordinary cases; and a Grand Chamber of seventeen for cases raising serious questions of interpretation or where a chamber relinquishes jurisdiction, or on referral within three months of a chamber judgment.</li><li>Judgments are binding on the respondent state under Article 46, and the Committee of Ministers supervises execution.</li></ul><h4>4. Admissibility and the volume problem</h4><p>The Court's success created its central difficulty: it receives far more applications than it can decide. Admissibility criteria under Article 35 are the filter.</p><ol><li>Exhaustion of domestic remedies.</li><li>Lodged within four months of the final domestic decision, reduced from six months by Protocol 15.</li><li>Not anonymous.</li><li>Not substantially the same as a matter already examined or submitted to another international procedure.</li><li>Not incompatible with the Convention, manifestly ill-founded, or an abuse of the right of application.</li><li>The applicant has suffered a significant disadvantage, unless respect for human rights requires examination on the merits.</li></ol><p>The significant disadvantage criterion, added by Protocol 14, was designed to allow the Court to decline trivial cases. The great majority of applications are declared inadmissible by a single judge, which is how the system copes.</p><div class=\"study-callout\"><strong>Note:</strong> The pilot judgment procedure is the Court's other structural response. Where many applications arise from the same systemic defect, the Court selects one, identifies the underlying problem, orders the state to introduce a general remedy within a time limit, and adjourns the similar cases. Broniowski v Poland in 2004 was the first. This technique has been influential well beyond Europe.</div><h4>5. The margin of appreciation</h4><div class=\"study-defn\"><strong>Margin of appreciation:</strong> The latitude allowed to national authorities in assessing whether an interference with a qualified right is necessary, reflecting their better position to evaluate local conditions and needs.</div><p>Its width varies with four factors.</p><div class=\"table-wrap\"><table class=\"study-table\"><thead><tr><th>Factor</th><th>Wider margin</th><th>Narrower margin</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>European consensus</td><td>No consensus among states</td><td>Clear consensus</td></tr><tr><td>Nature of the right</td><td>Economic and social policy, planning, taxation</td><td>Intimate aspects of private life, core political speech</td></tr><tr><td>Aim pursued</td><td>National security, protection of morals</td><td>Maintaining the authority of the judiciary</td></tr><tr><td>Quality of domestic process</td><td>Careful legislative or judicial balancing already done</td><td>No proper consideration of the competing interests</td></tr></tbody></table></div><p>The doctrine is defended as making subsidiarity workable and criticised as allowing the Court to avoid difficult questions. Protocol 15 put it in the preamble, which supporters read as confirmation and critics as an invitation to defer more.</p><div class=\"study-callout\"><strong>Example:</strong> In cases about the wearing of religious dress in public institutions, the Court has generally allowed a wide margin, citing the absence of European consensus and the sensitivity of the relationship between state and religion in each country. In cases about criminalising consensual private sexual conduct it has allowed almost none, because the conduct concerns the most intimate aspect of private life and a clear consensus exists. The same doctrine produces opposite outcomes because the four factors point in opposite directions.</div><h4>6. The living instrument doctrine</h4><div class=\"study-defn\"><strong>Living instrument:</strong> The principle that the Convention must be interpreted in the light of present-day conditions, not the intentions of its 1950 drafters.</div><p>Stated in Tyrer v United Kingdom in 1978, where judicial corporal punishment in the Isle of Man was held to be degrading despite having been unremarkable when the Convention was drafted. The doctrine has since supported developments on transgender recognition, the treatment of children born outside marriage, and the effective abolition of the death penalty in the Convention system.</p><p>It is constrained. The Court will not read in a right the Convention does not contain, and it looks for evidence of changed conditions in the practice of member states and in international materials rather than asserting change by fiat. That evidentiary discipline is what distinguishes evolutive interpretation from judicial legislation, and it is the point to make against the standard criticism.</p><h4>7. Execution of judgments</h4><ul><li>Article 46 obliges the state to abide by the final judgment; the Committee of Ministers supervises execution.</li><li>Execution has three components: payment of just satisfaction awarded under Article 41; individual measures to restore the applicant's position, such as reopening proceedings or releasing a detainee; and general measures to prevent repetition, such as legislative change.</li><li>Protocol 14 added an infringement procedure: the Committee of Ministers may, by a two-thirds majority, refer to the Court the question whether a state has failed to comply. It has been used once, in respect of Azerbaijan.</li><li>The backlog of unexecuted judgments, particularly those requiring general measures, is the system's most serious ongoing weakness.</li></ul><p>Russia was expelled from the Council of Europe in March 2022 and ceased to be a party to the Convention in September 2022. The Court retains jurisdiction over acts occurring before that date, but execution of those judgments is not occurring. This is the starkest illustration of the system's ultimate dependence on membership.</p><h4>8. How the examiner&#x27;s question should be answered</h4><p>Questions on Europe usually ask about the margin of appreciation or the Court's effectiveness. On the margin, always give the four factors and show them producing different outcomes in different cases, rather than describing the doctrine in the abstract. On effectiveness, give the volume figures conceptually, the pilot judgment response, the execution record and the Russian withdrawal, and reach a judgment. Do not confuse the ECHR with the European Union's Charter of Fundamental Rights, which is a separate instrument applying to EU institutions and to member states when implementing EU law.</p><h4>Summary</h4><ul><li>The ECHR dates from 1950 and is supervised by a single permanent Court since 1998, with 46 states parties.</li><li>Admissibility under Article 35 includes exhaustion, a four-month limit and the significant disadvantage criterion added by Protocol 14.</li><li>The pilot judgment procedure addresses systemic defects producing repetitive applications.</li><li>The margin of appreciation varies with European consensus, the nature of the right, the aim pursued and the quality of the domestic process.</li><li>The living instrument doctrine allows evolutive interpretation, disciplined by evidence of changed state practice.</li><li>Execution is supervised by the Committee of Ministers and requires payment, individual measures and general measures.</li></ul><h4>Key Terms</h4><div class=\"table-wrap\"><table class=\"study-table\"><thead><tr><th>Term</th><th>Meaning</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Margin of appreciation</td><td>The latitude allowed to national authorities in assessing the necessity of an interference with a qualified right.</td></tr><tr><td>Living instrument</td><td>The principle that the Convention is interpreted in light of present-day conditions.</td></tr><tr><td>Pilot judgment</td><td>A judgment identifying a systemic defect and requiring a general remedy, with similar cases adjourned.</td></tr><tr><td>Just satisfaction</td><td>Compensation awarded under Article 41 where domestic law allows only partial reparation.</td></tr><tr><td>Committee of Ministers</td><td>The Council of Europe body supervising execution of the Court&#x27;s judgments.</td></tr></tbody></table></div><h4>Review Questions</h4><h5>Multiple choice</h5><div class=\"study-mcq\"><p class=\"mcq-q\">Which factor narrows the margin of appreciation?</p><ol type=\"A\"><li>A clear consensus among European states on the question</li><li>A national security justification</li><li>The absence of any European consensus</li><li>A matter of economic policy</li></ol><details class=\"mcq-a\"><summary>Show answer</summary><p><strong>Answer: A.</strong> The margin is also narrow where the case concerns intimate aspects of private life or core political expression.</p></details></div><div class=\"study-mcq\"><p class=\"mcq-q\">What is the purpose of the pilot judgment procedure?</p><ol type=\"A\"><li>To identify a systemic defect, require a general remedy, and adjourn similar cases</li><li>To expedite single-judge decisions</li><li>To allow advisory opinions</li><li>To reduce the time limit for applications</li></ol><details class=\"mcq-a\"><summary>Show answer</summary><p><strong>Answer: A.</strong> Broniowski v Poland in 2004 was the first, and the technique has been adopted elsewhere.</p></details></div><div class=\"study-mcq\"><p class=\"mcq-q\">What did Tyrer v United Kingdom establish?</p><ol type=\"A\"><li>The Convention is a living instrument to be interpreted in light of present-day conditions</li><li>The margin of appreciation applies to corporal punishment</li><li>States may derogate from Article 3</li><li>Exhaustion is not required for degrading treatment claims</li></ol><details class=\"mcq-a\"><summary>Show answer</summary><p><strong>Answer: A.</strong> Judicial corporal punishment was held degrading despite being unremarkable when the Convention was drafted.</p></details></div><div class=\"study-mcq\"><p class=\"mcq-q\">What are the three components of executing a European Court judgment?</p><ol type=\"A\"><li>Payment of just satisfaction, individual measures and general measures</li><li>Apology, payment and legislative change</li><li>Payment, publication and reporting</li><li>Reopening proceedings, compensation and prosecution</li></ol><details class=\"mcq-a\"><summary>Show answer</summary><p><strong>Answer: A.</strong> Individual measures restore the applicant&#x27;s position; general measures prevent repetition and are the component most often outstanding.</p></details></div><h5>Short answer</h5><div class=\"study-sa\"><p class=\"sa-q\">Explain the four factors that determine the width of the margin of appreciation.</p><details class=\"sa-a\"><summary>Model answer</summary><p>First, European consensus: where states have converged on an answer the margin narrows sharply, and where practice varies widely it broadens. Second, the nature of the right and the interest at stake: intimate aspects of private life and core political expression attract almost no margin, while economic, planning and taxation policy attract a wide one. Third, the aim pursued: protection of morals and national security are treated as areas where national authorities are better placed, while maintaining the authority of the judiciary is not. Fourth, the quality of the domestic decision-making process: where the legislature or courts have genuinely weighed the competing interests, the Court defers more than where no such balancing occurred.</p></details></div><div class=\"study-sa\"><p class=\"sa-q\">Describe how a European Court judgment is executed and the main weakness of the process.</p><details class=\"sa-a\"><summary>Model answer</summary><p>Article 46 binds the respondent state to abide by the judgment and the Committee of Ministers supervises. Execution has three parts: payment of any just satisfaction awarded under Article 41; individual measures restoring the applicant&#x27;s position, such as reopening proceedings, releasing a detainee or expunging a record; and general measures preventing repetition, typically legislative or administrative reform. Protocol 14 added an infringement procedure allowing referral back to the Court where a state refuses to comply, used once in respect of Azerbaijan. The main weakness is the accumulation of judgments requiring general measures that remain unexecuted for years, because payment is easy and structural reform is not.</p></details></div><h4>Class Activity</h4><ol><li>Take two European Court cases reaching opposite conclusions on similar facts. Explain the different result by reference to the four margin of appreciation factors.</li><li>Find one pilot judgment. Summarise the systemic defect identified, the general measure required and whether the state complied within the deadline.</li></ol><h4>Assignment</h4><p>Write two pages on the European human rights system. Describe the Convention, its main protocols and the Court&#x27;s structure and formations, explain the admissibility criteria and how the Court manages case volume including pilot judgments, analyse the margin of appreciation with its four factors and the living instrument doctrine with its constraints, and describe execution of judgments and the system&#x27;s principal weaknesses.</p><p class=\"study-print\"><strong>Printable:</strong> use your browser\u2019s Print \u2192 Save as PDF to keep an offline copy.</p></div>",
  "The Inter-American Human Rights System": "<div class=\"study-note\"><div class=\"revision-banner\"><strong>Human Rights and International Humanitarian Law</strong><span>Module 5: Regional Human Rights Systems</span></div><h3 class=\"lesson-note-title\">LESSON NOTE: THE INTER-AMERICAN HUMAN RIGHTS SYSTEM</h3><div class=\"table-wrap\"><table class=\"study-table\"><thead><tr><th>Subject</th><th>Topic</th><th>Level</th><th>Lesson duration</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Human Rights and International Humanitarian Law</td><td>The Inter-American Human Rights System</td><td>Beginner to Advanced</td><td>45 minutes</td></tr></tbody></table></div><h4>Learning objectives</h4><p>By the end of this lesson you should be able to:</p><ul><li>Describe the instruments and organs of the Inter-American system</li><li>Explain the Commission&#x27;s dual role and the path of a case</li><li>Describe the Court&#x27;s distinctive reparations jurisprudence</li><li>Explain the conventionality control doctrine and the amnesty cases</li></ul><h4>1. Introduction</h4><p>The Inter-American system was shaped by the military dictatorships of the 1970s and 1980s. Its preoccupations, enforced disappearance, amnesty laws, state terror and impunity, produced the most ambitious reparations jurisprudence of any human rights court and a doctrine, conventionality control, that has no equivalent elsewhere.</p><h4>2. The instruments</h4><div class=\"table-wrap\"><table class=\"study-table\"><thead><tr><th>Instrument</th><th>Year</th><th>Note</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>American Declaration of the Rights and Duties of Man</td><td>1948</td><td>Adopted months before the UDHR; binds all OAS members through the OAS Charter, including those not party to the Convention</td></tr><tr><td>American Convention on Human Rights (Pact of San Jose)</td><td>1969, in force 1978</td><td>The principal treaty; around 23 states parties</td></tr><tr><td>Protocol of San Salvador</td><td>1988</td><td>Economic, social and cultural rights; direct petition available only for trade union rights and education</td></tr><tr><td>Protocol on abolition of the death penalty</td><td>1990</td><td></td></tr><tr><td>Inter-American Convention to Prevent and Punish Torture</td><td>1985</td><td></td></tr><tr><td>Inter-American Convention on Forced Disappearance of Persons</td><td>1994</td><td>The first treaty anywhere on this subject</td></tr><tr><td>Convention of Belem do Para on violence against women</td><td>1994</td><td>The first binding treaty on violence against women; allows individual petitions on Article 7</td></tr><tr><td>Conventions against racism and intolerance, and on older persons</td><td>2013, 2015</td><td></td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"study-callout\"><strong>Note:</strong> The American Declaration matters more than a declaration normally would. The Court held in a 1989 advisory opinion that it is a source of international obligation for OAS member states through the OAS Charter, which means the Commission can examine petitions against states that never ratified the Convention, including the United States and several Caribbean states. This is the reason the Commission's reach exceeds the Court's.</div><h4>3. The two organs</h4><div class=\"table-wrap\"><table class=\"study-table\"><thead><tr><th></th><th>Inter-American Commission</th><th>Inter-American Court</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Established</td><td>1959, Charter organ since 1967</td><td>1979, under the Convention</td></tr><tr><td>Seat</td><td>Washington DC</td><td>San Jose, Costa Rica</td></tr><tr><td>Membership</td><td>Seven, in personal capacity</td><td>Seven judges</td></tr><tr><td>Covers</td><td>All 35 OAS member states, under the Declaration or the Convention</td><td>Only states party to the Convention that have accepted its contentious jurisdiction</td></tr><tr><td>Functions</td><td>Promotion, country reports, on-site visits, thematic rapporteurships, precautionary measures, and processing petitions</td><td>Contentious cases, advisory opinions, provisional measures</td></tr><tr><td>Individual access</td><td>Direct petition</td><td>None; only the Commission or a state may bring a case</td></tr></tbody></table></div><h4>4. The path of a case</h4><ol><li><strong>Petition to the Commission</strong>, after exhaustion of domestic remedies, within six months of final decision. Exceptions to exhaustion apply where domestic law lacks due process, access was denied, or there is unwarranted delay.</li><li><strong>Admissibility decision.</strong> The Commission may also grant precautionary measures at any stage where there is a serious and urgent situation presenting a risk of irreparable harm.</li><li><strong>Merits.</strong> The Commission investigates, may hold hearings and conduct on-site visits, and attempts friendly settlement.</li><li><strong>Article 50 report.</strong> Confidential, with recommendations and a deadline.</li><li><strong>If the state does not comply</strong>, the Commission may refer the case to the Court, provided the state has accepted its contentious jurisdiction, or publish an Article 51 report.</li><li><strong>Court proceedings.</strong> Since the 2001 rules reform, the victims have autonomous standing before the Court once the case is referred, presenting their own arguments and evidence independently of the Commission.</li><li><strong>Judgment.</strong> Binding and final; the Court itself monitors compliance through compliance hearings and orders, which is unusual and effective.</li></ol><h4>5. Reparations</h4><p>Article 63(1) of the Convention empowers the Court to rule that the injured party be ensured the enjoyment of the right violated and, if appropriate, that the consequences be remedied and fair compensation paid. The Court has read this expansively, producing the richest reparations practice of any human rights tribunal.</p><div class=\"table-wrap\"><table class=\"study-table\"><thead><tr><th>Form</th><th>Content</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Restitution</td><td>Restoring the prior situation: reinstatement, return of property, annulment of a conviction</td></tr><tr><td>Compensation</td><td>Material damage, including lost earnings and consequential expenses, and moral damage</td></tr><tr><td>Rehabilitation</td><td>Medical and psychological care for victims and families, often ordered as a state-funded programme</td></tr><tr><td>Satisfaction</td><td>Public acknowledgment of responsibility, apology, publication of the judgment, memorials, restoration of the victim's memory</td></tr><tr><td>Guarantees of non-repetition</td><td>Legislative reform, training of officials, creation of institutions, human rights education programmes</td></tr><tr><td>Duty to investigate</td><td>An express order to investigate, prosecute and punish those responsible, with the outcome monitored</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"study-callout\"><strong>Example:</strong> In Velasquez Rodriguez v Honduras (1988), the Court's first contentious judgment, a student was disappeared by state agents. The Court established the due diligence doctrine: a state is responsible not only for acts of its agents but for failing to prevent, investigate, punish and remedy violations, including those by private actors. It awarded compensation and required investigation. That single judgment supplied the analytical framework used by every human rights body since for state responsibility in relation to private harm.</div><h4>6. Amnesty laws and conventionality control</h4><p>The system's defining contribution is its treatment of impunity.</p><ol><li><strong>Barrios Altos v Peru (2001).</strong> The Court held that amnesty provisions, prescription, and measures designed to eliminate responsibility for grave human rights violations such as torture, extrajudicial execution and disappearance are inadmissible because they prevent investigation and punishment, deny victims the truth and a remedy, and are incompatible with the Convention. It held the Peruvian amnesty laws lacked legal effect.</li><li><strong>Almonacid Arellano v Chile (2006).</strong> The Court articulated conventionality control: domestic judges are bound to ensure that the Convention's provisions are not undermined by contrary domestic law, and must therefore disapply national provisions incompatible with the Convention, taking account of the Court's own interpretation.</li><li><strong>Gelman v Uruguay (2011).</strong> The Court held that the incompatibility of an amnesty law with the Convention is not cured by its approval in a referendum, because the protection of fundamental rights is a limit on majority rule.</li><li><strong>The doctrine has spread.</strong> National supreme courts across the region have applied conventionality control, annulling amnesty laws and reopening prosecutions decades after the events.</li></ol><div class=\"study-defn\"><strong>Conventionality control:</strong> The obligation on domestic judges to review national law for compatibility with the American Convention, as interpreted by the Inter-American Court, and to disapply incompatible provisions.</div><div class=\"study-callout\"><strong>Note:</strong> Compare this with Europe. The European Court does not claim that national judges must disapply incompatible statutes; it leaves the means of execution to the state. Conventionality control is therefore the most far-reaching claim of authority made by any regional human rights court, and it explains both the system's impact and the political resistance it attracts.</div><h4>7. Strengths and weaknesses</h4><div class=\"table-wrap\"><table class=\"study-table\"><thead><tr><th>Strength</th><th>Weakness</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>The most developed reparations jurisprudence anywhere</td><td>Few states parties relative to OAS membership; major states have not ratified</td></tr><tr><td>Conventionality control gives judgments direct domestic effect</td><td>Trinidad and Tobago and Venezuela denounced the Convention</td></tr><tr><td>The Court monitors its own compliance</td><td>Chronic under-funding, with much of the budget from voluntary contributions</td></tr><tr><td>Victims have autonomous standing before the Court</td><td>Very long delays through the Commission stage</td></tr><tr><td>Precautionary and provisional measures used effectively in urgent cases</td><td>Political attacks on the Commission's budget and mandate during reform processes</td></tr></tbody></table></div><h4>8. How the examiner&#x27;s question should be answered</h4><p>For a comparative question, the Inter-American system's three distinctive contributions are the answer: the due diligence doctrine from Velasquez Rodriguez, the invalidity of amnesties for grave violations from Barrios Altos, and conventionality control from Almonacid Arellano. Name the case with each doctrine. Then note the structural point: the Commission covers all OAS states through the Declaration while the Court covers only Convention parties accepting its jurisdiction, which is why the Commission remains central here in a way the European Commission did not.</p><h4>Summary</h4><ul><li>The Inter-American system rests on the 1948 American Declaration, binding through the OAS Charter, and the 1969 American Convention.</li><li>The Commission covers all OAS members and receives petitions directly; the Court hears only cases referred by the Commission or a state.</li><li>Velasquez Rodriguez established the due diligence doctrine of state responsibility for failing to prevent, investigate, punish and remedy.</li><li>The Court&#x27;s reparations practice covers restitution, compensation, rehabilitation, satisfaction, guarantees of non-repetition and a duty to investigate.</li><li>Barrios Altos held amnesties for grave violations invalid, and Almonacid Arellano created conventionality control binding domestic judges.</li><li>Gelman held that a referendum cannot cure an amnesty&#x27;s incompatibility with the Convention.</li></ul><h4>Key Terms</h4><div class=\"table-wrap\"><table class=\"study-table\"><thead><tr><th>Term</th><th>Meaning</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Conventionality control</td><td>The duty of domestic judges to disapply national law incompatible with the American Convention as interpreted by the Court.</td></tr><tr><td>Due diligence</td><td>The standard from Velasquez Rodriguez requiring states to prevent, investigate, punish and remedy violations including by private actors.</td></tr><tr><td>Guarantees of non-repetition</td><td>Reparations measures such as legislative reform and official training designed to prevent recurrence.</td></tr><tr><td>Precautionary measures</td><td>Urgent protective measures ordered by the Commission where there is risk of irreparable harm.</td></tr></tbody></table></div><h4>Review Questions</h4><h5>Multiple choice</h5><div class=\"study-mcq\"><p class=\"mcq-q\">What did the Inter-American Court establish in Velasquez Rodriguez?</p><ol type=\"A\"><li>The due diligence doctrine of state responsibility to prevent, investigate, punish and remedy</li><li>That amnesties are invalid</li><li>Conventionality control</li><li>That victims have autonomous standing</li></ol><details class=\"mcq-a\"><summary>Show answer</summary><p><strong>Answer: A.</strong> It is the analytical foundation used by human rights bodies worldwide for responsibility in relation to private harm.</p></details></div><div class=\"study-mcq\"><p class=\"mcq-q\">What is conventionality control?</p><ol type=\"A\"><li>The duty of domestic judges to disapply national law incompatible with the American Convention</li><li>The Court&#x27;s review of treaty ratifications</li><li>Supervision of compliance by the OAS General Assembly</li><li>A procedure for advisory opinions</li></ol><details class=\"mcq-a\"><summary>Show answer</summary><p><strong>Answer: A.</strong> Articulated in Almonacid Arellano v Chile, it is the most far-reaching authority claim made by any regional human rights court.</p></details></div><div class=\"study-mcq\"><p class=\"mcq-q\">In Gelman v Uruguay, what did the Court hold about an amnesty approved by referendum?</p><ol type=\"A\"><li>Popular approval does not cure incompatibility with the Convention</li><li>A referendum validates an otherwise invalid amnesty</li><li>Referendums are outside the Court&#x27;s jurisdiction</li><li>Only parliamentary amnesties are invalid</li></ol><details class=\"mcq-a\"><summary>Show answer</summary><p><strong>Answer: A.</strong> The protection of fundamental rights operates as a limit on majority rule.</p></details></div><div class=\"study-mcq\"><p class=\"mcq-q\">Why can the Commission examine petitions against states that never ratified the American Convention?</p><ol type=\"A\"><li>The American Declaration binds all OAS members through the OAS Charter</li><li>The Convention applies to all OAS members automatically</li><li>The Court extended its jurisdiction by advisory opinion</li><li>Those states accepted jurisdiction separately</li></ol><details class=\"mcq-a\"><summary>Show answer</summary><p><strong>Answer: A.</strong> A 1989 advisory opinion confirmed the Declaration as a source of obligation, which is why the Commission&#x27;s reach exceeds the Court&#x27;s.</p></details></div><h5>Short answer</h5><div class=\"study-sa\"><p class=\"sa-q\">Describe the six forms of reparation the Inter-American Court orders.</p><details class=\"sa-a\"><summary>Model answer</summary><p>Restitution restores the prior situation, for example by reinstating a dismissed employee or annulling a conviction. Compensation covers material damage, including lost earnings and expenses, and moral damage. Rehabilitation provides medical and psychological care to victims and families, frequently as a state-funded programme. Satisfaction includes public acknowledgment of responsibility, apology, publication of the judgment and memorials restoring the victim&#x27;s memory. Guarantees of non-repetition require structural change such as legislative reform, official training and new institutions. Finally the Court orders an express duty to investigate, prosecute and punish those responsible, and monitors the outcome through compliance proceedings.</p></details></div><div class=\"study-sa\"><p class=\"sa-q\">Explain the Inter-American Court&#x27;s position on amnesty laws.</p><details class=\"sa-a\"><summary>Model answer</summary><p>In Barrios Altos v Peru the Court held that amnesty provisions, prescription periods and other measures designed to eliminate responsibility for grave human rights violations such as torture, extrajudicial execution and enforced disappearance are inadmissible, because they prevent investigation and punishment, deny victims access to the truth and to a remedy, and are therefore incompatible with the Convention. It declared the Peruvian laws devoid of legal effect. In Gelman v Uruguay it added that approval of such an amnesty by referendum does not cure the incompatibility, because fundamental rights protection limits what a majority may decide. Through conventionality control, domestic judges across the region have applied these holdings to reopen prosecutions.</p></details></div><h4>Class Activity</h4><ol><li>Read a summary of one Inter-American Court judgment. List every reparation ordered and classify each under the six headings in this lesson.</li><li>Compare conventionality control with the European approach to execution of judgments. Write one page on which model gives a regional court more real authority and at what cost.</li></ol><h4>Assignment</h4><p>Write two pages on the Inter-American human rights system. Describe the main instruments including the status of the American Declaration, set out the two organs and the path of a case, explain the Court&#x27;s reparations jurisprudence with its six forms, analyse the amnesty cases and conventionality control naming the leading decisions, and assess the system&#x27;s strengths and weaknesses.</p><p class=\"study-print\"><strong>Printable:</strong> use your browser\u2019s Print \u2192 Save as PDF to keep an offline copy.</p></div>",
  "Systems in Asia and the Arab World": "<div class=\"study-note\"><div class=\"revision-banner\"><strong>Human Rights and International Humanitarian Law</strong><span>Module 5: Regional Human Rights Systems</span></div><h3 class=\"lesson-note-title\">LESSON NOTE: SYSTEMS IN ASIA AND THE ARAB WORLD</h3><div class=\"table-wrap\"><table class=\"study-table\"><thead><tr><th>Subject</th><th>Topic</th><th>Level</th><th>Lesson duration</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Human Rights and International Humanitarian Law</td><td>Systems in Asia and the Arab World</td><td>Beginner to Advanced</td><td>40 minutes</td></tr></tbody></table></div><h4>Learning objectives</h4><p>By the end of this lesson you should be able to:</p><ul><li>Describe the ASEAN human rights arrangement and its limits</li><li>Describe the Arab Charter and its supervisory machinery</li><li>Explain why Asia has no region-wide system</li><li>Evaluate the Asian values debate</li></ul><h4>1. Introduction</h4><p>Two regions lack the machinery the other three have. Understanding why is more instructive than cataloguing what does not exist, because the reasons expose the political conditions a regional system requires.</p><h4>2. ASEAN</h4><div class=\"table-wrap\"><table class=\"study-table\"><thead><tr><th>Element</th><th>Detail</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>ASEAN Charter</td><td>2008; Article 14 provided for a human rights body</td></tr><tr><td>AICHR</td><td>ASEAN Intergovernmental Commission on Human Rights, established 2009</td></tr><tr><td>Composition</td><td>One representative per member state, appointed by and accountable to the appointing government</td></tr><tr><td>Mandate</td><td>Promotional and consultative; the terms of reference give it no protection mandate</td></tr><tr><td>Complaints</td><td>None; it cannot receive or investigate individual complaints</td></tr><tr><td>Declaration</td><td>ASEAN Human Rights Declaration, 2012</td></tr><tr><td>Related bodies</td><td>ACWC on women and children, 2010; ACMW on migrant workers</td></tr></tbody></table></div><p>The AICHR's limits follow from the ASEAN way: consensus decision-making and non-interference in internal affairs, both entrenched in the ASEAN Charter. A body composed of government appointees, deciding by consensus, in an organisation committed to non-interference, cannot criticise a member state.</p><div class=\"study-callout\"><strong>Note:</strong> The 2012 Declaration attracted substantial criticism, including from the UN High Commissioner. The objections centred on Article 6, which makes the enjoyment of rights balanced with the performance of duties; Article 7, which subjects universal rights to realisation in a regional and national context bearing in mind different political, economic, legal, social, cultural, historical and religious backgrounds; and Article 8, whose general limitation clause allows restrictions on grounds including national security and public morality without a necessity or proportionality requirement. Together these were read as authorising states to define the rights down.</div><h4>3. The Arab system</h4><div class=\"table-wrap\"><table class=\"study-table\"><thead><tr><th>Element</th><th>Detail</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Instrument</td><td>Arab Charter on Human Rights, adopted 1994, never in force; revised 2004, in force 2008</td></tr><tr><td>Body</td><td>Arab Human Rights Committee, seven experts, reviewing periodic state reports</td></tr><tr><td>Complaints</td><td>No individual complaints procedure</td></tr><tr><td>Court</td><td>Statute of the Arab Court of Human Rights approved 2014; not in force; provides for state access only, with individual access dependent on an optional declaration</td></tr><tr><td>Content</td><td>Covers a wide range of civil, political, economic and social rights; prohibits torture; provides for fair trial</td></tr></tbody></table></div><p>The 2004 revision improved substantially on the 1994 text but retained provisions attracting criticism, including permitting the death penalty for offences beyond the most serious crimes as understood in the ICCPR, permitting its application to persons under eighteen where national law so provides, and a general subordination of certain rights to Sharia and to national legislation. The then High Commissioner welcomed the modernisation while noting these inconsistencies with international standards.</p><p>The proposed Court has been criticised because states alone may bring cases, with individual and NGO access requiring an optional declaration and subject to the state's accreditation of the NGO. Critics have argued that as drafted it would provide the appearance of a system without the function.</p><h4>4. Why has Asia produced no region-wide system?</h4><ol><li><strong>No region-wide organisation.</strong> Europe had the Council of Europe, the Americas the OAS, Africa the OAU. Asia has no equivalent political body to host a human rights treaty, and ASEAN covers only Southeast Asia.</li><li><strong>Diversity.</strong> Asia contains more legal traditions, religions, political systems and levels of development than any other region, making shared standards harder to negotiate.</li><li><strong>Strong sovereignty doctrines.</strong> Many Asian states emerged from colonialism with an emphasis on non-interference that persists in their foreign policy.</li><li><strong>Absence of a founding catastrophe with a regional response.</strong> Europe's system followed the Second World War, and Latin America's court became effective after the dictatorships. Asia's comparable events did not produce a regional institutional response.</li><li><strong>Sub-regional fragmentation.</strong> South Asia, Southeast Asia, East Asia, Central Asia and the Pacific have little institutional overlap.</li></ol><p>There are partial substitutes: strong national human rights institutions in several states, active national courts including the Indian Supreme Court's public interest litigation, and a dense regional NGO network. The absence of a treaty system does not mean the absence of protection, and saying so avoids the caricature.</p><h4>5. The Asian values debate</h4><p>The debate crystallised around the 1993 Bangkok Declaration, adopted by Asian states before the Vienna World Conference. Its claims and the answers to them are as follows.</p><div class=\"table-wrap\"><table class=\"study-table\"><thead><tr><th>Claim</th><th>Response</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Rights must be considered in the context of national and regional particularities and various historical, cultural and religious backgrounds</td><td>Vienna Declaration paragraph 5 accepts that these must be borne in mind but affirms that it is the duty of states to promote and protect all human rights regardless of their systems</td></tr><tr><td>Community and order take priority over individual claims</td><td>The claim is contested within Asia; it was advanced most strongly by governments and least by the region's own human rights movements, dissidents and opposition parties</td></tr><tr><td>Economic development must come before civil and political rights</td><td>The empirical claim is weak; the Vienna Declaration states that the lack of development may not be invoked to justify the abridgement of internationally recognised human rights</td></tr><tr><td>Rights discourse is a Western imposition</td><td>Every region's traditions contain resources on dignity and obligation; and the states making the argument had themselves ratified the treaties</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"study-callout\"><strong>Note:</strong> The strongest version of the argument deserves a fair hearing: implementation genuinely does vary with context, and a system that ignores local institutions will fail. The weak version, that the state may define which rights its people have, is what Vienna rejected. Distinguishing the two is the mark of a good answer rather than dismissing the whole debate.</div><h4>6. How the examiner&#x27;s question should be answered</h4><p>A question here is usually 'why does Asia lack a regional system' or 'evaluate the Asian values debate'. For the first, give the five structural reasons and then note the partial substitutes so the answer is not merely negative. For the second, state the Bangkok claims accurately, give the Vienna response with the paragraph reference, distinguish the strong from the weak version of the argument, and note that the debate has moved on: the current questions are about implementation and resources rather than about whether rights apply.</p><h4>Summary</h4><ul><li>ASEAN&#x27;s AICHR is promotional only, composed of government appointees, and has no complaints procedure.</li><li>The 2012 ASEAN Declaration was criticised for balancing rights against duties, subjecting universality to national context, and a broad limitation clause.</li><li>The Arab Charter, in force since 2008, is supervised by a reporting committee; the proposed Arab Court is not operational and provides mainly state access.</li><li>Asia lacks a region-wide system because it has no region-wide organisation, immense diversity, strong sovereignty doctrines and sub-regional fragmentation.</li><li>The Bangkok Declaration&#x27;s claims were answered at Vienna, which accepted that context matters but affirmed the duty to protect all rights.</li></ul><h4>Key Terms</h4><div class=\"table-wrap\"><table class=\"study-table\"><thead><tr><th>Term</th><th>Meaning</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>AICHR</td><td>The ASEAN Intergovernmental Commission on Human Rights, a promotional body without complaints jurisdiction.</td></tr><tr><td>ASEAN way</td><td>Decision-making by consensus with non-interference in members&#x27; internal affairs.</td></tr><tr><td>Arab Charter on Human Rights</td><td>The 2004 revised regional instrument, in force 2008, supervised by a reporting committee.</td></tr><tr><td>Bangkok Declaration</td><td>The 1993 Asian regional statement emphasising particularities, answered by the Vienna Declaration.</td></tr></tbody></table></div><h4>Review Questions</h4><h5>Multiple choice</h5><div class=\"study-mcq\"><p class=\"mcq-q\">Why can the AICHR not address violations by a member state?</p><ol type=\"A\"><li>It is promotional only, composed of government appointees, and operates by consensus under a non-interference principle</li><li>It lacks funding</li><li>Its members are not lawyers</li><li>Its mandate expired</li></ol><details class=\"mcq-a\"><summary>Show answer</summary><p><strong>Answer: A.</strong> Its terms of reference give it no protection mandate and no power to receive complaints.</p></details></div><div class=\"study-mcq\"><p class=\"mcq-q\">What is the position of the proposed Arab Court of Human Rights?</p><ol type=\"A\"><li>Its statute was approved in 2014 but it is not operational and provides principally for state access</li><li>It has been operating since 2008</li><li>It has the same jurisdiction as the African Court</li><li>It was rejected by the Arab League</li></ol><details class=\"mcq-a\"><summary>Show answer</summary><p><strong>Answer: A.</strong> Individual and NGO access would depend on an optional declaration and state accreditation, which critics say would give the appearance of a system without the function.</p></details></div><div class=\"study-mcq\"><p class=\"mcq-q\">Which is a structural reason Asia has no region-wide human rights system?</p><ol type=\"A\"><li>There is no Asia-wide political organisation able to host one</li><li>Asian states have not ratified UN treaties</li><li>Asian constitutions prohibit it</li><li>The UN excluded Asia from regional arrangements</li></ol><details class=\"mcq-a\"><summary>Show answer</summary><p><strong>Answer: A.</strong> Europe had the Council of Europe, the Americas the OAS and Africa the OAU; ASEAN covers only Southeast Asia.</p></details></div><div class=\"study-mcq\"><p class=\"mcq-q\">How did the Vienna Declaration answer the Bangkok Declaration&#x27;s claim about particularities?</p><ol type=\"A\"><li>It accepted that particularities must be borne in mind but affirmed the duty of states to protect all human rights regardless of their systems</li><li>It rejected any relevance of culture</li><li>It endorsed the Bangkok position in full</li><li>It deferred the question to regional bodies</li></ol><details class=\"mcq-a\"><summary>Show answer</summary><p><strong>Answer: A.</strong> It also provided that the lack of development may not be invoked to justify abridging internationally recognised rights.</p></details></div><h5>Short answer</h5><div class=\"study-sa\"><p class=\"sa-q\">Give three criticisms of the 2012 ASEAN Human Rights Declaration.</p><details class=\"sa-a\"><summary>Model answer</summary><p>First, Article 6 balances the enjoyment of rights against the performance of corresponding duties, which allows a state to condition rights on approved conduct. Second, Article 7 states that the realisation of universal rights must be considered in the regional and national context bearing in mind different political, economic, legal, social, cultural, historical and religious backgrounds, which critics read as qualifying universality itself rather than merely acknowledging context. Third, Article 8 contains a general limitation clause permitting restrictions on broad grounds including national security and public morality without requiring that a limitation be necessary and proportionate, which is the safeguard that makes limitation clauses workable elsewhere.</p></details></div><div class=\"study-sa\"><p class=\"sa-q\">Distinguish the strong and weak versions of the Asian values argument.</p><details class=\"sa-a\"><summary>Model answer</summary><p>The defensible version holds that the implementation of human rights must take account of local institutions, resources and social organisation, and that a programme designed without reference to these will fail. That claim is compatible with the Vienna Declaration, which requires particularities to be borne in mind. The indefensible version holds that a state may decide which rights its people enjoy by reference to its own traditions, which would make the obligations self-defined and empty. Vienna rejected that expressly by affirming the duty of states to protect all human rights regardless of their political, economic and cultural systems. The distinction also matters empirically, because the strong version was advanced by governments while the region&#x27;s own human rights movements and opposition figures rejected it.</p></details></div><h4>Class Activity</h4><ol><li>Read the ASEAN Human Rights Declaration Articles 6 to 8. Rewrite each so that it conforms to the limitation standards used in the ICCPR, and explain each change.</li><li>Research one national human rights institution in Asia. Assess it against the Paris Principles and write half a page on whether it compensates for the absence of a regional system.</li></ol><h4>Assignment</h4><p>Write one and a half pages on human rights arrangements in Asia and the Arab world. Describe the ASEAN machinery and the criticisms of its 2012 Declaration, describe the Arab Charter and the proposed Arab Court, explain the structural reasons Asia has no region-wide system while noting the partial substitutes, and evaluate the Asian values debate distinguishing its defensible from its indefensible form.</p><p class=\"study-print\"><strong>Printable:</strong> use your browser\u2019s Print \u2192 Save as PDF to keep an offline copy.</p></div>",
  "Comparing the Regional Systems": "<div class=\"study-note\"><div class=\"revision-banner\"><strong>Human Rights and International Humanitarian Law</strong><span>Module 5: Regional Human Rights Systems</span></div><h3 class=\"lesson-note-title\">LESSON NOTE: COMPARING THE REGIONAL SYSTEMS</h3><div class=\"table-wrap\"><table class=\"study-table\"><thead><tr><th>Subject</th><th>Topic</th><th>Level</th><th>Lesson duration</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Human Rights and International Humanitarian Law</td><td>Comparing the Regional Systems</td><td>Beginner to Advanced</td><td>40 minutes</td></tr></tbody></table></div><h4>Learning objectives</h4><p>By the end of this lesson you should be able to:</p><ul><li>Compare the three established systems across defined axes</li><li>Explain what each system contributed to international human rights law</li><li>Assess relative effectiveness using appropriate criteria</li><li>Identify what each system could learn from the others</li></ul><h4>1. Introduction</h4><p>Comparison is a skill, not a list. A good comparative answer chooses axes, applies them consistently, explains differences by reference to each system's history, and reaches a judgement. This lesson supplies the axes and the judgements.</p><h4>2. The comparison across seven axes</h4><div class=\"table-wrap\"><table class=\"study-table\"><thead><tr><th>Axis</th><th>Europe</th><th>Americas</th><th>Africa</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Individual access to the court</td><td>Direct, as of right, against every party</td><td>None; only via the Commission</td><td>Only where the state made an Article 34(6) declaration</td></tr><tr><td>Bindingness</td><td>Binding judgments, Committee of Ministers supervises</td><td>Binding judgments, Court supervises its own compliance</td><td>Binding Court judgments; Commission issues recommendations</td></tr><tr><td>Subject matter</td><td>Civil and political in the ECHR; social rights in a separate charter</td><td>Civil and political in the Convention; social rights largely in a protocol with limited petition</td><td>All categories plus peoples' rights in one binding instrument</td></tr><tr><td>Remedies</td><td>Just satisfaction, individual and general measures</td><td>The most developed reparations practice of any tribunal</td><td>Developing; increasingly detailed orders in the Court</td></tr><tr><td>Domestic effect</td><td>Left to the state; no duty on judges to disapply statutes</td><td>Conventionality control obliges judges to disapply incompatible law</td><td>Varies with national constitutional arrangements</td></tr><tr><td>Case volume</td><td>Very high; the constraint is capacity</td><td>Moderate; the constraint is delay at the Commission stage</td><td>Low; the constraint is access</td></tr><tr><td>Principal weakness</td><td>Unexecuted general measures and political backlash</td><td>Few parties, denunciations and funding</td><td>Article 34(6) and resourcing</td></tr></tbody></table></div><h4>3. What each system contributed</h4><div class=\"table-wrap\"><table class=\"study-table\"><thead><tr><th>System</th><th>Contribution</th><th>Adopted elsewhere?</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Europe</td><td>Margin of appreciation</td><td>Cited by other bodies, though the African Commission and Inter-American Court apply it sparingly or not at all</td></tr><tr><td>Europe</td><td>Living instrument interpretation</td><td>Widely adopted, including by UN treaty bodies</td></tr><tr><td>Europe</td><td>Pilot judgment procedure</td><td>Adapted by the Inter-American Court and discussed in Africa</td></tr><tr><td>Americas</td><td>Due diligence for private acts (Velasquez Rodriguez)</td><td>Universal; adopted by the European Court, UN treaty bodies and CEDAW General Recommendation 19</td></tr><tr><td>Americas</td><td>Invalidity of amnesties for grave violations</td><td>Influential in transitional justice worldwide</td></tr><tr><td>Americas</td><td>Comprehensive reparations</td><td>Increasingly reflected in African Court orders and UN practice</td></tr><tr><td>Africa</td><td>Peoples' rights and collective land title</td><td>Cited well beyond the region, notably in indigenous rights litigation</td></tr><tr><td>Africa</td><td>Justiciability of social rights in a general instrument (SERAC)</td><td>Cited internationally as authority that these rights can be adjudicated</td></tr><tr><td>Africa</td><td>A court able to apply any treaty the state has ratified</td><td>Unique; no equivalent elsewhere</td></tr></tbody></table></div><h4>4. Measuring effectiveness</h4><p>Effectiveness cannot be measured by case numbers alone, which reward accessibility and penalise systems where states comply without litigation. Use four criteria together.</p><ol><li><strong>Access:</strong> can an ordinary victim actually reach the body? Europe scores highest, Africa lowest for the Court.</li><li><strong>Output quality:</strong> are the decisions reasoned, consistent and doctrinally developed? All three are strong; the Inter-American Court leads on remedies and the European on doctrine.</li><li><strong>Compliance:</strong> are judgments implemented? Europe leads on payment and individual measures, lags on general measures. The Inter-American record is mixed but its compliance monitoring is the most active. African compliance is weakest.</li><li><strong>Domestic absorption:</strong> do national courts and legislatures use the regional standard without being compelled? This is the deepest measure. The Inter-American system scores highest through conventionality control, Europe high through incorporation of the Convention in national law, Africa unevenly.</li></ol><div class=\"study-callout\"><strong>Note:</strong> A frequent examination trap is to conclude that Europe is simply the best system. The better answer notes that Europe operates among wealthy states with functioning courts, which is a precondition it did not create, and that the African and Inter-American systems face far harder conditions. Effectiveness relative to circumstances is a different ranking from effectiveness in absolute terms.</div><h4>5. Cross-learning</h4><div class=\"table-wrap\"><table class=\"study-table\"><thead><tr><th>System</th><th>Could adopt from</th><th>What</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Africa</td><td>Americas</td><td>Automatic individual access on ratification, removing the Article 34(6) filter</td></tr><tr><td>Africa</td><td>Europe</td><td>Pilot judgments to address systemic violations generating repetitive complaints</td></tr><tr><td>Europe</td><td>Americas</td><td>Fuller reparations, particularly guarantees of non-repetition and rehabilitation programmes</td></tr><tr><td>Europe</td><td>Africa</td><td>Bringing economic and social rights within the main instrument's enforcement</td></tr><tr><td>Americas</td><td>Europe</td><td>A larger membership and direct access to the Court, removing the Commission bottleneck</td></tr><tr><td>Americas</td><td>Africa</td><td>Sub-regional courts as an additional accessible route</td></tr></tbody></table></div><h4>6. A note on sub-regional courts</h4><p>Africa's sub-regional economic courts have acquired a human rights role that has no counterpart elsewhere.</p><ul><li>The ECOWAS Community Court of Justice has express human rights jurisdiction, direct individual access, and no exhaustion requirement, making it the most accessible international human rights forum in Africa.</li><li>The East African Court of Justice has decided human rights matters through the rule of law and good governance provisions of the EAC Treaty.</li><li>The SADC Tribunal decided the Campbell land cases against Zimbabwe and was suspended in 2010 and then reconstituted with jurisdiction limited to inter-state disputes, which is the clearest example anywhere of states dismantling a court that ruled against one of them.</li></ul><p>The SADC episode is worth stating carefully in any answer on effectiveness, because it shows that the constraint on regional human rights adjudication is ultimately political will, not legal design.</p><h4>7. How the examiner&#x27;s question should be answered</h4><p>Set out your axes at the start so the marker knows the structure. Compare across each axis rather than describing systems one after another. Explain at least two differences by reference to the region's history, because that shows understanding rather than memorisation. Include at least one African case and one Inter-American case by name. Conclude with a ranked judgement on a stated criterion, and note the difference between absolute effectiveness and effectiveness relative to the conditions each system operates in.</p><h4>Summary</h4><ul><li>Compare systems across access, bindingness, subject matter, remedies, domestic effect, volume and weaknesses rather than describing them in turn.</li><li>Europe contributed the margin of appreciation, living instrument interpretation and pilot judgments.</li><li>The Americas contributed due diligence, the invalidity of amnesties and comprehensive reparations.</li><li>Africa contributed peoples&#x27; rights, the justiciability of social rights in a general instrument, and a court applying any ratified treaty.</li><li>Effectiveness should be measured by access, output quality, compliance and domestic absorption together.</li><li>The SADC Tribunal&#x27;s suspension shows that political will, not legal design, is the ultimate constraint.</li></ul><h4>Key Terms</h4><div class=\"table-wrap\"><table class=\"study-table\"><thead><tr><th>Term</th><th>Meaning</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Comparative axis</td><td>A defined criterion applied consistently across systems to structure a comparison.</td></tr><tr><td>Domestic absorption</td><td>The extent to which national institutions apply a regional standard without being compelled.</td></tr><tr><td>Sub-regional court</td><td>An economic community court exercising human rights jurisdiction, as in ECOWAS and the EAC.</td></tr><tr><td>Effectiveness relative to circumstances</td><td>Assessment of a system&#x27;s performance against the conditions in which it operates.</td></tr></tbody></table></div><h4>Review Questions</h4><h5>Multiple choice</h5><div class=\"study-mcq\"><p class=\"mcq-q\">Which system permits direct individual access to its court against every state party?</p><ol type=\"A\"><li>Europe</li><li>The Americas</li><li>Africa</li><li>All three</li></ol><details class=\"mcq-a\"><summary>Show answer</summary><p><strong>Answer: A.</strong> The Inter-American Court is reached only through the Commission, and the African Court requires an Article 34(6) declaration.</p></details></div><div class=\"study-mcq\"><p class=\"mcq-q\">Which doctrine originated in the Inter-American system and is now applied worldwide?</p><ol type=\"A\"><li>Due diligence for state responsibility in relation to private acts</li><li>The margin of appreciation</li><li>The living instrument doctrine</li><li>Pilot judgments</li></ol><details class=\"mcq-a\"><summary>Show answer</summary><p><strong>Answer: A.</strong> Velasquez Rodriguez supplied the framework later adopted by the European Court, UN treaty bodies and CEDAW General Recommendation 19.</p></details></div><div class=\"study-mcq\"><p class=\"mcq-q\">What makes the ECOWAS Court the most accessible international human rights forum in Africa?</p><ol type=\"A\"><li>Direct individual access with no requirement to exhaust domestic remedies</li><li>Its power to award criminal penalties</li><li>Its jurisdiction over all AU states</li><li>Its automatic enforcement mechanism</li></ol><details class=\"mcq-a\"><summary>Show answer</summary><p><strong>Answer: A.</strong> Its human rights jurisdiction combined with open standing bypasses the Article 34(6) barrier facing the African Court.</p></details></div><div class=\"study-mcq\"><p class=\"mcq-q\">What does the suspension of the SADC Tribunal in 2010 demonstrate?</p><ol type=\"A\"><li>That the ultimate constraint on regional adjudication is political will rather than legal design</li><li>That sub-regional courts lack jurisdiction</li><li>That land cases are non-justiciable</li><li>That the African Court has exclusive competence</li></ol><details class=\"mcq-a\"><summary>Show answer</summary><p><strong>Answer: A.</strong> The Tribunal was reconstituted with jurisdiction limited to inter-state disputes after ruling against a member state.</p></details></div><h5>Short answer</h5><div class=\"study-sa\"><p class=\"sa-q\">Name three contributions of the African system to international human rights law.</p><details class=\"sa-a\"><summary>Model answer</summary><p>First, peoples&#x27; rights: the Charter makes collective rights to self-determination, natural resources, development, peace and environment justiciable, and the resulting jurisprudence on communal land title in cases such as Endorois and Ogiek is cited in indigenous rights litigation worldwide. Second, the justiciability of economic and social rights within a general human rights instrument, established in SERAC v Nigeria, which is relied on internationally as authority that these rights can be adjudicated. Third, the African Court&#x27;s jurisdiction to apply any human rights treaty the respondent state has ratified, which is unique and allows it to enforce UN treaties directly.</p></details></div><div class=\"study-sa\"><p class=\"sa-q\">Explain why case volume is a poor measure of a regional system&#x27;s effectiveness.</p><details class=\"sa-a\"><summary>Model answer</summary><p>High volume shows that people can reach the body, which reflects accessibility rather than protection, and it can equally reflect widespread violations and failing domestic courts. Low volume may mean the body is inaccessible, as in Africa where the Article 34(6) declaration closes the Court to most individuals, but it could in principle also mean domestic systems are resolving matters. Volume also says nothing about whether decisions are implemented. A sound assessment combines four criteria: whether an ordinary victim can actually reach the body, whether its output is well reasoned and doctrinally developed, whether judgments are complied with, and whether national institutions absorb the standard without being compelled.</p></details></div><h4>Class Activity</h4><ol><li>Build a full comparison table of the three systems using the seven axes in this lesson, filling every cell from your own reading rather than copying the table.</li><li>Choose one reform for the African system from the cross-learning table. Write a two-page proposal setting out the change, the instrument that would have to be amended, and the political obstacles.</li></ol><h4>Assignment</h4><p>Write two pages comparing the three established regional human rights systems. State your axes of comparison, compare across each, explain at least two differences by reference to regional history, identify what each system contributed to international human rights law naming at least one case per system, assess effectiveness using access, output, compliance and domestic absorption, and conclude with a ranked judgement and a note on the role of sub-regional courts.</p><p class=\"study-print\"><strong>Printable:</strong> use your browser\u2019s Print \u2192 Save as PDF to keep an offline copy.</p></div>",
  "Overview of the UN Human Rights System": "<div class=\"study-note\"><div class=\"revision-banner\"><strong>Human Rights and International Humanitarian Law</strong><span>Module 6: United Nations Human Rights Machinery</span></div><h3 class=\"lesson-note-title\">LESSON NOTE: OVERVIEW OF THE UN HUMAN RIGHTS SYSTEM</h3><div class=\"table-wrap\"><table class=\"study-table\"><thead><tr><th>Subject</th><th>Topic</th><th>Level</th><th>Lesson duration</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Human Rights and International Humanitarian Law</td><td>Overview of the UN Human Rights System</td><td>Beginner to Advanced</td><td>40 minutes</td></tr></tbody></table></div><h4>Learning objectives</h4><p>By the end of this lesson you should be able to:</p><ul><li>Distinguish Charter-based from treaty-based bodies</li><li>Map the principal organs and their human rights functions</li><li>Explain how the parts of the system relate to each other</li><li>Identify which mechanism to use for a given problem</li></ul><h4>1. Introduction</h4><p>The UN human rights system looks chaotic because it grew by accretion over eighty years rather than by design. It becomes manageable once you see that everything falls into one of two families, distinguished by the legal instrument that created it, and that the two families have different memberships, different powers and different politics.</p><h4>2. The fundamental distinction</h4><div class=\"table-wrap\"><table class=\"study-table\"><thead><tr><th></th><th>Charter-based bodies</th><th>Treaty-based bodies</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Created by</td><td>The UN Charter and resolutions under it</td><td>A specific human rights treaty</td></tr><tr><td>Cover</td><td>All 193 member states</td><td>Only states party to that treaty</td></tr><tr><td>Membership</td><td>States, elected by the General Assembly</td><td>Independent experts serving in a personal capacity</td></tr><tr><td>Character</td><td>Political</td><td>Quasi-judicial</td></tr><tr><td>Examples</td><td>Human Rights Council, Universal Periodic Review, special procedures</td><td>Human Rights Committee, CESCR, CERD, CEDAW, CAT, CRC, CRPD, CED, CMW, SPT</td></tr><tr><td>Main outputs</td><td>Resolutions, UPR recommendations, country and thematic reports</td><td>Concluding observations, General Comments, views on individual communications</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"study-callout\"><strong>Note:</strong> The single most useful consequence of the distinction: a Charter-based mechanism can examine any state on any human rights question, because its authority derives from UN membership. A treaty body can examine only its own treaty and only its own parties. If a state has ratified nothing, the UPR and special procedures are still available; the treaty bodies are not.</div><h4>3. The principal organs and their human rights roles</h4><div class=\"table-wrap\"><table class=\"study-table\"><thead><tr><th>Organ</th><th>Role</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>General Assembly</td><td>Adopts treaties and declarations; the Third Committee handles human rights items; receives the Council's report</td></tr><tr><td>Security Council</td><td>Addresses human rights where they affect international peace and security; establishes commissions of inquiry, sanctions and referrals to the ICC; mandates peacekeeping protection tasks</td></tr><tr><td>Economic and Social Council</td><td>Created the Commission on Human Rights in 1946 and the CESCR in 1985; a channel for NGO consultative status</td></tr><tr><td>International Court of Justice</td><td>Decides inter-state disputes, including under the Genocide Convention, ICERD and CAT; gives advisory opinions</td></tr><tr><td>Secretariat</td><td>OHCHR services most of the machinery; the Secretary-General exercises good offices and reports</td></tr><tr><td>Trusteeship Council</td><td>Suspended operation in 1994; historically relevant to self-determination</td></tr></tbody></table></div><h4>4. The Charter-based family in detail</h4><ol><li><strong>The Human Rights Council</strong>, created in 2006 to replace the Commission on Human Rights. Forty-seven member states elected by the General Assembly.</li><li><strong>The Universal Periodic Review</strong>, a peer review of every UN member state's record on a cycle, conducted by the Council.</li><li><strong>Special procedures</strong>: independent special rapporteurs, independent experts and working groups with thematic or country mandates.</li><li><strong>The complaint procedure</strong>, the confidential successor to the old 1503 procedure, addressing consistent patterns of gross and reliably attested violations.</li><li><strong>The Advisory Committee</strong>, eighteen experts functioning as the Council's think tank.</li><li><strong>Commissions of inquiry and fact-finding missions</strong>, established ad hoc by the Council, the Security Council or the Secretary-General.</li></ol><h4>5. The treaty-based family</h4><div class=\"table-wrap\"><table class=\"study-table\"><thead><tr><th>Committee</th><th>Treaty</th><th>Established</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>CERD</td><td>ICERD</td><td>1969</td></tr><tr><td>CCPR (Human Rights Committee)</td><td>ICCPR</td><td>1976</td></tr><tr><td>CEDAW Committee</td><td>CEDAW</td><td>1982</td></tr><tr><td>CAT Committee</td><td>CAT</td><td>1987</td></tr><tr><td>CRC Committee</td><td>CRC</td><td>1991</td></tr><tr><td>CESCR</td><td>ICESCR</td><td>1985, by ECOSOC resolution</td></tr><tr><td>CMW</td><td>Migrant Workers Convention</td><td>2004</td></tr><tr><td>SPT</td><td>OPCAT</td><td>2007</td></tr><tr><td>CRPD Committee</td><td>CRPD</td><td>2008</td></tr><tr><td>CED</td><td>Enforced Disappearance Convention</td><td>2011</td></tr></tbody></table></div><p>Each has up to four functions, depending on the treaty and the state's acceptance of optional procedures: reviewing periodic state reports, issuing General Comments, deciding individual communications, and conducting inquiries. The SPT is different: it does not review reports but conducts preventive visits.</p><h4>6. How the parts connect</h4><ul><li>The UPR draws on treaty body concluding observations and special procedures reports as two of its three information sources, so a treaty body finding reappears as a UPR recommendation that every state can raise.</li><li>Special procedures cite treaty body jurisprudence and vice versa, producing a shared body of interpretation.</li><li>OHCHR services both families and prepares the compilations used in the UPR.</li><li>The Council can create a commission of inquiry whose findings feed the Security Council and sometimes the ICC.</li><li>NGOs supply information to all of them, which is why NGO consultative status matters institutionally rather than symbolically.</li></ul><div class=\"study-callout\"><strong>Example:</strong> A pattern of deaths in police custody in a state might be raised in a CAT Committee concluding observation, taken up by the Special Rapporteur on torture through a communication to the government, cited by other states as a UPR recommendation, litigated in an individual communication to the Human Rights Committee, and, if it becomes systematic and widespread, form part of a commission of inquiry's findings. Each route has different requirements, timescales and outputs, and an advocate chooses among them deliberately.</div><h4>7. Choosing the right mechanism</h4><div class=\"table-wrap\"><table class=\"study-table\"><thead><tr><th>Situation</th><th>Best mechanism</th><th>Why</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>An individual violation, remedies exhausted, state accepted the procedure</td><td>Treaty body individual communication</td><td>Produces a reasoned finding on the specific case</td></tr><tr><td>Urgent risk to a named individual</td><td>Special procedure urgent appeal</td><td>Fast; can be sent within days and does not require exhaustion</td></tr><tr><td>A pattern of violations in a state that has ratified little</td><td>UPR, or a special procedure country visit</td><td>Charter-based mechanisms reach every state</td></tr><tr><td>A systemic legal defect</td><td>Treaty body reporting cycle</td><td>Concluding observations address law and policy rather than individual cases</td></tr><tr><td>Gross violations amounting to international crimes</td><td>Commission of inquiry, then Security Council or ICC</td><td>Designed for evidence gathering and accountability</td></tr><tr><td>Preventing torture in detention</td><td>OPCAT: SPT visit and the National Preventive Mechanism</td><td>Preventive rather than reactive</td></tr></tbody></table></div><h4>8. How the examiner&#x27;s question should be answered</h4><p>A question asking you to describe the UN system should open with the Charter-based and treaty-based distinction, because it organises everything that follows. Then take each family in turn with its membership, powers and outputs. Then explain the connections, since a system described as a list of separate boxes misses the point. Finish with the selection table above applied to one concrete problem, which demonstrates practical command rather than recall.</p><h4>Summary</h4><ul><li>UN human rights machinery divides into Charter-based bodies, covering all member states, and treaty-based bodies, covering only parties.</li><li>Charter-based bodies are political and composed of states; treaty bodies are quasi-judicial and composed of independent experts.</li><li>The Charter family comprises the Human Rights Council, the UPR, special procedures, the complaint procedure, the Advisory Committee and inquiries.</li><li>Ten treaty bodies review reports, issue General Comments, decide communications and conduct inquiries.</li><li>The families are interconnected: UPR draws on treaty body and special procedure output, and OHCHR services both.</li><li>Choosing a mechanism depends on urgency, whether the state has ratified, and whether the problem is individual or systemic.</li></ul><h4>Key Terms</h4><div class=\"table-wrap\"><table class=\"study-table\"><thead><tr><th>Term</th><th>Meaning</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Charter-based body</td><td>A human rights mechanism created under the UN Charter, covering all member states.</td></tr><tr><td>Treaty body</td><td>A committee of independent experts supervising a specific human rights treaty.</td></tr><tr><td>Concluding observations</td><td>A treaty body&#x27;s findings and recommendations following review of a state report.</td></tr><tr><td>OHCHR</td><td>The Office of the United Nations High Commissioner for Human Rights, which services the machinery.</td></tr></tbody></table></div><h4>Review Questions</h4><h5>Multiple choice</h5><div class=\"study-mcq\"><p class=\"mcq-q\">A state has ratified almost no human rights treaties. Which mechanism can still examine its record?</p><ol type=\"A\"><li>The Universal Periodic Review, because it covers all UN member states</li><li>The Human Rights Committee</li><li>The CAT Committee</li><li>None</li></ol><details class=\"mcq-a\"><summary>Show answer</summary><p><strong>Answer: A.</strong> Charter-based mechanisms derive authority from UN membership, not from ratification.</p></details></div><div class=\"study-mcq\"><p class=\"mcq-q\">Who sits on treaty bodies?</p><ol type=\"A\"><li>Independent experts serving in a personal capacity</li><li>Government representatives</li><li>Judges of the International Court of Justice</li><li>Staff of the Secretariat</li></ol><details class=\"mcq-a\"><summary>Show answer</summary><p><strong>Answer: A.</strong> Charter-based bodies such as the Human Rights Council are composed of states, which is the source of their political character.</p></details></div><div class=\"study-mcq\"><p class=\"mcq-q\">Which mechanism is fastest for an urgent risk to a named individual?</p><ol type=\"A\"><li>A special procedure urgent appeal</li><li>A treaty body individual communication</li><li>The Universal Periodic Review</li><li>A General Comment</li></ol><details class=\"mcq-a\"><summary>Show answer</summary><p><strong>Answer: A.</strong> Urgent appeals can be sent within days and do not require exhaustion of domestic remedies.</p></details></div><div class=\"study-mcq\"><p class=\"mcq-q\">Which treaty body conducts preventive visits rather than reviewing state reports?</p><ol type=\"A\"><li>The Subcommittee on Prevention of Torture</li><li>The Committee against Torture</li><li>The Human Rights Committee</li><li>The CRPD Committee</li></ol><details class=\"mcq-a\"><summary>Show answer</summary><p><strong>Answer: A.</strong> The SPT was created by OPCAT specifically to visit places of detention and to advise National Preventive Mechanisms.</p></details></div><h5>Short answer</h5><div class=\"study-sa\"><p class=\"sa-q\">Distinguish Charter-based from treaty-based bodies on four points.</p><details class=\"sa-a\"><summary>Model answer</summary><p>On legal basis, Charter-based bodies are created by the UN Charter and resolutions under it, while treaty bodies are created by a specific human rights treaty. On coverage, Charter-based bodies may examine all 193 member states, while a treaty body reaches only the parties to its own treaty. On composition, Charter-based bodies such as the Human Rights Council are made up of states, giving them a political character, while treaty bodies consist of independent experts serving in a personal capacity, giving them a quasi-judicial one. On output, Charter-based bodies produce resolutions, recommendations and country reports, while treaty bodies produce concluding observations, General Comments and views on individual communications.</p></details></div><div class=\"study-sa\"><p class=\"sa-q\">Explain how the Universal Periodic Review connects to the rest of the system.</p><details class=\"sa-a\"><summary>Model answer</summary><p>The review of each state is based on three documents: the state&#x27;s own report, a compilation by OHCHR of UN information including treaty body concluding observations and special procedures reports, and a summary of stakeholder submissions from national human rights institutions and NGOs. That means a treaty body finding or a rapporteur&#x27;s concern reappears in a forum where every UN member state can raise it as a recommendation, including against a state that has not ratified the treaty in question. The UPR therefore functions as a general amplifier for the specialised parts of the system rather than as a separate track.</p></details></div><h4>Class Activity</h4><ol><li>Draw a one-page map of the UN human rights system showing both families, their members and their outputs, with arrows for the connections described in this lesson.</li><li>Take one real human rights problem in your country. Using the selection table, identify which two mechanisms you would use and explain in a paragraph why those and not the others.</li></ol><h4>Assignment</h4><p>Write one and a half pages giving an overview of the UN human rights system. Explain the Charter-based and treaty-based distinction on legal basis, coverage, composition and output, list the bodies in each family with their functions, explain how the parts connect using a worked example, and set out how to choose the appropriate mechanism for a given problem.</p><p class=\"study-print\"><strong>Printable:</strong> use your browser\u2019s Print \u2192 Save as PDF to keep an offline copy.</p></div>",
  "The Human Rights Council": "<div class=\"study-note\"><div class=\"revision-banner\"><strong>Human Rights and International Humanitarian Law</strong><span>Module 6: United Nations Human Rights Machinery</span></div><h3 class=\"lesson-note-title\">LESSON NOTE: THE HUMAN RIGHTS COUNCIL</h3><div class=\"table-wrap\"><table class=\"study-table\"><thead><tr><th>Subject</th><th>Topic</th><th>Level</th><th>Lesson duration</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Human Rights and International Humanitarian Law</td><td>The Human Rights Council</td><td>Beginner to Advanced</td><td>40 minutes</td></tr></tbody></table></div><h4>Learning objectives</h4><p>By the end of this lesson you should be able to:</p><ul><li>Describe the composition, election and mandate of the Human Rights Council</li><li>Explain why the Commission on Human Rights was replaced</li><li>Describe the Council&#x27;s main procedures and outputs</li><li>Assess the criticisms made of the Council</li></ul><h4>1. Introduction</h4><p>The Human Rights Council is the UN's principal political human rights forum. It replaced a body that had lost credibility, and the reform question that produced it, how to stop a human rights body being captured by the states it should be scrutinising, has not been fully solved. That is what makes it worth studying.</p><h4>2. Why the Commission was replaced</h4><p>The Commission on Human Rights operated from 1946 to 2006. Its achievements were substantial: it drafted the UDHR and the Covenants and created the special procedures. By the early 2000s it faced four criticisms.</p><ol><li><strong>Membership.</strong> States with poor records sought and won election, sometimes explicitly to shield themselves and their allies.</li><li><strong>Selectivity.</strong> Country resolutions clustered on a small number of states while comparable situations elsewhere were never addressed.</li><li><strong>Politicisation.</strong> Bloc voting displaced consideration of the merits.</li><li><strong>Credibility.</strong> The Secretary-General's 2005 report In Larger Freedom stated that the Commission's declining credibility had cast a shadow on the reputation of the UN system as a whole.</li></ol><p>General Assembly resolution 60/251 of 2006 created the Council as a subsidiary organ of the General Assembly, with a review of its status after five years. That review, completed in 2011, left the arrangement in place.</p><h4>3. Composition and election</h4><div class=\"table-wrap\"><table class=\"study-table\"><thead><tr><th>Feature</th><th>Detail</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Members</td><td>47 states</td></tr><tr><td>Elected by</td><td>The General Assembly, by an absolute majority of 97 votes, in a secret ballot</td></tr><tr><td>Term</td><td>Three years, with no immediate re-election after two consecutive terms</td></tr><tr><td>Regional distribution</td><td>Africa 13, Asia-Pacific 13, Latin America and the Caribbean 8, Western European and Others 7, Eastern Europe 6</td></tr><tr><td>Criteria</td><td>Members must uphold the highest standards in the promotion and protection of human rights and fully cooperate with the Council; the General Assembly is to take into account the candidate's contribution and voluntary pledges</td></tr><tr><td>Suspension</td><td>The General Assembly may suspend a member committing gross and systematic violations, by a two-thirds majority of those present and voting</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"study-callout\"><strong>Note:</strong> Two design changes distinguished the Council from the Commission: election by the whole General Assembly requiring an absolute majority rather than by ECOSOC, and the suspension power. Suspension has been used twice, against Libya in 2011 and Russia in 2022. Both are worth citing, because they show the power is real but reserved for extreme cases.</div><h4>4. Structure and procedures</h4><div class=\"table-wrap\"><table class=\"study-table\"><thead><tr><th>Mechanism</th><th>Description</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Regular sessions</td><td>At least three a year, totalling no fewer than ten weeks, in Geneva</td></tr><tr><td>Special sessions</td><td>May be convened at the request of one member supported by one third of the membership; used for urgent situations</td></tr><tr><td>Universal Periodic Review</td><td>Peer review of every member state; the Council's signature innovation</td></tr><tr><td>Special procedures</td><td>Appointment and oversight of thematic and country mandate holders</td></tr><tr><td>Complaint procedure</td><td>Confidential; addresses consistent patterns of gross and reliably attested violations</td></tr><tr><td>Advisory Committee</td><td>Eighteen experts providing research and studies at the Council's request</td></tr><tr><td>Resolutions</td><td>Thematic and country-specific; may create mandates, commissions of inquiry and fact-finding missions</td></tr><tr><td>Interactive dialogues</td><td>With mandate holders, the High Commissioner and states under review</td></tr></tbody></table></div><p>The Council's most consequential outputs are usually its resolutions creating investigative mechanisms. A commission of inquiry can gather and preserve evidence, name patterns and, in some cases, prepare the ground for prosecution, which is the closest the political system comes to accountability.</p><h4>5. NGO participation</h4><ul><li>NGOs with ECOSOC consultative status may attend sessions, make written and oral statements, and organise side events.</li><li>This is a genuine channel of influence: NGO submissions feed the UPR stakeholder summary and often supply the evidence behind resolutions.</li><li>Reprisals against those who cooperate with the UN are a recognised problem. The Secretary-General reports annually on intimidation and reprisals, and the Assistant Secretary-General for Human Rights acts as senior official on the issue.</li><li>Restricting accreditation is a recurring method of limiting scrutiny, and disputes over NGO accreditation in the ECOSOC Committee on NGOs are a persistent feature of the system.</li></ul><h4>6. Criticisms and responses</h4><div class=\"table-wrap\"><table class=\"study-table\"><thead><tr><th>Criticism</th><th>Response</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>States with poor records are elected</td><td>True, but the Council was never designed as a club of the virtuous; a body excluding such states could not engage them. Election by absolute majority of the General Assembly and the suspension power are the safeguards, and competitive slates matter more than rules</td></tr><tr><td>Selectivity persists</td><td>Partly true. The UPR was designed precisely to answer this by reviewing every state equally, and it has done so. Country resolutions remain unevenly distributed</td></tr><tr><td>Bloc politics dominate</td><td>Real, but it is a political body composed of states; the expert mechanisms exist to supply the non-political element</td></tr><tr><td>Item 7 singles out one situation as a permanent agenda item</td><td>A genuine structural anomaly, defended on the ground of the situation's particular character and criticised as inconsistent with universality</td></tr><tr><td>Resolutions are not binding</td><td>Correct, but they create mandates, inquiries and evidence, and they shape what the Security Council and the ICC can act on</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"study-callout\"><strong>Note:</strong> A strong evaluative answer avoids both defensive and dismissive positions. State what the Council can and cannot do by design. It is a political forum whose value lies in agenda setting, mandate creation and evidence gathering, not in adjudication. Criticising it for failing to adjudicate mistakes its function.</div><h4>7. How the examiner&#x27;s question should be answered</h4><p>For 'has the Human Rights Council improved on the Commission?', identify the four criticisms of the Commission, describe the specific reforms designed to answer each, and assess each reform against evidence. Membership: the absolute majority requirement and the suspension power, used twice. Selectivity: the UPR, which reviews every state. Politicisation: unchanged, and arguably unchangeable in a body of states. Credibility: improved but contested. Conclude that the reforms fixed what institutional design can fix and left untouched what is inherent in an intergovernmental body.</p><h4>Summary</h4><ul><li>The Human Rights Council replaced the Commission in 2006 after criticism of its membership, selectivity, politicisation and lost credibility.</li><li>It has 47 members elected by an absolute majority of the General Assembly for three-year terms with regional distribution.</li><li>The General Assembly may suspend a member for gross and systematic violations, done twice: Libya in 2011 and Russia in 2022.</li><li>Its procedures include regular and special sessions, the UPR, special procedures, a confidential complaint procedure and an Advisory Committee.</li><li>Its most consequential outputs are resolutions creating commissions of inquiry that gather and preserve evidence.</li><li>It is a political body: its value lies in agenda setting, mandates and evidence, not adjudication.</li></ul><h4>Key Terms</h4><div class=\"table-wrap\"><table class=\"study-table\"><thead><tr><th>Term</th><th>Meaning</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Human Rights Council</td><td>The UN&#x27;s 47-member intergovernmental human rights body, a subsidiary organ of the General Assembly.</td></tr><tr><td>Resolution 60/251</td><td>The 2006 General Assembly resolution creating the Council.</td></tr><tr><td>Special session</td><td>A session convened outside the regular calendar to address an urgent situation.</td></tr><tr><td>Commission of inquiry</td><td>A body established to investigate a situation, gather evidence and report findings.</td></tr></tbody></table></div><h4>Review Questions</h4><h5>Multiple choice</h5><div class=\"study-mcq\"><p class=\"mcq-q\">How many members does the Human Rights Council have and how are they elected?</p><ol type=\"A\"><li>47, elected by an absolute majority of the General Assembly in a secret ballot</li><li>53, elected by ECOSOC</li><li>47, appointed by the Secretary-General</li><li>193, all UN member states</li></ol><details class=\"mcq-a\"><summary>Show answer</summary><p><strong>Answer: A.</strong> Election by the whole General Assembly requiring 97 votes was one of the two main design changes from the Commission.</p></details></div><div class=\"study-mcq\"><p class=\"mcq-q\">Which power did the Council gain that the Commission never had?</p><ol type=\"A\"><li>Suspension of a member for gross and systematic violations</li><li>Power to issue binding judgments</li><li>Power to refer cases to the ICC</li><li>Power to amend treaties</li></ol><details class=\"mcq-a\"><summary>Show answer</summary><p><strong>Answer: A.</strong> It has been used twice, against Libya in 2011 and Russia in 2022, showing it is real but reserved for extreme cases.</p></details></div><div class=\"study-mcq\"><p class=\"mcq-q\">Which reform was designed specifically to answer the charge of selectivity?</p><ol type=\"A\"><li>The Universal Periodic Review, which examines every UN member state</li><li>The Advisory Committee</li><li>The complaint procedure</li><li>Special sessions</li></ol><details class=\"mcq-a\"><summary>Show answer</summary><p><strong>Answer: A.</strong> Reviewing every state on the same cycle removes the argument that only some states are scrutinised.</p></details></div><div class=\"study-mcq\"><p class=\"mcq-q\">What is the most consequential type of Council resolution?</p><ol type=\"A\"><li>Those creating commissions of inquiry that gather and preserve evidence</li><li>Those condemning individual statements</li><li>Those setting the session calendar</li><li>Those adopting the annual report</li></ol><details class=\"mcq-a\"><summary>Show answer</summary><p><strong>Answer: A.</strong> Evidence gathered by inquiries can support later accountability, including before the Security Council or the International Criminal Court.</p></details></div><h5>Short answer</h5><div class=\"study-sa\"><p class=\"sa-q\">Give the four criticisms that led to the Commission on Human Rights being replaced.</p><details class=\"sa-a\"><summary>Model answer</summary><p>First, membership: states with poor records sought and obtained election, in some cases to shield themselves and their allies from scrutiny. Second, selectivity: country resolutions concentrated on a small number of states while comparable situations elsewhere went unaddressed. Third, politicisation: voting followed regional and political blocs rather than the merits of a situation. Fourth, credibility: the Secretary-General&#x27;s 2005 report In Larger Freedom concluded that the Commission&#x27;s declining credibility had cast a shadow on the reputation of the United Nations as a whole.</p></details></div><div class=\"study-sa\"><p class=\"sa-q\">Assess whether the Council fixed the problems of the Commission.</p><details class=\"sa-a\"><summary>Model answer</summary><p>On membership, election now requires an absolute majority of the whole General Assembly and members can be suspended, a power used against Libya and Russia; but states with poor records are still elected, largely because regional groups often present uncompetitive slates. On selectivity, the Universal Periodic Review genuinely reviews every state on the same cycle, which is a real answer, although country resolutions remain unevenly distributed and a permanent agenda item for one situation is a structural anomaly. On politicisation, nothing changed, and little could: a body composed of states will vote politically. The fair conclusion is that the reforms addressed what institutional design can address and left untouched what is inherent in an intergovernmental forum.</p></details></div><h4>Class Activity</h4><ol><li>Look up the current membership of the Human Rights Council. Identify three members whose own record has been criticised by a treaty body, and write a paragraph on whether their membership helps or harms the Council.</li><li>Find one special session of the Council. Summarise what triggered it, what it decided and what happened afterwards.</li></ol><h4>Assignment</h4><p>Write one and a half pages on the Human Rights Council. Explain why the Commission on Human Rights was replaced, describe the Council&#x27;s composition, election, regional distribution and suspension power, set out its main procedures and outputs, describe NGO participation and the problem of reprisals, and assess the principal criticisms with a reasoned conclusion.</p><p class=\"study-print\"><strong>Printable:</strong> use your browser\u2019s Print \u2192 Save as PDF to keep an offline copy.</p></div>",
  "The Universal Periodic Review": "<div class=\"study-note\"><div class=\"revision-banner\"><strong>Human Rights and International Humanitarian Law</strong><span>Module 6: United Nations Human Rights Machinery</span></div><h3 class=\"lesson-note-title\">LESSON NOTE: THE UNIVERSAL PERIODIC REVIEW</h3><div class=\"table-wrap\"><table class=\"study-table\"><thead><tr><th>Subject</th><th>Topic</th><th>Level</th><th>Lesson duration</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Human Rights and International Humanitarian Law</td><td>The Universal Periodic Review</td><td>Beginner to Advanced</td><td>40 minutes</td></tr></tbody></table></div><h4>Learning objectives</h4><p>By the end of this lesson you should be able to:</p><ul><li>Describe how the Universal Periodic Review works</li><li>Identify the three information sources and the documents produced</li><li>Explain the strengths and weaknesses of peer review</li><li>Explain how civil society can engage with the process</li></ul><h4>1. Introduction</h4><p>The Universal Periodic Review is the most genuinely novel feature of the reformed UN human rights machinery. Every UN member state is reviewed, on the same cycle, by other states, against the same body of standards. Nothing like it existed before 2008 and nothing comparable exists in any other field.</p><h4>2. The basics</h4><div class=\"table-wrap\"><table class=\"study-table\"><thead><tr><th>Feature</th><th>Detail</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Coverage</td><td>All 193 UN member states, without exception</td></tr><tr><td>Cycle</td><td>Roughly four and a half years; the fourth cycle began in 2022</td></tr><tr><td>Conducted by</td><td>The UPR Working Group, comprising all 47 Council members, with any UN member state able to participate in the dialogue</td></tr><tr><td>Basis of review</td><td>The UN Charter, the UDHR, treaties the state has ratified, voluntary pledges, and applicable international humanitarian law</td></tr><tr><td>Troika</td><td>Three states drawn by lot from different regional groups act as rapporteurs and prepare the report</td></tr><tr><td>Dialogue</td><td>Three and a half hours of interactive dialogue in Geneva</td></tr><tr><td>Outcome</td><td>A report listing recommendations, which the state accepts or notes</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"study-callout\"><strong>Note:</strong> The basis of review is broader than any treaty body's. It includes the UDHR and voluntary pledges, so a state that has ratified nothing can still be reviewed against the Declaration and against its own election promises to the Council. That breadth is the UPR's structural advantage.</div><h4>3. The three information sources</h4><ol><li><strong>The national report</strong>, prepared by the state, up to twenty pages, ideally through a broad national consultation.</li><li><strong>The OHCHR compilation</strong>, up to ten pages, drawing together treaty body concluding observations, special procedures reports and other UN documents.</li><li><strong>The stakeholder summary</strong>, up to ten pages, summarising submissions from national human rights institutions, NGOs, academics and others.</li></ol><p>The third source is the practical entry point for civil society. A well-prepared NGO submission that identifies a specific gap, cites evidence and proposes a precise recommendation has a realistic chance of appearing in the summary and being taken up by a reviewing state.</p><h4>4. The process step by step</h4><ol><li>Submission of the three documents ahead of the session.</li><li>Advance questions from states, submitted in writing before the dialogue.</li><li>The interactive dialogue: the state presents, other states comment and make recommendations, the state responds.</li><li>The Working Group adopts a report listing every recommendation made and by whom.</li><li>The state indicates, either immediately or by the following Council session, which recommendations it supports and which it notes.</li><li>The Council adopts the outcome at a plenary session, where the state, other states, national human rights institutions and NGOs may speak.</li><li>Implementation over the following cycle, sometimes with a voluntary mid-term report.</li><li>The next review begins with the implementation of the previous cycle's recommendations.</li></ol><div class=\"study-defn\"><strong>Supported and noted recommendations:</strong> A state 'supports' recommendations it accepts as commitments and 'notes' those it does not accept; noting is the diplomatic form of rejection but the recommendation remains on the record.</div><div class=\"study-callout\"><strong>Note:</strong> The distinction is examinable and often misunderstood. A noted recommendation has not disappeared: it stays in the outcome document, other states can raise it again next cycle, and civil society uses it as an advocacy reference. Acceptance rates across states average roughly three quarters of recommendations, which sounds high until you note that many recommendations are general encouragements rather than specific requests.</div><h4>5. Strengths</h4><ol><li><strong>Universality.</strong> No state is exempt, which removes the selectivity objection and gives the process a legitimacy country resolutions lack.</li><li><strong>State ownership.</strong> Because peers make the recommendations, states engage more than they do with expert bodies. Attendance has been effectively universal.</li><li><strong>Comprehensiveness.</strong> It covers all rights, not just those in one treaty, and reaches states that have ratified little.</li><li><strong>Amplification.</strong> It carries treaty body and special procedure findings into a political forum with far higher visibility.</li><li><strong>Domestic mobilisation.</strong> Preparing a national report and responding to recommendations creates a domestic process, often the first time some ministries have had to consider human rights obligations.</li><li><strong>Follow-up structure.</strong> The next review opens with implementation of the last, creating a ratchet.</li></ol><h4>6. Weaknesses</h4><ol><li><strong>Politicisation.</strong> Allied states use their speaking slots to praise rather than to scrutinise, diluting the dialogue and using up the limited time.</li><li><strong>Volume and vagueness.</strong> Some states receive hundreds of recommendations, many of them unmeasurable encouragements to 'continue efforts'.</li><li><strong>No enforcement.</strong> Nothing follows from non-implementation except being asked about it again.</li><li><strong>Self-reporting.</strong> The national report is the state's own account, and the counterweight is only ten pages of stakeholder summary.</li><li><strong>Time.</strong> Three and a half hours for an entire national record permits breadth but not depth.</li><li><strong>Reprisals.</strong> Individuals and organisations that submit information have faced intimidation, which the Secretary-General reports on annually.</li></ol><h4>7. Engaging effectively as civil society</h4><div class=\"table-wrap\"><table class=\"study-table\"><thead><tr><th>Step</th><th>Practice</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Prepare early</td><td>Submissions are due months before the session; the deadline is published well in advance</td></tr><tr><td>Be specific</td><td>Identify one gap, give the evidence, cite the relevant treaty provision and prior recommendation</td></tr><tr><td>Draft the recommendation</td><td>Write the exact wording you want a state to use: specific, measurable, achievable and time-bound</td></tr><tr><td>Coalition</td><td>Joint submissions from several organisations carry more weight and are more likely to be summarised</td></tr><tr><td>Lobby missions</td><td>Approach the Geneva missions of states likely to be sympathetic, well before the session</td></tr><tr><td>Follow up</td><td>Track which recommendations were supported and use them domestically as the government's own commitments</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"study-callout\"><strong>Example:</strong> A coalition submits evidence that pre-trial detainees are held for years without trial, cites the previous cycle's recommendation on the same point, and proposes the wording: 'Amend the Criminal Procedure Code to impose a statutory maximum period of pre-trial detention of twelve months, with automatic judicial review every ninety days.' Three states make that recommendation almost verbatim, the state supports it, and the coalition then has a government commitment it can cite to the ministry, to parliament and to the media. This is how the UPR produces change: not by enforcement but by converting an external criticism into a domestic commitment.</div><h4>8. How the examiner&#x27;s question should be answered</h4><p>For 'evaluate the UPR', name the three sources and the documents, describe the cycle, then balance strengths against weaknesses using the two lists above. The analytical point that lifts an answer is this: the UPR has no enforcement power and was never intended to have any, so its mechanism of influence is different. It works by universality, by peer pressure and by converting international recommendations into domestic commitments that local actors can then use. Judge it against that mechanism rather than against a court.</p><h4>Summary</h4><ul><li>The UPR reviews all 193 UN member states on a cycle of roughly four and a half years, against the Charter, the UDHR, ratified treaties, pledges and applicable IHL.</li><li>Three documents form the basis: the national report, the OHCHR compilation, and the stakeholder summary.</li><li>The state supports or notes each recommendation; noted recommendations remain on the record for future cycles.</li><li>Strengths include universality, state engagement, comprehensiveness, amplification of expert findings and domestic mobilisation.</li><li>Weaknesses include friendly-state padding, vague recommendations, absence of enforcement, self-reporting, limited time and reprisals.</li><li>Civil society influences the process by preparing early, drafting precise recommendation wording and lobbying sympathetic missions.</li></ul><h4>Key Terms</h4><div class=\"table-wrap\"><table class=\"study-table\"><thead><tr><th>Term</th><th>Meaning</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Universal Periodic Review</td><td>The peer review of every UN member state&#x27;s human rights record conducted by the Human Rights Council.</td></tr><tr><td>Troika</td><td>The three states drawn by lot to act as rapporteurs for a review.</td></tr><tr><td>Stakeholder summary</td><td>The OHCHR summary of submissions from national human rights institutions, NGOs and others.</td></tr><tr><td>Noted recommendation</td><td>A recommendation the state declines to accept, which nonetheless remains in the outcome document.</td></tr></tbody></table></div><h4>Review Questions</h4><h5>Multiple choice</h5><div class=\"study-mcq\"><p class=\"mcq-q\">Which states are subject to the Universal Periodic Review?</p><ol type=\"A\"><li>All 193 UN member states</li><li>Only members of the Human Rights Council</li><li>Only states party to the ICCPR</li><li>Only states selected by the Council</li></ol><details class=\"mcq-a\"><summary>Show answer</summary><p><strong>Answer: A.</strong> Universality is what answers the selectivity criticism levelled at the old Commission.</p></details></div><div class=\"study-mcq\"><p class=\"mcq-q\">What are the three documents on which a review is based?</p><ol type=\"A\"><li>The national report, the OHCHR compilation of UN information, and the stakeholder summary</li><li>The national report, a treaty body report and a Security Council report</li><li>Three NGO submissions</li><li>The state&#x27;s report and the troika&#x27;s report</li></ol><details class=\"mcq-a\"><summary>Show answer</summary><p><strong>Answer: A.</strong> The stakeholder summary is the practical entry point for civil society.</p></details></div><div class=\"study-mcq\"><p class=\"mcq-q\">What happens to a recommendation that a state notes rather than supports?</p><ol type=\"A\"><li>It remains in the outcome document and can be raised again in future cycles</li><li>It is deleted from the record</li><li>It becomes binding after one year</li><li>It is referred to a treaty body</li></ol><details class=\"mcq-a\"><summary>Show answer</summary><p><strong>Answer: A.</strong> Noting is the diplomatic form of rejection, but civil society continues to use noted recommendations as advocacy references.</p></details></div><div class=\"study-mcq\"><p class=\"mcq-q\">What is the UPR&#x27;s actual mechanism of influence?</p><ol type=\"A\"><li>Converting international recommendations into domestic commitments that local actors can invoke</li><li>Binding orders enforced by the Security Council</li><li>Fines imposed on non-compliant states</li><li>Suspension from the United Nations</li></ol><details class=\"mcq-a\"><summary>Show answer</summary><p><strong>Answer: A.</strong> It has no enforcement power by design, so it should be judged against peer pressure and domestic mobilisation rather than against a court.</p></details></div><h5>Short answer</h5><div class=\"study-sa\"><p class=\"sa-q\">Describe the eight stages of a Universal Periodic Review.</p><details class=\"sa-a\"><summary>Model answer</summary><p>First, the three documents are submitted: the state&#x27;s national report, the OHCHR compilation of UN information, and the stakeholder summary. Second, states submit advance written questions. Third, the interactive dialogue takes place in Geneva over three and a half hours, with the state presenting and other states commenting and recommending. Fourth, the Working Group adopts a report listing every recommendation and its author. Fifth, the state indicates which recommendations it supports and which it notes. Sixth, the Council adopts the outcome in plenary, where the state, other states, national institutions and NGOs may speak. Seventh, implementation follows over the cycle, sometimes with a voluntary mid-term report. Eighth, the next review opens with implementation of the previous cycle&#x27;s recommendations.</p></details></div><div class=\"study-sa\"><p class=\"sa-q\">Explain how a civil society organisation can influence a UPR outcome.</p><details class=\"sa-a\"><summary>Model answer</summary><p>It should prepare a submission well before the published deadline, identifying one specific gap rather than surveying everything, supporting it with evidence, and citing the relevant treaty provision and any recommendation from the previous cycle. It should draft the exact wording it wants a state to use, framed to be specific, measurable and time-bound, because reviewing states frequently adopt well-drafted proposals almost verbatim. Joint submissions from a coalition carry more weight and are more likely to be reflected in the stakeholder summary. It should then lobby the Geneva missions of sympathetic states in advance of the session. Finally it should track which recommendations were supported and use those domestically as the government&#x27;s own commitments.</p></details></div><h4>Class Activity</h4><ol><li>Find your country&#x27;s most recent UPR outcome. Count the recommendations supported and noted, and identify the three most specific and the three vaguest.</li><li>Draft a one-page UPR stakeholder submission on a real issue in your community, ending with two precisely worded recommendations.</li></ol><h4>Assignment</h4><p>Write one and a half pages on the Universal Periodic Review. Describe its coverage, cycle, basis of review and the role of the troika, explain the three information sources and the eight stages of a review, distinguish supported from noted recommendations, evaluate the strengths and weaknesses of peer review, and explain how civil society can engage effectively.</p><p class=\"study-print\"><strong>Printable:</strong> use your browser\u2019s Print \u2192 Save as PDF to keep an offline copy.</p></div>",
  "Treaty Bodies and State Reporting": "<div class=\"study-note\"><div class=\"revision-banner\"><strong>Human Rights and International Humanitarian Law</strong><span>Module 6: United Nations Human Rights Machinery</span></div><h3 class=\"lesson-note-title\">LESSON NOTE: TREATY BODIES AND STATE REPORTING</h3><div class=\"table-wrap\"><table class=\"study-table\"><thead><tr><th>Subject</th><th>Topic</th><th>Level</th><th>Lesson duration</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Human Rights and International Humanitarian Law</td><td>Treaty Bodies and State Reporting</td><td>Beginner to Advanced</td><td>40 minutes</td></tr></tbody></table></div><h4>Learning objectives</h4><p>By the end of this lesson you should be able to:</p><ul><li>Describe the composition and functions of the treaty bodies</li><li>Explain the reporting cycle and the documents involved</li><li>Explain the status and use of General Comments</li><li>Identify the system&#x27;s problems and the reform proposals</li></ul><h4>1. Introduction</h4><p>The treaty bodies are the technical core of the international human rights system. They are also the part most under strain: ten committees, over 170 experts, thousands of reports due, and a backlog that has grown faster than the resources allocated to clear it. This lesson covers both what they do and why the system struggles.</p><h4>2. Composition and functions</h4><div class=\"table-wrap\"><table class=\"study-table\"><thead><tr><th>Feature</th><th>Detail</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Members</td><td>Between ten and twenty-five per committee, depending on the treaty; over 170 in total</td></tr><tr><td>Status</td><td>Independent experts of high moral standing and recognised competence, serving in a personal capacity, not as government representatives</td></tr><tr><td>Election</td><td>By the states parties to the treaty, usually for four-year renewable terms</td></tr><tr><td>Working time</td><td>Part-time, typically two or three sessions a year, with an honorarium rather than a salary</td></tr></tbody></table></div><p>Each committee exercises up to four functions.</p><ol><li><strong>Reviewing periodic state reports</strong> and issuing concluding observations.</li><li><strong>Issuing General Comments or General Recommendations</strong>, which interpret the treaty.</li><li><strong>Deciding individual communications</strong>, where the state has accepted that procedure.</li><li><strong>Conducting inquiries</strong> into grave or systematic violations, where the state has not opted out.</li></ol><h4>3. The reporting cycle</h4><ol><li><strong>Initial report</strong>, usually due one or two years after entry into force for the state, then periodic reports at intervals set by each treaty, commonly four or five years.</li><li><strong>List of issues.</strong> The committee identifies the questions it wants answered. Under the simplified reporting procedure a list of issues prior to reporting is sent first and the state's replies constitute its report, which shortens the process considerably.</li><li><strong>Written replies</strong> from the state to the list of issues.</li><li><strong>Parallel reports</strong> from NGOs and national human rights institutions, sometimes called shadow reports, which supply the counterweight to the state's own account.</li><li><strong>Constructive dialogue</strong> with the state delegation, typically over one or two meetings.</li><li><strong>Concluding observations</strong>: positive aspects, principal subjects of concern, and recommendations.</li><li><strong>Follow-up procedure.</strong> Most committees select two to four priority recommendations for a response within one or two years, assessed by a follow-up rapporteur.</li><li><strong>Next cycle</strong>, which begins from the previous concluding observations.</li></ol><div class=\"study-callout\"><strong>Note:</strong> The parallel report is where advocacy has most purchase. Committees have limited independent research capacity, so a well-evidenced parallel report frequently shapes the list of issues and appears almost verbatim in the concluding observations. Preparing one is a core professional skill in this field.</div><h4>4. Writing an effective parallel report</h4><div class=\"table-wrap\"><table class=\"study-table\"><thead><tr><th>Element</th><th>Practice</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Structure</td><td>Follow the treaty's article order so the committee can map it against the state report</td></tr><tr><td>Focus</td><td>A small number of well-evidenced issues beats a comprehensive survey</td></tr><tr><td>Evidence</td><td>Primary data, case examples with dates, official statistics and their gaps</td></tr><tr><td>Contrast</td><td>Where the state report claims progress, say precisely what is inaccurate or incomplete</td></tr><tr><td>Questions</td><td>Propose the exact questions you want on the list of issues</td></tr><tr><td>Recommendations</td><td>Propose the exact wording you want in the concluding observations</td></tr><tr><td>Length</td><td>Respect the page limits; committees read what is short and discard what is not</td></tr></tbody></table></div><h4>5. General Comments</h4><div class=\"study-defn\"><strong>General Comment:</strong> An authoritative interpretation by a treaty body of a provision of its treaty, or of a cross-cutting theme, issued outside the context of any individual case.</div><p>They are the principal vehicle by which treaty law develops. Their legal status is contested but their practical authority is high.</p><ul><li>They are not binding in the way a judgment is. No treaty provides that a General Comment binds states parties.</li><li>They are the considered interpretation of the body the states themselves established to supervise the treaty, and are given considerable weight by national and international courts.</li><li>Some states have objected to particular General Comments as exceeding the committee's mandate, notably in relation to reservations and to the extension of rights beyond the text.</li><li>Courts across the world cite them routinely, which is the practical test of authority.</li></ul><div class=\"table-wrap\"><table class=\"study-table\"><thead><tr><th>General Comment</th><th>Body</th><th>Significance</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>CESCR GC 3</td><td>CESCR</td><td>Obligations of immediate effect and the minimum core</td></tr><tr><td>CESCR GC 14</td><td>CESCR</td><td>The right to health, with the availability, accessibility, acceptability and quality framework</td></tr><tr><td>CCPR GC 29</td><td>Human Rights Committee</td><td>Derogation; guarantees protecting non-derogable rights are themselves non-derogable</td></tr><tr><td>CCPR GC 31</td><td>Human Rights Committee</td><td>The nature of the general legal obligation, including positive duties and horizontal effect</td></tr><tr><td>CCPR GC 36</td><td>Human Rights Committee</td><td>The right to life, read expansively</td></tr><tr><td>CEDAW GR 19 and 35</td><td>CEDAW Committee</td><td>Gender-based violence as discrimination and the due diligence standard</td></tr><tr><td>CRC GC 14</td><td>CRC Committee</td><td>Best interests of the child as right, principle and rule of procedure</td></tr><tr><td>CRPD GC 1</td><td>CRPD Committee</td><td>Legal capacity and supported decision-making</td></tr></tbody></table></div><h4>6. Problems and reform</h4><div class=\"table-wrap\"><table class=\"study-table\"><thead><tr><th>Problem</th><th>Detail</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Reporting burden</td><td>A state party to all core treaties faces a report roughly every year across committees, plus follow-up, which few small states can sustain</td></tr><tr><td>Non-reporting</td><td>A large proportion of reports are overdue, some by decades; committees have adopted review in the absence of a report as a response</td></tr><tr><td>Backlog</td><td>Reports and individual communications wait years for consideration</td></tr><tr><td>Under-resourcing</td><td>Committees meet part-time on honoraria with limited secretariat support, while the number of treaties and parties has grown</td></tr><tr><td>Duplication</td><td>Similar questions are asked by several committees, since the treaties overlap substantially</td></tr><tr><td>Weak implementation</td><td>Concluding observations are frequently not implemented and repeat across cycles</td></tr></tbody></table></div><p>Reform proposals have included a single consolidated treaty body, which states have not accepted; a comprehensive reporting calendar with fixed predictable dates for every state, partially adopted; the simplified reporting procedure based on a list of issues prior to reporting, now widely used; alignment of methods across committees, advanced through the annual meeting of chairpersons; and digital tools for tracking recommendations across mechanisms, of which the UN human rights implementation index and national mechanisms for implementation, reporting and follow-up are the main examples.</p><div class=\"study-defn\"><strong>National Mechanism for Implementation, Reporting and Follow-up:</strong> A standing domestic body coordinating a state's engagement with all human rights mechanisms and tracking implementation of their recommendations.</div><h4>7. How the examiner&#x27;s question should be answered</h4><p>A question on treaty bodies usually asks about effectiveness. Give the reporting cycle accurately, because the marks for description depend on precision about the list of issues, parallel reports, constructive dialogue and follow-up. Then take the problems in the table and pair each with the reform attempted, showing which have worked. The simplified reporting procedure has genuinely reduced burden; the consolidated treaty body has not happened. Conclude by noting that the treaty bodies' real contribution is interpretive: the General Comments are used far beyond the reporting cycle and outlast any individual dialogue.</p><h4>Summary</h4><ul><li>Ten treaty bodies of independent experts review reports, issue General Comments, decide communications and conduct inquiries.</li><li>The reporting cycle runs from list of issues through parallel reports and constructive dialogue to concluding observations and follow-up.</li><li>Parallel reports from NGOs are the main counterweight to state self-reporting and frequently shape the outcome.</li><li>General Comments are not binding but carry high practical authority and are cited by courts worldwide.</li><li>The system suffers from reporting burden, non-reporting, backlog, under-resourcing, duplication and weak implementation.</li><li>The simplified reporting procedure and national implementation mechanisms are the reforms that have gained traction.</li></ul><h4>Key Terms</h4><div class=\"table-wrap\"><table class=\"study-table\"><thead><tr><th>Term</th><th>Meaning</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Concluding observations</td><td>A treaty body&#x27;s findings and recommendations after reviewing a state report.</td></tr><tr><td>List of issues</td><td>The questions a committee puts to a state, which under the simplified procedure precede and replace the report.</td></tr><tr><td>Parallel report</td><td>An NGO or national institution submission providing an alternative account to the state report.</td></tr><tr><td>General Comment</td><td>An authoritative treaty body interpretation of a provision or theme.</td></tr><tr><td>Constructive dialogue</td><td>The public exchange between a committee and a state delegation during review.</td></tr></tbody></table></div><h4>Review Questions</h4><h5>Multiple choice</h5><div class=\"study-mcq\"><p class=\"mcq-q\">What is the simplified reporting procedure?</p><ol type=\"A\"><li>The committee sends a list of issues prior to reporting and the state&#x27;s replies constitute its report</li><li>The state submits a shorter report with no dialogue</li><li>Reports are replaced by a questionnaire from the Secretariat</li><li>The committee reviews the state without any state input</li></ol><details class=\"mcq-a\"><summary>Show answer</summary><p><strong>Answer: A.</strong> It shortens the process considerably and has been the most widely adopted of the reform proposals.</p></details></div><div class=\"study-mcq\"><p class=\"mcq-q\">Why do parallel reports matter so much?</p><ol type=\"A\"><li>Committees have limited independent research capacity, so well-evidenced parallel reports often shape the list of issues and the concluding observations</li><li>They are legally binding on the state</li><li>They replace the state report</li><li>They are required by every treaty</li></ol><details class=\"mcq-a\"><summary>Show answer</summary><p><strong>Answer: A.</strong> This is why preparing one is a core professional skill in human rights practice.</p></details></div><div class=\"study-mcq\"><p class=\"mcq-q\">What is the legal status of a General Comment?</p><ol type=\"A\"><li>Not binding, but the considered interpretation of the supervisory body and given considerable weight by courts</li><li>Binding on all states parties</li><li>Binding only on states that voted for it</li><li>A recommendation with no legal significance</li></ol><details class=\"mcq-a\"><summary>Show answer</summary><p><strong>Answer: A.</strong> Some states have objected to particular General Comments as exceeding a committee&#x27;s mandate, but courts cite them routinely.</p></details></div><div class=\"study-mcq\"><p class=\"mcq-q\">Which reform proposal have states not accepted?</p><ol type=\"A\"><li>A single consolidated treaty body replacing the ten committees</li><li>The simplified reporting procedure</li><li>A comprehensive reporting calendar</li><li>Alignment of working methods across committees</li></ol><details class=\"mcq-a\"><summary>Show answer</summary><p><strong>Answer: A.</strong> It would require amending every treaty and would reduce specialised expertise, and states have shown no appetite for it.</p></details></div><h5>Short answer</h5><div class=\"study-sa\"><p class=\"sa-q\">Describe the treaty body reporting cycle.</p><details class=\"sa-a\"><summary>Model answer</summary><p>A state submits an initial report shortly after the treaty enters into force for it, then periodic reports at intervals of four or five years. The committee issues a list of issues identifying the questions it wants answered, or under the simplified procedure sends the list before any report so that the replies constitute the report. NGOs and national human rights institutions submit parallel reports providing an alternative account. A constructive dialogue is then held with the state delegation over one or two meetings. The committee issues concluding observations setting out positive aspects, principal subjects of concern and recommendations. It selects a small number of priority recommendations for follow-up within one or two years, assessed by a follow-up rapporteur, and the next cycle begins from those observations.</p></details></div><div class=\"study-sa\"><p class=\"sa-q\">Give four problems with the treaty body system and the reform attempted for each.</p><details class=\"sa-a\"><summary>Model answer</summary><p>The reporting burden, since a state party to all core treaties faces a report almost every year, has been addressed by the simplified reporting procedure and a comprehensive reporting calendar. Widespread non-reporting has been met by committees adopting the practice of reviewing a state in the absence of a report. Backlog and under-resourcing prompted the proposal of a single consolidated treaty body, which states rejected, so committees have instead extended session time where funding allows. Duplication between overlapping treaties has been addressed by aligning working methods through the annual meeting of chairpersons, and weak implementation by encouraging states to establish standing national mechanisms for implementation, reporting and follow-up.</p></details></div><h4>Class Activity</h4><ol><li>Obtain your country&#x27;s most recent concluding observations from any treaty body. Identify which recommendations were repeated from the previous cycle, which indicates non-implementation.</li><li>Draft a two-page parallel report on one issue, following the structure in this lesson and ending with three proposed questions for the list of issues and three proposed recommendations.</li></ol><h4>Assignment</h4><p>Write one and a half pages on treaty bodies and state reporting. Describe the composition and four functions of the treaty bodies, set out the reporting cycle in full including parallel reports and follow-up, explain what General Comments are and what authority they carry with two examples, and analyse the system&#x27;s principal problems alongside the reforms attempted for each.</p><p class=\"study-print\"><strong>Printable:</strong> use your browser\u2019s Print \u2192 Save as PDF to keep an offline copy.</p></div>",
  "Special Procedures and Rapporteurs": "<div class=\"study-note\"><div class=\"revision-banner\"><strong>Human Rights and International Humanitarian Law</strong><span>Module 6: United Nations Human Rights Machinery</span></div><h3 class=\"lesson-note-title\">LESSON NOTE: SPECIAL PROCEDURES AND RAPPORTEURS</h3><div class=\"table-wrap\"><table class=\"study-table\"><thead><tr><th>Subject</th><th>Topic</th><th>Level</th><th>Lesson duration</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Human Rights and International Humanitarian Law</td><td>Special Procedures and Rapporteurs</td><td>Beginner to Advanced</td><td>40 minutes</td></tr></tbody></table></div><h4>Learning objectives</h4><p>By the end of this lesson you should be able to:</p><ul><li>Explain what special procedures are and how mandate holders are appointed</li><li>Distinguish thematic from country mandates</li><li>Describe the four working methods and their uses</li><li>Explain how to submit information effectively</li></ul><h4>1. Introduction</h4><p>Special procedures are the most flexible and often the fastest part of the UN human rights system. A mandate holder can send a letter to a government within days, visit a country, report publicly and speak to the media, none of which a treaty body can do. For urgent situations they are usually the first mechanism to use.</p><h4>2. What they are</h4><div class=\"study-defn\"><strong>Special procedures:</strong> Independent human rights experts appointed by the Human Rights Council with mandates to report and advise on human rights from a thematic or country-specific perspective.</div><div class=\"table-wrap\"><table class=\"study-table\"><thead><tr><th>Feature</th><th>Detail</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Types of mandate holder</td><td>Special Rapporteur, Independent Expert, or a Working Group of five members with regional distribution</td></tr><tr><td>Appointment</td><td>By the President of the Human Rights Council, following a public call, review by a Consultative Group, and consultation; formally endorsed by the Council</td></tr><tr><td>Term</td><td>Up to six years, in two three-year terms</td></tr><tr><td>Status</td><td>Unpaid and independent; they are not UN staff and do not represent any government</td></tr><tr><td>Number of mandates</td><td>More than forty thematic and a smaller number of country mandates, with the exact figure changing as the Council creates and ends them</td></tr><tr><td>Governing framework</td><td>A Code of Conduct adopted by the Council and a Manual of Operations developed by the mandate holders themselves</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"study-callout\"><strong>Note:</strong> Independence is the defining feature and the source of both their value and the friction they generate. Because they are unpaid and not UN staff, they can say things no official can. Because they are appointed by a political body, states periodically attempt to constrain them through the Code of Conduct, resolutions narrowing mandates, or refusal of access.</div><h4>3. Thematic and country mandates</h4><div class=\"table-wrap\"><table class=\"study-table\"><thead><tr><th></th><th>Thematic</th><th>Country</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Covers</td><td>One issue, worldwide</td><td>One state's overall situation</td></tr><tr><td>Examples of subject</td><td>Torture, extrajudicial executions, freedom of expression, human rights defenders, the right to food, water and sanitation, housing, health, education, migrants, trafficking, slavery, arbitrary detention, enforced disappearances, indigenous peoples, older persons, disability, minority issues, business and human rights, environment, privacy, counter-terrorism, unilateral coercive measures</td><td>Established for a specific state by Council resolution</td></tr><tr><td>Consent</td><td>Can address any state without its consent for communications; a visit requires an invitation</td><td>Frequently established without the state's cooperation</td></tr><tr><td>Politics</td><td>Less contested; states accept thematic scrutiny more readily</td><td>Highly contested; states routinely refuse access to country mandate holders</td></tr></tbody></table></div><p>Because country mandates are politically difficult to create, thematic mandate holders often carry the burden of examining a specific situation, either jointly or through successive communications. Joint communications from several thematic mandate holders on one situation are common and carry more weight than a single letter.</p><h4>4. The four working methods</h4><ol><li><strong>Communications.</strong> Letters to governments about alleged violations, in two forms. An urgent appeal is used where the violation is ongoing or imminent and there is a risk of irreparable harm; it asks the state to act immediately. A letter of allegation concerns violations that have already occurred. Both are published, with the government's reply, in a communications report to the Council, usually after sixty days.</li><li><strong>Country visits.</strong> Conducted only with the state's consent. A visit typically lasts one to two weeks, includes meetings with officials, civil society and victims, visits to places of detention, and ends with preliminary observations at a press conference followed by a full report to the Council. Standing invitations, extended by around a third of UN member states, are open invitations to all thematic mandate holders.</li><li><strong>Thematic reports.</strong> Annual or biannual reports developing the normative content of the mandate. Many of the most influential soft law standards began here, including the Guiding Principles on Business and Human Rights and the Guiding Principles on Internal Displacement.</li><li><strong>Advocacy and awareness.</strong> Public statements, press releases, engagement with media, contributions to treaty negotiations, and technical advice to states.</li></ol><div class=\"study-defn\"><strong>Urgent appeal:</strong> A communication sent where a violation is ongoing or imminent and there is a risk of irreparable harm, calling on the state to take immediate action.</div><div class=\"study-callout\"><strong>Note:</strong> Communications do not require exhaustion of domestic remedies, do not require the state to have ratified any treaty, and can be sent within days. Those three features together explain why they are the mechanism of choice in emergencies. What they cannot do is produce a binding determination.</div><h4>5. Submitting information effectively</h4><div class=\"table-wrap\"><table class=\"study-table\"><thead><tr><th>Element</th><th>Requirement</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Identify the right mandate</td><td>Match the subject precisely; check whether a joint submission to several mandates is appropriate</td></tr><tr><td>Victim details</td><td>Name, age, occupation, and consent to disclosure of identity to the government, or an explicit request for confidentiality</td></tr><tr><td>The facts</td><td>Date, place, what happened, who did it, in chronological order and without rhetoric</td></tr><tr><td>Attribution</td><td>Evidence connecting the act to the state or to actors it should have controlled</td></tr><tr><td>Domestic steps</td><td>What was reported to whom and what happened, even though exhaustion is not required</td></tr><tr><td>Legal basis</td><td>The provisions engaged, national and international</td></tr><tr><td>Requested action</td><td>What you want the mandate holder to ask the government to do</td></tr><tr><td>Channel</td><td>The OHCHR online submission form for communications, or the mandate's dedicated address</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"study-callout\"><strong>Example:</strong> A human rights defender is arrested at 3am, held incommunicado and denied access to a lawyer. A submission the same day to the Special Rapporteur on the situation of human rights defenders, the Working Group on Arbitrary Detention and the Special Rapporteur on torture, setting out the facts, the legal provisions and a request for an urgent appeal, can produce a joint letter to the government within days. The letter will not free the detainee by itself, but it establishes an international record, signals scrutiny, and in practice frequently improves treatment and accelerates access to counsel.</div><h4>6. Strengths and limits</h4><div class=\"table-wrap\"><table class=\"study-table\"><thead><tr><th>Strength</th><th>Limit</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Fast: urgent appeals can be sent in days</td><td>No binding power; findings are recommendations</td></tr><tr><td>Covers all states regardless of ratification</td><td>Country visits require consent, which is often refused</td></tr><tr><td>Independent, so able to speak plainly</td><td>Unpaid and under-resourced, with minimal secretariat support</td></tr><tr><td>Develops normative standards through thematic reports</td><td>Vulnerable to political attempts to narrow mandates</td></tr><tr><td>No exhaustion requirement</td><td>Reprisals against those who provide information are a documented problem</td></tr><tr><td>Can address non-state actors and businesses</td><td>Coverage is uneven; some issues have no mandate</td></tr></tbody></table></div><h4>7. How the examiner&#x27;s question should be answered</h4><p>Define special procedures and state the appointment process and independence, because independence explains everything else. Distinguish thematic from country mandates and note why country mandates are politically harder. Give the four working methods, with the distinction between urgent appeals and letters of allegation stated precisely. Then give the three features that make communications the emergency mechanism: no exhaustion, no ratification requirement, and speed. Close with strengths against limits and a concrete illustration.</p><h4>Summary</h4><ul><li>Special procedures are independent, unpaid experts appointed by the Human Rights Council with thematic or country mandates.</li><li>Mandate holders serve up to six years, are not UN staff, and are governed by a Code of Conduct and a Manual of Operations.</li><li>Their four working methods are communications, country visits, thematic reports, and advocacy.</li><li>Urgent appeals concern ongoing or imminent violations; letters of allegation concern violations that have occurred.</li><li>Communications require no exhaustion of remedies and no ratification, and can be sent within days.</li><li>Thematic reports have produced influential soft law, including the Guiding Principles on Business and Human Rights.</li></ul><h4>Key Terms</h4><div class=\"table-wrap\"><table class=\"study-table\"><thead><tr><th>Term</th><th>Meaning</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Special procedures</td><td>Independent expert mandates of the Human Rights Council covering a theme or a country.</td></tr><tr><td>Urgent appeal</td><td>A communication about an ongoing or imminent violation with a risk of irreparable harm.</td></tr><tr><td>Letter of allegation</td><td>A communication concerning a violation that has already occurred.</td></tr><tr><td>Standing invitation</td><td>An open invitation from a state to all thematic mandate holders to visit at any time.</td></tr></tbody></table></div><h4>Review Questions</h4><h5>Multiple choice</h5><div class=\"study-mcq\"><p class=\"mcq-q\">Which three features make special procedure communications the mechanism of choice in emergencies?</p><ol type=\"A\"><li>No exhaustion requirement, no ratification requirement, and they can be sent within days</li><li>Binding effect, speed and confidentiality</li><li>State consent, funding and enforcement</li><li>Judicial process, appeal and compensation</li></ol><details class=\"mcq-a\"><summary>Show answer</summary><p><strong>Answer: A.</strong> What they cannot do is produce a binding determination, which is the trade-off for that speed and reach.</p></details></div><div class=\"study-mcq\"><p class=\"mcq-q\">What distinguishes an urgent appeal from a letter of allegation?</p><ol type=\"A\"><li>An urgent appeal concerns an ongoing or imminent violation with a risk of irreparable harm</li><li>An urgent appeal is confidential</li><li>A letter of allegation is sent only to treaty parties</li><li>An urgent appeal requires Council approval</li></ol><details class=\"mcq-a\"><summary>Show answer</summary><p><strong>Answer: A.</strong> A letter of allegation concerns violations that have already occurred, so the request is investigation rather than immediate protective action.</p></details></div><div class=\"study-mcq\"><p class=\"mcq-q\">What is a standing invitation?</p><ol type=\"A\"><li>An open invitation from a state to all thematic mandate holders to visit at any time</li><li>A permanent seat on the Human Rights Council</li><li>An invitation to submit a parallel report</li><li>A treaty body&#x27;s request for a special report</li></ol><details class=\"mcq-a\"><summary>Show answer</summary><p><strong>Answer: A.</strong> Around a third of UN member states have extended one, and doing so is a common UPR recommendation.</p></details></div><div class=\"study-mcq\"><p class=\"mcq-q\">Which soft law standard originated in a special procedures thematic report?</p><ol type=\"A\"><li>The Guiding Principles on Business and Human Rights</li><li>The Universal Declaration of Human Rights</li><li>The Convention Against Torture</li><li>The Rome Statute</li></ol><details class=\"mcq-a\"><summary>Show answer</summary><p><strong>Answer: A.</strong> The Guiding Principles on Internal Displacement have a similar origin, which shows how thematic mandates develop normative content.</p></details></div><h5>Short answer</h5><div class=\"study-sa\"><p class=\"sa-q\">Describe the four working methods of special procedures.</p><details class=\"sa-a\"><summary>Model answer</summary><p>First, communications: letters to governments about alleged violations, sent either as urgent appeals where harm is imminent or as letters of allegation where the violation has occurred, and published with any government reply. Second, country visits, conducted only with the state&#x27;s consent, involving meetings with officials, civil society and victims, visits to detention facilities, preliminary observations at a press conference and a full report to the Council. Third, thematic reports developing the normative content of the mandate, several of which have produced influential soft law standards. Fourth, advocacy and awareness work, including public statements, media engagement, contributions to treaty negotiations and technical advice to states.</p></details></div><div class=\"study-sa\"><p class=\"sa-q\">What should a submission to a special procedure contain?</p><details class=\"sa-a\"><summary>Model answer</summary><p>It should identify the correct mandate or mandates, since joint submissions are often appropriate. It should give the victim&#x27;s details with either consent to disclose the identity to the government or an explicit request for confidentiality. It should set out the facts chronologically and without rhetoric: date, place, what happened and who did it. It should explain what connects the act to the state or to actors the state should have controlled. It should record what was reported domestically and what resulted, even though exhaustion is not required. It should cite the national and international provisions engaged. And it should state precisely what action the mandate holder is being asked to request of the government.</p></details></div><h4>Class Activity</h4><ol><li>Choose one thematic mandate. Read its most recent report and summarise in one page the main standard it develops and the recommendations it makes.</li><li>Draft a one-page communication to a special procedure about a real or realistic case, following the eight elements in this lesson.</li></ol><h4>Assignment</h4><p>Write one and a half pages on special procedures. Define them, explain how mandate holders are appointed and why independence matters, distinguish thematic from country mandates and explain why the latter are politically harder, describe the four working methods with the distinction between urgent appeals and letters of allegation, explain how to submit information effectively, and set out the strengths and limits of the mechanism.</p><p class=\"study-print\"><strong>Printable:</strong> use your browser\u2019s Print \u2192 Save as PDF to keep an offline copy.</p></div>",
  "The Office of the High Commissioner": "<div class=\"study-note\"><div class=\"revision-banner\"><strong>Human Rights and International Humanitarian Law</strong><span>Module 6: United Nations Human Rights Machinery</span></div><h3 class=\"lesson-note-title\">LESSON NOTE: THE OFFICE OF THE HIGH COMMISSIONER</h3><div class=\"table-wrap\"><table class=\"study-table\"><thead><tr><th>Subject</th><th>Topic</th><th>Level</th><th>Lesson duration</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Human Rights and International Humanitarian Law</td><td>The Office of the High Commissioner</td><td>Beginner to Advanced</td><td>40 minutes</td></tr></tbody></table></div><h4>Learning objectives</h4><p>By the end of this lesson you should be able to:</p><ul><li>Describe the mandate and creation of the High Commissioner for Human Rights</li><li>Describe the structure and functions of OHCHR</li><li>Explain the role of field presences and technical cooperation</li><li>Explain the Paris Principles and national human rights institutions</li></ul><h4>1. Introduction</h4><p>OHCHR is the operational arm of the international human rights system. It does not decide cases or adopt resolutions; it services the bodies that do, deploys staff to the field, and provides the analytical and secretariat capacity without which the rest of the machinery would not function.</p><h4>2. Creation and mandate</h4><div class=\"table-wrap\"><table class=\"study-table\"><thead><tr><th>Element</th><th>Detail</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Created</td><td>General Assembly resolution 48/141 of December 1993, following the recommendation of the Vienna World Conference</td></tr><tr><td>Rank</td><td>Under-Secretary-General; appointed by the Secretary-General with the approval of the General Assembly for four years, renewable once</td></tr><tr><td>Principal responsibility</td><td>The UN official with principal responsibility for United Nations human rights activities, under the direction of the Secretary-General</td></tr><tr><td>Seat</td><td>Geneva, with an office in New York and presences worldwide</td></tr></tbody></table></div><p>Resolution 48/141 gives the High Commissioner a wide mandate: to promote and protect all human rights, to provide advisory services and technical assistance, to coordinate human rights across the UN system, to engage in dialogue with governments, to prevent violations, and to respond to serious violations. The last two are the source of most of the friction the post attracts, because prevention and response require public criticism of states.</p><h4>3. Functions of the Office</h4><ol><li><strong>Secretariat support.</strong> OHCHR services the Human Rights Council, the UPR, the treaty bodies and the special procedures, including preparing the UPR compilation and stakeholder summary.</li><li><strong>Monitoring and analysis.</strong> Country and thematic reporting, including public reports on situations of concern.</li><li><strong>Field operations.</strong> Country offices, regional offices, human rights components of peace operations, and human rights advisers within UN country teams.</li><li><strong>Technical cooperation.</strong> Assistance with legislative reform, national action plans, training of officials, and support for national human rights institutions.</li><li><strong>Standard setting support.</strong> Servicing negotiations of new instruments and producing professional training and practical guidance material.</li><li><strong>Coordination and mainstreaming.</strong> Integrating human rights across UN development, humanitarian and peace and security work, including through the Human Rights Up Front initiative and the Call to Action for Human Rights.</li></ol><h4>4. Field presences</h4><div class=\"table-wrap\"><table class=\"study-table\"><thead><tr><th>Type</th><th>Function</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Country office</td><td>Full presence with a monitoring and technical cooperation mandate, established by agreement with the host state</td></tr><tr><td>Regional office</td><td>Covers several states, providing support where no country office exists</td></tr><tr><td>Human rights component of a peace operation</td><td>Integrated into a peacekeeping or political mission; monitors, reports and advises the mission</td></tr><tr><td>Human rights adviser</td><td>Deployed to a UN Resident Coordinator's office to support the country team</td></tr></tbody></table></div><p>Field presences are the part of the system closest to victims. They monitor and document, support national institutions, train police and prison staff, and in peace operations advise on protection of civilians. Their most sensitive function is public reporting on the host state, which is also the function most frequently resisted.</p><div class=\"study-callout\"><strong>Note:</strong> The tension at the heart of field work is between access and candour. An office that reports frankly may have its agreement terminated or its staff denied visas; an office that stays silent to preserve access loses its purpose. There is no general solution, and how a given office handles it is a legitimate subject of assessment.</div><h4>5. National human rights institutions and the Paris Principles</h4><div class=\"study-defn\"><strong>National human rights institution:</strong> A body established by a state with a constitutional or legislative mandate to promote and protect human rights, independent of government.</div><p>The Principles relating to the Status of National Institutions, adopted by the General Assembly in 1993 and known as the Paris Principles, set the criteria.</p><ol><li><strong>Mandate.</strong> As broad as possible, set out in a constitutional or legislative text specifying composition and competence.</li><li><strong>Independence.</strong> Guaranteed by statute, including in the appointment and dismissal of members and in the security of their tenure.</li><li><strong>Pluralism.</strong> Composition reflecting the social forces of civil society involved in human rights.</li><li><strong>Adequate resources.</strong> Sufficient funding to have its own staff and premises, so as not to depend on the government financially in a way that undermines independence.</li><li><strong>Adequate powers of investigation.</strong> Freedom to consider any question within its competence on its own initiative, to hear any person and obtain any information, and to address public opinion directly.</li></ol><p>Institutions are accredited by the Global Alliance of National Human Rights Institutions through its Sub-Committee on Accreditation. Status A means full compliance and carries speaking rights in the Human Rights Council and participation in the UPR. Status B means partial compliance without those rights. Accreditation is reviewed periodically, and institutions have been downgraded where independence was compromised.</p><div class=\"table-wrap\"><table class=\"study-table\"><thead><tr><th>Function of an NHRI</th><th>Example</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Complaints handling</td><td>Receiving and investigating individual complaints, with power to recommend remedies</td></tr><tr><td>Advice on legislation</td><td>Reviewing bills for compatibility with human rights obligations</td></tr><tr><td>Monitoring</td><td>Inspecting places of detention, often as the designated National Preventive Mechanism under OPCAT</td></tr><tr><td>Reporting</td><td>Submitting independent information to treaty bodies and the UPR</td></tr><tr><td>Education</td><td>Public awareness and training programmes</td></tr><tr><td>Inquiry</td><td>Conducting national inquiries into systemic issues</td></tr></tbody></table></div><h4>6. Assessing OHCHR</h4><div class=\"table-wrap\"><table class=\"study-table\"><thead><tr><th>Strength</th><th>Constraint</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>The only permanent professional human rights capacity in the UN system</td><td>Chronically underfunded; the human rights pillar receives a small share of the regular UN budget</td></tr><tr><td>Field presences bring monitoring close to victims</td><td>Dependence on host state consent for country offices</td></tr><tr><td>Independent public voice of the High Commissioner</td><td>Political pressure on the post, and on individual High Commissioners who speak plainly</td></tr><tr><td>Servicing all the machinery gives it a system-wide view</td><td>Heavy reliance on voluntary contributions, which raises questions about donor influence on priorities</td></tr><tr><td>Technical cooperation produces concrete legal and institutional change</td><td>Technical cooperation can be used by states as an alternative to accountability</td></tr></tbody></table></div><h4>7. How the examiner&#x27;s question should be answered</h4><p>Distinguish the High Commissioner, who is a person with a mandate, from OHCHR, which is the office supporting that mandate and servicing the machinery. Give resolution 48/141 and 1993 as the origin, since the Vienna Conference link is worth a mark. On national institutions, always give the five Paris Principles and the A and B accreditation distinction, because that is the examinable content, and note that accreditation can be downgraded, which shows the system has teeth. Close with the access and candour tension in field work, which is the analytical point.</p><h4>Summary</h4><ul><li>The post of High Commissioner was created by General Assembly resolution 48/141 in 1993 following the Vienna World Conference.</li><li>OHCHR services the Council, the UPR, the treaty bodies and special procedures, and conducts monitoring, field work and technical cooperation.</li><li>Field presences include country offices, regional offices, peace operation components and human rights advisers.</li><li>The Paris Principles require a broad mandate, independence, pluralism, adequate resources and adequate powers of investigation.</li><li>Accreditation as Status A carries speaking rights at the Council; institutions can be downgraded to B if independence is compromised.</li><li>OHCHR&#x27;s central constraints are underfunding, dependence on host state consent, and reliance on voluntary contributions.</li></ul><h4>Key Terms</h4><div class=\"table-wrap\"><table class=\"study-table\"><thead><tr><th>Term</th><th>Meaning</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>OHCHR</td><td>The Office of the United Nations High Commissioner for Human Rights.</td></tr><tr><td>Resolution 48/141</td><td>The 1993 General Assembly resolution creating the post of High Commissioner.</td></tr><tr><td>Paris Principles</td><td>The criteria for the status of national human rights institutions.</td></tr><tr><td>Status A accreditation</td><td>Full compliance with the Paris Principles, carrying participation rights in the Human Rights Council.</td></tr><tr><td>Field presence</td><td>An OHCHR office, mission component or adviser deployed in a country.</td></tr></tbody></table></div><h4>Review Questions</h4><h5>Multiple choice</h5><div class=\"study-mcq\"><p class=\"mcq-q\">When and how was the post of High Commissioner for Human Rights created?</p><ol type=\"A\"><li>By General Assembly resolution 48/141 in 1993, following the Vienna World Conference</li><li>By the UN Charter in 1945</li><li>By the Human Rights Council in 2006</li><li>By ECOSOC in 1946</li></ol><details class=\"mcq-a\"><summary>Show answer</summary><p><strong>Answer: A.</strong> The Vienna Declaration recommended the post and the General Assembly acted the same year.</p></details></div><div class=\"study-mcq\"><p class=\"mcq-q\">Which is one of the five Paris Principles?</p><ol type=\"A\"><li>Pluralism in composition, reflecting the social forces of civil society</li><li>Appointment of members by the head of state alone</li><li>Reporting exclusively to the executive</li><li>A mandate limited to civil and political rights</li></ol><details class=\"mcq-a\"><summary>Show answer</summary><p><strong>Answer: A.</strong> The others are a broad statutory mandate, guaranteed independence, adequate resources and adequate powers of investigation.</p></details></div><div class=\"study-mcq\"><p class=\"mcq-q\">What does Status A accreditation give a national human rights institution?</p><ol type=\"A\"><li>Speaking rights in the Human Rights Council and participation in the UPR</li><li>Power to issue binding orders</li><li>Immunity from national law</li><li>A seat on a treaty body</li></ol><details class=\"mcq-a\"><summary>Show answer</summary><p><strong>Answer: A.</strong> Status B means partial compliance without those rights, and institutions can be downgraded when independence is compromised.</p></details></div><div class=\"study-mcq\"><p class=\"mcq-q\">What is the central tension in OHCHR field work?</p><ol type=\"A\"><li>Between candour in public reporting and maintaining access through host state consent</li><li>Between monitoring and training</li><li>Between Geneva and New York</li><li>Between treaty bodies and the Council</li></ol><details class=\"mcq-a\"><summary>Show answer</summary><p><strong>Answer: A.</strong> An office that reports frankly may lose its agreement, while an office that stays silent loses its purpose.</p></details></div><h5>Short answer</h5><div class=\"study-sa\"><p class=\"sa-q\">List the five Paris Principles and explain why each matters.</p><details class=\"sa-a\"><summary>Model answer</summary><p>A broad mandate set out in constitutional or legislative text, so the institution&#x27;s competence cannot be narrowed administratively. Guaranteed independence, including in appointment, dismissal and security of tenure, without which the institution cannot criticise the government that created it. Pluralism in composition, reflecting the social forces involved in human rights, so the institution is not captured by one interest. Adequate resources with its own staff and premises, because financial dependence on a ministry is a practical form of control. And adequate powers of investigation: the freedom to take up any question within its competence on its own initiative, to hear any person, to obtain any information and to address public opinion directly.</p></details></div><div class=\"study-sa\"><p class=\"sa-q\">Describe four functions of OHCHR.</p><details class=\"sa-a\"><summary>Model answer</summary><p>It provides secretariat support to the Human Rights Council, the Universal Periodic Review, the treaty bodies and the special procedures, including preparing the compilation and stakeholder summary used in each review. It monitors and analyses situations, producing country and thematic reports including public reports on situations of concern. It maintains field presences, whether country offices, regional offices, human rights components of peace operations, or advisers within UN country teams. And it provides technical cooperation, assisting states with legislative reform, national action plans, training of officials and the establishment of national human rights institutions.</p></details></div><h4>Class Activity</h4><ol><li>Find out whether your country has a national human rights institution, what its accreditation status is, and when it was last reviewed. Assess it against the five Paris Principles in one page.</li><li>Read one OHCHR country report. Identify three recommendations addressed to the government and explain how a national institution could take each forward.</li></ol><h4>Assignment</h4><p>Write one and a half pages on the Office of the High Commissioner for Human Rights. Describe the creation and mandate of the post, set out the functions of the Office, explain the types of field presence and the tension between candour and access, explain the Paris Principles and the accreditation system for national human rights institutions, and assess OHCHR&#x27;s strengths and constraints.</p><p class=\"study-print\"><strong>Printable:</strong> use your browser\u2019s Print \u2192 Save as PDF to keep an offline copy.</p></div>",
  "Individual Complaints Mechanisms": "<div class=\"study-note\"><div class=\"revision-banner\"><strong>Human Rights and International Humanitarian Law</strong><span>Module 6: United Nations Human Rights Machinery</span></div><h3 class=\"lesson-note-title\">LESSON NOTE: INDIVIDUAL COMPLAINTS MECHANISMS</h3><div class=\"table-wrap\"><table class=\"study-table\"><thead><tr><th>Subject</th><th>Topic</th><th>Level</th><th>Lesson duration</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Human Rights and International Humanitarian Law</td><td>Individual Complaints Mechanisms</td><td>Beginner to Advanced</td><td>45 minutes</td></tr></tbody></table></div><h4>Learning objectives</h4><p>By the end of this lesson you should be able to:</p><ul><li>Identify which treaty bodies accept individual communications and on what basis</li><li>State the admissibility requirements</li><li>Describe the procedure from submission to follow-up</li><li>Explain the status of views and the problem of compliance</li></ul><h4>1. Introduction</h4><p>For an individual whose rights have been violated, the individual communication is the closest the UN system comes to a remedy. It is slow, non-binding and available only against some states, but it produces a reasoned finding on the specific case, which nothing else in the UN system does.</p><h4>2. Which bodies, and on what basis</h4><div class=\"table-wrap\"><table class=\"study-table\"><thead><tr><th>Committee</th><th>Basis of the procedure</th><th>Note</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Human Rights Committee</td><td>First Optional Protocol to the ICCPR</td><td>The largest body of jurisprudence</td></tr><tr><td>CERD</td><td>ICERD Article 14 declaration</td><td></td></tr><tr><td>CAT Committee</td><td>CAT Article 22 declaration</td><td>Many Article 3 non-refoulement cases</td></tr><tr><td>CEDAW Committee</td><td>Optional Protocol 1999</td><td>Also has an inquiry procedure</td></tr><tr><td>CRPD Committee</td><td>Optional Protocol 2006</td><td></td></tr><tr><td>CED</td><td>Convention Article 31 declaration</td><td>Also the Article 30 urgent action procedure</td></tr><tr><td>CESCR</td><td>Optional Protocol 2008, in force 2013</td><td>Applies a reasonableness standard</td></tr><tr><td>CRC Committee</td><td>Third Optional Protocol 2011, in force 2014</td><td></td></tr><tr><td>CMW</td><td>Convention Article 77 declaration</td><td>In force after the required declarations were reached</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"study-callout\"><strong>Note:</strong> The critical point is that acceptance is always optional and separate from ratification of the treaty itself. A state may be party to the ICCPR without being party to the First Optional Protocol, in which case no individual may complain to the Human Rights Committee about it. The first question in any problem is therefore not 'has the state ratified the treaty' but 'has it accepted the complaints procedure'.</div><h4>3. Admissibility requirements</h4><ol><li><strong>Standing.</strong> The author must be a victim, or act with the victim's authorisation, or show why authorisation could not be obtained. Actio popularis, a complaint by someone not personally affected, is inadmissible.</li><li><strong>Jurisdiction ratione materiae.</strong> The complaint must concern a right protected by that treaty. A social security complaint cannot go to the Human Rights Committee unless framed under Article 26.</li><li><strong>Jurisdiction ratione temporis.</strong> The facts must post-date entry into force of the procedure for the state, unless the violation continues, as with enforced disappearance.</li><li><strong>Jurisdiction ratione loci and personae.</strong> The victim must be within the state's territory or subject to its jurisdiction, which extends to effective control abroad.</li><li><strong>Exhaustion of domestic remedies</strong>, subject to the usual exceptions for unavailability, ineffectiveness and unreasonable prolongation.</li><li><strong>No parallel examination.</strong> The same matter must not be under examination by another international procedure. Note the variation: some states have entered reservations extending this to matters already examined, which bars a complaint that has been to a regional body.</li><li><strong>Not an abuse of the right of submission</strong>, which includes excessive and unexplained delay after exhaustion.</li><li><strong>Sufficiently substantiated.</strong> A bare assertion without supporting material is declared inadmissible for lack of substantiation, and this is in practice the most common ground of rejection.</li></ol><h4>4. The procedure</h4><ol><li><strong>Submission</strong> in writing, signed, in one of the working languages, using the model complaint form where available.</li><li><strong>Registration</strong> by the Petitions and Urgent Actions Section of OHCHR, and transmission to the state.</li><li><strong>Interim measures.</strong> The committee may request the state not to take irreversible action, typically to halt a deportation or an execution, while the case is pending. Compliance with interim measures is treated by the committees as an obligation flowing from the state's acceptance of the procedure, and failure to comply is itself found to be a breach.</li><li><strong>State observations</strong> on admissibility and merits, usually within six months.</li><li><strong>Author's comments</strong> on the state's observations.</li><li><strong>Examination in closed session.</strong> There are no oral hearings and no witnesses; the procedure is entirely written.</li><li><strong>Views</strong> adopted, finding a violation or not, and if a violation, specifying the remedy required. Individual opinions, concurring or dissenting, may be appended.</li><li><strong>Follow-up.</strong> A special rapporteur for follow-up seeks information from the state on implementation and grades the response.</li></ol><div class=\"study-callout\"><strong>Note:</strong> The absence of oral hearings surprises students. Everything turns on the written record, which means the quality of the initial submission largely determines the outcome. There is no opportunity to repair a weak submission through advocacy at a hearing.</div><h4>5. The status of views</h4><p>Views are not binding judgments. The question is what they are instead, and General Comment 33 of the Human Rights Committee gives the standard answer.</p><ul><li>The Committee is not a judicial body, but its views exhibit some important characteristics of a judicial decision: they are arrived at in a judicial spirit, by impartial and independent experts, applying the terms of the Covenant.</li><li>The views represent an authoritative determination by the body established under the Covenant itself and entrusted with its interpretation.</li><li>A state party that fails to provide the remedy indicated fails in its obligation under Article 2(3) to provide an effective remedy, and acts in bad faith by ignoring the determination.</li><li>The obligation to cooperate with the procedure in good faith follows from the state's own decision to accept it.</li></ul><p>Compliance is the weak point. Full implementation is achieved in a minority of cases, and non-response is common. The follow-up rapporteur's grading system exists precisely because so many cases require chasing. This is the standard criticism, and any evaluative answer must state it.</p><h4>6. Choosing between mechanisms</h4><div class=\"table-wrap\"><table class=\"study-table\"><thead><tr><th>Consideration</th><th>Guidance</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Speed</td><td>Special procedures are far faster; communications take years</td></tr><tr><td>Bindingness</td><td>A regional court, where available, gives a binding judgment; prefer it</td></tr><tr><td>Coverage</td><td>Check which procedures the state has accepted before drafting anything</td></tr><tr><td>Parallel examination</td><td>Do not run the same matter in two international procedures; check for reservations extending the bar to concluded cases</td></tr><tr><td>Subject matter</td><td>Choose the treaty whose provision most directly covers the harm; ICCPR Article 26 is often the widest route</td></tr><tr><td>Remedy sought</td><td>If the aim is to stop an imminent removal or execution, seek interim measures immediately and in the same submission</td></tr></tbody></table></div><h4>7. How the examiner&#x27;s question should be answered</h4><p>In a problem question, take admissibility in the order above and reach a conclusion on each limb; that structure alone earns most of the procedural marks. Always begin with whether the state has accepted the procedure, since that disposes of many questions immediately. Then apply the substantive right. On remedies, state that views are not binding, give the General Comment 33 formulation rather than simply saying 'non-binding', and note the compliance record. If the facts involve imminent removal, mention interim measures, because omitting them is a serious practical error.</p><h4>Summary</h4><ul><li>Nine treaty bodies can consider individual communications, but always only where the state has separately accepted the procedure.</li><li>Admissibility requires victim standing, subject matter and temporal jurisdiction, exhaustion, no parallel examination, no abuse, and sufficient substantiation.</li><li>The procedure is entirely written, with no oral hearings, so the quality of the initial submission is decisive.</li><li>Committees may request interim measures to prevent irreversible harm, and non-compliance is itself treated as a breach.</li><li>General Comment 33 describes views as authoritative determinations that a state cannot in good faith ignore.</li><li>Compliance is the system&#x27;s weakest point, which is why a follow-up rapporteur grades state responses.</li></ul><h4>Key Terms</h4><div class=\"table-wrap\"><table class=\"study-table\"><thead><tr><th>Term</th><th>Meaning</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Individual communication</td><td>A complaint by an individual to a treaty body alleging a violation of the treaty by a state that has accepted the procedure.</td></tr><tr><td>Interim measures</td><td>A request that the state refrain from irreversible action while a case is pending.</td></tr><tr><td>Views</td><td>A treaty body&#x27;s reasoned finding on an individual communication.</td></tr><tr><td>Actio popularis</td><td>A complaint brought by a person not personally affected, which is inadmissible.</td></tr><tr><td>Substantiation</td><td>The requirement that a complaint be supported by sufficient material rather than bare assertion.</td></tr></tbody></table></div><h4>Review Questions</h4><h5>Multiple choice</h5><div class=\"study-mcq\"><p class=\"mcq-q\">What is the first question to ask before drafting an individual communication?</p><ol type=\"A\"><li>Whether the state has accepted the complaints procedure, which is separate from ratifying the treaty</li><li>Whether domestic remedies exist</li><li>Whether the violation is serious</li><li>Whether an NGO will support the case</li></ol><details class=\"mcq-a\"><summary>Show answer</summary><p><strong>Answer: A.</strong> A state may be party to the ICCPR without being party to the First Optional Protocol, in which case no complaint is possible.</p></details></div><div class=\"study-mcq\"><p class=\"mcq-q\">What is the most common ground on which communications are declared inadmissible?</p><ol type=\"A\"><li>Insufficient substantiation</li><li>Lack of standing</li><li>Parallel examination</li><li>Abuse of the right of submission</li></ol><details class=\"mcq-a\"><summary>Show answer</summary><p><strong>Answer: A.</strong> A bare assertion without supporting material fails, and there is no hearing at which to repair the submission.</p></details></div><div class=\"study-mcq\"><p class=\"mcq-q\">Why do interim measures matter in a deportation case?</p><ol type=\"A\"><li>They ask the state to refrain from irreversible action while the case is pending, and non-compliance is itself a breach</li><li>They make the eventual views binding</li><li>They shorten the procedure</li><li>They allow an oral hearing</li></ol><details class=\"mcq-a\"><summary>Show answer</summary><p><strong>Answer: A.</strong> Omitting to request them where removal is imminent is a serious practical error, because the case may become moot.</p></details></div><div class=\"study-mcq\"><p class=\"mcq-q\">How does General Comment 33 describe the Human Rights Committee&#x27;s views?</p><ol type=\"A\"><li>An authoritative determination by the body entrusted with interpreting the Covenant, which a state cannot in good faith ignore</li><li>A binding judgment enforceable by the Security Council</li><li>A non-binding suggestion of no legal weight</li><li>A recommendation to the General Assembly</li></ol><details class=\"mcq-a\"><summary>Show answer</summary><p><strong>Answer: A.</strong> Failure to provide the indicated remedy also breaches the Article 2(3) duty to provide an effective remedy.</p></details></div><h5>Short answer</h5><div class=\"study-sa\"><p class=\"sa-q\">List the admissibility requirements for an individual communication.</p><details class=\"sa-a\"><summary>Model answer</summary><p>The author must be a victim or duly authorised to act for one, so a complaint by an unaffected person is inadmissible. The complaint must concern a right protected by the treaty in question. The facts must fall within the period since the procedure entered into force for the state, unless the violation is continuing. The victim must have been within the state&#x27;s territory or jurisdiction, which extends to areas under its effective control. Domestic remedies must be exhausted, subject to exceptions for unavailability, ineffectiveness and unreasonable prolongation. The same matter must not be under examination by another international procedure, and some states have reserved to extend this bar to concluded examinations. The submission must not be an abuse of the right of submission, which includes long unexplained delay. And it must be sufficiently substantiated.</p></details></div><div class=\"study-sa\"><p class=\"sa-q\">Explain the status of treaty body views and the compliance problem.</p><details class=\"sa-a\"><summary>Model answer</summary><p>Views are not binding judgments; no treaty provides that they bind the state. General Comment 33 describes them instead as authoritative determinations reached in a judicial spirit by an impartial and independent body established under the Covenant and entrusted with its interpretation, so that a state which fails to provide the indicated remedy breaches its separate obligation to provide an effective remedy and acts in bad faith. In practice full implementation is achieved in a minority of cases and non-response is common, which is why each committee appoints a follow-up rapporteur who seeks information from the state and grades the reply. The gap between the authority claimed for views and their actual implementation is the standard criticism of the procedure.</p></details></div><h4>Class Activity</h4><ol><li>Read one set of views from any treaty body. Identify how each admissibility requirement was addressed and what remedy the committee specified.</li><li>Draft the admissibility section of a communication on a realistic set of facts, addressing each requirement in order and anticipating the state&#x27;s objections.</li></ol><h4>Assignment</h4><p>Write two pages on individual complaints mechanisms. Identify which treaty bodies accept communications and on what basis, set out the admissibility requirements in order, describe the procedure from submission through interim measures to views and follow-up, explain the status of views using General Comment 33, discuss the compliance problem, and explain how to choose between this and other mechanisms.</p><p class=\"study-print\"><strong>Printable:</strong> use your browser\u2019s Print \u2192 Save as PDF to keep an offline copy.</p></div>",
  "What Is International Humanitarian Law?": "<div class=\"study-note\"><div class=\"revision-banner\"><strong>Human Rights and International Humanitarian Law</strong><span>Module 7: Introduction to International Humanitarian Law</span></div><h3 class=\"lesson-note-title\">LESSON NOTE: WHAT IS INTERNATIONAL HUMANITARIAN LAW?</h3><div class=\"table-wrap\"><table class=\"study-table\"><thead><tr><th>Subject</th><th>Topic</th><th>Level</th><th>Lesson duration</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Human Rights and International Humanitarian Law</td><td>What Is International Humanitarian Law?</td><td>Beginner to Advanced</td><td>40 minutes</td></tr></tbody></table></div><h4>Learning objectives</h4><p>By the end of this lesson you should be able to:</p><ul><li>Define international humanitarian law and state its purpose</li><li>Distinguish jus ad bellum from jus in bello</li><li>Identify the sources and the two historical branches</li><li>Explain who is bound by IHL and when it applies</li></ul><h4>1. Introduction</h4><p>International humanitarian law accepts something most people find uncomfortable: that wars happen, and that regulating how they are fought saves lives even when preventing them fails. It does not ask whether a war is just. It asks how those fighting it must behave, and it applies the same rules to both sides regardless of who started it.</p><h4>2. Meaning</h4><div class=\"study-defn\"><strong>International humanitarian law:</strong> The body of rules which, in times of armed conflict, seeks to protect persons who are not or are no longer participating in hostilities, and to restrict the means and methods of warfare.</div><p>The definition has two halves and they correspond to the two historical branches of the subject.</p><div class=\"table-wrap\"><table class=\"study-table\"><thead><tr><th>Branch</th><th>Origin</th><th>Concern</th><th>Modern location</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Geneva law</td><td>The 1864 Geneva Convention onwards</td><td>Protection of persons: the wounded, the sick, shipwrecked, prisoners of war, civilians</td><td>The four Geneva Conventions of 1949</td></tr><tr><td>Hague law</td><td>The Hague Conventions of 1899 and 1907</td><td>Conduct of hostilities: permissible means and methods, weapons, targeting</td><td>The Hague Regulations, now largely reproduced in Additional Protocol I</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"study-callout\"><strong>Note:</strong> The two branches merged in practice with Additional Protocol I in 1977, which contains both protective and conduct-of-hostilities rules. The distinction is now largely historical, but examiners still ask for it and it explains why the modern treaty structure looks the way it does.</div><h4>3. Jus ad bellum and jus in bello</h4><div class=\"table-wrap\"><table class=\"study-table\"><thead><tr><th></th><th>Jus ad bellum</th><th>Jus in bello</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Question</td><td>When may a state resort to force?</td><td>How must force be conducted?</td></tr><tr><td>Source</td><td>UN Charter Article 2(4), Article 51, Chapter VII</td><td>Geneva Conventions, Additional Protocols, Hague law, custom</td></tr><tr><td>Applies to</td><td>The decision to go to war</td><td>The conduct of the war</td></tr><tr><td>Symmetry</td><td>Asymmetric: one side may be the aggressor</td><td>Symmetric: identical obligations on both sides</td></tr></tbody></table></div><p>The separation is fundamental and is the point students most often get wrong. IHL applies equally to all parties irrespective of the legality of the resort to force. A state defending itself against unlawful aggression has exactly the same obligations towards enemy prisoners and civilians as the aggressor.</p><ol><li><strong>Why the separation exists.</strong> Every party to every conflict believes its cause is just. If obligations depended on the justice of the cause, no party would consider itself bound.</li><li><strong>The consequence.</strong> A war crime is a war crime whoever commits it. 'They started it' is not a defence, and neither is 'our cause is just'.</li><li><strong>The textual basis.</strong> The preamble to Additional Protocol I states that the Conventions and the Protocol must be fully applied in all circumstances without any adverse distinction based on the nature or origin of the armed conflict or on the causes espoused by the parties.</li></ol><div class=\"study-callout\"><strong>Example:</strong> State A invades State B without justification. State B repels the invasion. During the fighting, State B's forces shoot prisoners of war. State B has committed war crimes. The unlawfulness of State A's invasion is a question of jus ad bellum and is entirely irrelevant to State B's obligations under jus in bello. This example is worth memorising because the point recurs in almost every IHL problem question.</div><h4>4. Sources</h4><div class=\"table-wrap\"><table class=\"study-table\"><thead><tr><th>Source</th><th>Content</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Treaties</td><td>The four Geneva Conventions of 1949, universally ratified; Additional Protocols I, II and III; Hague Conventions; weapons treaties; the 1954 Cultural Property Convention</td></tr><tr><td>Custom</td><td>Extensive. The ICRC customary IHL study of 2005 identified 161 rules, most applicable in both international and non-international conflict</td></tr><tr><td>General principles</td><td>Humanity, military necessity, distinction, proportionality</td></tr><tr><td>Judicial decisions</td><td>ICTY, ICTR, ICC, ICJ, and national courts, as subsidiary means of determining the law</td></tr><tr><td>Soft law and manuals</td><td>Military manuals, the San Remo Manual on naval warfare, the Tallinn Manual on cyber operations, the ICRC Commentaries</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"study-callout\"><strong>Note:</strong> Custom matters more in IHL than in most fields, for a practical reason: the treaty rules for non-international armed conflict are thin, comprising common Article 3 and Additional Protocol II, while customary law fills much of the gap. The ICRC study found that the great majority of its rules apply in both types of conflict, which substantially narrows the difference between the two regimes.</div><h4>5. Who is bound</h4><ul><li><strong>States party to the treaties.</strong> All 196 states are party to the Geneva Conventions, which is universal ratification, achieved by no other treaty regime.</li><li><strong>All states, through custom.</strong> A state not party to Additional Protocol I is still bound by those of its rules that reflect customary law.</li><li><strong>Non-state armed groups.</strong> Bound by common Article 3 and by customary law applicable in non-international conflict, and by Additional Protocol II where its threshold is met. The usual explanation is that a state's ratification binds all persons and entities within its territory, though other theories exist.</li><li><strong>Individuals.</strong> Personally criminally responsible for serious violations, whatever their rank or office.</li><li><strong>Multinational forces.</strong> UN and regional forces are bound; the Secretary-General's 1999 bulletin sets out the fundamental principles applicable to UN forces.</li></ul><h4>6. When it applies</h4><div class=\"table-wrap\"><table class=\"study-table\"><thead><tr><th>Question</th><th>Answer</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Trigger</td><td>The existence of an armed conflict as a matter of fact, not a declaration of war</td></tr><tr><td>Declaration required?</td><td>No. Common Article 2 applies to any armed conflict even if a state of war is not recognised by one of them</td></tr><tr><td>Duration</td><td>From the start of the conflict until the general close of military operations, and for protected persons until their final release, repatriation or re-establishment</td></tr><tr><td>Territorial scope</td><td>The whole territory of the parties, not only the immediate battlefield</td></tr><tr><td>Reciprocity</td><td>Not required. Obligations must be respected in all circumstances; a violation by one party does not permit violations by the other</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"study-defn\"><strong>Non-reciprocity:</strong> The principle that IHL obligations are not conditional on the other party's compliance; belligerent reprisals are tightly restricted and prohibited entirely against protected persons and objects.</div><h4>7. How the examiner&#x27;s question should be answered</h4><p>Open with the definition and both halves, protection of persons and restriction of means and methods, since a definition covering only one half is incomplete. Then give jus ad bellum and jus in bello with the reason for the separation and the worked example, because that is the conceptual point being tested. Then sources, noting the special importance of custom for non-international conflict. Then who is bound, remembering non-state armed groups and individuals. Close with non-reciprocity, which students frequently omit and which is one of the features that distinguishes IHL from ordinary treaty law.</p><h4>Summary</h4><ul><li>IHL protects those not or no longer participating in hostilities and restricts the means and methods of warfare.</li><li>Geneva law protects persons; Hague law regulates the conduct of hostilities; the branches merged in Additional Protocol I.</li><li>Jus ad bellum governs resort to force and jus in bello its conduct; the two are strictly separate and IHL applies equally to all parties.</li><li>Sources are treaties, extensive custom, general principles, judicial decisions and manuals; custom fills the thin treaty law on internal conflict.</li><li>States, non-state armed groups, individuals and multinational forces are all bound.</li><li>Application is triggered by the factual existence of armed conflict, requires no declaration, and does not depend on reciprocity.</li></ul><h4>Key Terms</h4><div class=\"table-wrap\"><table class=\"study-table\"><thead><tr><th>Term</th><th>Meaning</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>International humanitarian law</td><td>The law protecting those not participating in hostilities and restricting means and methods of warfare.</td></tr><tr><td>Jus ad bellum</td><td>The law governing when a state may resort to armed force.</td></tr><tr><td>Jus in bello</td><td>The law governing how armed force must be conducted.</td></tr><tr><td>Geneva law</td><td>The branch concerned with protection of persons.</td></tr><tr><td>Hague law</td><td>The branch concerned with the conduct of hostilities.</td></tr></tbody></table></div><h4>Review Questions</h4><h5>Multiple choice</h5><div class=\"study-mcq\"><p class=\"mcq-q\">A state defending itself against unlawful aggression mistreats prisoners of war. What is the position?</p><ol type=\"A\"><li>It has committed war crimes; the lawfulness of the other side&#x27;s resort to force is irrelevant</li><li>It is excused because the other side was the aggressor</li><li>IHL does not apply to a defending state</li><li>Only the aggressor can commit war crimes</li></ol><details class=\"mcq-a\"><summary>Show answer</summary><p><strong>Answer: A.</strong> Jus ad bellum and jus in bello are separate, and IHL binds all parties equally regardless of the cause.</p></details></div><div class=\"study-mcq\"><p class=\"mcq-q\">What triggers the application of IHL?</p><ol type=\"A\"><li>The factual existence of an armed conflict</li><li>A formal declaration of war</li><li>A Security Council resolution</li><li>Recognition of belligerency by third states</li></ol><details class=\"mcq-a\"><summary>Show answer</summary><p><strong>Answer: A.</strong> Common Article 2 applies to any armed conflict even where a state of war is not recognised by one of the parties.</p></details></div><div class=\"study-mcq\"><p class=\"mcq-q\">Why is customary law especially important in IHL?</p><ol type=\"A\"><li>Treaty rules for non-international armed conflict are thin, and custom fills much of the gap</li><li>Few states have ratified the Geneva Conventions</li><li>Treaties do not bind non-state actors at all</li><li>Custom overrides treaty law in wartime</li></ol><details class=\"mcq-a\"><summary>Show answer</summary><p><strong>Answer: A.</strong> The ICRC study identified 161 customary rules, most applicable in both types of conflict, which narrows the difference between the two regimes.</p></details></div><div class=\"study-mcq\"><p class=\"mcq-q\">Does a violation by one party permit the other to violate in return?</p><ol type=\"A\"><li>No; IHL obligations are not conditional on reciprocity</li><li>Yes, proportionate reprisals are always lawful</li><li>Yes, if the Security Council approves</li><li>Only in non-international armed conflict</li></ol><details class=\"mcq-a\"><summary>Show answer</summary><p><strong>Answer: A.</strong> Belligerent reprisals are tightly restricted and prohibited entirely against protected persons and objects.</p></details></div><h5>Short answer</h5><div class=\"study-sa\"><p class=\"sa-q\">Explain the separation of jus ad bellum and jus in bello and why it exists.</p><details class=\"sa-a\"><summary>Model answer</summary><p>Jus ad bellum governs whether a state may lawfully resort to armed force, drawing on the UN Charter&#x27;s prohibition of the use of force, the right of self-defence and Security Council authorisation. Jus in bello governs how hostilities must be conducted once a conflict exists. The two are strictly separate: IHL applies identically to every party regardless of which of them acted unlawfully in resorting to force. The reason is practical. Every party to every conflict believes its cause is just, so if obligations depended on the justice of the cause no party would accept them. The consequence is that a war crime is a war crime whoever commits it, and neither &#x27;they started it&#x27; nor &#x27;our cause is just&#x27; is a defence.</p></details></div><div class=\"study-sa\"><p class=\"sa-q\">Identify who is bound by international humanitarian law.</p><details class=\"sa-a\"><summary>Model answer</summary><p>All 196 states are party to the Geneva Conventions, which is universal ratification. States that have not ratified Additional Protocol I remain bound by those of its rules that reflect customary law. Non-state armed groups are bound by common Article 3 and by the customary law of non-international armed conflict, and by Additional Protocol II where its threshold is met, on the usual view because a state&#x27;s ratification binds all persons and entities in its territory. Individuals bear personal criminal responsibility for serious violations regardless of rank or office. Multinational forces, including United Nations forces, are also bound, and the Secretary-General&#x27;s 1999 bulletin sets out the principles applicable to them.</p></details></div><h4>Class Activity</h4><ol><li>Take one current armed conflict reported in the news. Identify the parties, and for each state whether it is bound by treaty, by custom, or both, and why.</li><li>Write two paragraphs explaining to someone with no legal training why the law treats an aggressor and a defender identically once fighting begins.</li></ol><h4>Assignment</h4><p>Write one and a half pages introducing international humanitarian law. Define it covering both halves, explain the Geneva and Hague branches, set out the separation of jus ad bellum and jus in bello with a worked example and the reason for the separation, describe the sources noting the role of custom, identify who is bound including non-state groups and individuals, and explain when the law applies and why reciprocity is not required.</p><p class=\"study-print\"><strong>Printable:</strong> use your browser\u2019s Print \u2192 Save as PDF to keep an offline copy.</p></div>",
  "The History and Development of IHL": "<div class=\"study-note\"><div class=\"revision-banner\"><strong>Human Rights and International Humanitarian Law</strong><span>Module 7: Introduction to International Humanitarian Law</span></div><h3 class=\"lesson-note-title\">LESSON NOTE: THE HISTORY AND DEVELOPMENT OF IHL</h3><div class=\"table-wrap\"><table class=\"study-table\"><thead><tr><th>Subject</th><th>Topic</th><th>Level</th><th>Lesson duration</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Human Rights and International Humanitarian Law</td><td>The History and Development of IHL</td><td>Beginner to Advanced</td><td>40 minutes</td></tr></tbody></table></div><h4>Learning objectives</h4><p>By the end of this lesson you should be able to:</p><ul><li>Trace the development of IHL from customary restraints to modern codification</li><li>Explain the significance of Solferino and the founding of the Red Cross</li><li>Describe the main codification milestones</li><li>Explain what drove each expansion of the law</li></ul><h4>1. Introduction</h4><p>Restraints on warfare are far older than the treaties. What is modern is the idea that those restraints should be written down, agreed between states, and enforced. This lesson traces how that happened, and the pattern that emerges is consistent: the law expands after a catastrophe exposes what the previous version failed to cover.</p><h4>2. Pre-modern restraints</h4><ul><li>Customary limits on warfare appear in most civilisations. The Hindu Laws of Manu prohibited certain weapons and the killing of a disarmed enemy. Chinese military writing counselled restraint towards prisoners.</li><li>Islamic law developed detailed rules through the caliph Abu Bakr's instructions and later jurists: no killing of women, children, the aged or those in religious retreat, no destruction of crops or livestock beyond necessity, and humane treatment of captives.</li><li>In West Africa, the Charter of Mande and associated traditions restrained the treatment of captives and non-combatants.</li><li>Medieval European chivalry and the Peace and Truce of God movements restrained warfare among Christians, though the restraints did not extend beyond them.</li><li>The common feature is that these restraints were customary, religious or professional rather than legal in the modern sense, and they generally bound only those within the relevant community.</li></ul><h4>3. The nineteenth century turn</h4><div class=\"table-wrap\"><table class=\"study-table\"><thead><tr><th>Year</th><th>Event</th><th>Significance</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>1859</td><td>The Battle of Solferino</td><td>Henry Dunant, a Swiss businessman, witnessed around forty thousand dead and wounded left on the field with almost no medical care</td></tr><tr><td>1862</td><td>A Memory of Solferino</td><td>Dunant's book proposed national relief societies of trained volunteers, and an international agreement protecting them</td></tr><tr><td>1863</td><td>Founding of the International Committee</td><td>The Geneva Committee, later the ICRC, was established to pursue both proposals</td></tr><tr><td>1863</td><td>The Lieber Code</td><td>Instructions for the Government of Armies of the United States in the Field, drafted by Francis Lieber, the first modern codification of the law of war in a single document</td></tr><tr><td>1864</td><td>First Geneva Convention</td><td>Amelioration of the condition of the wounded in armies in the field; twelve states; the first multilateral humanitarian treaty</td></tr><tr><td>1868</td><td>St Petersburg Declaration</td><td>Prohibited explosive projectiles under 400 grammes and stated the foundational principle that the only legitimate object of war is to weaken the military forces of the enemy</td></tr><tr><td>1899, 1907</td><td>Hague Peace Conferences</td><td>Codified the conduct of hostilities: the Hague Regulations on land warfare, restrictions on weapons, and the Martens Clause</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"study-callout\"><strong>Note:</strong> The St Petersburg Declaration's statement of purpose is the origin of two principles taught later in this course. That the only legitimate object is to weaken enemy military forces is the origin of distinction. That this object would be exceeded by employing arms which uselessly aggravate the suffering of disabled men is the origin of the prohibition of unnecessary suffering.</div><h4>4. The twentieth century expansions</h4><ol><li><strong>After the First World War.</strong> The 1925 Geneva Protocol prohibited the use of asphyxiating and poisonous gases and bacteriological methods, responding directly to gas warfare. The 1929 Geneva Conventions improved the treatment of the wounded and, for the first time, dealt comprehensively with prisoners of war.</li><li><strong>After the Second World War.</strong> The four Geneva Conventions of 1949 were the largest single expansion. The Fourth Convention on the protection of civilians was entirely new and responded to the fact that the previous law had almost nothing to say about the treatment of civilians in occupied territory. Common Article 3 extended minimum protections to non-international armed conflict for the first time.</li><li><strong>After the wars of decolonisation and Vietnam.</strong> The 1977 Additional Protocols responded to guerrilla warfare, aerial bombardment of cities and national liberation movements. Protocol I codified the conduct of hostilities for international conflict and brought wars of national liberation within it; Protocol II developed the law of non-international armed conflict beyond common Article 3.</li><li><strong>After the conflicts of the 1990s.</strong> The ad hoc tribunals for the former Yugoslavia and Rwanda transformed enforcement, established that serious violations in non-international conflict entail individual criminal responsibility, and produced the jurisprudence on which the Rome Statute of 1998 was built.</li></ol><h4>5. Weapons treaties</h4><div class=\"table-wrap\"><table class=\"study-table\"><thead><tr><th>Treaty</th><th>Year</th><th>Effect</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Geneva Gas Protocol</td><td>1925</td><td>Prohibited use of chemical and bacteriological weapons</td></tr><tr><td>Biological Weapons Convention</td><td>1972</td><td>Prohibited development, production and stockpiling</td></tr><tr><td>Convention on Certain Conventional Weapons</td><td>1980</td><td>Framework with protocols on fragments undetectable by X-ray, mines and booby-traps, incendiary weapons, blinding laser weapons, and explosive remnants of war</td></tr><tr><td>Chemical Weapons Convention</td><td>1993</td><td>Comprehensive prohibition with a verification organisation</td></tr><tr><td>Ottawa Convention</td><td>1997</td><td>Banned anti-personnel mines</td></tr><tr><td>Convention on Cluster Munitions</td><td>2008</td><td>Banned cluster munitions</td></tr><tr><td>Arms Trade Treaty</td><td>2013</td><td>Regulated transfers, including a prohibition where the arms would be used for war crimes</td></tr><tr><td>Treaty on the Prohibition of Nuclear Weapons</td><td>2017</td><td>Prohibited nuclear weapons for its parties; nuclear-armed states have not joined</td></tr></tbody></table></div><p>The Ottawa and Oslo processes are notable for their method. Both were driven by coalitions of smaller states working with non-governmental organisations, negotiated outside the consensus-bound disarmament machinery, and produced treaties in a fraction of the usual time. That model has since been used for the nuclear weapons treaty.</p><h4>6. The pattern</h4><p>Four observations hold across the whole history and are worth stating in any answer on development.</p><ol><li><strong>The law is reactive.</strong> Each major instrument responds to the failures of the preceding conflict rather than anticipating the next one.</li><li><strong>Expansion runs from combatants to civilians.</strong> The 1864 Convention protected wounded soldiers. By 1949 the law protected civilians in occupied territory. By 1977 it regulated attacks affecting the civilian population generally.</li><li><strong>Expansion runs from international to internal conflict.</strong> Nothing in 1864, a single article in 1949, a protocol in 1977, and by the 1990s a customary regime covering most of the same ground.</li><li><strong>Enforcement lagged codification by a century.</strong> The rules were largely settled by 1949; the institutions capable of trying those who break them arrived in the 1990s.</li></ol><h4>7. How the examiner&#x27;s question should be answered</h4><p>Do not write a chronology. Organise the history around the pattern: pre-modern customary restraint, the nineteenth century turn to codification with Solferino as the pivot, the twentieth century expansions each traced to the conflict that prompted it, and the enforcement turn of the 1990s. Give dates and instrument names precisely, because that is where the descriptive marks are, but spend the analysis on why each expansion happened. Mention non-European antecedents; an answer that begins in Geneva in 1864 is incomplete.</p><h4>Summary</h4><ul><li>Customary, religious and professional restraints on warfare long predate codification and exist across civilisations.</li><li>Solferino in 1859 prompted Dunant&#x27;s proposals, leading to the ICRC in 1863 and the first Geneva Convention in 1864.</li><li>The Lieber Code of 1863 was the first modern codification; the St Petersburg Declaration of 1868 stated the founding principles.</li><li>The Hague Conferences codified the conduct of hostilities; the 1949 Conventions added comprehensive civilian protection and common Article 3.</li><li>The 1977 Additional Protocols responded to guerrilla warfare and aerial bombardment; the 1990s tribunals transformed enforcement.</li><li>The law is reactive, expands from combatants to civilians and from international to internal conflict, and enforcement lagged codification by a century.</li></ul><h4>Key Terms</h4><div class=\"table-wrap\"><table class=\"study-table\"><thead><tr><th>Term</th><th>Meaning</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Solferino</td><td>The 1859 battle whose aftermath prompted Dunant&#x27;s proposals and the founding of the Red Cross movement.</td></tr><tr><td>Lieber Code</td><td>The 1863 United States instructions constituting the first modern codification of the law of war.</td></tr><tr><td>St Petersburg Declaration</td><td>The 1868 instrument stating that the only legitimate object of war is to weaken enemy military forces.</td></tr><tr><td>Ottawa process</td><td>The method of negotiating a weapons ban outside consensus disarmament machinery, used for landmines in 1997.</td></tr></tbody></table></div><h4>Review Questions</h4><h5>Multiple choice</h5><div class=\"study-mcq\"><p class=\"mcq-q\">What did Henry Dunant propose after witnessing Solferino?</p><ol type=\"A\"><li>National relief societies of trained volunteers and an international agreement protecting them</li><li>A ban on artillery</li><li>An international court for war crimes</li><li>Compulsory arbitration between states</li></ol><details class=\"mcq-a\"><summary>Show answer</summary><p><strong>Answer: A.</strong> Both proposals were realised: the Geneva Committee in 1863 and the first Geneva Convention in 1864.</p></details></div><div class=\"study-mcq\"><p class=\"mcq-q\">Which principle originates in the St Petersburg Declaration?</p><ol type=\"A\"><li>That the only legitimate object of war is to weaken the enemy&#x27;s military forces</li><li>The best interests of the child</li><li>Proportionality in self-defence</li><li>Universal jurisdiction</li></ol><details class=\"mcq-a\"><summary>Show answer</summary><p><strong>Answer: A.</strong> Its statement that this object would be exceeded by arms uselessly aggravating suffering is also the origin of the prohibition of unnecessary suffering.</p></details></div><div class=\"study-mcq\"><p class=\"mcq-q\">What was new about the Fourth Geneva Convention of 1949?</p><ol type=\"A\"><li>It provided comprehensive protection for civilians, particularly in occupied territory</li><li>It created the ICRC</li><li>It prohibited chemical weapons</li><li>It established the International Criminal Court</li></ol><details class=\"mcq-a\"><summary>Show answer</summary><p><strong>Answer: A.</strong> Common Article 3, extending minimum protections to non-international conflict, was the other major innovation of 1949.</p></details></div><div class=\"study-mcq\"><p class=\"mcq-q\">What prompted the 1977 Additional Protocols?</p><ol type=\"A\"><li>Guerrilla warfare, aerial bombardment of cities and wars of national liberation</li><li>The invention of nuclear weapons</li><li>The founding of the United Nations</li><li>The Rwandan genocide</li></ol><details class=\"mcq-a\"><summary>Show answer</summary><p><strong>Answer: A.</strong> Protocol I codified the conduct of hostilities for international conflict while Protocol II developed the law of non-international armed conflict.</p></details></div><h5>Short answer</h5><div class=\"study-sa\"><p class=\"sa-q\">Explain the pattern by which international humanitarian law has developed.</p><details class=\"sa-a\"><summary>Model answer</summary><p>The law has been reactive: each major instrument responds to the failures exposed by the preceding conflict rather than anticipating the next. Its protective scope has expanded from combatants to civilians, beginning with wounded soldiers in 1864, extending to prisoners of war in 1929, to civilians in occupied territory in 1949, and to the civilian population generally in 1977. It has also expanded from international to internal conflict, from nothing in 1864 to a single article in 1949, a full protocol in 1977, and a substantial customary regime by the 1990s. Finally, enforcement lagged codification by roughly a century: the rules were largely settled in 1949 but the institutions capable of trying those who break them arrived only with the ad hoc tribunals of the 1990s.</p></details></div><div class=\"study-sa\"><p class=\"sa-q\">Give three examples of pre-modern restraints on warfare from different traditions.</p><details class=\"sa-a\"><summary>Model answer</summary><p>Hindu law, in the Laws of Manu, prohibited certain classes of weapon and forbade killing an enemy who had been disarmed or who had surrendered. Islamic law, from the instructions attributed to the caliph Abu Bakr and developed by later jurists, forbade the killing of women, children, the aged and those in religious retreat, prohibited destruction of crops and livestock beyond necessity, and required humane treatment of captives. In West Africa, the Charter of Mande and associated traditions restrained the treatment of captives and non-combatants. Medieval European chivalry and the Peace and Truce of God movements provide a fourth example, though like the others their restraints generally bound only members of the relevant community.</p></details></div><h4>Class Activity</h4><ol><li>Draw a timeline of IHL from 1859 to the present, marking each instrument and, beneath it, the conflict or event that prompted it.</li><li>Research the Ottawa process that produced the landmine ban. Write one page on why negotiating outside the consensus disarmament machinery succeeded where earlier attempts had failed.</li></ol><h4>Assignment</h4><p>Write one and a half pages on the history and development of international humanitarian law. Describe pre-modern restraints across at least two traditions, explain the significance of Solferino and the nineteenth century turn to codification, trace the twentieth century expansions linking each to the conflict that prompted it, summarise the main weapons treaties, and identify the four patterns that characterise the field&#x27;s development.</p><p class=\"study-print\"><strong>Printable:</strong> use your browser\u2019s Print \u2192 Save as PDF to keep an offline copy.</p></div>",
  "The Geneva Conventions of 1949": "<div class=\"study-note\"><div class=\"revision-banner\"><strong>Human Rights and International Humanitarian Law</strong><span>Module 7: Introduction to International Humanitarian Law</span></div><h3 class=\"lesson-note-title\">LESSON NOTE: THE GENEVA CONVENTIONS OF 1949</h3><div class=\"table-wrap\"><table class=\"study-table\"><thead><tr><th>Subject</th><th>Topic</th><th>Level</th><th>Lesson duration</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Human Rights and International Humanitarian Law</td><td>The Geneva Conventions of 1949</td><td>Beginner to Advanced</td><td>45 minutes</td></tr></tbody></table></div><h4>Learning objectives</h4><p>By the end of this lesson you should be able to:</p><ul><li>Identify the subject matter of each of the four Conventions</li><li>Explain the significance of universal ratification</li><li>Set out the content and importance of common Article 3</li><li>Describe the grave breaches regime and common Article 1</li></ul><h4>1. Introduction</h4><p>The four Geneva Conventions of 1949 are the foundation of modern international humanitarian law. They are the only treaties in existence ratified by every state in the world. Two provisions common to all four, Article 1 and Article 3, carry disproportionate weight and are the parts most often examined.</p><h4>2. The four Conventions</h4><div class=\"table-wrap\"><table class=\"study-table\"><thead><tr><th>Convention</th><th>Full subject</th><th>Articles</th><th>Protects</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>First</td><td>Amelioration of the condition of the wounded and sick in armed forces in the field</td><td>64</td><td>Wounded and sick combatants on land, medical personnel, units and transports</td></tr><tr><td>Second</td><td>Amelioration of the condition of wounded, sick and shipwrecked members of armed forces at sea</td><td>63</td><td>The same categories at sea, plus hospital ships</td></tr><tr><td>Third</td><td>Treatment of prisoners of war</td><td>143</td><td>Prisoners of war, from capture to final release and repatriation</td></tr><tr><td>Fourth</td><td>Protection of civilian persons in time of war</td><td>159</td><td>Civilians in the hands of a party of which they are not nationals, and the population of occupied territory</td></tr></tbody></table></div><p>The First and Second update earlier conventions of 1864, 1906 and 1929. The Third substantially expands the 1929 prisoners of war convention. The Fourth was entirely new in 1949, responding to the treatment of civilians in occupied Europe, which the pre-war law had left almost wholly unregulated.</p><h4>3. Universal ratification and its meaning</h4><ul><li>All 196 states are party to all four Conventions. No other treaty regime has achieved this.</li><li>The practical consequence is that in any international armed conflict, both parties are bound by treaty and no state can argue it is outside the regime.</li><li>The International Court of Justice stated in the Nicaragua case that common Article 3 reflects elementary considerations of humanity and constitutes a minimum yardstick applicable in all armed conflicts.</li><li>Universality also supports the customary status of the Conventions' core rules, which matters for the Additional Protocols, whose ratification is high but not universal.</li></ul><h4>4. Common articles</h4><div class=\"table-wrap\"><table class=\"study-table\"><thead><tr><th>Article</th><th>Content</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>1</td><td>The parties undertake to respect and to ensure respect for the Conventions in all circumstances</td></tr><tr><td>2</td><td>Application to all cases of declared war or of any other armed conflict between two or more parties, even if the state of war is not recognised, and to all cases of partial or total occupation even if it meets with no armed resistance</td></tr><tr><td>3</td><td>Minimum protections in armed conflict not of an international character</td></tr></tbody></table></div><p>Common Article 1 is more significant than its brevity suggests. 'To ensure respect' has been read as imposing an obligation not only to comply oneself but to use influence to bring other parties into compliance. The International Court of Justice relied on this reading in the Nicaragua case and in the Wall advisory opinion. Its practical implications, particularly for arms transfers and support to parties to a conflict, are actively litigated and debated.</p><h4>5. Common Article 3 in detail</h4><p>Common Article 3 is often called a convention in miniature. It applies in armed conflict not of an international character occurring in the territory of a party, and binds each party to the conflict, including non-state armed groups.</p><p>It requires that persons taking no active part in the hostilities, including members of armed forces who have laid down their arms and those placed hors de combat by sickness, wounds, detention or any other cause, be in all circumstances treated humanely, without any adverse distinction founded on race, colour, religion or faith, sex, birth or wealth, or any other similar criteria.</p><p>It prohibits, at any time and in any place whatsoever, in respect of those persons:</p><ol><li>Violence to life and person, in particular murder of all kinds, mutilation, cruel treatment and torture.</li><li>Taking of hostages.</li><li>Outrages upon personal dignity, in particular humiliating and degrading treatment.</li><li>The passing of sentences and the carrying out of executions without previous judgment pronounced by a regularly constituted court affording all the judicial guarantees which are recognised as indispensable by civilised peoples.</li></ol><p>It further requires that the wounded and sick be collected and cared for, permits an impartial humanitarian body such as the ICRC to offer its services, encourages the parties to bring the other provisions of the Conventions into force by special agreement, and provides that the application of the article does not affect the legal status of the parties to the conflict.</p><div class=\"study-callout\"><strong>Note:</strong> That last provision was essential to adoption. Governments feared that applying the law to a rebellion would confer legitimacy on the rebels. Article 3 states expressly that it does not. Any answer on why states accepted Article 3 must mention this clause.</div><h4>6. Grave breaches</h4><div class=\"study-defn\"><strong>Grave breach:</strong> A serious violation of the Geneva Conventions, listed in each Convention, in respect of which states parties are obliged to search for and either prosecute or extradite the alleged offender, irrespective of nationality.</div><div class=\"table-wrap\"><table class=\"study-table\"><thead><tr><th>Convention</th><th>Grave breaches include</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>All four</td><td>Wilful killing; torture or inhuman treatment, including biological experiments; wilfully causing great suffering or serious injury to body or health</td></tr><tr><td>First and Second</td><td>Extensive destruction and appropriation of property not justified by military necessity and carried out unlawfully and wantonly</td></tr><tr><td>Third</td><td>Compelling a prisoner of war to serve in the forces of the hostile power; wilfully depriving a prisoner of war of the rights of fair and regular trial</td></tr><tr><td>Fourth</td><td>Unlawful deportation or transfer; unlawful confinement; taking of hostages; compelling a protected person to serve in the forces of a hostile power; depriving a protected person of a fair and regular trial</td></tr></tbody></table></div><p>Additional Protocol I extends the list, adding among others attacks on the civilian population, indiscriminate attacks affecting civilians in the knowledge that excessive incidental harm will result, attacks on works containing dangerous forces, and the transfer by an occupying power of parts of its own civilian population into occupied territory.</p><div class=\"study-callout\"><strong>Note:</strong> The grave breaches regime applies only in international armed conflict. Serious violations of common Article 3 are nonetheless war crimes: the ICTY held in Tadic that customary international law imposes individual criminal responsibility for serious violations committed in internal conflict, and the Rome Statute Article 8(2)(c) codifies this. So the grave breaches label is narrower than the category of war crime.</div><h4>7. Implementation obligations</h4><ul><li>Dissemination: states must disseminate the Conventions as widely as possible, including in military instruction and, if possible, civil instruction.</li><li>Legislation: states must enact legislation providing effective penal sanctions for grave breaches.</li><li>Legal advisers: Additional Protocol I requires legal advisers to be available to advise military commanders.</li><li>Protecting Powers: the Conventions provide for neutral states to safeguard the interests of the parties, a system that has scarcely been used in practice, with the ICRC acting as a substitute.</li><li>The enquiry procedure and the International Humanitarian Fact-Finding Commission established under Additional Protocol I Article 90, which remains almost entirely unused.</li></ul><h4>8. How the examiner&#x27;s question should be answered</h4><p>Name the four Conventions accurately, since giving the subject matter of each is the basic descriptive requirement. State universal ratification and what follows from it. Then spend most of the answer on common Article 3: its threshold, who it binds, its four prohibitions, the humane treatment obligation, the ICRC's right of initiative, and the legal status clause. Then grave breaches, being careful to say that the regime is confined to international armed conflict while serious violations in internal conflict are still war crimes under custom and the Rome Statute. Finish with common Article 1, since the duty to ensure respect is where much current argument lies.</p><h4>Summary</h4><ul><li>The four Conventions of 1949 cover the wounded and sick on land, at sea, prisoners of war, and civilians, and are universally ratified.</li><li>The Fourth Convention was new in 1949 and responded to the unregulated treatment of civilians in occupied territory.</li><li>Common Article 1 requires parties to respect and to ensure respect for the Conventions in all circumstances.</li><li>Common Article 3 sets minimum protections in non-international conflict, binds non-state groups, and states expressly that it does not affect the legal status of the parties.</li><li>Grave breaches trigger a duty to prosecute or extradite, but the regime applies only in international armed conflict.</li><li>Serious violations of common Article 3 are nonetheless war crimes under customary law and the Rome Statute.</li></ul><h4>Key Terms</h4><div class=\"table-wrap\"><table class=\"study-table\"><thead><tr><th>Term</th><th>Meaning</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Common Article 3</td><td>The provision in all four Conventions setting minimum humane treatment standards in non-international armed conflict.</td></tr><tr><td>Grave breach</td><td>A listed serious violation triggering an obligation to prosecute or extradite regardless of nationality.</td></tr><tr><td>Protecting Power</td><td>A neutral state designated to safeguard the interests of a party to a conflict.</td></tr><tr><td>Duty to ensure respect</td><td>The obligation under common Article 1 to use influence to bring other parties into compliance.</td></tr><tr><td>Hors de combat</td><td>A person who is in the power of an adverse party, or who has surrendered, or who is incapacitated by wounds or sickness.</td></tr></tbody></table></div><h4>Review Questions</h4><h5>Multiple choice</h5><div class=\"study-mcq\"><p class=\"mcq-q\">Which Geneva Convention was entirely new in 1949?</p><ol type=\"A\"><li>The Fourth, on the protection of civilian persons in time of war</li><li>The First, on the wounded and sick in the field</li><li>The Second, on shipwrecked members of armed forces</li><li>The Third, on prisoners of war</li></ol><details class=\"mcq-a\"><summary>Show answer</summary><p><strong>Answer: A.</strong> It responded to the treatment of civilians in occupied Europe, which the pre-war law had left almost wholly unregulated.</p></details></div><div class=\"study-mcq\"><p class=\"mcq-q\">Why does common Article 3 state that its application does not affect the legal status of the parties?</p><ol type=\"A\"><li>Because governments feared that applying the law to a rebellion would confer legitimacy on the rebels</li><li>To exclude non-state groups from its scope</li><li>To limit it to declared wars</li><li>To reserve jurisdiction to national courts</li></ol><details class=\"mcq-a\"><summary>Show answer</summary><p><strong>Answer: A.</strong> Without that clause states would not have accepted any regulation of internal conflict.</p></details></div><div class=\"study-mcq\"><p class=\"mcq-q\">Does the grave breaches regime apply in non-international armed conflict?</p><ol type=\"A\"><li>No, but serious violations of common Article 3 are still war crimes under custom and the Rome Statute</li><li>Yes, in full</li><li>Yes, but only for wilful killing</li><li>No, and such violations are not crimes</li></ol><details class=\"mcq-a\"><summary>Show answer</summary><p><strong>Answer: A.</strong> The ICTY held in Tadic that customary law imposes individual criminal responsibility for serious violations in internal conflict.</p></details></div><div class=\"study-mcq\"><p class=\"mcq-q\">What does the duty to &#x27;ensure respect&#x27; in common Article 1 require?</p><ol type=\"A\"><li>Using influence to bring other parties into compliance, not merely complying oneself</li><li>Only self-compliance</li><li>Reporting violations to the Security Council</li><li>Ratifying the Additional Protocols</li></ol><details class=\"mcq-a\"><summary>Show answer</summary><p><strong>Answer: A.</strong> The International Court of Justice relied on this reading in the Nicaragua case and the Wall advisory opinion.</p></details></div><h5>Short answer</h5><div class=\"study-sa\"><p class=\"sa-q\">Set out the protections in common Article 3.</p><details class=\"sa-a\"><summary>Model answer</summary><p>Persons taking no active part in hostilities, including members of armed forces who have laid down their arms and those placed hors de combat by sickness, wounds, detention or any other cause, must in all circumstances be treated humanely, without adverse distinction founded on race, colour, religion or faith, sex, birth, wealth or any similar criteria. Four things are prohibited at any time and in any place: violence to life and person, in particular murder, mutilation, cruel treatment and torture; taking of hostages; outrages upon personal dignity, in particular humiliating and degrading treatment; and sentences or executions without previous judgment by a regularly constituted court affording all indispensable judicial guarantees. The wounded and sick must be collected and cared for, an impartial humanitarian body such as the ICRC may offer its services, and the article&#x27;s application does not affect the legal status of the parties.</p></details></div><div class=\"study-sa\"><p class=\"sa-q\">Explain the grave breaches regime and its limits.</p><details class=\"sa-a\"><summary>Model answer</summary><p>Each of the four Conventions lists grave breaches, including wilful killing, torture or inhuman treatment, and wilfully causing great suffering, with further breaches specific to each Convention such as unlawful deportation or denial of a fair trial to protected persons. In respect of a grave breach, every state party is obliged to search for the alleged offender and either bring them before its own courts or hand them over to another party for trial, regardless of the offender&#x27;s nationality or where the act occurred. Additional Protocol I extends the list. The limit is that the regime applies only in international armed conflict; in non-international conflict there are no grave breaches as such, although serious violations of common Article 3 constitute war crimes under customary law and under Article 8(2)(c) of the Rome Statute.</p></details></div><h4>Class Activity</h4><ol><li>Read common Article 3 in full and rewrite it in plain language for a training session with community leaders. Keep every obligation but remove the legal vocabulary.</li><li>Take one reported incident from a current internal conflict. Identify which of the four Article 3 prohibitions it engages and what evidence would be needed to establish it.</li></ol><h4>Assignment</h4><p>Write two pages on the Geneva Conventions of 1949. Identify the subject matter of each Convention and explain what was new in 1949, state the significance of universal ratification, set out common Article 3 in full and explain why the legal status clause was necessary, describe the grave breaches regime and its limits, explain the duty to ensure respect under common Article 1, and list the implementation obligations the Conventions impose.</p><p class=\"study-print\"><strong>Printable:</strong> use your browser\u2019s Print \u2192 Save as PDF to keep an offline copy.</p></div>",
  "The Additional Protocols": "<div class=\"study-note\"><div class=\"revision-banner\"><strong>Human Rights and International Humanitarian Law</strong><span>Module 7: Introduction to International Humanitarian Law</span></div><h3 class=\"lesson-note-title\">LESSON NOTE: THE ADDITIONAL PROTOCOLS</h3><div class=\"table-wrap\"><table class=\"study-table\"><thead><tr><th>Subject</th><th>Topic</th><th>Level</th><th>Lesson duration</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Human Rights and International Humanitarian Law</td><td>The Additional Protocols</td><td>Beginner to Advanced</td><td>40 minutes</td></tr></tbody></table></div><h4>Learning objectives</h4><p>By the end of this lesson you should be able to:</p><ul><li>Describe the content and scope of Additional Protocols I, II and III</li><li>Explain the Protocol II threshold and how it differs from common Article 3</li><li>Identify the principal innovations of Protocol I</li><li>Explain why ratification is not universal</li></ul><h4>1. Introduction</h4><p>The 1949 Conventions protect people in the hands of an enemy. They say very little about how attacks may be conducted. The 1977 Additional Protocols filled that gap and extended the law of internal conflict beyond the single article of 1949. They are the most important IHL instruments after the Conventions themselves.</p><h4>2. Why the Protocols were needed</h4><ol><li><strong>Conduct of hostilities was largely unregulated by treaty.</strong> The Hague Regulations of 1907 were outdated and did not address aerial bombardment, which had become the dominant means of attacking cities.</li><li><strong>Common Article 3 was too thin.</strong> A single article could not govern the conduct of the substantial internal conflicts of the 1960s and 1970s.</li><li><strong>Guerrilla warfare did not fit the combatant rules.</strong> Fighters who did not wear uniforms and did not carry arms openly at all times fell outside the criteria for prisoner of war status.</li><li><strong>Wars of national liberation.</strong> Newly independent states argued these were international rather than internal conflicts, and pressed for their inclusion.</li><li><strong>Civilian casualties had become the dominant feature of warfare</strong>, and the law contained no explicit rule of proportionality in attack.</li></ol><h4>3. Additional Protocol I</h4><div class=\"table-wrap\"><table class=\"study-table\"><thead><tr><th>Part</th><th>Content</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>I</td><td>General provisions, including Article 1(4) extending the Protocol to wars against colonial domination, alien occupation and racist regimes</td></tr><tr><td>II</td><td>Wounded, sick and shipwrecked; medical transportation</td></tr><tr><td>III</td><td>Methods and means of warfare; combatant and prisoner of war status; the prohibition of perfidy</td></tr><tr><td>IV</td><td>Civilian population: the general protection against effects of hostilities, precautions, civil defence, relief</td></tr><tr><td>V</td><td>Execution of the Conventions and Protocol, including grave breaches and the Fact-Finding Commission</td></tr></tbody></table></div><p>Its principal innovations are the following.</p><ol><li><strong>Codification of distinction.</strong> Article 48 states the basic rule that parties shall at all times distinguish between the civilian population and combatants and between civilian objects and military objectives.</li><li><strong>Definition of military objective.</strong> Article 52(2) limits attacks to objects which by their nature, location, purpose or use make an effective contribution to military action and whose destruction offers a definite military advantage.</li><li><strong>Prohibition of indiscriminate attacks.</strong> Article 51(4) and (5), including area bombardment treating separated military objectives in a populated area as a single objective.</li><li><strong>The proportionality rule.</strong> Article 51(5)(b) prohibits attacks expected to cause incidental civilian harm excessive in relation to the concrete and direct military advantage anticipated.</li><li><strong>Precautions in attack and against the effects of attack.</strong> Articles 57 and 58.</li><li><strong>Relaxed combatant criteria.</strong> Article 44(3) allows a combatant who cannot distinguish themselves to retain status by carrying arms openly during each military engagement and while visible to the adversary preceding the launch of an attack.</li><li><strong>Protection of the natural environment.</strong> Articles 35(3) and 55.</li><li><strong>Prohibition of starvation of civilians as a method of warfare.</strong> Article 54.</li><li><strong>Legal advisers and dissemination obligations.</strong> Articles 82 and 83.</li></ol><h4>4. Additional Protocol II</h4><div class=\"table-wrap\"><table class=\"study-table\"><thead><tr><th>Feature</th><th>Detail</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Applies to</td><td>Non-international armed conflicts on the territory of a party between its armed forces and dissident armed forces or other organised armed groups</td></tr><tr><td>Threshold</td><td>The group must be under responsible command and exercise such control over a part of the territory as to enable them to carry out sustained and concerted military operations and to implement the Protocol</td></tr><tr><td>Excluded</td><td>Situations of internal disturbances and tensions, such as riots, isolated and sporadic acts of violence and other acts of a similar nature</td></tr><tr><td>Length</td><td>28 articles, of which 18 are substantive</td></tr><tr><td>Relationship to Article 3</td><td>Develops and supplements common Article 3 without modifying its existing conditions of application</td></tr></tbody></table></div><p>Its substantive content covers humane treatment and fundamental guarantees, protection of persons deprived of liberty, penal prosecutions with detailed fair trial guarantees, protection of the wounded, sick and shipwrecked and of medical and religious personnel, protection of the civilian population including the prohibition of starvation and of forced displacement, and protection of cultural objects and places of worship.</p><div class=\"study-callout\"><strong>Note:</strong> The threshold is the examinable point. Protocol II applies to fewer conflicts than common Article 3, because it requires territorial control sufficient to conduct sustained operations and to implement the Protocol. It also does not apply to conflicts between two non-state armed groups, since it requires the state's armed forces to be a party. Common Article 3 has neither restriction, which is why it remains the baseline.</div><h4>5. Additional Protocol III</h4><ul><li>Adopted in 2005, it created an additional distinctive emblem, the red crystal, alongside the red cross and red crescent.</li><li>It has the same status and protection as the existing emblems.</li><li>The purpose was to provide an emblem free of any religious or political connotation, resolving a long-standing dispute that had prevented some national societies from being recognised.</li><li>It also allows the incorporation of another emblem within the crystal for indicative use in a state's own territory.</li></ul><h4>6. Ratification and the reasons for non-participation</h4><div class=\"table-wrap\"><table class=\"study-table\"><thead><tr><th>Instrument</th><th>Approximate parties</th><th>Notes</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Geneva Conventions</td><td>196</td><td>Universal</td></tr><tr><td>Additional Protocol I</td><td>174</td><td>Several militarily significant states are not party</td></tr><tr><td>Additional Protocol II</td><td>169</td><td></td></tr><tr><td>Additional Protocol III</td><td>Around 80</td><td></td></tr></tbody></table></div><p>The main objections raised by states that have not ratified Protocol I are the following.</p><ol><li>Article 1(4), which brings wars of national liberation within international armed conflict, was objected to as politicising the law by making its application depend on the character of the cause.</li><li>Article 44(3), relaxing the requirement for combatants to distinguish themselves, was objected to as weakening the protection of civilians by permitting fighters to blend into the population.</li><li>Some provisions on reprisals and on the protection of the environment were regarded as going beyond custom.</li><li>Concerns about the effect of the Protocol on nuclear weapons, addressed by some states through declarations on ratification.</li></ol><div class=\"study-callout\"><strong>Note:</strong> Non-ratification matters less than it appears. The ICRC customary law study concluded that most of Protocol I's core rules, including distinction, the definition of military objective, proportionality and precautions, are customary and therefore bind all states. A state that has not ratified is bound by those rules anyway. Saying this is essential to a complete answer.</div><h4>7. How the examiner&#x27;s question should be answered</h4><p>Take the three Protocols in order with their dates and subject matter. On Protocol I, give the five or six principal innovations rather than describing the whole instrument, and note that they are now largely customary. On Protocol II, the marks are in the threshold: state it precisely, contrast it with common Article 3 on both territorial control and conflicts between non-state groups, and say which applies more widely. On non-ratification, give the actual objections rather than asserting that states simply refused, and then make the customary law point.</p><h4>Summary</h4><ul><li>The Additional Protocols of 1977 regulate the conduct of hostilities and develop the law of non-international armed conflict.</li><li>Protocol I codified distinction, the definition of military objective, the prohibition of indiscriminate attacks, proportionality and precautions.</li><li>Protocol I Article 1(4) extends international armed conflict to wars against colonial domination, alien occupation and racist regimes.</li><li>Protocol II has a higher threshold than common Article 3, requiring territorial control and state armed forces as a party.</li><li>Protocol III of 2005 created the red crystal emblem free of religious or political connotation.</li><li>Most of Protocol I&#x27;s core rules are customary, so non-ratifying states are bound by them regardless.</li></ul><h4>Key Terms</h4><div class=\"table-wrap\"><table class=\"study-table\"><thead><tr><th>Term</th><th>Meaning</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Additional Protocol I</td><td>The 1977 instrument on international armed conflict, codifying the conduct of hostilities.</td></tr><tr><td>Additional Protocol II</td><td>The 1977 instrument developing the law of non-international armed conflict above a defined threshold.</td></tr><tr><td>Red crystal</td><td>The additional emblem created by Additional Protocol III in 2005.</td></tr><tr><td>Article 1(4)</td><td>The provision extending Protocol I to wars against colonial domination, alien occupation and racist regimes.</td></tr></tbody></table></div><h4>Review Questions</h4><h5>Multiple choice</h5><div class=\"study-mcq\"><p class=\"mcq-q\">Which applies to a wider range of conflicts, common Article 3 or Additional Protocol II?</p><ol type=\"A\"><li>Common Article 3, because Protocol II requires territorial control and state armed forces as a party</li><li>Additional Protocol II</li><li>They apply identically</li><li>Neither applies to internal conflict</li></ol><details class=\"mcq-a\"><summary>Show answer</summary><p><strong>Answer: A.</strong> Protocol II also does not cover conflicts between two non-state armed groups, which is why Article 3 remains the baseline.</p></details></div><div class=\"study-mcq\"><p class=\"mcq-q\">What is the effect of Article 52(2) of Additional Protocol I?</p><ol type=\"A\"><li>It defines military objectives by nature, location, purpose or use, and by the definite military advantage from their destruction</li><li>It prohibits all attacks in populated areas</li><li>It defines combatant status</li><li>It creates the Fact-Finding Commission</li></ol><details class=\"mcq-a\"><summary>Show answer</summary><p><strong>Answer: A.</strong> The definition is the operative test for whether an object may lawfully be attacked.</p></details></div><div class=\"study-mcq\"><p class=\"mcq-q\">Why did some states object to Article 44(3) of Protocol I?</p><ol type=\"A\"><li>They argued that relaxing the requirement for combatants to distinguish themselves weakens civilian protection</li><li>It prohibited nuclear weapons</li><li>It abolished prisoner of war status</li><li>It applied only to guerrilla groups</li></ol><details class=\"mcq-a\"><summary>Show answer</summary><p><strong>Answer: A.</strong> The provision allows a combatant who cannot distinguish themselves to retain status by carrying arms openly during engagements and while visible before an attack.</p></details></div><div class=\"study-mcq\"><p class=\"mcq-q\">What follows from the customary status of Protocol I&#x27;s core rules?</p><ol type=\"A\"><li>States that have not ratified are nonetheless bound by distinction, proportionality and precautions</li><li>Non-ratifying states are unregulated</li><li>The Protocol binds only its parties</li><li>Custom applies only in internal conflict</li></ol><details class=\"mcq-a\"><summary>Show answer</summary><p><strong>Answer: A.</strong> The ICRC customary law study concluded that most of these rules bind all states regardless of ratification.</p></details></div><h5>Short answer</h5><div class=\"study-sa\"><p class=\"sa-q\">State the threshold for the application of Additional Protocol II and contrast it with common Article 3.</p><details class=\"sa-a\"><summary>Model answer</summary><p>Protocol II applies to armed conflicts on the territory of a state party between its armed forces and dissident armed forces or other organised armed groups which, under responsible command, exercise such control over part of the territory as to enable them to carry out sustained and concerted military operations and to implement the Protocol. It excludes internal disturbances and tensions such as riots and isolated sporadic violence. Common Article 3 requires neither territorial control nor the participation of state armed forces, so it also covers conflicts between two non-state groups and conflicts where a group holds no territory. Article 3 therefore applies to a wider range of situations and remains the baseline, with Protocol II adding detail where its higher threshold is met.</p></details></div><div class=\"study-sa\"><p class=\"sa-q\">Give five principal innovations of Additional Protocol I.</p><details class=\"sa-a\"><summary>Model answer</summary><p>First, the codification of the principle of distinction in Article 48, requiring parties at all times to distinguish between civilians and combatants and between civilian objects and military objectives. Second, the definition of military objective in Article 52(2) by reference to nature, location, purpose or use and to the definite military advantage offered. Third, the prohibition of indiscriminate attacks in Article 51(4) and (5), including area bombardment. Fourth, the proportionality rule in Article 51(5)(b), prohibiting attacks whose expected incidental civilian harm would be excessive in relation to the concrete and direct military advantage anticipated. Fifth, the obligations of precaution in attack and against the effects of attack in Articles 57 and 58. Others include the prohibition of starvation of civilians as a method of warfare and protection of the natural environment.</p></details></div><h4>Class Activity</h4><ol><li>Compare the fair trial guarantees in Additional Protocol II Article 6 with those in ICCPR Article 14. List the differences and explain why they exist.</li><li>Take a current internal conflict and assess whether it meets the Protocol II threshold. Set out the evidence for each element and state your conclusion.</li></ol><h4>Assignment</h4><p>Write one and a half pages on the Additional Protocols. Explain why they were needed, set out the principal innovations of Protocol I, state the Protocol II threshold and contrast it with common Article 3, describe Protocol III and the purpose of the red crystal, and account for the pattern of ratification while explaining why non-ratification matters less than it appears.</p><p class=\"study-print\"><strong>Printable:</strong> use your browser\u2019s Print \u2192 Save as PDF to keep an offline copy.</p></div>",
  "Classifying Armed Conflicts": "<div class=\"study-note\"><div class=\"revision-banner\"><strong>Human Rights and International Humanitarian Law</strong><span>Module 7: Introduction to International Humanitarian Law</span></div><h3 class=\"lesson-note-title\">LESSON NOTE: CLASSIFYING ARMED CONFLICTS</h3><div class=\"table-wrap\"><table class=\"study-table\"><thead><tr><th>Subject</th><th>Topic</th><th>Level</th><th>Lesson duration</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Human Rights and International Humanitarian Law</td><td>Classifying Armed Conflicts</td><td>Beginner to Advanced</td><td>45 minutes</td></tr></tbody></table></div><h4>Learning objectives</h4><p>By the end of this lesson you should be able to:</p><ul><li>Distinguish international from non-international armed conflict</li><li>State the criteria for each and apply them</li><li>Explain internationalisation and the overall control test</li><li>Explain why classification matters in practice</li></ul><h4>1. Introduction</h4><p>Classification is the first question in every IHL problem, because the applicable rules depend on it. It is also the question most often answered too quickly. The two categories have different thresholds, different tests and different consequences, and situations frequently fall into both at once.</p><h4>2. The two categories</h4><div class=\"table-wrap\"><table class=\"study-table\"><thead><tr><th></th><th>International armed conflict</th><th>Non-international armed conflict</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Parties</td><td>Two or more states</td><td>A state and an organised armed group, or two or more such groups</td></tr><tr><td>Threshold</td><td>Any resort to armed force between states; no intensity requirement</td><td>Intensity of violence and organisation of the parties</td></tr><tr><td>Treaty law</td><td>The four Conventions in full, Additional Protocol I</td><td>Common Article 3, Additional Protocol II if its threshold is met</td></tr><tr><td>Combatant status</td><td>Yes, with prisoner of war status on capture</td><td>No; no combatant immunity and no prisoner of war status</td></tr><tr><td>Occupation law</td><td>Applies</td><td>Does not apply</td></tr><tr><td>Grave breaches</td><td>Apply</td><td>Do not apply, though serious violations are still war crimes</td></tr></tbody></table></div><h4>3. International armed conflict: the criteria</h4><p>Common Article 2 applies to all cases of declared war or of any other armed conflict which may arise between two or more of the parties, even if the state of war is not recognised by one of them, and to all cases of partial or total occupation even if the occupation meets with no armed resistance.</p><ul><li><strong>No intensity threshold.</strong> The ICRC's position, reflected in the updated Commentaries, is that any resort to armed force between states triggers the law, however brief and whatever the number of casualties. The reasoning is protective: a single captured soldier needs the Third Convention immediately.</li><li><strong>No declaration required.</strong> The factual test governs.</li><li><strong>Occupation without resistance counts.</strong> This closed a gap exposed by occupations accepted under duress before 1949.</li><li><strong>Article 1(4) of Protocol I adds wars of national liberation</strong> for its parties, in which peoples fight colonial domination, alien occupation or racist regimes in the exercise of self-determination.</li></ul><div class=\"study-callout\"><strong>Note:</strong> Some states argue for a minimum intensity threshold in international armed conflict, so that a border skirmish would not trigger the whole regime. The ICRC position is the better one for examination purposes: state it, note the contrary view, and give the protective reason.</div><h4>4. Non-international armed conflict: the two criteria</h4><p>The Tadic decision of the ICTY Appeals Chamber in 1995 gave the definition now generally accepted: a non-international armed conflict exists whenever there is protracted armed violence between governmental authorities and organised armed groups or between such groups within a state. Two criteria follow.</p><div class=\"table-wrap\"><table class=\"study-table\"><thead><tr><th>Criterion</th><th>Indicative factors</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Intensity of the violence</td><td>Number, duration and gravity of individual confrontations; type of weapons used; number of persons and type of forces engaged; number of casualties; extent of material destruction; displacement of civilians; whether the state deployed its armed forces rather than police; whether the Security Council has addressed the situation</td></tr><tr><td>Organisation of the armed group</td><td>A command structure; headquarters; the ability to plan and carry out coordinated operations; control over territory; a disciplinary system; the ability to recruit, train and supply; the ability to speak with one voice and negotiate</td></tr></tbody></table></div><p>Neither criterion requires all factors. They are indicators assessed together, and the assessment is factual, not dependent on any party's characterisation.</p><div class=\"study-callout\"><strong>Example:</strong> A government faces demonstrations that turn violent, with looting and clashes with police over several weeks, and dozens of arrests. There is no armed group with a command structure and the police handle the situation. This is internal disturbance, not armed conflict: IHL does not apply and human rights law with its law enforcement standards governs. If a faction then forms with a command structure, acquires weapons, holds a district and fights the army in sustained engagements over months, both criteria are met and common Article 3 applies. Nothing about this depends on the government calling it a rebellion, an insurgency or a criminal matter.</div><h4>5. Internationalisation</h4><p>A conflict that appears internal may be international in whole or part.</p><ol><li><strong>Direct intervention by a foreign state against the territorial state.</strong> That relationship is an international armed conflict, running in parallel with the internal one.</li><li><strong>Intervention supporting the territorial state against a rebel group.</strong> The conflict with the group remains non-international, since both intervening and territorial state are on the same side.</li><li><strong>Foreign control over a non-state group.</strong> If a state controls a group fighting another state, the conflict is international. The test is the contested point.</li><li><strong>Recognition of belligerency</strong>, a historical doctrine now of little practical significance.</li></ol><div class=\"table-wrap\"><table class=\"study-table\"><thead><tr><th>Test</th><th>Source</th><th>Requirement</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Effective control</td><td>ICJ, Nicaragua (1986)</td><td>Control over the specific operations in the course of which the violations occurred</td></tr><tr><td>Overall control</td><td>ICTY Appeals Chamber, Tadic (1999)</td><td>Financing, training, equipping or providing operational support, plus a role in organising, coordinating or planning the group's military actions; specific instructions for each operation are not required</td></tr></tbody></table></div><p>The ICJ in the Genocide case of 2007 maintained effective control as the test for attributing conduct to a state for the purpose of state responsibility, while accepting that overall control may be appropriate for the different question of classifying a conflict. That distinction is the answer to the apparent conflict between the two courts, and stating it is the mark of a careful answer.</p><h4>6. Why classification matters</h4><div class=\"table-wrap\"><table class=\"study-table\"><thead><tr><th>Consequence</th><th>International</th><th>Non-international</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Prisoner of war status</td><td>Yes</td><td>No</td></tr><tr><td>Combatant immunity for lawful acts of war</td><td>Yes</td><td>No; fighters may be prosecuted under domestic law for taking up arms</td></tr><tr><td>Law of occupation</td><td>Applies</td><td>Does not apply</td></tr><tr><td>Grave breaches regime</td><td>Applies</td><td>Does not apply</td></tr><tr><td>Detention authority</td><td>Regulated in detail by the Third and Fourth Conventions</td><td>Sparse treaty regulation; a recognised legal gap</td></tr><tr><td>Applicable treaty rules</td><td>The full Conventions and Protocol I</td><td>Common Article 3, Protocol II if applicable, and custom</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"study-callout\"><strong>Note:</strong> The convergence point matters as much as the differences. The ICRC customary law study found that the great majority of its 161 rules apply in both types of conflict. So while classification still determines status questions, detention authority and the grave breaches regime, it makes much less difference than it once did to the rules governing the conduct of hostilities.</div><h4>7. How the examiner&#x27;s question should be answered</h4><p>Classify first and explicitly, before applying any substantive rule. Identify the parties. For a state-on-state element, note that no intensity threshold applies. For an internal element, apply intensity and organisation with the indicative factors, and reach a conclusion on each. Check for internationalisation and, if a foreign state supports a group, apply overall control while explaining its relationship to effective control. Consider whether more than one conflict is running in parallel, which is common and frequently missed. Only then apply the substantive law, and say which regime you are applying and why.</p><h4>Summary</h4><ul><li>International armed conflict arises from any resort to armed force between states, with no intensity threshold and no need for a declaration.</li><li>Non-international armed conflict requires intensity of violence and organisation of the armed group, assessed on indicative factors.</li><li>Internal disturbances and tensions fall below the threshold and are governed by human rights law, not IHL.</li><li>A conflict may be internationalised by direct foreign intervention against the territorial state or by foreign overall control of a group.</li><li>Effective control governs attribution for state responsibility; overall control governs classification of a conflict.</li><li>Classification determines combatant and prisoner of war status, occupation law and grave breaches, but most conduct-of-hostilities rules now apply in both.</li></ul><h4>Key Terms</h4><div class=\"table-wrap\"><table class=\"study-table\"><thead><tr><th>Term</th><th>Meaning</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>International armed conflict</td><td>Armed conflict between two or more states, including occupation.</td></tr><tr><td>Non-international armed conflict</td><td>Protracted armed violence between a state and an organised armed group, or between such groups.</td></tr><tr><td>Overall control</td><td>The Tadic test for classifying a conflict as international where a state controls a non-state group.</td></tr><tr><td>Internal disturbances and tensions</td><td>Situations of violence below the armed conflict threshold, governed by human rights law.</td></tr><tr><td>Internationalisation</td><td>The transformation of an apparently internal conflict into an international one.</td></tr></tbody></table></div><h4>Review Questions</h4><h5>Multiple choice</h5><div class=\"study-mcq\"><p class=\"mcq-q\">Is there an intensity threshold for international armed conflict?</p><ol type=\"A\"><li>No; on the ICRC view any resort to armed force between states suffices</li><li>Yes, one hundred casualties</li><li>Yes, sustained operations over thirty days</li><li>Only where war is formally declared</li></ol><details class=\"mcq-a\"><summary>Show answer</summary><p><strong>Answer: A.</strong> The protective reason is that a single captured soldier needs the Third Convention immediately.</p></details></div><div class=\"study-mcq\"><p class=\"mcq-q\">What are the two criteria for a non-international armed conflict?</p><ol type=\"A\"><li>Intensity of the violence and organisation of the armed group</li><li>Territorial control and foreign support</li><li>A declaration and Security Council notification</li><li>Casualty numbers and duration alone</li></ol><details class=\"mcq-a\"><summary>Show answer</summary><p><strong>Answer: A.</strong> The Tadic formulation of protracted armed violence between a state and organised groups, or between such groups, is the accepted definition.</p></details></div><div class=\"study-mcq\"><p class=\"mcq-q\">Which test determines whether foreign involvement makes a conflict international?</p><ol type=\"A\"><li>Overall control, from the ICTY Appeals Chamber in Tadic</li><li>Effective control, from the ICJ in Nicaragua</li><li>Strict liability</li><li>The Security Council&#x27;s characterisation</li></ol><details class=\"mcq-a\"><summary>Show answer</summary><p><strong>Answer: A.</strong> The ICJ maintained effective control for attribution in state responsibility while accepting overall control may suit the different question of conflict classification.</p></details></div><div class=\"study-mcq\"><p class=\"mcq-q\">Which consequence does NOT follow from classifying a conflict as non-international?</p><ol type=\"A\"><li>The law of occupation applies</li><li>Common Article 3 applies</li><li>Serious violations are war crimes</li><li>Fighters may be prosecuted under domestic law for taking up arms</li></ol><details class=\"mcq-a\"><summary>Show answer</summary><p><strong>Answer: A.</strong> Occupation law and prisoner of war status apply only in international armed conflict.</p></details></div><h5>Short answer</h5><div class=\"study-sa\"><p class=\"sa-q\">Set out the indicative factors for the two criteria of a non-international armed conflict.</p><details class=\"sa-a\"><summary>Model answer</summary><p>For intensity: the number, duration and gravity of individual confrontations; the types of weapons used; the number of persons and type of forces engaged; the number of casualties; the extent of material destruction; the displacement of civilians; whether the state deployed its regular armed forces rather than relying on police; and whether the Security Council has taken up the situation. For organisation of the group: the existence of a command structure and headquarters; the ability to plan and execute coordinated military operations; control over territory; a disciplinary system enforcing rules on members; the ability to recruit, train, arm and supply; and the ability to speak with one voice and to negotiate. Neither list is cumulative; the factors are weighed together and the assessment is factual.</p></details></div><div class=\"study-sa\"><p class=\"sa-q\">Explain the relationship between the effective control and overall control tests.</p><details class=\"sa-a\"><summary>Model answer</summary><p>The International Court of Justice held in Nicaragua, and reaffirmed in the Genocide case, that conduct of a non-state group is attributable to a state for the purposes of state responsibility only where the state had effective control over the specific operations in which the violations occurred. The ICTY Appeals Chamber in Tadic held that for the different purpose of classifying a conflict, overall control suffices: financing, training, equipping or providing operational support to a group, together with a role in organising, coordinating or planning its military actions, without any need for instructions on each operation. The two tests are not in conflict once the questions are separated: effective control answers whether a state is responsible for particular acts, overall control answers whether a conflict is international.</p></details></div><h4>Class Activity</h4><ol><li>Take three current situations of violence from the news. Classify each, setting out the parties, the applicable criteria and your conclusion, and note where you would need more facts.</li><li>Write a short bench memorandum advising a commander whether captured members of an armed group are entitled to prisoner of war status, explaining what turns on the classification.</li></ol><h4>Assignment</h4><p>Write two pages on the classification of armed conflicts. Distinguish the two categories and their thresholds, state the criteria for each with the indicative factors for intensity and organisation, explain internationalisation and the relationship between the effective control and overall control tests, set out the practical consequences of classification, and note the extent to which customary law has narrowed the difference between the regimes.</p><p class=\"study-print\"><strong>Printable:</strong> use your browser\u2019s Print \u2192 Save as PDF to keep an offline copy.</p></div>",
  "IHL and Human Rights Law Compared": "<div class=\"study-note\"><div class=\"revision-banner\"><strong>Human Rights and International Humanitarian Law</strong><span>Module 7: Introduction to International Humanitarian Law</span></div><h3 class=\"lesson-note-title\">LESSON NOTE: IHL AND HUMAN RIGHTS LAW COMPARED</h3><div class=\"table-wrap\"><table class=\"study-table\"><thead><tr><th>Subject</th><th>Topic</th><th>Level</th><th>Lesson duration</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Human Rights and International Humanitarian Law</td><td>IHL and Human Rights Law Compared</td><td>Beginner to Advanced</td><td>45 minutes</td></tr></tbody></table></div><h4>Learning objectives</h4><p>By the end of this lesson you should be able to:</p><ul><li>Compare the origin, scope and operation of the two bodies of law</li><li>Explain the lex specialis principle and its limits</li><li>Explain the extraterritorial application of human rights law</li><li>Apply both bodies of law to a situation of armed conflict</li></ul><h4>1. Introduction</h4><p>For much of the twentieth century these were treated as separate fields: human rights for peace, humanitarian law for war. That separation collapsed. Both now apply in armed conflict, and the interesting questions are about how they interact rather than which one governs.</p><h4>2. The comparison</h4><div class=\"table-wrap\"><table class=\"study-table\"><thead><tr><th></th><th>International humanitarian law</th><th>International human rights law</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Applies</td><td>Only in armed conflict</td><td>At all times, in peace and in war</td></tr><tr><td>Binds</td><td>States, non-state armed groups, individuals</td><td>Primarily states; non-state actors increasingly, with contested basis</td></tr><tr><td>Beneficiaries</td><td>Protected persons defined by status and situation</td><td>All persons within jurisdiction</td></tr><tr><td>Derogation</td><td>No derogation; the law is designed for emergency</td><td>Derogation permitted for some rights in declared emergency</td></tr><tr><td>Reciprocity</td><td>Expressly not required</td><td>Not required</td></tr><tr><td>Purpose</td><td>Balancing humanity against military necessity</td><td>Protecting the individual against the state</td></tr><tr><td>Enforcement</td><td>Individual criminal responsibility, state responsibility, ICRC, Protecting Powers</td><td>Treaty bodies, regional courts, national courts, political mechanisms</td></tr><tr><td>Individual complaint</td><td>No general mechanism</td><td>Available under many treaties and all regional systems</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"study-callout\"><strong>Note:</strong> The single most important structural difference: IHL accepts that some killing is lawful. A combatant may be attacked at any time, whether or not they pose an immediate threat, and incidental civilian death is lawful if not excessive. Human rights law starts from the position that killing is lawful only where strictly necessary to protect life. Reconciling those two starting points is the whole of the interaction problem.</div><h4>3. Convergence</h4><ol><li>The International Court of Justice held in the Nuclear Weapons advisory opinion of 1996 that the protection of the ICCPR does not cease in time of war, except by derogation under Article 4.</li><li>The Court confirmed and developed this in the Wall advisory opinion of 2004 and in Armed Activities (DRC v Uganda) of 2005, holding that both bodies of law apply and that some matters fall under one, some under the other, and some under both.</li><li>Human rights treaty bodies routinely address situations of armed conflict, and General Comment 36 on the right to life expressly addresses the relationship with IHL.</li><li>Regional courts, particularly the European Court in cases arising from Chechnya, Iraq and Ukraine, have applied human rights law to hostilities.</li><li>IHL treaties themselves borrow human rights language: the fundamental guarantees in Additional Protocol I Article 75 and Protocol II Articles 4 to 6 are recognisably a human rights catalogue.</li></ol><h4>4. Lex specialis</h4><div class=\"study-defn\"><strong>Lex specialis derogat legi generali:</strong> The interpretive principle that where two rules apply to the same situation, the more specific governs.</div><p>The Court applied this in the Nuclear Weapons opinion, holding that whether a particular loss of life through the use of a weapon in warfare is an arbitrary deprivation of life contrary to ICCPR Article 6 falls to be determined by the applicable lex specialis, namely the law applicable in armed conflict. Three points must be made about this.</p><ol><li><strong>It is a rule of interpretation, not of displacement.</strong> Human rights law continues to apply; IHL informs the content of its open-textured terms, principally 'arbitrary'.</li><li><strong>It operates rule by rule, not body by body.</strong> IHL is the specialised law for targeting in hostilities. Human rights law is the specialised law for, say, the conditions of detention or the conduct of a trial, where IHL is less detailed.</li><li><strong>Its usefulness is contested.</strong> Many writers and some bodies now prefer to speak of complementarity and of harmonious interpretation, arguing that lex specialis suggests a hierarchy that does not exist and produces poor results where the two bodies genuinely conflict.</li></ol><div class=\"study-callout\"><strong>Example:</strong> A soldier shoots a member of an opposing armed force who is walking unarmed between positions during an international armed conflict. Under IHL this is lawful: a combatant may be targeted at any time. Under a pure human rights analysis it would be an arbitrary deprivation of life, since no immediate threat existed. The lex specialis approach resolves this by reading 'arbitrary' in Article 6 through IHL, so the killing is not arbitrary. Change the facts to a police officer shooting a suspected criminal in a state at peace, and human rights law's necessity standard applies without qualification.</div><h4>5. Extraterritorial application</h4><p>Human rights treaties apply to persons within a state's jurisdiction. Whether that extends abroad is central, because it determines whether human rights law reaches a state's forces operating overseas.</p><div class=\"table-wrap\"><table class=\"study-table\"><thead><tr><th>Model</th><th>Basis</th><th>Example</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Spatial</td><td>Effective control over an area</td><td>A state occupying foreign territory owes Convention obligations there</td></tr><tr><td>Personal</td><td>Authority and control over an individual</td><td>A person detained by a state's forces abroad is within its jurisdiction</td></tr><tr><td>Public powers</td><td>Exercise of public powers normally exercised by the territorial government</td><td>A state exercising security and administrative functions abroad with the consent of the territorial state</td></tr></tbody></table></div><p>The European Court has developed all three, and the trend has been to expand jurisdiction. The Human Rights Committee's General Comment 36 goes further, stating that a state party has an obligation to respect and ensure the right to life of all persons over whose enjoyment of that right it exercises power or effective control, which extends to persons located outside its territory. The extent to which conduct of hostilities abroad brings persons within jurisdiction remains genuinely unsettled and is worth flagging as such rather than overstating.</p><h4>6. Where each body is stronger</h4><div class=\"table-wrap\"><table class=\"study-table\"><thead><tr><th>Question</th><th>Governed principally by</th><th>Why</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Targeting during hostilities</td><td>IHL</td><td>Detailed rules on distinction, proportionality and precautions</td></tr><tr><td>Use of force in law enforcement, including riot control during a conflict</td><td>Human rights law</td><td>Necessity and proportionality to protect life; IHL has no equivalent detail</td></tr><tr><td>Grounds and procedure for detention in non-international conflict</td><td>Human rights law fills the gap</td><td>Common Article 3 and Protocol II say almost nothing about grounds or review</td></tr><tr><td>Treatment of prisoners of war</td><td>IHL</td><td>The Third Convention is far more detailed than any human rights instrument</td></tr><tr><td>Fair trial</td><td>Both, converging</td><td>Protocol I Article 75 and Protocol II Article 6 restate human rights guarantees</td></tr><tr><td>Remedy and reparation for individuals</td><td>Human rights law</td><td>IHL has no individual complaint mechanism</td></tr><tr><td>Occupation administration</td><td>IHL, supplemented by human rights law</td><td>The Fourth Convention and Hague Regulations govern, with human rights filling gaps</td></tr></tbody></table></div><h4>7. How the examiner&#x27;s question should be answered</h4><p>Never say that human rights law stops in wartime; that is the error the question is designed to catch. State that both apply, citing Nuclear Weapons and the Wall. Then explain lex specialis accurately as an interpretive principle operating rule by rule, and note the shift towards complementarity. Then apply both bodies to the facts, saying for each issue which provides the operative standard and why. On extraterritoriality, give the three jurisdictional models and note what remains unsettled. Reaching a definite conclusion on each issue, rather than describing the debate, is what separates a strong answer.</p><h4>Summary</h4><ul><li>IHL applies only in armed conflict; human rights law applies at all times, and both apply simultaneously in conflict.</li><li>The International Court of Justice confirmed the parallel application in Nuclear Weapons, the Wall and Armed Activities.</li><li>Lex specialis is an interpretive principle operating rule by rule, not a rule displacing one body of law wholesale.</li><li>IHL informs the meaning of &#x27;arbitrary&#x27; deprivation of life in hostilities; human rights law governs law enforcement and detention procedure.</li><li>Extraterritorial application rests on spatial control, personal authority and control, or the exercise of public powers.</li><li>Many writers now prefer complementarity and harmonious interpretation to lex specialis.</li></ul><h4>Key Terms</h4><div class=\"table-wrap\"><table class=\"study-table\"><thead><tr><th>Term</th><th>Meaning</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Lex specialis</td><td>The principle that the more specific rule governs where two rules apply to the same situation.</td></tr><tr><td>Complementarity of regimes</td><td>The approach treating IHL and human rights law as mutually reinforcing rather than hierarchically ordered.</td></tr><tr><td>Extraterritorial jurisdiction</td><td>The application of human rights obligations to conduct outside a state&#x27;s own territory.</td></tr><tr><td>Effective control over an area</td><td>The spatial basis for extraterritorial human rights jurisdiction.</td></tr></tbody></table></div><h4>Review Questions</h4><h5>Multiple choice</h5><div class=\"study-mcq\"><p class=\"mcq-q\">Does human rights law cease to apply during armed conflict?</p><ol type=\"A\"><li>No; it continues to apply alongside IHL, subject to any lawful derogation</li><li>Yes, IHL replaces it entirely</li><li>Only in international armed conflict</li><li>Only where the state declares an emergency</li></ol><details class=\"mcq-a\"><summary>Show answer</summary><p><strong>Answer: A.</strong> The International Court of Justice so held in the Nuclear Weapons advisory opinion and confirmed it in the Wall and Armed Activities.</p></details></div><div class=\"study-mcq\"><p class=\"mcq-q\">How does lex specialis operate?</p><ol type=\"A\"><li>As an interpretive principle applied rule by rule, informing open-textured terms such as &#x27;arbitrary&#x27;</li><li>By displacing human rights law entirely during conflict</li><li>By giving human rights law priority</li><li>By requiring a state to choose one regime</li></ol><details class=\"mcq-a\"><summary>Show answer</summary><p><strong>Answer: A.</strong> IHL is the specialised law for targeting; human rights law is more specialised for detention procedure and fair trial.</p></details></div><div class=\"study-mcq\"><p class=\"mcq-q\">Which body of law principally governs the grounds and review of detention in a non-international armed conflict?</p><ol type=\"A\"><li>Human rights law, because common Article 3 and Protocol II say almost nothing about grounds or review</li><li>IHL, through the Third Geneva Convention</li><li>Neither; detention is unregulated</li><li>Domestic law exclusively</li></ol><details class=\"mcq-a\"><summary>Show answer</summary><p><strong>Answer: A.</strong> The absence of treaty rules on internment in non-international conflict is a recognised gap in IHL.</p></details></div><div class=\"study-mcq\"><p class=\"mcq-q\">Which is a recognised basis for extraterritorial human rights jurisdiction?</p><ol type=\"A\"><li>Authority and control over an individual, such as a person detained by a state&#x27;s forces abroad</li><li>Nationality of the victim alone</li><li>Membership of the same regional organisation</li><li>Ratification of the Geneva Conventions</li></ol><details class=\"mcq-a\"><summary>Show answer</summary><p><strong>Answer: A.</strong> The other recognised bases are effective control over an area and the exercise of public powers normally exercised by the territorial government.</p></details></div><h5>Short answer</h5><div class=\"study-sa\"><p class=\"sa-q\">Compare international humanitarian law and international human rights law on five points.</p><details class=\"sa-a\"><summary>Model answer</summary><p>On temporal scope, IHL applies only during armed conflict while human rights law applies at all times. On who is bound, IHL binds states, non-state armed groups and individuals, while human rights law binds primarily states, with the position of non-state actors contested. On derogation, human rights law permits derogation from some rights in a declared emergency, while IHL has no derogation clause because it is already designed for emergency. On purpose, IHL balances humanity against military necessity and accepts that some killing is lawful, while human rights law protects the individual against the state and treats killing as lawful only where strictly necessary. On enforcement, IHL relies on individual criminal responsibility, state responsibility and the ICRC, while human rights law offers individual complaint mechanisms that IHL lacks entirely.</p></details></div><div class=\"study-sa\"><p class=\"sa-q\">Explain the lex specialis principle and two criticisms of it.</p><details class=\"sa-a\"><summary>Model answer</summary><p>Lex specialis holds that where two rules apply to the same situation, the more specific governs. The International Court of Justice applied it in the Nuclear Weapons opinion, holding that whether a loss of life in warfare is an arbitrary deprivation under ICCPR Article 6 is determined by reference to the law applicable in armed conflict. It is an interpretive principle: human rights law continues to apply, and IHL supplies content to its open-textured terms. The first criticism is that it suggests a hierarchy between the two bodies that does not exist, when in fact each is more specialised for different questions. The second is that it gives no useful answer where the two genuinely conflict rather than where one merely elaborates the other, which is why many writers and bodies now prefer to speak of complementarity and harmonious interpretation.</p></details></div><h4>Class Activity</h4><ol><li>Take one incident from a current armed conflict. Identify three legal issues it raises and state, for each, which body of law supplies the operative standard and why.</li><li>Read the passage on IHL in General Comment 36 on the right to life. Summarise in one page how the Human Rights Committee describes the relationship between the two bodies of law.</li></ol><h4>Assignment</h4><p>Write two pages comparing international humanitarian law and international human rights law. Set out the differences in scope, addressees, derogation, purpose and enforcement, explain the convergence of the two bodies with reference to the leading International Court of Justice opinions, analyse lex specialis with its criticisms, explain the bases of extraterritorial human rights jurisdiction, and identify which body supplies the operative standard for at least five distinct questions.</p><p class=\"study-print\"><strong>Printable:</strong> use your browser\u2019s Print \u2192 Save as PDF to keep an offline copy.</p></div>",
  "The Role of the ICRC": "<div class=\"study-note\"><div class=\"revision-banner\"><strong>Human Rights and International Humanitarian Law</strong><span>Module 7: Introduction to International Humanitarian Law</span></div><h3 class=\"lesson-note-title\">LESSON NOTE: THE ROLE OF THE ICRC</h3><div class=\"table-wrap\"><table class=\"study-table\"><thead><tr><th>Subject</th><th>Topic</th><th>Level</th><th>Lesson duration</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Human Rights and International Humanitarian Law</td><td>The Role of the ICRC</td><td>Beginner to Advanced</td><td>40 minutes</td></tr></tbody></table></div><h4>Learning objectives</h4><p>By the end of this lesson you should be able to:</p><ul><li>Describe the ICRC&#x27;s mandate and legal basis</li><li>Explain the seven Fundamental Principles</li><li>Distinguish the components of the International Red Cross and Red Crescent Movement</li><li>Explain the ICRC&#x27;s working methods and the confidentiality question</li></ul><h4>1. Introduction</h4><p>The ICRC occupies a position no other organisation holds. It is a private association under Swiss law that exercises functions conferred by treaties binding every state in the world. Understanding how that anomaly works, and what it costs the organisation, is the substance of this lesson.</p><h4>2. Mandate and legal basis</h4><div class=\"table-wrap\"><table class=\"study-table\"><thead><tr><th>Source</th><th>Content</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>The four Geneva Conventions</td><td>Rights of access to prisoners of war and civilian internees; the right of humanitarian initiative under common Article 3; functions as a substitute for Protecting Powers</td></tr><tr><td>Additional Protocols</td><td>Further functions including in relation to the missing and to relief operations</td></tr><tr><td>Statutes of the Movement</td><td>Adopted by the International Conference, which brings together the Movement and all states party to the Conventions; confer the mandate to work for the faithful application of IHL and to promote its development</td></tr><tr><td>Customary practice</td><td>Long-standing acceptance by states of the ICRC's role</td></tr></tbody></table></div><p>Two consequences follow. The ICRC's functions are conferred by treaty, not delegated by any government, which is the foundation of its independence. And because the International Conference includes all states party to the Conventions, resolutions adopted there carry a weight no ordinary NGO resolution could.</p><h4>3. The seven Fundamental Principles</h4><ol><li><strong>Humanity.</strong> To prevent and alleviate human suffering wherever it may be found, to protect life and health and to ensure respect for the human being.</li><li><strong>Impartiality.</strong> No discrimination as to nationality, race, religious beliefs, class or political opinions; relief is guided solely by needs, giving priority to the most urgent cases of distress.</li><li><strong>Neutrality.</strong> In order to continue to enjoy the confidence of all, the Movement may not take sides in hostilities or engage at any time in controversies of a political, racial, religious or ideological nature.</li><li><strong>Independence.</strong> National societies, while auxiliaries to the public authorities and subject to the laws of their countries, must maintain autonomy so that they can at all times act in accordance with the principles of the Movement.</li><li><strong>Voluntary service.</strong> The Movement is a voluntary relief movement not prompted in any manner by desire for gain.</li><li><strong>Unity.</strong> There can be only one Red Cross or Red Crescent society in any one country, open to all and carrying on its work throughout the territory.</li><li><strong>Universality.</strong> The Movement is worldwide, and all societies have equal status and share equal responsibilities and duties in helping each other.</li></ol><div class=\"study-callout\"><strong>Note:</strong> Distinguish impartiality from neutrality; students routinely conflate them. Impartiality concerns how assistance is allocated: by need alone, without discrimination between recipients. Neutrality concerns the organisation's relationship to the conflict: not taking sides, not engaging in political controversy. An organisation can be impartial in distributing aid while abandoning neutrality by publicly condemning one party, and that combination is precisely what several other humanitarian organisations deliberately adopt.</div><h4>4. The three components of the Movement</h4><div class=\"table-wrap\"><table class=\"study-table\"><thead><tr><th>Component</th><th>Nature</th><th>Role</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>ICRC</td><td>A private association under Swiss law with international legal personality</td><td>Operations in armed conflict; visits to detainees; guardian of IHL; leads in international and non-international armed conflict</td></tr><tr><td>IFRC</td><td>The International Federation of Red Cross and Red Crescent Societies</td><td>Coordinates assistance in natural disasters and health emergencies outside conflict; supports national societies</td></tr><tr><td>National Societies</td><td>One per country, recognised by the ICRC and admitted to the Federation</td><td>Auxiliaries to their public authorities in the humanitarian field; first responders in their own country</td></tr></tbody></table></div><p>A national society must satisfy ten conditions for recognition, including being constituted in a state party to the Conventions, being the only national society in that country, being recognised as an auxiliary by its government, being independent, using a recognised emblem, and applying the Fundamental Principles.</p><p>The International Conference of the Red Cross and Red Crescent, held roughly every four years, brings together the three components with all states party to the Geneva Conventions. It is the Movement's highest deliberative body and one of the few forums where states and humanitarian organisations deliberate on equal footing.</p><h4>5. Working methods</h4><ol><li><strong>Protection.</strong> Visits to prisoners of war, civilian internees and detainees, conducted under standard conditions: access to all detainees and all places of detention, private interviews without witnesses, the ability to repeat visits, and registration of detainees.</li><li><strong>Restoring family links.</strong> Tracing missing persons, exchanging Red Cross messages, reuniting families, and forensic work on the identification of human remains.</li><li><strong>Assistance.</strong> Water, sanitation, health care, food, shelter and economic security programmes.</li><li><strong>Prevention.</strong> Dissemination of IHL to armed forces, armed groups, universities and civil society; support for national implementation of IHL; the Advisory Service on IHL.</li><li><strong>Development of the law.</strong> The 2005 customary IHL study; the updated Commentaries on the Conventions; expert processes on detention, direct participation in hostilities and other questions.</li></ol><h4>6. Confidentiality and its limits</h4><p>The ICRC's standard working method is bilateral confidential dialogue: it reports what it finds to the detaining or controlling authority, not to the public. This is the most criticised feature of the organisation and the reasoning behind it should be stated fairly.</p><div class=\"table-wrap\"><table class=\"study-table\"><thead><tr><th>The case for confidentiality</th><th>The case against</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>It is the price of access; authorities admit the ICRC because it will not publicise</td><td>Silence can appear to legitimise, and has historically done so</td></tr><tr><td>Access allows registration of detainees, which is itself protection against disappearance</td><td>Confidential representations may be ignored indefinitely with no consequence</td></tr><tr><td>Sustained dialogue changes practice over time in ways public condemnation does not</td><td>Other organisations demonstrate that public reporting can also produce change</td></tr><tr><td>Losing access harms the detainees the organisation exists to protect</td><td>The public record of violations is left incomplete</td></tr></tbody></table></div><p>The ICRC's own position is that confidentiality is a working method, not an absolute. Its public denunciation policy permits public statements where violations are major and repeated, where delegates have witnessed them directly or their existence is established by reliable sources, where confidential representations have failed, and where publicity is in the interest of those affected. All four conditions must be met, which is why public denunciations are rare.</p><div class=\"study-callout\"><strong>Note:</strong> The organisation's conduct during the Second World War, in particular its decision not to make a public appeal about the extermination camps, is the historical case usually raised. The ICRC has itself acknowledged this as a failure. A complete answer states the policy, states the four conditions, and acknowledges that case rather than defending the record uncritically.</div><h4>7. Testimonial immunity</h4><p>The ICTY held in the Simic case in 1999 that the ICRC has a right under customary international law to non-disclosure of information relating to its work, so its personnel cannot be compelled to testify. Rule 73 of the ICTY Rules and Article 73 of the ICC Rules of Procedure and Evidence reflect this. The rationale is that if ICRC delegates could be called as witnesses, states and armed groups would not grant access. The consequence is a genuine tension between the organisation's protective work and criminal accountability, and it is a legitimate subject of criticism as well as a necessary condition of the work.</p><h4>8. How the examiner&#x27;s question should be answered</h4><p>Give the treaty basis of the mandate first, because it distinguishes the ICRC from every NGO. List all seven principles and take care to distinguish impartiality from neutrality. Separate the three components of the Movement, since confusing the ICRC with the Federation or with a national society is a common and costly error. On confidentiality, present both sides, give the four conditions for public denunciation, and acknowledge the Second World War case. Mention testimonial immunity, which shows reading beyond the basic account.</p><h4>Summary</h4><ul><li>The ICRC&#x27;s functions derive from the Geneva Conventions and the Statutes of the Movement, not from any government&#x27;s delegation.</li><li>The seven Fundamental Principles are humanity, impartiality, neutrality, independence, voluntary service, unity and universality.</li><li>Impartiality concerns allocating aid by need; neutrality concerns not taking sides in the conflict.</li><li>The Movement has three components: the ICRC, the International Federation, and the National Societies.</li><li>The ICRC works through protection visits, restoring family links, assistance, prevention and development of the law.</li><li>Confidentiality is a working method with four conditions permitting public denunciation, and the ICRC holds testimonial immunity before international tribunals.</li></ul><h4>Key Terms</h4><div class=\"table-wrap\"><table class=\"study-table\"><thead><tr><th>Term</th><th>Meaning</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>ICRC</td><td>The International Committee of the Red Cross, a private Swiss association with treaty-conferred functions.</td></tr><tr><td>Impartiality</td><td>Providing assistance according to need alone, without discrimination between recipients.</td></tr><tr><td>Neutrality</td><td>Not taking sides in hostilities or engaging in political, racial, religious or ideological controversy.</td></tr><tr><td>Right of humanitarian initiative</td><td>The ICRC&#x27;s entitlement under common Article 3 to offer its services to parties to a conflict.</td></tr><tr><td>Testimonial immunity</td><td>The ICRC&#x27;s customary right of non-disclosure, so its personnel cannot be compelled to testify.</td></tr></tbody></table></div><h4>Review Questions</h4><h5>Multiple choice</h5><div class=\"study-mcq\"><p class=\"mcq-q\">What distinguishes impartiality from neutrality?</p><ol type=\"A\"><li>Impartiality concerns allocating aid by need alone; neutrality concerns not taking sides in the conflict</li><li>They are two names for the same principle</li><li>Impartiality applies to states, neutrality to the Movement</li><li>Neutrality concerns funding sources</li></ol><details class=\"mcq-a\"><summary>Show answer</summary><p><strong>Answer: A.</strong> An organisation can distribute aid impartially while abandoning neutrality by publicly condemning a party, which some humanitarian organisations deliberately do.</p></details></div><div class=\"study-mcq\"><p class=\"mcq-q\">What is the legal basis of the ICRC&#x27;s mandate?</p><ol type=\"A\"><li>The Geneva Conventions, the Additional Protocols and the Statutes of the Movement</li><li>A resolution of the UN General Assembly</li><li>Swiss domestic law alone</li><li>A treaty between the ICRC and each state</li></ol><details class=\"mcq-a\"><summary>Show answer</summary><p><strong>Answer: A.</strong> Because the functions are conferred by treaty rather than delegated by any government, the ICRC&#x27;s independence has a legal foundation.</p></details></div><div class=\"study-mcq\"><p class=\"mcq-q\">Which condition is required before the ICRC will make a public denunciation?</p><ol type=\"A\"><li>Confidential representations must have failed and publicity must be in the interest of those affected</li><li>A Security Council request</li><li>Agreement of the state concerned</li><li>A resolution of the International Conference</li></ol><details class=\"mcq-a\"><summary>Show answer</summary><p><strong>Answer: A.</strong> The violations must also be major and repeated, and either witnessed by delegates or established by reliable sources; all four conditions must be met.</p></details></div><div class=\"study-mcq\"><p class=\"mcq-q\">Why do international tribunals recognise ICRC testimonial immunity?</p><ol type=\"A\"><li>Because if delegates could be compelled to testify, states and armed groups would deny the organisation access</li><li>Because the ICRC is a Swiss entity</li><li>Because its reports are unreliable</li><li>Because it is a party to the Rome Statute</li></ol><details class=\"mcq-a\"><summary>Show answer</summary><p><strong>Answer: A.</strong> The ICTY established this in Simic, and it creates a genuine tension between protection work and criminal accountability.</p></details></div><h5>Short answer</h5><div class=\"study-sa\"><p class=\"sa-q\">Name the seven Fundamental Principles and state what each requires.</p><details class=\"sa-a\"><summary>Model answer</summary><p>Humanity requires preventing and alleviating suffering wherever found, protecting life and health and ensuring respect for the human being. Impartiality requires assistance according to need alone, with priority to the most urgent cases and no discrimination on nationality, race, religion, class or political opinion. Neutrality requires not taking sides in hostilities and not engaging in political, racial, religious or ideological controversy. Independence requires national societies, though auxiliaries to public authorities, to retain the autonomy to act in accordance with the Movement&#x27;s principles. Voluntary service requires that the Movement not be prompted by desire for gain. Unity requires one society per country, open to all, working throughout the territory. Universality means the Movement is worldwide and all societies have equal status and equal duties to assist one another.</p></details></div><div class=\"study-sa\"><p class=\"sa-q\">Set out the case for and against the ICRC&#x27;s policy of confidentiality.</p><details class=\"sa-a\"><summary>Model answer</summary><p>In favour: confidentiality is the price of access, since authorities admit the ICRC precisely because it will not publicise what it finds, and access permits the registration of detainees which is itself protection against disappearance. Sustained private dialogue changes institutional practice in ways public condemnation often does not, and losing access harms the very detainees the organisation exists to protect. Against: silence can appear to legitimise, as the organisation&#x27;s own conduct in the Second World War showed; confidential representations can be ignored indefinitely with no consequence; other humanitarian organisations demonstrate that public reporting can also produce change; and the public record of violations is left incomplete. The ICRC&#x27;s own position is that confidentiality is a working method rather than an absolute, and it permits public denunciation where four cumulative conditions are met.</p></details></div><h4>Class Activity</h4><ol><li>Find one ICRC public statement or denunciation. Assess it against the four conditions and explain whether each was satisfied on the facts.</li><li>Research your national Red Cross or Red Crescent society. Identify three of its activities and explain which Fundamental Principle each most directly expresses.</li></ol><h4>Assignment</h4><p>Write one and a half pages on the role of the ICRC. Explain the legal basis of its mandate and why that makes it different from a non-governmental organisation, list and explain the seven Fundamental Principles taking care to distinguish impartiality from neutrality, distinguish the three components of the Movement, describe the ICRC&#x27;s five main working methods, and discuss the confidentiality policy with the conditions for public denunciation and the arguments on both sides.</p><p class=\"study-print\"><strong>Printable:</strong> use your browser\u2019s Print \u2192 Save as PDF to keep an offline copy.</p></div>",
  "The Principle of Distinction": "<div class=\"study-note\"><div class=\"revision-banner\"><strong>Human Rights and International Humanitarian Law</strong><span>Module 8: The Core Principles of IHL</span></div><h3 class=\"lesson-note-title\">LESSON NOTE: THE PRINCIPLE OF DISTINCTION</h3><div class=\"table-wrap\"><table class=\"study-table\"><thead><tr><th>Subject</th><th>Topic</th><th>Level</th><th>Lesson duration</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Human Rights and International Humanitarian Law</td><td>The Principle of Distinction</td><td>Beginner to Advanced</td><td>45 minutes</td></tr></tbody></table></div><h4>Learning objectives</h4><p>By the end of this lesson you should be able to:</p><ul><li>State the principle of distinction and its legal sources</li><li>Define military objective and civilian object and apply the test</li><li>Explain direct participation in hostilities and its consequences</li><li>Explain the presumption in cases of doubt and the prohibition of human shields</li></ul><h4>1. Introduction</h4><p>Distinction is the cardinal principle of the law of armed conflict. Every other targeting rule depends on it: proportionality only operates once you know who is a civilian, and precautions only matter once you know what may be attacked. The International Court of Justice described it in the Nuclear Weapons opinion as one of the cardinal principles constituting the fabric of humanitarian law and as intransgressible.</p><h4>2. The principle</h4><blockquote class=\"study-quote\">The Parties to the conflict shall at all times distinguish between the civilian population and combatants and between civilian objects and military objectives and accordingly shall direct their operations only against military objectives.</blockquote><p>That is Article 48 of Additional Protocol I, the basic rule. It is reflected in Articles 51 and 52, in customary rules 1 and 7 of the ICRC study, and applies in both international and non-international armed conflict.</p><div class=\"table-wrap\"><table class=\"study-table\"><thead><tr><th>May be attacked</th><th>May not be attacked</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Combatants</td><td>Civilians, unless and for such time as they directly participate in hostilities</td></tr><tr><td>Members of organised armed groups with a continuous combat function</td><td>Persons hors de combat</td></tr><tr><td>Military objectives</td><td>Civilian objects</td></tr><tr><td></td><td>Objects indispensable to the survival of the civilian population, medical units, cultural property, works containing dangerous forces, the natural environment</td></tr></tbody></table></div><h4>3. Who is a combatant</h4><div class=\"table-wrap\"><table class=\"study-table\"><thead><tr><th>Category</th><th>Basis</th><th>Consequence</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Members of the armed forces of a party, other than medical and religious personnel</td><td>AP I Art 43</td><td>May be attacked at any time; entitled to prisoner of war status on capture; immune from prosecution for lawful acts of war</td></tr><tr><td>Members of other militias and volunteer corps meeting four conditions</td><td>GC III Art 4A(2)</td><td>Responsible command, a fixed distinctive sign recognisable at a distance, carrying arms openly, conducting operations in accordance with the laws of war</td></tr><tr><td>Levee en masse</td><td>GC III Art 4A(6)</td><td>Inhabitants of unoccupied territory who spontaneously take up arms on the approach of the enemy, carrying arms openly and respecting the laws of war</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"study-callout\"><strong>Note:</strong> In non-international armed conflict there is no combatant status. Members of state armed forces may be attacked; members of an organised armed group may be attacked where they have a continuous combat function. But neither enjoys combatant immunity: a member of an armed group may be prosecuted under domestic law for the mere act of fighting, even if they broke no rule of IHL. This asymmetry is one of the strongest disincentives to compliance by armed groups, and it is worth noting in any answer on the subject.</div><h4>4. Military objectives</h4><div class=\"study-defn\"><strong>Military objective:</strong> Those objects which by their nature, location, purpose or use make an effective contribution to military action and whose total or partial destruction, capture or neutralisation, in the circumstances ruling at the time, offers a definite military advantage (AP I Article 52(2)).</div><p>The definition is cumulative: both limbs must be satisfied. Take each element.</p><div class=\"table-wrap\"><table class=\"study-table\"><thead><tr><th>Element</th><th>Meaning</th><th>Example</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Nature</td><td>The object is military by its inherent character</td><td>A tank, a barracks, a weapons depot</td></tr><tr><td>Location</td><td>The object's position makes it militarily significant</td><td>A bridge on the only supply route; a mountain pass</td></tr><tr><td>Purpose</td><td>Intended future use</td><td>A civilian factory being converted to munitions production</td></tr><tr><td>Use</td><td>Current actual use</td><td>A school being used as a command post</td></tr><tr><td>Effective contribution</td><td>A real, not speculative, contribution to military action</td><td>Not the general war effort or the war-sustaining economy</td></tr><tr><td>Definite military advantage</td><td>Concrete and perceptible, in the circumstances ruling at the time</td><td>Not hypothetical or merely potential advantage</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"study-callout\"><strong>Note:</strong> Two contested points. First, some states argue 'war-sustaining' objects, such as oil installations funding an adversary, may be targeted. The prevailing view rejects this: the contribution must be to military action, not to the economy that funds it, or the category would have no limit. Second, the definition is dynamic. A school is a civilian object today and a military objective the moment it becomes a command post, and it reverts when that use ends.</div><h4>5. Direct participation in hostilities</h4><p>Civilians lose protection 'unless and for such time as they take a direct part in hostilities'. The ICRC's 2009 Interpretive Guidance sets out three cumulative criteria for an act to constitute direct participation.</p><ol><li><strong>Threshold of harm.</strong> The act must be likely to adversely affect the military operations or capacity of a party, or to inflict death, injury or destruction on protected persons or objects.</li><li><strong>Direct causation.</strong> There must be a direct causal link between the act and the harm, either from that act or from a coordinated operation of which it forms an integral part.</li><li><strong>Belligerent nexus.</strong> The act must be specifically designed to cause the required harm in support of one party and to the detriment of another.</li></ol><div class=\"table-wrap\"><table class=\"study-table\"><thead><tr><th>Direct participation</th><th>Not direct participation</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Firing a weapon at enemy forces</td><td>Producing weapons in a factory</td></tr><tr><td>Laying a mine or an improvised explosive device</td><td>Supplying food or general funding</td></tr><tr><td>Driving ammunition to a firing position</td><td>Political support or propaganda</td></tr><tr><td>Acting as a spotter identifying targets for an attack</td><td>Working as a civilian in a defence ministry</td></tr><tr><td>Electronic interference with military systems</td><td>Providing medical care, including to fighters</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"study-defn\"><strong>Continuous combat function:</strong> A role within an organised armed group consisting of the continuous preparation, execution or command of acts amounting to direct participation, which makes the person targetable for as long as that function is assumed.</div><div class=\"study-callout\"><strong>Note:</strong> The 'revolving door' problem is the practical difficulty. A civilian who fights at night and farms by day regains protection between engagements, which is protective but operationally awkward. The Interpretive Guidance addresses it through the continuous combat function concept for members of organised armed groups, who are targetable continuously, while genuine civilians who participate sporadically regain protection between acts. Some states dispute this framework, particularly the ICRC's restrictive position on measures short of capture, so present it as the ICRC's guidance and note that it is not universally accepted.</div><h4>6. Doubt, human shields and identification</h4><ol><li><strong>Presumption in case of doubt.</strong> AP I Article 50(1) provides that in case of doubt whether a person is a civilian, that person shall be considered a civilian. Article 52(3) provides the same for objects normally dedicated to civilian purposes, such as a place of worship, a house or a school.</li><li><strong>The presence of combatants does not deprive a population of its civilian character.</strong> AP I Article 50(3): the presence within the civilian population of individuals who do not come within the definition of civilians does not deprive the population of its civilian character.</li><li><strong>Human shields are prohibited.</strong> AP I Article 51(7) prohibits using the presence or movements of the civilian population to render points immune from attack or to shield military operations. This is a war crime under the Rome Statute.</li><li><strong>Unlawful shielding does not remove the attacker's obligations.</strong> AP I Article 51(8) states expressly that violations of the prohibition do not release the parties from their obligations with respect to the civilian population, including the duty to take precautions and the proportionality rule. The shielded civilians remain protected and must still be counted in the proportionality assessment.</li></ol><div class=\"study-callout\"><strong>Example:</strong> An armed group positions an anti-aircraft gun in the courtyard of an occupied apartment building. The group has committed a war crime by using human shields. The gun is a military objective and may be attacked. But the attacking force must still take all feasible precautions, must still assess whether expected civilian harm would be excessive, and may not treat the residents as having forfeited protection. If proportionality fails, the attack is unlawful notwithstanding the other side's crime. This is the single most commonly mishandled point in problem questions and is worth a full paragraph in any answer.</div><h4>7. How the examiner&#x27;s question should be answered</h4><p>State Article 48 and identify distinction as customary and applicable in both conflict types. Apply the Article 52(2) definition element by element rather than asserting a conclusion, and say explicitly which of nature, location, purpose or use you rely on. If a person's status is in issue, apply the three direct participation criteria in order. Always address the presumption of civilian status in case of doubt. If human shields appear, state both that their use is a crime and that the attacker's obligations are unaffected. Never conclude that an attack is lawful on distinction alone: distinction is necessary but not sufficient, and proportionality and precautions must follow.</p><h4>Summary</h4><ul><li>Distinction requires parties to distinguish at all times between civilians and combatants and between civilian objects and military objectives.</li><li>A military objective must both make an effective contribution to military action and offer a definite military advantage from its destruction.</li><li>The definition is dynamic: a civilian object becomes a military objective by use and reverts when that use ends.</li><li>Civilians lose protection only for such time as they directly participate, judged by threshold of harm, direct causation and belligerent nexus.</li><li>In case of doubt, a person or a normally civilian object must be presumed civilian.</li><li>Using human shields is a war crime, but it does not release the attacker from precautions or proportionality.</li></ul><h4>Key Terms</h4><div class=\"table-wrap\"><table class=\"study-table\"><thead><tr><th>Term</th><th>Meaning</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Distinction</td><td>The obligation to direct operations only against military objectives.</td></tr><tr><td>Military objective</td><td>An object making an effective contribution to military action whose destruction offers a definite military advantage.</td></tr><tr><td>Direct participation in hostilities</td><td>Conduct satisfying the threshold of harm, direct causation and belligerent nexus criteria, causing loss of civilian protection.</td></tr><tr><td>Continuous combat function</td><td>A continuous role in an organised armed group making a person targetable throughout.</td></tr><tr><td>Human shields</td><td>Using the presence of civilians to render points immune from attack, which is prohibited and a war crime.</td></tr></tbody></table></div><h4>Review Questions</h4><h5>Multiple choice</h5><div class=\"study-mcq\"><p class=\"mcq-q\">A school is being used as an enemy command post. What is its status?</p><ol type=\"A\"><li>A military objective by use, reverting to civilian object status when that use ends</li><li>A civilian object that may never be attacked</li><li>A military objective permanently once so used</li><li>A dual-use object outside the definition</li></ol><details class=\"mcq-a\"><summary>Show answer</summary><p><strong>Answer: A.</strong> The Article 52(2) definition is dynamic, turning on the circumstances ruling at the time.</p></details></div><div class=\"study-mcq\"><p class=\"mcq-q\">An armed group places a weapon among civilians to deter attack. What follows for the attacker?</p><ol type=\"A\"><li>The weapon may be attacked, but precautions and proportionality still apply and the civilians remain protected</li><li>The civilians lose protection</li><li>The attack becomes automatically lawful</li><li>The attacker must not attack under any circumstances</li></ol><details class=\"mcq-a\"><summary>Show answer</summary><p><strong>Answer: A.</strong> Article 51(8) states expressly that violations of the shielding prohibition do not release the parties from their obligations towards the civilian population.</p></details></div><div class=\"study-mcq\"><p class=\"mcq-q\">Which of these is direct participation in hostilities?</p><ol type=\"A\"><li>Acting as a spotter identifying targets for an imminent attack</li><li>Working in a munitions factory</li><li>Providing general funding to a party</li><li>Giving medical care to wounded fighters</li></ol><details class=\"mcq-a\"><summary>Show answer</summary><p><strong>Answer: A.</strong> The act must satisfy threshold of harm, direct causation and belligerent nexus; factory work fails direct causation and medical care lacks belligerent nexus.</p></details></div><div class=\"study-mcq\"><p class=\"mcq-q\">What is the position where there is doubt whether a person is a civilian?</p><ol type=\"A\"><li>They must be considered a civilian</li><li>They may be attacked if the doubt is reasonable</li><li>The commander decides on the balance of probabilities</li><li>IHL is silent</li></ol><details class=\"mcq-a\"><summary>Show answer</summary><p><strong>Answer: A.</strong> Article 50(1) states the presumption, and Article 52(3) provides the equivalent for objects normally dedicated to civilian purposes.</p></details></div><h5>Short answer</h5><div class=\"study-sa\"><p class=\"sa-q\">Apply the definition of a military objective element by element to a bridge in a town.</p><details class=\"sa-a\"><summary>Model answer</summary><p>The first limb asks whether the bridge makes an effective contribution to military action. This may be by location, if it lies on a route the adversary is using or must use to move forces or supplies, or by use, if military traffic is crossing it. Mere civilian use does not qualify, and a contribution to the general economy rather than to military action is insufficient. The second limb asks whether the bridge&#x27;s total or partial destruction, capture or neutralisation offers a definite military advantage in the circumstances ruling at the time, meaning a concrete and perceptible advantage rather than a hypothetical or potential one. Both limbs must be satisfied. If they are, the bridge is a military objective, but the attack must still satisfy proportionality and the obligations of precaution.</p></details></div><div class=\"study-sa\"><p class=\"sa-q\">Explain the three criteria for direct participation in hostilities.</p><details class=\"sa-a\"><summary>Model answer</summary><p>Under the ICRC Interpretive Guidance the criteria are cumulative. The threshold of harm requires that the act be likely to adversely affect the military operations or military capacity of a party, or to inflict death, injury or destruction on protected persons or objects. Direct causation requires a direct causal link between the act and that harm, arising either from the act itself or from a coordinated military operation of which the act forms an integral part, which excludes contributions to the general war effort such as manufacturing weapons. Belligerent nexus requires that the act be specifically designed to cause the required harm in support of one party and to the detriment of another, which excludes violence unconnected with the conflict and excludes impartial medical care.</p></details></div><h4>Class Activity</h4><ol><li>Take five objects in your own town. For each, state the circumstances in which it would become a military objective and the circumstances in which it would revert to civilian status.</li><li>Write a one-page briefing for a commander on how to apply the presumption of civilian status in conditions of poor visibility and unreliable intelligence.</li></ol><h4>Assignment</h4><p>Write two pages on the principle of distinction. State the principle and its sources, explain who may be attacked in international and non-international conflict and note the absence of combatant immunity in the latter, apply the definition of a military objective element by element, explain direct participation in hostilities with the three criteria and the revolving door problem, and set out the presumption in cases of doubt and the rules on human shields.</p><p class=\"study-print\"><strong>Printable:</strong> use your browser\u2019s Print \u2192 Save as PDF to keep an offline copy.</p></div>",
  "The Principle of Proportionality": "<div class=\"study-note\"><div class=\"revision-banner\"><strong>Human Rights and International Humanitarian Law</strong><span>Module 8: The Core Principles of IHL</span></div><h3 class=\"lesson-note-title\">LESSON NOTE: THE PRINCIPLE OF PROPORTIONALITY</h3><div class=\"table-wrap\"><table class=\"study-table\"><thead><tr><th>Subject</th><th>Topic</th><th>Level</th><th>Lesson duration</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Human Rights and International Humanitarian Law</td><td>The Principle of Proportionality</td><td>Beginner to Advanced</td><td>45 minutes</td></tr></tbody></table></div><h4>Learning objectives</h4><p>By the end of this lesson you should be able to:</p><ul><li>State the proportionality rule and its legal source</li><li>Identify what goes on each side of the assessment</li><li>Explain the standard of assessment and the perspective from which it is judged</li><li>Apply the rule to a concrete targeting decision</li></ul><h4>1. Introduction</h4><p>Proportionality is the rule that permits the killing of civilians. That is an uncomfortable sentence but it is the accurate description: the law accepts that lawful attacks on military objectives will sometimes kill civilians, and proportionality sets the limit. Understanding exactly what goes on each side of the balance, and how the assessment is made, is the most operationally important content in this module.</p><h4>2. The rule</h4><blockquote class=\"study-quote\">Launching an attack which may be expected to cause incidental loss of civilian life, injury to civilians, damage to civilian objects, or a combination thereof, which would be excessive in relation to the concrete and direct military advantage anticipated.</blockquote><p>That is Additional Protocol I Article 51(5)(b), which defines such an attack as indiscriminate and therefore prohibited. The same formulation appears in Article 57(2)(a)(iii) as a precaution and in Article 57(2)(b) as a duty to cancel or suspend. Rome Statute Article 8(2)(b)(iv) makes the intentional launching of such an attack a war crime, adding the word 'clearly' before 'excessive' and requiring the perpetrator's knowledge.</p><div class=\"study-callout\"><strong>Note:</strong> Proportionality in IHL is not the same as proportionality in jus ad bellum, which concerns whether a state's use of force in self-defence is proportionate to the armed attack. Nor is it the same as proportionality in human rights law, which asks whether a restriction on a right is a proportionate means to a legitimate aim. Three different tests share one name, and conflating them is a standard error.</div><h4>3. What goes on each side</h4><div class=\"table-wrap\"><table class=\"study-table\"><thead><tr><th>Side of the balance</th><th>Included</th><th>Excluded</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Expected incidental harm</td><td>Civilian deaths and injuries; damage to civilian objects; reasonably foreseeable reverberating effects, such as deaths from destruction of a water treatment plant</td><td>Harm to combatants; harm to persons directly participating; damage to military objectives</td></tr><tr><td>Anticipated military advantage</td><td>Concrete and direct advantage from the attack, which may be assessed from the attack as a whole rather than isolated parts</td><td>Political advantage; advantage to morale; long-term or speculative advantage; the general war effort</td></tr></tbody></table></div><p>Four points require care.</p><ol><li><strong>Combatant casualties never count as incidental harm.</strong> The balance is between civilian harm and military advantage, not between casualties on both sides.</li><li><strong>Reverberating or indirect effects are increasingly accepted as within the assessment</strong> where they are reasonably foreseeable. Destroying an electrical grid may kill few people directly and many indirectly through the failure of hospitals and water treatment. The ICRC and a growing body of state practice require these to be considered.</li><li><strong>'Concrete and direct' excludes speculative advantage.</strong> Several states declared on ratification that the advantage is to be assessed from the attack considered as a whole and not from isolated parts, which is generally accepted, but this does not license aggregating an entire campaign into one advantage.</li><li><strong>Political and morale advantage does not count.</strong> Attacking to break civilian will is prohibited outright by Article 51(2), which forbids acts or threats of violence the primary purpose of which is to spread terror among the civilian population.</li></ol><h4>4. The standard: excessive, not equal</h4><div class=\"study-defn\"><strong>Excessive:</strong> Clearly out of proportion; the test is not whether civilian harm exceeds military advantage numerically but whether it is disproportionate to it.</div><p>Three features of the standard must be stated accurately.</p><ul><li><strong>There is no ratio.</strong> No number of civilian deaths is permitted per military objective. Anyone offering a figure is inventing it.</li><li><strong>The values are incommensurable.</strong> Civilian lives and military advantage are not measured in the same units, which is why the test is 'excessive' rather than 'greater than'.</li><li><strong>The threshold is deliberately high for criminal liability.</strong> The Rome Statute requires the harm to be 'clearly' excessive and requires knowledge, so the criminal standard is stricter than the IHL rule. An attack can violate IHL without meeting the threshold for prosecution.</li></ul><h4>5. The perspective of assessment</h4><p>This is the point that decides most real cases.</p><ol><li><strong>Ex ante, not ex post.</strong> The test is what was expected and anticipated at the time of the decision, not what actually happened. An attack expected to kill two civilians which kills two hundred because of an unforeseeable secondary explosion is not thereby unlawful, though the failure to foresee may itself breach the duty of precaution.</li><li><strong>The reasonable commander standard.</strong> The ICTY final report on the NATO bombing campaign framed the question as whether a reasonable, reasonably well-informed person in the circumstances of the actual perpetrator, making reasonable use of the information available, could have expected excessive civilian casualties.</li><li><strong>On the information reasonably available.</strong> A commander is judged on the intelligence they had or should have obtained, which links proportionality directly to the precautionary duty to verify.</li><li><strong>Good faith is necessary but not sufficient.</strong> A genuinely held but unreasonable assessment does not satisfy the standard.</li></ol><div class=\"study-callout\"><strong>Example:</strong> A commander plans to strike a munitions depot at 2am, when intelligence indicates the adjacent residential block is occupied by roughly forty people, and estimates that blast effects may kill or injure up to five. The depot supplies the artillery shelling a defended town. On these facts the attack is likely proportionate. Change the facts: the same depot is struck at 3pm on a market day, with three hundred civilians in the immediate area and an expectation of fifty or more deaths, when waiting eleven hours would reduce that to five with no loss of advantage. The second attack is likely disproportionate, and it also breaches the precautionary duty to choose the timing that minimises civilian harm.</div><h4>6. Proportionality and precautions together</h4><p>The two rules are distinct but interlocking, and answers that treat them as one lose marks.</p><div class=\"table-wrap\"><table class=\"study-table\"><thead><tr><th></th><th>Proportionality</th><th>Precautions</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Question</td><td>Is the expected harm excessive relative to the advantage?</td><td>Has everything feasible been done to minimise harm?</td></tr><tr><td>Source</td><td>AP I Arts 51(5)(b) and 57(2)(a)(iii)</td><td>AP I Arts 57 and 58</td></tr><tr><td>Timing</td><td>At the decision to attack, and continuously</td><td>Before and during the attack, and by the defender in advance</td></tr><tr><td>Failure</td><td>The attack is unlawful and may be a war crime</td><td>The attack is unlawful even if the proportionality balance would have been satisfied</td></tr></tbody></table></div><p>An attack can be proportionate and still unlawful for failure to take feasible precautions, for example by using an inaccurate weapon when a precise one was available. Conversely a commander who takes every precaution may still be prohibited from attacking if the residual expected harm remains excessive.</p><h4>7. How the examiner&#x27;s question should be answered</h4><p>Quote or paraphrase the rule accurately, including 'expected', 'incidental', 'excessive', 'concrete and direct' and 'anticipated'. Those five words carry the whole test. Then set out both sides of the balance and say expressly that combatant casualties are excluded and that reverberating effects are included. State that there is no ratio. Apply the reasonable commander standard ex ante on the information available. Then deal with precautions separately, since the two rules are distinct. Conclude with a definite view: examiners reward a reasoned conclusion over a survey of considerations.</p><h4>Summary</h4><ul><li>Proportionality prohibits attacks expected to cause incidental civilian harm excessive in relation to the concrete and direct military advantage anticipated.</li><li>Combatant casualties are excluded from the harm side; reasonably foreseeable reverberating effects are included.</li><li>Political advantage, morale effects and speculative or long-term advantage do not count as military advantage.</li><li>There is no permissible ratio; the values are incommensurable and the test is excessiveness, not equality.</li><li>Assessment is ex ante, by the standard of a reasonable, reasonably well-informed commander on the information available.</li><li>Proportionality and precautions are distinct: an attack may be proportionate yet unlawful for failure to take feasible precautions.</li></ul><h4>Key Terms</h4><div class=\"table-wrap\"><table class=\"study-table\"><thead><tr><th>Term</th><th>Meaning</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Proportionality in attack</td><td>The prohibition of attacks whose expected incidental civilian harm would be excessive relative to the anticipated military advantage.</td></tr><tr><td>Incidental harm</td><td>Civilian deaths, injuries and damage to civilian objects expected to result from an attack on a military objective.</td></tr><tr><td>Concrete and direct military advantage</td><td>A real and perceptible advantage anticipated from the attack, excluding speculative, political or morale benefit.</td></tr><tr><td>Reverberating effects</td><td>Reasonably foreseeable indirect consequences of an attack, such as deaths from destruction of water infrastructure.</td></tr><tr><td>Reasonable commander standard</td><td>The test of what a reasonably well-informed person in the actual circumstances could have expected.</td></tr></tbody></table></div><h4>Review Questions</h4><h5>Multiple choice</h5><div class=\"study-mcq\"><p class=\"mcq-q\">Which of these counts as incidental harm in the proportionality assessment?</p><ol type=\"A\"><li>Reasonably foreseeable civilian deaths from destruction of a water treatment plant</li><li>Enemy combatants killed in the strike</li><li>Damage to the military objective itself</li><li>Losses among the attacking force</li></ol><details class=\"mcq-a\"><summary>Show answer</summary><p><strong>Answer: A.</strong> Only civilian harm goes on that side of the balance, and reverberating effects are included where reasonably foreseeable.</p></details></div><div class=\"study-mcq\"><p class=\"mcq-q\">How many civilian deaths are permitted per military objective?</p><ol type=\"A\"><li>There is no ratio; the test is whether the expected harm is excessive relative to the advantage</li><li>Three</li><li>Ten</li><li>It depends on the type of weapon</li></ol><details class=\"mcq-a\"><summary>Show answer</summary><p><strong>Answer: A.</strong> The values are incommensurable, which is why the standard is excessiveness rather than a numerical comparison.</p></details></div><div class=\"study-mcq\"><p class=\"mcq-q\">From what perspective is proportionality assessed?</p><ol type=\"A\"><li>Ex ante, by a reasonable and reasonably well-informed commander on the information available</li><li>Ex post, by reference to actual casualties</li><li>By the affected civilian population</li><li>By an international tribunal applying hindsight</li></ol><details class=\"mcq-a\"><summary>Show answer</summary><p><strong>Answer: A.</strong> An unforeseeable outcome does not make an attack unlawful, though a failure to obtain available information may breach the duty of precaution.</p></details></div><div class=\"study-mcq\"><p class=\"mcq-q\">Can an attack be proportionate but still unlawful?</p><ol type=\"A\"><li>Yes, if feasible precautions were not taken, for example by using an inaccurate weapon when a precise one was available</li><li>No; proportionality is the complete test</li><li>Only in non-international armed conflict</li><li>Only if a war crime is charged</li></ol><details class=\"mcq-a\"><summary>Show answer</summary><p><strong>Answer: A.</strong> Proportionality and precautions are distinct obligations under Articles 51 and 57, and each must be satisfied independently.</p></details></div><h5>Short answer</h5><div class=\"study-sa\"><p class=\"sa-q\">State what goes on each side of the proportionality assessment.</p><details class=\"sa-a\"><summary>Model answer</summary><p>On the harm side: expected incidental loss of civilian life, injury to civilians and damage to civilian objects, including reasonably foreseeable reverberating effects such as deaths resulting from the destruction of water or electrical infrastructure. Excluded are harm to combatants, harm to civilians directly participating in hostilities at the time, and damage to the military objective itself. On the advantage side: the concrete and direct military advantage anticipated, which may be assessed from the attack considered as a whole rather than from isolated parts. Excluded are political advantage, effects on enemy or civilian morale, long-term or speculative advantage, and contribution to the general war effort.</p></details></div><div class=\"study-sa\"><p class=\"sa-q\">Explain how proportionality and precautions differ and give an example of an attack that satisfies one but not the other.</p><details class=\"sa-a\"><summary>Model answer</summary><p>Proportionality asks whether the expected incidental civilian harm would be excessive in relation to the concrete and direct military advantage anticipated. Precautions ask whether everything feasible has been done to verify the target, choose means and methods minimising civilian harm, select the timing and the objective that cause least harm, give effective advance warning where circumstances permit, and cancel or suspend if it becomes apparent the attack would be unlawful. An attack on a legitimate military objective causing modest expected civilian harm may be proportionate, yet unlawful because the commander used an unguided munition when a precision weapon was available and would have reduced the harm further. Conversely a commander may take every feasible precaution and still be prohibited from attacking because the residual expected harm remains excessive.</p></details></div><h4>Class Activity</h4><ol><li>Take a reported airstrike from any conflict. List what you would need to know to assess proportionality, distinguishing information available at the time from information available only afterwards.</li><li>In pairs, one takes the role of a commander and the other of a legal adviser. Work through a targeting decision aloud, with the adviser required to raise every element of the proportionality test in order.</li></ol><h4>Assignment</h4><p>Write two pages on the principle of proportionality. State the rule with its sources and distinguish it from proportionality in jus ad bellum and in human rights law, set out precisely what goes on each side of the assessment, explain why there is no permissible ratio, explain the ex ante reasonable commander standard, distinguish proportionality from precautions with an example of each failing independently, and apply the rule to a worked targeting decision.</p><p class=\"study-print\"><strong>Printable:</strong> use your browser\u2019s Print \u2192 Save as PDF to keep an offline copy.</p></div>",
  "Military Necessity": "<div class=\"study-note\"><div class=\"revision-banner\"><strong>Human Rights and International Humanitarian Law</strong><span>Module 8: The Core Principles of IHL</span></div><h3 class=\"lesson-note-title\">LESSON NOTE: MILITARY NECESSITY</h3><div class=\"table-wrap\"><table class=\"study-table\"><thead><tr><th>Subject</th><th>Topic</th><th>Level</th><th>Lesson duration</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Human Rights and International Humanitarian Law</td><td>Military Necessity</td><td>Beginner to Advanced</td><td>40 minutes</td></tr></tbody></table></div><h4>Learning objectives</h4><p>By the end of this lesson you should be able to:</p><ul><li>Define military necessity and state its correct function in IHL</li><li>Explain why it is not a general defence</li><li>Identify the provisions in which necessity operates expressly</li><li>Explain the relationship between necessity and humanity</li></ul><h4>1. Introduction</h4><p>Military necessity is the most misused concept in the law of armed conflict. It is regularly invoked as though it justified departing from any rule when operations require it. It does the opposite: the rules already incorporate necessity, and having done so they are absolute unless they say otherwise.</p><h4>2. Meaning</h4><div class=\"study-defn\"><strong>Military necessity:</strong> The principle permitting only those measures which are actually necessary to accomplish a legitimate military purpose and which are not otherwise prohibited by international humanitarian law.</div><p>The definition has two limbs and the second is the one usually forgotten. A measure must be necessary, and it must not be prohibited. Necessity is a limit on what may be done, not a licence.</p><p>The classical formulation comes from the Lieber Code of 1863: military necessity consists in the necessity of those measures which are indispensable for securing the ends of the war, and which are lawful according to the modern law and usages of war. The phrase 'and which are lawful' has been in the definition from the beginning.</p><h4>3. Why necessity is not a general defence</h4><ol><li><strong>The rules already balance it.</strong> Every IHL rule is the product of a negotiated balance between military necessity and humanity. Permitting necessity to override the resulting rule would undo the balance and leave nothing.</li><li><strong>The treaties say so by implication.</strong> Where the drafters intended necessity to operate as an exception, they said so expressly in the particular provision. The presence of express necessity clauses in some articles shows that necessity does not operate generally in the others.</li><li><strong>The Nuremberg tribunals rejected it.</strong> The doctrine of Kriegsraeson geht vor Kriegsmanier, that the necessities of war take precedence over the rules of war, was argued by the defence in several post-war trials and rejected. The Hostage case and the Krupp case both held that military necessity cannot justify violations of positive rules.</li><li><strong>Some rules are expressly absolute.</strong> Common Article 3 prohibits its four categories of conduct 'at any time and in any place whatsoever'. The prohibition of torture admits no exception. No necessity argument can reach these.</li></ol><div class=\"study-callout\"><strong>Note:</strong> Kriegsraeson is worth naming in an answer. It is the doctrine that necessity in war overrides the law of war, and its rejection at Nuremberg is the historical foundation of the modern position. Naming it demonstrates that you know what the argument is called and that it has been decided.</div><h4>4. Where necessity operates expressly</h4><div class=\"table-wrap\"><table class=\"study-table\"><thead><tr><th>Provision</th><th>Necessity clause</th><th>Effect</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>GC IV Art 53</td><td>Destruction of property in occupied territory prohibited except where rendered absolutely necessary by military operations</td><td>A high threshold; ordinary convenience is insufficient</td></tr><tr><td>GC IV Art 49(2)</td><td>Evacuation of an area permitted where the security of the population or imperative military reasons so demand</td><td>Temporary, with return as soon as hostilities in the area have ceased</td></tr><tr><td>GC I Art 33 and 34</td><td>Use of medical buildings and material of aid societies, subject to conditions</td><td>Limited exception</td></tr><tr><td>Hague Regulations Art 23(g)</td><td>Destruction or seizure of enemy property forbidden unless imperatively demanded by the necessities of war</td><td></td></tr><tr><td>Hague Regulations Art 43</td><td>The occupant must restore and ensure public order and safety while respecting the laws in force unless absolutely prevented</td><td>Constrains legislative change in occupied territory</td></tr><tr><td>1954 Cultural Property Convention Art 4(2)</td><td>Waiver of protection only in cases where military necessity imperatively requires</td><td>The Second Protocol of 1999 tightened this considerably</td></tr></tbody></table></div><p>Note the language in each: 'absolutely necessary', 'imperative', 'imperatively demanded'. These are deliberately high thresholds. A general assertion that operations would be easier does not satisfy them, and the burden of establishing the necessity lies on the party invoking it.</p><h4>5. Necessity and humanity</h4><p>The two principles are the poles between which the whole field is constructed.</p><div class=\"table-wrap\"><table class=\"study-table\"><thead><tr><th></th><th>Military necessity</th><th>Humanity</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Requires</td><td>Achieving the legitimate military purpose</td><td>Minimising suffering not required by that purpose</td></tr><tr><td>Permits</td><td>Measures necessary and lawful</td><td>Nothing; it is a constraint</td></tr><tr><td>Prohibits</td><td>Measures not necessary, even if lawful in themselves</td><td>Suffering beyond what necessity requires</td></tr></tbody></table></div><p>Every rule in IHL can be understood as a settled point of balance between them. The prohibition of unnecessary suffering is where the two meet most directly: a weapon causing suffering beyond what is needed to render an enemy hors de combat fails on both principles at once, because the excess suffering serves no military purpose.</p><div class=\"study-callout\"><strong>Example:</strong> A commander wishes to burn crops in occupied territory to deny food to an insurgent group operating in the area. Article 53 of the Fourth Convention prohibits destruction of property except where rendered absolutely necessary by military operations. Article 54 of Additional Protocol I prohibits attacking, destroying or removing objects indispensable to the survival of the civilian population, including foodstuffs and agricultural areas for their production, for the specific purpose of denying their sustenance value to the civilian population, whatever the motive. If the crops feed the civilian population, the necessity argument fails at the second limb: the measure is prohibited outright and no degree of military necessity licenses it.</div><h4>6. Necessity as a restraining principle</h4><p>The less familiar half of the doctrine is that necessity restrains as well as permits. Measures not necessary to the military purpose are unlawful even if no specific rule prohibits them.</p><ul><li>Destruction serving no military purpose is wanton destruction and a grave breach.</li><li>Continued attack on a force that has surrendered serves no military purpose and is prohibited as attacking persons hors de combat.</li><li>The ICRC's Interpretive Guidance argues that where a person can be captured rather than killed without additional risk, the use of lethal force may exceed what is actually necessary. This is contested by several states, and should be presented as the ICRC's view rather than as settled law.</li><li>Pillage, prohibited absolutely, is the clearest case of conduct serving personal rather than military ends.</li></ul><h4>7. How the examiner&#x27;s question should be answered</h4><p>The question almost always presents a commander invoking necessity to justify a prohibited act. Answer in four steps. Define necessity with both limbs, emphasising 'not otherwise prohibited'. State that it is not a general defence and name Kriegsraeson and its rejection at Nuremberg. Check whether the specific provision in issue contains an express necessity clause, and if it does, apply the high threshold its wording sets. If it does not, conclude that necessity is unavailable. Then add the restraining function, since a complete answer shows necessity working in both directions.</p><h4>Summary</h4><ul><li>Military necessity permits only measures actually necessary to a legitimate military purpose and not otherwise prohibited by IHL.</li><li>It is not a general defence: the rules already incorporate the balance between necessity and humanity.</li><li>The doctrine of Kriegsraeson, that necessity overrides the law of war, was argued and rejected at Nuremberg.</li><li>Necessity operates only where a provision says so expressly, using high thresholds such as &#x27;absolutely necessary&#x27; or &#x27;imperative&#x27;.</li><li>The burden of establishing necessity lies on the party invoking it.</li><li>Necessity also restrains: measures serving no military purpose, such as wanton destruction and pillage, are unlawful.</li></ul><h4>Key Terms</h4><div class=\"table-wrap\"><table class=\"study-table\"><thead><tr><th>Term</th><th>Meaning</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Military necessity</td><td>The principle permitting only measures necessary to a legitimate military purpose and not otherwise prohibited.</td></tr><tr><td>Kriegsraeson</td><td>The rejected doctrine that the necessities of war take precedence over the rules of war.</td></tr><tr><td>Wanton destruction</td><td>Destruction of property not justified by military necessity, a grave breach of the Conventions.</td></tr><tr><td>Imperative military reasons</td><td>The high threshold required by provisions such as GC IV Article 49(2).</td></tr></tbody></table></div><h4>Review Questions</h4><h5>Multiple choice</h5><div class=\"study-mcq\"><p class=\"mcq-q\">Can military necessity justify a violation of a rule of IHL?</p><ol type=\"A\"><li>No; the rules already incorporate necessity, and it operates only where a provision says so expressly</li><li>Yes, whenever operations require it</li><li>Yes, in international armed conflict only</li><li>Yes, if the commander acts in good faith</li></ol><details class=\"mcq-a\"><summary>Show answer</summary><p><strong>Answer: A.</strong> The doctrine that necessity overrides the law of war, known as Kriegsraeson, was argued and rejected in the post-war trials.</p></details></div><div class=\"study-mcq\"><p class=\"mcq-q\">What threshold does GC IV Article 53 set for destruction of property in occupied territory?</p><ol type=\"A\"><li>Destruction rendered absolutely necessary by military operations</li><li>Any military advantage</li><li>Convenience of the occupying force</li><li>Authorisation by the occupying commander</li></ol><details class=\"mcq-a\"><summary>Show answer</summary><p><strong>Answer: A.</strong> The wording is deliberately high, and the burden of establishing the necessity lies on the party invoking it.</p></details></div><div class=\"study-mcq\"><p class=\"mcq-q\">Which case rejected the Kriegsraeson doctrine?</p><ol type=\"A\"><li>The Hostage case, among the post-war Nuremberg trials</li><li>Tadic</li><li>Nicaragua</li><li>The Wall advisory opinion</li></ol><details class=\"mcq-a\"><summary>Show answer</summary><p><strong>Answer: A.</strong> The Krupp case reached the same conclusion, holding that military necessity cannot justify violations of positive rules.</p></details></div><div class=\"study-mcq\"><p class=\"mcq-q\">How does military necessity restrain rather than permit?</p><ol type=\"A\"><li>Measures serving no military purpose, such as wanton destruction and pillage, are unlawful even without a specific prohibition</li><li>It requires commanders to attack all lawful targets</li><li>It obliges states to declare war</li><li>It permits reprisals</li></ol><details class=\"mcq-a\"><summary>Show answer</summary><p><strong>Answer: A.</strong> Continued attack on a force that has surrendered is another example, since it serves no military purpose.</p></details></div><h5>Short answer</h5><div class=\"study-sa\"><p class=\"sa-q\">Define military necessity and explain both limbs of the definition.</p><details class=\"sa-a\"><summary>Model answer</summary><p>Military necessity permits only those measures which are actually necessary to accomplish a legitimate military purpose and which are not otherwise prohibited by international humanitarian law. The first limb is a test of necessity: the measure must genuinely be required for the military purpose, so that action serving convenience, revenge or personal gain fails. The second limb, which is the one usually omitted, is a test of legality: even a genuinely necessary measure is unlawful if a rule of IHL prohibits it. The formulation has contained both limbs since the Lieber Code of 1863, which defined necessity as those measures indispensable for securing the ends of the war and lawful according to the modern law and usages of war.</p></details></div><div class=\"study-sa\"><p class=\"sa-q\">Explain why military necessity is not a general defence, giving two supporting reasons.</p><details class=\"sa-a\"><summary>Model answer</summary><p>First, every rule of IHL is itself the product of a negotiated balance between military necessity and humanity, so allowing necessity to override the resulting rule would dissolve the balance and leave no restraint at all. Second, where the drafters intended necessity to operate as an exception they said so expressly in the particular provision, using high thresholds such as &#x27;absolutely necessary&#x27; or &#x27;imperatively demanded&#x27;; the presence of those clauses in some articles establishes that necessity does not operate in the others. Historically, the doctrine of Kriegsraeson, that the necessities of war take precedence over its rules, was argued by defendants in the post-war trials and rejected in the Hostage and Krupp cases, which held that military necessity cannot justify violations of positive rules of law.</p></details></div><h4>Class Activity</h4><ol><li>Find three provisions of the Geneva Conventions or Additional Protocols containing an express necessity clause. Write out the exact wording of each and rank them by strictness.</li><li>Take a scenario in which a commander wishes to demolish houses to clear a firing line. Write a legal adviser&#x27;s one-page opinion applying both limbs of the necessity test and the relevant provisions.</li></ol><h4>Assignment</h4><p>Write one and a half pages on military necessity. Define it with both limbs, explain why it is not a general defence with reference to the rejection of Kriegsraeson at Nuremberg, identify the provisions in which necessity operates expressly and the thresholds their wording sets, explain the relationship between necessity and humanity, and give examples of necessity operating as a restraint rather than a permission.</p><p class=\"study-print\"><strong>Printable:</strong> use your browser\u2019s Print \u2192 Save as PDF to keep an offline copy.</p></div>",
  "Precautions in Attack": "<div class=\"study-note\"><div class=\"revision-banner\"><strong>Human Rights and International Humanitarian Law</strong><span>Module 8: The Core Principles of IHL</span></div><h3 class=\"lesson-note-title\">LESSON NOTE: PRECAUTIONS IN ATTACK</h3><div class=\"table-wrap\"><table class=\"study-table\"><thead><tr><th>Subject</th><th>Topic</th><th>Level</th><th>Lesson duration</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Human Rights and International Humanitarian Law</td><td>Precautions in Attack</td><td>Beginner to Advanced</td><td>45 minutes</td></tr></tbody></table></div><h4>Learning objectives</h4><p>By the end of this lesson you should be able to:</p><ul><li>State the obligations of constant care and feasible precautions</li><li>List the specific precautions required of an attacker</li><li>Explain the requirement of effective advance warning</li><li>Explain precautions against the effects of attack owed by the defender</li></ul><h4>1. Introduction</h4><p>Distinction says what may be attacked. Proportionality says how much incidental harm is tolerable. Precautions say what must actually be done before and during the attack to make those judgements sound and to reduce the harm. In practice most avoidable civilian deaths result from failures of precaution rather than from deliberate targeting of civilians.</p><h4>2. The general obligation</h4><blockquote class=\"study-quote\">In the conduct of military operations, constant care shall be taken to spare the civilian population, civilians and civilian objects.</blockquote><p>Additional Protocol I Article 57(1). Two features matter. The duty is one of constant care, applying throughout operations and not only at the moment of attack. And it applies to the conduct of military operations generally, which is broader than 'attacks'.</p><div class=\"study-defn\"><strong>Feasible precautions:</strong> Those precautions which are practicable or practically possible taking into account all circumstances ruling at the time, including humanitarian and military considerations.</div><div class=\"study-callout\"><strong>Note:</strong> The feasibility standard is not a counsel of perfection. It does not require a commander to accept defeat or unreasonable risk to their own forces. But 'feasible' is an objective standard: a precaution is feasible if it was practically possible in the circumstances, and a party cannot make a precaution infeasible by choosing not to acquire the capability. Several states have entered understandings that the standard is judged on information reasonably available at the relevant time.</div><h4>3. The specific precautions in attack</h4><p>Article 57(2) sets out what those who plan or decide upon an attack must do.</p><ol><li><strong>Verify.</strong> Do everything feasible to verify that the objective is neither a civilian nor a civilian object, is not subject to special protection, and is a military objective within Article 52(2).</li><li><strong>Choose means and methods.</strong> Take all feasible precautions in the choice of means and methods of attack with a view to avoiding, and in any event minimising, incidental civilian harm. This governs weapon selection, fuzing, angle of attack, and the timing of the strike.</li><li><strong>Assess proportionality.</strong> Refrain from launching an attack expected to cause excessive incidental harm.</li><li><strong>Cancel or suspend.</strong> Cancel or suspend an attack if it becomes apparent that the objective is not a military one, is subject to special protection, or that the attack would be disproportionate. This is a continuing duty that runs up to and during the attack.</li><li><strong>Warn.</strong> Give effective advance warning of attacks which may affect the civilian population, unless circumstances do not permit.</li><li><strong>Choose between objectives.</strong> Where a choice is possible between several military objectives offering a similar military advantage, select the objective the attack on which may be expected to cause the least danger to civilian lives and to civilian objects (Article 57(3)).</li></ol><h4>4. Effective advance warning</h4><p>The warning requirement is qualified in two ways and the qualifications matter.</p><ul><li>The warning must be <strong>effective</strong>: it must reach those at risk, in a form they can understand, with enough time and information to allow them to take protective action, and to a place they can actually reach.</li><li>It is required only for attacks <strong>which may affect the civilian population</strong>.</li><li>It is excused where <strong>circumstances do not permit</strong>, principally where warning would compromise the operation by allowing a mobile target to escape or would endanger the attacking force.</li></ul><div class=\"table-wrap\"><table class=\"study-table\"><thead><tr><th>Likely effective</th><th>Likely ineffective</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Specific as to area and time, giving hours to evacuate</td><td>A general instruction to leave a large city with no safe route</td></tr><tr><td>Delivered in the local language through channels the population uses</td><td>A broadcast on a frequency the population cannot receive</td></tr><tr><td>Identifying a safe route and destination</td><td>A warning issued minutes before the strike</td></tr><tr><td>Followed by an actual opportunity to leave</td><td>A warning where the roads out are themselves being struck</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"study-callout\"><strong>Note:</strong> A warning does not convert those who remain into legitimate targets. Civilians who cannot leave, will not leave, or did not receive the warning remain civilians and must still be counted in the proportionality assessment. The proposition that failure to heed a warning forfeits protection has no basis in law, and stating this expressly is a reliable mark-earner.</div><h4>5. Precautions against the effects of attack</h4><p>Article 58 places obligations on the defending party, and answers frequently omit them.</p><ol><li>Endeavour to remove the civilian population, individual civilians and civilian objects from the vicinity of military objectives.</li><li>Avoid locating military objectives within or near densely populated areas.</li><li>Take the other necessary precautions to protect the civilian population under their control against the dangers resulting from military operations.</li></ol><p>These duties are qualified by 'to the maximum extent feasible', which recognises that a state cannot relocate its whole population or rebuild its military infrastructure during a conflict. But a party that deliberately co-locates military assets with civilians breaches Article 58 and, if the purpose is to shield them, Article 51(7).</p><div class=\"study-callout\"><strong>Note:</strong> The defender's breach does not diminish the attacker's obligations. This is the same point as with human shields and it recurs constantly. Both parties' duties are independent, and each is judged on its own conduct.</div><h4>6. Precautions in practice</h4><div class=\"table-wrap\"><table class=\"study-table\"><thead><tr><th>Decision</th><th>Precautionary content</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Intelligence</td><td>Use all reasonably available sources; a single unverified source is unlikely to satisfy the verification duty for a strike in a populated area</td></tr><tr><td>Weapon selection</td><td>Prefer precision munitions where available and effective; consider blast radius and fragmentation pattern</td></tr><tr><td>Fuzing and angle</td><td>Delayed fuzing to reduce surface blast; attack angle to direct debris away from civilian structures</td></tr><tr><td>Timing</td><td>Strike when the fewest civilians are present, where this does not forgo the advantage</td></tr><tr><td>Pattern of life analysis</td><td>Observation over time to establish civilian presence and routine</td></tr><tr><td>Collateral damage estimation</td><td>A structured methodology producing an estimate that can be reviewed</td></tr><tr><td>Abort criteria</td><td>Pre-set conditions requiring the attack to be cancelled, and the authority to invoke them</td></tr><tr><td>Post-strike assessment</td><td>Review of actual outcome, feeding back into future precautions</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"study-callout\"><strong>Example:</strong> A strike on a vehicle believed to carry a commander is planned in a residential street. Verification rests on a single intercepted call. No pattern of life observation is conducted. An unguided munition is used because the precision weapon is reserved for a later operation. No warning is given because the target is mobile, which is a legitimate reason. Twelve civilians die. Even if a court accepted that the proportionality assessment was defensible on the information the commander had, the attack breaches the precautionary obligations to verify with all feasible means and to choose means and methods minimising civilian harm. This is the typical shape of a precautions failure: not a decision to kill civilians, but a series of omissions each of which was feasible to avoid.</div><h4>7. How the examiner&#x27;s question should be answered</h4><p>Treat precautions as a separate head of analysis after distinction and proportionality; do not fold them in. Go through the six Article 57 obligations in order and reach a finding on each. Deal with warning by applying the effectiveness criteria rather than noting merely that a warning was or was not given, and state that those who remain do not lose protection. Then take Article 58 and assess the defender separately, saying explicitly that a defender's breach does not reduce the attacker's duties. Concluding that an attack was unlawful for failure of precaution, even where proportionality was arguably satisfied, is often the correct answer and demonstrates command of the distinction.</p><h4>Summary</h4><ul><li>Article 57(1) requires constant care to spare civilians throughout military operations, not only at the moment of attack.</li><li>Feasible precautions are those practically possible in the circumstances, judged objectively on information reasonably available.</li><li>The attacker must verify the target, choose means and methods minimising harm, assess proportionality, cancel or suspend, warn, and choose the least harmful objective.</li><li>A warning must be effective in content, timing and reach, and those who remain do not lose civilian protection.</li><li>Article 58 requires the defender to remove civilians from the vicinity of military objectives and to avoid locating objectives in populated areas.</li><li>Each party&#x27;s precautionary duties are independent: a defender&#x27;s breach does not reduce the attacker&#x27;s obligations.</li></ul><h4>Key Terms</h4><div class=\"table-wrap\"><table class=\"study-table\"><thead><tr><th>Term</th><th>Meaning</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Constant care</td><td>The continuing obligation to spare civilians throughout the conduct of military operations.</td></tr><tr><td>Feasible precautions</td><td>Precautions practically possible taking account of all circumstances ruling at the time.</td></tr><tr><td>Effective advance warning</td><td>A warning reaching those at risk with sufficient time, information and a usable means of escape.</td></tr><tr><td>Duty to cancel or suspend</td><td>The continuing obligation to abort an attack that becomes unlawful.</td></tr><tr><td>Precautions against effects</td><td>The defender&#x27;s obligations under Article 58 to separate civilians from military objectives.</td></tr></tbody></table></div><h4>Review Questions</h4><h5>Multiple choice</h5><div class=\"study-mcq\"><p class=\"mcq-q\">Civilians remain in an area after an effective warning is given. What is their status?</p><ol type=\"A\"><li>They remain civilians and must still be counted in the proportionality assessment</li><li>They become legitimate targets</li><li>They are treated as directly participating</li><li>They lose protection after twenty-four hours</li></ol><details class=\"mcq-a\"><summary>Show answer</summary><p><strong>Answer: A.</strong> There is no rule that failure to heed a warning forfeits protection; many civilians cannot leave or never received the warning.</p></details></div><div class=\"study-mcq\"><p class=\"mcq-q\">Which is a precaution required by Article 57(2)?</p><ol type=\"A\"><li>Choosing, among objectives offering a similar military advantage, the one causing least danger to civilians</li><li>Obtaining Security Council authorisation</li><li>Notifying the ICRC of every attack</li><li>Using only precision weapons in all circumstances</li></ol><details class=\"mcq-a\"><summary>Show answer</summary><p><strong>Answer: A.</strong> The others are to verify the target, choose means and methods minimising harm, assess proportionality, cancel or suspend, and give effective warning.</p></details></div><div class=\"study-mcq\"><p class=\"mcq-q\">What does &#x27;feasible&#x27; mean in the context of precautions?</p><ol type=\"A\"><li>Practicable or practically possible taking into account all circumstances ruling at the time, including humanitarian and military considerations</li><li>Whatever the commander considers convenient</li><li>Physically possible regardless of risk</li><li>Required only where cost-free</li></ol><details class=\"mcq-a\"><summary>Show answer</summary><p><strong>Answer: A.</strong> It is an objective standard, and a party cannot make a precaution infeasible by choosing not to acquire the relevant capability.</p></details></div><div class=\"study-mcq\"><p class=\"mcq-q\">A defending party places artillery in a residential district. What follows?</p><ol type=\"A\"><li>The defender breaches Article 58, but the attacker&#x27;s precautionary and proportionality obligations are unaffected</li><li>The attacker may disregard proportionality</li><li>The residents lose civilian status</li><li>The attack becomes automatically lawful</li></ol><details class=\"mcq-a\"><summary>Show answer</summary><p><strong>Answer: A.</strong> Each party&#x27;s obligations are independent and each is judged on its own conduct.</p></details></div><h5>Short answer</h5><div class=\"study-sa\"><p class=\"sa-q\">List the six precautions required of an attacker under Article 57.</p><details class=\"sa-a\"><summary>Model answer</summary><p>First, do everything feasible to verify that the objective is a military objective and is not a civilian person or object or subject to special protection. Second, take all feasible precautions in the choice of means and methods of attack to avoid and in any event minimise incidental civilian harm, which governs weapon selection, fuzing, angle and timing. Third, refrain from launching an attack expected to cause excessive incidental harm. Fourth, cancel or suspend the attack if it becomes apparent that the target is not military, is specially protected, or that the attack would be disproportionate. Fifth, give effective advance warning of attacks which may affect the civilian population unless circumstances do not permit. Sixth, where a choice is possible between objectives offering a similar military advantage, select the one whose attack may be expected to cause the least danger to civilians and civilian objects.</p></details></div><div class=\"study-sa\"><p class=\"sa-q\">What makes an advance warning effective?</p><details class=\"sa-a\"><summary>Model answer</summary><p>It must actually reach those at risk, which requires using channels the population can receive and a language they understand. It must be specific enough about the area and the timing to allow people to know whether they are affected. It must be given far enough in advance to permit evacuation, so a warning minutes before a strike is not effective. It must leave a usable route and a reachable destination, so a warning to leave when the roads out are themselves under attack is not effective. And it must be followed by a genuine opportunity to leave. A warning that fails these tests does not discharge the obligation, and even an effective warning does not deprive those who remain of their protected status.</p></details></div><h4>Class Activity</h4><ol><li>Take a reported strike that caused civilian casualties. Go through the six Article 57 precautions and identify which appear to have been taken and which not, noting what further information you would need.</li><li>Draft an evacuation warning for a real town, specifying the area, the timing, the route and the destination. Then assess your own draft against the effectiveness criteria in this lesson.</li></ol><h4>Assignment</h4><p>Write two pages on precautions. State the obligation of constant care and define feasibility, list and explain the six precautions required of an attacker, explain what makes an advance warning effective and why those who remain retain protection, set out the defender&#x27;s obligations under Article 58, explain why each party&#x27;s duties are independent, and illustrate a typical precautions failure with a worked example.</p><p class=\"study-print\"><strong>Printable:</strong> use your browser\u2019s Print \u2192 Save as PDF to keep an offline copy.</p></div>",
  "Humanity and the Martens Clause": "<div class=\"study-note\"><div class=\"revision-banner\"><strong>Human Rights and International Humanitarian Law</strong><span>Module 8: The Core Principles of IHL</span></div><h3 class=\"lesson-note-title\">LESSON NOTE: HUMANITY AND THE MARTENS CLAUSE</h3><div class=\"table-wrap\"><table class=\"study-table\"><thead><tr><th>Subject</th><th>Topic</th><th>Level</th><th>Lesson duration</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Human Rights and International Humanitarian Law</td><td>Humanity and the Martens Clause</td><td>Beginner to Advanced</td><td>40 minutes</td></tr></tbody></table></div><h4>Learning objectives</h4><p>By the end of this lesson you should be able to:</p><ul><li>State the principle of humanity and its function</li><li>Quote and explain the Martens Clause</li><li>Explain the debates about the clause&#x27;s legal effect</li><li>Apply the clause to a situation not covered by treaty</li></ul><h4>1. Introduction</h4><p>Humanity is the principle that gives the field its name. It also does specific legal work through the Martens Clause, a provision drafted in 1899 to break a diplomatic deadlock, which has outlasted the dispute it settled and become one of the most cited passages in international law.</p><h4>2. The principle of humanity</h4><div class=\"study-defn\"><strong>Humanity:</strong> The principle prohibiting the infliction of suffering, injury or destruction not actually necessary for the accomplishment of legitimate military purposes.</div><p>It operates in three ways.</p><ol><li><strong>As a general principle</strong> informing the interpretation of every rule, so that where a provision admits two readings the one better protecting persons is preferred.</li><li><strong>As the counterweight to military necessity</strong>, with each rule of IHL representing a settled balance between the two.</li><li><strong>As a gap-filler</strong>, through the Martens Clause, where no specific rule applies.</li></ol><p>The International Court of Justice referred in the Corfu Channel case to 'elementary considerations of humanity, even more exacting in peace than in war', and in the Nicaragua case held that common Article 3 reflects such elementary considerations and constitutes a minimum yardstick in all armed conflicts. Those two phrases are worth knowing verbatim.</p><h4>3. The Martens Clause</h4><blockquote class=\"study-quote\">Until a more complete code of the laws of war has been issued, the High Contracting Parties deem it expedient to declare that, in cases not included in the Regulations adopted by them, the inhabitants and the belligerents remain under the protection and the rule of the principles of the law of nations, as they result from the usages established among civilized peoples, from the laws of humanity, and the dictates of the public conscience.</blockquote><p>That is the original formulation in the preamble to the 1899 Hague Convention II, drafted by Fyodor Martens, the Russian delegate. Its origin was practical: the conference was deadlocked over the status of civilians who resisted an occupying force, with small states wanting them protected as belligerents and great powers wanting them treated as criminals. The clause allowed the conference to conclude by declaring that cases not covered remained under the protection of general principles.</p><p>It has been restated in every subsequent generation of instruments.</p><div class=\"table-wrap\"><table class=\"study-table\"><thead><tr><th>Instrument</th><th>Location</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>1907 Hague Convention IV</td><td>Preamble</td></tr><tr><td>The four Geneva Conventions of 1949</td><td>The denunciation articles: GC I Art 63, GC II Art 62, GC III Art 142, GC IV Art 158</td></tr><tr><td>Additional Protocol I</td><td>Article 1(2), in the operative text rather than the preamble</td></tr><tr><td>Additional Protocol II</td><td>Preamble</td></tr><tr><td>Convention on Certain Conventional Weapons</td><td>Preamble</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"study-callout\"><strong>Note:</strong> Its placement in Additional Protocol I Article 1(2) rather than in a preamble is significant, because it makes the clause part of the operative text of a treaty rather than an aid to interpretation of one. Mentioning this shows attention to the instruments rather than to the passage alone.</div><h4>4. What the clause means: three readings</h4><div class=\"table-wrap\"><table class=\"study-table\"><thead><tr><th>Reading</th><th>Content</th><th>Assessment</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Narrow</td><td>It merely confirms that customary international law continues to apply where treaty law does not cover a situation</td><td>Correct but makes the clause redundant, since custom would apply anyway</td></tr><tr><td>Broad</td><td>The laws of humanity and the dictates of public conscience are themselves independent sources of legal obligation</td><td>Difficult to accept, since it would create binding rules without state practice or opinio juris</td></tr><tr><td>Intermediate</td><td>The clause prevents the argument that what is not prohibited is permitted, and requires the laws of humanity and public conscience to be taken into account in interpreting existing rules and in assessing new means and methods</td><td>The prevailing view</td></tr></tbody></table></div><p>The intermediate reading is the one to state as the answer, giving the other two as the poles between which it sits. Its most important consequence is the rejection of the a contrario argument. Without the clause, a party could argue that a novel weapon or method is lawful precisely because no treaty prohibits it. The clause blocks that inference.</p><h4>5. Judicial treatment</h4><ol><li><strong>The Nuclear Weapons advisory opinion (1996).</strong> The International Court of Justice described the clause as an effective means of addressing the rapid evolution of military technology and confirmed its customary status. It did not, however, hold that the clause by itself rendered nuclear weapons unlawful.</li><li><strong>Kupreskic (ICTY, 2000).</strong> The Trial Chamber relied on the clause in holding that the principles of humanity and the dictates of public conscience have turned into customary rules, and used it to support a restrictive reading of belligerent reprisals against civilians.</li><li><strong>Krupp (Nuremberg, 1948).</strong> The tribunal described the clause as much more than a pious declaration, treating it as a general clause making the usages established among civilised peoples, the laws of humanity and the dictates of public conscience the legal yardstick where specific provisions do not apply.</li><li><strong>National courts</strong> have invoked it in cases on weapons and on the treatment of detainees, generally in the intermediate sense.</li></ol><h4>6. Applying the clause</h4><p>The clause is most useful in three situations.</p><ul><li><strong>New technologies.</strong> Autonomous weapon systems, cyber operations and novel munitions are not addressed by name in any treaty. The clause requires them to be assessed against humanity and public conscience, not simply permitted for want of a prohibition.</li><li><strong>Gaps between regimes.</strong> Where a situation falls between international and non-international armed conflict, or between armed conflict and law enforcement, the clause supplies a floor.</li><li><strong>Interpretation of open terms.</strong> Words like 'unnecessary suffering', 'inhuman treatment' and 'outrages upon personal dignity' are given content by reference to humanity and public conscience.</li></ul><div class=\"study-callout\"><strong>Example:</strong> A state develops an autonomous weapon system that selects and engages targets without human intervention. No treaty prohibits such a system by name. The a contrario argument would be that it is therefore lawful. The Martens Clause blocks that argument and requires the system to be assessed against the principles of humanity and the dictates of public conscience, alongside the ordinary requirements that it be capable of distinction, of proportionality assessment and of the precautions the law demands. The clause does not by itself prohibit the system, but it prevents the absence of a specific rule from being treated as permission. This is exactly the function Martens designed it for in 1899.</div><h4>7. How the examiner&#x27;s question should be answered</h4><p>Quote the clause, or enough of it to show you know the three elements: usages established among peoples, the laws of humanity, and the dictates of public conscience. Explain its 1899 origin, because the diplomatic deadlock explains its drafting. Set out the three readings and adopt the intermediate one with reasons. Cite Nuclear Weapons and Kupreskic. Then apply it to a modern problem, since the clause is most persuasive when shown doing work rather than described. Note that it does not by itself prohibit anything, which is the qualification that distinguishes an accurate answer from an enthusiastic one.</p><h4>Summary</h4><ul><li>Humanity prohibits suffering, injury or destruction not necessary for a legitimate military purpose.</li><li>The Martens Clause preserves the protection of the principles of the law of nations, the laws of humanity and the dictates of public conscience in cases not covered by treaty.</li><li>It originated in 1899 as a way of breaking a deadlock over civilians resisting occupation and has been restated in every subsequent generation of instruments.</li><li>Three readings compete; the prevailing intermediate reading blocks the argument that what is not prohibited is permitted.</li><li>The International Court of Justice confirmed its customary status in the Nuclear Weapons opinion and the ICTY relied on it in Kupreskic.</li><li>It is most useful for new technologies, gaps between regimes, and interpreting open-textured terms.</li></ul><h4>Key Terms</h4><div class=\"table-wrap\"><table class=\"study-table\"><thead><tr><th>Term</th><th>Meaning</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Humanity</td><td>The principle prohibiting suffering not necessary for a legitimate military purpose.</td></tr><tr><td>Martens Clause</td><td>The provision preserving the protection of general principles in cases not covered by treaty.</td></tr><tr><td>Dictates of public conscience</td><td>One element of the Martens Clause, referring to widely shared moral judgement about acceptable conduct in war.</td></tr><tr><td>A contrario argument</td><td>The inference that what is not prohibited is permitted, which the Martens Clause blocks.</td></tr></tbody></table></div><h4>Review Questions</h4><h5>Multiple choice</h5><div class=\"study-mcq\"><p class=\"mcq-q\">What is the prevailing interpretation of the Martens Clause?</p><ol type=\"A\"><li>It blocks the argument that what is not prohibited is permitted and requires humanity and public conscience to inform interpretation</li><li>It creates binding rules independently of state practice</li><li>It merely restates that custom applies</li><li>It applies only to nuclear weapons</li></ol><details class=\"mcq-a\"><summary>Show answer</summary><p><strong>Answer: A.</strong> The narrow reading makes the clause redundant and the broad reading would create obligations without practice or opinio juris.</p></details></div><div class=\"study-mcq\"><p class=\"mcq-q\">Where does the Martens Clause appear in the operative text of a treaty rather than a preamble?</p><ol type=\"A\"><li>Additional Protocol I Article 1(2)</li><li>Hague Convention IV of 1907</li><li>Additional Protocol II</li><li>The 1899 Hague Convention II</li></ol><details class=\"mcq-a\"><summary>Show answer</summary><p><strong>Answer: A.</strong> Its placement in the operative text makes it part of the treaty&#x27;s obligations rather than an aid to interpretation.</p></details></div><div class=\"study-mcq\"><p class=\"mcq-q\">Why was the clause originally drafted in 1899?</p><ol type=\"A\"><li>To break a deadlock over the status of civilians resisting an occupying force</li><li>To prohibit chemical weapons</li><li>To create the International Committee of the Red Cross</li><li>To define military objectives</li></ol><details class=\"mcq-a\"><summary>Show answer</summary><p><strong>Answer: A.</strong> Small states wanted such civilians protected as belligerents while great powers wanted them treated as criminals; the clause allowed the conference to conclude.</p></details></div><div class=\"study-mcq\"><p class=\"mcq-q\">How does the Martens Clause apply to autonomous weapon systems?</p><ol type=\"A\"><li>It prevents the absence of a specific prohibition from being treated as permission, requiring assessment against humanity and public conscience</li><li>It prohibits them outright</li><li>It has no application to new technology</li><li>It requires Security Council authorisation</li></ol><details class=\"mcq-a\"><summary>Show answer</summary><p><strong>Answer: A.</strong> The International Court of Justice described the clause as an effective means of addressing the rapid evolution of military technology.</p></details></div><h5>Short answer</h5><div class=\"study-sa\"><p class=\"sa-q\">Quote the substance of the Martens Clause and identify its three elements.</p><details class=\"sa-a\"><summary>Model answer</summary><p>In cases not covered by the applicable rules, the inhabitants and the belligerents remain under the protection and the rule of the principles of the law of nations, as they result from the usages established among civilised peoples, from the laws of humanity, and from the dictates of the public conscience. The three elements are therefore the established usages of peoples, which points to custom; the laws of humanity, which points to the general principle prohibiting suffering beyond military necessity; and the dictates of public conscience, which points to widely shared moral judgement about what is acceptable in war. The clause has been restated in the 1907 Hague Convention, in the denunciation articles of all four Geneva Conventions, in Additional Protocol I Article 1(2) and in the preamble to Additional Protocol II.</p></details></div><div class=\"study-sa\"><p class=\"sa-q\">Explain the three competing readings of the Martens Clause and say which is correct.</p><details class=\"sa-a\"><summary>Model answer</summary><p>The narrow reading treats the clause as merely confirming that customary international law continues to apply where treaty law is silent; this is accurate but makes the clause redundant, since custom would apply in any event. The broad reading treats the laws of humanity and the dictates of public conscience as independent sources of binding obligation; this is difficult to accept because it would generate legal rules without state practice or opinio juris. The intermediate reading, which prevails, holds that the clause prevents the argument that whatever is not expressly prohibited is permitted, and requires humanity and public conscience to be taken into account in interpreting existing rules and in assessing new means and methods of warfare. Its practical importance lies in blocking the a contrario inference, particularly in relation to new technologies.</p></details></div><h4>Class Activity</h4><ol><li>Choose one emerging military technology. Write one page assessing it under the Martens Clause, being careful to distinguish what the clause requires from what it prohibits.</li><li>Find the passage on the Martens Clause in the Nuclear Weapons advisory opinion. Summarise what the Court did and did not decide about the clause&#x27;s effect.</li></ol><h4>Assignment</h4><p>Write one and a half pages on humanity and the Martens Clause. State the principle of humanity and its three functions, quote the Martens Clause and explain its 1899 origin and its restatement in later instruments, set out the three competing readings and defend the intermediate one, cite the judicial treatment in Nuclear Weapons and Kupreskic, and apply the clause to one modern problem while noting the limits of what it can achieve.</p><p class=\"study-print\"><strong>Printable:</strong> use your browser\u2019s Print \u2192 Save as PDF to keep an offline copy.</p></div>",
  "Prohibition of Unnecessary Suffering": "<div class=\"study-note\"><div class=\"revision-banner\"><strong>Human Rights and International Humanitarian Law</strong><span>Module 8: The Core Principles of IHL</span></div><h3 class=\"lesson-note-title\">LESSON NOTE: PROHIBITION OF UNNECESSARY SUFFERING</h3><div class=\"table-wrap\"><table class=\"study-table\"><thead><tr><th>Subject</th><th>Topic</th><th>Level</th><th>Lesson duration</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Human Rights and International Humanitarian Law</td><td>Prohibition of Unnecessary Suffering</td><td>Beginner to Advanced</td><td>40 minutes</td></tr></tbody></table></div><h4>Learning objectives</h4><p>By the end of this lesson you should be able to:</p><ul><li>State the prohibition of superfluous injury and unnecessary suffering</li><li>Explain the test and how it is applied to weapons</li><li>Identify weapons prohibited on this basis</li><li>Explain the legal review of new weapons under Article 36</li></ul><h4>1. Introduction</h4><p>Distinction and proportionality protect civilians. This principle protects combatants. It is the recognition that even a person who may lawfully be killed may not lawfully be made to suffer more than is needed to put them out of the fight.</p><h4>2. The prohibition</h4><blockquote class=\"study-quote\">It is prohibited to employ weapons, projectiles and material and methods of warfare of a nature to cause superfluous injury or unnecessary suffering.</blockquote><p>Additional Protocol I Article 35(2), reflecting Hague Regulations Article 23(e) and customary rule 70. It applies in both international and non-international armed conflict.</p><div class=\"study-defn\"><strong>Superfluous injury or unnecessary suffering:</strong> Injury or suffering greater than that unavoidable to achieve legitimate military objectives, that is, to render an adversary hors de combat.</div><p>Its origin is the St Petersburg Declaration of 1868, which stated that the only legitimate object states should endeavour to accomplish during war is to weaken the military forces of the enemy, that for this purpose it is sufficient to disable the greatest possible number of men, and that this object would be exceeded by the employment of arms which uselessly aggravate the sufferings of disabled men or render their death inevitable.</p><h4>3. The test</h4><p>The assessment weighs the injury or suffering caused against the military utility of the weapon. Four factors are considered.</p><ol><li><strong>The nature and severity of the injury.</strong> Including whether it causes permanent disability or disfigurement, whether the wound is treatable, and the degree of pain.</li><li><strong>Inevitability of death.</strong> A weapon that renders death inevitable, rather than incapacitating, exceeds the military purpose.</li><li><strong>Availability of alternatives.</strong> Whether a weapon achieving the same military effect with less suffering exists and is available.</li><li><strong>Military utility.</strong> The concrete military advantage the weapon provides, since the test is comparative and not absolute.</li></ol><div class=\"study-callout\"><strong>Note:</strong> The test is comparative, which is why it is difficult to apply. All weapons cause suffering; the question is whether the suffering is disproportionate to the military purpose, given that the same purpose could often be achieved otherwise. A weapon of great military value causing severe injury may be lawful, while a weapon of marginal value causing the same injury may not.</div><h4>4. Weapons prohibited on this basis</h4><div class=\"table-wrap\"><table class=\"study-table\"><thead><tr><th>Weapon</th><th>Instrument</th><th>Reason</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Explosive projectiles under 400 grammes</td><td>St Petersburg Declaration 1868</td><td>Death rendered inevitable where a solid projectile would incapacitate</td></tr><tr><td>Expanding or dumdum bullets</td><td>Hague Declaration 1899</td><td>Expand or flatten easily in the body, causing wounds far beyond what is needed to disable</td></tr><tr><td>Poison and poisoned weapons</td><td>Hague Regulations Art 23(a)</td><td>Cause suffering beyond military need; also indiscriminate</td></tr><tr><td>Chemical weapons</td><td>Geneva Gas Protocol 1925; CWC 1993</td><td>Suffering out of proportion to military purpose; also indiscriminate</td></tr><tr><td>Biological weapons</td><td>BWC 1972</td><td>Same, with uncontrollable effects</td></tr><tr><td>Weapons injuring by fragments not detectable by X-ray</td><td>CCW Protocol I 1980</td><td>Untreatable wounds serving no additional military purpose</td></tr><tr><td>Blinding laser weapons</td><td>CCW Protocol IV 1995</td><td>Permanent blindness where incapacitation could be achieved otherwise</td></tr><tr><td>Anti-personnel mines</td><td>Ottawa Convention 1997</td><td>Primarily indiscriminate, but injury patterns also relevant</td></tr></tbody></table></div><p>Protocol IV on blinding laser weapons is the clearest modern application and the best example to give. It prohibited a weapon before it was deployed, on the reasoning that permanent blindness is a form of suffering disproportionate to the military purpose when incapacitation can be achieved by other means. It is the only pre-emptive weapons prohibition in the field.</p><h4>5. Distinguishing the two prohibitions on weapons</h4><div class=\"table-wrap\"><table class=\"study-table\"><thead><tr><th></th><th>Superfluous injury and unnecessary suffering</th><th>Indiscriminate by nature</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Protects</td><td>Combatants</td><td>Civilians</td></tr><tr><td>Test</td><td>Suffering disproportionate to military purpose</td><td>Cannot be directed at a specific military objective, or effects cannot be limited as required</td></tr><tr><td>Source</td><td>AP I Art 35(2)</td><td>AP I Art 51(4)(b) and (c)</td></tr><tr><td>Example</td><td>Expanding bullets</td><td>Biological weapons; unguided long-range rockets fired at a city</td></tr></tbody></table></div><p>Some weapons violate both. Chemical and biological weapons cause disproportionate suffering to combatants and cannot be controlled so as to distinguish civilians. Stating which prohibition is engaged, and not conflating them, is a mark of precision.</p><h4>6. Article 36 legal review of new weapons</h4><blockquote class=\"study-quote\">In the study, development, acquisition or adoption of a new weapon, means or method of warfare, a High Contracting Party is under an obligation to determine whether its employment would, in some or all circumstances, be prohibited by this Protocol or by any other rule of international law applicable to the High Contracting Party.</blockquote><p>Additional Protocol I Article 36. Four features are worth noting.</p><ol><li><strong>It covers means and methods, not only weapons.</strong> A new tactic is within scope.</li><li><strong>It applies at the study and development stage,</strong> not merely on adoption, so the review must be capable of stopping a programme early.</li><li><strong>It requires assessment 'in some or all circumstances',</strong> so a weapon lawful in some settings may need restrictions on its use.</li><li><strong>It does not prescribe how the review is conducted.</strong> Only a minority of states are known to have a formal review mechanism, which is the principal weakness of the provision.</li></ol><div class=\"table-wrap\"><table class=\"study-table\"><thead><tr><th>A review should assess</th><th>Against</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Whether the weapon is prohibited by name</td><td>Specific treaties binding the state</td></tr><tr><td>Whether it causes superfluous injury or unnecessary suffering</td><td>AP I Art 35(2)</td></tr><tr><td>Whether it is indiscriminate by nature</td><td>AP I Art 51(4)</td></tr><tr><td>Whether it causes widespread, long-term and severe damage to the environment</td><td>AP I Arts 35(3) and 55</td></tr><tr><td>Whether it can be used consistently with distinction, proportionality and precautions</td><td>AP I Arts 48, 51, 57</td></tr><tr><td>Whether it is contrary to the principles of humanity and the dictates of public conscience</td><td>The Martens Clause</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"study-callout\"><strong>Example:</strong> A state develops a directed energy weapon intended to incapacitate personnel through severe pain without lasting injury. An Article 36 review would ask whether the pain inflicted is disproportionate to the military purpose, whether long-term effects have been established, whether the weapon can be directed at a specific military objective, whether its use could be controlled as required by the law, and whether it can be employed consistently with precautions. The absence of a treaty naming the weapon does not conclude the review; the Martens Clause requires the remaining questions to be asked.</div><h4>7. How the examiner&#x27;s question should be answered</h4><p>State the rule and its St Petersburg origin, because the origin explains the logic. Give the four factors of the comparative test and stress that it is comparative rather than absolute. Give examples of prohibited weapons with the reason for each, using blinding lasers as the clearest modern case and noting that it is the only pre-emptive prohibition. Distinguish this prohibition from indiscriminateness, which protects a different group by a different test. Close with Article 36, its four features and the checklist a review should apply, since that is the operative mechanism by which the principle is actually enforced.</p><h4>Summary</h4><ul><li>It is prohibited to employ weapons or methods of a nature to cause superfluous injury or unnecessary suffering.</li><li>The prohibition protects combatants and originates in the St Petersburg Declaration of 1868.</li><li>The test is comparative, weighing severity of injury, inevitability of death and availability of alternatives against military utility.</li><li>Prohibited weapons include expanding bullets, poison, chemical and biological weapons, undetectable fragments and blinding lasers.</li><li>Blinding laser weapons are the only weapon prohibited pre-emptively before deployment.</li><li>Additional Protocol I Article 36 requires legal review of new weapons, means and methods at the study and development stage.</li></ul><h4>Key Terms</h4><div class=\"table-wrap\"><table class=\"study-table\"><thead><tr><th>Term</th><th>Meaning</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Superfluous injury or unnecessary suffering</td><td>Injury or suffering greater than that unavoidable to render an adversary hors de combat.</td></tr><tr><td>St Petersburg Declaration</td><td>The 1868 instrument stating that the legitimate object of war is to disable enemy forces, not to aggravate their suffering.</td></tr><tr><td>Article 36 review</td><td>The obligation to determine whether a new weapon, means or method would be prohibited by international law.</td></tr><tr><td>Indiscriminate by nature</td><td>A weapon that cannot be directed at a specific military objective or whose effects cannot be limited as the law requires.</td></tr></tbody></table></div><h4>Review Questions</h4><h5>Multiple choice</h5><div class=\"study-mcq\"><p class=\"mcq-q\">Whom does the prohibition of unnecessary suffering primarily protect?</p><ol type=\"A\"><li>Combatants</li><li>Civilians</li><li>Medical personnel</li><li>Prisoners of war only</li></ol><details class=\"mcq-a\"><summary>Show answer</summary><p><strong>Answer: A.</strong> The prohibition of indiscriminate weapons protects civilians; this rule protects those who may lawfully be attacked.</p></details></div><div class=\"study-mcq\"><p class=\"mcq-q\">Why were expanding bullets prohibited in 1899?</p><ol type=\"A\"><li>They expand in the body causing wounds far beyond what is needed to disable a combatant</li><li>They cannot be aimed accurately</li><li>They contaminate the environment</li><li>They are undetectable by X-ray</li></ol><details class=\"mcq-a\"><summary>Show answer</summary><p><strong>Answer: A.</strong> The test is comparative: the additional suffering serves no additional military purpose, since a non-expanding round already incapacitates.</p></details></div><div class=\"study-mcq\"><p class=\"mcq-q\">What is distinctive about the prohibition of blinding laser weapons?</p><ol type=\"A\"><li>It prohibited a weapon pre-emptively, before deployment</li><li>It applies only in international armed conflict</li><li>It permits use against materiel</li><li>It requires a five-year review</li></ol><details class=\"mcq-a\"><summary>Show answer</summary><p><strong>Answer: A.</strong> CCW Protocol IV of 1995 is the only pre-emptive weapons prohibition in the field.</p></details></div><div class=\"study-mcq\"><p class=\"mcq-q\">When does the Article 36 obligation to review a new weapon arise?</p><ol type=\"A\"><li>At the study, development, acquisition or adoption stage</li><li>Only on first operational use</li><li>Only where a treaty names the weapon</li><li>On request by the ICRC</li></ol><details class=\"mcq-a\"><summary>Show answer</summary><p><strong>Answer: A.</strong> Applying at the study stage means the review must be capable of stopping a programme early rather than validating a completed system.</p></details></div><h5>Short answer</h5><div class=\"study-sa\"><p class=\"sa-q\">Explain the test for superfluous injury or unnecessary suffering.</p><details class=\"sa-a\"><summary>Model answer</summary><p>The test weighs the injury or suffering a weapon causes against its military utility, and it is comparative rather than absolute, since every weapon causes suffering. Four factors are considered: the nature and severity of the injury, including whether it produces permanent disability or disfigurement and whether the wound is treatable; whether death is rendered inevitable rather than the adversary merely incapacitated; whether an alternative weapon exists and is available that achieves the same military effect with less suffering; and the concrete military advantage the weapon provides. A weapon of substantial military value causing severe injury may be lawful, while one of marginal value causing the same injury may not, which is why the availability of alternatives carries so much weight.</p></details></div><div class=\"study-sa\"><p class=\"sa-q\">What should an Article 36 legal review assess?</p><details class=\"sa-a\"><summary>Model answer</summary><p>It should determine whether the weapon, means or method is prohibited by name in any treaty binding the state. It should assess whether it is of a nature to cause superfluous injury or unnecessary suffering under Article 35(2), and whether it is indiscriminate by nature under Article 51(4) because it cannot be directed at a specific military objective or its effects cannot be limited as the law requires. It should consider whether it may be expected to cause widespread, long-term and severe damage to the natural environment. It should assess whether the weapon can in practice be used consistently with distinction, proportionality and the obligations of precaution, including in the circumstances of its intended employment. And where no specific rule applies, the Martens Clause requires assessment against the principles of humanity and the dictates of public conscience.</p></details></div><h4>Class Activity</h4><ol><li>Choose one weapon currently in service. Apply the four factors of the test to it and write a short conclusion, identifying what further technical information you would need.</li><li>Find out whether your country has a formal Article 36 review mechanism. If it does, describe its composition and procedure; if not, draft a one-page proposal for establishing one.</li></ol><h4>Assignment</h4><p>Write one and a half pages on the prohibition of unnecessary suffering. State the rule and its origin in the St Petersburg Declaration, explain the comparative test and its four factors, list weapons prohibited on this basis with the reason for each and identify the one pre-emptive prohibition, distinguish this rule from the prohibition of indiscriminate weapons, and explain the Article 36 review obligation and the checklist a review should apply.</p><p class=\"study-print\"><strong>Printable:</strong> use your browser\u2019s Print \u2192 Save as PDF to keep an offline copy.</p></div>",
  "Applying the Principles Together": "<div class=\"study-note\"><div class=\"revision-banner\"><strong>Human Rights and International Humanitarian Law</strong><span>Module 8: The Core Principles of IHL</span></div><h3 class=\"lesson-note-title\">LESSON NOTE: APPLYING THE PRINCIPLES TOGETHER</h3><div class=\"table-wrap\"><table class=\"study-table\"><thead><tr><th>Subject</th><th>Topic</th><th>Level</th><th>Lesson duration</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Human Rights and International Humanitarian Law</td><td>Applying the Principles Together</td><td>Beginner to Advanced</td><td>45 minutes</td></tr></tbody></table></div><h4>Learning objectives</h4><p>By the end of this lesson you should be able to:</p><ul><li>Set out a structured method for analysing a targeting decision</li><li>Apply distinction, proportionality, precautions and necessity in sequence</li><li>Identify the common errors in applying the principles</li><li>Work through a complete problem</li></ul><h4>1. Introduction</h4><p>The principles are taught separately and applied together. In practice a commander, a legal adviser or an investigator works through them in a fixed order, because each step depends on the one before. This lesson sets out that order and works a complete problem through it.</p><h4>2. The sequence</h4><ol><li><strong>Classify the conflict.</strong> International, non-international, or both running in parallel. This determines which rules apply.</li><li><strong>Identify the target's status.</strong> Is it a military objective under Article 52(2), applying nature, location, purpose or use, and both limbs? Is the person a combatant, a member of an armed group with a continuous combat function, or a civilian? If a civilian, are they directly participating, applying the three criteria? Apply the presumption in case of doubt.</li><li><strong>Check for special protection.</strong> Medical units and transports, religious personnel, cultural property, works containing dangerous forces, objects indispensable to civilian survival, the natural environment, civil defence, and persons hors de combat.</li><li><strong>Check the means and methods.</strong> Is the weapon prohibited by name? Is it indiscriminate by nature? Does it cause superfluous injury? Is the method prohibited, such as perfidy, denial of quarter or starvation of civilians?</li><li><strong>Assess proportionality.</strong> Expected incidental civilian harm, including reasonably foreseeable reverberating effects, against the concrete and direct military advantage anticipated. Excessive or not, assessed ex ante on available information.</li><li><strong>Work through precautions.</strong> Verification, choice of means and methods, timing, choice between objectives, warning, and the continuing duty to cancel or suspend.</li><li><strong>Check necessity as a restraint.</strong> Does the action serve a genuine military purpose, and does the specific provision engaged contain an express necessity clause with a threshold to satisfy?</li><li><strong>Reach a conclusion on each step</strong>, and state which step, if any, the attack fails at.</li></ol><div class=\"study-callout\"><strong>Note:</strong> Each step is independent. An attack that passes distinction may fail proportionality. An attack that passes both may fail precautions. Answers that stop at the first satisfied requirement are the most common cause of lost marks, and in practice the most common cause of unlawful attacks.</div><h4>3. The common errors</h4><div class=\"table-wrap\"><table class=\"study-table\"><thead><tr><th>Error</th><th>Correction</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Concluding an attack is lawful because the target is a military objective</td><td>Distinction is necessary but not sufficient; proportionality and precautions must follow</td></tr><tr><td>Counting enemy combatant deaths as incidental harm</td><td>Only civilian harm goes on that side of the proportionality balance</td></tr><tr><td>Treating a defender's unlawful shielding as removing the attacker's obligations</td><td>AP I Art 51(8) states expressly that it does not</td></tr><tr><td>Treating a warning as removing protection from those who remain</td><td>No such rule exists; they remain civilians and count in proportionality</td></tr><tr><td>Assessing proportionality with hindsight</td><td>The standard is ex ante, on what a reasonable commander could have expected</td></tr><tr><td>Invoking military necessity to override a rule</td><td>Necessity operates only where a provision says so, with a high threshold</td></tr><tr><td>Applying a numerical ratio of civilian deaths to military value</td><td>No ratio exists in law</td></tr><tr><td>Forgetting to classify the conflict</td><td>The applicable rules differ, particularly on status and detention</td></tr><tr><td>Treating precautions as part of proportionality</td><td>They are separate obligations, each capable of independent breach</td></tr></tbody></table></div><h4>4. Worked problem</h4><p><strong>Facts.</strong> In a non-international armed conflict, an organised armed group with a command structure and control of two districts has been shelling the capital. Intelligence indicates that the group's regional commander is meeting subordinates in the upper floor of a three-storey building. The ground floor is a bakery; the middle floor houses four families. The meeting is scheduled for 11am. Aerial observation over two days confirms the pattern of civilian occupancy. The available munitions are a 500-pound unguided bomb and a smaller precision-guided munition; the precision weapon is allocated to a separate operation the following day but could be reassigned. The commander orders the unguided bomb at 11am without warning. Eleven civilians die, including four children. The armed group commander is killed.</p><ol><li><strong>Classification.</strong> Non-international armed conflict: the group has a command structure, holds territory and conducts sustained operations, and the violence is intense. Common Article 3 and customary law apply, and Additional Protocol II if ratified and its threshold met.</li><li><strong>Target status.</strong> The regional commander of an organised armed group holds a continuous combat function and is targetable. The meeting location is a military objective by use for the duration of the meeting. Both limbs of Article 52(2) are satisfied: the meeting contributes effectively to military action and its neutralisation offers a definite advantage. No presumption of civilian status arises, since the observation is specific.</li><li><strong>Special protection.</strong> None engaged. The bakery is a civilian object but is not within a specially protected category.</li><li><strong>Means and methods.</strong> A 500-pound unguided bomb is not prohibited as such, but its use here is the critical issue at the precautions stage. No prohibited method is used; there is no perfidy and no denial of quarter.</li><li><strong>Proportionality.</strong> Expected civilian harm was high and known: four families on the middle floor, a bakery in operation at 11am, and a weapon whose blast would destroy the whole structure. The advantage is real, the removal of a regional commander. The assessment is finely balanced but the choice of weapon meant the entire building would be destroyed, which places the expected harm at the level of the whole civilian occupancy. On these facts a reasonable commander could have expected the harm to be excessive.</li><li><strong>Precautions.</strong> This is where the attack clearly fails. Verification was adequate. But the choice of means was not: a precision munition was available and reassignment was possible, and using the unguided bomb was not the choice minimising civilian harm. The timing was not the least harmful: the building was occupied at 11am and observation over two days would have identified quieter periods, or the commander's movements outside the building. No warning was given, and while warning would have allowed the target to escape, that reason engages only the warning obligation and does not excuse the failures on means and timing. The duty to choose between objectives was not considered.</li><li><strong>Necessity.</strong> The attack served a genuine military purpose, so necessity as a restraint is satisfied. No express necessity clause is engaged.</li><li><strong>Conclusion.</strong> The attack is unlawful. It fails the precautionary obligations in the choice of means and of timing, and on the facts it also fails proportionality. The failure of precaution is the clearer finding, and it is independently sufficient.</li></ol><div class=\"study-callout\"><strong>Note:</strong> Notice the structure of the conclusion. It identifies the step at which the attack fails, gives the clearest ground first, and states that the ground is independently sufficient. That is exactly how a legal adviser or an investigator writes it, and it is what an examiner is looking for.</div><h4>5. A checklist for practice</h4><div class=\"table-wrap\"><table class=\"study-table\"><thead><tr><th>Question</th><th>Where it comes from</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>What kind of conflict is this?</td><td>Common Arts 2 and 3; Tadic criteria</td></tr><tr><td>Is the target a military objective, or a person who may be attacked?</td><td>AP I Arts 48, 50, 51(3), 52(2); ICRC Interpretive Guidance</td></tr><tr><td>Is anything specially protected involved?</td><td>GC I-IV; AP I Arts 53-56; 1954 Convention</td></tr><tr><td>Is the weapon or method lawful?</td><td>AP I Arts 35, 37, 51(4); weapons treaties</td></tr><tr><td>Is the expected civilian harm excessive?</td><td>AP I Art 51(5)(b)</td></tr><tr><td>Has every feasible precaution been taken?</td><td>AP I Arts 57, 58</td></tr><tr><td>Does the action serve a military purpose, and is any necessity threshold met?</td><td>GC IV Arts 49, 53; Hague Regs Art 23(g)</td></tr><tr><td>At which step, if any, does this fail?</td><td>The conclusion</td></tr></tbody></table></div><h4>6. How the examiner&#x27;s question should be answered</h4><p>Use the eight-step sequence as your answer's structure and signpost each step. Reach an explicit conclusion at every step rather than accumulating observations. Where a step is satisfied, say so briefly and move on; spend your words on the steps that are contested. Identify the step at which the attack fails, state whether that failure is independently sufficient, and say what the party should have done differently. A recommendation is what distinguishes an answer written by someone who could advise a commander from one written by someone who has only read the rules.</p><h4>Summary</h4><ul><li>Analyse targeting decisions in a fixed sequence: classify, status, special protection, means and methods, proportionality, precautions, necessity, conclusion.</li><li>Each step is independent; an attack satisfying distinction may still fail proportionality or precautions.</li><li>The commonest errors are stopping at distinction, counting combatant deaths as incidental harm, and applying hindsight.</li><li>A defender&#x27;s unlawful conduct never reduces the attacker&#x27;s obligations.</li><li>A conclusion should identify the step at which an attack fails and say whether that failure is independently sufficient.</li><li>Precautions failures, particularly in the choice of weapon and timing, are the most frequent basis for unlawfulness in practice.</li></ul><h4>Key Terms</h4><div class=\"table-wrap\"><table class=\"study-table\"><thead><tr><th>Term</th><th>Meaning</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Targeting analysis</td><td>The structured sequence for determining the lawfulness of an attack.</td></tr><tr><td>Independently sufficient ground</td><td>A single failure that renders an attack unlawful regardless of the other steps.</td></tr><tr><td>Choice of means and methods</td><td>The precautionary obligation governing weapon selection, fuzing, angle and timing.</td></tr><tr><td>Continuous combat function</td><td>The role making a member of an organised armed group targetable throughout.</td></tr></tbody></table></div><h4>Review Questions</h4><h5>Multiple choice</h5><div class=\"study-mcq\"><p class=\"mcq-q\">What is the first step in analysing a targeting decision?</p><ol type=\"A\"><li>Classifying the armed conflict, since this determines which rules apply</li><li>Assessing proportionality</li><li>Checking the weapon</li><li>Identifying the commander</li></ol><details class=\"mcq-a\"><summary>Show answer</summary><p><strong>Answer: A.</strong> Classification determines status questions, detention authority and which treaty provisions govern.</p></details></div><div class=\"study-mcq\"><p class=\"mcq-q\">An attack satisfies distinction and proportionality but a precision weapon was available and unused. What follows?</p><ol type=\"A\"><li>The attack is unlawful for failure to take a feasible precaution in the choice of means</li><li>The attack is lawful, since the first two tests were met</li><li>The failure is relevant only to sentencing</li><li>Proportionality must be recalculated</li></ol><details class=\"mcq-a\"><summary>Show answer</summary><p><strong>Answer: A.</strong> Precautions are an independent obligation under Article 57 and can be breached even where the proportionality balance is satisfied.</p></details></div><div class=\"study-mcq\"><p class=\"mcq-q\">Which is a common error in applying the principles?</p><ol type=\"A\"><li>Treating enemy combatant deaths as incidental civilian harm in the proportionality assessment</li><li>Classifying the conflict before applying the rules</li><li>Applying the presumption of civilian status in case of doubt</li><li>Assessing proportionality ex ante</li></ol><details class=\"mcq-a\"><summary>Show answer</summary><p><strong>Answer: A.</strong> Only civilian harm goes on that side of the balance; the assessment is between civilian harm and military advantage.</p></details></div><div class=\"study-mcq\"><p class=\"mcq-q\">How should a conclusion on lawfulness be stated?</p><ol type=\"A\"><li>By identifying the step at which the attack fails and whether that failure is independently sufficient</li><li>By listing all the considerations without deciding</li><li>By stating only whether civilians died</li><li>By reference to the number of casualties alone</li></ol><details class=\"mcq-a\"><summary>Show answer</summary><p><strong>Answer: A.</strong> Adding what the party should have done differently is what distinguishes advice from description.</p></details></div><h5>Short answer</h5><div class=\"study-sa\"><p class=\"sa-q\">Set out the eight steps for analysing a targeting decision.</p><details class=\"sa-a\"><summary>Model answer</summary><p>First, classify the conflict as international, non-international, or both running in parallel, since this determines the applicable rules. Second, identify the target&#x27;s status: whether an object is a military objective under both limbs of Article 52(2), and whether a person is a combatant, a member of an organised armed group with a continuous combat function, or a civilian who may be directly participating. Third, check whether any specially protected person or object is involved. Fourth, check whether the weapon and the method are lawful. Fifth, assess proportionality, weighing expected incidental civilian harm including reverberating effects against the concrete and direct military advantage anticipated. Sixth, work through the precautionary obligations of verification, choice of means and methods, timing, choice between objectives, warning, and the duty to cancel or suspend. Seventh, check necessity as a restraint and apply any express necessity clause engaged. Eighth, reach a conclusion identifying the step at which the attack fails.</p></details></div><div class=\"study-sa\"><p class=\"sa-q\">Give four common errors in applying the IHL principles and correct each.</p><details class=\"sa-a\"><summary>Model answer</summary><p>Concluding that an attack is lawful because the target is a military objective: distinction is necessary but not sufficient, and proportionality and precautions must be applied independently. Counting enemy combatant deaths as incidental harm: only civilian harm goes on that side of the proportionality balance. Treating a defender&#x27;s unlawful use of human shields as releasing the attacker: Article 51(8) states expressly that it does not, and the shielded civilians still count in the proportionality assessment. Assessing proportionality with hindsight: the standard is what a reasonable, reasonably well-informed commander could have expected at the time on the information available, not what actually occurred.</p></details></div><h4>Class Activity</h4><ol><li>Work the module&#x27;s worked problem again, changing one fact at a time, and note at which step the analysis changes and why.</li><li>In groups of three, take the roles of commander, legal adviser and investigator. Run a targeting scenario through the eight steps aloud, with the investigator required to identify at which step the attack would fail and what evidence would establish it.</li></ol><h4>Assignment</h4><p>Write two pages applying the core principles together. Set out the eight-step sequence for analysing a targeting decision, explain why each step is independent, list at least five common errors with corrections, and then work a full problem of your own construction through the sequence, reaching an explicit conclusion at each step and stating both the step at which the attack fails and what the party should have done differently.</p><p class=\"study-print\"><strong>Printable:</strong> use your browser\u2019s Print \u2192 Save as PDF to keep an offline copy.</p></div>",
  "Civilians and Civilian Objects": "<div class=\"study-note\"><div class=\"revision-banner\"><strong>Human Rights and International Humanitarian Law</strong><span>Module 9: Protected Persons and Objects</span></div><h3 class=\"lesson-note-title\">LESSON NOTE: CIVILIANS AND CIVILIAN OBJECTS</h3><div class=\"table-wrap\"><table class=\"study-table\"><thead><tr><th>Subject</th><th>Topic</th><th>Level</th><th>Lesson duration</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Human Rights and International Humanitarian Law</td><td>Civilians and Civilian Objects</td><td>Beginner to Advanced</td><td>45 minutes</td></tr></tbody></table></div><h4>Learning objectives</h4><p>By the end of this lesson you should be able to:</p><ul><li>Define civilian and civilian object in international humanitarian law</li><li>State the general protection of the civilian population</li><li>Explain the position of dual-use objects</li><li>Set out the specific protections in the Fourth Geneva Convention</li></ul><h4>1. Introduction</h4><p>Civilians are defined negatively in international humanitarian law: a civilian is anyone who is not a combatant. That drafting choice, which looks awkward, is deliberate. It closes the gap that a positive definition would leave, because anyone who does not fall within a listed combatant category is protected.</p><h4>2. Definitions</h4><div class=\"study-defn\"><strong>Civilian:</strong> Any person who is not a member of the armed forces of a party to the conflict or a participant in a levee en masse; in case of doubt, the person shall be considered a civilian (AP I Article 50(1)).</div><div class=\"study-defn\"><strong>Civilian object:</strong> All objects which are not military objectives; in case of doubt whether an object normally dedicated to civilian purposes is being used to make an effective contribution to military action, it shall be presumed not to be so used (AP I Articles 52(1) and 52(3)).</div><p>Three consequences follow from the negative definition.</p><ul><li>The categories are exhaustive: everyone is either a combatant or a civilian, with no third category of unlawful combatant enjoying no protection. A civilian who directly participates loses protection from attack for that period but remains a civilian and retains the fundamental guarantees.</li><li>The presumption operates in favour of protection in both cases.</li><li>The presence of combatants within a civilian population does not deprive the population of its civilian character (Article 50(3)).</li></ul><h4>3. General protection of the civilian population</h4><p>Additional Protocol I Article 51 contains the core protections, all of which reflect customary law applicable in both types of conflict.</p><ol><li><strong>Article 51(2):</strong> the civilian population as such, as well as individual civilians, shall not be the object of attack. Acts or threats of violence the primary purpose of which is to spread terror among the civilian population are prohibited.</li><li><strong>Article 51(4):</strong> indiscriminate attacks are prohibited, meaning those not directed at a specific military objective, those employing a method or means which cannot be directed at a specific military objective, and those employing a method or means whose effects cannot be limited as required.</li><li><strong>Article 51(5)(a):</strong> area bombardment is indiscriminate, meaning an attack treating as a single military objective a number of clearly separated and distinct military objectives located in a city, town, village or other area containing a similar concentration of civilians.</li><li><strong>Article 51(5)(b):</strong> disproportionate attacks are indiscriminate.</li><li><strong>Article 51(6):</strong> reprisals against the civilian population are prohibited.</li><li><strong>Article 51(7):</strong> using civilians to shield military objectives is prohibited.</li></ol><div class=\"study-callout\"><strong>Note:</strong> Article 51(2)'s prohibition of terror attacks is worth separate attention. It covers not only violence but threats of violence whose primary purpose is to spread terror. The ICTY convicted on this basis in the Galic case concerning the shelling and sniping of Sarajevo, holding that the crime of terror requires the specific intent to spread terror among the civilian population. It is one of the few IHL rules with a specific intent element.</div><h4>4. Dual-use objects</h4><div class=\"study-defn\"><strong>Dual-use object:</strong> An object serving both civilian and military functions, such as a power station supplying both hospitals and a military command centre, or a bridge used by both civilian traffic and military convoys.</div><p>The law contains no separate category of dual-use object. The analysis is the ordinary one.</p><ol><li>Apply Article 52(2). If the object's use makes an effective contribution to military action and its destruction offers a definite military advantage, it is a military objective, even though it also serves civilians.</li><li>The civilian function does not immunise it, but it does enter the proportionality assessment as expected incidental harm, including reverberating effects.</li><li>The precautionary obligations apply with particular force: consider whether partial destruction or temporary disabling would achieve the advantage, whether the timing can reduce harm, and whether an alternative objective exists.</li><li>Some objects are protected regardless of dual use. Objects indispensable to the survival of the civilian population under Article 54 and works containing dangerous forces under Article 56 have their own regimes.</li></ol><div class=\"study-callout\"><strong>Example:</strong> An electrical grid supplies a military headquarters and also the water treatment plants serving two million people. It is a military objective by use. But the proportionality assessment must include the reasonably foreseeable deaths from the collapse of water treatment and hospital services, not merely the immediate blast casualties. Precautions require asking whether disabling the specific supply to the headquarters, or a temporary and reversible effect, would achieve the advantage. Attacks on electrical infrastructure are the standard illustration of why reverberating effects matter, because the direct casualties are almost always low and the indirect toll can be very high.</div><h4>5. Specific protections in the Fourth Geneva Convention</h4><p>The Fourth Convention protects persons who find themselves in the hands of a party or occupying power of which they are not nationals. It is worth distinguishing its two main parts.</p><div class=\"table-wrap\"><table class=\"study-table\"><thead><tr><th>Part</th><th>Scope</th><th>Key provisions</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Part II (Arts 13-26)</td><td>The whole of the populations of the countries in conflict, without adverse distinction</td><td>Hospital and safety zones; protection of the wounded and sick, hospitals and their staff; the passage of relief consignments of medical supplies, food and clothing for children under fifteen, expectant mothers and maternity cases; family news; reunion of dispersed families</td></tr><tr><td>Part III (Arts 27-141)</td><td>Protected persons: those in the hands of a party of which they are not nationals</td><td>Fundamental guarantees, treatment of aliens, occupied territory, internment</td></tr></tbody></table></div><p>Article 27 is the general statement: protected persons are entitled in all circumstances to respect for their persons, honour, family rights, religious convictions and practices, and manners and customs. They must at all times be humanely treated and protected against all acts of violence or threats thereof, and against insults and public curiosity. Women must be especially protected against any attack on their honour, in particular against rape, enforced prostitution or any form of indecent assault.</p><ol><li><strong>Article 32:</strong> prohibition of any measure causing physical suffering or extermination, expressly including murder, torture, corporal punishment, mutilation and medical or scientific experiments not necessitated by medical treatment.</li><li><strong>Article 33:</strong> prohibition of collective penalties, all measures of intimidation or terrorism, pillage, and reprisals against protected persons and their property.</li><li><strong>Article 34:</strong> prohibition of the taking of hostages.</li><li><strong>Article 49:</strong> prohibition of individual or mass forcible transfers and deportations from occupied territory, and prohibition of the occupying power transferring parts of its own civilian population into the territory it occupies.</li><li><strong>Articles 41-43 and 78:</strong> internment and assigned residence only where absolutely necessary for security, with a right to reconsideration by an appropriate court or administrative board and periodic review.</li></ol><h4>6. Non-international armed conflict</h4><p>The protections in internal conflict are less detailed but cover the same ground.</p><ul><li>Common Article 3: humane treatment, and prohibition of violence to life and person, hostage-taking, outrages upon personal dignity, and sentences without fair trial.</li><li>Additional Protocol II Article 4: fundamental guarantees, adding prohibitions of collective punishment, terrorism, slavery, pillage and threats to commit any of these.</li><li>Additional Protocol II Article 13: the civilian population and individual civilians shall enjoy general protection against the dangers arising from military operations, and shall not be the object of attack, unless and for such time as they take a direct part in hostilities.</li><li>Additional Protocol II Article 14: prohibition of starvation as a method of combat and of attacking objects indispensable to survival.</li><li>Additional Protocol II Article 17: prohibition of forced movement of civilians unless required by their security or imperative military reasons.</li><li>Customary law supplies most of the remaining content, including the definition of military objective, proportionality and precautions.</li></ul><h4>7. How the examiner&#x27;s question should be answered</h4><p>Define civilian and civilian object negatively and state the two presumptions. Set out Article 51's protections in order, treating the terror prohibition separately and citing Galic. On dual-use objects, say clearly that there is no separate legal category and then run the ordinary analysis, giving reverberating effects real weight. On the Fourth Convention, distinguish Part II from Part III, since the scope differs, and give the specific prohibitions by article number. Close by noting what applies in non-international conflict, since most contemporary conflicts are of that character.</p><h4>Summary</h4><ul><li>A civilian is anyone who is not a combatant, and a civilian object is any object that is not a military objective.</li><li>Both definitions carry presumptions in favour of protection, and the presence of combatants does not deprive a population of its civilian character.</li><li>Article 51 prohibits attacks on civilians, terror attacks, indiscriminate and area attacks, disproportionate attacks, reprisals and human shielding.</li><li>There is no separate legal category of dual-use object; such objects are assessed under Article 52(2) with reverberating effects entering proportionality.</li><li>The Fourth Convention protects the whole population in Part II and protected persons in the hands of an adverse party in Part III.</li><li>Common Article 3 and Additional Protocol II provide the equivalent protections in non-international armed conflict.</li></ul><h4>Key Terms</h4><div class=\"table-wrap\"><table class=\"study-table\"><thead><tr><th>Term</th><th>Meaning</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Civilian</td><td>Any person who is not a member of the armed forces or a participant in a levee en masse.</td></tr><tr><td>Civilian object</td><td>Any object that is not a military objective.</td></tr><tr><td>Dual-use object</td><td>An object serving both civilian and military functions, assessed under the ordinary military objective test.</td></tr><tr><td>Terror attack</td><td>Acts or threats of violence whose primary purpose is to spread terror among the civilian population.</td></tr><tr><td>Protected person</td><td>A person in the hands of a party or occupying power of which they are not a national.</td></tr></tbody></table></div><h4>Review Questions</h4><h5>Multiple choice</h5><div class=\"study-mcq\"><p class=\"mcq-q\">How is a civilian defined in international humanitarian law?</p><ol type=\"A\"><li>Negatively, as anyone who is not a member of the armed forces or a participant in a levee en masse</li><li>Positively, as a person engaged in civilian employment</li><li>By nationality</li><li>By residence in a non-combat zone</li></ol><details class=\"mcq-a\"><summary>Show answer</summary><p><strong>Answer: A.</strong> The negative definition closes the gap a positive definition would leave, since anyone outside the listed combatant categories is protected.</p></details></div><div class=\"study-mcq\"><p class=\"mcq-q\">What is the legal position of a power station serving both a military headquarters and civilian water treatment?</p><ol type=\"A\"><li>It is assessed under the ordinary military objective test, with reverberating civilian harm entering proportionality</li><li>It is immune from attack because it serves civilians</li><li>It may be attacked without any proportionality assessment</li><li>It falls into a special dual-use category with its own rules</li></ol><details class=\"mcq-a\"><summary>Show answer</summary><p><strong>Answer: A.</strong> There is no separate category of dual-use object in the law; the civilian function does not immunise it but does count as expected harm.</p></details></div><div class=\"study-mcq\"><p class=\"mcq-q\">Which case established liability for the crime of spreading terror among a civilian population?</p><ol type=\"A\"><li>Galic, before the ICTY, concerning the shelling and sniping of Sarajevo</li><li>Tadic</li><li>Nicaragua</li><li>Kupreskic</li></ol><details class=\"mcq-a\"><summary>Show answer</summary><p><strong>Answer: A.</strong> The offence requires the specific intent to spread terror, making it one of the few IHL rules with a specific intent element.</p></details></div><div class=\"study-mcq\"><p class=\"mcq-q\">What does Fourth Geneva Convention Article 33 prohibit?</p><ol type=\"A\"><li>Collective penalties, measures of intimidation or terrorism, pillage and reprisals against protected persons</li><li>Internment in all circumstances</li><li>Any restriction on movement</li><li>Trials of protected persons</li></ol><details class=\"mcq-a\"><summary>Show answer</summary><p><strong>Answer: A.</strong> Article 34 separately prohibits the taking of hostages and Article 32 prohibits measures causing physical suffering or extermination.</p></details></div><h5>Short answer</h5><div class=\"study-sa\"><p class=\"sa-q\">Explain why civilians are defined negatively and what follows from it.</p><details class=\"sa-a\"><summary>Model answer</summary><p>A civilian is defined as any person who is not a member of the armed forces of a party or a participant in a levee en masse. The definition is negative so that no gap can open: a positive definition would inevitably omit categories of person who would then be left unprotected. Three consequences follow. The categories are exhaustive, so there is no third status of unlawful combatant falling outside all protection; a civilian who directly participates loses protection from attack for that period but remains a civilian and keeps the fundamental guarantees. The presumption in case of doubt operates in favour of civilian status. And the presence of combatants within a civilian population does not deprive that population of its civilian character.</p></details></div><div class=\"study-sa\"><p class=\"sa-q\">How should a dual-use object be analysed?</p><details class=\"sa-a\"><summary>Model answer</summary><p>There is no separate legal category of dual-use object, so the ordinary analysis applies. First ask whether the object satisfies Article 52(2): does its nature, location, purpose or use make an effective contribution to military action, and would its destruction, capture or neutralisation offer a definite military advantage in the circumstances ruling at the time? If both limbs are met it is a military objective, and its civilian function does not immunise it. That function does, however, enter the proportionality assessment as expected incidental harm, including reasonably foreseeable reverberating effects such as deaths from the failure of water treatment or hospital power. Precautions then apply with particular force: whether partial or temporary disabling would achieve the advantage, whether timing can reduce harm, and whether an alternative objective exists.</p></details></div><h4>Class Activity</h4><ol><li>Identify five objects in your town that would be dual-use in an armed conflict. For each, list the reverberating effects that destruction would cause and who would be affected.</li><li>Read Articles 27 to 34 of the Fourth Geneva Convention. Rewrite them as a one-page plain-language guide for community leaders explaining what protections civilians have.</li></ol><h4>Assignment</h4><p>Write two pages on civilians and civilian objects. Give both definitions and explain why they are framed negatively, set out the protections in Article 51 including the prohibition of terror attacks with reference to Galic, explain how dual-use objects are analysed and why reverberating effects matter, summarise the specific protections in the Fourth Geneva Convention by article, and state the equivalent protections in non-international armed conflict.</p><p class=\"study-print\"><strong>Printable:</strong> use your browser\u2019s Print \u2192 Save as PDF to keep an offline copy.</p></div>",
  "The Wounded, Sick and Shipwrecked": "<div class=\"study-note\"><div class=\"revision-banner\"><strong>Human Rights and International Humanitarian Law</strong><span>Module 9: Protected Persons and Objects</span></div><h3 class=\"lesson-note-title\">LESSON NOTE: THE WOUNDED, SICK AND SHIPWRECKED</h3><div class=\"table-wrap\"><table class=\"study-table\"><thead><tr><th>Subject</th><th>Topic</th><th>Level</th><th>Lesson duration</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Human Rights and International Humanitarian Law</td><td>The Wounded, Sick and Shipwrecked</td><td>Beginner to Advanced</td><td>40 minutes</td></tr></tbody></table></div><h4>Learning objectives</h4><p>By the end of this lesson you should be able to:</p><ul><li>Define wounded, sick and shipwrecked in international humanitarian law</li><li>State the obligations to search for, collect and care for them</li><li>Explain the principle of non-discrimination in medical care</li><li>Describe the rules on the dead and the missing</li></ul><h4>1. Introduction</h4><p>This is where modern international humanitarian law began. The 1864 Convention did one thing: it required armies to care for the wounded, whichever side they belonged to. That obligation, and the principle behind it, remains the foundation of the whole Geneva branch of the law.</p><h4>2. Definitions</h4><div class=\"study-defn\"><strong>Wounded and sick:</strong> Persons, whether military or civilian, who because of trauma, disease or other physical or mental disorder or disability are in need of medical assistance or care and who refrain from any act of hostility (AP I Article 8(a)).</div><div class=\"study-defn\"><strong>Shipwrecked:</strong> Persons, whether military or civilian, who are in peril at sea or in other waters as a result of misfortune affecting them or the vessel or aircraft carrying them and who refrain from any act of hostility (AP I Article 8(b)).</div><div class=\"study-callout\"><strong>Note:</strong> Two features of these definitions matter. First, they cover civilians as well as military personnel; the 1949 Conventions restricted the First and Second Conventions to armed forces, and Additional Protocol I extended the definitions. Second, the qualifying condition is refraining from any act of hostility. A wounded fighter who continues to fire is not protected; a wounded fighter who cannot or does not fight is protected regardless of which side they belong to.</div><h4>3. The core obligations</h4><p>Geneva Convention I Article 12 and Additional Protocol I Article 10 state the substance.</p><ol><li><strong>Respect and protect.</strong> The wounded and sick must be respected and protected in all circumstances, meaning they may not be attacked and must be shielded from harm.</li><li><strong>Humane treatment and care.</strong> They must be treated humanely and cared for by the party in whose power they find themselves, with the least possible delay.</li><li><strong>No adverse distinction.</strong> No distinction founded on any grounds other than medical ones. Only urgent medical reasons authorise priority in the order of treatment.</li><li><strong>Prohibited acts.</strong> Any attempt on their lives or violence to their persons is strictly prohibited, including murder, extermination, torture, biological experiments, wilfully leaving them without medical assistance or care, and wilfully exposing them to contagion or infection.</li><li><strong>Consent to treatment.</strong> No person may be subjected to any medical procedure not indicated by their state of health and not consistent with generally accepted medical standards; even consent does not validate a procedure that fails this test.</li><li><strong>Abandonment.</strong> A party compelled to abandon wounded or sick to the enemy must, so far as military considerations permit, leave with them medical personnel and material to assist in their care.</li></ol><h4>4. Search, collection and evacuation</h4><p>Geneva Convention I Article 15 and Additional Protocol I Article 17 impose active duties.</p><ul><li>At all times, and particularly after an engagement, parties must without delay take all possible measures to search for and collect the wounded and sick, to protect them against pillage and ill-treatment, and to ensure their adequate care.</li><li>They must search for the dead and prevent their being despoiled.</li><li>Whenever circumstances permit, an armistice, suspension of fire or local arrangement should be arranged to permit the removal, exchange and transport of the wounded left on the battlefield.</li><li>The civilian population must be permitted, even on their own initiative, to collect and care for the wounded and sick, and no one may be harmed, prosecuted, convicted or punished for such humanitarian acts.</li><li>Parties may appeal to the civilian population and to relief societies to collect and care for the wounded, and must grant those responding the necessary protection and facilities.</li></ul><div class=\"study-callout\"><strong>Note:</strong> The provision protecting those who care for the wounded from prosecution is important in practice and frequently violated. In several conflicts medical personnel and ordinary civilians have been prosecuted for treating members of an armed group. Additional Protocol I Article 16 and Protocol II Article 10 state expressly that no person shall be punished for carrying out medical activities compatible with medical ethics, regardless of the person benefiting.</div><h4>5. Non-discrimination and medical ethics</h4><div class=\"table-wrap\"><table class=\"study-table\"><thead><tr><th>Rule</th><th>Content</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Triage by need alone</td><td>Order of treatment determined only by urgent medical reasons; nationality, allegiance, race, religion, sex, political opinion and similar criteria are irrelevant</td></tr><tr><td>No compulsion to act contrary to ethics</td><td>No one may be compelled to perform acts contrary to the rules of medical ethics or to other medical rules designed for the benefit of the wounded and sick</td></tr><tr><td>No punishment for medical activity</td><td>No person may be punished for carrying out medical activities compatible with medical ethics, whoever the beneficiary</td></tr><tr><td>Limited reporting duty</td><td>Persons engaged in medical activities may not be compelled to give information about the wounded and sick in their care where such information would prove harmful to the patients or their families, subject to compulsory notification of communicable diseases</td></tr><tr><td>Records</td><td>Records of medical procedures must be kept and be available</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"study-callout\"><strong>Example:</strong> A field hospital receives twelve casualties: seven from its own forces, four from the opposing force and one civilian. Two of the enemy wounded and one of its own are in immediate danger of death. Triage must place those three first, in the order their medical condition requires, regardless of allegiance. A commander ordering that all friendly casualties be treated before any enemy casualty is ordering a violation of Article 12, and medical personnel are entitled and obliged to refuse.</div><h4>6. The dead and the missing</h4><p>Additional Protocol I Part II Section III addresses missing and dead persons, and this section is frequently overlooked in answers.</p><ol><li><strong>The right of families to know.</strong> Article 32 states that the activities of the parties in this section are prompted mainly by the right of families to know the fate of their relatives. This is one of the earliest articulations of what human rights law later called the right to the truth.</li><li><strong>Search for the missing.</strong> Article 33 requires each party, as soon as circumstances permit and at the latest from the end of active hostilities, to search for persons reported missing by an adverse party, and to transmit all relevant information.</li><li><strong>Treatment of remains.</strong> Article 34 requires the remains of persons who died in occupation or in detention to be respected, and gravesites to be respected, maintained and marked. Parties must facilitate access by families, return of remains and personal effects.</li><li><strong>Burial.</strong> Geneva Convention I Article 17 requires that the dead be honourably interred if possible according to the rites of their religion, that graves be grouped by nationality where possible, properly maintained and marked, and that cremation be avoided except for imperative reasons of hygiene or the religion of the deceased, with reasons recorded.</li><li><strong>Identification.</strong> Bodies must be examined, if possible by a medical examination, before burial or cremation, with a view to confirming death, establishing identity and enabling a report to be made. Half of the double identity disc, or the whole single disc, must remain on the body.</li><li><strong>Graves Registration Service.</strong> Parties must organise one at the outset of hostilities and exchange information through the Information Bureau.</li></ol><div class=\"study-callout\"><strong>Note:</strong> The prohibition of mutilating dead bodies and of despoiling them is customary and appears as rule 113 of the ICRC study. Outrages upon the dignity of the dead constitute a war crime under the Rome Statute as outrages upon personal dignity, and the ICC has so held.</div><h4>7. Non-international armed conflict</h4><p>Common Article 3 requires that the wounded and sick be collected and cared for. Additional Protocol II Articles 7 to 11 develop this substantially: protection and care without adverse distinction, search and collection, protection of medical and religious personnel, the general protection of the medical mission, and protection of medical units and transports. Customary rules 109 to 113 apply the search, care, dead and missing obligations in both types of conflict.</p><h4>8. How the examiner&#x27;s question should be answered</h4><p>Give the two definitions and stress that they cover civilians and that the qualifying condition is refraining from hostile acts. Then set out the obligations in two groups: passive duties to respect, protect and treat, and active duties to search, collect and evacuate. Make non-discrimination and triage by medical need alone a separate paragraph with a worked example, since it is the point most often examined. Then deal with the dead and the missing, which most candidates omit entirely and which is therefore a reliable way to distinguish an answer. Note the customary status of the rules in non-international conflict.</p><h4>Summary</h4><ul><li>The wounded, sick and shipwrecked are defined to include civilians and are protected provided they refrain from acts of hostility.</li><li>They must be respected, protected, treated humanely and cared for with the least possible delay by the party in whose power they are.</li><li>No distinction may be made on any grounds other than medical ones; only urgent medical reasons authorise priority.</li><li>Parties must search for and collect the wounded and dead after an engagement, and civilians who help may not be punished.</li><li>No one may be punished for carrying out medical activities compatible with medical ethics, whoever the beneficiary.</li><li>The families&#x27; right to know underpins the obligations to search for the missing, identify the dead and respect gravesites.</li></ul><h4>Key Terms</h4><div class=\"table-wrap\"><table class=\"study-table\"><thead><tr><th>Term</th><th>Meaning</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Wounded and sick</td><td>Persons in need of medical care who refrain from any act of hostility.</td></tr><tr><td>Shipwrecked</td><td>Persons in peril at sea as a result of misfortune, who refrain from any act of hostility.</td></tr><tr><td>Adverse distinction</td><td>Differential treatment on grounds other than medical need, which is prohibited.</td></tr><tr><td>Right of families to know</td><td>The interest underpinning the obligations concerning the missing and the dead.</td></tr><tr><td>Graves Registration Service</td><td>The body each party must organise to record and maintain gravesites.</td></tr></tbody></table></div><h4>Review Questions</h4><h5>Multiple choice</h5><div class=\"study-mcq\"><p class=\"mcq-q\">On what basis may priority in medical treatment be given?</p><ol type=\"A\"><li>Urgent medical reasons alone</li><li>Nationality of the casualty</li><li>Rank</li><li>Whether the casualty is friendly or enemy</li></ol><details class=\"mcq-a\"><summary>Show answer</summary><p><strong>Answer: A.</strong> Geneva Convention I Article 12 prohibits any distinction founded on grounds other than medical ones.</p></details></div><div class=\"study-mcq\"><p class=\"mcq-q\">May a civilian be prosecuted for treating a wounded member of an armed group?</p><ol type=\"A\"><li>No; no person may be punished for medical activities compatible with medical ethics, whoever the beneficiary</li><li>Yes, if the group is proscribed</li><li>Yes, unless they report the patient</li><li>Only in non-international armed conflict</li></ol><details class=\"mcq-a\"><summary>Show answer</summary><p><strong>Answer: A.</strong> Additional Protocol I Article 16 and Protocol II Article 10 state this expressly, and the rule is frequently violated in practice.</p></details></div><div class=\"study-mcq\"><p class=\"mcq-q\">What underpins the obligations concerning missing persons in Additional Protocol I?</p><ol type=\"A\"><li>The right of families to know the fate of their relatives</li><li>The need to establish criminal responsibility</li><li>The interests of the detaining power</li><li>The requirements of military intelligence</li></ol><details class=\"mcq-a\"><summary>Show answer</summary><p><strong>Answer: A.</strong> Article 32 states this expressly, and it is an early articulation of what human rights law later called the right to the truth.</p></details></div><div class=\"study-mcq\"><p class=\"mcq-q\">What must be done with the dead before burial?</p><ol type=\"A\"><li>They must be examined, if possible medically, to confirm death, establish identity and enable a report</li><li>They must be cremated</li><li>They must be returned immediately to the adverse party</li><li>Nothing beyond burial</li></ol><details class=\"mcq-a\"><summary>Show answer</summary><p><strong>Answer: A.</strong> Half of a double identity disc, or a whole single disc, must remain on the body, and cremation is avoided except for imperative reasons of hygiene or religion.</p></details></div><h5>Short answer</h5><div class=\"study-sa\"><p class=\"sa-q\">State the core obligations towards the wounded and sick.</p><details class=\"sa-a\"><summary>Model answer</summary><p>They must be respected and protected in all circumstances, meaning they may neither be attacked nor left exposed to harm. They must be treated humanely and cared for, with the least possible delay, by the party in whose power they find themselves. No distinction may be made on grounds other than medical ones, and only urgent medical reasons authorise priority in the order of treatment. Attempts on their lives and violence to their persons are strictly prohibited, including murder, torture, biological experiments and wilfully leaving them without care or exposing them to infection. No medical procedure may be carried out that is not indicated by the person&#x27;s state of health and consistent with accepted medical standards. And a party compelled to abandon wounded to the enemy must so far as military considerations permit leave medical personnel and material with them.</p></details></div><div class=\"study-sa\"><p class=\"sa-q\">Describe the obligations concerning the dead.</p><details class=\"sa-a\"><summary>Model answer</summary><p>Parties must search for the dead after an engagement and prevent their being despoiled or mutilated. Bodies must be examined, where possible medically, before burial or cremation, to confirm death, establish identity and permit a report to be made, with half of a double identity disc remaining on the body. The dead must be honourably interred, if possible according to the rites of their religion, with graves grouped by nationality where possible, properly maintained and marked so they can always be found. Cremation must be avoided except for imperative reasons of hygiene or the religion of the deceased, with the reasons recorded. Each party must organise a Graves Registration Service at the outset of hostilities. Remains and gravesites must be respected, families given access, and remains and personal effects returned.</p></details></div><h4>Class Activity</h4><ol><li>Design a triage protocol for a field clinic in a conflict area. Specify the criteria for priority and include a paragraph explaining to staff why allegiance is legally irrelevant.</li><li>Research how one country has handled the identification of persons who went missing during a past conflict. Write one page on the obligations engaged and how far they were met.</li></ol><h4>Assignment</h4><p>Write one and a half pages on the wounded, sick and shipwrecked. Give the definitions and explain their scope and the qualifying condition, set out the passive and active obligations separately, explain non-discrimination and triage with an example, describe the protection of those who care for the wounded, and set out the obligations concerning the dead and the missing including the right of families to know.</p><p class=\"study-print\"><strong>Printable:</strong> use your browser\u2019s Print \u2192 Save as PDF to keep an offline copy.</p></div>",
  "Prisoners of War": "<div class=\"study-note\"><div class=\"revision-banner\"><strong>Human Rights and International Humanitarian Law</strong><span>Module 9: Protected Persons and Objects</span></div><h3 class=\"lesson-note-title\">LESSON NOTE: PRISONERS OF WAR</h3><div class=\"table-wrap\"><table class=\"study-table\"><thead><tr><th>Subject</th><th>Topic</th><th>Level</th><th>Lesson duration</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Human Rights and International Humanitarian Law</td><td>Prisoners of War</td><td>Beginner to Advanced</td><td>45 minutes</td></tr></tbody></table></div><h4>Learning objectives</h4><p>By the end of this lesson you should be able to:</p><ul><li>Identify who is entitled to prisoner of war status</li><li>State the fundamental rules of treatment in the Third Geneva Convention</li><li>Explain the questioning, discipline and trial rules</li><li>Explain release and repatriation and the position of those denied status</li></ul><h4>1. Introduction</h4><p>The Third Geneva Convention is the longest of the four, with 143 articles, because it regulates a relationship that may last years. Its central idea is simple and is worth stating at the outset: captivity is not punishment. A prisoner of war has committed no crime by fighting, and internment exists solely to prevent their return to the fight.</p><h4>2. Who qualifies</h4><p>Geneva Convention III Article 4A lists six categories.</p><ol><li>Members of the armed forces of a party to the conflict, and members of militias or volunteer corps forming part of those armed forces.</li><li>Members of other militias and volunteer corps, including organised resistance movements, belonging to a party and operating in or outside their own territory even if that territory is occupied, provided they satisfy four conditions: being commanded by a person responsible for their subordinates; having a fixed distinctive sign recognisable at a distance; carrying arms openly; and conducting their operations in accordance with the laws and customs of war.</li><li>Members of regular armed forces professing allegiance to a government or authority not recognised by the detaining power.</li><li>Persons who accompany the armed forces without being members, such as civilian supply contractors, war correspondents and welfare personnel, provided they have authorisation and an identity card.</li><li>Members of crews of the merchant marine and civil aircraft of the parties.</li><li>Inhabitants of a non-occupied territory who on the approach of the enemy spontaneously take up arms to resist the invading forces, without having had time to form themselves into regular armed units, provided they carry arms openly and respect the laws and customs of war.</li></ol><p>Additional Protocol I Article 44(3) modifies the position for its parties. Recognising that in some situations an armed combatant cannot distinguish themselves, it provides that they retain combatant status provided they carry arms openly during each military engagement and during such time as they are visible to the adversary while engaged in a military deployment preceding the launching of an attack.</p><div class=\"study-callout\"><strong>Note:</strong> Article 5 of the Third Convention supplies the crucial procedural rule. Should any doubt arise as to whether a person having committed a belligerent act and fallen into enemy hands belongs to any of the Article 4 categories, that person enjoys the protection of the Convention until their status has been determined by a competent tribunal. Executive assertion is not enough: a tribunal must decide. This provision is central to any problem involving contested status.</div><h4>3. Fundamental rules of treatment</h4><div class=\"table-wrap\"><table class=\"study-table\"><thead><tr><th>Article</th><th>Rule</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>12</td><td>Prisoners are in the hands of the detaining power, not the individuals who captured them; the state is responsible for their treatment</td></tr><tr><td>13</td><td>Must at all times be humanely treated; any unlawful act or omission causing death or seriously endangering health is a grave breach; must be protected against violence, intimidation, insults and public curiosity; reprisals are prohibited</td></tr><tr><td>14</td><td>Entitled in all circumstances to respect for their persons and honour; women must be treated with all regard due to their sex</td></tr><tr><td>15</td><td>The detaining power must provide free of charge for their maintenance and the medical attention their state of health requires</td></tr><tr><td>16</td><td>All prisoners must be treated alike, without adverse distinction, subject to privileged treatment on grounds of health, age or professional qualifications</td></tr><tr><td>17</td><td>Bound to give only surname, first names, rank, date of birth and army or serial number; no physical or mental torture or any other form of coercion may be inflicted to secure information; those who refuse may not be threatened, insulted or exposed to unpleasant or disadvantageous treatment</td></tr></tbody></table></div><p>Detailed provisions then govern quarters, food and clothing, hygiene and medical attention, religious and intellectual activities, discipline, labour, financial resources, relations with the exterior and relations with the authorities.</p><ol><li><strong>Labour.</strong> Officers may not be compelled to work. Non-commissioned officers may be required only to do supervisory work. Other ranks may be required to work, but only in prescribed categories, and never on work of a military character or purpose, nor on unhealthy or dangerous work unless they volunteer. They must be paid.</li><li><strong>Correspondence.</strong> Prisoners must be allowed to send and receive letters and cards, and to receive relief shipments. A capture card must be sent within one week of arrival at a camp.</li><li><strong>Representation.</strong> Prisoners elect a prisoners' representative who deals with the authorities on their behalf.</li><li><strong>Access.</strong> Representatives of the Protecting Power and of the ICRC must be permitted to go to all places where prisoners are held and to interview them without witnesses.</li></ol><h4>4. Discipline and trial</h4><div class=\"table-wrap\"><table class=\"study-table\"><thead><tr><th>Feature</th><th>Rule</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Applicable law</td><td>Prisoners are subject to the laws, regulations and orders in force in the armed forces of the detaining power</td></tr><tr><td>Disciplinary preferred</td><td>Where a choice exists, disciplinary rather than judicial measures must be preferred</td></tr><tr><td>Disciplinary punishments</td><td>Limited to a fine, discontinuance of privileges above treatment provided by the Convention, fatigue duties not exceeding two hours daily, and confinement; maximum thirty days</td></tr><tr><td>Escape</td><td>Successful escape means no punishment for earlier escape-related offences; unsuccessful escape is liable only to disciplinary punishment; weapons may be used against escaping prisoners only as an extreme measure and always preceded by warnings</td></tr><tr><td>Judicial guarantees</td><td>Trial only by a court offering the essential guarantees of independence and impartiality; the same courts and procedures as for members of the detaining power's own forces; right to defence by qualified counsel, to an interpreter, to call witnesses and to appeal</td></tr><tr><td>No retroactivity</td><td>No prisoner may be tried for an act not forbidden by the law of the detaining power or by international law at the time it was committed</td></tr><tr><td>Death penalty</td><td>Where imposed, may not be carried out before six months from the notification to the Protecting Power</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"study-callout\"><strong>Note:</strong> The most important consequence of prisoner of war status is combatant immunity: a prisoner may not be prosecuted for lawful acts of war, including killing enemy soldiers. They may be prosecuted for war crimes and for ordinary crimes, but not for participating in hostilities. This is the practical meaning of the statement that captivity is not punishment.</div><h4>5. Release and repatriation</h4><ol><li><strong>General rule.</strong> Article 118 requires that prisoners be released and repatriated without delay after the cessation of active hostilities.</li><li><strong>Direct repatriation during hostilities.</strong> Article 109 and 110 require repatriation of the seriously wounded and seriously sick, whose condition has deteriorated to the point that recovery within a year is not expected, and permit accommodation in a neutral country for others.</li><li><strong>Non-refoulement.</strong> The obligation to repatriate is now read subject to the principle that a prisoner may not be returned where they face a real risk of persecution or torture. This was contested at the time of Korea and is now generally accepted.</li><li><strong>Continued detention.</strong> A prisoner against whom criminal proceedings for an indictable offence are pending may be detained until the end of the proceedings and if necessary until completion of the punishment.</li><li><strong>Costs.</strong> The detaining power bears the costs of repatriation.</li></ol><h4>6. Persons denied prisoner of war status</h4><p>A person who takes part in hostilities but does not qualify under Article 4, and does not benefit from Article 44(3), is not a prisoner of war. This raises the question of what protection they have, which is frequently examined.</p><ol><li>They are not outside the law. Additional Protocol I Article 45 provides that a person who has taken part in hostilities and is not entitled to prisoner of war status has the right to assert entitlement before a judicial tribunal, and in the meantime is presumed a prisoner of war if they claim the status or appear so entitled.</li><li>Article 75 of Additional Protocol I provides fundamental guarantees to any person in the power of a party who does not benefit from more favourable treatment: humane treatment, prohibition of murder, torture, corporal punishment, mutilation, outrages upon personal dignity, hostage-taking and collective punishment, and detailed fair trial guarantees. Article 75 is widely accepted as customary.</li><li>Where the Fourth Convention applies, they may be a protected person under it, with the security derogations in Article 5 available to the detaining power but the fundamental guarantees preserved.</li><li>Human rights law applies in parallel.</li><li>The consequence is that such a person may be prosecuted under domestic law for taking up arms, since they have no combatant immunity, but may not be denied humane treatment or a fair trial. The term 'unlawful combatant' describes this position but does not create a category outside the law.</li></ol><h4>7. How the examiner&#x27;s question should be answered</h4><p>Work through the Article 4 categories rather than asserting a conclusion on status, and apply the four conditions individually where category two is in play. Always mention Article 5 and the competent tribunal requirement where status is contested. On treatment, give Articles 13, 14, 16 and 17 by number and explain what Article 17 permits by way of questioning, since that is the operationally significant rule. State combatant immunity expressly. On persons denied status, give Article 45, Article 75 and the customary status of Article 75, and say clearly that there is no gap in protection.</p><h4>Summary</h4><ul><li>Article 4 lists six categories entitled to prisoner of war status, with four conditions applying to militias and resistance movements.</li><li>Where status is in doubt, Article 5 requires that the person be treated as a prisoner of war until a competent tribunal determines otherwise.</li><li>Prisoners must be humanely treated at all times, maintained free of charge, and are bound to give only name, rank, date of birth and serial number.</li><li>No coercion may be used to obtain information, and those who refuse may not be threatened or disadvantaged.</li><li>Combatant immunity means a prisoner may not be prosecuted for lawful acts of war, though war crimes and ordinary crimes remain prosecutable.</li><li>Persons denied status retain the fundamental guarantees of Article 75, which is customary, so there is no category outside the law.</li></ul><h4>Key Terms</h4><div class=\"table-wrap\"><table class=\"study-table\"><thead><tr><th>Term</th><th>Meaning</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Prisoner of war</td><td>A person falling within one of the six Article 4 categories who has fallen into the power of the enemy.</td></tr><tr><td>Combatant immunity</td><td>Immunity from prosecution for lawful acts of war, attaching to those entitled to combatant status.</td></tr><tr><td>Article 5 tribunal</td><td>The competent tribunal that must determine status where doubt arises.</td></tr><tr><td>Capture card</td><td>The notification a prisoner must be permitted to send within one week of arrival at a camp.</td></tr><tr><td>Article 75 guarantees</td><td>The customary fundamental guarantees owed to any person in the power of a party to a conflict.</td></tr></tbody></table></div><h4>Review Questions</h4><h5>Multiple choice</h5><div class=\"study-mcq\"><p class=\"mcq-q\">A captured fighter&#x27;s entitlement to prisoner of war status is disputed. What must happen?</p><ol type=\"A\"><li>They are treated as a prisoner of war until a competent tribunal determines their status</li><li>The detaining authority decides administratively</li><li>They are treated as a civilian</li><li>They are released</li></ol><details class=\"mcq-a\"><summary>Show answer</summary><p><strong>Answer: A.</strong> Article 5 requires a tribunal; an executive assertion of status is not sufficient.</p></details></div><div class=\"study-mcq\"><p class=\"mcq-q\">What information may a prisoner of war be required to give?</p><ol type=\"A\"><li>Surname, first names, rank, date of birth and army or serial number</li><li>Everything relevant to military operations</li><li>Unit dispositions</li><li>Nothing at all</li></ol><details class=\"mcq-a\"><summary>Show answer</summary><p><strong>Answer: A.</strong> No physical or mental torture or any other coercion may be used, and those who refuse may not be threatened or disadvantaged.</p></details></div><div class=\"study-mcq\"><p class=\"mcq-q\">What is the practical meaning of combatant immunity?</p><ol type=\"A\"><li>A prisoner may not be prosecuted for lawful acts of war such as killing enemy soldiers</li><li>A prisoner cannot be detained</li><li>A prisoner is immune from all prosecution</li><li>A prisoner may not be questioned</li></ol><details class=\"mcq-a\"><summary>Show answer</summary><p><strong>Answer: A.</strong> War crimes and ordinary crimes remain prosecutable; only participation in hostilities is immune.</p></details></div><div class=\"study-mcq\"><p class=\"mcq-q\">What protection has a person who took part in hostilities but is denied prisoner of war status?</p><ol type=\"A\"><li>The customary fundamental guarantees of Additional Protocol I Article 75, including humane treatment and fair trial</li><li>None; they are outside the law</li><li>Only domestic law</li><li>Full prisoner of war protection regardless</li></ol><details class=\"mcq-a\"><summary>Show answer</summary><p><strong>Answer: A.</strong> Article 45 also gives a right to assert entitlement before a judicial tribunal, with a presumption of status in the meantime.</p></details></div><h5>Short answer</h5><div class=\"study-sa\"><p class=\"sa-q\">State the four conditions applying to militias and resistance movements under Article 4A(2).</p><details class=\"sa-a\"><summary>Model answer</summary><p>They must be commanded by a person responsible for their subordinates, which requires a genuine command structure capable of enforcing discipline. They must have a fixed distinctive sign recognisable at a distance, which need not be a uniform but must be identifiable. They must carry arms openly. And they must conduct their operations in accordance with the laws and customs of war. All four must be satisfied. For parties to Additional Protocol I, Article 44(3) modifies the position where a combatant cannot distinguish themselves, allowing them to retain status provided they carry arms openly during each military engagement and while visible to the adversary during a deployment preceding an attack.</p></details></div><div class=\"study-sa\"><p class=\"sa-q\">Explain what happens to prisoners of war at the end of hostilities.</p><details class=\"sa-a\"><summary>Model answer</summary><p>Article 118 requires that prisoners be released and repatriated without delay after the cessation of active hostilities, and the detaining power bears the costs. During hostilities, the seriously wounded and seriously sick whose recovery is not expected within a year must be directly repatriated, and others may be accommodated in a neutral country. The general obligation is now read subject to non-refoulement, so a prisoner may not be returned against their will where they face a real risk of persecution or torture; this was contested at the time of the Korean War and is now generally accepted. A prisoner against whom proceedings for an indictable offence are pending may be detained until those proceedings, and if necessary any resulting punishment, are complete.</p></details></div><h4>Class Activity</h4><ol><li>Read Article 17 of the Third Geneva Convention. Write a one-page instruction for interrogators setting out what questioning is permitted and what is prohibited, with examples of each.</li><li>Take a contested detention from any conflict and assess whether the detainees should have been given an Article 5 tribunal, explaining what difference it would have made.</li></ol><h4>Assignment</h4><p>Write two pages on prisoners of war. Set out the six Article 4 categories with the four conditions applying to militias, explain the Article 5 tribunal requirement, state the fundamental rules of treatment by article number including the limits on questioning, explain combatant immunity and the rules on discipline and trial, describe release and repatriation with the non-refoulement qualification, and explain the protection available to persons denied prisoner of war status.</p><p class=\"study-print\"><strong>Printable:</strong> use your browser\u2019s Print \u2192 Save as PDF to keep an offline copy.</p></div>",
  "Medical Personnel and Facilities": "<div class=\"study-note\"><div class=\"revision-banner\"><strong>Human Rights and International Humanitarian Law</strong><span>Module 9: Protected Persons and Objects</span></div><h3 class=\"lesson-note-title\">LESSON NOTE: MEDICAL PERSONNEL AND FACILITIES</h3><div class=\"table-wrap\"><table class=\"study-table\"><thead><tr><th>Subject</th><th>Topic</th><th>Level</th><th>Lesson duration</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Human Rights and International Humanitarian Law</td><td>Medical Personnel and Facilities</td><td>Beginner to Advanced</td><td>40 minutes</td></tr></tbody></table></div><h4>Learning objectives</h4><p>By the end of this lesson you should be able to:</p><ul><li>Identify who counts as medical personnel and what units are protected</li><li>State the protection owed and the conditions on which it may be lost</li><li>Explain the rules on the distinctive emblems and their misuse</li><li>Describe the protection of medical transports and the problem of attacks on health care</li></ul><h4>1. Introduction</h4><p>Protecting medical services is protection of a second order: the point is not the personnel themselves but the wounded they treat. That is why the protection is conditional on the medical function and why its loss is governed by such careful rules.</p><h4>2. Who and what is protected</h4><div class=\"table-wrap\"><table class=\"study-table\"><thead><tr><th>Category</th><th>Definition</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Medical personnel</td><td>Persons assigned by a party exclusively to medical purposes, to the administration of medical units, or to the operation or administration of medical transports; may be permanent or temporary, military or civilian</td></tr><tr><td>Religious personnel</td><td>Military or civilian persons such as chaplains exclusively engaged in ministry and attached to armed forces, medical units or civil defence organisations</td></tr><tr><td>Medical units</td><td>Establishments and other units, military or civilian, organised for medical purposes: hospitals, blood transfusion centres, preventive medicine centres, medical depots and pharmaceutical stores</td></tr><tr><td>Medical transports</td><td>Any means of transportation assigned exclusively to medical transport and under the control of a competent authority: ambulances, hospital ships, medical aircraft, trains</td></tr></tbody></table></div><p>The key word throughout is 'exclusively'. A vehicle used sometimes for ammunition and sometimes for casualties is not a medical transport. A building used as a hospital and also as a command post is not a protected medical unit.</p><h4>3. The protection</h4><ol><li><strong>Respect and protect in all circumstances.</strong> Medical units and personnel may not be attacked and must be allowed to carry out their functions.</li><li><strong>No interference.</strong> Parties must not interfere with medical activities, and medical personnel must be granted all available help in performing their duties.</li><li><strong>No compulsion to act contrary to ethics.</strong> Medical personnel may not be compelled to perform acts contrary to medical ethics, nor to refrain from acts required by them.</li><li><strong>No punishment for medical activity.</strong> No person may be punished for carrying out medical activities compatible with medical ethics, whoever the beneficiary.</li><li><strong>Limited disclosure duty.</strong> Medical personnel may not be compelled to give information concerning the wounded and sick in their care where such information would prove harmful to them or their families, subject to compulsory notification of communicable diseases.</li><li><strong>Retention.</strong> Captured medical and religious personnel are not prisoners of war. They may be retained only if the state of health and the number of prisoners require it, and while retained they must be permitted to continue their medical work and receive at least the benefits of the Third Convention. Those not retained must be returned.</li></ol><h4>4. Loss of protection</h4><p>Protection ceases only if the unit or personnel are used to commit, outside their humanitarian function, acts harmful to the enemy. Two conditions attach and both must be satisfied.</p><ol><li><strong>The act must be harmful to the enemy and outside the humanitarian function.</strong> Treating enemy wounded is never such an act. Using the unit as a firing position, an ammunition store, a shelter for able-bodied combatants or an observation post is.</li><li><strong>A warning must be given, with a reasonable time limit, and disregarded.</strong> Additional Protocol I Article 13(1) and Geneva Convention I Article 21 both require this. The warning must set a time limit wherever appropriate, and protection ceases only after that limit expires unheeded.</li></ol><p>Geneva Convention I Article 22 lists conditions that do not deprive a unit of protection, and this list decides many problem questions.</p><ul><li>That the personnel are armed and use the arms in their own defence or that of the wounded and sick in their charge.</li><li>That the unit is protected by a picket, sentries or an escort in the absence of armed orderlies.</li><li>That small arms and ammunition taken from the wounded and sick, and not yet handed to the proper service, are found in the unit.</li><li>That personnel and material of the veterinary service are found in the unit without forming an integral part of it.</li><li>That the humanitarian activities of the unit extend to the care of civilian wounded and sick.</li></ul><div class=\"study-callout\"><strong>Example:</strong> Soldiers guard a field hospital with rifles and shoot at looters attempting to enter. The hospital retains its protection: Article 22 expressly permits arms used in defence of the unit and its patients. Change the facts so that a mortar team fires from the hospital courtyard. That is an act harmful to the enemy outside the humanitarian function, and protection may be lost, but only after a warning setting a reasonable time limit has gone unheeded. Even then the wounded inside remain protected, and the ordinary rules of proportionality and precaution apply with full force to any attack.</div><h4>5. The distinctive emblems</h4><div class=\"table-wrap\"><table class=\"study-table\"><thead><tr><th>Emblem</th><th>Instrument</th><th>Note</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Red cross on white</td><td>1864 onwards</td><td>The original; a reversal of the Swiss flag</td></tr><tr><td>Red crescent</td><td>Recognised in 1929</td><td>Adopted by states that objected to the cross</td></tr><tr><td>Red lion and sun</td><td>Recognised in 1929</td><td>No longer used since Iran adopted the crescent in 1980</td></tr><tr><td>Red crystal</td><td>Additional Protocol III, 2005</td><td>Free of religious or political connotation; equal status</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"table-wrap\"><table class=\"study-table\"><thead><tr><th>Use</th><th>Meaning</th><th>Size</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Protective</td><td>Signals that the person or object is protected under the Conventions</td><td>As large as possible; must be visible from a distance</td></tr><tr><td>Indicative</td><td>Shows a connection with the Movement, outside armed conflict use</td><td>Small; must not be confused with the protective use</td></tr></tbody></table></div><ol><li><strong>Misuse in general</strong> is prohibited: use by anyone not entitled, or for any purpose other than that authorised.</li><li><strong>Improper use to feign protected status while conducting hostilities is perfidy.</strong> Additional Protocol I Article 37 lists the feigning of protected status by the use of signs, emblems or uniforms as perfidy, and Article 85(3)(f) makes perfidious use of the emblem resulting in death or serious injury a grave breach.</li><li><strong>States must legislate</strong> to prevent and repress misuse at all times, including in peacetime, and to control the use of the name and emblem.</li><li><strong>The emblem does not confer protection; it signals it.</strong> A medical unit is protected because it is a medical unit, not because it displays an emblem, and its absence does not license attack where the medical character is known or should be known.</li></ol><h4>6. Medical transports and attacks on health care</h4><ul><li>Medical transports must be respected and protected. Medical aircraft flying over areas controlled by an adverse party require prior agreement, which is a practical constraint on their use.</li><li>Hospital ships may not be attacked or captured; their names and descriptions must be notified ten days before employment.</li><li>Attacks on medical facilities and personnel have become a defining feature of recent conflicts. The Security Council adopted resolution 2286 in 2016 condemning attacks on medical personnel and facilities in armed conflict and demanding compliance.</li><li>The ICRC's Health Care in Danger initiative documented that the most damaging effect is not the immediate casualties but the withdrawal of health services from an area, which multiplies deaths from untreated conditions over months and years.</li><li>The Rome Statute makes intentionally directing attacks against hospitals and places where the sick and wounded are collected, provided they are not military objectives, a war crime in both international and non-international armed conflict.</li></ul><div class=\"study-callout\"><strong>Note:</strong> The practical lesson from the Health Care in Danger work is that the reverberating effects of an attack on a hospital dwarf the direct ones. When a hospital closes, deliveries become unattended, chronic conditions go untreated, immunisation stops and the effects persist long after the conflict. Any proportionality assessment involving a medical facility that ignores this is inadequate.</div><h4>7. How the examiner&#x27;s question should be answered</h4><p>Define the categories and stress the word 'exclusively'. Set out the protection, including the rules on retention of captured medical personnel, which candidates often miss. On loss of protection, give both conditions and then run the Article 22 list, because problem questions are usually built around one of those five situations. On emblems, distinguish protective from indicative use and state that the emblem signals rather than confers protection. Close with the reverberating effects point, since it converts a descriptive answer into an analytical one.</p><h4>Summary</h4><ul><li>Medical and religious personnel, units and transports are protected where they are assigned exclusively to medical or religious functions.</li><li>They must be respected and protected, may not be compelled to act against medical ethics, and may not be punished for treating anyone.</li><li>Captured medical personnel are not prisoners of war and may be retained only while needed to care for prisoners.</li><li>Protection is lost only for acts harmful to the enemy outside the humanitarian function, and only after a warning with a reasonable time limit goes unheeded.</li><li>Being armed for self-defence, having a sentry, or treating civilian wounded do not deprive a unit of protection.</li><li>The emblem signals rather than confers protection; perfidious use causing death or serious injury is a grave breach.</li></ul><h4>Key Terms</h4><div class=\"table-wrap\"><table class=\"study-table\"><thead><tr><th>Term</th><th>Meaning</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Medical personnel</td><td>Persons assigned exclusively to medical purposes, units or transports by a party to the conflict.</td></tr><tr><td>Acts harmful to the enemy</td><td>Conduct outside the humanitarian function that can cause protection to be lost after warning.</td></tr><tr><td>Protective use of the emblem</td><td>Display signalling that a person or object is protected under the Conventions.</td></tr><tr><td>Retained personnel</td><td>Captured medical and religious personnel kept to care for prisoners, who are not themselves prisoners of war.</td></tr><tr><td>Health Care in Danger</td><td>The ICRC initiative documenting attacks on health care and their reverberating effects.</td></tr></tbody></table></div><h4>Review Questions</h4><h5>Multiple choice</h5><div class=\"study-mcq\"><p class=\"mcq-q\">A field hospital&#x27;s guards use rifles to repel looters. What is the effect on its protection?</p><ol type=\"A\"><li>None; arms used in defence of the unit and its patients do not deprive it of protection</li><li>Protection is lost immediately</li><li>Protection is lost after a warning</li><li>The unit becomes a military objective</li></ol><details class=\"mcq-a\"><summary>Show answer</summary><p><strong>Answer: A.</strong> Geneva Convention I Article 22 lists this expressly among the conditions that do not deprive a unit of protection.</p></details></div><div class=\"study-mcq\"><p class=\"mcq-q\">What must happen before a medical unit loses protection for acts harmful to the enemy?</p><ol type=\"A\"><li>A warning must be given, setting a reasonable time limit where appropriate, and go unheeded</li><li>Nothing; protection ceases automatically</li><li>A tribunal must so determine</li><li>The ICRC must be notified</li></ol><details class=\"mcq-a\"><summary>Show answer</summary><p><strong>Answer: A.</strong> Both conditions are required: the act must be harmful and outside the humanitarian function, and the warning must expire unheeded.</p></details></div><div class=\"study-mcq\"><p class=\"mcq-q\">What is the status of captured medical personnel?</p><ol type=\"A\"><li>They are not prisoners of war and may be retained only while needed to care for prisoners</li><li>They are prisoners of war like any combatant</li><li>They must be released immediately in all cases</li><li>They may be prosecuted for treating enemy wounded</li></ol><details class=\"mcq-a\"><summary>Show answer</summary><p><strong>Answer: A.</strong> While retained they must be permitted to continue their medical work and receive at least the benefits of the Third Convention.</p></details></div><div class=\"study-mcq\"><p class=\"mcq-q\">Does the absence of a distinctive emblem permit an attack on a medical facility?</p><ol type=\"A\"><li>No; the emblem signals protection rather than conferring it, and the medical character may be known otherwise</li><li>Yes, protection depends on display</li><li>Yes, unless the ICRC has been notified</li><li>Only in non-international armed conflict</li></ol><details class=\"mcq-a\"><summary>Show answer</summary><p><strong>Answer: A.</strong> Perfidious use of the emblem causing death or serious injury is separately a grave breach.</p></details></div><h5>Short answer</h5><div class=\"study-sa\"><p class=\"sa-q\">State the conditions under which a medical unit loses its protection.</p><details class=\"sa-a\"><summary>Model answer</summary><p>Two conditions must both be satisfied. First, the unit must be used to commit, outside its humanitarian function, acts harmful to the enemy: using it as a firing position, an ammunition store, an observation post or a shelter for able-bodied combatants would qualify, whereas treating enemy wounded never does. Second, a warning must be given, setting a reasonable time limit wherever appropriate, and that limit must expire without the harmful use ceasing. Even then, the wounded and sick inside remain protected, and any attack must still satisfy proportionality and the obligations of precaution. Geneva Convention I Article 22 lists conditions that do not deprive a unit of protection, including that personnel are armed for self-defence, that a sentry or escort is present, that arms taken from the wounded are held, and that civilian wounded are treated.</p></details></div><div class=\"study-sa\"><p class=\"sa-q\">Distinguish the protective from the indicative use of the distinctive emblem.</p><details class=\"sa-a\"><summary>Model answer</summary><p>Protective use signals that the person, unit or transport displaying the emblem is protected under the Geneva Conventions and may not be attacked. It is used during armed conflict, and the emblem must be as large as possible and clearly visible from a distance so that it can be recognised in time to prevent an attack. Indicative use shows a connection with the International Red Cross and Red Crescent Movement, typically by a national society outside armed conflict, and the emblem must be small so it cannot be confused with protective use. Misuse of either kind is prohibited and states must legislate to prevent it, and using the emblem to feign protected status while conducting hostilities is perfidy, which is a grave breach where it results in death or serious injury.</p></details></div><h4>Class Activity</h4><ol><li>Take a reported attack on a hospital in any conflict. Assess whether the two conditions for loss of protection were satisfied and what evidence would be needed to establish each.</li><li>Design a one-page emblem policy for a hospital in a conflict area, covering who may display it, in what size, and what conduct within the facility would put its protection at risk.</li></ol><h4>Assignment</h4><p>Write one and a half pages on medical personnel and facilities. Define the protected categories and explain the significance of exclusivity, set out the protection owed including the rules on retained personnel, state the two conditions for loss of protection and the Article 22 list of conditions that do not cause loss, explain the distinctive emblems and the difference between protective and indicative use, and discuss attacks on health care with attention to reverberating effects.</p><p class=\"study-print\"><strong>Printable:</strong> use your browser\u2019s Print \u2192 Save as PDF to keep an offline copy.</p></div>",
  "Detention in Armed Conflict": "<div class=\"study-note\"><div class=\"revision-banner\"><strong>Human Rights and International Humanitarian Law</strong><span>Module 9: Protected Persons and Objects</span></div><h3 class=\"lesson-note-title\">LESSON NOTE: DETENTION IN ARMED CONFLICT</h3><div class=\"table-wrap\"><table class=\"study-table\"><thead><tr><th>Subject</th><th>Topic</th><th>Level</th><th>Lesson duration</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Human Rights and International Humanitarian Law</td><td>Detention in Armed Conflict</td><td>Beginner to Advanced</td><td>45 minutes</td></tr></tbody></table></div><h4>Learning objectives</h4><p>By the end of this lesson you should be able to:</p><ul><li>Distinguish the grounds and regimes for detention in armed conflict</li><li>Explain internment under the Fourth Geneva Convention</li><li>Explain the legal gap concerning detention in non-international conflict</li><li>State the procedural safeguards applicable to all detainees</li></ul><h4>1. Introduction</h4><p>Detention is the point at which international humanitarian law and human rights law most obviously pull in different directions. IHL accepts detention without criminal charge as a normal feature of armed conflict. Human rights law treats it as exceptional and demands judicial review. Reconciling them is the most difficult problem in this module.</p><h4>2. The three regimes</h4><div class=\"table-wrap\"><table class=\"study-table\"><thead><tr><th>Regime</th><th>Who</th><th>Ground</th><th>Instrument</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Prisoner of war</td><td>Combatants and others in Art 4 categories</td><td>Status alone; no individual assessment needed</td><td>GC III</td></tr><tr><td>Internment of protected persons</td><td>Civilians in the hands of a party or occupying power of which they are not nationals</td><td>Imperative reasons of security, individually assessed</td><td>GC IV Arts 41-43, 68, 78-135</td></tr><tr><td>Criminal detention</td><td>Anyone</td><td>Suspicion of an offence, on charge and trial</td><td>GC III, GC IV, AP I Art 75, human rights law</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"study-callout\"><strong>Note:</strong> Confusing these is the most common error. A prisoner of war is detained because of who they are and needs no individual security assessment. A civilian internee is detained because of what they are assessed as likely to do, requires an individual determination, and has a right to review. A criminal detainee is detained because of what they are alleged to have done and has the full fair trial guarantees.</div><h4>3. Internment under the Fourth Convention</h4><ol><li><strong>Ground.</strong> In a party's own territory, Article 41 and 42 permit internment or assigned residence only if the security of the detaining power makes it absolutely necessary. In occupied territory, Article 78 permits it for imperative reasons of security.</li><li><strong>Individual assessment.</strong> Collective internment of a nationality or group is unlawful. The assessment must be of the individual and of the actual threat they pose.</li><li><strong>Initial review.</strong> Article 43 requires that the internment be reconsidered as soon as possible by an appropriate court or administrative board. Article 78 requires a right of appeal decided with the least possible delay.</li><li><strong>Periodic review.</strong> At least twice yearly, with a view to the favourable amendment of the initial decision if circumstances permit.</li><li><strong>Release.</strong> Article 132 requires release as soon as the reasons for internment cease to exist, and Article 133 requires release as soon as possible after the close of hostilities.</li><li><strong>Conditions.</strong> Articles 79 to 135 govern internment in detail: separate accommodation from prisoners of war and from persons deprived of liberty for other reasons, family accommodation where possible, food and clothing sufficient, medical attention, religious and intellectual activity, an internee committee, correspondence and relief, and no internment in areas particularly exposed to the dangers of war.</li></ol><div class=\"study-callout\"><strong>Example:</strong> An occupying power detains four hundred men from a district after an attack on its forces, on the basis that the perpetrators came from the district. This is unlawful on three grounds. It is collective, not individual. No imperative security reason has been established for each detainee. And it is in substance a collective penalty, prohibited by Article 33. The lawful course requires an individual assessment of each person against the imperative security standard, with a right of appeal decided with the least possible delay and review at least twice yearly.</div><h4>4. The gap in non-international armed conflict</h4><p>This is the most contested question in contemporary IHL and is worth stating carefully.</p><ol><li><strong>Common Article 3 and Additional Protocol II presuppose detention</strong>, since Protocol II Article 5 sets out how persons deprived of liberty must be treated, but neither provides grounds for detention or a review procedure.</li><li><strong>One view</strong> is that IHL of non-international armed conflict contains an implicit power to intern, on the reasoning that if the law regulates the treatment of internees it must contemplate their internment, and that the power to use lethal force implies the lesser power to detain.</li><li><strong>The other view</strong> is that IHL provides no legal basis for detention in non-international conflict, so the basis must be found in domestic law or a Security Council mandate, and that human rights law supplies the applicable safeguards including judicial review.</li><li><strong>The ICRC's position</strong> is that whatever the legal basis, the procedural safeguards must be provided: grounds prescribed in advance, an individual decision, information about the reasons, registration, an initial review by an independent and impartial body, periodic review, and release when the grounds cease.</li><li><strong>The practical position</strong> is that states detain in these conflicts and courts differ on the basis. The Copenhagen Process Principles of 2012 attempted to set out good practice for detention in international military operations, though they are not binding and were criticised for weak safeguards.</li></ol><div class=\"study-callout\"><strong>Note:</strong> The correct answer in an examination is to state the gap, give both views fairly, state that the safeguards are required whichever view is taken, and note that human rights law fills much of the space. An answer asserting that IHL clearly authorises or clearly prohibits such detention is overstating a genuinely unsettled question.</div><h4>5. Safeguards applicable to all detainees</h4><div class=\"table-wrap\"><table class=\"study-table\"><thead><tr><th>Safeguard</th><th>Source</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Humane treatment in all circumstances</td><td>Common Art 3; GC III Art 13; GC IV Art 27; AP I Art 75</td></tr><tr><td>Prohibition of torture and cruel, inhuman or degrading treatment</td><td>Common Art 3; AP I Art 75; CAT; ICCPR Art 7</td></tr><tr><td>Registration of detainees and notification of detention</td><td>GC III Art 122-123; GC IV Art 136-141; CED Art 17</td></tr><tr><td>Prohibition of secret detention and of unacknowledged detention</td><td>CED Art 17; customary IHL</td></tr><tr><td>Access by the ICRC</td><td>GC III Art 126; GC IV Art 143; common Art 3 right of initiative</td></tr><tr><td>Contact with family and correspondence</td><td>GC III Art 70-71; GC IV Art 106-107; AP II Art 5(2)(b)</td></tr><tr><td>Separation of men and women, and of children from adults, except family units</td><td>GC IV Art 76, 82; AP I Art 75(5); AP II Art 5(2)(a)</td></tr><tr><td>Adequate food, water, clothing, hygiene and medical care</td><td>GC III Arts 25-32; GC IV Arts 85-92; AP II Art 5(1)</td></tr><tr><td>No detention in areas exposed to the dangers of combat</td><td>GC III Art 23; GC IV Art 83</td></tr><tr><td>Fair trial guarantees where charged</td><td>GC III Arts 82-108; GC IV Arts 64-78; AP I Art 75(4); AP II Art 6</td></tr></tbody></table></div><p>Additional Protocol I Article 75(4) is the fullest statement of fair trial guarantees in IHL and is accepted as customary. It requires an impartial and regularly constituted court respecting the generally recognised principles of regular judicial procedure, and lists prompt information of the charge, necessary rights and means of defence, individual criminal responsibility, no retroactivity, presumption of innocence, right to be present, no compulsion to confess, right to examine witnesses, no double jeopardy, public pronouncement of judgment, and advice on remedies.</p><h4>6. Interaction with human rights law</h4><div class=\"table-wrap\"><table class=\"study-table\"><thead><tr><th>Question</th><th>IHL</th><th>Human rights law</th><th>Result</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Grounds for detaining a prisoner of war</td><td>Status alone suffices</td><td>Would require individual assessment</td><td>IHL governs as lex specialis</td></tr><tr><td>Grounds for interning a civilian</td><td>Imperative security reasons, individually assessed</td><td>Prescribed by law, necessary and proportionate</td><td>Largely convergent</td></tr><tr><td>Review body</td><td>Court or administrative board</td><td>Court, under ICCPR Art 9(4)</td><td>Contested; ICCPR General Comment 35 requires court review</td></tr><tr><td>Detention in non-international conflict</td><td>No express grounds</td><td>Requires legal basis and judicial review</td><td>Human rights law fills the gap</td></tr><tr><td>Conditions of detention</td><td>Detailed rules in GC III and IV</td><td>Art 10 ICCPR, Mandela Rules</td><td>Mutually reinforcing</td></tr></tbody></table></div><p>General Comment 35 of the Human Rights Committee states that security detention presents severe risks of arbitrary deprivation of liberty and that, if used at all, it must be limited to imperative reasons of security, must not last longer than absolutely necessary, must be subject to prompt review by a court, and requires derogation where it departs from Article 9. That position is more demanding than the Fourth Convention's administrative board and is the sharpest point of divergence between the two regimes.</p><h4>7. How the examiner&#x27;s question should be answered</h4><p>Identify which regime applies before anything else, since the grounds and procedures differ entirely. For internment, state the standard as 'imperative reasons of security' or 'absolutely necessary', insist on individual assessment, and give the review timetable. On non-international conflict, present the gap accurately with both views and the ICRC's safeguards position rather than picking a side without argument. Then list the safeguards applicable regardless of regime. Close with the human rights interaction, giving General Comment 35 as the point of divergence.</p><h4>Summary</h4><ul><li>Three detention regimes exist: prisoner of war status, internment of protected persons, and criminal detention, each with different grounds.</li><li>Internment requires an individual assessment against an imperative security standard, with initial review and review at least twice yearly.</li><li>Collective internment of a group or nationality is unlawful and amounts to a collective penalty.</li><li>IHL of non-international armed conflict provides no express grounds or procedure for detention, and the basis is genuinely contested.</li><li>Whatever the basis, procedural safeguards are required: grounds prescribed in advance, individual decision, registration, independent review and release when grounds cease.</li><li>Additional Protocol I Article 75(4) states the fullest fair trial guarantees in IHL and is customary.</li></ul><h4>Key Terms</h4><div class=\"table-wrap\"><table class=\"study-table\"><thead><tr><th>Term</th><th>Meaning</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Internment</td><td>Non-criminal detention of a protected person for imperative reasons of security.</td></tr><tr><td>Imperative reasons of security</td><td>The standard justifying internment in occupied territory, requiring an individual assessment.</td></tr><tr><td>Periodic review</td><td>The obligation to reconsider internment at least twice yearly with a view to release.</td></tr><tr><td>Secret detention</td><td>Unacknowledged detention, prohibited by customary IHL and the Enforced Disappearance Convention.</td></tr><tr><td>Article 75(4)</td><td>The customary statement of fair trial guarantees applicable to any person in the power of a party.</td></tr></tbody></table></div><h4>Review Questions</h4><h5>Multiple choice</h5><div class=\"study-mcq\"><p class=\"mcq-q\">What standard justifies interning a civilian in occupied territory?</p><ol type=\"A\"><li>Imperative reasons of security, assessed individually</li><li>Membership of a suspect community</li><li>Residence in a district where an attack occurred</li><li>An administrative order without reasons</li></ol><details class=\"mcq-a\"><summary>Show answer</summary><p><strong>Answer: A.</strong> Collective internment is unlawful and amounts to a collective penalty prohibited by Article 33.</p></details></div><div class=\"study-mcq\"><p class=\"mcq-q\">How often must internment be reviewed under the Fourth Geneva Convention?</p><ol type=\"A\"><li>At least twice yearly, with a view to favourable amendment of the decision</li><li>Once a year</li><li>Only on the internee&#x27;s application</li><li>Only at the end of hostilities</li></ol><details class=\"mcq-a\"><summary>Show answer</summary><p><strong>Answer: A.</strong> There must also be an initial reconsideration as soon as possible by an appropriate court or administrative board.</p></details></div><div class=\"study-mcq\"><p class=\"mcq-q\">What is the position on detention in non-international armed conflict?</p><ol type=\"A\"><li>IHL provides no express grounds or procedure, and whether it contains an implicit power is genuinely contested</li><li>IHL clearly authorises internment</li><li>IHL clearly prohibits all detention</li><li>The Security Council must authorise each detention</li></ol><details class=\"mcq-a\"><summary>Show answer</summary><p><strong>Answer: A.</strong> The ICRC&#x27;s position is that whatever the basis, procedural safeguards including independent review and periodic review must be provided.</p></details></div><div class=\"study-mcq\"><p class=\"mcq-q\">Which provision states the fullest fair trial guarantees in international humanitarian law?</p><ol type=\"A\"><li>Additional Protocol I Article 75(4)</li><li>Common Article 3</li><li>Geneva Convention III Article 17</li><li>Geneva Convention IV Article 27</li></ol><details class=\"mcq-a\"><summary>Show answer</summary><p><strong>Answer: A.</strong> It is accepted as customary and applies to any person in the power of a party who does not benefit from more favourable treatment.</p></details></div><h5>Short answer</h5><div class=\"study-sa\"><p class=\"sa-q\">Distinguish the three detention regimes in armed conflict.</p><details class=\"sa-a\"><summary>Model answer</summary><p>Prisoner of war detention applies to combatants and others within the Article 4 categories. The ground is status alone, so no individual security assessment is required, and the purpose is simply to prevent return to the fight. Internment applies to civilians in the hands of a party or occupying power of which they are not nationals. The ground is an individual assessment that the person poses a security threat, expressed as absolute necessity in a party&#x27;s own territory and imperative reasons of security in occupied territory, and it carries rights of initial and periodic review. Criminal detention applies to anyone suspected of an offence, requires charge and trial, and carries the full fair trial guarantees. Confusing the three, and in particular treating internment as though it needed no individual justification, is the most common error.</p></details></div><div class=\"study-sa\"><p class=\"sa-q\">Explain the legal gap concerning detention in non-international armed conflict.</p><details class=\"sa-a\"><summary>Model answer</summary><p>Common Article 3 and Additional Protocol II both presuppose that persons will be deprived of liberty, since Protocol II Article 5 regulates their treatment, but neither provides grounds for detention or any review procedure. One view holds that an implicit power to intern exists, reasoning that the law would not regulate the treatment of internees if internment were unlawful, and that the power to use lethal force implies the lesser power to detain. The competing view holds that IHL supplies no legal basis, so it must be found in domestic law or a Security Council mandate, with human rights law supplying safeguards including judicial review. The ICRC&#x27;s position is that whichever view is taken, the safeguards must be provided: grounds prescribed in advance, an individual decision, information about the reasons, registration, independent initial review, periodic review, and release when the grounds cease.</p></details></div><h4>Class Activity</h4><ol><li>Draft an internment review procedure satisfying the Fourth Geneva Convention. Specify the composition of the review body, the timetable, the internee&#x27;s rights and the test to be applied.</li><li>Compare the review requirements in Geneva Convention IV Article 43 with those in ICCPR Article 9(4) as explained in General Comment 35. Write one page on where they diverge and what a state should do.</li></ol><h4>Assignment</h4><p>Write two pages on detention in armed conflict. Distinguish the three regimes with their grounds and procedures, explain internment under the Fourth Geneva Convention including the standard, the requirement of individual assessment and the review timetable, set out the legal gap in non-international armed conflict giving both views and the ICRC&#x27;s safeguards position, list the safeguards applicable to all detainees, and explain the interaction with human rights law with reference to General Comment 35.</p><p class=\"study-print\"><strong>Printable:</strong> use your browser\u2019s Print \u2192 Save as PDF to keep an offline copy.</p></div>",
  "Humanitarian Relief and Access": "<div class=\"study-note\"><div class=\"revision-banner\"><strong>Human Rights and International Humanitarian Law</strong><span>Module 9: Protected Persons and Objects</span></div><h3 class=\"lesson-note-title\">LESSON NOTE: HUMANITARIAN RELIEF AND ACCESS</h3><div class=\"table-wrap\"><table class=\"study-table\"><thead><tr><th>Subject</th><th>Topic</th><th>Level</th><th>Lesson duration</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Human Rights and International Humanitarian Law</td><td>Humanitarian Relief and Access</td><td>Beginner to Advanced</td><td>40 minutes</td></tr></tbody></table></div><h4>Learning objectives</h4><p>By the end of this lesson you should be able to:</p><ul><li>State the obligations concerning humanitarian relief operations</li><li>Explain the consent requirement and when consent may be withheld</li><li>Explain the prohibition of starvation as a method of warfare</li><li>Describe the humanitarian principles and the problem of access</li></ul><h4>1. Introduction</h4><p>The primary obligation to meet the needs of a population lies with the party controlling the territory. Where that party cannot or will not meet them, relief operations by others become necessary, and the law then has to reconcile the population's needs with the state's control over its own territory. That reconciliation is the subject of this lesson.</p><h4>2. The primary obligation</h4><ol><li>In occupied territory, Geneva Convention IV Article 55 requires the occupying power, to the fullest extent of the means available to it, to ensure the food and medical supplies of the population, and to bring in necessary foodstuffs, medical stores and other articles if the resources of the territory are inadequate.</li><li>Article 56 requires the occupying power to ensure and maintain, to the fullest extent of the means available, medical and hospital establishments and services, public health and hygiene.</li><li>Article 60 makes clear that relief consignments in no way relieve the occupying power of its responsibilities under Articles 55, 56 and 59.</li><li>In other situations, Additional Protocol I Article 69 and Protocol II Article 18(2) impose corresponding obligations on the party concerned to provide for the population's survival needs.</li></ol><h4>3. Relief operations and consent</h4><p>Where a population is not adequately provided with supplies essential to its survival, relief actions which are humanitarian and impartial in character and conducted without any adverse distinction shall be undertaken, subject to the agreement of the parties concerned. The formulation appears in Additional Protocol I Article 70(1) and Protocol II Article 18(2).</p><ol><li><strong>Consent is required</strong> from the state concerned. The reference to 'the parties concerned' is generally read as the state on whose territory the operation is to take place, and not as requiring the consent of every party to the conflict.</li><li><strong>Consent may not be arbitrarily withheld.</strong> This is the crucial qualification. A refusal is arbitrary where it is not based on valid reasons, where it is discriminatory, where it violates other obligations such as the prohibition of starvation, or where it is unreasonable in the circumstances.</li><li><strong>Valid reasons for refusal or conditions</strong> include imperative military necessity requiring temporary restriction of movement in a particular area, genuine security concerns about a particular route or convoy, and concerns that the operation is not genuinely impartial.</li><li><strong>Invalid reasons</strong> include denying supplies in order to weaken a population supporting an adversary, using access as a bargaining chip in negotiations, and blanket refusals unrelated to any specific operational concern.</li><li><strong>Right of control.</strong> Parties permitting passage of relief consignments have the right to prescribe technical arrangements, including search, and to make permission conditional on distribution being made under the local supervision of a Protecting Power. They may satisfy themselves that consignments will be used for the population and not diverted.</li><li><strong>Rapid and unimpeded passage.</strong> Parties, including those not party to the conflict, must allow and facilitate rapid and unimpeded passage of all relief consignments, equipment and personnel, and must not divert or delay their forwarding.</li></ol><div class=\"study-callout\"><strong>Note:</strong> The distinction between requiring consent and permitting arbitrary refusal is the whole of the law here. A state may say 'not through that road today because there is fighting on it'. It may not say 'not at all, because the population in that area supports the other side'. Being able to state that distinction precisely, with examples on each side, is what an answer on access requires.</div><h4>4. Relief personnel</h4><ul><li>Additional Protocol I Article 71 provides that where necessary, relief personnel may form part of the assistance provided in any relief action, subject to the approval of the party in whose territory they will carry out their duties.</li><li>Such personnel must be respected and protected.</li><li>Each party receiving relief consignments must to the fullest extent practicable assist relief personnel in carrying out their mission.</li><li>Only in case of imperative military necessity may the activities of relief personnel be limited or their movements temporarily restricted.</li><li>Relief personnel must not exceed the terms of their mission, and must take account of the security requirements of the party in whose territory they are working.</li><li>Attacks on personnel involved in a humanitarian assistance mission, as long as they are entitled to the protection given to civilians, are a war crime under the Rome Statute in both international and non-international armed conflict.</li></ul><h4>5. Starvation as a method of warfare</h4><p>Additional Protocol I Article 54 and Protocol II Article 14 prohibit starvation of civilians as a method of warfare.</p><ol><li><strong>The prohibition of the method.</strong> Starvation of civilians as a method of warfare is prohibited outright.</li><li><strong>Objects indispensable to survival.</strong> It is prohibited to attack, destroy, remove or render useless objects indispensable to the survival of the civilian population, such as foodstuffs, agricultural areas for the production of foodstuffs, crops, livestock, drinking water installations and supplies, and irrigation works.</li><li><strong>The purpose element.</strong> The prohibition applies where the purpose is to deny their sustenance value to the civilian population or to the adverse party, whatever the motive, whether to starve out civilians, to cause them to move away, or for any other motive.</li><li><strong>Limited exception in Protocol I.</strong> Article 54(3) permits action against such objects used solely as sustenance for members of the armed forces, or in direct support of military action, but even then no action may be taken which may be expected to leave the civilian population with such inadequate food or water as to cause its starvation or force its movement.</li><li><strong>Siege warfare.</strong> Sieges are not prohibited as such, but a siege conducted so as to starve the civilian population is unlawful, and the besieging party must allow relief and, under Geneva Convention IV Article 17, endeavour to conclude local agreements for the removal of the wounded, sick, infirm, aged, children and maternity cases from besieged areas.</li><li><strong>Criminality.</strong> Rome Statute Article 8(2)(b)(xxv) makes intentionally using starvation of civilians as a method of warfare a war crime in international armed conflict, and a 2019 amendment extended it to non-international armed conflict.</li></ol><h4>6. The humanitarian principles and access in practice</h4><div class=\"table-wrap\"><table class=\"study-table\"><thead><tr><th>Principle</th><th>Content</th><th>Why it matters for access</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Humanity</td><td>Addressing suffering wherever found, with particular attention to the most vulnerable</td><td>The justification for the operation</td></tr><tr><td>Neutrality</td><td>Not taking sides in hostilities or engaging in controversies</td><td>Without it, parties treat the operation as support for the enemy</td></tr><tr><td>Impartiality</td><td>Assistance on the basis of need alone, without discrimination</td><td>The legal condition for relief operations under AP I Art 70</td></tr><tr><td>Independence</td><td>Autonomy from the political, economic, military or other objectives of any actor</td><td>Distinguishes the operation from a party's own aims</td></tr></tbody></table></div><p>The last three are not merely ethical commitments; they are the practical conditions of access. A party that believes an operation is aiding its adversary will obstruct it, and the principles exist to make that belief unreasonable.</p><ol><li><strong>Counter-terrorism measures</strong> have become a significant obstacle. Sanctions regimes and criminal prohibitions on material support have in some cases exposed humanitarian organisations to legal risk for operating in areas controlled by designated groups. Security Council resolution 2664 of 2022 introduced a humanitarian carve-out across UN sanctions regimes to address this.</li><li><strong>Bureaucratic impediments</strong> are the most common form of obstruction in practice: visa refusals, registration requirements, customs delays, travel permit systems and taxation of relief goods. They achieve denial without an explicit refusal.</li><li><strong>Insecurity</strong> and attacks on aid workers restrict access independently of any state decision.</li><li><strong>Deconfliction</strong>, the sharing of the coordinates of humanitarian facilities with parties to a conflict, is intended to prevent attacks but depends on parties acting on the information.</li></ol><h4>7. How the examiner&#x27;s question should be answered</h4><p>Begin with the primary obligation of the controlling party, since relief is a secondary mechanism. Then state the consent rule and immediately state the arbitrariness qualification, with examples of valid and invalid grounds for refusal. Set out the right of technical control, since it explains why consent is required at all. Then take starvation separately, giving the purpose element and the siege point. Close with the practical obstacles, particularly bureaucratic impediments, because an answer that treats access as purely a question of consent misses how denial actually happens.</p><h4>Summary</h4><ul><li>The party controlling territory bears the primary obligation to meet the population&#x27;s survival needs.</li><li>Relief operations that are humanitarian and impartial require the consent of the state concerned, but consent may not be arbitrarily withheld.</li><li>Valid grounds for refusal include imperative military necessity and genuine security concerns; denying supplies to weaken a population is invalid.</li><li>Parties may prescribe technical arrangements and satisfy themselves that consignments are not diverted, but must allow rapid and unimpeded passage.</li><li>Starvation of civilians as a method of warfare is prohibited, as is attacking objects indispensable to survival, and both are war crimes.</li><li>Bureaucratic impediments, counter-terrorism measures and insecurity are the principal practical obstacles to access.</li></ul><h4>Key Terms</h4><div class=\"table-wrap\"><table class=\"study-table\"><thead><tr><th>Term</th><th>Meaning</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Arbitrary withholding of consent</td><td>Refusal of a relief operation without valid reasons, or that is discriminatory or unreasonable in the circumstances.</td></tr><tr><td>Objects indispensable to survival</td><td>Foodstuffs, crops, livestock, water installations and irrigation works, which may not be attacked or rendered useless.</td></tr><tr><td>Impartiality</td><td>Provision of assistance on the basis of need alone, the legal condition for a relief operation.</td></tr><tr><td>Bureaucratic impediments</td><td>Administrative obstacles such as visa, permit and customs requirements used to obstruct relief.</td></tr><tr><td>Deconfliction</td><td>Sharing coordinates of humanitarian facilities with parties to prevent attacks on them.</td></tr></tbody></table></div><h4>Review Questions</h4><h5>Multiple choice</h5><div class=\"study-mcq\"><p class=\"mcq-q\">May a state refuse consent to a humanitarian relief operation?</p><ol type=\"A\"><li>Only on valid grounds; consent may not be arbitrarily withheld</li><li>Yes, for any reason, since it is sovereign</li><li>No; consent is never required</li><li>Only with Security Council approval</li></ol><details class=\"mcq-a\"><summary>Show answer</summary><p><strong>Answer: A.</strong> Imperative military necessity or genuine security concerns about a route may justify refusal or conditions; denying supplies to weaken a population may not.</p></details></div><div class=\"study-mcq\"><p class=\"mcq-q\">Which is an invalid ground for refusing relief?</p><ol type=\"A\"><li>Denying supplies to weaken a population that supports the adversary</li><li>Fighting on the proposed route today</li><li>Genuine doubt about a particular convoy&#x27;s impartiality</li><li>A requirement to search consignments</li></ol><details class=\"mcq-a\"><summary>Show answer</summary><p><strong>Answer: A.</strong> Parties retain a right of technical control including search, but may not use access as a weapon or a bargaining chip.</p></details></div><div class=\"study-mcq\"><p class=\"mcq-q\">What does the prohibition of starvation as a method of warfare cover?</p><ol type=\"A\"><li>Attacking, destroying, removing or rendering useless objects indispensable to civilian survival for the purpose of denying their sustenance value</li><li>Only deliberate mass killing by famine</li><li>Only sieges</li><li>Only attacks on food convoys</li></ol><details class=\"mcq-a\"><summary>Show answer</summary><p><strong>Answer: A.</strong> The prohibition applies whatever the motive, and a 2019 amendment extended the war crime to non-international armed conflict.</p></details></div><div class=\"study-mcq\"><p class=\"mcq-q\">What is the most common practical obstacle to humanitarian access?</p><ol type=\"A\"><li>Bureaucratic impediments such as visa refusals, permits, customs delays and registration requirements</li><li>Explicit refusals of consent</li><li>Lack of funding</li><li>Absence of treaty obligations</li></ol><details class=\"mcq-a\"><summary>Show answer</summary><p><strong>Answer: A.</strong> They achieve denial without any explicit refusal, which makes them harder to challenge legally.</p></details></div><h5>Short answer</h5><div class=\"study-sa\"><p class=\"sa-q\">Explain the consent requirement for relief operations and when refusal is arbitrary.</p><details class=\"sa-a\"><summary>Model answer</summary><p>Relief actions that are humanitarian and impartial in character and conducted without adverse distinction must be undertaken where a population is not adequately provided with supplies essential to its survival, subject to the agreement of the party concerned. Consent is therefore required, but it may not be arbitrarily withheld. A refusal is arbitrary where it rests on no valid reason, where it is discriminatory, where it would breach another obligation such as the prohibition of starvation, or where it is unreasonable in the circumstances. Valid grounds include imperative military necessity requiring temporary restriction in a particular area, genuine security concerns about a specific route or convoy, and doubts about the impartiality of the operation. Invalid grounds include withholding supplies to weaken a population supporting an adversary and using access as leverage in negotiations.</p></details></div><div class=\"study-sa\"><p class=\"sa-q\">Set out the prohibition of starvation as a method of warfare.</p><details class=\"sa-a\"><summary>Model answer</summary><p>Starvation of civilians as a method of warfare is prohibited outright by Additional Protocol I Article 54 and Protocol II Article 14. It is prohibited to attack, destroy, remove or render useless objects indispensable to the survival of the civilian population, including foodstuffs, agricultural areas producing them, crops, livestock, drinking water installations and supplies, and irrigation works, where the purpose is to deny their sustenance value to civilians or to the adverse party, whatever the motive. Protocol I permits limited action against such objects used solely to sustain armed forces or in direct support of military action, but not where it may be expected to leave the civilian population with food or water so inadequate as to cause starvation or force movement. Sieges are not prohibited as such, but a siege conducted so as to starve civilians is unlawful, and intentionally using starvation as a method of warfare is a war crime in both types of conflict.</p></details></div><h4>Class Activity</h4><ol><li>Take one situation where humanitarian access has been restricted. Classify each restriction as a valid ground, an invalid ground or a bureaucratic impediment, and explain your classification.</li><li>Draft a one-page negotiation brief for a humanitarian organisation seeking access, setting out the legal basis of the request, the technical arrangements you would offer, and your responses to three likely objections.</li></ol><h4>Assignment</h4><p>Write one and a half pages on humanitarian relief and access. State the primary obligation of the controlling party, explain the consent requirement and the arbitrariness qualification with examples on both sides, describe the right of technical control and the duty of rapid and unimpeded passage, set out the prohibition of starvation and its application to sieges, explain the four humanitarian principles as conditions of access, and describe the main practical obstacles.</p><p class=\"study-print\"><strong>Printable:</strong> use your browser\u2019s Print \u2192 Save as PDF to keep an offline copy.</p></div>",
  "Cultural Property and the Environment": "<div class=\"study-note\"><div class=\"revision-banner\"><strong>Human Rights and International Humanitarian Law</strong><span>Module 9: Protected Persons and Objects</span></div><h3 class=\"lesson-note-title\">LESSON NOTE: CULTURAL PROPERTY AND THE ENVIRONMENT</h3><div class=\"table-wrap\"><table class=\"study-table\"><thead><tr><th>Subject</th><th>Topic</th><th>Level</th><th>Lesson duration</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Human Rights and International Humanitarian Law</td><td>Cultural Property and the Environment</td><td>Beginner to Advanced</td><td>40 minutes</td></tr></tbody></table></div><h4>Learning objectives</h4><p>By the end of this lesson you should be able to:</p><ul><li>Describe the protection of cultural property and its levels</li><li>Explain the obligations of safeguarding and respect</li><li>State the protection of the natural environment in armed conflict</li><li>Explain works and installations containing dangerous forces</li></ul><h4>1. Introduction</h4><p>These two subjects are grouped because both concern objects protected for reasons beyond their immediate value to any person: cultural property because its destruction is a loss to humanity as a whole, and the environment because its damage outlasts the conflict by generations.</p><h4>2. Cultural property: the instruments</h4><div class=\"table-wrap\"><table class=\"study-table\"><thead><tr><th>Instrument</th><th>Year</th><th>Effect</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Hague Regulations Art 27 and 56</td><td>1907</td><td>Buildings dedicated to religion, art, science and charity, and historic monuments, to be spared as far as possible; seizure and destruction of such property forbidden</td></tr><tr><td>Roerich Pact</td><td>1935</td><td>Regional treaty in the Americas protecting artistic and scientific institutions</td></tr><tr><td>Hague Convention for the Protection of Cultural Property</td><td>1954</td><td>The principal instrument; general protection and special protection; the blue shield emblem</td></tr><tr><td>First Protocol</td><td>1954</td><td>Prevents export of cultural property from occupied territory and requires return</td></tr><tr><td>Second Protocol</td><td>1999</td><td>Enhanced protection; individual criminal responsibility; a Committee; applies in non-international conflict</td></tr><tr><td>Additional Protocol I Art 53 and Protocol II Art 16</td><td>1977</td><td>Prohibits acts of hostility against historic monuments, works of art or places of worship constituting the cultural or spiritual heritage of peoples</td></tr><tr><td>Rome Statute Art 8(2)(b)(ix) and (e)(iv)</td><td>1998</td><td>War crime to direct attacks against buildings dedicated to religion, education, art, science or charitable purposes and historic monuments, in both types of conflict</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"study-defn\"><strong>Cultural property:</strong> Movable or immovable property of great importance to the cultural heritage of every people, such as monuments of architecture, art or history, archaeological sites, works of art, manuscripts, books and scientific collections; buildings whose main purpose is to preserve or exhibit them; and centres containing a large amount of cultural property (1954 Convention Article 1).</div><h4>3. The three levels of protection</h4><div class=\"table-wrap\"><table class=\"study-table\"><thead><tr><th>Level</th><th>Instrument</th><th>Requirements</th><th>Waiver</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>General protection</td><td>1954 Convention Arts 2-4</td><td>Safeguarding in peacetime and respect during conflict; applies to all cultural property</td><td>Where military necessity imperatively requires</td></tr><tr><td>Special protection</td><td>1954 Convention Arts 8-11</td><td>Entry in the International Register; limited to refuges and very important centres, situated at adequate distance from military objectives and not used for military purposes</td><td>Only in exceptional cases of unavoidable military necessity, determined by an officer commanding at least a division</td></tr><tr><td>Enhanced protection</td><td>Second Protocol 1999 Arts 10-14</td><td>Cultural heritage of the greatest importance for humanity; protected by adequate domestic legal and administrative measures; not used for military purposes and a declaration to that effect</td><td>Only if and for as long as it has by its use become a military objective, and only where no feasible alternative exists, by decision of the highest operational level of command, with effective advance warning where circumstances permit</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"study-callout\"><strong>Note:</strong> Special protection has been almost entirely unused, because its conditions, particularly adequate distance from any military objective, proved impossible to satisfy in practice. Enhanced protection under the Second Protocol was designed to replace it, with a tighter waiver and a functioning register. Noting that special protection failed and why is more informative than describing its terms.</div><h4>4. The two duties: safeguard and respect</h4><ol><li><strong>Safeguarding (Article 3).</strong> In peacetime, parties must prepare for the safeguarding of cultural property against the foreseeable effects of armed conflict: inventories, refuges, planning for emergency measures against fire or structural collapse, preparation for removal or in situ protection, and designation of competent authorities.</li><li><strong>Respect (Article 4).</strong> Parties must refrain from any use of cultural property, its immediate surroundings or its protective appliances for purposes likely to expose it to destruction or damage, and must refrain from any act of hostility directed against it. They must prohibit, prevent and if necessary stop theft, pillage, misappropriation and vandalism, and must not requisition movable cultural property.</li><li><strong>Occupation (Article 5).</strong> An occupying power must as far as possible support the competent national authorities in safeguarding and preserving cultural property, and if urgent measures are necessary and the national authorities are unable to take them, must take the most necessary measures of preservation in close cooperation with those authorities.</li><li><strong>Emblem.</strong> The blue shield may be displayed, singly for general protection and in a triangular arrangement of three for special protection.</li></ol><div class=\"study-callout\"><strong>Example:</strong> An army establishes a command post inside a UNESCO-listed mosque. Two separate breaches occur. The party using the site breaches Article 4(1) by using cultural property for a purpose likely to expose it to destruction. The other party may in principle attack it, since it has become a military objective by use, but only if the waiver conditions are met, and if the site has enhanced protection the decision must be taken at the highest operational level, no feasible alternative must exist, and effective advance warning must be given where circumstances permit. The Second Protocol makes attacking property under enhanced protection, and using such property in support of military action, serious violations attracting individual criminal responsibility.</div><h4>5. The Second Protocol&#x27;s criminal provisions</h4><p>Article 15 lists five serious violations attracting individual criminal responsibility, with an obligation to criminalise them in domestic law and to establish jurisdiction, including universal jurisdiction based on presence for the first three.</p><ol><li>Making cultural property under enhanced protection the object of attack.</li><li>Using cultural property under enhanced protection or its immediate surroundings in support of military action.</li><li>Extensive destruction or appropriation of cultural property protected under the Convention and Protocol.</li><li>Making cultural property protected under the Convention and Protocol the object of attack.</li><li>Theft, pillage or misappropriation of, or acts of vandalism directed against, protected cultural property.</li></ol><p>The International Criminal Court convicted Ahmad Al Faqi Al Mahdi in 2016 for directing attacks against religious and historic buildings in Timbuktu, the first conviction at the Court for the destruction of cultural heritage as a war crime. The reparations order recognised harm to the community and to the international community.</p><h4>6. The natural environment</h4><div class=\"table-wrap\"><table class=\"study-table\"><thead><tr><th>Source</th><th>Rule</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>AP I Art 35(3)</td><td>Prohibits methods or means of warfare intended, or which may be expected, to cause widespread, long-term and severe damage to the natural environment</td></tr><tr><td>AP I Art 55</td><td>Requires care to protect the natural environment against such damage, including a prohibition on attacks against the environment by way of reprisals</td></tr><tr><td>ENMOD Convention 1976</td><td>Prohibits military or other hostile use of environmental modification techniques having widespread, long-lasting or severe effects</td></tr><tr><td>Rome Statute Art 8(2)(b)(iv)</td><td>War crime to launch an attack knowing it will cause widespread, long-term and severe damage to the natural environment clearly excessive in relation to the concrete and direct overall military advantage anticipated</td></tr><tr><td>Customary law</td><td>The environment is civilian in character, so distinction, proportionality and precautions apply to it; destruction not required by military necessity is prohibited</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"study-callout\"><strong>Note:</strong> The Protocol I threshold is cumulative and very high: damage must be widespread AND long-term AND severe. ENMOD's threshold is disjunctive: widespread OR long-lasting OR severe. That difference is a favourite examination point. The practical consequence is that Articles 35(3) and 55 have rarely if ever been found to be breached, and the environment is protected in practice mainly by the ordinary rules on civilian objects, which is why the ICRC's 2020 Guidelines on the Protection of the Natural Environment in Armed Conflict emphasise that route.</div><h4>7. Works and installations containing dangerous forces</h4><p>Additional Protocol I Article 56 and Protocol II Article 15 give special protection to dams, dykes and nuclear electrical generating stations.</p><ul><li>They must not be made the object of attack, even where they are military objectives, if such attack may cause the release of dangerous forces and consequent severe losses among the civilian population.</li><li>Other military objectives located at or in the vicinity of these works are subject to the same protection.</li><li>The protection ceases only in narrow circumstances: for a dam or dyke, if it is used in regular, significant and direct support of military operations and attack is the only feasible way to terminate that support; for a nuclear station, if it provides electric power in such support and attack is the only feasible way to terminate it.</li><li>Even where protection ceases, all practical precautions must be taken to avoid the release of dangerous forces.</li><li>A special sign of three bright orange circles may be used to identify them.</li><li>Parties must endeavour to avoid locating military objectives in the vicinity of such works.</li></ul><h4>8. How the examiner&#x27;s question should be answered</h4><p>On cultural property, name the 1954 Convention and both Protocols, set out the three levels with their waivers, and say that special protection failed in practice while enhanced protection replaced it. Cite Al Mahdi. On the environment, give the cumulative Protocol I threshold, contrast it with ENMOD's disjunctive one, explain why the specific provisions have proved almost unusable, and conclude that the ordinary civilian object rules do the real work. On dangerous forces, state that the protection survives even where the installation is a military objective, which is the point that distinguishes Article 56 from every other targeting rule.</p><h4>Summary</h4><ul><li>Cultural property is protected by the 1954 Hague Convention and its two Protocols, with general, special and enhanced levels of protection.</li><li>Parties must safeguard cultural property in peacetime and respect it during conflict, refraining from military use and from acts of hostility.</li><li>Special protection failed in practice; enhanced protection under the 1999 Second Protocol replaced it with a tighter waiver.</li><li>The Second Protocol creates individual criminal responsibility for five serious violations, and Al Mahdi was the first ICC conviction for cultural destruction.</li><li>Protocol I prohibits damage to the environment that is widespread, long-term and severe cumulatively, while ENMOD uses a disjunctive threshold.</li><li>Works containing dangerous forces may not be attacked even where they are military objectives, if attack may release dangerous forces causing severe civilian losses.</li></ul><h4>Key Terms</h4><div class=\"table-wrap\"><table class=\"study-table\"><thead><tr><th>Term</th><th>Meaning</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Cultural property</td><td>Property of great importance to the cultural heritage of every people, as defined in the 1954 Convention.</td></tr><tr><td>Enhanced protection</td><td>The Second Protocol regime for cultural heritage of the greatest importance for humanity, with a narrow waiver.</td></tr><tr><td>Blue shield</td><td>The distinctive emblem marking cultural property protected under the 1954 Convention.</td></tr><tr><td>Widespread, long-term and severe</td><td>The cumulative threshold for environmental damage prohibited by Additional Protocol I.</td></tr><tr><td>Works containing dangerous forces</td><td>Dams, dykes and nuclear electrical generating stations, given special protection under Article 56.</td></tr></tbody></table></div><h4>Review Questions</h4><h5>Multiple choice</h5><div class=\"study-mcq\"><p class=\"mcq-q\">Why was special protection under the 1954 Convention almost never used?</p><ol type=\"A\"><li>Its conditions, particularly adequate distance from any military objective, proved impossible to satisfy in practice</li><li>States refused to ratify the Convention</li><li>It required Security Council approval</li><li>It applied only in occupied territory</li></ol><details class=\"mcq-a\"><summary>Show answer</summary><p><strong>Answer: A.</strong> Enhanced protection under the 1999 Second Protocol was designed to replace it with workable conditions and a functioning register.</p></details></div><div class=\"study-mcq\"><p class=\"mcq-q\">How do the environmental thresholds in Additional Protocol I and ENMOD differ?</p><ol type=\"A\"><li>Protocol I requires damage to be widespread, long-term and severe cumulatively, while ENMOD requires widespread, long-lasting or severe</li><li>They are identical</li><li>ENMOD is stricter in every respect</li><li>Protocol I applies only to nuclear weapons</li></ol><details class=\"mcq-a\"><summary>Show answer</summary><p><strong>Answer: A.</strong> The cumulative threshold is why Articles 35(3) and 55 have rarely if ever been found breached in practice.</p></details></div><div class=\"study-mcq\"><p class=\"mcq-q\">May a dam that is a military objective be attacked?</p><ol type=\"A\"><li>Not if the attack may cause release of dangerous forces and consequent severe civilian losses, unless narrow conditions are met</li><li>Yes, like any military objective</li><li>Never, in any circumstances</li><li>Only with ICRC consent</li></ol><details class=\"mcq-a\"><summary>Show answer</summary><p><strong>Answer: A.</strong> Protection ceases only where the dam is used in regular, significant and direct support of military operations and attack is the only feasible way to terminate that support.</p></details></div><div class=\"study-mcq\"><p class=\"mcq-q\">What was significant about the Al Mahdi case?</p><ol type=\"A\"><li>It was the first International Criminal Court conviction for the destruction of cultural heritage as a war crime</li><li>It established universal jurisdiction over cultural property offences</li><li>It created the enhanced protection regime</li><li>It concerned the natural environment</li></ol><details class=\"mcq-a\"><summary>Show answer</summary><p><strong>Answer: A.</strong> The reparations order recognised harm to the local community and to the international community.</p></details></div><h5>Short answer</h5><div class=\"study-sa\"><p class=\"sa-q\">Set out the three levels of protection for cultural property and their waivers.</p><details class=\"sa-a\"><summary>Model answer</summary><p>General protection under the 1954 Convention applies to all cultural property and requires safeguarding in peacetime and respect during conflict; it may be waived where military necessity imperatively requires. Special protection applies to a small number of refuges and very important centres entered in the International Register, which must be situated at an adequate distance from any military objective and not used for military purposes; the waiver operates only in exceptional cases of unavoidable military necessity and must be determined by an officer commanding at least a division. Enhanced protection under the 1999 Second Protocol applies to cultural heritage of the greatest importance for humanity that is protected by adequate domestic measures and not used for military purposes; it is lost only if and for as long as the property has by its use become a military objective, attack must be the only feasible means of terminating that use, the decision must be taken at the highest operational level of command, and effective advance warning must be given where circumstances permit.</p></details></div><div class=\"study-sa\"><p class=\"sa-q\">Explain why the specific environmental provisions of Additional Protocol I have had little practical effect.</p><details class=\"sa-a\"><summary>Model answer</summary><p>Articles 35(3) and 55 prohibit methods or means of warfare intended or expected to cause damage to the natural environment that is widespread, long-term and severe. The threshold is cumulative, so all three must be established, and each is set at a high level; long-term has been understood in the drafting history as measured in decades. As a result the provisions have rarely if ever been found to be breached, even in conflicts causing very substantial environmental harm. The ENMOD Convention uses a disjunctive threshold of widespread, long-lasting or severe, but applies only to the deliberate use of environmental modification techniques rather than to ordinary damage. In practice the environment is protected mainly by the ordinary rules: it is civilian in character, so distinction, proportionality and precautions apply to it, and destruction not required by military necessity is prohibited.</p></details></div><h4>Class Activity</h4><ol><li>Identify three sites of cultural importance in your country. For each, state which level of protection it could qualify for and what steps the state would need to take to secure that protection.</li><li>Take one conflict that caused significant environmental damage. Assess whether the Additional Protocol I threshold would have been met and, if not, which ordinary rules would apply instead.</li></ol><h4>Assignment</h4><p>Write one and a half pages on cultural property and the environment. Name the principal instruments protecting cultural property, set out the three levels of protection with their waivers and explain why special protection failed, describe the duties to safeguard and respect and the Second Protocol&#x27;s criminal provisions with reference to Al Mahdi, state the environmental protections and explain why the Protocol I threshold has had little practical effect, and describe the special regime for works containing dangerous forces.</p><p class=\"study-print\"><strong>Printable:</strong> use your browser\u2019s Print \u2192 Save as PDF to keep an offline copy.</p></div>",
  "Journalists and Humanitarian Workers": "<div class=\"study-note\"><div class=\"revision-banner\"><strong>Human Rights and International Humanitarian Law</strong><span>Module 9: Protected Persons and Objects</span></div><h3 class=\"lesson-note-title\">LESSON NOTE: JOURNALISTS AND HUMANITARIAN WORKERS</h3><div class=\"table-wrap\"><table class=\"study-table\"><thead><tr><th>Subject</th><th>Topic</th><th>Level</th><th>Lesson duration</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Human Rights and International Humanitarian Law</td><td>Journalists and Humanitarian Workers</td><td>Beginner to Advanced</td><td>40 minutes</td></tr></tbody></table></div><h4>Learning objectives</h4><p>By the end of this lesson you should be able to:</p><ul><li>State the protection of journalists in armed conflict</li><li>Distinguish war correspondents from embedded and independent journalists</li><li>State the protection of humanitarian and peacekeeping personnel</li><li>Explain the risks and the accountability gap</li></ul><h4>1. Introduction</h4><p>Journalists and aid workers are civilians who deliberately go where the fighting is. The law protects them as civilians and gives them almost nothing extra, which is a deliberate choice: creating a special protected status for journalists was proposed repeatedly and rejected, on the ground that it would create a category open to abuse and might weaken the general civilian protection.</p><h4>2. Journalists: the legal framework</h4><div class=\"table-wrap\"><table class=\"study-table\"><thead><tr><th>Category</th><th>Status</th><th>Source</th><th>On capture</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>War correspondent accredited to the armed forces</td><td>Civilian accompanying the armed forces</td><td>GC III Art 4A(4)</td><td>Entitled to prisoner of war status</td></tr><tr><td>Journalist engaged in dangerous professional missions</td><td>Civilian</td><td>AP I Art 79</td><td>Not a prisoner of war; protected as a civilian</td></tr><tr><td>Embedded journalist</td><td>Civilian; the status depends on the arrangement, and accreditation may bring them within Art 4A(4)</td><td>GC III Art 4A(4); AP I Art 79</td><td>Depends on accreditation</td></tr><tr><td>Journalist who is a member of the armed forces</td><td>Combatant</td><td>AP I Art 43</td><td>Prisoner of war</td></tr></tbody></table></div><p>Additional Protocol I Article 79 is the central provision. Journalists engaged in dangerous professional missions in areas of armed conflict shall be considered as civilians, shall be protected as such provided they take no action adversely affecting their status as civilians, and may obtain an identity card attesting to their status.</p><div class=\"study-callout\"><strong>Note:</strong> The identity card does not confer protection. Article 79 says the journalist shall be considered a civilian; the card merely attests to that. A journalist without a card is equally protected, and this point is worth stating because it parallels the rule on the distinctive emblem.</div><h4>3. What journalists may and may not do</h4><ul><li><strong>They may report on military operations.</strong> Reporting is not direct participation in hostilities and does not affect civilian status, however unwelcome the reporting is to a party.</li><li><strong>They may not be targeted for what they report.</strong> Attacking a journalist to prevent or punish reporting is an attack on a civilian and a war crime.</li><li><strong>They lose protection only by directly participating.</strong> Carrying and using weapons, or transmitting targeting information to a party, would do so; reporting would not.</li><li><strong>Media facilities are civilian objects</strong> unless they make an effective contribution to military action and their destruction offers a definite military advantage. Propaganda alone does not make a broadcaster a military objective; the ICTY's review committee on the NATO campaign considered the bombing of Serbian state television and concluded that the use of a broadcaster for propaganda would not by itself justify attack, though integration into a military command and control network could.</li><li><strong>Incitement is different.</strong> A radio station directing killings, as in Rwanda, is a different case, and those responsible were convicted of incitement to genocide, though this concerns criminal responsibility for the broadcasts rather than the lawfulness of attacking the transmitter.</li></ul><h4>4. Humanitarian personnel</h4><ol><li><strong>General position.</strong> Humanitarian relief personnel are civilians and are protected as such, and their supplies and installations are civilian objects.</li><li><strong>Additional Protocol I Article 71.</strong> Relief personnel participating in relief actions must be respected and protected; parties must assist them; their activities may be limited or movements restricted only in case of imperative military necessity.</li><li><strong>Rome Statute Article 8(2)(b)(iii) and (e)(iii).</strong> Intentionally directing attacks against personnel, installations, material, units or vehicles involved in a humanitarian assistance or peacekeeping mission in accordance with the UN Charter is a war crime in both international and non-international armed conflict, as long as they are entitled to the protection given to civilians or civilian objects.</li><li><strong>Safety Convention 1994.</strong> The Convention on the Safety of United Nations and Associated Personnel requires states to criminalise attacks on such personnel and to extradite or prosecute; its 2005 Optional Protocol extends it to operations delivering humanitarian, political or development assistance in peacebuilding, and to emergency humanitarian assistance.</li><li><strong>Security Council resolution 1502 of 2003</strong> condemns attacks on humanitarian personnel and requests reporting on their safety.</li></ol><h4>5. Peacekeepers</h4><p>The status of a peacekeeper depends on the mission's function at the time, not on the mandate's label.</p><div class=\"table-wrap\"><table class=\"study-table\"><thead><tr><th>Situation</th><th>Status</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Peacekeepers not taking direct part in hostilities</td><td>Civilians; protected; attacks on them are war crimes</td></tr><tr><td>Peacekeepers using force in self-defence</td><td>Remain protected; self-defence is not direct participation</td></tr><tr><td>Peacekeepers who become a party to the conflict, for example by conducting offensive operations against an armed group</td><td>Members of a party's forces for as long as that is so; may be attacked; IHL applies to their own conduct</td></tr></tbody></table></div><p>The Special Court for Sierra Leone in the Sesay case and the ICC in Abu Garda and Banda addressed attacks on peacekeepers, confirming that the protection tracks civilian status and is lost only on the same basis as for any civilian.</p><div class=\"study-callout\"><strong>Note:</strong> The Secretary-General's 1999 bulletin on observance by United Nations forces of international humanitarian law sets out the fundamental principles applicable to UN forces when engaged as combatants in situations of armed conflict. It is worth citing, since it answers the question of whether UN forces are bound.</div><h4>6. The risks and the accountability gap</h4><div class=\"table-wrap\"><table class=\"study-table\"><thead><tr><th>Risk</th><th>Effect</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Deliberate targeting</td><td>Killing of journalists to suppress reporting and of aid workers to deny relief</td></tr><tr><td>Incidental harm</td><td>Presence in areas under attack; failure of deconfliction</td></tr><tr><td>Detention and prosecution</td><td>Journalists charged with terrorism or espionage offences for reporting; aid workers charged under counter-terrorism laws for operating in areas controlled by designated groups</td></tr><tr><td>Denial of access</td><td>Visa refusals and permit systems used to exclude both</td></tr><tr><td>Impunity</td><td>Very few killings of journalists result in a conviction, a pattern documented consistently by press freedom organisations</td></tr></tbody></table></div><ol><li><strong>The legal framework is adequate; enforcement is not.</strong> Attacks on journalists and aid workers are already war crimes, so the gap is not in the rules.</li><li><strong>Investigation is the weak point.</strong> Most incidents occur in places where no functioning investigation follows, and the parties concerned investigate themselves.</li><li><strong>UN Security Council resolution 2222 of 2015</strong> on the protection of journalists in armed conflict, and resolution 1738 of 2006, both call for accountability and for the prevention of impunity.</li><li><strong>UNESCO's Director-General reports</strong> on the safety of journalists and the danger of impunity, tracking judicial follow-up on killings and consistently finding a low resolution rate.</li><li><strong>Practical measures</strong> include deconfliction, safety training, protective equipment, and negotiated humanitarian notification systems, none of which substitute for compliance.</li></ol><h4>7. How the examiner&#x27;s question should be answered</h4><p>State first that journalists and humanitarian workers are civilians and are protected as such, and that no special status was created deliberately. Then take the categories: war correspondents accredited under Article 4A(4) get prisoner of war status; other journalists get Article 79. Say that the identity card attests rather than confers. Address media facilities and the propaganda question, since that is where the analytical content lies. For peacekeepers, tie status to function rather than mandate and cite Abu Garda or Sesay. Close with the accountability gap, and be precise that the deficiency is in enforcement rather than in the rules.</p><h4>Summary</h4><ul><li>Journalists and humanitarian workers are civilians and are protected as such; no special protected status was created.</li><li>War correspondents accredited to the armed forces are entitled to prisoner of war status on capture under Article 4A(4).</li><li>Additional Protocol I Article 79 protects journalists on dangerous professional missions; the identity card attests rather than confers protection.</li><li>Reporting is not direct participation, and media facilities are civilian objects unless integrated into military functions.</li><li>Attacks on humanitarian and peacekeeping personnel entitled to civilian protection are war crimes under the Rome Statute.</li><li>The protection of peacekeepers tracks their function: they lose it only if they become a party to the conflict.</li></ul><h4>Key Terms</h4><div class=\"table-wrap\"><table class=\"study-table\"><thead><tr><th>Term</th><th>Meaning</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>War correspondent</td><td>A journalist accredited to and accompanying the armed forces, entitled to prisoner of war status on capture.</td></tr><tr><td>Article 79</td><td>The Additional Protocol I provision protecting journalists engaged in dangerous professional missions as civilians.</td></tr><tr><td>Humanitarian notification system</td><td>A negotiated arrangement sharing the location of humanitarian facilities and movements with parties to a conflict.</td></tr><tr><td>Safety Convention</td><td>The 1994 treaty requiring criminalisation of attacks on United Nations and associated personnel.</td></tr></tbody></table></div><h4>Review Questions</h4><h5>Multiple choice</h5><div class=\"study-mcq\"><p class=\"mcq-q\">What is the status of a journalist on a dangerous professional mission?</p><ol type=\"A\"><li>A civilian, protected as such provided they take no action adversely affecting that status</li><li>A combatant</li><li>A protected person with special status</li><li>A prisoner of war if captured</li></ol><details class=\"mcq-a\"><summary>Show answer</summary><p><strong>Answer: A.</strong> Only war correspondents accredited to and accompanying the armed forces are entitled to prisoner of war status, under Article 4A(4).</p></details></div><div class=\"study-mcq\"><p class=\"mcq-q\">Does the journalist&#x27;s identity card confer protection?</p><ol type=\"A\"><li>No; it attests to a status the journalist has as a civilian in any event</li><li>Yes; without it there is no protection</li><li>Yes, but only in international armed conflict</li><li>It confers prisoner of war status</li></ol><details class=\"mcq-a\"><summary>Show answer</summary><p><strong>Answer: A.</strong> The position parallels the distinctive emblem, which signals rather than confers protection.</p></details></div><div class=\"study-mcq\"><p class=\"mcq-q\">Is a broadcaster used for propaganda a military objective?</p><ol type=\"A\"><li>Not on that basis alone; integration into a military command and control network could make it one</li><li>Yes, propaganda always makes it a military objective</li><li>No, in no circumstances</li><li>Only if the state declares it so</li></ol><details class=\"mcq-a\"><summary>Show answer</summary><p><strong>Answer: A.</strong> The ICTY review committee on the NATO campaign considered this question and reached that conclusion.</p></details></div><div class=\"study-mcq\"><p class=\"mcq-q\">When do peacekeepers lose their protection from attack?</p><ol type=\"A\"><li>When they become a party to the conflict, for example by conducting offensive operations</li><li>When they carry weapons</li><li>When they use force in self-defence</li><li>When their mandate is under Chapter VII</li></ol><details class=\"mcq-a\"><summary>Show answer</summary><p><strong>Answer: A.</strong> Protection tracks function rather than mandate, and self-defence does not amount to direct participation.</p></details></div><h5>Short answer</h5><div class=\"study-sa\"><p class=\"sa-q\">Distinguish the categories of journalist in armed conflict and their status.</p><details class=\"sa-a\"><summary>Model answer</summary><p>A war correspondent accredited to and accompanying the armed forces is a civilian who accompanies the forces without being a member, falling within Geneva Convention III Article 4A(4), and is entitled to prisoner of war status on capture provided they hold authorisation and an identity card. A journalist engaged in a dangerous professional mission who is not so accredited is a civilian under Additional Protocol I Article 79, protected as such but not entitled to prisoner of war status; they may obtain an identity card, which attests to rather than confers that status. An embedded journalist&#x27;s position depends on the terms of the arrangement and whether it amounts to accreditation. A journalist who is a member of the armed forces is a combatant. In every case protection is lost only by direct participation in hostilities, and reporting is never direct participation.</p></details></div><div class=\"study-sa\"><p class=\"sa-q\">Explain the accountability gap concerning attacks on journalists and aid workers.</p><details class=\"sa-a\"><summary>Model answer</summary><p>The legal framework is not deficient: attacks on journalists and on humanitarian personnel entitled to civilian protection are already war crimes under the Rome Statute in both types of armed conflict, and the Safety Convention requires states to criminalise attacks on United Nations and associated personnel and to extradite or prosecute. The deficiency is in enforcement. Most incidents occur where no functioning investigation follows, and the party concerned typically investigates itself. UNESCO&#x27;s reporting on the safety of journalists consistently records a low rate of judicial resolution for killings, and Security Council resolutions 1738 and 2222 have called for an end to impunity without changing that pattern. Practical measures such as deconfliction, safety training and humanitarian notification systems reduce risk but do not substitute for accountability.</p></details></div><h4>Class Activity</h4><ol><li>Research the outcome of three reported killings of journalists in armed conflict. Record whether any investigation took place and whether anyone was convicted, and write a paragraph on what your findings show.</li><li>Draft a one-page safety and legal briefing for a journalist deploying to a conflict area, covering their status, what conduct would jeopardise it, and what documentation to carry and why.</li></ol><h4>Assignment</h4><p>Write one and a half pages on journalists and humanitarian workers. Explain why no special protected status was created, distinguish the categories of journalist and their status on capture, explain what conduct does and does not affect protection and how media facilities are assessed, set out the protection of humanitarian and peacekeeping personnel including the relevant Rome Statute provisions, explain how peacekeepers&#x27; status tracks their function, and analyse the accountability gap.</p><p class=\"study-print\"><strong>Printable:</strong> use your browser\u2019s Print \u2192 Save as PDF to keep an offline copy.</p></div>",
  "Restrictions on Weapons": "<div class=\"study-note\"><div class=\"revision-banner\"><strong>Human Rights and International Humanitarian Law</strong><span>Module 10: Means and Methods of Warfare</span></div><h3 class=\"lesson-note-title\">LESSON NOTE: RESTRICTIONS ON WEAPONS</h3><div class=\"table-wrap\"><table class=\"study-table\"><thead><tr><th>Subject</th><th>Topic</th><th>Level</th><th>Lesson duration</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Human Rights and International Humanitarian Law</td><td>Restrictions on Weapons</td><td>Beginner to Advanced</td><td>40 minutes</td></tr></tbody></table></div><h4>Learning objectives</h4><p>By the end of this lesson you should be able to:</p><ul><li>Distinguish means from methods of warfare</li><li>State the two general prohibitions governing weapons</li><li>Describe the Convention on Certain Conventional Weapons and its protocols</li><li>Explain how a weapon is assessed for lawfulness</li></ul><h4>1. Introduction</h4><p>The law does not prohibit weapons because they are frightening or because they kill efficiently. It prohibits them for two reasons only: because they cause suffering beyond what is needed to put a fighter out of the fight, or because they cannot be aimed. Every specific weapons prohibition traces back to one of those two general rules, and often to both.</p><h4>2. Means and methods</h4><div class=\"table-wrap\"><table class=\"study-table\"><thead><tr><th></th><th>Means of warfare</th><th>Methods of warfare</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Definition</td><td>The weapons, weapon systems and platforms used</td><td>The ways in which weapons are used, and tactics generally</td></tr><tr><td>Examples</td><td>Rifles, artillery, mines, aircraft, cyber tools</td><td>Siege, bombardment patterns, perfidy, denial of quarter, starvation</td></tr><tr><td>Governed by</td><td>Weapons prohibitions and the general rules</td><td>The general rules and specific method prohibitions</td></tr></tbody></table></div><p>Additional Protocol I Article 35(1) states the foundational rule: in any armed conflict, the right of the parties to the conflict to choose methods or means of warfare is not unlimited. That sentence, drawn from the Hague Regulations, is the premise of everything in this module.</p><h4>3. The two general prohibitions</h4><div class=\"table-wrap\"><table class=\"study-table\"><thead><tr><th>Prohibition</th><th>Source</th><th>Protects</th><th>Test</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Superfluous injury or unnecessary suffering</td><td>AP I Art 35(2); Hague Regs Art 23(e); customary rule 70</td><td>Combatants</td><td>Whether the suffering is disproportionate to the military purpose of rendering the adversary hors de combat</td></tr><tr><td>Indiscriminate by nature</td><td>AP I Art 51(4)(b) and (c); customary rule 71</td><td>Civilians</td><td>Whether the weapon can be directed at a specific military objective and whether its effects can be limited as the law requires</td></tr></tbody></table></div><p>A weapon is indiscriminate by nature in two situations. First, where it cannot be directed at a specific military objective, as with an unguided long-range rocket fired at a city. Second, where its effects cannot be limited as required by IHL, as with a biological agent whose spread cannot be controlled. Note the phrase 'by nature': a weapon that can be used discriminately but is used indiscriminately in a particular attack is not prohibited as such, though the attack is unlawful.</p><div class=\"study-callout\"><strong>Note:</strong> Distinguishing 'indiscriminate by nature' from 'used indiscriminately' is the point examiners test. Unguided artillery is not prohibited as a weapon; firing it into a residential district in the hope of hitting a military objective is an indiscriminate attack. Prohibiting the weapon and prohibiting the attack are different legal conclusions with different consequences.</div><h4>4. The Convention on Certain Conventional Weapons</h4><p>The CCW of 1980 is a framework convention: it contains general provisions and its substance lies in annexed protocols, which states may accept selectively. Adherence to at least two protocols is required.</p><div class=\"table-wrap\"><table class=\"study-table\"><thead><tr><th>Protocol</th><th>Year</th><th>Subject</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>I</td><td>1980</td><td>Weapons injuring by fragments not detectable in the human body by X-ray</td></tr><tr><td>II</td><td>1980, amended 1996</td><td>Mines, booby-traps and other devices; the 1996 amendment extended it to non-international conflict and tightened restrictions</td></tr><tr><td>III</td><td>1980</td><td>Incendiary weapons</td></tr><tr><td>IV</td><td>1995</td><td>Blinding laser weapons</td></tr><tr><td>V</td><td>2003</td><td>Explosive remnants of war</td></tr></tbody></table></div><ol><li><strong>Protocol I</strong> prohibits any weapon whose primary effect is to injure by fragments which in the human body escape detection by X-ray. The reasoning is that such fragments cannot be located and removed, so the wound is untreatable, and this additional suffering serves no military purpose.</li><li><strong>Protocol III</strong> prohibits making the civilian population or civilian objects the object of attack by incendiary weapons, prohibits attacking a military objective located within a concentration of civilians by air-delivered incendiary weapons, and permits such attack by other incendiary means only where the objective is clearly separated and all feasible precautions are taken. It has been criticised for the air-delivered distinction and for a definition that excludes munitions with incidental incendiary effects such as some illuminants and smoke.</li><li><strong>Protocol IV</strong> prohibits employing laser weapons specifically designed as their sole or one of their combat functions to cause permanent blindness to unenhanced vision, and prohibits their transfer. It is the only pre-emptive weapons ban.</li><li><strong>Protocol V</strong> addresses the clearance, removal and destruction of explosive remnants of war after hostilities, allocating responsibility to the party in control of the territory with assistance from the user, and requiring recording and warning.</li></ol><div class=\"study-callout\"><strong>Note:</strong> The CCW operates by consensus, which is why the landmine and cluster munition bans were negotiated outside it. Understanding that consensus rule explains most of the institutional history in this module: where the CCW could not act, coalitions of states and civil society went elsewhere.</div><h4>5. Weapons prohibited or restricted: the full picture</h4><div class=\"table-wrap\"><table class=\"study-table\"><thead><tr><th>Weapon</th><th>Status</th><th>Instrument</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Poison and poisoned weapons</td><td>Prohibited</td><td>Hague Regs Art 23(a); customary</td></tr><tr><td>Expanding bullets</td><td>Prohibited</td><td>Hague Declaration 1899; Rome Statute Art 8</td></tr><tr><td>Explosive projectiles under 400g</td><td>Prohibited</td><td>St Petersburg Declaration 1868</td></tr><tr><td>Chemical weapons</td><td>Prohibited absolutely, with destruction obligations</td><td>Geneva Gas Protocol 1925; CWC 1993</td></tr><tr><td>Biological weapons</td><td>Prohibited absolutely</td><td>Geneva Gas Protocol 1925; BWC 1972</td></tr><tr><td>Undetectable fragments</td><td>Prohibited</td><td>CCW Protocol I</td></tr><tr><td>Blinding lasers</td><td>Prohibited</td><td>CCW Protocol IV</td></tr><tr><td>Anti-personnel mines</td><td>Prohibited for parties</td><td>Ottawa Convention 1997</td></tr><tr><td>Cluster munitions</td><td>Prohibited for parties</td><td>Convention on Cluster Munitions 2008</td></tr><tr><td>Booby-traps attached to protected objects</td><td>Prohibited</td><td>CCW Amended Protocol II</td></tr><tr><td>Incendiary weapons against civilians</td><td>Prohibited; restricted against military objectives in civilian areas</td><td>CCW Protocol III</td></tr><tr><td>Nuclear weapons</td><td>Prohibited for parties to the 2017 treaty; not prohibited by name for others</td><td>TPNW 2017; Nuclear Weapons advisory opinion</td></tr></tbody></table></div><h4>6. Assessing a weapon for lawfulness</h4><ol><li><strong>Is it prohibited by name</strong> in a treaty binding this state, or by customary law?</li><li><strong>Is it of a nature to cause superfluous injury or unnecessary suffering?</strong> Apply the comparative test: severity, inevitability of death, availability of alternatives, military utility.</li><li><strong>Is it indiscriminate by nature?</strong> Can it be directed at a specific military objective, and can its effects be limited as required?</li><li><strong>May it be expected to cause widespread, long-term and severe damage to the natural environment?</strong></li><li><strong>Can it in practice be used consistently with distinction, proportionality and precautions</strong> in the circumstances of its intended use? A weapon lawful in open terrain may be unlawful in a city.</li><li><strong>Is it contrary to the principles of humanity and the dictates of public conscience?</strong> The Martens Clause requires the question even where no rule applies.</li><li><strong>Are restrictions on use required rather than prohibition?</strong> Article 36 requires assessment 'in some or all circumstances', so the answer may be conditional.</li></ol><div class=\"study-callout\"><strong>Example:</strong> A state acquires a thermobaric munition producing a sustained high-pressure blast wave. It is not prohibited by name. On unnecessary suffering, the injury mechanism is severe but so is that of ordinary high explosive, and the comparative test is unlikely to prohibit it as such. On indiscriminateness, it can be aimed, so it is not indiscriminate by nature, but its blast radius and effects in enclosed spaces mean that in an urban environment the expected civilian harm may routinely be excessive. The correct outcome of the review is therefore not a prohibition but a restriction on use: permitted against fortified positions in open terrain, prohibited in populated areas. That conditional outcome is what Article 36 contemplates and what an examiner is looking for.</div><h4>7. How the examiner&#x27;s question should be answered</h4><p>Begin with Article 35(1), that the right to choose means and methods is not unlimited, since it frames everything. Give the two general prohibitions with what each protects and its test, and distinguish 'indiscriminate by nature' from 'used indiscriminately'. Describe the CCW as a framework convention with selectively accepted protocols and explain the consequence of its consensus rule. Then apply the seven-step assessment to whatever weapon the question supplies, and remember that restriction rather than prohibition is often the correct conclusion.</p><h4>Summary</h4><ul><li>Means are the weapons used; methods are the ways they are used, and the right to choose either is not unlimited.</li><li>Two general prohibitions govern weapons: superfluous injury or unnecessary suffering, protecting combatants, and indiscriminateness by nature, protecting civilians.</li><li>A weapon indiscriminate by nature is prohibited as such; a discriminate weapon used indiscriminately makes the attack, not the weapon, unlawful.</li><li>The CCW is a framework convention with five protocols accepted selectively, and its consensus rule pushed the landmine and cluster munition bans outside it.</li><li>Assessment proceeds through name prohibitions, the two general rules, environmental damage, practical usability, and the Martens Clause.</li><li>The correct outcome of a review is often a restriction on use rather than a prohibition.</li></ul><h4>Key Terms</h4><div class=\"table-wrap\"><table class=\"study-table\"><thead><tr><th>Term</th><th>Meaning</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Means of warfare</td><td>The weapons, weapon systems and platforms used in armed conflict.</td></tr><tr><td>Methods of warfare</td><td>The ways in which weapons are used and the tactics employed.</td></tr><tr><td>Indiscriminate by nature</td><td>A weapon that cannot be directed at a specific military objective or whose effects cannot be limited as required.</td></tr><tr><td>Framework convention</td><td>A treaty containing general provisions whose substance lies in separately accepted protocols.</td></tr><tr><td>Explosive remnants of war</td><td>Unexploded and abandoned ordnance remaining after hostilities, addressed by CCW Protocol V.</td></tr></tbody></table></div><h4>Review Questions</h4><h5>Multiple choice</h5><div class=\"study-mcq\"><p class=\"mcq-q\">What is the difference between a weapon that is indiscriminate by nature and one used indiscriminately?</p><ol type=\"A\"><li>The former is prohibited as a weapon; the latter makes the particular attack unlawful without prohibiting the weapon</li><li>There is no difference</li><li>Only the latter is a war crime</li><li>The former applies only in internal conflict</li></ol><details class=\"mcq-a\"><summary>Show answer</summary><p><strong>Answer: A.</strong> Unguided artillery is lawful as a weapon but firing it into a residential district in the hope of hitting a military objective is an indiscriminate attack.</p></details></div><div class=\"study-mcq\"><p class=\"mcq-q\">Why were the landmine and cluster munition bans negotiated outside the CCW?</p><ol type=\"A\"><li>The CCW operates by consensus, which blocked agreement</li><li>The CCW covers only nuclear weapons</li><li>Those weapons were already lawful</li><li>The CCW had no member states</li></ol><details class=\"mcq-a\"><summary>Show answer</summary><p><strong>Answer: A.</strong> Coalitions of smaller states working with civil society negotiated elsewhere and produced treaties far more quickly.</p></details></div><div class=\"study-mcq\"><p class=\"mcq-q\">Which CCW protocol prohibits a weapon before it was ever deployed?</p><ol type=\"A\"><li>Protocol IV on blinding laser weapons</li><li>Protocol I on undetectable fragments</li><li>Protocol III on incendiary weapons</li><li>Protocol V on explosive remnants of war</li></ol><details class=\"mcq-a\"><summary>Show answer</summary><p><strong>Answer: A.</strong> It is the only pre-emptive weapons prohibition in international humanitarian law.</p></details></div><div class=\"study-mcq\"><p class=\"mcq-q\">What is often the correct outcome of a legal review of a new weapon?</p><ol type=\"A\"><li>A restriction on the circumstances of use rather than an outright prohibition</li><li>Always a prohibition</li><li>Always approval</li><li>Referral to the Security Council</li></ol><details class=\"mcq-a\"><summary>Show answer</summary><p><strong>Answer: A.</strong> Article 36 requires assessment of whether employment would be prohibited in some or all circumstances, which contemplates conditional outcomes.</p></details></div><h5>Short answer</h5><div class=\"study-sa\"><p class=\"sa-q\">State the two general prohibitions governing weapons and explain what each protects.</p><details class=\"sa-a\"><summary>Model answer</summary><p>The first is the prohibition of weapons of a nature to cause superfluous injury or unnecessary suffering, in Additional Protocol I Article 35(2) and customary rule 70. It protects combatants, and the test is comparative: whether the injury or suffering caused exceeds what is needed to render the adversary hors de combat, taking account of severity, whether death is rendered inevitable, whether alternatives achieving the same effect with less suffering exist, and the weapon&#x27;s military utility. The second is the prohibition of weapons that are indiscriminate by nature, in Article 51(4)(b) and (c) and customary rule 71. It protects civilians, and a weapon is caught either because it cannot be directed at a specific military objective or because its effects cannot be limited as international humanitarian law requires.</p></details></div><div class=\"study-sa\"><p class=\"sa-q\">Set out the steps in assessing whether a weapon is lawful.</p><details class=\"sa-a\"><summary>Model answer</summary><p>First, ask whether it is prohibited by name in a treaty binding the state or by customary law. Second, apply the comparative test for superfluous injury or unnecessary suffering. Third, ask whether it is indiscriminate by nature, both in whether it can be aimed and in whether its effects can be limited. Fourth, ask whether it may be expected to cause widespread, long-term and severe damage to the natural environment. Fifth, ask whether it can in practice be used consistently with distinction, proportionality and precautions in the circumstances of its intended employment, since a weapon lawful in open terrain may be unlawful in a city. Sixth, apply the Martens Clause where no specific rule governs. Seventh, consider whether the correct outcome is a restriction on use rather than a prohibition, since Article 36 requires assessment in some or all circumstances.</p></details></div><h4>Class Activity</h4><ol><li>Choose one weapon in current use. Work through the seven assessment steps and write a short review concluding either that it is lawful, unlawful, or lawful subject to stated restrictions.</li><li>Research why CCW Protocol III on incendiary weapons has been criticised. Write one page setting out the two main criticisms and what an amendment would need to change.</li></ol><h4>Assignment</h4><p>Write one and a half pages on restrictions on weapons. Distinguish means from methods and state the rule in Article 35(1), set out the two general prohibitions with what each protects and its test, distinguish weapons indiscriminate by nature from discriminate weapons used indiscriminately, describe the CCW and its five protocols and explain the effect of its consensus rule, and set out the steps for assessing a weapon&#x27;s lawfulness with a worked example.</p><p class=\"study-print\"><strong>Printable:</strong> use your browser\u2019s Print \u2192 Save as PDF to keep an offline copy.</p></div>",
  "Chemical and Biological Weapons": "<div class=\"study-note\"><div class=\"revision-banner\"><strong>Human Rights and International Humanitarian Law</strong><span>Module 10: Means and Methods of Warfare</span></div><h3 class=\"lesson-note-title\">LESSON NOTE: CHEMICAL AND BIOLOGICAL WEAPONS</h3><div class=\"table-wrap\"><table class=\"study-table\"><thead><tr><th>Subject</th><th>Topic</th><th>Level</th><th>Lesson duration</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Human Rights and International Humanitarian Law</td><td>Chemical and Biological Weapons</td><td>Beginner to Advanced</td><td>40 minutes</td></tr></tbody></table></div><h4>Learning objectives</h4><p>By the end of this lesson you should be able to:</p><ul><li>Describe the prohibitions on chemical and biological weapons</li><li>Explain the Chemical Weapons Convention and its verification machinery</li><li>Explain the weakness of the Biological Weapons Convention</li><li>Discuss riot control agents and the herbicide question</li></ul><h4>1. Introduction</h4><p>Chemical and biological weapons are prohibited more comprehensively than any other category. They are also the category where the gap between the prohibition and the verification machinery is widest, because the chemical regime has an organisation with inspectors and the biological regime has almost nothing.</p><h4>2. The 1925 Geneva Protocol</h4><ul><li>The Protocol for the Prohibition of the Use in War of Asphyxiating, Poisonous or Other Gases, and of Bacteriological Methods of Warfare, adopted in Geneva in 1925 following the use of gas in the First World War.</li><li>It prohibits use only, not development, production or stockpiling.</li><li>Many states entered reservations preserving the right to retaliate in kind, effectively converting it into a no-first-use undertaking; most such reservations have since been withdrawn.</li><li>It is now regarded as customary, and the prohibition of use of chemical and biological weapons binds all states regardless of party status.</li><li>Its limitation to use is what made the later conventions necessary.</li></ul><h4>3. The Biological Weapons Convention 1972</h4><div class=\"table-wrap\"><table class=\"study-table\"><thead><tr><th>Feature</th><th>Detail</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Full title</td><td>Convention on the Prohibition of the Development, Production and Stockpiling of Bacteriological (Biological) and Toxin Weapons and on their Destruction</td></tr><tr><td>In force</td><td>1975; the first treaty banning an entire category of weapon</td></tr><tr><td>Prohibits</td><td>Development, production, stockpiling, acquisition and retention of microbial or other biological agents or toxins of types and in quantities that have no justification for prophylactic, protective or other peaceful purposes, and of weapons and means of delivery designed to use them</td></tr><tr><td>General purpose criterion</td><td>The prohibition turns on purpose and quantity rather than on a list of agents, so it covers agents not yet invented</td></tr><tr><td>Destruction</td><td>Required within nine months of entry into force</td></tr><tr><td>Verification</td><td>None. There is no organisation, no inspectorate and no routine verification</td></tr><tr><td>Compliance machinery</td><td>Consultative meetings; complaint to the Security Council under Article VI</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"study-callout\"><strong>Note:</strong> The absence of verification is the central fact about the BWC and must appear in any answer. A protocol establishing a verification regime was negotiated through the 1990s and rejected in 2001, and negotiations have not resumed on that basis. The Convention instead relies on confidence-building measures submitted voluntarily, an Implementation Support Unit of a handful of staff, and national implementation. The result is the strongest prohibition in the field resting on the weakest institutional foundation.</div><h4>4. The Chemical Weapons Convention 1993</h4><div class=\"table-wrap\"><table class=\"study-table\"><thead><tr><th>Feature</th><th>Detail</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>In force</td><td>1997</td></tr><tr><td>Parties</td><td>193, close to universal</td></tr><tr><td>Prohibits</td><td>Development, production, acquisition, stockpiling, retention, transfer and use of chemical weapons; also assisting or encouraging anyone to engage in prohibited activity, and use of riot control agents as a method of warfare</td></tr><tr><td>Requires</td><td>Destruction of stockpiles and production facilities under international verification</td></tr><tr><td>Organisation</td><td>The OPCW, based in The Hague, with a Technical Secretariat and an inspectorate</td></tr><tr><td>Verification</td><td>Declarations, routine inspection of declared facilities, and challenge inspection of any facility at short notice</td></tr><tr><td>Attribution</td><td>The 2018 conference decision gave the OPCW power to identify perpetrators, not merely to confirm that chemical weapons were used</td></tr></tbody></table></div><p>The Convention defines chemical weapons by the general purpose criterion: toxic chemicals and their precursors, except where intended for purposes not prohibited under the Convention and of types and quantities consistent with such purposes. Purposes not prohibited include industrial, agricultural, research, medical and pharmaceutical purposes, protective purposes, military purposes not connected with the use of chemical weapons, and law enforcement including domestic riot control.</p><ol><li><strong>Schedules.</strong> Three schedules of chemicals attract different verification requirements, from Schedule 1 agents with no significant peaceful use to Schedule 3 chemicals produced industrially in large volumes.</li><li><strong>Challenge inspection.</strong> Any state party may request an inspection of any facility or location in any other state party, and the inspected state cannot refuse; it may only negotiate managed access. The procedure has never been invoked, which is itself a subject of comment.</li><li><strong>Investigation of alleged use.</strong> A separate procedure, used in several instances.</li><li><strong>The Investigation and Identification Team,</strong> established in 2018, attributes responsibility for confirmed use.</li><li><strong>Novichok agents</strong> were added to Schedule 1 by decision in 2019 following their use, showing the schedules can be updated.</li></ol><h4>5. Riot control agents</h4><div class=\"study-defn\"><strong>Riot control agent:</strong> Any chemical not listed in a Schedule which can produce rapidly in humans sensory irritation or disabling physical effects that disappear within a short time following termination of exposure (CWC Article II(7)).</div><ol><li>Riot control agents may lawfully be used for law enforcement purposes, including domestic riot control.</li><li>Their use as a method of warfare is prohibited by CWC Article I(5).</li><li>The prohibition exists for a practical reason drawn from experience: in war, tear gas has been used to force personnel out of shelters and bunkers into the open where they can be shot, and its use also risks escalation because a party attacked with gas cannot readily tell whether the agent is a riot control agent or a lethal one.</li><li>The distinction between law enforcement and warfare is therefore critical, and it is not always easy to draw during an armed conflict where a state conducts both operations.</li></ol><h4>6. Herbicides and incapacitating agents</h4><ul><li><strong>Herbicides.</strong> The CWC preamble records the parties' recognition of the prohibition embodied in relevant agreements and principles of international law of the use of herbicides as a method of warfare. Their use to destroy crops indispensable to the survival of the civilian population is separately prohibited by Additional Protocol I Article 54 and Protocol II Article 14. The ENMOD Convention may also apply where the use amounts to environmental modification.</li><li><strong>Incapacitating chemical agents.</strong> Central nervous system acting chemicals proposed for law enforcement raise the most difficult current question. The problem is that a dose sufficient to incapacitate reliably in an uncontrolled setting will kill some of those exposed, since the margin between incapacitating and lethal doses is narrow and varies with body weight, health and exposure. The ICRC's position is that their use should be limited to situations where the law enforcement exception genuinely applies, and it has warned that their development risks eroding the norm against chemical weapons. Several states parties have called for a clear position that the law enforcement exception does not extend to them.</li></ul><h4>7. Enforcement and the criminal dimension</h4><ol><li>Employing poison or poisoned weapons, and employing asphyxiating, poisonous or other gases and all analogous liquids, materials or devices, are war crimes under Rome Statute Article 8(2)(b)(xvii) and (xviii) in international armed conflict, and were added for non-international armed conflict by amendment in 2010.</li><li>Biological weapons are not listed by name in the Rome Statute, an omission arising from the negotiation rather than from any view that their use is lawful.</li><li>Security Council resolution 1540 of 2004 requires all states to adopt and enforce measures preventing non-state actors from acquiring nuclear, chemical or biological weapons.</li><li>The Security Council has established mechanisms to investigate use in particular conflicts, and the OPCW's identification function now supplies attribution that can support prosecution.</li></ol><h4>8. How the examiner&#x27;s question should be answered</h4><p>Take the three instruments in order: the 1925 Protocol prohibits use only; the BWC prohibits everything but verifies nothing; the CWC prohibits everything and verifies through the OPCW. That structure is the answer to most questions in this area. State the general purpose criterion, since it is what makes both conventions future-proof. On riot control agents, give the law enforcement exception and the prohibition as a method of warfare, and explain the reason for the latter rather than merely stating it. Close with the BWC verification gap and the incapacitating agents question, which are the two live problems.</p><h4>Summary</h4><ul><li>The 1925 Geneva Protocol prohibits use of chemical and biological weapons but not development, production or stockpiling.</li><li>The Biological Weapons Convention 1972 prohibits development, production and stockpiling but has no verification organisation or inspectorate.</li><li>The Chemical Weapons Convention 1993 prohibits the full range of activities and is verified by the OPCW through declarations, routine and challenge inspection.</li><li>Both conventions use a general purpose criterion, so they cover agents not yet invented.</li><li>Riot control agents may be used for law enforcement but their use as a method of warfare is prohibited.</li><li>Incapacitating chemical agents are the most difficult current question because the margin between incapacitating and lethal doses is narrow.</li></ul><h4>Key Terms</h4><div class=\"table-wrap\"><table class=\"study-table\"><thead><tr><th>Term</th><th>Meaning</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>General purpose criterion</td><td>The approach defining prohibited agents by purpose and quantity rather than by a list, so new agents are covered.</td></tr><tr><td>OPCW</td><td>The Organisation for the Prohibition of Chemical Weapons, which verifies the Chemical Weapons Convention.</td></tr><tr><td>Challenge inspection</td><td>A CWC procedure allowing any party to demand inspection of any facility in another party at short notice.</td></tr><tr><td>Riot control agent</td><td>A chemical producing rapid sensory irritation or disabling effects that disappear shortly after exposure ends.</td></tr><tr><td>Incapacitating chemical agent</td><td>A central nervous system acting chemical whose incapacitating and lethal doses are narrowly separated.</td></tr></tbody></table></div><h4>Review Questions</h4><h5>Multiple choice</h5><div class=\"study-mcq\"><p class=\"mcq-q\">What is the central weakness of the Biological Weapons Convention?</p><ol type=\"A\"><li>It has no verification organisation, inspectorate or routine verification</li><li>It permits stockpiling</li><li>Few states have ratified it</li><li>It applies only in international armed conflict</li></ol><details class=\"mcq-a\"><summary>Show answer</summary><p><strong>Answer: A.</strong> A verification protocol negotiated through the 1990s was rejected in 2001, leaving voluntary confidence-building measures and a very small support unit.</p></details></div><div class=\"study-mcq\"><p class=\"mcq-q\">May riot control agents be used in armed conflict?</p><ol type=\"A\"><li>No; their use as a method of warfare is prohibited, though they may be used for law enforcement</li><li>Yes, freely</li><li>Yes, if non-lethal</li><li>Only against combatants</li></ol><details class=\"mcq-a\"><summary>Show answer</summary><p><strong>Answer: A.</strong> The prohibition exists partly because gas has been used to force personnel into the open to be shot, and partly because it risks escalation.</p></details></div><div class=\"study-mcq\"><p class=\"mcq-q\">What does the general purpose criterion achieve?</p><ol type=\"A\"><li>It defines prohibited agents by purpose and quantity rather than by a list, so agents not yet invented are covered</li><li>It exempts military research</li><li>It limits the conventions to listed chemicals</li><li>It permits stockpiling for defence</li></ol><details class=\"mcq-a\"><summary>Show answer</summary><p><strong>Answer: A.</strong> Both the Biological and Chemical Weapons Conventions use it, which is what makes them durable.</p></details></div><div class=\"study-mcq\"><p class=\"mcq-q\">What new power did the OPCW acquire in 2018?</p><ol type=\"A\"><li>The power to identify the perpetrators of confirmed chemical weapons use, not merely to confirm that use occurred</li><li>The power to prosecute</li><li>The power to impose sanctions</li><li>The power to conduct challenge inspections</li></ol><details class=\"mcq-a\"><summary>Show answer</summary><p><strong>Answer: A.</strong> The Investigation and Identification Team supplies attribution that can support later prosecution.</p></details></div><h5>Short answer</h5><div class=\"study-sa\"><p class=\"sa-q\">Compare the Biological and Chemical Weapons Conventions.</p><details class=\"sa-a\"><summary>Model answer</summary><p>Both prohibit development, production, stockpiling, acquisition and retention, and both use a general purpose criterion defining prohibited agents by purpose and quantity rather than by list, so future agents are covered. The difference is institutional. The Chemical Weapons Convention created the OPCW, with a technical secretariat and an inspectorate, and provides for declarations, routine inspection of declared facilities, challenge inspection of any location at short notice which the inspected state cannot refuse, investigation of alleged use, and since 2018 attribution of responsibility. The Biological Weapons Convention created nothing comparable: there is no organisation, no inspectorate and no routine verification, only voluntary confidence-building measures, a very small implementation support unit, and the possibility of complaint to the Security Council. A verification protocol was negotiated in the 1990s and rejected in 2001.</p></details></div><div class=\"study-sa\"><p class=\"sa-q\">Explain why riot control agents may be used for law enforcement but not as a method of warfare.</p><details class=\"sa-a\"><summary>Model answer</summary><p>The Chemical Weapons Convention permits the use of toxic chemicals for law enforcement purposes including domestic riot control, but Article I(5) prohibits the use of riot control agents as a method of warfare. Two reasons support the prohibition. First, in armed conflict such agents have historically been used to force personnel out of shelters and bunkers into the open where they can then be shot, so an apparently non-lethal agent becomes part of a lethal method. Second, a party subjected to a gas attack cannot readily determine whether the agent is a riot control agent or a lethal one, so use risks escalation to lethal chemical warfare. The difficulty in practice is that a state may conduct both law enforcement and military operations in the same conflict, and the line between them is not always easy to draw.</p></details></div><h4>Class Activity</h4><ol><li>Research one instance in which the OPCW investigated alleged chemical weapons use. Summarise the procedure followed, the finding and what followed from it.</li><li>Draft a one-page briefing arguing either for or against extending the law enforcement exception to incapacitating chemical agents, addressing the strongest argument on the other side.</li></ol><h4>Assignment</h4><p>Write one and a half pages on chemical and biological weapons. Describe the 1925 Geneva Protocol and its limitation to use, set out the Biological Weapons Convention and explain its verification gap, describe the Chemical Weapons Convention and the OPCW&#x27;s verification machinery including challenge inspection and attribution, explain the general purpose criterion, and discuss riot control agents, herbicides and incapacitating agents.</p><p class=\"study-print\"><strong>Printable:</strong> use your browser\u2019s Print \u2192 Save as PDF to keep an offline copy.</p></div>",
  "Landmines and Cluster Munitions": "<div class=\"study-note\"><div class=\"revision-banner\"><strong>Human Rights and International Humanitarian Law</strong><span>Module 10: Means and Methods of Warfare</span></div><h3 class=\"lesson-note-title\">LESSON NOTE: LANDMINES AND CLUSTER MUNITIONS</h3><div class=\"table-wrap\"><table class=\"study-table\"><thead><tr><th>Subject</th><th>Topic</th><th>Level</th><th>Lesson duration</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Human Rights and International Humanitarian Law</td><td>Landmines and Cluster Munitions</td><td>Beginner to Advanced</td><td>40 minutes</td></tr></tbody></table></div><h4>Learning objectives</h4><p>By the end of this lesson you should be able to:</p><ul><li>Describe the regime governing landmines under CCW Amended Protocol II</li><li>Describe the Ottawa Convention and its obligations</li><li>Describe the Convention on Cluster Munitions</li><li>Explain the humanitarian disarmament model and its limits</li></ul><h4>1. Introduction</h4><p>Landmines and cluster munitions produced the two most successful disarmament campaigns of the modern period, and both were achieved outside the ordinary machinery. Understanding how, and why the method worked, matters as much as the content of the treaties.</p><h4>2. The problem</h4><div class=\"table-wrap\"><table class=\"study-table\"><thead><tr><th>Weapon</th><th>Why it is a humanitarian problem</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Anti-personnel mine</td><td>Remains active for decades after a conflict ends; cannot distinguish a soldier's footstep from a child's; denies agricultural land and water sources to communities; injuries typically involve traumatic amputation, requiring lifelong prosthetic care in states least able to provide it</td></tr><tr><td>Cluster munition</td><td>Disperses dozens or hundreds of submunitions over a wide area, so the strike footprint cannot be confined to a military objective in a populated area; a proportion fail to detonate and function thereafter as de facto mines; the failure rate rises sharply with soft ground, vegetation and low-angle release</td></tr></tbody></table></div><p>In both cases the harm is characteristically post-conflict and falls on civilians, which is why the campaigns framed the issue as a humanitarian problem rather than an arms control problem. That framing was a deliberate strategy and it worked.</p><h4>3. CCW Amended Protocol II</h4><ul><li>The original Protocol II of 1980 restricted the use of mines, booby-traps and other devices. It applied only in international armed conflict and its restrictions were weak.</li><li>The 1996 amendment extended the Protocol to non-international armed conflict, prohibited undetectable anti-personnel mines, required remotely delivered mines to have self-destruction and self-deactivation mechanisms, prohibited the use of mines against the civilian population and indiscriminate use, required recording of minefields, and placed responsibility for clearance on the party in control after hostilities.</li><li>It prohibits booby-traps attached to protected objects: the sick, wounded or dead, medical facilities and equipment, religious objects, cultural property, food and drink, children's toys, and objects of a clearly religious nature.</li><li>It restricts rather than prohibits, and this was precisely why campaigners regarded it as inadequate and pursued a ban elsewhere.</li></ul><h4>4. The Ottawa Convention 1997</h4><div class=\"table-wrap\"><table class=\"study-table\"><thead><tr><th>Feature</th><th>Detail</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Full title</td><td>Convention on the Prohibition of the Use, Stockpiling, Production and Transfer of Anti-Personnel Mines and on their Destruction</td></tr><tr><td>In force</td><td>1999; over 160 parties</td></tr><tr><td>Core prohibition</td><td>Never under any circumstances to use, develop, produce, acquire, stockpile, retain or transfer anti-personnel mines, or to assist, encourage or induce anyone to do so</td></tr><tr><td>Stockpile destruction</td><td>Within four years of entry into force for the state</td></tr><tr><td>Clearance</td><td>All mined areas under the state's jurisdiction or control cleared within ten years, with extension possible</td></tr><tr><td>Victim assistance</td><td>Article 6(3): each party in a position to do so shall provide assistance for the care and rehabilitation, and social and economic reintegration, of mine victims</td></tr><tr><td>Transparency</td><td>Annual Article 7 reports on stockpiles, mined areas, destruction and assistance</td></tr><tr><td>Reservations</td><td>Article 19 prohibits reservations</td></tr><tr><td>Definition</td><td>An anti-personnel mine is one designed to be exploded by the presence, proximity or contact of a person; mines designed to be detonated by a vehicle and equipped with anti-handling devices are not covered</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"study-callout\"><strong>Note:</strong> Anti-vehicle mines are outside the Ottawa Convention and remain governed by Amended Protocol II. This is the main gap and is worth stating. It also explains why the definition of an anti-handling device mattered so much in negotiation: a sensitive anti-handling device on an anti-vehicle mine can function as an anti-personnel mine in practice.</div><h4>5. The Convention on Cluster Munitions 2008</h4><div class=\"table-wrap\"><table class=\"study-table\"><thead><tr><th>Feature</th><th>Detail</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Adopted</td><td>Dublin, 2008; in force 2010; over 110 parties</td></tr><tr><td>Core prohibition</td><td>Never under any circumstances to use, develop, produce, acquire, stockpile, retain or transfer cluster munitions, or assist anyone to do so</td></tr><tr><td>Definition</td><td>A conventional munition designed to disperse or release explosive submunitions each weighing less than 20 kilograms</td></tr><tr><td>Exclusions</td><td>Munitions with fewer than ten submunitions, each weighing more than four kilograms, each capable of detecting and engaging a single target object, and each with electronic self-destruction and self-deactivation features; also flares, smoke, pyrotechnics, chaff, and direct-fire munitions</td></tr><tr><td>Stockpile destruction</td><td>Within eight years</td></tr><tr><td>Clearance</td><td>Within ten years of contaminated areas under the state's jurisdiction or control</td></tr><tr><td>Victim assistance</td><td>Article 5, the most detailed victim assistance provision in any disarmament treaty</td></tr><tr><td>Interoperability</td><td>Article 21 permits parties to engage in military cooperation with non-parties, while requiring them to discourage use and not to expressly request use</td></tr></tbody></table></div><p>Article 5 on victim assistance is a genuine innovation and deserves attention. It requires each party to provide age and gender sensitive assistance including medical care, rehabilitation and psychological support, to provide for social and economic inclusion, to collect reliable data, to assess needs, to develop a national plan and budget with a timeframe, to designate a focal point, and to closely consult with and actively involve victims and their representative organisations. It converts a humanitarian aspiration into a set of specified state duties, and it borrows deliberately from the disability rights framework.</p><h4>6. The humanitarian disarmament model</h4><ol><li><strong>Frame the issue as humanitarian.</strong> The campaigns presented the weapons as a problem of civilian harm rather than of military balance, which shifted the burden of justification onto users.</li><li><strong>Build a coalition of civil society and smaller states.</strong> The International Campaign to Ban Landmines and the Cluster Munition Coalition each worked with a core group of committed states.</li><li><strong>Leave the consensus forum.</strong> Both treaties were negotiated outside the CCW because its consensus rule allowed a small number of major users to block progress.</li><li><strong>Accept that major powers will not join initially.</strong> The strategy was to establish a norm that would constrain non-parties through stigma, market effects on producers and pressure in coalition operations.</li><li><strong>Include victim assistance and clearance.</strong> Treating the treaty as a humanitarian instrument rather than an arms control one broadened the coalition and gave affected states a direct interest.</li><li><strong>Build strong transparency and meeting machinery.</strong> Annual reporting and regular meetings of states parties sustain pressure over time.</li></ol><p>The model's limits are equally clear. Several of the largest producers and stockpilers of both weapons remain outside both treaties. Use has continued in conflicts involving non-parties and non-state armed groups. Clearance deadlines have been extended repeatedly, and victim assistance obligations depend on funding that has not always followed. The honest assessment is that the model created a strong norm with substantial practical effect on production, trade and stockpiles, while falling short of universal prohibition.</p><div class=\"study-callout\"><strong>Example:</strong> The clearest evidence of the norm's effect is commercial rather than legal. After the Ottawa Convention, the international trade in anti-personnel mines effectively ceased, producers exited the market, and financial institutions in many states adopted policies against investing in producers. None of this bound non-parties, and none of it was required by the treaty, but it changed the practical availability of the weapon. That is what a stigmatising norm does, and it is the strongest argument for the model.</div><h4>7. The African dimension</h4><ul><li>African states were among the strongest supporters of both treaties, reflecting the burden of contamination on the continent.</li><li>Mine clearance in Angola, Mozambique, Chad, Zimbabwe and elsewhere has taken decades and remains incomplete in several states.</li><li>The Ottawa Convention's first review conference was held in Nairobi in 2004, producing the Nairobi Action Plan.</li><li>The African Union has consistently supported universalisation, and the great majority of African states are party to both treaties.</li><li>Victim assistance obligations intersect directly with the CRPD, since most mine and cluster munition survivors are persons with disabilities, and the two frameworks should be implemented together rather than separately.</li></ul><h4>8. How the examiner&#x27;s question should be answered</h4><p>Explain the humanitarian problem before the law, because the treaties only make sense against it. Distinguish Amended Protocol II, which restricts, from the two ban treaties, which prohibit. Give the core obligations of each ban treaty in the same order: prohibition, stockpile destruction, clearance, victim assistance, transparency. Identify the gaps: anti-vehicle mines, the cluster munition exclusions, non-participation by major producers, and the interoperability provision. Then assess the humanitarian disarmament model with both its achievements and its limits, and use the commercial effect as evidence.</p><h4>Summary</h4><ul><li>Anti-personnel mines and cluster munitions cause characteristically post-conflict civilian harm, which is why the campaigns framed them as humanitarian problems.</li><li>CCW Amended Protocol II restricts rather than prohibits, and its perceived inadequacy prompted the ban campaigns.</li><li>The Ottawa Convention prohibits anti-personnel mines absolutely, with stockpile destruction, clearance, victim assistance and reporting obligations, and permits no reservations.</li><li>The Convention on Cluster Munitions prohibits them with defined exclusions, and contains the most detailed victim assistance provision in any disarmament treaty.</li><li>Both were negotiated outside the consensus-bound CCW by coalitions of smaller states and civil society.</li><li>The model created a strong stigmatising norm with real effects on trade and production, without achieving universal participation.</li></ul><h4>Key Terms</h4><div class=\"table-wrap\"><table class=\"study-table\"><thead><tr><th>Term</th><th>Meaning</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Anti-personnel mine</td><td>A mine designed to be exploded by the presence, proximity or contact of a person.</td></tr><tr><td>Cluster munition</td><td>A conventional munition designed to disperse explosive submunitions each weighing less than twenty kilograms.</td></tr><tr><td>Humanitarian disarmament</td><td>The model of negotiating weapons bans outside consensus forums by framing the issue as civilian harm.</td></tr><tr><td>Victim assistance</td><td>Treaty obligations to provide medical care, rehabilitation, psychological support and social and economic inclusion for survivors.</td></tr><tr><td>Interoperability provision</td><td>Convention on Cluster Munitions Article 21, permitting military cooperation with non-parties subject to conditions.</td></tr></tbody></table></div><h4>Review Questions</h4><h5>Multiple choice</h5><div class=\"study-mcq\"><p class=\"mcq-q\">Why were anti-vehicle mines left outside the Ottawa Convention?</p><ol type=\"A\"><li>The definition covers only mines designed to be exploded by the presence, proximity or contact of a person</li><li>They cause no civilian harm</li><li>They are prohibited by another ban treaty</li><li>They were prohibited earlier</li></ol><details class=\"mcq-a\"><summary>Show answer</summary><p><strong>Answer: A.</strong> They remain governed by CCW Amended Protocol II, and sensitive anti-handling devices were a major negotiating issue for this reason.</p></details></div><div class=\"study-mcq\"><p class=\"mcq-q\">What distinguishes CCW Amended Protocol II from the Ottawa Convention?</p><ol type=\"A\"><li>Protocol II restricts the use of mines while Ottawa prohibits anti-personnel mines outright</li><li>Protocol II applies only to cluster munitions</li><li>Ottawa applies only in international armed conflict</li><li>Protocol II has more parties</li></ol><details class=\"mcq-a\"><summary>Show answer</summary><p><strong>Answer: A.</strong> The perceived inadequacy of a restriction-based approach is what prompted campaigners to negotiate a ban elsewhere.</p></details></div><div class=\"study-mcq\"><p class=\"mcq-q\">What is innovative about Article 5 of the Convention on Cluster Munitions?</p><ol type=\"A\"><li>It converts victim assistance into specified state duties including data collection, national planning, budgeting and involvement of victims</li><li>It permits use in self-defence</li><li>It creates an inspection regime</li><li>It prohibits reservations</li></ol><details class=\"mcq-a\"><summary>Show answer</summary><p><strong>Answer: A.</strong> It borrows deliberately from the disability rights framework and is the most detailed such provision in any disarmament treaty.</p></details></div><div class=\"study-mcq\"><p class=\"mcq-q\">What is the strongest evidence that the humanitarian disarmament model works?</p><ol type=\"A\"><li>The international trade in anti-personnel mines effectively ceased and producers exited the market</li><li>All states joined the treaties</li><li>The Security Council enforced the bans</li><li>Non-parties were prosecuted</li></ol><details class=\"mcq-a\"><summary>Show answer</summary><p><strong>Answer: A.</strong> None of that was required by the treaty or binding on non-parties, which is precisely how a stigmatising norm operates.</p></details></div><h5>Short answer</h5><div class=\"study-sa\"><p class=\"sa-q\">Set out the core obligations of the Ottawa Convention.</p><details class=\"sa-a\"><summary>Model answer</summary><p>Each party undertakes never under any circumstances to use, develop, produce, acquire, stockpile, retain or transfer anti-personnel mines, or to assist, encourage or induce anyone to engage in prohibited activity. Stockpiles must be destroyed within four years of entry into force for that state. All mined areas under the state&#x27;s jurisdiction or control must be cleared within ten years, with extensions available on application. Each party in a position to do so must provide assistance for the care, rehabilitation and social and economic reintegration of mine victims. Annual transparency reports must be submitted covering stockpiles, mined areas, destruction and assistance. No reservations are permitted. Anti-vehicle mines fall outside the definition and remain governed by CCW Amended Protocol II.</p></details></div><div class=\"study-sa\"><p class=\"sa-q\">Explain the humanitarian disarmament model and assess it.</p><details class=\"sa-a\"><summary>Model answer</summary><p>The model frames a weapon as a problem of civilian harm rather than of military balance, builds a coalition of civil society organisations and committed smaller states, and negotiates outside consensus-bound forums such as the CCW where major users can block progress. It accepts that major powers will not join initially, relying instead on stigma, effects on production and trade, and pressure within coalitions. It includes clearance and victim assistance so that affected states have a direct interest, and it builds transparency reporting and regular meetings to sustain pressure. Its achievements are substantial: the trade in anti-personnel mines effectively ended, producers exited the market and financial institutions adopted divestment policies. Its limits are equally clear: several of the largest producers and stockpilers remain outside both treaties, use has continued by non-parties and armed groups, clearance deadlines have been extended repeatedly, and victim assistance depends on funding that has not always followed.</p></details></div><h4>Class Activity</h4><ol><li>Find out whether your country is party to the Ottawa Convention and the Convention on Cluster Munitions, and whether it has submitted its transparency reports. Summarise the position in one paragraph.</li><li>Design a victim assistance plan for a mine-affected district, covering the six elements required by the Convention on Cluster Munitions Article 5 and explaining how it would connect to disability rights obligations.</li></ol><h4>Assignment</h4><p>Write one and a half pages on landmines and cluster munitions. Explain the humanitarian problem each weapon causes, describe CCW Amended Protocol II and why campaigners regarded it as inadequate, set out the core obligations of the Ottawa Convention and the Convention on Cluster Munitions in parallel, identify the main gaps in each, and assess the humanitarian disarmament model with evidence of both its achievements and its limits.</p><p class=\"study-print\"><strong>Printable:</strong> use your browser\u2019s Print \u2192 Save as PDF to keep an offline copy.</p></div>",
  "Perfidy, Ruses and Starvation": "<div class=\"study-note\"><div class=\"revision-banner\"><strong>Human Rights and International Humanitarian Law</strong><span>Module 10: Means and Methods of Warfare</span></div><h3 class=\"lesson-note-title\">LESSON NOTE: PERFIDY, RUSES AND STARVATION</h3><div class=\"table-wrap\"><table class=\"study-table\"><thead><tr><th>Subject</th><th>Topic</th><th>Level</th><th>Lesson duration</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Human Rights and International Humanitarian Law</td><td>Perfidy, Ruses and Starvation</td><td>Beginner to Advanced</td><td>40 minutes</td></tr></tbody></table></div><h4>Learning objectives</h4><p>By the end of this lesson you should be able to:</p><ul><li>Define perfidy and distinguish it from lawful ruses</li><li>Identify the acts constituting perfidy and their criminal consequences</li><li>Explain the prohibition of denial of quarter</li><li>Explain the prohibition of starvation and pillage as methods of warfare</li></ul><h4>1. Introduction</h4><p>This lesson covers prohibited methods rather than prohibited weapons. Each of them attacks something the law depends on: perfidy destroys the reliability of the signals by which protection is claimed, denial of quarter removes the incentive to surrender, and starvation and pillage turn the civilian population into the target.</p><h4>2. Perfidy</h4><div class=\"study-defn\"><strong>Perfidy:</strong> Acts inviting the confidence of an adversary to lead them to believe that they are entitled to, or are obliged to accord, protection under the rules of international law applicable in armed conflict, with intent to betray that confidence (AP I Article 37(1)).</div><p>The definition has three elements, all of which must be established.</p><ol><li><strong>An act inviting the confidence of an adversary</strong> regarding entitlement to or obligation to accord protection under IHL.</li><li><strong>Intent to betray that confidence.</strong></li><li><strong>The killing, injuring or capturing</strong> of an adversary by resort to that act. Article 37 prohibits killing, injuring or capturing by perfidy; the act alone without that result is not perfidy in the treaty sense, though it may be an unlawful improper use of an emblem or sign.</li></ol><p>Article 37(1) gives four examples.</p><ul><li>Feigning an intent to negotiate under a flag of truce or of surrender.</li><li>Feigning incapacitation by wounds or sickness.</li><li>Feigning civilian, non-combatant status.</li><li>Feigning protected status by the use of signs, emblems or uniforms of the United Nations or of neutral or other states not parties to the conflict.</li></ul><div class=\"study-callout\"><strong>Note:</strong> Why perfidy is prohibited is more important than the list. If a surrender signal cannot be trusted, no one will accept surrenders. If a red cross cannot be trusted, medical units will be attacked. Perfidy is prohibited because it destroys the currency of the protective signals on which the whole system depends, and it harms most those who rely on those signals honestly.</div><h4>3. Ruses of war</h4><div class=\"study-defn\"><strong>Ruse of war:</strong> An act intended to mislead an adversary or to induce them to act recklessly, which infringes no rule of international law applicable in armed conflict and which is not perfidious because it does not invite the confidence of an adversary with respect to protection under that law (AP I Article 37(2)).</div><div class=\"table-wrap\"><table class=\"study-table\"><thead><tr><th>Lawful ruse</th><th>Perfidy</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Camouflage and concealment</td><td>Feigning civilian status to approach and kill</td></tr><tr><td>Decoys, dummy tanks and false installations</td><td>Feigning surrender to draw the enemy into the open</td></tr><tr><td>Mock operations and feints</td><td>Feigning wounds to attack a medic</td></tr><tr><td>Misinformation and false radio traffic</td><td>Using an ambulance to transport an assault team</td></tr><tr><td>Simulating quiet or inactivity</td><td>Using the red cross to shield an attack</td></tr><tr><td>Psychological operations</td><td>Wearing enemy uniform in combat</td></tr></tbody></table></div><p>The distinction is not between deception and honesty. War permits deception. The line is whether the deception exploits the protective rules of IHL. Camouflage exploits the enemy's eyes; feigning surrender exploits the enemy's obligation to accept a surrender.</p><ol><li><strong>Enemy uniforms and flags.</strong> Additional Protocol I Article 39(2) prohibits making use of the flags, military emblems, insignia or uniforms of adverse parties while engaging in attacks or in order to shield, favour, protect or impede military operations. Their use outside those situations is not prohibited by that article, and practice on espionage and escape has varied.</li><li><strong>Neutral and UN emblems.</strong> Article 39(1) prohibits the use in an armed conflict of the flags, emblems, insignia or uniforms of neutral or other states not parties to the conflict. The improper use of the UN emblem is prohibited by Article 38(2).</li><li><strong>The distinctive emblem.</strong> Improper use of the red cross, red crescent or red crystal is prohibited by Article 38(1), and perfidious use resulting in death or serious injury is a grave breach under Article 85(3)(f).</li></ol><h4>4. Denial of quarter</h4><blockquote class=\"study-quote\">It is prohibited to order that there shall be no survivors, to threaten an adversary therewith or to conduct hostilities on this basis.</blockquote><p>Additional Protocol I Article 40, and Protocol II Article 4(1) for non-international armed conflict. Three points follow.</p><ol><li>The prohibition covers the order, the threat and the conduct of hostilities on that basis. A commander who issues such an order commits the offence whether or not it is carried out.</li><li>It is a war crime under Rome Statute Article 8(2)(b)(xii) and (e)(x), declaring that no quarter will be given.</li><li>The rationale is the same as for perfidy: if surrender will not be accepted, no one surrenders, fighting continues to the death on both sides, and casualties rise. Prohibiting denial of quarter is in the interest of every party.</li></ol><p>Related is Article 41, which protects persons hors de combat. A person is hors de combat if in the power of an adverse party, if they clearly express an intention to surrender, or if they have been rendered unconscious or are otherwise incapacitated by wounds or sickness and are therefore incapable of defending themselves, provided in each case they abstain from any hostile act and do not attempt to escape. Attacking such a person is prohibited and is a war crime. Article 42 separately prohibits attacking a person parachuting from an aircraft in distress during their descent, though airborne troops are excluded.</p><h4>5. Starvation as a method of warfare</h4><p>Covered in the relief lesson from the perspective of access; here it is treated as a prohibited method.</p><ol><li>Additional Protocol I Article 54(1) and Protocol II Article 14 prohibit starvation of civilians as a method of warfare, in terms.</li><li>Article 54(2) prohibits attacking, destroying, removing or rendering useless objects indispensable to the survival of the civilian population, for the specific purpose of denying their sustenance value to the civilian population or to the adverse party, whatever the motive.</li><li>Article 54(3) provides a narrow exception for objects used as sustenance solely for members of the armed forces or in direct support of military action, but no action may be taken which may be expected to leave the civilian population with such inadequate food or water as to cause its starvation or force its movement.</li><li>Article 54(5) permits a party to derogate from these protections within its own national territory under its own control where required by imperative military necessity in defence against invasion, which is the scorched earth exception.</li><li>Rome Statute Article 8(2)(b)(xxv) makes intentionally using starvation of civilians as a method of warfare a war crime in international armed conflict, extended to non-international armed conflict by the 2019 amendment.</li></ol><h4>6. Pillage and other prohibited methods</h4><div class=\"table-wrap\"><table class=\"study-table\"><thead><tr><th>Method</th><th>Rule</th><th>Source</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Pillage</td><td>Prohibited absolutely; also a war crime</td><td>GC IV Art 33; AP II Art 4(2)(g); Rome Statute Art 8</td></tr><tr><td>Collective punishment</td><td>Prohibited</td><td>GC IV Art 33; AP I Art 75(2)(d); AP II Art 4(2)(b)</td></tr><tr><td>Hostage-taking</td><td>Prohibited; a grave breach</td><td>Common Art 3; GC IV Art 34; Rome Statute Art 8</td></tr><tr><td>Terror against the civilian population</td><td>Prohibited</td><td>AP I Art 51(2); AP II Art 13(2)</td></tr><tr><td>Forced displacement</td><td>Prohibited unless required by civilian security or imperative military reasons</td><td>GC IV Art 49; AP II Art 17</td></tr><tr><td>Compelling nationals of the adverse party to take part in operations against their own country</td><td>Prohibited; a grave breach</td><td>GC III Art 130; GC IV Art 147; Rome Statute Art 8</td></tr><tr><td>Declaring abolished the rights and actions of the nationals of the hostile party</td><td>Prohibited</td><td>Hague Regs Art 23(h); Rome Statute Art 8</td></tr><tr><td>Reprisals against protected persons and objects</td><td>Prohibited</td><td>GC I-IV; AP I Arts 20, 51-56</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"study-callout\"><strong>Example:</strong> Soldiers enter a village after the defenders withdraw, take livestock, radios and money for themselves, and burn the houses. Three distinct violations occur. Taking property for private gain is pillage, prohibited absolutely and a war crime. Burning the houses is destruction of property not justified by military necessity, a grave breach where extensive and carried out wantonly. If the purpose was to punish the village for the defenders' presence, it is also a collective penalty prohibited by Article 33. Identifying all three, rather than describing the incident generally, is what a competent legal analysis does.</div><h4>7. How the examiner&#x27;s question should be answered</h4><p>For perfidy, give the three-element definition and apply each element to the facts; the third, that a person must be killed, injured or captured by resort to the act, is the one most often missed. Then distinguish ruses with the principle that deception is lawful unless it exploits the protective rules. On denial of quarter, state that the order and the threat are themselves prohibited. On starvation, give the purpose element and the Article 54(3) and (5) exceptions, which candidates rarely know. Where facts disclose several prohibited methods, identify each separately rather than characterising the conduct once.</p><h4>Summary</h4><ul><li>Perfidy requires an act inviting the adversary&#x27;s confidence in protection, intent to betray it, and the killing, injuring or capturing of an adversary by resort to it.</li><li>Ruses are lawful: deception is prohibited only where it exploits the protective rules of international humanitarian law.</li><li>Using enemy uniforms while engaging in attacks or to shield operations is prohibited, as is use of neutral and United Nations emblems.</li><li>Ordering that there be no survivors, threatening it, or conducting hostilities on that basis is prohibited and is a war crime.</li><li>Persons hors de combat may not be attacked, and Article 42 protects those parachuting from an aircraft in distress.</li><li>Starvation of civilians as a method of warfare is prohibited, with narrow exceptions in Article 54(3) and a scorched earth derogation in Article 54(5).</li></ul><h4>Key Terms</h4><div class=\"table-wrap\"><table class=\"study-table\"><thead><tr><th>Term</th><th>Meaning</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Perfidy</td><td>Killing, injuring or capturing an adversary by inviting and betraying their confidence in protection under IHL.</td></tr><tr><td>Ruse of war</td><td>Lawful deception that does not exploit the protective rules of international humanitarian law.</td></tr><tr><td>Denial of quarter</td><td>Ordering, threatening or conducting hostilities on the basis that there will be no survivors.</td></tr><tr><td>Hors de combat</td><td>A person in the power of an adverse party, expressing surrender, or incapacitated, who abstains from hostile acts.</td></tr><tr><td>Pillage</td><td>Taking property for private gain, prohibited absolutely and a war crime.</td></tr></tbody></table></div><h4>Review Questions</h4><h5>Multiple choice</h5><div class=\"study-mcq\"><p class=\"mcq-q\">Which is perfidy rather than a lawful ruse?</p><ol type=\"A\"><li>Feigning surrender in order to draw the enemy into the open and attack them</li><li>Using camouflage netting</li><li>Deploying dummy tanks</li><li>Broadcasting false radio traffic</li></ol><details class=\"mcq-a\"><summary>Show answer</summary><p><strong>Answer: A.</strong> The line is whether the deception exploits the protective rules of IHL; camouflage exploits the enemy&#x27;s eyes, feigned surrender exploits their obligation to accept it.</p></details></div><div class=\"study-mcq\"><p class=\"mcq-q\">What must occur for an act to constitute perfidy under Article 37?</p><ol type=\"A\"><li>An adversary must be killed, injured or captured by resort to the act</li><li>The act alone suffices</li><li>A commander must order it</li><li>An emblem must be involved</li></ol><details class=\"mcq-a\"><summary>Show answer</summary><p><strong>Answer: A.</strong> The act without that result may still be an unlawful improper use of an emblem or sign, but it is not perfidy in the treaty sense.</p></details></div><div class=\"study-mcq\"><p class=\"mcq-q\">Is it an offence to order that no quarter be given even if no one is killed as a result?</p><ol type=\"A\"><li>Yes; the order and the threat are themselves prohibited and constitute a war crime</li><li>No; a death is required</li><li>Only in international armed conflict</li><li>Only if the order is written</li></ol><details class=\"mcq-a\"><summary>Show answer</summary><p><strong>Answer: A.</strong> Article 40 prohibits ordering it, threatening it and conducting hostilities on that basis, and the rationale is that unaccepted surrender raises casualties on both sides.</p></details></div><div class=\"study-mcq\"><p class=\"mcq-q\">What does Article 54(5) of Additional Protocol I permit?</p><ol type=\"A\"><li>A party to derogate from the protection of objects indispensable to survival within its own territory under its own control where imperative military necessity requires it in defence against invasion</li><li>Attacks on food supplies anywhere</li><li>Sieges of any duration</li><li>Destruction of crops in occupied territory</li></ol><details class=\"mcq-a\"><summary>Show answer</summary><p><strong>Answer: A.</strong> It is the scorched earth exception and is confined to a party&#x27;s own national territory under its own control.</p></details></div><h5>Short answer</h5><div class=\"study-sa\"><p class=\"sa-q\">Define perfidy and distinguish it from a lawful ruse with examples.</p><details class=\"sa-a\"><summary>Model answer</summary><p>Perfidy consists of acts inviting the confidence of an adversary to lead them to believe they are entitled to, or obliged to accord, protection under international humanitarian law, with intent to betray that confidence, where an adversary is killed, injured or captured by resort to that act. Examples include feigning surrender or an intent to negotiate under a flag of truce, feigning incapacitation by wounds, feigning civilian status, and feigning protected status through United Nations or neutral emblems or uniforms. A ruse of war is an act intended to mislead the adversary that infringes no rule of international law and does not invite confidence with respect to protection under that law: camouflage, decoys, feints, false radio traffic and simulated inactivity are all lawful. The distinction is not between deception and honesty, since war permits deception, but between deception that exploits the enemy&#x27;s senses and deception that exploits the protective rules on which the system depends.</p></details></div><div class=\"study-sa\"><p class=\"sa-q\">Explain why perfidy and denial of quarter are prohibited.</p><details class=\"sa-a\"><summary>Model answer</summary><p>Both prohibitions protect the reliability of signals on which the whole protective system rests. If a flag of truce, a surrender, a red cross or an apparent civilian cannot be trusted, then combatants will stop honouring those signals: surrenders will be refused, medical units attacked and civilians treated as threats. The people harmed most are those who use the signals honestly. Denial of quarter operates on the same logic in reverse: if a party announces that no survivors will be taken, the adversary has no reason to surrender and will fight to the death, so casualties rise on both sides. Each prohibition is therefore in the interest of every party to a conflict, which is why both are absolute and why the order and the threat, not merely the outcome, are prohibited in the case of denial of quarter.</p></details></div><h4>Class Activity</h4><ol><li>Take five examples of battlefield deception from military history. Classify each as a lawful ruse or as perfidy and explain your reasoning in one sentence each.</li><li>Draft a one-page instruction for soldiers explaining what deception is permitted and what is prohibited, using concrete examples rather than legal terminology.</li></ol><h4>Assignment</h4><p>Write one and a half pages on prohibited methods of warfare. Define perfidy with its three elements and explain why it is prohibited, distinguish it from lawful ruses with examples and set out the rules on enemy and neutral emblems, explain the prohibition of denial of quarter and the protection of persons hors de combat, set out the prohibition of starvation with its exceptions, and list the other prohibited methods with their sources.</p><p class=\"study-print\"><strong>Printable:</strong> use your browser\u2019s Print \u2192 Save as PDF to keep an offline copy.</p></div>",
  "Explosive Weapons in Populated Areas": "<div class=\"study-note\"><div class=\"revision-banner\"><strong>Human Rights and International Humanitarian Law</strong><span>Module 10: Means and Methods of Warfare</span></div><h3 class=\"lesson-note-title\">LESSON NOTE: EXPLOSIVE WEAPONS IN POPULATED AREAS</h3><div class=\"table-wrap\"><table class=\"study-table\"><thead><tr><th>Subject</th><th>Topic</th><th>Level</th><th>Lesson duration</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Human Rights and International Humanitarian Law</td><td>Explosive Weapons in Populated Areas</td><td>Beginner to Advanced</td><td>40 minutes</td></tr></tbody></table></div><h4>Learning objectives</h4><p>By the end of this lesson you should be able to:</p><ul><li>Explain what explosive weapons with wide area effects are</li><li>Describe the humanitarian pattern of harm they produce</li><li>Apply the targeting rules to their use in populated areas</li><li>Describe the 2022 political declaration and the policy debate</li></ul><h4>1. Introduction</h4><p>No treaty prohibits explosive weapons in populated areas. The issue arises entirely from the application of existing rules to a recurring pattern of use, and it is the most active area of contemporary humanitarian policy. It also illustrates how a norm develops before any treaty exists.</p><h4>2. What the problem is</h4><div class=\"study-defn\"><strong>Explosive weapons with wide area effects:</strong> Explosive weapons whose effects extend over a large area, whether because of a large destructive radius, a lack of accuracy, or the delivery of multiple munitions across an area.</div><div class=\"table-wrap\"><table class=\"study-table\"><thead><tr><th>Source of wide area effect</th><th>Examples</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Large blast and fragmentation radius</td><td>Large aircraft bombs; heavy artillery shells; thermobaric munitions</td></tr><tr><td>Inaccuracy</td><td>Unguided rockets; improvised rocket-assisted munitions; indirect fire without observation</td></tr><tr><td>Multiple munitions across an area</td><td>Multiple launch rocket systems; cluster munitions; mortar barrages</td></tr></tbody></table></div><p>Recorded data compiled by monitoring organisations has consistently shown that when explosive weapons are used in populated areas, the great majority of those killed and injured are civilians, and that the proportion is far lower when the same weapons are used elsewhere. That single statistical contrast is the empirical foundation of the entire debate.</p><h4>3. The pattern of harm</h4><ol><li><strong>Direct casualties.</strong> Deaths and injuries from blast and fragmentation, with characteristic injury patterns including traumatic amputation, blast lung, burns and penetrating fragment wounds.</li><li><strong>Damage to housing.</strong> Destruction of the housing stock is the leading driver of displacement.</li><li><strong>Damage to essential services.</strong> Water, electricity, sanitation, health facilities and schools are concentrated in populated areas and are damaged both directly and through interconnected systems.</li><li><strong>Reverberating effects.</strong> Loss of water treatment produces disease; loss of electricity closes hospitals and cold chains; loss of schools interrupts education for years. These effects typically cause more deaths over time than the original blast.</li><li><strong>Explosive remnants.</strong> Unexploded ordnance in rubble kills and injures long after hostilities and obstructs reconstruction.</li><li><strong>Displacement and its consequences.</strong> Protracted displacement produces its own health, education and protection harms.</li></ol><div class=\"study-callout\"><strong>Note:</strong> The reverberating effects point is the analytical heart of this topic. A strike that kills three people directly may, by disabling a water treatment plant, contribute to hundreds of deaths from waterborne disease over the following year. If the proportionality assessment counts only the three, it is not assessing the actual expected harm. The ICRC and a growing body of state practice hold that reasonably foreseeable reverberating effects must be included.</div><h4>4. Applying the existing rules</h4><div class=\"table-wrap\"><table class=\"study-table\"><thead><tr><th>Rule</th><th>Application in populated areas</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Distinction</td><td>Military objectives in a city are typically surrounded by civilians and civilian objects; identification must be correspondingly precise</td></tr><tr><td>Indiscriminate attacks</td><td>Article 51(5)(a) treats as indiscriminate an attack treating several clearly separated military objectives in a populated area as a single objective, which directly addresses area bombardment</td></tr><tr><td>Weapons indiscriminate by nature</td><td>A weapon whose effects cannot be limited as required is prohibited; in a populated area, a weapon with a wide effect radius may be incapable of being limited even though it is lawful elsewhere</td></tr><tr><td>Proportionality</td><td>Expected harm must include reverberating effects; the concentration of civilians and services in a city makes excessive harm far more likely</td></tr><tr><td>Precautions</td><td>Weapon selection, fuzing, angle of attack and timing carry greater weight; the duty to choose between objectives and to warn is more often engaged</td></tr><tr><td>Objects indispensable to survival and works containing dangerous forces</td><td>Water systems and power stations are concentrated in cities and attract their own protections</td></tr></tbody></table></div><p>The correct legal statement is therefore not that using explosive weapons in populated areas is unlawful, but that the existing rules apply with particular stringency and that certain weapons, lawful in open terrain, cannot in practice satisfy them in a city.</p><h4>5. The 2022 Political Declaration</h4><div class=\"table-wrap\"><table class=\"study-table\"><thead><tr><th>Feature</th><th>Detail</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Full name</td><td>Political Declaration on Strengthening the Protection of Civilians from the Humanitarian Consequences arising from the use of Explosive Weapons in Populated Areas</td></tr><tr><td>Adopted</td><td>Dublin, November 2022, following a process led by Ireland</td></tr><tr><td>Status</td><td>Political commitment, not a treaty; not legally binding</td></tr><tr><td>Core commitment</td><td>To restrict or refrain from the use of explosive weapons in populated areas when their use may be expected to cause harm to civilians or civilian objects</td></tr><tr><td>Other commitments</td><td>Reviewing and improving national policies and practices; collecting and sharing data on harm; facilitating humanitarian access; assisting victims; sharing good practice on operational policy</td></tr></tbody></table></div><ol><li><strong>The 'restrict or refrain' formulation</strong> was the central negotiating compromise. Campaigners sought a commitment to avoid use of explosive weapons with wide area effects in populated areas; several militarily active states resisted any presumption against use. The final wording commits states to restrict or refrain where harm may be expected, which is weaker than a presumption but stronger than a restatement of existing law.</li><li><strong>Data collection</strong> is a significant commitment in practice, because the debate has been shaped by data and better data will shape policy further.</li><li><strong>The declaration does not create law</strong>, and stating that clearly matters. Its function is to build practice, and consistent practice supported by a sense of obligation is how custom forms.</li><li><strong>Implementation</strong> is through national policy: rules of engagement, weapon allocation, collateral damage estimation methodology, and post-strike assessment.</li></ol><h4>6. Good practice in operational policy</h4><ul><li>Establishing a presumption against the use of weapons with a wide effect radius in populated areas, rebuttable by specific justification at an appropriate command level.</li><li>Setting weapon-specific restrictions by environment: which munitions may be used in which settings.</li><li>Requiring collateral damage estimation methodology that incorporates reverberating effects and the presence of essential services.</li><li>Mapping critical infrastructure in advance and treating water, power and medical facilities as no-strike locations absent specific authorisation.</li><li>Requiring pattern of life analysis before strikes in populated areas.</li><li>Post-strike assessment and systematic recording of civilian harm, with a feedback loop into targeting policy.</li><li>Making amends: acknowledgment and assistance to those harmed, distinct from any admission of unlawfulness.</li></ul><div class=\"study-callout\"><strong>Example:</strong> A force adopts a rule that indirect fire from artillery and multiple launch rocket systems is prohibited within a defined distance of a populated area unless authorised at brigade level with a documented collateral damage estimate that includes water and power infrastructure. This is not required by any treaty. It is a policy choice implementing existing precautionary obligations, and it is exactly the kind of measure the 2022 declaration commits states to develop. Where enough forces adopt such rules, the practice may in time harden into custom.</div><h4>7. How the examiner&#x27;s question should be answered</h4><p>State clearly at the outset that no treaty prohibits these weapons in populated areas and that the question is one of applying existing rules. Explain wide area effects with the three sources. Set out the pattern of harm, giving reverberating effects the most attention. Then apply each targeting rule with the specific stringency it acquires in a city, and make the point that a weapon lawful in open terrain may be incapable of lawful use in a populated area. Describe the 2022 declaration accurately as a political commitment with the 'restrict or refrain' formulation, and explain how such declarations contribute to custom without themselves being law.</p><h4>Summary</h4><ul><li>No treaty prohibits explosive weapons in populated areas; the issue is the application of existing rules to a recurring pattern of use.</li><li>Wide area effects arise from a large destructive radius, from inaccuracy, or from delivering multiple munitions across an area.</li><li>When these weapons are used in populated areas the great majority of casualties are civilians, and the proportion is far lower elsewhere.</li><li>Reverberating effects, including deaths from loss of water, power and health services, must be included in the proportionality assessment.</li><li>A weapon lawful in open terrain may be incapable of satisfying the rules in a city, which is a conclusion about use rather than about the weapon.</li><li>The 2022 Political Declaration commits states to restrict or refrain from such use where civilian harm may be expected, and builds practice rather than creating law.</li></ul><h4>Key Terms</h4><div class=\"table-wrap\"><table class=\"study-table\"><thead><tr><th>Term</th><th>Meaning</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Wide area effects</td><td>Effects extending over a large area through blast radius, inaccuracy, or the delivery of multiple munitions.</td></tr><tr><td>Reverberating effects</td><td>Indirect consequences of an attack, such as deaths from the collapse of water or health services.</td></tr><tr><td>Political Declaration</td><td>The 2022 Dublin commitment on explosive weapons in populated areas, politically but not legally binding.</td></tr><tr><td>Collateral damage estimation</td><td>The structured methodology producing an estimate of expected civilian harm before a strike.</td></tr><tr><td>Making amends</td><td>Acknowledgment and assistance to those harmed, distinct from any admission of unlawfulness.</td></tr></tbody></table></div><h4>Review Questions</h4><h5>Multiple choice</h5><div class=\"study-mcq\"><p class=\"mcq-q\">Is the use of explosive weapons in populated areas prohibited by treaty?</p><ol type=\"A\"><li>No; the issue is the application of existing rules on distinction, proportionality and precautions</li><li>Yes, by the 2022 Political Declaration</li><li>Yes, by CCW Protocol V</li><li>Yes, in non-international armed conflict only</li></ol><details class=\"mcq-a\"><summary>Show answer</summary><p><strong>Answer: A.</strong> The 2022 declaration is a political commitment, not a treaty, and its function is to build practice rather than to create law.</p></details></div><div class=\"study-mcq\"><p class=\"mcq-q\">What is the empirical foundation of the debate?</p><ol type=\"A\"><li>Data showing that the great majority of casualties are civilian when these weapons are used in populated areas, and a far lower proportion elsewhere</li><li>Judgments of the International Criminal Court</li><li>A Security Council resolution</li><li>The Martens Clause</li></ol><details class=\"mcq-a\"><summary>Show answer</summary><p><strong>Answer: A.</strong> That statistical contrast is what shifted the issue from a general concern to a specific policy question.</p></details></div><div class=\"study-mcq\"><p class=\"mcq-q\">Why do reverberating effects matter so much in this context?</p><ol type=\"A\"><li>Loss of water, power and health services often causes more deaths over time than the original blast</li><li>They are easier to measure than direct casualties</li><li>They affect only combatants</li><li>They are expressly listed in Additional Protocol I</li></ol><details class=\"mcq-a\"><summary>Show answer</summary><p><strong>Answer: A.</strong> A proportionality assessment counting only immediate casualties is not assessing the actual expected harm.</p></details></div><div class=\"study-mcq\"><p class=\"mcq-q\">What was the central compromise in the 2022 Political Declaration?</p><ol type=\"A\"><li>The &#x27;restrict or refrain&#x27; formulation, weaker than a presumption against use but stronger than a restatement of existing law</li><li>A binding prohibition on cluster munitions</li><li>Establishment of an inspection regime</li><li>A ban on artillery</li></ol><details class=\"mcq-a\"><summary>Show answer</summary><p><strong>Answer: A.</strong> Campaigners sought a commitment to avoid wide area effect weapons in populated areas and several militarily active states resisted any presumption against use.</p></details></div><h5>Short answer</h5><div class=\"study-sa\"><p class=\"sa-q\">Explain how the existing targeting rules apply with particular stringency in populated areas.</p><details class=\"sa-a\"><summary>Model answer</summary><p>Distinction requires more precise identification, because military objectives in a city are surrounded by civilians and civilian objects. Article 51(5)(a) directly addresses area bombardment by treating as indiscriminate any attack that treats several clearly separated military objectives in a populated area as a single objective. The prohibition of weapons indiscriminate by nature acquires practical bite, because a weapon whose effects cannot be limited as the law requires may be incapable of lawful use in a city even though it is lawful in open terrain. Proportionality is harder to satisfy because civilians and essential services are concentrated, and the expected harm must include reasonably foreseeable reverberating effects. Precautions carry greater weight in weapon selection, fuzing, angle and timing, and the duties to choose between objectives and to give effective warning are more often engaged.</p></details></div><div class=\"study-sa\"><p class=\"sa-q\">Describe the 2022 Political Declaration and explain its legal significance.</p><details class=\"sa-a\"><summary>Model answer</summary><p>The Political Declaration on Strengthening the Protection of Civilians from the Humanitarian Consequences arising from the use of Explosive Weapons in Populated Areas was adopted in Dublin in November 2022 after a process led by Ireland. Its core commitment is to restrict or refrain from the use of explosive weapons in populated areas when their use may be expected to cause harm to civilians or civilian objects. It also commits states to review and improve national policies and practices, to collect and share data on civilian harm, to facilitate humanitarian access, to assist victims and to share good practice. It is a political commitment and creates no legal obligation. Its significance lies in building consistent state practice through national rules of engagement, weapon allocation policies and collateral damage estimation methodology, since practice supported by a sense of obligation is how customary law forms.</p></details></div><h4>Class Activity</h4><ol><li>Take a reported strike in a populated area. List the direct effects and then the reverberating effects, and estimate which produced more harm over the following year.</li><li>Draft a two-page operational policy on the use of indirect fire in populated areas, covering weapon restrictions, authorisation levels, estimation methodology and post-strike assessment.</li></ol><h4>Assignment</h4><p>Write one and a half pages on explosive weapons in populated areas. Explain what wide area effects are and how they arise, describe the characteristic pattern of harm with attention to reverberating effects, apply each targeting rule with the stringency it acquires in a city and explain why a weapon lawful elsewhere may be unusable there, describe the 2022 Political Declaration and its central compromise, and explain how such a declaration contributes to the development of law without itself being law.</p><p class=\"study-print\"><strong>Printable:</strong> use your browser\u2019s Print \u2192 Save as PDF to keep an offline copy.</p></div>",
  "New Technologies and Cyber Operations": "<div class=\"study-note\"><div class=\"revision-banner\"><strong>Human Rights and International Humanitarian Law</strong><span>Module 10: Means and Methods of Warfare</span></div><h3 class=\"lesson-note-title\">LESSON NOTE: NEW TECHNOLOGIES AND CYBER OPERATIONS</h3><div class=\"table-wrap\"><table class=\"study-table\"><thead><tr><th>Subject</th><th>Topic</th><th>Level</th><th>Lesson duration</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Human Rights and International Humanitarian Law</td><td>New Technologies and Cyber Operations</td><td>Beginner to Advanced</td><td>45 minutes</td></tr></tbody></table></div><h4>Learning objectives</h4><p>By the end of this lesson you should be able to:</p><ul><li>Explain how international humanitarian law applies to cyber operations</li><li>Describe the debates about autonomous weapon systems</li><li>Explain the issues raised by artificial intelligence in targeting</li><li>Apply the Article 36 review obligation to new technologies</li></ul><h4>1. Introduction</h4><p>New technologies do not create legal vacuums. International humanitarian law applies to any means or method of warfare, however novel, and the Martens Clause prevents the absence of a specific rule from being treated as permission. What new technologies do create is genuine difficulty in applying rules designed for physical objects to things that are not physical, not directed by a person in real time, or not fully explicable.</p><h4>2. Cyber operations</h4><div class=\"study-defn\"><strong>Cyber operation:</strong> An operation against or via a computer or computer system through a data stream, whether to gather information, to disrupt or degrade functionality, or to cause physical damage.</div><ol><li><strong>IHL applies.</strong> This is now broadly accepted, though a small number of states initially argued otherwise. The International Group of Experts producing the Tallinn Manual and its second edition, the ICRC, and the great majority of states that have published national positions all take this view.</li><li><strong>The threshold question.</strong> IHL applies only in armed conflict, so the first question is whether a cyber operation can itself trigger one. The prevailing view is that a cyber operation causing physical damage, injury or death comparable to a kinetic attack can amount to an armed conflict, while espionage, data theft and disruption falling short of that do not.</li><li><strong>What is an 'attack'.</strong> Additional Protocol I Article 49 defines attacks as acts of violence against the adversary. Whether an operation causing no physical damage, such as one disabling a system without breaking anything, is an attack is genuinely contested. The narrow view requires physical damage or injury. The broader view, taken by the ICRC, is that an operation rendering an object useless is an attack even without physical destruction, because otherwise the civilian population could be deprived of essential services with no rule engaged.</li><li><strong>Data as an object.</strong> Whether civilian data is a civilian object is unresolved. If it is not, deleting the medical records of a hospital or the civil registry of a state engages no targeting rule, which most commentators regard as an unacceptable result. The ICRC argues that essential civilian data should be protected, and several states have adopted that position.</li><li><strong>Interconnection.</strong> Military and civilian networks share infrastructure to an extent that has no parallel in the physical world, so the dual-use analysis and the assessment of reverberating effects are unusually demanding.</li><li><strong>Attribution.</strong> Technical attribution of a cyber operation is difficult and slow, which affects both compliance assessment and accountability.</li></ol><div class=\"study-callout\"><strong>Note:</strong> The Tallinn Manual is not a treaty and has no legal status. It is the product of an independent group of experts and states its rules as the group's assessment of existing law. Cite it as such: describing it as binding, or as the position of NATO, is inaccurate and is the kind of error examiners look for.</div><h4>3. Autonomous weapon systems</h4><div class=\"study-defn\"><strong>Autonomous weapon system:</strong> A weapon system that, once activated, can select and engage targets without further intervention by a human operator.</div><div class=\"table-wrap\"><table class=\"study-table\"><thead><tr><th>Position</th><th>Content</th><th>Held by</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Prohibition</td><td>A legally binding instrument prohibiting systems that cannot be used with meaningful human control, and regulating others</td><td>A large group of states, the ICRC in substance, and civil society campaigns</td></tr><tr><td>Regulation</td><td>Existing law suffices; the answer is rigorous Article 36 review and national policy on human control</td><td>Several militarily advanced states</td></tr><tr><td>No new instrument</td><td>Existing law applies and no new instrument is needed or desirable</td><td>A small number of states</td></tr></tbody></table></div><p>The substantive concerns are five and should be stated separately.</p><ol><li><strong>Distinction.</strong> Can a system reliably distinguish civilians from combatants, and recognise a person hors de combat or attempting to surrender, in an unstructured environment?</li><li><strong>Proportionality.</strong> The assessment requires a contextual value judgement about whether expected harm is excessive relative to anticipated advantage. Whether that judgement can be delegated to a system at all, rather than whether a system can perform it accurately, is the deeper question.</li><li><strong>Precautions.</strong> The duty to cancel or suspend requires continuing judgement as circumstances change.</li><li><strong>Accountability.</strong> If a system commits what would be a war crime, who is responsible? The programmer, the commander who deployed it, the state? Individual criminal responsibility requires a mental element that a system cannot possess, and the risk is an accountability gap.</li><li><strong>Dignity.</strong> A distinct argument, independent of performance: that a decision to take human life should not be made by a machine, regardless of how accurately it could be made. This is a Martens Clause argument, resting on the dictates of public conscience.</li></ol><p>The CCW Group of Governmental Experts has considered the question since 2014 and adopted eleven guiding principles in 2019, including that IHL applies fully, that human responsibility for decisions on the use of weapon systems must be retained, and that accountability must be ensured. Progress towards an instrument has been blocked by the consensus rule, and the discussion has increasingly moved to the General Assembly, which adopted resolutions on the subject from 2023.</p><div class=\"study-defn\"><strong>Meaningful human control:</strong> The concept, not yet defined in any instrument, that a human must exercise sufficient judgement and control over the use of force for legal and moral responsibility to attach.</div><h4>4. Artificial intelligence in targeting</h4><p>Distinct from autonomous weapons, and increasingly the more immediate issue: AI systems that generate or recommend targets for human decision.</p><ol><li><strong>Speed and volume.</strong> A system generating targets faster than humans can meaningfully review them creates a risk that human involvement becomes nominal. The precautionary duty to verify requires actual verification, not the appearance of it.</li><li><strong>Automation bias.</strong> Human operators tend to accept machine recommendations, particularly under time pressure and high workload. A human in the loop who approves without independent assessment does not satisfy the verification obligation.</li><li><strong>Explainability.</strong> Where a system's output cannot be explained, the commander cannot assess its reliability, which is required for the verification duty.</li><li><strong>Training data.</strong> A system trained on biased or unrepresentative data will produce systematically skewed outputs, and the bias may be invisible to the operator.</li><li><strong>Aggregation of predictive indicators.</strong> Systems that infer status from patterns of behaviour, association or communication risk treating civilians as targets on the basis of correlation, which is not the legal test for direct participation.</li><li><strong>Record and review.</strong> Systems should log the basis of recommendations so that decisions can be reviewed afterwards, which is necessary for accountability.</li></ol><h4>5. Applying Article 36 to new technologies</h4><div class=\"table-wrap\"><table class=\"study-table\"><thead><tr><th>Question</th><th>New technology application</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Is it prohibited by name?</td><td>Usually not, since the technology postdates the treaties</td></tr><tr><td>Superfluous injury?</td><td>Rarely the issue for cyber or autonomy; relevant for novel munitions</td></tr><tr><td>Indiscriminate by nature?</td><td>Central for cyber tools that propagate uncontrollably, and for autonomous systems in unstructured environments</td></tr><tr><td>Can it be used consistently with the rules?</td><td>The decisive question: can the system be operated so that distinction, proportionality and precautions are actually satisfied?</td></tr><tr><td>Environmental damage?</td><td>Relevant for some novel weapons</td></tr><tr><td>Martens Clause</td><td>Directly engaged by autonomy and by the dignity argument</td></tr><tr><td>Restrictions rather than prohibition?</td><td>Usually the practical outcome: permitted in defined environments with defined human involvement</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"study-callout\"><strong>Example:</strong> A state develops a loitering munition that can be operated in two modes: human-selected targets, and an autonomous mode selecting vehicles matching a signature within a defined area. An Article 36 review would likely conclude that the system is lawful in the first mode; that the autonomous mode may be lawful over open terrain against a narrowly defined military signature with a short loiter time and a defined geographic box; and that the autonomous mode cannot satisfy the rules in a populated area, where distinguishing a military from a civilian vehicle by signature is unreliable and where a proportionality assessment cannot be made in advance. The review's output is a set of use restrictions, not a single verdict. That is what a well-conducted review of a new technology looks like.</div><h4>6. How the examiner&#x27;s question should be answered</h4><p>Open by stating that IHL applies to new technologies and that the Martens Clause blocks any argument from the absence of a specific rule. For cyber, take the threshold question, the definition of attack, and the data question in that order, and identify which are settled and which are contested; saying 'this is unresolved' where it is unresolved is a strength, not a weakness. For autonomy, give the three state positions and the five substantive concerns, and keep the dignity argument separate from the performance arguments, since they are logically independent. For AI in targeting, focus on whether human involvement is real or nominal. Close with Article 36 and the point that the outcome is usually a restriction on use.</p><h4>Summary</h4><ul><li>International humanitarian law applies to new technologies, and the Martens Clause prevents the absence of a specific rule from meaning permission.</li><li>Cyber operations causing physical damage comparable to kinetic attack can trigger armed conflict; espionage and disruption below that threshold do not.</li><li>Whether a cyber operation causing no physical damage is an attack, and whether civilian data is a civilian object, are both contested.</li><li>Autonomous weapons raise five concerns: distinction, proportionality, precautions, accountability, and human dignity.</li><li>The dignity argument is independent of performance and rests on the Martens Clause.</li><li>Artificial intelligence in targeting risks making human involvement nominal through speed, automation bias and unexplainable output.</li></ul><h4>Key Terms</h4><div class=\"table-wrap\"><table class=\"study-table\"><thead><tr><th>Term</th><th>Meaning</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Cyber operation</td><td>An operation against or via a computer system through a data stream.</td></tr><tr><td>Autonomous weapon system</td><td>A system that once activated can select and engage targets without further human intervention.</td></tr><tr><td>Meaningful human control</td><td>Sufficient human judgement over the use of force for legal and moral responsibility to attach.</td></tr><tr><td>Automation bias</td><td>The tendency of operators to accept machine recommendations without independent assessment.</td></tr><tr><td>Tallinn Manual</td><td>A non-binding expert assessment of how international law applies to cyber operations.</td></tr></tbody></table></div><h4>Review Questions</h4><h5>Multiple choice</h5><div class=\"study-mcq\"><p class=\"mcq-q\">What is the status of the Tallinn Manual?</p><ol type=\"A\"><li>A non-binding assessment by an independent group of experts of how existing law applies</li><li>A binding treaty</li><li>The official position of NATO</li><li>A Security Council instrument</li></ol><details class=\"mcq-a\"><summary>Show answer</summary><p><strong>Answer: A.</strong> Describing it as binding or as an alliance position is inaccurate and is a common error.</p></details></div><div class=\"study-mcq\"><p class=\"mcq-q\">Which cyber question remains genuinely contested?</p><ol type=\"A\"><li>Whether an operation causing no physical damage is an &#x27;attack&#x27; and whether civilian data is a civilian object</li><li>Whether IHL applies to cyber operations at all</li><li>Whether cyber operations can occur during armed conflict</li><li>Whether states may conduct cyber espionage in peacetime</li></ol><details class=\"mcq-a\"><summary>Show answer</summary><p><strong>Answer: A.</strong> The ICRC argues that an operation rendering an object useless is an attack and that essential civilian data should be protected, but state practice varies.</p></details></div><div class=\"study-mcq\"><p class=\"mcq-q\">Which concern about autonomous weapons is independent of how well the system performs?</p><ol type=\"A\"><li>The dignity argument that a decision to take human life should not be made by a machine</li><li>Whether it can distinguish civilians</li><li>Whether it can assess proportionality</li><li>Whether it can cancel an attack</li></ol><details class=\"mcq-a\"><summary>Show answer</summary><p><strong>Answer: A.</strong> It is a Martens Clause argument resting on the dictates of public conscience rather than on capability.</p></details></div><div class=\"study-mcq\"><p class=\"mcq-q\">Why does automation bias matter for the precautionary obligations?</p><ol type=\"A\"><li>An operator who approves a machine recommendation without independent assessment has not actually verified the target</li><li>It slows down decision-making</li><li>It affects only autonomous systems</li><li>It is prohibited by CCW Protocol IV</li></ol><details class=\"mcq-a\"><summary>Show answer</summary><p><strong>Answer: A.</strong> The duty under Article 57 requires actual verification, and nominal human involvement does not discharge it.</p></details></div><h5>Short answer</h5><div class=\"study-sa\"><p class=\"sa-q\">Set out the main unresolved questions about the application of IHL to cyber operations.</p><details class=\"sa-a\"><summary>Model answer</summary><p>First, the threshold question: whether a cyber operation can itself trigger an armed conflict. The prevailing view is that an operation causing physical damage, injury or death comparable to a kinetic attack can do so, while espionage, data theft and disruption below that level cannot. Second, what constitutes an attack: the narrow view requires physical damage or injury, while the broader view taken by the ICRC treats an operation rendering an object useless as an attack, on the ground that otherwise essential civilian services could be disabled with no rule engaged. Third, whether civilian data is a civilian object: if it is not, deleting a hospital&#x27;s medical records or a state&#x27;s civil registry engages no targeting rule, a result most commentators reject. Fourth, how to handle the interconnection of military and civilian networks in the dual-use analysis. Fifth, the practical difficulty of attribution, which affects both compliance assessment and accountability.</p></details></div><div class=\"study-sa\"><p class=\"sa-q\">Explain the five concerns raised by autonomous weapon systems.</p><details class=\"sa-a\"><summary>Model answer</summary><p>First, distinction: whether a system can reliably distinguish civilians from combatants and recognise a person hors de combat or attempting to surrender in an unstructured environment. Second, proportionality: the assessment requires a contextual value judgement about excessiveness, and the deeper question is whether such a judgement can be delegated to a machine at all rather than whether it can be performed accurately. Third, precautions: the duty to cancel or suspend requires continuing judgement as circumstances change. Fourth, accountability: individual criminal responsibility requires a mental element a machine cannot possess, so there is a risk of a gap in which no person is responsible for what would otherwise be a war crime. Fifth, dignity: the argument, independent of performance, that a decision to take human life should not be made by a machine, which rests on the Martens Clause and the dictates of public conscience.</p></details></div><h4>Class Activity</h4><ol><li>Read one state&#x27;s published national position on the application of international law to cyber operations. Summarise its answers to the threshold question, the definition of attack, and the status of data.</li><li>Draft a one-page policy on human involvement in AI-assisted targeting, specifying what the human must do at each stage for the verification obligation to be satisfied.</li></ol><h4>Assignment</h4><p>Write two pages on new technologies and international humanitarian law. Explain why new technologies create no legal vacuum, set out how IHL applies to cyber operations and identify which questions are settled and which contested, describe the debate on autonomous weapon systems with the state positions and the five substantive concerns keeping the dignity argument separate, discuss the risks of artificial intelligence in targeting, and explain how the Article 36 review applies with a worked example.</p><p class=\"study-print\"><strong>Printable:</strong> use your browser\u2019s Print \u2192 Save as PDF to keep an offline copy.</p></div>",
  "Enforcing the Law of Weapons": "<div class=\"study-note\"><div class=\"revision-banner\"><strong>Human Rights and International Humanitarian Law</strong><span>Module 10: Means and Methods of Warfare</span></div><h3 class=\"lesson-note-title\">LESSON NOTE: ENFORCING THE LAW OF WEAPONS</h3><div class=\"table-wrap\"><table class=\"study-table\"><thead><tr><th>Subject</th><th>Topic</th><th>Level</th><th>Lesson duration</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Human Rights and International Humanitarian Law</td><td>Enforcing the Law of Weapons</td><td>Beginner to Advanced</td><td>40 minutes</td></tr></tbody></table></div><h4>Learning objectives</h4><p>By the end of this lesson you should be able to:</p><ul><li>Describe the mechanisms for enforcing weapons law</li><li>Explain the Arms Trade Treaty and transfer controls</li><li>Explain the role of national implementation and Article 36 review</li><li>Assess the effectiveness of the enforcement architecture</li></ul><h4>1. Introduction</h4><p>Prohibiting a weapon and stopping its use are different achievements. This lesson covers the mechanisms that exist between the two, and they are more varied than students expect: verification, transfer control, criminal responsibility, national review, stigmatisation and market pressure all play a part, and no single one is sufficient.</p><h4>2. The mechanisms</h4><div class=\"table-wrap\"><table class=\"study-table\"><thead><tr><th>Mechanism</th><th>How it works</th><th>Example</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Verification</td><td>Declarations, routine and challenge inspection by an international organisation</td><td>OPCW under the Chemical Weapons Convention</td></tr><tr><td>Transparency reporting</td><td>Annual reports on stockpiles, destruction and clearance, published and compared</td><td>Ottawa Convention Art 7; Cluster Munitions Art 7</td></tr><tr><td>Meetings of states parties</td><td>Regular review of implementation, extension requests and compliance concerns</td><td>Both ban treaties; the CCW review conferences</td></tr><tr><td>Individual criminal responsibility</td><td>Prosecution of those who use prohibited weapons</td><td>Rome Statute Art 8; national war crimes legislation</td></tr><tr><td>State responsibility</td><td>Claims between states for breach</td><td>ICJ; countermeasures</td></tr><tr><td>Transfer control</td><td>Prohibiting export where the weapon would be used unlawfully</td><td>Arms Trade Treaty; national export licensing</td></tr><tr><td>National legal review</td><td>Assessing new weapons before adoption</td><td>AP I Art 36</td></tr><tr><td>Stigmatisation and market effects</td><td>Norm pressure causing producers to exit and investors to divest</td><td>The anti-personnel mine trade after Ottawa</td></tr><tr><td>Security Council action</td><td>Sanctions, investigation mechanisms, referral to the ICC</td><td>Resolution 1540; chemical weapons investigation mechanisms</td></tr></tbody></table></div><h4>3. The Arms Trade Treaty 2013</h4><div class=\"table-wrap\"><table class=\"study-table\"><thead><tr><th>Feature</th><th>Detail</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>In force</td><td>2014</td></tr><tr><td>Scope</td><td>Battle tanks, armoured combat vehicles, large-calibre artillery, combat aircraft, attack helicopters, warships, missiles and missile launchers, small arms and light weapons; also ammunition and parts and components</td></tr><tr><td>Article 6 prohibitions</td><td>No transfer where it would violate Security Council measures, or the state's other international obligations, or where the state knows the arms would be used in the commission of genocide, crimes against humanity, grave breaches of the Geneva Conventions, attacks on civilians or civilian objects, or other war crimes</td></tr><tr><td>Article 7 assessment</td><td>Where not prohibited by Article 6, the exporting state must assess the potential that the arms would contribute to or undermine peace and security, or could be used to commit or facilitate a serious violation of IHL or human rights law, terrorism or transnational organised crime; and must consider mitigation measures</td></tr><tr><td>Article 7(4)</td><td>Requires the exporting state to take into account the risk of the arms being used to commit or facilitate serious acts of gender-based violence or violence against women and children</td></tr><tr><td>Article 7(3)</td><td>If, after considering mitigation, there remains an overriding risk of any of the negative consequences, the state shall not authorise the export</td></tr><tr><td>Reporting</td><td>Annual reports on authorised or actual exports and imports</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"study-callout\"><strong>Note:</strong> Article 6 sets a knowledge standard and Article 7 a risk standard. That difference decides most Arms Trade Treaty arguments. Under Article 6 a transfer is prohibited outright where the state knows the arms would be used for the listed crimes. Under Article 7 the state must conduct an assessment and refuse where an overriding risk remains after mitigation. Litigation in several national courts has concerned whether an assessment was properly conducted rather than whether knowledge existed, because the risk standard is easier to engage.</div><h4>4. Common Article 1 and transfers</h4><p>Independently of the Arms Trade Treaty, common Article 1 of the Geneva Conventions requires states to respect and to ensure respect for the Conventions in all circumstances.</p><ul><li>The ICRC's interpretation, supported by the International Court of Justice in the Nicaragua case and the Wall advisory opinion, is that the duty to ensure respect includes a negative obligation not to encourage, aid or assist violations, and a positive obligation to use influence to bring parties into compliance.</li><li>Applied to arms transfers, the negative obligation means a state may not transfer weapons where it is expected, in view of the facts or knowledge of past patterns, that they would be used to violate the Conventions.</li><li>This standard is argued to be broader than the Arms Trade Treaty's, since it does not require the recipient to be a party to any instrument and applies to all states party to the Conventions, which is all states.</li><li>It has been relied on in national litigation challenging export licences, with varying success.</li></ul><h4>5. National implementation</h4><ol><li><strong>Criminal legislation.</strong> States must enact offences covering use of prohibited weapons, with penalties, and establish jurisdiction including universal jurisdiction where required.</li><li><strong>Export controls.</strong> Licensing systems implementing treaty obligations and applying the Article 6 and 7 tests.</li><li><strong>Article 36 review mechanisms.</strong> A standing body or procedure to review new weapons, means and methods. Only a minority of states are known to have one, which is the largest gap in the architecture.</li><li><strong>Stockpile management and destruction.</strong> Implementing treaty deadlines and preventing diversion.</li><li><strong>Military doctrine, rules of engagement and training.</strong> Weapons law has effect on the battlefield only through these instruments, not through treaties directly.</li><li><strong>Clearance and victim assistance programmes.</strong> Meeting post-conflict obligations.</li></ol><div class=\"study-defn\"><strong>Article 36 review mechanism:</strong> A standing national procedure for determining whether a new weapon, means or method of warfare would be prohibited by international law, required by Additional Protocol I Article 36.</div><h4>6. Assessing the architecture</h4><div class=\"table-wrap\"><table class=\"study-table\"><thead><tr><th>Strength</th><th>Weakness</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>The chemical weapons regime has genuine verification with an inspectorate and challenge inspection</td><td>The biological weapons regime has no verification at all</td></tr><tr><td>Ban treaties have produced measurable falls in production, trade and stockpiles</td><td>Major producers and users remain outside the two ban treaties</td></tr><tr><td>Individual criminal responsibility exists for use of several categories</td><td>Prosecutions for weapons offences are very rare in practice</td></tr><tr><td>The Arms Trade Treaty created a common transfer standard</td><td>Several of the largest exporters are not parties, and the risk assessment is conducted by the exporting state itself</td></tr><tr><td>Article 36 requires review of every new weapon</td><td>Only a minority of states are known to have a review mechanism</td></tr><tr><td>Stigmatisation has produced real market effects</td><td>Norms constrain reputation-sensitive actors, not all actors</td></tr><tr><td>Transparency reporting enables comparison and pressure</td><td>Reporting is incomplete and non-reporting carries no consequence</td></tr></tbody></table></div><ol><li><strong>The single highest-value reform</strong> is generally identified as universalising Article 36 review mechanisms, because a review conducted before adoption prevents problems that no later mechanism can cure, and because the obligation already exists.</li><li><strong>The second</strong> is verification for the Biological Weapons Convention, though the political obstacles that defeated the 2001 protocol have not changed.</li><li><strong>The third</strong> is strengthening transfer control, since most weapons used unlawfully were transferred lawfully at some point.</li></ol><h4>7. How the examiner&#x27;s question should be answered</h4><p>Do not treat enforcement as a single thing. Set out the mechanisms in a table and note which treaties use which, since the variation is the substance of the answer. On the Arms Trade Treaty, distinguish the Article 6 knowledge standard from the Article 7 risk standard precisely, because that distinction decides real cases. Add common Article 1 as an independent and broader basis. Identify national implementation as where enforcement actually happens, and name Article 36 review as the largest gap. Conclude with a ranked reform proposal rather than a general call for better compliance.</p><h4>Summary</h4><ul><li>Enforcement of weapons law runs through verification, reporting, criminal responsibility, transfer control, national review, stigmatisation and Security Council action.</li><li>The Arms Trade Treaty prohibits transfers under Article 6 on a knowledge standard and requires refusal under Article 7 where an overriding risk remains after mitigation.</li><li>Common Article 1&#x27;s duty to ensure respect provides an independent and arguably broader basis for restricting transfers.</li><li>National implementation through criminal law, export controls, doctrine, rules of engagement and training is where enforcement actually operates.</li><li>Only a minority of states are known to have an Article 36 review mechanism, which is the largest gap in the architecture.</li><li>Stigmatisation produces real effects on production and trade but constrains only reputation-sensitive actors.</li></ul><h4>Key Terms</h4><div class=\"table-wrap\"><table class=\"study-table\"><thead><tr><th>Term</th><th>Meaning</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Arms Trade Treaty</td><td>The 2013 treaty establishing common standards for the international transfer of conventional arms.</td></tr><tr><td>Article 6 prohibition</td><td>The absolute bar on transfer where the state knows the arms would be used for listed crimes.</td></tr><tr><td>Overriding risk</td><td>The Article 7 threshold at which an export must be refused after mitigation is considered.</td></tr><tr><td>Duty to ensure respect</td><td>The common Article 1 obligation not to aid violations and to use influence to secure compliance.</td></tr><tr><td>Universalisation</td><td>Extending participation in a treaty regime to all states.</td></tr></tbody></table></div><h4>Review Questions</h4><h5>Multiple choice</h5><div class=\"study-mcq\"><p class=\"mcq-q\">What is the difference between Articles 6 and 7 of the Arms Trade Treaty?</p><ol type=\"A\"><li>Article 6 prohibits transfer on a knowledge standard; Article 7 requires refusal where an overriding risk remains after mitigation</li><li>Article 6 covers ammunition and Article 7 covers weapons</li><li>Article 6 applies to imports and Article 7 to exports</li><li>They are identical</li></ol><details class=\"mcq-a\"><summary>Show answer</summary><p><strong>Answer: A.</strong> Litigation has concentrated on Article 7 because the risk standard is easier to engage than proof of knowledge.</p></details></div><div class=\"study-mcq\"><p class=\"mcq-q\">How does common Article 1 bear on arms transfers?</p><ol type=\"A\"><li>The duty to ensure respect includes not encouraging, aiding or assisting violations, which restricts transfers where violations are expected</li><li>It requires all transfers to be reported</li><li>It prohibits all arms transfers</li><li>It applies only to parties to the Arms Trade Treaty</li></ol><details class=\"mcq-a\"><summary>Show answer</summary><p><strong>Answer: A.</strong> It binds every state party to the Geneva Conventions, which is every state, so its reach is wider than the Arms Trade Treaty&#x27;s.</p></details></div><div class=\"study-mcq\"><p class=\"mcq-q\">What is generally identified as the largest gap in the enforcement architecture?</p><ol type=\"A\"><li>Only a minority of states are known to have an Article 36 weapons review mechanism</li><li>There are too few treaties</li><li>Weapons are not criminalised</li><li>No state reports on transfers</li></ol><details class=\"mcq-a\"><summary>Show answer</summary><p><strong>Answer: A.</strong> A review conducted before adoption prevents problems no later mechanism can cure, and the obligation already exists.</p></details></div><div class=\"study-mcq\"><p class=\"mcq-q\">Through what does weapons law actually reach the battlefield?</p><ol type=\"A\"><li>Military doctrine, rules of engagement and training</li><li>Treaties applied directly by soldiers</li><li>Decisions of the International Court of Justice</li><li>Reports of the Secretary-General</li></ol><details class=\"mcq-a\"><summary>Show answer</summary><p><strong>Answer: A.</strong> National implementation is where enforcement operates, which is why criminal legislation, export controls and training matter more than further treaty drafting.</p></details></div><h5>Short answer</h5><div class=\"study-sa\"><p class=\"sa-q\">Describe the mechanisms available for enforcing the law of weapons.</p><details class=\"sa-a\"><summary>Model answer</summary><p>Verification by an international organisation with declarations, routine inspection and challenge inspection, as under the Chemical Weapons Convention. Transparency reporting on stockpiles, destruction and clearance, as under both ban treaties, allowing comparison and pressure. Regular meetings of states parties reviewing implementation and compliance. Individual criminal responsibility for use of prohibited weapons under the Rome Statute and national war crimes legislation. State responsibility claims between states. Transfer control through the Arms Trade Treaty and national export licensing. National legal review of new weapons under Additional Protocol I Article 36. Stigmatisation producing market effects on producers and investors. And Security Council action through sanctions, investigation mechanisms and referral to the International Criminal Court.</p></details></div><div class=\"study-sa\"><p class=\"sa-q\">Assess the effectiveness of the weapons enforcement architecture and identify the highest-value reform.</p><details class=\"sa-a\"><summary>Model answer</summary><p>The architecture is uneven. The chemical weapons regime has genuine verification with an inspectorate and challenge inspection, while the biological weapons regime has none. The ban treaties on mines and cluster munitions have produced measurable reductions in production, trade and stockpiles, but major producers remain outside them. Individual criminal responsibility exists but prosecutions for weapons offences are very rare. The Arms Trade Treaty created a common transfer standard, but several of the largest exporters are not parties and the risk assessment is conducted by the exporting state itself. Transparency reporting is incomplete and non-reporting carries no consequence. The highest-value reform is generally identified as universalising Article 36 review mechanisms, since only a minority of states are known to have one, the obligation already exists, and a review conducted before adoption prevents problems that no later mechanism can cure.</p></details></div><h4>Class Activity</h4><ol><li>Find out whether your country is party to the Arms Trade Treaty and whether it has submitted annual reports. Summarise its export licensing procedure in one paragraph.</li><li>Draft the terms of reference for a national Article 36 review mechanism, specifying its composition, when it is triggered, what it assesses and what form its output takes.</li></ol><h4>Assignment</h4><p>Write one and a half pages on enforcing the law of weapons. Set out the available mechanisms and note which treaties use which, explain the Arms Trade Treaty distinguishing the Article 6 knowledge standard from the Article 7 risk standard, explain how common Article 1 restricts transfers independently, describe the components of national implementation, and assess the architecture&#x27;s strengths and weaknesses, concluding with a ranked reform proposal.</p><p class=\"study-print\"><strong>Printable:</strong> use your browser\u2019s Print \u2192 Save as PDF to keep an offline copy.</p></div>",
  "War Crimes": "<div class=\"study-note\"><div class=\"revision-banner\"><strong>Human Rights and International Humanitarian Law</strong><span>Module 11: Accountability and International Criminal Law</span></div><h3 class=\"lesson-note-title\">LESSON NOTE: WAR CRIMES</h3><div class=\"table-wrap\"><table class=\"study-table\"><thead><tr><th>Subject</th><th>Topic</th><th>Level</th><th>Lesson duration</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Human Rights and International Humanitarian Law</td><td>War Crimes</td><td>Beginner to Advanced</td><td>45 minutes</td></tr></tbody></table></div><h4>Learning objectives</h4><p>By the end of this lesson you should be able to:</p><ul><li>Define war crimes and state the two elements every war crime requires</li><li>Distinguish grave breaches from other serious violations</li><li>List the main categories of war crime under the Rome Statute</li><li>Explain the nexus requirement and how it is proved</li></ul><h4>1. Introduction</h4><p>International humanitarian law tells combatants what they may not do. International criminal law is what happens when they do it anyway. A war crime is the point at which a violation of the law of armed conflict becomes a crime for which an individual person, not a state, can be tried and imprisoned.</p><h4>2. Meaning</h4><div class=\"study-defn\"><strong>War crime:</strong> A serious violation of international humanitarian law entailing individual criminal responsibility under international law.</div><p>Two elements must be established for any war crime, and answers that omit either lose marks.</p><ol><li><strong>A serious violation of a rule of international humanitarian law.</strong> Not every breach is a crime. The rule must protect important values and its breach must involve grave consequences for the victim.</li><li><strong>A nexus to an armed conflict.</strong> The act must be closely related to the hostilities. This is what separates a war crime from an ordinary murder that happens to occur during a war.</li></ol><div class=\"study-callout\"><strong>Note:</strong> The nexus requirement is the element most often tested and most often mishandled. The ICTY in Kunarac held that the conflict must have played a substantial part in the perpetrator's ability to commit the crime, their decision to commit it, the manner of its commission, or the purpose for which it was committed. It does not require the act to occur during actual fighting or at the front line.</div><h4>3. Grave breaches and other serious violations</h4><div class=\"table-wrap\"><table class=\"study-table\"><thead><tr><th></th><th>Grave breaches</th><th>Other serious violations</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Source</td><td>Listed in each Geneva Convention and AP I Art 85</td><td>Customary law; Rome Statute Art 8(2)(b), (c), (e)</td></tr><tr><td>Conflict type</td><td>International armed conflict only</td><td>Both international and non-international</td></tr><tr><td>Victims</td><td>Protected persons and property only</td><td>Wider</td></tr><tr><td>Duty on states</td><td>Mandatory universal jurisdiction: search for and prosecute or extradite</td><td>Permissive universal jurisdiction in most states</td></tr><tr><td>Examples</td><td>Wilful killing, torture, unlawful deportation, hostage-taking, denying a fair trial</td><td>Attacking civilians, pillage, using prohibited weapons, conscripting children</td></tr></tbody></table></div><p>The Tadic decision of 1995 established that serious violations of the law applicable in non-international armed conflict also entail individual criminal responsibility under customary law. Before Tadic it was widely argued that internal conflicts produced no war crimes at all. That holding is the single most consequential development in this field and should be cited in any answer on the subject.</p><h4>4. The Rome Statute categories</h4><p>Article 8 divides war crimes into four lists. Knowing the structure is worth more than memorising all fifty offences.</p><div class=\"table-wrap\"><table class=\"study-table\"><thead><tr><th>Article</th><th>Applies in</th><th>Content</th><th>Number</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>8(2)(a)</td><td>International</td><td>Grave breaches of the Geneva Conventions</td><td>8 offences</td></tr><tr><td>8(2)(b)</td><td>International</td><td>Other serious violations of the laws and customs applicable in international armed conflict</td><td>26 offences</td></tr><tr><td>8(2)(c)</td><td>Non-international</td><td>Serious violations of common Article 3</td><td>4 offences</td></tr><tr><td>8(2)(e)</td><td>Non-international</td><td>Other serious violations of the laws and customs applicable in non-international armed conflict</td><td>19 offences</td></tr></tbody></table></div><p>Grouping the offences thematically is the way to hold them.</p><ul><li><strong>Against persons:</strong> wilful killing, torture, inhuman treatment, biological experiments, rape and other sexual violence, hostage-taking, outrages upon personal dignity, sentences without fair trial.</li><li><strong>Against the civilian population:</strong> intentionally directing attacks against civilians or civilian objects, disproportionate attacks, attacking undefended places, starvation as a method of warfare.</li><li><strong>Against protected persons and objects:</strong> attacking medical units, personnel using the distinctive emblems, humanitarian assistance or peacekeeping personnel, buildings dedicated to religion, education, art, science or charity, and historic monuments.</li><li><strong>Prohibited methods:</strong> perfidy, denial of quarter, killing or wounding treacherously, improper use of flags or emblems, compelling nationals of the hostile party to fight against their own country.</li><li><strong>Prohibited weapons:</strong> poison, asphyxiating gases, expanding bullets; and by later amendment, in both conflict types.</li><li><strong>Against children:</strong> conscripting or enlisting children under fifteen or using them to participate actively in hostilities.</li><li><strong>Displacement and property:</strong> unlawful deportation or transfer, the transfer by an occupying power of its own population into occupied territory, pillage, destruction of property not justified by military necessity.</li></ul><h4>5. Mental element</h4><p>Rome Statute Article 30 supplies the default. A person is criminally responsible only if the material elements are committed with intent and knowledge.</p><div class=\"table-wrap\"><table class=\"study-table\"><thead><tr><th>Element</th><th>Requirement</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Intent as to conduct</td><td>The person means to engage in the conduct</td></tr><tr><td>Intent as to consequence</td><td>The person means to cause the consequence, or is aware it will occur in the ordinary course of events</td></tr><tr><td>Knowledge</td><td>Awareness that a circumstance exists or a consequence will occur in the ordinary course of events</td></tr></tbody></table></div><p>Particular offences vary this. The proportionality war crime in Article 8(2)(b)(iv) requires the perpetrator to have known the attack would cause harm 'clearly excessive' in relation to the anticipated advantage, which is a higher threshold than the underlying IHL rule. The child soldier offence has been held to require that the accused knew or should have known the recruits were under fifteen, which is a lower threshold. Reading the Elements of Crimes rather than the Statute alone is what distinguishes a precise answer.</p><div class=\"study-callout\"><strong>Example:</strong> Lubanga, the ICC's first judgment in 2012, convicted on conscripting and enlisting children under fifteen and using them to participate actively in hostilities in a non-international armed conflict. The Court held that 'active participation' extends beyond combat to roles such as scouts, spies, porters and bodyguards, because those roles expose the child to the risks of hostilities. The case is the standard citation for the child soldier offence and for the scope of active participation.</div><h4>6. Proving a war crime</h4><ol><li><strong>Establish the armed conflict</strong> and classify it, since the applicable list of offences depends on classification.</li><li><strong>Establish the nexus</strong> using the Kunarac factors: the perpetrator's status as a combatant, the victim's status as a non-combatant, whether the act served an ultimate military goal, and whether it was committed in the context of official duties.</li><li><strong>Establish the material elements</strong> of the specific offence charged, using the Elements of Crimes.</li><li><strong>Establish the mental element</strong> under Article 30 as modified by the specific offence.</li><li><strong>Establish the mode of liability</strong>: direct commission, ordering, aiding and abetting, common purpose, or command responsibility.</li></ol><h4>7. How the examiner&#x27;s question should be answered</h4><p>Define a war crime with both elements and explain the nexus with Kunarac. Distinguish grave breaches from other serious violations across the five axes in the table, and state clearly that the grave breaches regime is confined to international armed conflict while Tadic established criminal responsibility in internal conflict too. Then apply the Rome Statute list that matches your classification. Do not recite all fifty offences; group them thematically and apply the ones the facts engage. Finish with the mental element and the mode of liability, since a war crime with no identified perpetrator and no mode of liability is not a chargeable case.</p><h4>Summary</h4><ul><li>A war crime requires a serious violation of international humanitarian law plus a nexus to an armed conflict.</li><li>The Kunarac test asks whether the conflict played a substantial part in the ability, decision, manner or purpose of the crime.</li><li>Grave breaches apply only in international armed conflict and carry mandatory universal jurisdiction.</li><li>Tadic established that serious violations in non-international armed conflict also entail individual criminal responsibility.</li><li>Rome Statute Article 8 has four lists, two for each conflict type, totalling fifty-seven offences.</li><li>Article 30 supplies intent and knowledge as the default mental element, varied by particular offences.</li></ul><h4>Key Terms</h4><div class=\"table-wrap\"><table class=\"study-table\"><thead><tr><th>Term</th><th>Meaning</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>War crime</td><td>A serious violation of international humanitarian law entailing individual criminal responsibility.</td></tr><tr><td>Nexus</td><td>The close relationship between the act and the armed conflict that makes a violation a war crime.</td></tr><tr><td>Grave breach</td><td>A listed serious violation in international armed conflict carrying mandatory universal jurisdiction.</td></tr><tr><td>Elements of Crimes</td><td>The ICC instrument specifying the material and mental elements of each offence.</td></tr><tr><td>Active participation</td><td>The Lubanga concept extending beyond combat to scouts, spies, porters and bodyguards.</td></tr></tbody></table></div><h4>Review Questions</h4><h5>Multiple choice</h5><div class=\"study-mcq\"><p class=\"mcq-q\">What distinguishes a war crime from an ordinary murder committed during a war?</p><ol type=\"A\"><li>A nexus to the armed conflict, meaning the conflict played a substantial part in the crime</li><li>The rank of the perpetrator</li><li>The number of victims</li><li>Whether a weapon was used</li></ol><details class=\"mcq-a\"><summary>Show answer</summary><p><strong>Answer: A.</strong> The Kunarac test looks at the perpetrator&#x27;s ability and decision to commit the act, its manner and its purpose.</p></details></div><div class=\"study-mcq\"><p class=\"mcq-q\">Which regime applies only in international armed conflict?</p><ol type=\"A\"><li>Grave breaches of the Geneva Conventions</li><li>Serious violations of common Article 3</li><li>Crimes against humanity</li><li>Genocide</li></ol><details class=\"mcq-a\"><summary>Show answer</summary><p><strong>Answer: A.</strong> Tadic nonetheless established that serious violations in internal conflict entail individual criminal responsibility under customary law.</p></details></div><div class=\"study-mcq\"><p class=\"mcq-q\">What did Lubanga establish about &#x27;active participation&#x27; by children?</p><ol type=\"A\"><li>It extends beyond combat to roles such as scouts, spies, porters and bodyguards</li><li>It is limited to firing a weapon</li><li>It requires enlistment for over six months</li><li>It applies only in international armed conflict</li></ol><details class=\"mcq-a\"><summary>Show answer</summary><p><strong>Answer: A.</strong> Those roles expose the child to the risks of hostilities, which is the rationale for including them.</p></details></div><div class=\"study-mcq\"><p class=\"mcq-q\">What mental element does Rome Statute Article 30 supply by default?</p><ol type=\"A\"><li>Intent and knowledge</li><li>Negligence</li><li>Strict liability</li><li>Recklessness</li></ol><details class=\"mcq-a\"><summary>Show answer</summary><p><strong>Answer: A.</strong> Particular offences vary it, so the Elements of Crimes must be read alongside the Statute.</p></details></div><h5>Short answer</h5><div class=\"study-sa\"><p class=\"sa-q\">State the two elements of a war crime and explain the second.</p><details class=\"sa-a\"><summary>Model answer</summary><p>First, a serious violation of a rule of international humanitarian law, meaning a rule protecting important values whose breach involves grave consequences for the victim; not every breach of the law of armed conflict is a crime. Second, a nexus to an armed conflict, which is what separates a war crime from an ordinary crime that happens to occur during wartime. The ICTY in Kunarac held that the conflict must have played a substantial part in the perpetrator&#x27;s ability to commit the crime, their decision to commit it, the manner of its commission, or the purpose for which it was committed. The act need not occur during fighting or near the front line, and relevant indicators include whether the perpetrator was a combatant, whether the victim was a non-combatant, and whether the act was committed in the context of official duties.</p></details></div><div class=\"study-sa\"><p class=\"sa-q\">Compare grave breaches with other serious violations.</p><details class=\"sa-a\"><summary>Model answer</summary><p>Grave breaches are listed in each of the four Geneva Conventions and extended by Additional Protocol I Article 85. They apply only in international armed conflict and only to protected persons and property, and they carry a mandatory obligation on every state party to search for alleged offenders and either prosecute them or extradite them, regardless of nationality. Other serious violations derive from customary law and appear in Rome Statute Article 8(2)(b), (c) and (e). They cover both international and non-international armed conflict, reach a wider range of victims and conduct, and generally attract permissive rather than mandatory universal jurisdiction. Examples of grave breaches include wilful killing, torture and hostage-taking; examples of other serious violations include attacking civilians, pillage and conscripting children.</p></details></div><h4>Class Activity</h4><ol><li>Take one documented incident from any conflict. Work through the five proof steps and identify what evidence would be needed at each stage.</li><li>Read Rome Statute Article 8(2)(c) and (e). List which offences have no equivalent in Article 8(2)(b), and suggest why the lists differ.</li></ol><h4>Assignment</h4><p>Write two pages on war crimes. Define a war crime with both elements and explain the nexus requirement using Kunarac, distinguish grave breaches from other serious violations across at least four axes, explain the significance of Tadic for internal conflicts, set out the structure of Rome Statute Article 8 and group the offences thematically, explain the mental element under Article 30 and how particular offences vary it, and set out the five steps in proving a case.</p><p class=\"study-print\"><strong>Printable:</strong> use your browser\u2019s Print \u2192 Save as PDF to keep an offline copy.</p></div>",
  "Genocide": "<div class=\"study-note\"><div class=\"revision-banner\"><strong>Human Rights and International Humanitarian Law</strong><span>Module 11: Accountability and International Criminal Law</span></div><h3 class=\"lesson-note-title\">LESSON NOTE: GENOCIDE</h3><div class=\"table-wrap\"><table class=\"study-table\"><thead><tr><th>Subject</th><th>Topic</th><th>Level</th><th>Lesson duration</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Human Rights and International Humanitarian Law</td><td>Genocide</td><td>Beginner to Advanced</td><td>45 minutes</td></tr></tbody></table></div><h4>Learning objectives</h4><p>By the end of this lesson you should be able to:</p><ul><li>State the definition of genocide and its five prohibited acts</li><li>Explain the special intent requirement and how it is proved</li><li>Explain the meaning of the four protected groups and &#x27;in part&#x27;</li><li>Describe the punishable acts beyond genocide itself</li></ul><h4>1. Introduction</h4><p>Genocide is often called the crime of crimes, and it is the hardest of the international crimes to prove. The difficulty is not the killing, which is usually evident, but the state of mind: the prosecution must establish an intent to destroy a group as such, and that intent is rarely written down.</p><h4>2. The definition</h4><blockquote class=\"study-quote\">Genocide means any of the following acts committed with intent to destroy, in whole or in part, a national, ethnical, racial or religious group, as such.</blockquote><p>Article II of the 1948 Genocide Convention, reproduced verbatim in ICTY Statute Article 4, ICTR Statute Article 2 and Rome Statute Article 6. The definition has not changed in over seventy years, which is unusual and significant: it means the jurisprudence of every tribunal is mutually usable.</p><p>The five prohibited acts are exhaustive.</p><ol><li>Killing members of the group.</li><li>Causing serious bodily or mental harm to members of the group.</li><li>Deliberately inflicting on the group conditions of life calculated to bring about its physical destruction in whole or in part.</li><li>Imposing measures intended to prevent births within the group.</li><li>Forcibly transferring children of the group to another group.</li></ol><div class=\"study-callout\"><strong>Note:</strong> Note what is absent. Cultural destruction is not listed: destroying a group's language, religious buildings or cultural identity is not genocide unless it forms evidence of intent to destroy the group physically or biologically. Political groups are also absent, deliberately excluded during the 1948 drafting at the insistence of several states. Both omissions are standard essay material.</div><h4>3. The special intent</h4><div class=\"study-defn\"><strong>Dolus specialis:</strong> The specific intent to destroy, in whole or in part, a protected group as such, which is the distinguishing element of genocide.</div><p>The mental element is therefore double. The perpetrator must intend the underlying act, such as killing, and must additionally intend by that act to contribute to the destruction of the group. Three features follow.</p><ol><li><strong>Motive is irrelevant.</strong> Whether the perpetrator acted from hatred, obedience, fear or gain does not matter, provided the intent to destroy the group is present.</li><li><strong>'As such' means the group is targeted because of its group identity</strong>, not merely that the victims happen to belong to it.</li><li><strong>Intent is not the same as a plan.</strong> A plan or policy is not a legal element, though the ICTY has accepted that in practice genocide is almost impossible without one, and its existence is powerful evidence.</li></ol><p>Because confessions are rare, intent is normally inferred. The tribunals have identified the factors from which it may be drawn.</p><ul><li>The general context and the perpetration of other culpable acts systematically directed against the same group.</li><li>The scale of atrocities committed and their general nature in a region or country.</li><li>The deliberate and systematic targeting of victims on account of their membership of a particular group, while excluding members of other groups.</li><li>The repetition of destructive and discriminatory acts.</li><li>The existence of a plan or policy, and utterances of the accused.</li><li>The targeting of the group's leadership, and of men of military age, as evidence of intent to destroy the group's capacity to survive.</li></ul><div class=\"study-callout\"><strong>Note:</strong> Inference must be the only reasonable inference available on the evidence. Where the facts are equally consistent with an intent to displace a population rather than destroy it, the charge fails. This is why many mass atrocity cases are prosecuted as crimes against humanity instead: the conduct is proved but the special intent is not.</div><h4>4. The protected groups</h4><div class=\"table-wrap\"><table class=\"study-table\"><thead><tr><th>Group</th><th>Approach</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>National</td><td>Persons sharing a legal bond based on common citizenship, coupled with reciprocity of rights and duties</td></tr><tr><td>Ethnical</td><td>A group whose members share a common language or culture</td></tr><tr><td>Racial</td><td>Based on hereditary physical traits often identified with a geographical region</td></tr><tr><td>Religious</td><td>A group sharing the same religion, denomination or mode of worship</td></tr></tbody></table></div><p>Two points recur in the case law.</p><ol><li><strong>The list is exhaustive.</strong> Political, social, economic and gender groups are not protected. Attempts to read them in have failed.</li><li><strong>Identification may be subjective.</strong> The ICTR in Akayesu wrestled with whether Tutsi constituted an ethnic group under the strict definitions, since Hutu and Tutsi shared a language, religion and territory. The tribunals settled on a partly subjective approach: a group may be identified by the perpetrators' perception of it, supported by objective indicators such as official identity documents recording ethnicity. A purely subjective test, however, has been rejected, because it would let the perpetrator define the crime.</li></ol><h4>5. &#x27;In whole or in part&#x27;</h4><p>The destruction intended need not be total, but the part must be substantial. The tribunals assess substantiality by several criteria taken together.</p><div class=\"table-wrap\"><table class=\"study-table\"><thead><tr><th>Criterion</th><th>Content</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Numeric</td><td>The targeted part must be substantial relative to the whole group</td></tr><tr><td>Prominence</td><td>Whether the targeted part is emblematic or essential to the survival of the group</td></tr><tr><td>Area of control</td><td>The part may be assessed within the geographic area over which the perpetrators had control</td></tr><tr><td>Leadership</td><td>Destruction of a group's leadership may support intent to destroy the group</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"study-callout\"><strong>Example:</strong> Krstic, decided by the ICTY in 2004, concerned Srebrenica. The Appeals Chamber held that the intent to destroy the Bosnian Muslims of Srebrenica, some forty thousand people, amounted to intent to destroy a substantial part of the group, because Srebrenica was strategically and symbolically prominent and its fall would eliminate the Bosnian Muslim presence in the region. The Chamber also reduced Krstic's own liability from committing genocide to aiding and abetting it, which illustrates that the special intent must be proved against each accused individually.</div><h4>6. The other punishable acts</h4><p>Genocide Convention Article III and Rome Statute Article 25(3)(e) make more than the completed crime punishable.</p><ol><li><strong>Genocide</strong> itself.</li><li><strong>Conspiracy to commit genocide.</strong></li><li><strong>Direct and public incitement to commit genocide.</strong> Punishable whether or not genocide follows, which makes it an inchoate offence.</li><li><strong>Attempt to commit genocide.</strong></li><li><strong>Complicity in genocide.</strong></li></ol><p>Incitement is the most practically important of these. The ICTR convicted in the Media case concerning radio broadcasts and a newspaper that dehumanised the Tutsi population and called for their elimination. The requirements are that the incitement be direct, meaning a specific call to act rather than vague hostility, and public, meaning communicated to a number of individuals in a public place or through mass media. Cultural context is admissible in determining whether coded language was understood as a direct call.</p><div class=\"study-callout\"><strong>Note:</strong> Note the structural point about the Rome Statute: incitement is listed as a mode of liability under Article 25(3)(e) and applies only to genocide, not to the other crimes. Suggesting that incitement to crimes against humanity is an ICC offence is a common error.</div><h4>7. State obligations</h4><ul><li>The Genocide Convention obliges parties to prevent and to punish genocide.</li><li>The International Court of Justice held in the Bosnia v Serbia judgment of 2007 that the duty to prevent is an obligation of conduct, not result: a state must employ all means reasonably available to it, and it breaches the duty by manifestly failing to take measures within its power, whether or not genocide would in fact have been averted.</li><li>The Court also held that a state may itself commit genocide, so state responsibility and individual criminal responsibility run in parallel.</li><li>Article IX gives the Court jurisdiction over disputes about the Convention, which is why the Convention is one of the few routes to contentious proceedings on atrocity questions.</li><li>The duty to prevent is not territorially limited: it applies wherever a state has the capacity to influence those likely to commit genocide.</li></ul><h4>8. How the examiner&#x27;s question should be answered</h4><p>State the definition verbatim if you can, since precision matters here more than anywhere. Take the five acts, then spend most of the answer on the special intent: define dolus specialis, explain that motive is irrelevant, list the inference factors, and state that inference must be the only reasonable one available. Address the protected groups and note the exclusion of political groups. Apply the substantiality criteria with Krstic. If the facts show mass atrocity but ambiguous intent, say so and explain that crimes against humanity is the appropriate charge. That conclusion demonstrates judgement rather than eagerness to find the gravest label.</p><h4>Summary</h4><ul><li>Genocide requires one of five listed acts committed with intent to destroy a national, ethnical, racial or religious group as such.</li><li>The special intent, dolus specialis, is the distinguishing element, and motive is irrelevant to it.</li><li>Intent is normally inferred from context, scale, systematic targeting and utterances, and the inference must be the only reasonable one.</li><li>The four protected groups are exhaustive; political groups were deliberately excluded in 1948.</li><li>The intended destruction must be of a substantial part, assessed by number, prominence, area of control and leadership.</li><li>Conspiracy, direct and public incitement, attempt and complicity are separately punishable.</li></ul><h4>Key Terms</h4><div class=\"table-wrap\"><table class=\"study-table\"><thead><tr><th>Term</th><th>Meaning</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Dolus specialis</td><td>The specific intent to destroy a protected group in whole or in part, as such.</td></tr><tr><td>Protected group</td><td>A national, ethnical, racial or religious group; the list is exhaustive.</td></tr><tr><td>Substantial part</td><td>The portion of a group whose intended destruction suffices, assessed by number, prominence, control area and leadership.</td></tr><tr><td>Direct and public incitement</td><td>An inchoate offence punishable whether or not genocide follows.</td></tr><tr><td>Obligation of conduct</td><td>The duty to prevent genocide by employing all means reasonably available, regardless of outcome.</td></tr></tbody></table></div><h4>Review Questions</h4><h5>Multiple choice</h5><div class=\"study-mcq\"><p class=\"mcq-q\">Which is NOT a protected group under the Genocide Convention?</p><ol type=\"A\"><li>A political group</li><li>A religious group</li><li>An ethnical group</li><li>A national group</li></ol><details class=\"mcq-a\"><summary>Show answer</summary><p><strong>Answer: A.</strong> Political groups were deliberately excluded during the 1948 drafting at the insistence of several states.</p></details></div><div class=\"study-mcq\"><p class=\"mcq-q\">What must be shown for intent to be inferred from circumstantial evidence?</p><ol type=\"A\"><li>That the inference of genocidal intent is the only reasonable one available on the evidence</li><li>That a written plan existed</li><li>That the perpetrator confessed</li><li>That the whole group was destroyed</li></ol><details class=\"mcq-a\"><summary>Show answer</summary><p><strong>Answer: A.</strong> Where the facts are equally consistent with an intent to displace rather than destroy, the charge fails and crimes against humanity is the appropriate alternative.</p></details></div><div class=\"study-mcq\"><p class=\"mcq-q\">What did Krstic decide about &#x27;in part&#x27;?</p><ol type=\"A\"><li>Intent to destroy the Bosnian Muslims of Srebrenica was intent to destroy a substantial part, given the area&#x27;s prominence</li><li>That a majority of the group must be targeted</li><li>That geography is irrelevant</li><li>That leadership targeting is insufficient</li></ol><details class=\"mcq-a\"><summary>Show answer</summary><p><strong>Answer: A.</strong> The Appeals Chamber also reduced Krstic&#x27;s liability to aiding and abetting, showing special intent must be proved against each accused.</p></details></div><div class=\"study-mcq\"><p class=\"mcq-q\">Which offence is punishable whether or not genocide actually occurs?</p><ol type=\"A\"><li>Direct and public incitement to commit genocide</li><li>Complicity in genocide</li><li>Aiding and abetting genocide</li><li>Command responsibility</li></ol><details class=\"mcq-a\"><summary>Show answer</summary><p><strong>Answer: A.</strong> It is an inchoate offence, and under the Rome Statute incitement applies only to genocide, not to the other crimes.</p></details></div><h5>Short answer</h5><div class=\"study-sa\"><p class=\"sa-q\">Explain the special intent requirement in genocide and how it is proved.</p><details class=\"sa-a\"><summary>Model answer</summary><p>Genocide requires a double mental element: intent as to the underlying act, such as killing, and additionally the specific intent to destroy a national, ethnical, racial or religious group in whole or in part, as such. Motive is irrelevant, so hatred, obedience, fear or gain make no difference provided the intent to destroy is present, and &#x27;as such&#x27; means the group is targeted because of its identity rather than incidentally. A plan or policy is not a legal element, though in practice it is powerful evidence. Because confessions are rare, intent is inferred from the general context, the scale and nature of the atrocities, systematic targeting of one group while excluding others, repetition of destructive acts, utterances of the accused, and the targeting of leadership and of men of military age. The inference must be the only reasonable one available on the evidence.</p></details></div><div class=\"study-sa\"><p class=\"sa-q\">List the five prohibited acts of genocide and identify what the definition omits.</p><details class=\"sa-a\"><summary>Model answer</summary><p>The five acts are killing members of the group; causing serious bodily or mental harm to members of the group; deliberately inflicting conditions of life calculated to bring about the group&#x27;s physical destruction in whole or in part; imposing measures intended to prevent births within the group; and forcibly transferring children of the group to another group. Two omissions matter. Cultural destruction is not included, so destroying a group&#x27;s language, religious buildings or heritage is not genocide unless it evidences intent to destroy the group physically or biologically. Political groups are also excluded from the protected categories, having been deliberately left out during the 1948 drafting.</p></details></div><h4>Class Activity</h4><ol><li>Take a situation described in the media as genocide. Apply the definition element by element and state whether the label is legally accurate, or whether crimes against humanity fits better.</li><li>Read a summary of the ICTR Media case. Identify what made the broadcasts &#x27;direct&#x27; rather than merely hostile, and write a paragraph on where you would draw that line.</li></ol><h4>Assignment</h4><p>Write two pages on genocide. Give the definition and the five prohibited acts, explain the special intent requirement and the factors from which intent is inferred, discuss the protected groups including the exclusion of political groups and the subjective identification problem in Akayesu, explain &#x27;in whole or in part&#x27; using Krstic, set out the other punishable acts with attention to incitement, and describe the state obligations established in Bosnia v Serbia.</p><p class=\"study-print\"><strong>Printable:</strong> use your browser\u2019s Print \u2192 Save as PDF to keep an offline copy.</p></div>",
  "Crimes Against Humanity": "<div class=\"study-note\"><div class=\"revision-banner\"><strong>Human Rights and International Humanitarian Law</strong><span>Module 11: Accountability and International Criminal Law</span></div><h3 class=\"lesson-note-title\">LESSON NOTE: CRIMES AGAINST HUMANITY</h3><div class=\"table-wrap\"><table class=\"study-table\"><thead><tr><th>Subject</th><th>Topic</th><th>Level</th><th>Lesson duration</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Human Rights and International Humanitarian Law</td><td>Crimes Against Humanity</td><td>Beginner to Advanced</td><td>45 minutes</td></tr></tbody></table></div><h4>Learning objectives</h4><p>By the end of this lesson you should be able to:</p><ul><li>Define crimes against humanity and state the contextual element</li><li>Explain &#x27;widespread or systematic&#x27; and the policy requirement</li><li>List the underlying acts under the Rome Statute</li><li>Distinguish crimes against humanity from war crimes and genocide</li></ul><h4>1. Introduction</h4><p>Crimes against humanity occupy the space between war crimes, which need an armed conflict, and genocide, which needs a special intent. They require neither. That makes them the most widely applicable of the three categories and, in practice, the charge on which most atrocity cases succeed.</p><h4>2. The definition</h4><div class=\"study-defn\"><strong>Crimes against humanity:</strong> Any of the listed acts when committed as part of a widespread or systematic attack directed against any civilian population, with knowledge of the attack (Rome Statute Article 7).</div><p>The definition has a contextual element and an underlying act. Both must be established, and the contextual element is where the analysis lies.</p><div class=\"table-wrap\"><table class=\"study-table\"><thead><tr><th>Component</th><th>Requirement</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>An attack</td><td>A course of conduct involving the multiple commission of listed acts against a civilian population; it need not be a military attack and may occur in peacetime</td></tr><tr><td>Widespread OR systematic</td><td>Disjunctive: either suffices</td></tr><tr><td>Directed against any civilian population</td><td>The population must be the primary object, not an incidental victim</td></tr><tr><td>Pursuant to a State or organizational policy</td><td>Required by the Rome Statute Art 7(2)(a)</td></tr><tr><td>Knowledge of the attack</td><td>The perpetrator must know their act formed part of it, though not the details or the full extent</td></tr></tbody></table></div><h4>3. Widespread or systematic</h4><div class=\"table-wrap\"><table class=\"study-table\"><thead><tr><th></th><th>Widespread</th><th>Systematic</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Refers to</td><td>Scale</td><td>Organisation</td></tr><tr><td>Assessed by</td><td>The number of victims and the geographic reach of the attack</td><td>The organised nature of the acts and the improbability of their random occurrence; patterns and repetition</td></tr><tr><td>Illustration</td><td>A single act affecting a very large number of victims</td><td>A small number of acts carried out according to a plan or a recurring method</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"study-callout\"><strong>Note:</strong> The disjunctive is critical. Because either suffices, a highly organised campaign with relatively few victims qualifies, and so does a very large but disorganised wave of violence. Writing 'widespread and systematic' is a substantive error, not a slip, and examiners mark it as one.</div><h4>4. The policy requirement</h4><p>Rome Statute Article 7(2)(a) requires that the attack be committed pursuant to or in furtherance of a State or organizational policy. Three points must be made.</p><ol><li><strong>It is a Rome Statute requirement, not a customary one.</strong> The ICTY held in Kunarac that customary international law contains no policy element. The ICC applies it because the Statute says so.</li><li><strong>The policy need not be formal or written.</strong> It may be inferred from the manner in which the acts occur, from patterns, from the mobilisation of resources, and from public statements.</li><li><strong>'Organizational' extends beyond states.</strong> The scope is contested. In the Kenya authorisation decision the Pre-Trial Chamber held that a non-state organisation qualifies if it has the capability to perform acts infringing basic human values, with indicators including a responsible command, the means to carry out a widespread or systematic attack, and an established hierarchy. A dissent argued the organisation must be state-like. That disagreement is worth naming in an answer.</li></ol><h4>5. The underlying acts</h4><div class=\"table-wrap\"><table class=\"study-table\"><thead><tr><th>Act</th><th>Notes</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Murder</td><td></td></tr><tr><td>Extermination</td><td>Includes the intentional infliction of conditions of life calculated to destroy part of a population</td></tr><tr><td>Enslavement</td><td>Exercise of powers attaching to the right of ownership, including trafficking in persons</td></tr><tr><td>Deportation or forcible transfer of population</td><td>Deportation crosses a border; forcible transfer is internal</td></tr><tr><td>Imprisonment or severe deprivation of physical liberty</td><td>In violation of fundamental rules of international law</td></tr><tr><td>Torture</td><td>Note: unlike CAT, no requirement of official involvement or a specific purpose</td></tr><tr><td>Sexual violence offences</td><td>Rape, sexual slavery, enforced prostitution, forced pregnancy, enforced sterilisation, and any other comparable form</td></tr><tr><td>Persecution</td><td>Intentional and severe deprivation of fundamental rights by reason of the identity of a group, on political, racial, national, ethnic, cultural, religious, gender or other universally recognised impermissible grounds</td></tr><tr><td>Enforced disappearance</td><td>Arrest, detention or abduction followed by refusal to acknowledge, with intent to remove from the protection of the law for a prolonged period</td></tr><tr><td>Apartheid</td><td>Inhumane acts committed in the context of an institutionalised regime of systematic oppression and domination by one racial group over another, with the intention of maintaining that regime</td></tr><tr><td>Other inhumane acts</td><td>Of a similar character intentionally causing great suffering or serious injury; a residual category, narrowly construed</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"study-callout\"><strong>Note:</strong> Two definitional differences from other regimes are examinable. Torture as a crime against humanity requires neither a public official nor a specific purpose, unlike the CAT Article 1 definition, so the threshold is in that respect lower. Persecution must be committed in connection with another act within Article 7 or another crime within the Court's jurisdiction, which is a limitation the other underlying acts do not carry.</div><h4>6. Distinguishing the three core crimes</h4><div class=\"table-wrap\"><table class=\"study-table\"><thead><tr><th></th><th>War crimes</th><th>Crimes against humanity</th><th>Genocide</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Armed conflict required</td><td>Yes</td><td>No</td><td>No</td></tr><tr><td>Contextual element</td><td>Nexus to the conflict</td><td>Widespread or systematic attack on a civilian population</td><td>None beyond the acts</td></tr><tr><td>Special intent</td><td>No</td><td>No, but knowledge of the attack</td><td>Yes, intent to destroy the group</td></tr><tr><td>Protected victims</td><td>Persons and objects protected by IHL</td><td>Any civilian population</td><td>Members of four listed groups</td></tr><tr><td>Single act sufficient</td><td>Yes, one act can be a war crime</td><td>Yes, if part of the wider attack</td><td>Yes, if committed with the special intent</td></tr></tbody></table></div><p>The three categories overlap and a single act can be all three at once. A murder committed during an armed conflict, as part of a systematic attack on a civilian population, with intent to destroy an ethnic group, is a war crime, a crime against humanity and genocide simultaneously. Charging in the alternative is normal practice.</p><div class=\"study-callout\"><strong>Example:</strong> Prosecutors facing mass atrocity evidence typically charge crimes against humanity as the primary count and genocide in the alternative. The reason is evidential: the conduct and the contextual element are usually provable from patterns and scale, while the special intent for genocide requires an inference that must exclude every other reasonable explanation. The Krstic outcome, where genocide was found but the accused's own liability reduced to aiding and abetting, shows how narrow that path is.</div><h4>7. Development of the category</h4><ol><li>The term entered international law in the 1915 declaration by France, Great Britain and Russia concerning the Armenian massacres.</li><li>The Nuremberg Charter of 1945 gave it its first legal definition, but tied it to war crimes or crimes against peace, so it could not reach peacetime atrocities.</li><li>The ICTY Statute retained a nexus to armed conflict; the Tadic Appeals Chamber then held that customary law imposes no such requirement.</li><li>The ICTR Statute required a discriminatory intent for all crimes against humanity, which is narrower than customary law and than the Rome Statute.</li><li>The Rome Statute of 1998 dropped the armed conflict nexus entirely and produced the modern definition, adding the policy element.</li><li>There is still no dedicated treaty on crimes against humanity, unlike genocide and the Geneva Conventions. The International Law Commission adopted draft articles in 2019 and the General Assembly has been considering negotiation of a convention. Noting this gap is a good closing point in an essay.</li></ol><h4>8. How the examiner&#x27;s question should be answered</h4><p>Lead with the contextual element, because that is what the question is testing. Write 'widespread or systematic' and say expressly that the test is disjunctive. Deal with the policy requirement and note that it is a Rome Statute innovation rejected as customary in Kunarac, and give the Kenya decision's indicators for an organisation. Then apply the specific underlying act, using the Article 7(2) definitions rather than ordinary meanings. Close by distinguishing the three core crimes on the axes in the table, and note the absence of a dedicated convention.</p><h4>Summary</h4><ul><li>Crimes against humanity require a listed act committed as part of a widespread or systematic attack against any civilian population, with knowledge of the attack.</li><li>The test is disjunctive: widespread refers to scale, systematic to organisation, and either suffices.</li><li>The Rome Statute adds a State or organizational policy requirement that the ICTY held is not part of customary law.</li><li>No armed conflict is required, which distinguishes the category from war crimes.</li><li>Torture as a crime against humanity needs neither official involvement nor a specific purpose.</li><li>There is still no dedicated convention on crimes against humanity, though draft articles exist.</li></ul><h4>Key Terms</h4><div class=\"table-wrap\"><table class=\"study-table\"><thead><tr><th>Term</th><th>Meaning</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Contextual element</td><td>The widespread or systematic attack against a civilian population within which the underlying act occurs.</td></tr><tr><td>Widespread</td><td>Large scale, assessed by the number of victims and the geographic reach of the attack.</td></tr><tr><td>Systematic</td><td>Organised in nature, showing patterns and the improbability of random occurrence.</td></tr><tr><td>Persecution</td><td>Severe deprivation of fundamental rights by reason of group identity, requiring connection to another listed crime.</td></tr><tr><td>Policy element</td><td>The Rome Statute requirement that the attack further a State or organizational policy.</td></tr></tbody></table></div><h4>Review Questions</h4><h5>Multiple choice</h5><div class=\"study-mcq\"><p class=\"mcq-q\">What is the contextual element of crimes against humanity?</p><ol type=\"A\"><li>A widespread or systematic attack directed against any civilian population</li><li>An international armed conflict</li><li>Intent to destroy a protected group</li><li>A formal declaration of emergency</li></ol><details class=\"mcq-a\"><summary>Show answer</summary><p><strong>Answer: A.</strong> The test is disjunctive: either widespread or systematic suffices, and writing &#x27;and&#x27; is a substantive error.</p></details></div><div class=\"study-mcq\"><p class=\"mcq-q\">Is an armed conflict required for crimes against humanity?</p><ol type=\"A\"><li>No; the Rome Statute dropped the nexus and Tadic held customary law imposes none</li><li>Yes, in all cases</li><li>Yes, but only a non-international one</li><li>Only where the accused is a combatant</li></ol><details class=\"mcq-a\"><summary>Show answer</summary><p><strong>Answer: A.</strong> The Nuremberg Charter tied the category to war, which is why it could not reach peacetime atrocities.</p></details></div><div class=\"study-mcq\"><p class=\"mcq-q\">How does torture as a crime against humanity differ from torture under the Convention Against Torture?</p><ol type=\"A\"><li>It requires neither official involvement nor a specific purpose</li><li>It requires a higher level of severity</li><li>It applies only in armed conflict</li><li>It requires a discriminatory motive</li></ol><details class=\"mcq-a\"><summary>Show answer</summary><p><strong>Answer: A.</strong> The CAT Article 1 definition requires intention, a prohibited purpose and official involvement or acquiescence.</p></details></div><div class=\"study-mcq\"><p class=\"mcq-q\">Which underlying act must be connected to another crime within the Court&#x27;s jurisdiction?</p><ol type=\"A\"><li>Persecution</li><li>Murder</li><li>Enslavement</li><li>Apartheid</li></ol><details class=\"mcq-a\"><summary>Show answer</summary><p><strong>Answer: A.</strong> That connection requirement is a limitation the other underlying acts do not carry.</p></details></div><h5>Short answer</h5><div class=\"study-sa\"><p class=\"sa-q\">Explain &#x27;widespread or systematic&#x27; and the policy requirement.</p><details class=\"sa-a\"><summary>Model answer</summary><p>Widespread refers to the scale of the attack, assessed by the number of victims and its geographic reach. Systematic refers to its organised nature, shown by patterns, repetition and the improbability that the acts occurred randomly. The two are disjunctive, so a highly organised campaign with relatively few victims qualifies and so does a large but disorganised wave of violence. Separately, Rome Statute Article 7(2)(a) requires the attack to be committed pursuant to or in furtherance of a State or organizational policy. This is a Statute requirement rather than a customary one, the ICTY having held in Kunarac that customary law contains no policy element. The policy need not be formal or written and may be inferred from the manner of the acts, from resource mobilisation and from public statements.</p></details></div><div class=\"study-sa\"><p class=\"sa-q\">Distinguish the three core international crimes.</p><details class=\"sa-a\"><summary>Model answer</summary><p>War crimes require a nexus to an armed conflict and protect persons and objects protected by international humanitarian law; no special intent is needed. Crimes against humanity require no armed conflict but require that the act form part of a widespread or systematic attack directed against any civilian population, with the perpetrator&#x27;s knowledge of that attack. Genocide requires neither an armed conflict nor a widespread attack, but requires the special intent to destroy a national, ethnical, racial or religious group in whole or in part, as such. The categories overlap and a single act can constitute all three at once, which is why charging in the alternative is standard practice.</p></details></div><h4>Class Activity</h4><ol><li>Take a situation of mass violence outside any armed conflict. Assess whether it satisfies the contextual element, addressing scale, organisation and the policy requirement separately.</li><li>Research the International Law Commission&#x27;s draft articles on crimes against humanity. Write one page on what a convention would add that the Rome Statute does not provide.</li></ol><h4>Assignment</h4><p>Write two pages on crimes against humanity. Give the definition and break down the contextual element, explain widespread and systematic as disjunctive alternatives, analyse the policy requirement including its status in customary law and the meaning of &#x27;organizational&#x27;, list and explain the underlying acts noting how torture and persecution differ from their other definitions, distinguish the three core crimes across at least four axes, and trace the category&#x27;s development from Nuremberg to the present.</p><p class=\"study-print\"><strong>Printable:</strong> use your browser\u2019s Print \u2192 Save as PDF to keep an offline copy.</p></div>",
  "The International Criminal Court": "<div class=\"study-note\"><div class=\"revision-banner\"><strong>Human Rights and International Humanitarian Law</strong><span>Module 11: Accountability and International Criminal Law</span></div><h3 class=\"lesson-note-title\">LESSON NOTE: THE INTERNATIONAL CRIMINAL COURT</h3><div class=\"table-wrap\"><table class=\"study-table\"><thead><tr><th>Subject</th><th>Topic</th><th>Level</th><th>Lesson duration</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Human Rights and International Humanitarian Law</td><td>The International Criminal Court</td><td>Beginner to Advanced</td><td>45 minutes</td></tr></tbody></table></div><h4>Learning objectives</h4><p>By the end of this lesson you should be able to:</p><ul><li>Describe the establishment, structure and jurisdiction of the ICC</li><li>Explain complementarity and admissibility</li><li>Explain the trigger mechanisms and the role of the Prosecutor</li><li>Assess the criticisms made of the Court</li></ul><h4>1. Introduction</h4><p>The International Criminal Court is the first permanent international criminal tribunal. Understanding it means understanding two things above all: that it can only act where states do not, and that it has no police force. Almost every criticism of the Court traces back to one of those two facts.</p><h4>2. Establishment and structure</h4><div class=\"table-wrap\"><table class=\"study-table\"><thead><tr><th>Feature</th><th>Detail</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Founding treaty</td><td>Rome Statute, adopted 17 July 1998, in force 1 July 2002</td></tr><tr><td>Parties</td><td>Approximately 125 states</td></tr><tr><td>Seat</td><td>The Hague</td></tr><tr><td>Legal nature</td><td>A treaty-based international organisation, not a United Nations organ, though it has a relationship agreement with the UN</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"table-wrap\"><table class=\"study-table\"><thead><tr><th>Organ</th><th>Function</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Presidency</td><td>Three judges; administration of the Court other than the Office of the Prosecutor</td></tr><tr><td>Judicial Divisions</td><td>Pre-Trial, Trial and Appeals; eighteen judges in total</td></tr><tr><td>Office of the Prosecutor</td><td>Independent; conducts preliminary examinations, investigations and prosecutions</td></tr><tr><td>Registry</td><td>Non-judicial administration, victim and witness support, defence support, detention</td></tr><tr><td>Assembly of States Parties</td><td>Oversight and legislative body; elects judges and the Prosecutor; adopts the budget</td></tr><tr><td>Trust Fund for Victims</td><td>Implements reparations orders and provides assistance to victims independently of any conviction</td></tr></tbody></table></div><h4>3. Jurisdiction</h4><p>The Court's jurisdiction has four dimensions, and all must be satisfied.</p><ol><li><strong>Ratione materiae.</strong> Genocide, crimes against humanity, war crimes, and the crime of aggression.</li><li><strong>Ratione temporis.</strong> Only crimes committed after 1 July 2002, or after entry into force for a state that joined later, unless that state has lodged an Article 12(3) declaration accepting jurisdiction retroactively to 2002.</li><li><strong>Ratione loci and personae.</strong> Crimes committed on the territory of a state party, or by a national of a state party. A Security Council referral overrides these limits.</li><li><strong>Admissibility.</strong> Considered separately below.</li></ol><p>The crime of aggression has a distinct and more restrictive regime, activated in 2018 following the Kampala amendments of 2010. The Court may exercise jurisdiction over aggression only where both the aggressor and the victim state have ratified the amendments, with an opt-out available, and Security Council referrals operate differently. This asymmetry is deliberate and is the price of agreement.</p><div class=\"study-defn\"><strong>Crime of aggression:</strong> The planning, preparation, initiation or execution, by a person in a position effectively to exercise control over or to direct the political or military action of a State, of an act of aggression which by its character, gravity and scale constitutes a manifest violation of the Charter of the United Nations.</div><h4>4. Complementarity</h4><div class=\"study-defn\"><strong>Complementarity:</strong> The principle that the International Criminal Court is a court of last resort, exercising jurisdiction only where national jurisdictions are unwilling or genuinely unable to carry out the investigation or prosecution.</div><p>Article 17 makes a case inadmissible where it is being investigated or prosecuted by a state with jurisdiction, or has been investigated and that state decided not to prosecute, unless the state is unwilling or unable genuinely to proceed.</p><div class=\"table-wrap\"><table class=\"study-table\"><thead><tr><th>Test</th><th>Indicators</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Unwillingness</td><td>Proceedings undertaken to shield the person from criminal responsibility; unjustified delay inconsistent with an intent to bring the person to justice; proceedings not conducted independently or impartially and inconsistent with an intent to bring the person to justice</td></tr><tr><td>Inability</td><td>Total or substantial collapse or unavailability of the national judicial system, such that the state cannot obtain the accused, the evidence or testimony, or is otherwise unable to carry out proceedings</td></tr></tbody></table></div><p>Two further points complete the picture.</p><ul><li><strong>The same person, same conduct test.</strong> National proceedings render a case inadmissible only if they cover substantially the same conduct against the same person. Prosecuting a different individual, or the same individual for unrelated conduct, does not oust the Court.</li><li><strong>Gravity.</strong> Article 17(1)(d) makes a case inadmissible where it is not of sufficient gravity to justify further action, which operates as an independent filter even where no state is acting.</li></ul><div class=\"study-callout\"><strong>Note:</strong> Complementarity is often described as a weakness. The better view is that it is the Court's central design feature and its main practical achievement: the credible prospect of ICC action has prompted several states to legislate the Rome Statute crimes into domestic law and to conduct their own proceedings. That indirect effect reaches far more cases than the Court's own docket ever could.</div><h4>5. Trigger mechanisms</h4><div class=\"table-wrap\"><table class=\"study-table\"><thead><tr><th>Trigger</th><th>Article</th><th>Effect</th><th>Note</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>State party referral</td><td>13(a), 14</td><td>A state party refers a situation</td><td>Includes self-referrals, which have been common</td></tr><tr><td>Security Council referral</td><td>13(b)</td><td>Acting under Chapter VII</td><td>Overrides the territorial and nationality limits, so it can reach non-parties</td></tr><tr><td>Proprio motu</td><td>13(c), 15</td><td>The Prosecutor initiates on their own authority</td><td>Requires Pre-Trial Chamber authorisation</td></tr></tbody></table></div><p>The proprio motu power was among the most contested provisions at Rome. The safeguard is judicial: the Prosecutor must obtain authorisation from a Pre-Trial Chamber, which examines whether there is a reasonable basis to proceed and whether the case appears to fall within jurisdiction.</p><p>Before any investigation, the Office conducts a preliminary examination assessing jurisdiction, admissibility and the interests of justice. Article 53 allows the Prosecutor to decline where an investigation would not serve the interests of justice, a discretion that has been used sparingly and is subject to judicial review.</p><p>Article 16 allows the Security Council to defer an investigation or prosecution for twelve months, renewable, by a resolution under Chapter VII. It is the political brake on the system.</p><h4>6. Procedure and victims</h4><ol><li><strong>Preliminary examination</strong>, then investigation on authorisation or referral.</li><li><strong>Arrest warrant or summons</strong> issued by the Pre-Trial Chamber. The Court has no police force and depends entirely on state cooperation for arrests, which is its single greatest practical weakness.</li><li><strong>Confirmation of charges</strong> before the Pre-Trial Chamber, requiring substantial grounds to believe the person committed the crimes.</li><li><strong>Trial</strong> before the Trial Chamber. No trials in absentia are permitted.</li><li><strong>Judgment and sentence</strong>, with a maximum of thirty years or life where justified by extreme gravity.</li><li><strong>Appeal</strong> on grounds of procedural error, error of fact or error of law.</li><li><strong>Reparations</strong> under Article 75, ordered against the convicted person and implemented through the Trust Fund for Victims.</li></ol><p>Victim participation is a genuine innovation. Article 68(3) allows victims to present their views and concerns at stages the Court considers appropriate, through legal representatives, independently of being called as witnesses. No previous international criminal tribunal permitted this.</p><h4>7. Criticisms and responses</h4><div class=\"table-wrap\"><table class=\"study-table\"><thead><tr><th>Criticism</th><th>Response</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Disproportionate focus on Africa</td><td>Several early situations were self-referrals by African states themselves, and Security Council referrals are outside the Prosecutor's control. The Office has since opened examinations and investigations in other regions. The criticism nonetheless had force in the Court's first decade and shaped African Union relations</td></tr><tr><td>Slow and expensive</td><td>Accurate. Cases have taken many years and cost substantially. The response is that the crimes are complex, the evidence is distant and contested, and full fair trial rights are non-negotiable</td></tr><tr><td>Few convictions</td><td>Accurate. The number of completed convictions is small relative to the Court's cost and lifespan, and several high-profile cases collapsed</td></tr><tr><td>Depends on states for arrests</td><td>Structural and unanswerable within the current design. Suspects at liberty in cooperative states can be surrendered; those protected by their own governments cannot</td></tr><tr><td>Major powers are outside</td><td>Several permanent members of the Security Council are not parties, which produces an asymmetry the Court cannot cure</td></tr><tr><td>Peace versus justice</td><td>Prosecution may complicate negotiated settlements. The counter-argument is that impunity entrenches recurrence, and Article 53 gives limited room for the interests of justice</td></tr></tbody></table></div><p>A fair overall assessment says three things. The Court's direct output is modest. Its indirect effect through complementarity, domestic legislation and the normalisation of accountability is larger and harder to measure. And its central constraint, dependence on state cooperation, was chosen deliberately by the states that created it.</p><h4>8. How the examiner&#x27;s question should be answered</h4><p>Set out the four dimensions of jurisdiction before anything else, since most problem questions turn on one of them. Treat complementarity as a two-stage inquiry, unwillingness and inability, and apply the same person, same conduct test. Name the three triggers and note that Security Council referral overrides the territorial and nationality limits. On evaluation, do not simply list criticisms: pair each with its response and reach a judgement, and separate the Court's direct output from its indirect effect through complementarity.</p><h4>Summary</h4><ul><li>The ICC was established by the Rome Statute of 1998, in force 2002, with jurisdiction over genocide, crimes against humanity, war crimes and aggression.</li><li>Jurisdiction requires subject matter, temporal, territorial or national, and admissibility conditions all to be met.</li><li>Complementarity makes the Court one of last resort, acting only where a state is unwilling or genuinely unable to proceed.</li><li>The same person, same conduct test governs whether national proceedings render a case inadmissible.</li><li>Three triggers exist: state party referral, Security Council referral, and the Prosecutor acting proprio motu with judicial authorisation.</li><li>The Court has no police force and depends on state cooperation for arrests, which is its greatest practical weakness.</li></ul><h4>Key Terms</h4><div class=\"table-wrap\"><table class=\"study-table\"><thead><tr><th>Term</th><th>Meaning</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Complementarity</td><td>The principle that the ICC acts only where national jurisdictions are unwilling or genuinely unable to proceed.</td></tr><tr><td>Proprio motu</td><td>The Prosecutor&#x27;s power to initiate an investigation independently, subject to Pre-Trial Chamber authorisation.</td></tr><tr><td>Same person, same conduct</td><td>The test determining whether national proceedings displace an ICC case.</td></tr><tr><td>Article 16 deferral</td><td>The Security Council&#x27;s power to suspend an investigation or prosecution for renewable twelve-month periods.</td></tr><tr><td>Trust Fund for Victims</td><td>The body implementing reparations and assisting victims independently of any conviction.</td></tr></tbody></table></div><h4>Review Questions</h4><h5>Multiple choice</h5><div class=\"study-mcq\"><p class=\"mcq-q\">What does complementarity mean?</p><ol type=\"A\"><li>The Court acts only where national jurisdictions are unwilling or genuinely unable to investigate or prosecute</li><li>The Court shares jurisdiction equally with national courts</li><li>The Court reviews national judgments on appeal</li><li>The Court applies national law</li></ol><details class=\"mcq-a\"><summary>Show answer</summary><p><strong>Answer: A.</strong> Its main practical achievement is prompting states to legislate the Rome Statute crimes domestically and conduct their own proceedings.</p></details></div><div class=\"study-mcq\"><p class=\"mcq-q\">Which trigger allows the Court to reach crimes in a state that is not a party?</p><ol type=\"A\"><li>A Security Council referral under Chapter VII</li><li>A state party referral</li><li>The Prosecutor acting proprio motu</li><li>An Article 12(3) declaration by a third state</li></ol><details class=\"mcq-a\"><summary>Show answer</summary><p><strong>Answer: A.</strong> It overrides the ordinary territorial and nationality limits on jurisdiction.</p></details></div><div class=\"study-mcq\"><p class=\"mcq-q\">A state prosecutes a different individual for the same events. Is the ICC case inadmissible?</p><ol type=\"A\"><li>No; the same person, same conduct test requires proceedings against the same person for substantially the same conduct</li><li>Yes, any national proceedings suffice</li><li>Yes, if the state is a party</li><li>Only if a conviction results</li></ol><details class=\"mcq-a\"><summary>Show answer</summary><p><strong>Answer: A.</strong> Prosecuting a different individual, or the same individual for unrelated conduct, does not oust the Court.</p></details></div><div class=\"study-mcq\"><p class=\"mcq-q\">What is the Court&#x27;s greatest practical weakness?</p><ol type=\"A\"><li>It has no police force and depends entirely on states to arrest and surrender suspects</li><li>It cannot try war crimes</li><li>It has no appeals procedure</li><li>It cannot order reparations</li></ol><details class=\"mcq-a\"><summary>Show answer</summary><p><strong>Answer: A.</strong> This constraint was chosen deliberately by the states that created the Court and cannot be cured within the current design.</p></details></div><h5>Short answer</h5><div class=\"study-sa\"><p class=\"sa-q\">Explain the admissibility test under Article 17.</p><details class=\"sa-a\"><summary>Model answer</summary><p>A case is inadmissible where it is being investigated or prosecuted by a state with jurisdiction, or has been investigated and that state decided not to prosecute, unless the state is unwilling or genuinely unable to proceed. Unwillingness is shown where proceedings are undertaken to shield the person from responsibility, where there is unjustified delay inconsistent with an intent to bring them to justice, or where proceedings are not independent or impartial and are inconsistent with such an intent. Inability is shown where the national judicial system has totally or substantially collapsed or is unavailable, so the state cannot obtain the accused, the evidence or testimony. National proceedings displace an ICC case only if they concern substantially the same conduct against the same person. Separately, a case is inadmissible if it is not of sufficient gravity.</p></details></div><div class=\"study-sa\"><p class=\"sa-q\">Give three criticisms of the ICC with a response to each.</p><details class=\"sa-a\"><summary>Model answer</summary><p>First, disproportionate focus on Africa: several early situations were self-referrals by African states, and Security Council referrals lie outside the Prosecutor&#x27;s control, though the criticism had real force in the Court&#x27;s first decade and damaged relations with the African Union. Second, slowness and expense: this is accurate, and the response is that the crimes are complex, the evidence distant and contested, and full fair trial rights cannot be traded for speed. Third, dependence on states for arrests: this is structural and unanswerable within the current design, since the Court has no police force, so suspects protected by their own governments remain at liberty. A fair assessment separates the Court&#x27;s modest direct output from its larger indirect effect through complementarity and domestic legislation.</p></details></div><h4>Class Activity</h4><ol><li>Check whether your country is a party to the Rome Statute and whether it has enacted implementing legislation creating the Rome Statute crimes in domestic law. Report the position in one paragraph.</li><li>Take one ICC case that collapsed or ended in acquittal. Identify the reason and write half a page on what it shows about the Court&#x27;s evidentiary difficulties.</li></ol><h4>Assignment</h4><p>Write two pages on the International Criminal Court. Describe its establishment, organs and the four dimensions of its jurisdiction including the special regime for aggression, explain complementarity and the Article 17 admissibility test with the same person same conduct requirement, set out the three trigger mechanisms and the safeguards on the Prosecutor, describe the procedure and victim participation, and assess the Court by pairing each major criticism with its response.</p><p class=\"study-print\"><strong>Printable:</strong> use your browser\u2019s Print \u2192 Save as PDF to keep an offline copy.</p></div>",
  "Ad Hoc Tribunals and Hybrid Courts": "<div class=\"study-note\"><div class=\"revision-banner\"><strong>Human Rights and International Humanitarian Law</strong><span>Module 11: Accountability and International Criminal Law</span></div><h3 class=\"lesson-note-title\">LESSON NOTE: AD HOC TRIBUNALS AND HYBRID COURTS</h3><div class=\"table-wrap\"><table class=\"study-table\"><thead><tr><th>Subject</th><th>Topic</th><th>Level</th><th>Lesson duration</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Human Rights and International Humanitarian Law</td><td>Ad Hoc Tribunals and Hybrid Courts</td><td>Beginner to Advanced</td><td>40 minutes</td></tr></tbody></table></div><h4>Learning objectives</h4><p>By the end of this lesson you should be able to:</p><ul><li>Describe the ad hoc tribunals for the former Yugoslavia and Rwanda</li><li>Explain their legal basis and principal contributions</li><li>Describe the main hybrid and internationalised courts</li><li>Compare the models and assess their advantages</li></ul><h4>1. Introduction</h4><p>The permanent Court came late. Between Nuremberg and Rome, and since, accountability has been delivered by tribunals created for a single situation. Their jurisprudence built most of the law the ICC now applies, and the hybrid model that followed them remains the most likely vehicle for accountability in many situations today.</p><h4>2. The ad hoc tribunals</h4><div class=\"table-wrap\"><table class=\"study-table\"><thead><tr><th></th><th>ICTY</th><th>ICTR</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Created</td><td>Security Council resolution 827, 1993</td><td>Security Council resolution 955, 1994</td></tr><tr><td>Legal basis</td><td>Chapter VII of the UN Charter, as a measure to restore international peace and security</td><td>The same</td></tr><tr><td>Seat</td><td>The Hague</td><td>Arusha</td></tr><tr><td>Jurisdiction</td><td>Grave breaches, violations of the laws or customs of war, genocide, crimes against humanity, in the former Yugoslavia from 1991</td><td>Genocide, crimes against humanity, violations of common Article 3 and Additional Protocol II, in Rwanda and by Rwandans in neighbouring states during 1994</td></tr><tr><td>Primacy</td><td>Primacy over national courts</td><td>Primacy over national courts</td></tr><tr><td>Closed</td><td>2017</td><td>2015</td></tr><tr><td>Successor</td><td>International Residual Mechanism for Criminal Tribunals</td><td>The same</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"study-callout\"><strong>Note:</strong> Note the contrast with the ICC on two points. The ad hoc tribunals were created by Security Council resolution, not by treaty, so all UN member states were bound to cooperate. And they had primacy over national courts, the opposite of complementarity. Those two features made them more powerful in their own situations and impossible to replicate generally.</div><h4>3. What the tribunals established</h4><div class=\"table-wrap\"><table class=\"study-table\"><thead><tr><th>Case</th><th>Contribution</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Tadic (ICTY, 1995 and 1999)</td><td>Jurisdiction and the definition of armed conflict; individual criminal responsibility for serious violations in non-international conflict; the overall control test; the joint criminal enterprise doctrine</td></tr><tr><td>Akayesu (ICTR, 1998)</td><td>First conviction for genocide by an international tribunal; held that rape and sexual violence can constitute genocide and crimes against humanity</td></tr><tr><td>Celebici (ICTY, 1998)</td><td>Command responsibility, including that of civilian superiors; the elements of the doctrine</td></tr><tr><td>Furundzija (ICTY, 1998)</td><td>The prohibition of torture as jus cogens; the elements of torture in international criminal law</td></tr><tr><td>Kunarac (ICTY, 2001)</td><td>Enslavement and rape as crimes against humanity; the nexus test for war crimes; no policy element in customary crimes against humanity</td></tr><tr><td>Krstic (ICTY, 2004)</td><td>Genocide at Srebrenica; the meaning of 'in part'</td></tr><tr><td>Media case (ICTR, 2003)</td><td>Direct and public incitement to genocide through mass media</td></tr><tr><td>Galic (ICTY, 2006)</td><td>The crime of spreading terror among a civilian population</td></tr></tbody></table></div><p>Taken together this is the jurisprudential foundation of modern international criminal law. Almost every doctrine the ICC applies was first worked out in these two tribunals, which is why their decisions are still cited constantly despite both being closed.</p><h4>4. Criticisms of the ad hoc model</h4><ol><li><strong>Cost.</strong> Both tribunals consumed a very substantial share of the UN budget over two decades for a limited number of completed cases.</li><li><strong>Distance.</strong> Sitting in The Hague and Arusha put proceedings far from the affected populations, weakening their demonstrative effect. The ICTR's outreach programme was a response to this criticism.</li><li><strong>Selectivity.</strong> Tribunals were created for two situations and not for others equally grave, which invited the charge of political selection.</li><li><strong>Length.</strong> Trials ran for years, and some accused died before judgment.</li><li><strong>Limited legacy for national systems.</strong> Because they had primacy and operated separately, they built little capacity in the countries concerned. The hybrid model was designed to answer this.</li></ol><h4>5. Hybrid and internationalised courts</h4><div class=\"study-defn\"><strong>Hybrid court:</strong> A tribunal combining international and national elements in its legal basis, applicable law, composition or location.</div><div class=\"table-wrap\"><table class=\"study-table\"><thead><tr><th>Court</th><th>Basis</th><th>Features</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Special Court for Sierra Leone</td><td>Agreement between the UN and Sierra Leone, 2002</td><td>Sat in Freetown; mixed international and national judges; applied international and some Sierra Leonean law; first to convict for recruitment of child soldiers and for forced marriage as a crime against humanity; convicted a former head of state, Charles Taylor</td></tr><tr><td>Extraordinary Chambers in the Courts of Cambodia</td><td>Cambodian law with UN participation, 2003</td><td>Chambers within the national system; majority Cambodian judges with a supermajority voting rule; extensive civil party participation</td></tr><tr><td>Special Panels for Serious Crimes, Timor-Leste</td><td>UN transitional administration regulation, 2000</td><td>Panels within the district court; mixed benches</td></tr><tr><td>Regulation 64 Panels, Kosovo</td><td>UN transitional administration regulation, 2000</td><td>International judges and prosecutors inserted into the domestic system</td></tr><tr><td>Special Tribunal for Lebanon</td><td>Security Council resolution 1757, 2007</td><td>Applied Lebanese criminal law; the only such tribunal with terrorism jurisdiction; permitted trials in absentia</td></tr><tr><td>Extraordinary African Chambers</td><td>Agreement between the African Union and Senegal, 2013</td><td>Sat in Dakar; tried and convicted Hissene Habre in 2016 under universal jurisdiction</td></tr><tr><td>Kosovo Specialist Chambers</td><td>Kosovo law, 2015</td><td>Sits in The Hague; entirely international staff applying Kosovo law</td></tr><tr><td>Special Criminal Court, Central African Republic</td><td>National law with UN support, 2015</td><td>Sits in Bangui; mixed national and international judges and prosecutors</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"study-callout\"><strong>Example:</strong> The Extraordinary African Chambers are the most instructive example for this course. Senegal was obliged by the Convention Against Torture, as the International Court of Justice held in Belgium v Senegal, either to prosecute Hissene Habre or to extradite him. The African Union and Senegal created special chambers within the Senegalese court system, with an African Union appointed president, and Habre was convicted in 2016 of crimes against humanity, war crimes and torture. It demonstrates an African regional solution to an African accountability problem, executed through a national court system, and it is the natural case to cite in any answer written in this region.</div><h4>6. Comparing the models</h4><div class=\"table-wrap\"><table class=\"study-table\"><thead><tr><th></th><th>Ad hoc tribunal</th><th>Hybrid court</th><th>ICC</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Basis</td><td>Security Council resolution</td><td>Treaty or national law with international participation</td><td>Multilateral treaty</td></tr><tr><td>Relationship to national courts</td><td>Primacy</td><td>Integrated or parallel</td><td>Complementarity</td></tr><tr><td>Location</td><td>Usually distant</td><td>Usually in or near the affected country</td><td>The Hague</td></tr><tr><td>Applicable law</td><td>International</td><td>Mixed international and national</td><td>The Rome Statute</td></tr><tr><td>Capacity building</td><td>Minimal</td><td>Substantial, a core rationale</td><td>Indirect, through complementarity</td></tr><tr><td>Cost</td><td>Very high</td><td>Lower, but funding is often precarious</td><td>High, but shared among parties</td></tr><tr><td>Perceived legitimacy locally</td><td>Variable, distance is a problem</td><td>Higher where genuinely integrated</td><td>Variable</td></tr><tr><td>Duration</td><td>Open-ended, in practice decades</td><td>Defined mandate</td><td>Permanent</td></tr></tbody></table></div><p>The advantages of the hybrid model are proximity to victims and witnesses, use of national law and language, capacity building within the domestic system, and lower cost. Its weaknesses are precarious funding, which has repeatedly disrupted these courts, vulnerability to political interference where the state retains control, and difficulty securing the independence of national staff in a divided society.</p><h4>7. How the examiner&#x27;s question should be answered</h4><p>Distinguish the three models on their legal basis first, because everything else follows from it: Security Council resolution gives primacy and universal cooperation duties, treaty gives complementarity and limited membership, national law with international support gives proximity and capacity but political exposure. Name specific courts with specific contributions rather than describing hybrids generally. Cite at least one African example, and the Extraordinary African Chambers is the strongest. Conclude by identifying which model best fits a situation you specify, with reasons.</p><h4>Summary</h4><ul><li>The ICTY and ICTR were created by Security Council resolutions under Chapter VII and had primacy over national courts.</li><li>Their jurisprudence built most of modern international criminal law, including Tadic, Akayesu, Celebici, Furundzija, Kunarac and Krstic.</li><li>They were criticised for cost, distance from affected populations, selectivity, length and limited national capacity building.</li><li>Hybrid courts combine international and national elements in basis, law, composition or location.</li><li>The Special Court for Sierra Leone first convicted for child soldier recruitment and forced marriage, and convicted a former head of state.</li><li>The Extraordinary African Chambers convicted Hissene Habre in Dakar, an African regional solution executed through a national system.</li></ul><h4>Key Terms</h4><div class=\"table-wrap\"><table class=\"study-table\"><thead><tr><th>Term</th><th>Meaning</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Ad hoc tribunal</td><td>A tribunal created by the Security Council for a single situation, with primacy over national courts.</td></tr><tr><td>Hybrid court</td><td>A tribunal combining international and national elements in its basis, law, composition or location.</td></tr><tr><td>Primacy</td><td>The power of a tribunal to require national courts to defer to it, the opposite of complementarity.</td></tr><tr><td>Residual Mechanism</td><td>The body carrying out the remaining functions of the ICTY and ICTR after their closure.</td></tr><tr><td>Extraordinary African Chambers</td><td>The chambers within the Senegalese system that convicted Hissene Habre in 2016.</td></tr></tbody></table></div><h4>Review Questions</h4><h5>Multiple choice</h5><div class=\"study-mcq\"><p class=\"mcq-q\">What was the legal basis of the ICTY and ICTR?</p><ol type=\"A\"><li>Security Council resolutions under Chapter VII of the UN Charter</li><li>A multilateral treaty</li><li>General Assembly resolutions</li><li>Agreements with the affected states</li></ol><details class=\"mcq-a\"><summary>Show answer</summary><p><strong>Answer: A.</strong> That basis bound all UN member states to cooperate and gave the tribunals primacy over national courts.</p></details></div><div class=\"study-mcq\"><p class=\"mcq-q\">How does primacy differ from complementarity?</p><ol type=\"A\"><li>Primacy requires national courts to defer to the tribunal; complementarity makes the court one of last resort</li><li>They are the same principle</li><li>Primacy applies only to genocide</li><li>Complementarity applies only to hybrid courts</li></ol><details class=\"mcq-a\"><summary>Show answer</summary><p><strong>Answer: A.</strong> The ad hoc tribunals had primacy while the ICC operates on complementarity, which is the opposite arrangement.</p></details></div><div class=\"study-mcq\"><p class=\"mcq-q\">Which case first held that rape can constitute genocide?</p><ol type=\"A\"><li>Akayesu before the ICTR</li><li>Tadic before the ICTY</li><li>Krstic before the ICTY</li><li>Lubanga before the ICC</li></ol><details class=\"mcq-a\"><summary>Show answer</summary><p><strong>Answer: A.</strong> Akayesu in 1998 was also the first conviction for genocide by an international tribunal.</p></details></div><div class=\"study-mcq\"><p class=\"mcq-q\">What makes the Extraordinary African Chambers significant?</p><ol type=\"A\"><li>An African regional solution executed through a national court system, convicting Hissene Habre under universal jurisdiction</li><li>It was the first hybrid court</li><li>It sat in The Hague</li><li>It applied only international law</li></ol><details class=\"mcq-a\"><summary>Show answer</summary><p><strong>Answer: A.</strong> Senegal was obliged under the Convention Against Torture either to prosecute or extradite, as the International Court of Justice held in Belgium v Senegal.</p></details></div><h5>Short answer</h5><div class=\"study-sa\"><p class=\"sa-q\">Give four criticisms of the ad hoc tribunal model.</p><details class=\"sa-a\"><summary>Model answer</summary><p>First, cost: both tribunals absorbed a very large share of the United Nations budget across two decades for a limited number of completed cases. Second, distance: sitting in The Hague and Arusha placed proceedings far from the affected populations, weakening their demonstrative and reconciliatory effect and prompting outreach programmes in response. Third, selectivity: tribunals were created for two situations while others equally grave received none, inviting the charge that accountability followed political convenience. Fourth, limited legacy: because the tribunals had primacy and operated separately from national systems, they built little domestic capacity, which is precisely the gap the hybrid model was designed to fill. Length is a fifth, with trials running for years and some accused dying before judgment.</p></details></div><div class=\"study-sa\"><p class=\"sa-q\">Explain the advantages and weaknesses of hybrid courts.</p><details class=\"sa-a\"><summary>Model answer</summary><p>Their advantages are proximity to victims, witnesses and the affected society, which strengthens legitimacy and demonstrative effect; the use of national law and language, making proceedings intelligible locally; capacity building within the domestic justice system through mixed benches and shared working; and lower cost than a fully international tribunal. Their weaknesses are precarious funding, since many depend on voluntary contributions and have had operations disrupted; vulnerability to political interference where the state retains substantial control over appointments or budget; and the difficulty of securing genuine independence for national judges and prosecutors in a society still divided by the events being tried.</p></details></div><h4>Class Activity</h4><ol><li>Choose one hybrid court and research its funding arrangements. Write half a page on how funding affected its work.</li><li>Design an accountability mechanism for a hypothetical post-conflict state. Choose among the three models, justify the choice, and specify the composition, applicable law and location.</li></ol><h4>Assignment</h4><p>Write one and a half pages on ad hoc tribunals and hybrid courts. Describe the ICTY and ICTR with their legal basis and the doctrines they established, set out the criticisms of the ad hoc model, describe at least four hybrid courts with their distinctive features including one African example, compare the three accountability models across at least five axes, and assess the advantages and weaknesses of the hybrid approach.</p><p class=\"study-print\"><strong>Printable:</strong> use your browser\u2019s Print \u2192 Save as PDF to keep an offline copy.</p></div>",
  "Universal Jurisdiction": "<div class=\"study-note\"><div class=\"revision-banner\"><strong>Human Rights and International Humanitarian Law</strong><span>Module 11: Accountability and International Criminal Law</span></div><h3 class=\"lesson-note-title\">LESSON NOTE: UNIVERSAL JURISDICTION</h3><div class=\"table-wrap\"><table class=\"study-table\"><thead><tr><th>Subject</th><th>Topic</th><th>Level</th><th>Lesson duration</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Human Rights and International Humanitarian Law</td><td>Universal Jurisdiction</td><td>Beginner to Advanced</td><td>40 minutes</td></tr></tbody></table></div><h4>Learning objectives</h4><p>By the end of this lesson you should be able to:</p><ul><li>Define universal jurisdiction and distinguish it from other bases of jurisdiction</li><li>Identify the crimes over which it may be exercised and its treaty bases</li><li>Distinguish absolute from conditional universal jurisdiction</li><li>Explain immunities and the practical obstacles</li></ul><h4>1. Introduction</h4><p>Universal jurisdiction is the proposition that some crimes are so grave that any state may try them, whoever committed them and wherever they occurred. It is the only mechanism in international criminal law that does not depend on a link between the crime and the prosecuting state, and it is correspondingly contested.</p><h4>2. The bases of jurisdiction</h4><div class=\"table-wrap\"><table class=\"study-table\"><thead><tr><th>Basis</th><th>Link required</th><th>Example</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Territorial</td><td>The crime occurred on the state's territory</td><td>A theft in Monrovia tried in Liberia</td></tr><tr><td>Active personality</td><td>The perpetrator is a national of the state</td><td>A citizen prosecuted at home for an offence abroad</td></tr><tr><td>Passive personality</td><td>The victim is a national of the state</td><td>Prosecuting the killing of a national abroad</td></tr><tr><td>Protective</td><td>The crime threatens the state's vital interests</td><td>Counterfeiting its currency abroad</td></tr><tr><td>Universal</td><td>None</td><td>Any state may try piracy, torture or war crimes</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"study-defn\"><strong>Universal jurisdiction:</strong> Jurisdiction over certain grave crimes based solely on the nature of the crime, without any link between the crime and the prosecuting state.</div><h4>3. The crimes covered</h4><div class=\"table-wrap\"><table class=\"study-table\"><thead><tr><th>Crime</th><th>Basis</th><th>Nature of the obligation</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Piracy</td><td>Custom</td><td>The historical origin; permissive</td></tr><tr><td>Grave breaches of the Geneva Conventions</td><td>GC I Art 49, GC II Art 50, GC III Art 129, GC IV Art 146</td><td>Mandatory: search for and prosecute or extradite</td></tr><tr><td>Torture</td><td>CAT Arts 5(2) and 7</td><td>Mandatory where the alleged offender is present</td></tr><tr><td>Enforced disappearance</td><td>CED Arts 9 and 11</td><td>Mandatory where present</td></tr><tr><td>Genocide</td><td>Custom; the Convention itself provides only territorial and international jurisdiction</td><td>Permissive in practice; many states have legislated for it</td></tr><tr><td>Crimes against humanity</td><td>Custom</td><td>Permissive; no dedicated convention</td></tr><tr><td>Apartheid</td><td>Apartheid Convention Art IV</td><td>Mandatory for parties</td></tr><tr><td>Slavery, and certain terrorism and aircraft offences</td><td>Various conventions</td><td>Extradite or prosecute regimes</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"study-callout\"><strong>Note:</strong> The distinction between mandatory and permissive matters. For grave breaches and torture, a state party is obliged to act if the suspect is on its territory. For genocide and crimes against humanity, universal jurisdiction is generally permissive under custom, so a state may act but is not required to. Getting this the wrong way round is a common error, because students assume the graver the crime the stronger the duty.</div><h4>4. Absolute and conditional</h4><div class=\"table-wrap\"><table class=\"study-table\"><thead><tr><th></th><th>Absolute (in absentia)</th><th>Conditional (presence-based)</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Requires</td><td>No presence of the accused; proceedings may begin in absentia</td><td>The accused must be present on the territory</td></tr><tr><td>Practised by</td><td>A small number of states, and in a narrowing form</td><td>The great majority</td></tr><tr><td>Rationale</td><td>No safe haven, and investigations can begin before a suspect travels</td><td>Respects sovereignty; avoids trials with no prospect of enforcement</td></tr><tr><td>Criticism</td><td>Risk of politically motivated complaints and diplomatic friction</td><td>Depends on the suspect choosing to travel</td></tr></tbody></table></div><p>Several states that had adopted broad absolute regimes narrowed them after diplomatic disputes, typically by requiring a link such as residence of the suspect or the victim, or by inserting a prosecutorial filter requiring the consent of a law officer before a case proceeds. That trajectory, from broad enactment to narrowing, is the pattern to describe in an answer.</p><h4>5. Immunities</h4><p>Immunity is the largest legal obstacle, and the distinction between its two forms decides most cases.</p><div class=\"table-wrap\"><table class=\"study-table\"><thead><tr><th></th><th>Immunity ratione personae (personal)</th><th>Immunity ratione materiae (functional)</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Attaches to</td><td>The office: serving head of state, head of government, foreign minister</td><td>The act: official acts of state officials</td></tr><tr><td>Duration</td><td>Only while in office</td><td>Continues after leaving office</td></tr><tr><td>Scope</td><td>Absolute, covering official and private acts</td><td>Limited to official acts</td></tr><tr><td>Before national courts</td><td>Applies fully while in office</td><td>Widely held not to bar prosecution for international crimes</td></tr><tr><td>Before international courts</td><td>Rome Statute Art 27 removes it for the ICC</td><td>Removed</td></tr></tbody></table></div><ol><li><strong>Arrest Warrant (DRC v Belgium), ICJ 2002.</strong> A serving foreign minister enjoys full personal immunity from the criminal jurisdiction of foreign national courts, even for international crimes. The Court identified four situations where accountability remains possible: prosecution at home, waiver by the state, prosecution after leaving office for acts committed before or after the term or in a private capacity, and prosecution before an international criminal court with jurisdiction.</li><li><strong>Pinochet, House of Lords 1999.</strong> A former head of state could not claim functional immunity for torture, because torture as defined in the Convention Against Torture is committed by public officials, so treating it as an immune official act would defeat the Convention entirely.</li><li><strong>Rome Statute Article 27.</strong> Official capacity is irrelevant before the ICC and immunities do not bar the Court's jurisdiction. Article 98 nonetheless complicates surrender by states that owe immunity obligations to third states, and the interaction has been repeatedly litigated.</li><li><strong>The International Law Commission</strong> has worked on immunity of state officials from foreign criminal jurisdiction, and its draft articles proposing exceptions for international crimes have been contested by several states.</li></ol><div class=\"study-callout\"><strong>Note:</strong> The clean summary: personal immunity blocks a foreign national prosecution while the person holds office, whoever they are and whatever the charge. Functional immunity does not block prosecution for international crimes, whether before national or international courts. Stating those two sentences accurately answers most immunity questions.</div><h4>6. Practical obstacles</h4><ul><li><strong>Evidence.</strong> Witnesses, documents and crime scenes are in another country, often one that will not cooperate.</li><li><strong>Cost.</strong> Investigations abroad are expensive and compete with domestic priorities.</li><li><strong>Political will.</strong> Prosecutions strain bilateral relations, which is why many systems interpose a prosecutorial or ministerial filter.</li><li><strong>Fair trial.</strong> Distance from the evidence creates real risks for the defence as well as the prosecution.</li><li><strong>Victim and witness protection.</strong> Witnesses who testify may face reprisals at home.</li><li><strong>Selectivity.</strong> If only some states exercise it and only against nationals of weaker states, the practice attracts the same criticism levelled at the ICC.</li></ul><div class=\"study-callout\"><strong>Example:</strong> Structural investigation units offer a partial answer to several of these. Some states have established dedicated war crimes units that build case files on situations before any suspect arrives, gathering testimony from refugee and diaspora communities. When a suspect later enters the jurisdiction, a case already exists. This has produced a growing number of convictions concerning conflicts in other regions, and it is the most significant practical development in the field in recent years.</div><h4>7. How the examiner&#x27;s question should be answered</h4><p>Define universal jurisdiction against the other four bases, since the contrast makes the concept clear. Then separate mandatory from permissive regimes with the treaty citations, because that distinction decides whether a state must act or merely may. Distinguish absolute from conditional and describe the narrowing trend. On immunity, use the two-sentence summary and cite Arrest Warrant and Pinochet. Close with the practical obstacles and the structural investigation response, which shows awareness of current practice rather than only doctrine.</p><h4>Summary</h4><ul><li>Universal jurisdiction rests solely on the nature of the crime, with no link required to the prosecuting state.</li><li>It is mandatory for grave breaches, torture and enforced disappearance, and generally permissive for genocide and crimes against humanity.</li><li>Absolute universal jurisdiction permits proceedings in absentia; most states require the suspect&#x27;s presence.</li><li>Personal immunity bars foreign national prosecution of serving senior officials for any offence; functional immunity does not bar prosecution for international crimes.</li><li>Arrest Warrant identified four routes to accountability that survive personal immunity.</li><li>Structural investigation units building case files in advance are the most significant recent practical development.</li></ul><h4>Key Terms</h4><div class=\"table-wrap\"><table class=\"study-table\"><thead><tr><th>Term</th><th>Meaning</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Universal jurisdiction</td><td>Jurisdiction based solely on the nature of the crime, requiring no link to the prosecuting state.</td></tr><tr><td>Immunity ratione personae</td><td>Immunity attaching to high office, absolute but lasting only while in office.</td></tr><tr><td>Immunity ratione materiae</td><td>Functional immunity for official acts, which does not bar prosecution for international crimes.</td></tr><tr><td>Conditional universal jurisdiction</td><td>Jurisdiction requiring the presence of the accused on the territory.</td></tr><tr><td>Structural investigation</td><td>Building a case file on a situation before any suspect is present in the jurisdiction.</td></tr></tbody></table></div><h4>Review Questions</h4><h5>Multiple choice</h5><div class=\"study-mcq\"><p class=\"mcq-q\">Over which crime is universal jurisdiction mandatory rather than permissive?</p><ol type=\"A\"><li>Grave breaches of the Geneva Conventions</li><li>Genocide</li><li>Crimes against humanity</li><li>Aggression</li></ol><details class=\"mcq-a\"><summary>Show answer</summary><p><strong>Answer: A.</strong> Torture under CAT and enforced disappearance are also mandatory where the suspect is present; genocide and crimes against humanity are generally permissive under custom.</p></details></div><div class=\"study-mcq\"><p class=\"mcq-q\">What did the International Court of Justice hold in Arrest Warrant?</p><ol type=\"A\"><li>A serving foreign minister has full personal immunity from foreign national criminal jurisdiction, even for international crimes</li><li>Functional immunity bars all prosecutions</li><li>Universal jurisdiction does not exist</li><li>Heads of state may never be prosecuted</li></ol><details class=\"mcq-a\"><summary>Show answer</summary><p><strong>Answer: A.</strong> The Court identified four remaining routes: prosecution at home, waiver, prosecution after leaving office, and prosecution before an international court.</p></details></div><div class=\"study-mcq\"><p class=\"mcq-q\">Why did the House of Lords reject functional immunity for torture in Pinochet?</p><ol type=\"A\"><li>Torture under the Convention is committed by public officials, so treating it as an immune official act would defeat the Convention</li><li>Chile had waived immunity</li><li>Pinochet was no longer a national</li><li>Torture is not an official act by definition</li></ol><details class=\"mcq-a\"><summary>Show answer</summary><p><strong>Answer: A.</strong> Functional immunity is widely held not to bar prosecution for international crimes, unlike personal immunity.</p></details></div><div class=\"study-mcq\"><p class=\"mcq-q\">What is a structural investigation unit?</p><ol type=\"A\"><li>A dedicated unit building case files on a situation before any suspect enters the jurisdiction</li><li>A branch of the International Criminal Court</li><li>A treaty body reviewing state reports</li><li>A hybrid court</li></ol><details class=\"mcq-a\"><summary>Show answer</summary><p><strong>Answer: A.</strong> It gathers testimony from refugee and diaspora communities so a case exists if a suspect later arrives.</p></details></div><h5>Short answer</h5><div class=\"study-sa\"><p class=\"sa-q\">Distinguish the two forms of immunity and their effect on universal jurisdiction.</p><details class=\"sa-a\"><summary>Model answer</summary><p>Immunity ratione personae attaches to the office rather than the act, and covers a serving head of state, head of government or foreign minister. It is absolute, extending to official and private acts alike, and it bars prosecution before foreign national courts even for international crimes, as the International Court of Justice held in Arrest Warrant. It ends when the person leaves office. Immunity ratione materiae attaches to official acts and continues after office, but it is widely held not to bar prosecution for international crimes, as the House of Lords found in Pinochet, on the reasoning that torture is by definition committed by officials so treating it as an immune official act would defeat the Convention. Before international courts, Rome Statute Article 27 removes both.</p></details></div><div class=\"study-sa\"><p class=\"sa-q\">Explain the difference between mandatory and permissive universal jurisdiction with examples.</p><details class=\"sa-a\"><summary>Model answer</summary><p>Mandatory universal jurisdiction obliges a state to act. For grave breaches of the Geneva Conventions, each party must search for alleged offenders and either bring them before its own courts or hand them over for trial, regardless of nationality or place of commission. The Convention Against Torture imposes the same duty where the alleged offender is present on the territory, as does the Enforced Disappearance Convention. Permissive universal jurisdiction allows but does not require a state to act. Genocide and crimes against humanity fall here under customary law, since the Genocide Convention itself provides only for territorial and international jurisdiction, though many states have legislated to give their courts universal jurisdiction over both. Students commonly assume the graver the crime the stronger the duty, which is the wrong way round.</p></details></div><h4>Class Activity</h4><ol><li>Find out whether your country&#x27;s law provides for universal jurisdiction, over which crimes, and whether presence of the accused is required. Summarise the position in one paragraph.</li><li>Take a real universal jurisdiction prosecution from any country. Identify how the evidence was obtained and what practical obstacles the prosecution had to overcome.</li></ol><h4>Assignment</h4><p>Write one and a half pages on universal jurisdiction. Define it against the other bases of jurisdiction, identify the crimes it covers and distinguish mandatory from permissive regimes with treaty citations, explain absolute and conditional forms and the narrowing trend, analyse the two forms of immunity with reference to Arrest Warrant and Pinochet, and describe the practical obstacles together with the structural investigation response.</p><p class=\"study-print\"><strong>Printable:</strong> use your browser\u2019s Print \u2192 Save as PDF to keep an offline copy.</p></div>",
  "Command Responsibility": "<div class=\"study-note\"><div class=\"revision-banner\"><strong>Human Rights and International Humanitarian Law</strong><span>Module 11: Accountability and International Criminal Law</span></div><h3 class=\"lesson-note-title\">LESSON NOTE: COMMAND RESPONSIBILITY</h3><div class=\"table-wrap\"><table class=\"study-table\"><thead><tr><th>Subject</th><th>Topic</th><th>Level</th><th>Lesson duration</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Human Rights and International Humanitarian Law</td><td>Command Responsibility</td><td>Beginner to Advanced</td><td>45 minutes</td></tr></tbody></table></div><h4>Learning objectives</h4><p>By the end of this lesson you should be able to:</p><ul><li>State the doctrine of command responsibility and its three elements</li><li>Distinguish it from direct modes of liability</li><li>Explain the differing standards for military and civilian superiors</li><li>Apply the doctrine to a set of facts</li></ul><h4>1. Introduction</h4><p>Most people who order atrocities do not commit them personally, and most who commit them are not the people whose decisions made them possible. Command responsibility is the doctrine that closes that gap. It holds a superior liable not for what they did but for what they failed to prevent or punish.</p><h4>2. The modes of liability</h4><p>Command responsibility is one route among several, and identifying the right one is the first step in any analysis.</p><div class=\"table-wrap\"><table class=\"study-table\"><thead><tr><th>Mode</th><th>Rome Statute</th><th>Content</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Commission</td><td>25(3)(a)</td><td>Committing individually, jointly with another, or through another person</td></tr><tr><td>Ordering, soliciting, inducing</td><td>25(3)(b)</td><td>Directing or persuading another to commit the crime</td></tr><tr><td>Aiding, abetting, assisting</td><td>25(3)(c)</td><td>Providing means or assistance for the purpose of facilitating the crime</td></tr><tr><td>Contributing to a group crime</td><td>25(3)(d)</td><td>Intentional contribution to a crime by a group acting with a common purpose</td></tr><tr><td>Inciting genocide</td><td>25(3)(e)</td><td>Direct and public incitement, genocide only</td></tr><tr><td>Attempt</td><td>25(3)(f)</td><td>Taking a substantial step towards commission</td></tr><tr><td>Command responsibility</td><td>28</td><td>Failure to prevent or punish crimes by subordinates</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"study-callout\"><strong>Note:</strong> Command responsibility is a mode of liability for omission, and it is distinct from ordering. A superior who orders a crime is liable under Article 25(3)(b) as a principal, not under Article 28. Charging a superior under Article 28 when the evidence shows an order is a mistake, and the reverse is a common error in student answers.</div><h4>3. The three elements</h4><p>Article 28 codifies what the ICTY worked out in Celebici. All three elements must be established.</p><ol><li><strong>A superior-subordinate relationship.</strong> The superior must have had effective control over the subordinates, meaning the material ability to prevent or punish their crimes. Formal appointment is neither necessary nor sufficient. What matters is actual authority.</li><li><strong>The mental element.</strong> The superior knew or, in the circumstances at the time, should have known that the subordinates were committing or about to commit the crimes.</li><li><strong>Failure to act.</strong> The superior failed to take all necessary and reasonable measures within their power to prevent or repress the commission of the crimes, or to submit the matter to the competent authorities for investigation and prosecution.</li></ol><div class=\"study-defn\"><strong>Effective control:</strong> The material ability to prevent or punish the criminal conduct of subordinates, which is the decisive test of a superior-subordinate relationship.</div><p>Indicators of effective control include the power to issue orders and to secure compliance, the authority to appoint, promote, discipline or dismiss, the capacity to transmit reports to authorities capable of investigating, the position in the chain of command, and the actual conduct of subordinates in obeying.</p><h4>4. Military and civilian superiors</h4><div class=\"table-wrap\"><table class=\"study-table\"><thead><tr><th></th><th>Military commander, Art 28(a)</th><th>Civilian superior, Art 28(b)</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Who</td><td>A military commander or person effectively acting as one</td><td>Other superior-subordinate relationships</td></tr><tr><td>Knowledge standard</td><td>Knew or, owing to the circumstances at the time, should have known</td><td>Knew, or consciously disregarded information which clearly indicated the crimes</td></tr><tr><td>Scope of activities</td><td>All crimes by forces under effective command and control</td><td>Only crimes concerning activities within the superior's effective responsibility and control</td></tr><tr><td>Duty</td><td>Prevent, repress, or submit to competent authorities</td><td>The same</td></tr></tbody></table></div><p>The civilian standard is deliberately higher and narrower. 'Should have known' imposes an affirmative duty of inquiry on military commanders, reflecting their professional obligation to know what their forces are doing. 'Consciously disregarded information which clearly indicated' requires that information was available and was wilfully ignored, which is closer to recklessness. And the civilian's liability is confined to activities within their effective responsibility, so a minister is not liable for everything done by everyone in their ministry.</p><div class=\"study-callout\"><strong>Note:</strong> Celebici established that the doctrine reaches civilian superiors at all, which was contested. The Rome Statute then split the standard in two. Explaining why the standards differ, rather than merely stating that they do, is what a strong answer does.</div><h4>5. The duty to prevent and the duty to punish</h4><p>These are two separate duties and a superior can breach either independently.</p><div class=\"table-wrap\"><table class=\"study-table\"><thead><tr><th></th><th>Duty to prevent</th><th>Duty to punish</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Arises</td><td>Before or during commission, once the superior knows or should know</td><td>After commission</td></tr><tr><td>Content</td><td>Measures within the superior's material power to stop the crimes: orders, removal of personnel, changed deployment, protest to higher command</td><td>Investigation, disciplinary action, or transmission to competent authorities</td></tr><tr><td>Independence</td><td>Cannot be discharged by punishing afterwards</td><td>Cannot be discharged by having tried to prevent</td></tr></tbody></table></div><p>The point that a superior who fails to prevent cannot make good by punishing afterwards is settled and frequently examined. The two duties are cumulative.</p><ol><li><strong>Necessary and reasonable measures</strong> is judged against the superior's material possibility, not against an abstract ideal. A commander without the power to prosecute discharges the duty by reporting to those who can.</li><li><strong>Causation</strong> is contested. Article 28 requires the crimes to have been committed 'as a result of' the failure to exercise control properly, which suggests a causal link for the duty to prevent. Most commentary holds that no causation is required for the duty to punish, since by definition the crime has already occurred.</li><li><strong>The superior is not liable for the underlying crime as a principal.</strong> Liability is for the failure, though sentencing has generally reflected the gravity of the subordinates' crimes.</li></ol><h4>6. Applying the doctrine</h4><div class=\"study-callout\"><strong>Example:</strong> A battalion commander receives two reports over three weeks that a company under his command has beaten detainees. He takes no action beyond a verbal instruction at a briefing. In the fourth week the company kills six detainees. Apply the elements. Effective control is established by his command position and the material ability to redeploy, discipline and report the company. The mental element is satisfied at the higher level: he actually knew of the earlier beatings, and on the 'should have known' standard the reports plainly required inquiry. On failure, a verbal instruction is not, on these facts, all necessary and reasonable measures: he could have removed the company commander, ordered an investigation, or reported to higher command, and did none of those. He is liable under Article 28(a) for failing to prevent the killings, and separately for failing to punish the beatings. Note what the analysis does not need: no order, no presence, no knowledge of the specific killings in advance.</div><h4>7. How the examiner&#x27;s question should be answered</h4><p>Identify the mode of liability first and say why command responsibility rather than ordering. Then take the three elements in order and reach a conclusion on each, giving the effective control indicators rather than asserting the relationship. Say whether the superior is military or civilian and apply the correct knowledge standard, quoting the wording. Treat prevention and punishment as separate duties and analyse each. Finish by stating that liability is for the failure and noting the causation question, since that shows command of the doctrine's structure rather than its outline.</p><h4>Summary</h4><ul><li>Command responsibility holds a superior liable by omission for failing to prevent or punish crimes by subordinates.</li><li>It is distinct from ordering, which is principal liability under Article 25(3)(b).</li><li>The three elements are effective control, the requisite knowledge, and failure to take all necessary and reasonable measures.</li><li>Effective control means the material ability to prevent or punish, and formal appointment is neither necessary nor sufficient.</li><li>Military commanders are judged on &#x27;knew or should have known&#x27;; civilian superiors on conscious disregard of clear information.</li><li>The duties to prevent and to punish are separate and cumulative, and punishing afterwards does not cure a failure to prevent.</li></ul><h4>Key Terms</h4><div class=\"table-wrap\"><table class=\"study-table\"><thead><tr><th>Term</th><th>Meaning</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Command responsibility</td><td>Liability of a superior for failing to prevent or punish crimes committed by subordinates.</td></tr><tr><td>Effective control</td><td>The material ability to prevent or punish subordinates&#x27; criminal conduct.</td></tr><tr><td>Should have known</td><td>The military commander&#x27;s standard, imposing an affirmative duty of inquiry.</td></tr><tr><td>Conscious disregard</td><td>The civilian superior&#x27;s standard, requiring wilful ignoring of information clearly indicating crimes.</td></tr><tr><td>Necessary and reasonable measures</td><td>Steps within the superior&#x27;s material power to prevent, repress or report the crimes.</td></tr></tbody></table></div><h4>Review Questions</h4><h5>Multiple choice</h5><div class=\"study-mcq\"><p class=\"mcq-q\">A general orders subordinates to shoot prisoners. Under which provision is he liable?</p><ol type=\"A\"><li>Article 25(3)(b) for ordering, as a principal</li><li>Article 28 for command responsibility</li><li>Article 25(3)(c) for aiding and abetting</li><li>Article 28(b) as a civilian superior</li></ol><details class=\"mcq-a\"><summary>Show answer</summary><p><strong>Answer: A.</strong> Command responsibility is a mode of liability for omission and is distinct from giving an order.</p></details></div><div class=\"study-mcq\"><p class=\"mcq-q\">What is the decisive test of a superior-subordinate relationship?</p><ol type=\"A\"><li>Effective control, meaning the material ability to prevent or punish</li><li>Formal rank</li><li>A written appointment</li><li>Physical presence at the scene</li></ol><details class=\"mcq-a\"><summary>Show answer</summary><p><strong>Answer: A.</strong> Formal appointment is neither necessary nor sufficient; actual authority is what matters.</p></details></div><div class=\"study-mcq\"><p class=\"mcq-q\">How does the civilian superior&#x27;s knowledge standard differ from the military one?</p><ol type=\"A\"><li>Civilians must have consciously disregarded information clearly indicating the crimes, which is narrower than &#x27;should have known&#x27;</li><li>It is identical</li><li>Civilians are held to a lower threshold of proof</li><li>Civilians have no liability at all</li></ol><details class=\"mcq-a\"><summary>Show answer</summary><p><strong>Answer: A.</strong> The military standard imposes an affirmative duty of inquiry reflecting a commander&#x27;s professional obligation to know what their forces are doing.</p></details></div><div class=\"study-mcq\"><p class=\"mcq-q\">A commander fails to prevent crimes but punishes the perpetrators afterwards. What follows?</p><ol type=\"A\"><li>He remains liable for the failure to prevent; the duties are separate and cumulative</li><li>He is fully exonerated</li><li>He is liable only for the punishment failure</li><li>Liability depends on the sentence imposed</li></ol><details class=\"mcq-a\"><summary>Show answer</summary><p><strong>Answer: A.</strong> The duty to prevent arises before or during commission and cannot be discharged retrospectively.</p></details></div><h5>Short answer</h5><div class=\"study-sa\"><p class=\"sa-q\">State the three elements of command responsibility.</p><details class=\"sa-a\"><summary>Model answer</summary><p>First, a superior-subordinate relationship, established by effective control, meaning the superior had the material ability to prevent or punish the subordinates&#x27; criminal conduct; formal appointment is neither necessary nor sufficient, and indicators include the power to issue orders and secure compliance, authority to appoint, discipline or dismiss, capacity to report to investigating authorities, and position in the chain of command. Second, the mental element: for a military commander, that they knew or, owing to the circumstances at the time, should have known the crimes were being or were about to be committed; for a civilian superior, that they knew or consciously disregarded information clearly indicating them. Third, failure to take all necessary and reasonable measures within their power to prevent or repress the crimes or to submit the matter to the competent authorities.</p></details></div><div class=\"study-sa\"><p class=\"sa-q\">Explain why the duties to prevent and to punish are treated as separate.</p><details class=\"sa-a\"><summary>Model answer</summary><p>The duty to prevent arises once the superior knows or should know that crimes are being or are about to be committed, and requires measures within their material power to stop them, such as issuing orders, redeploying or removing personnel, or protesting to higher command. The duty to punish arises after commission and requires investigation, disciplinary action, or transmission of the matter to authorities competent to investigate and prosecute. They are cumulative rather than alternative, so a superior who failed to prevent cannot make good that failure by punishing the perpetrators afterwards, and equally a superior who tried to prevent but then took no action against those responsible breaches the second duty. Where a superior lacks the power to prosecute, reporting to those who have it discharges the duty.</p></details></div><h4>Class Activity</h4><ol><li>Take a documented incident involving subordinates. Work through the three elements and identify what evidence would establish effective control.</li><li>Draft a short checklist a commander could use on receiving a report of misconduct by their unit, designed to discharge both the duty to prevent and the duty to punish.</li></ol><h4>Assignment</h4><p>Write two pages on command responsibility. Distinguish it from the other modes of liability and explain why it is a doctrine of omission, set out the three elements with the indicators of effective control, compare the knowledge standards for military commanders and civilian superiors and explain why they differ, analyse the duties to prevent and to punish as separate and cumulative obligations, address the causation question, and apply the doctrine to a worked set of facts.</p><p class=\"study-print\"><strong>Printable:</strong> use your browser\u2019s Print \u2192 Save as PDF to keep an offline copy.</p></div>",
  "Monitoring and Documentation": "<div class=\"study-note\"><div class=\"revision-banner\"><strong>Human Rights and International Humanitarian Law</strong><span>Module 12: Human Rights in Practice</span></div><h3 class=\"lesson-note-title\">LESSON NOTE: MONITORING AND DOCUMENTATION</h3><div class=\"table-wrap\"><table class=\"study-table\"><thead><tr><th>Subject</th><th>Topic</th><th>Level</th><th>Lesson duration</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Human Rights and International Humanitarian Law</td><td>Monitoring and Documentation</td><td>Beginner to Advanced</td><td>45 minutes</td></tr></tbody></table></div><h4>Learning objectives</h4><p>By the end of this lesson you should be able to:</p><ul><li>Distinguish monitoring, documentation and investigation</li><li>Explain the standards of proof used in human rights fact-finding</li><li>Describe the documentation cycle and the rules for handling evidence</li><li>Apply the do no harm principle to a monitoring decision</li></ul><h4>1. Introduction</h4><p>Everything in the preceding modules depends on facts. A treaty body cannot find a violation, a court cannot convict and an advocate cannot persuade without a record of what happened, who did it and how it is known. Documentation is the least glamorous part of this field and the part on which all the rest rests.</p><h4>2. Three activities, often confused</h4><div class=\"table-wrap\"><table class=\"study-table\"><thead><tr><th>Activity</th><th>Purpose</th><th>Output</th><th>Standard</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Monitoring</td><td>Observing a situation over time to identify patterns and changes</td><td>Periodic reports, trend analysis</td><td>Systematic observation</td></tr><tr><td>Documentation</td><td>Recording specific incidents with supporting material</td><td>Case files, incident records, databases</td><td>Verified accounts</td></tr><tr><td>Investigation</td><td>Establishing what happened in a defined incident, including responsibility</td><td>Findings, attribution</td><td>A stated standard of proof</td></tr></tbody></table></div><p>Monitoring is continuous and broad; documentation is incident-specific; investigation is targeted and reaches conclusions about responsibility. A human rights organisation typically does all three, but confusing them produces reports that observe without concluding, or conclude without evidence.</p><h4>3. Standards of proof</h4><p>Human rights fact-finding is not a criminal trial and should not pretend to be. Naming the standard applied is essential to credibility.</p><div class=\"table-wrap\"><table class=\"study-table\"><thead><tr><th>Standard</th><th>Meaning</th><th>Typical use</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Reasonable grounds to suspect</td><td>A reasonable person would suspect the incident occurred</td><td>Early warning, urgent appeals</td></tr><tr><td>Reasonable grounds to believe</td><td>A reliable body of material consistent with other verified circumstances, on which a reasonable person could conclude the incident occurred</td><td>Most commissions of inquiry and fact-finding missions</td></tr><tr><td>Balance of probabilities</td><td>More likely than not</td><td>Some national inquiries and civil claims</td></tr><tr><td>Clear and convincing evidence</td><td>Substantially more probable than not</td><td>Some treaty body and regional court findings</td></tr><tr><td>Beyond reasonable doubt</td><td>The criminal standard</td><td>Criminal trials only</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"study-callout\"><strong>Note:</strong> Most United Nations commissions of inquiry apply 'reasonable grounds to believe'. This is deliberately lower than the criminal standard because a commission is not determining guilt; it is establishing whether there is a sufficient basis to report a violation and to warrant further investigation. A report that claims criminal certainty on inquiry evidence damages its own credibility and can prejudice later prosecutions.</div><h4>4. The documentation cycle</h4><ol><li><strong>Plan.</strong> Define the scope, the legal framework to be applied, the information needed and the risks.</li><li><strong>Collect.</strong> Testimony, documents, physical evidence, photographs and video, satellite imagery, medical and forensic records, official records.</li><li><strong>Verify.</strong> Corroborate each material fact from independent sources; assess source reliability and information credibility separately.</li><li><strong>Analyse.</strong> Apply the legal framework: identify the right or rule engaged, the conduct, the attribution and the pattern.</li><li><strong>Record.</strong> Store securely with a consistent structure, so a fact can be traced to its source years later.</li><li><strong>Report.</strong> Present findings with the standard of proof stated, distinguishing what is established from what is alleged.</li><li><strong>Follow up.</strong> Track what the authorities do, since the response is itself evidence.</li></ol><h4>5. Handling evidence</h4><div class=\"table-wrap\"><table class=\"study-table\"><thead><tr><th>Rule</th><th>Why</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Record the source of every fact</td><td>A fact whose source cannot be traced cannot be defended when challenged</td></tr><tr><td>Separate source reliability from information credibility</td><td>A generally reliable source can be wrong about a particular fact, and an unreliable source can be right</td></tr><tr><td>Corroborate independently</td><td>Two accounts derived from the same original source are one source, not two</td></tr><tr><td>Preserve originals</td><td>Work from copies; never annotate an original document or photograph</td></tr><tr><td>Maintain a chain of custody</td><td>Record who handled each item, when and why</td></tr><tr><td>Retain metadata</td><td>Photograph and video metadata establishes time and place; stripping it destroys evidential value</td></tr><tr><td>Distinguish observation from inference</td><td>Write what was seen separately from what it is thought to mean</td></tr><tr><td>Date everything</td><td>Including the date of recording, not only the date of the incident</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"study-defn\"><strong>Chain of custody:</strong> The documented sequence of persons who have held an item of evidence, establishing that it has not been altered or substituted.</div><div class=\"study-callout\"><strong>Note:</strong> A common failure is collecting powerful material that cannot later be used because its provenance was not recorded. A photograph of an atrocity with no date, no location, no photographer and no metadata proves nothing, however shocking it is. Provenance is what converts material into evidence.</div><h4>6. Do no harm</h4><div class=\"study-defn\"><strong>Do no harm:</strong> The principle that documentation must not expose victims, witnesses, communities or staff to greater risk than the documentation is worth.</div><ol><li><strong>Assess risk before collecting.</strong> Ask who is endangered if this material is found, published or leaked.</li><li><strong>Obtain informed consent</strong> covering what will be recorded, how it will be used, who will see it, and what identifying details will be published.</li><li><strong>Allow withdrawal.</strong> Consent given today can be withdrawn later, and the record must make that possible.</li><li><strong>Minimise identifying detail</strong> in anything published, and consider whether a combination of non-identifying details identifies a person in a small community.</li><li><strong>Secure the data.</strong> Encryption, access control, and a plan for what happens if a device is seized.</li><li><strong>Do not create expectations you cannot meet.</strong> Documentation rarely produces a remedy quickly, and saying so honestly is part of consent.</li><li><strong>Consider the effect on the person of retelling.</strong> Repeated interviews by successive organisations are a recognised harm.</li></ol><div class=\"study-callout\"><strong>Example:</strong> A team documents forced evictions in a rural district. The material is strong, but the community is small and the three families willing to speak are readily identifiable from the details that make the account compelling: the location of their land, the crops destroyed and the dates. Publishing as collected would expose them. The options are to aggregate the accounts so no family is identifiable, to delay publication until the families can relocate, to publish with the families' informed agreement having explained the specific risk, or to pass the material confidentially to a mechanism that can act without publication. Choosing among those is a do no harm judgement, and it belongs to the families as much as to the organisation.</div><h4>7. Digital and open source material</h4><ul><li>Open source investigation using satellite imagery, social media video and flight tracking has become central, particularly where physical access is denied.</li><li>Verification requires establishing the date, the location and the authenticity of the material. Geolocation matches visible features to mapped terrain; chronolocation uses shadows, weather records and known events.</li><li>Content must be preserved immediately, since platforms remove material that documents violations, sometimes as part of automated moderation.</li><li>The Berkeley Protocol on Digital Open Source Investigations sets out professional standards for this work and is the reference to cite.</li><li>Open source material corroborates and directs; it rarely establishes responsibility on its own, and it must be integrated with testimony rather than replacing it.</li></ul><h4>8. How the examiner&#x27;s question should be answered</h4><p>Distinguish the three activities before anything else, since a question about monitoring often expects documentation and investigation to be separated from it. Always name the standard of proof you are applying and explain why it is appropriate for the body in question. Give the documentation cycle as a sequence rather than a list of activities. On evidence, the point that earns marks is provenance: explain that material without a traceable source is not evidence. Treat do no harm as a decision-making framework applied to specific facts, not as a slogan.</p><h4>Summary</h4><ul><li>Monitoring observes patterns over time, documentation records specific incidents, and investigation establishes responsibility.</li><li>Most commissions of inquiry apply &#x27;reasonable grounds to believe&#x27;, which is deliberately lower than the criminal standard.</li><li>The documentation cycle runs from planning through collection, verification, analysis, recording, reporting and follow-up.</li><li>Source reliability and information credibility must be assessed separately, and independent corroboration means genuinely independent sources.</li><li>Provenance converts material into evidence; material with no traceable source proves nothing however striking it is.</li><li>Do no harm requires risk assessment, informed consent that can be withdrawn, data security and honest expectations.</li></ul><h4>Key Terms</h4><div class=\"table-wrap\"><table class=\"study-table\"><thead><tr><th>Term</th><th>Meaning</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Reasonable grounds to believe</td><td>The standard used by most commissions of inquiry, requiring a reliable body of material consistent with other verified circumstances.</td></tr><tr><td>Chain of custody</td><td>The documented sequence of persons who have held an item of evidence.</td></tr><tr><td>Do no harm</td><td>The principle that documentation must not expose people to risk disproportionate to its value.</td></tr><tr><td>Provenance</td><td>The traceable origin of a piece of material, without which it is not evidence.</td></tr><tr><td>Berkeley Protocol</td><td>The professional standard for digital open source investigations.</td></tr></tbody></table></div><h4>Review Questions</h4><h5>Multiple choice</h5><div class=\"study-mcq\"><p class=\"mcq-q\">What standard of proof do most United Nations commissions of inquiry apply?</p><ol type=\"A\"><li>Reasonable grounds to believe</li><li>Beyond reasonable doubt</li><li>Balance of probabilities</li><li>Absolute certainty</li></ol><details class=\"mcq-a\"><summary>Show answer</summary><p><strong>Answer: A.</strong> A commission is not determining guilt, so claiming criminal certainty damages credibility and can prejudice later prosecutions.</p></details></div><div class=\"study-mcq\"><p class=\"mcq-q\">Two witnesses give matching accounts, both having heard it from the same person. How many sources is this?</p><ol type=\"A\"><li>One, because the accounts derive from a single original source</li><li>Two independent sources</li><li>Three, counting the original</li><li>It depends on their reliability</li></ol><details class=\"mcq-a\"><summary>Show answer</summary><p><strong>Answer: A.</strong> Genuine corroboration requires sources that are independent of one another, not repetition of the same original account.</p></details></div><div class=\"study-mcq\"><p class=\"mcq-q\">Why does provenance matter so much in documentation?</p><ol type=\"A\"><li>Material whose source, date and location cannot be traced is not usable as evidence, however striking it is</li><li>It determines the standard of proof</li><li>It establishes the legal framework</li><li>It is required by treaty</li></ol><details class=\"mcq-a\"><summary>Show answer</summary><p><strong>Answer: A.</strong> Recording the source of every fact is what allows a finding to be defended when it is challenged.</p></details></div><div class=\"study-mcq\"><p class=\"mcq-q\">Which is required by the do no harm principle?</p><ol type=\"A\"><li>Informed consent covering use and publication, which the person may later withdraw</li><li>Publishing all material collected</li><li>Anonymous collection only</li><li>Avoiding all interviews with victims</li></ol><details class=\"mcq-a\"><summary>Show answer</summary><p><strong>Answer: A.</strong> It also requires risk assessment before collection, minimising identifying detail, securing data and not creating expectations that cannot be met.</p></details></div><h5>Short answer</h5><div class=\"study-sa\"><p class=\"sa-q\">Distinguish monitoring, documentation and investigation.</p><details class=\"sa-a\"><summary>Model answer</summary><p>Monitoring is the continuous observation of a situation over time in order to identify patterns, trends and changes, and its output is periodic reporting. Documentation is incident-specific: it records particular events with supporting material, producing case files and structured records that can be retrieved and relied on later. Investigation is targeted at a defined incident and goes further than both, seeking to establish what happened and who was responsible, and it must state the standard of proof it applies. Organisations typically do all three, but conflating them produces reports that observe without reaching conclusions, or that assert conclusions the evidence does not support.</p></details></div><div class=\"study-sa\"><p class=\"sa-q\">Set out six rules for handling evidence.</p><details class=\"sa-a\"><summary>Model answer</summary><p>Record the source of every fact, since an untraceable fact cannot be defended when challenged. Assess the reliability of the source separately from the credibility of the particular information, because a reliable source can be wrong and an unreliable one right. Corroborate from genuinely independent sources, remembering that two accounts derived from one original are a single source. Preserve originals and work only from copies, never annotating the original. Maintain a chain of custody recording who handled each item, when and why. Retain metadata on photographs and video, since it establishes time and place and stripping it destroys evidential value. A seventh rule is to record observation separately from inference.</p></details></div><h4>Class Activity</h4><ol><li>Take a recent incident reported locally. Draft a one-page documentation record covering source, date, location, account, corroboration and the standard of proof you would apply.</li><li>Write an informed consent script for interviewing a witness, covering what is recorded, how it will be used, who will see it, and how consent can be withdrawn.</li></ol><h4>Assignment</h4><p>Write two pages on monitoring and documentation. Distinguish the three activities, set out the standards of proof and explain which is appropriate for a commission of inquiry and why, describe the documentation cycle in sequence, give at least six rules for handling evidence and explain why provenance is decisive, apply the do no harm principle to a concrete scenario, and describe the role and limits of digital open source material.</p><p class=\"study-print\"><strong>Printable:</strong> use your browser\u2019s Print \u2192 Save as PDF to keep an offline copy.</p></div>",
  "Interviewing Victims and Witnesses Safely": "<div class=\"study-note\"><div class=\"revision-banner\"><strong>Human Rights and International Humanitarian Law</strong><span>Module 12: Human Rights in Practice</span></div><h3 class=\"lesson-note-title\">LESSON NOTE: INTERVIEWING VICTIMS AND WITNESSES SAFELY</h3><div class=\"table-wrap\"><table class=\"study-table\"><thead><tr><th>Subject</th><th>Topic</th><th>Level</th><th>Lesson duration</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Human Rights and International Humanitarian Law</td><td>Interviewing Victims and Witnesses Safely</td><td>Beginner to Advanced</td><td>45 minutes</td></tr></tbody></table></div><h4>Learning objectives</h4><p>By the end of this lesson you should be able to:</p><ul><li>Explain trauma-informed interviewing and why it produces better evidence</li><li>Plan and structure an interview</li><li>Apply informed consent, confidentiality and referral obligations</li><li>Identify and avoid the common interviewing errors</li></ul><h4>1. Introduction</h4><p>An interview is both an evidence-gathering exercise and an encounter with a person who has been harmed. Those two purposes are usually assumed to be in tension. They are not: the techniques that protect the interviewee also produce more accurate and more complete accounts, and the techniques that harm them also corrupt the evidence.</p><h4>2. Why trauma-informed practice produces better evidence</h4><div class=\"table-wrap\"><table class=\"study-table\"><thead><tr><th>Effect of trauma</th><th>Consequence for the account</th><th>What the interviewer should do</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Fragmented encoding of memory</td><td>The account is out of order and incomplete</td><td>Do not treat disorder as inconsistency; allow free recall before questions</td></tr><tr><td>Vivid sensory detail with poor chronology</td><td>Strong recall of sounds and smells, weak recall of times</td><td>Anchor time to external events rather than clock times</td></tr><tr><td>Avoidance</td><td>The person skips the worst part</td><td>Signal that they control the pace; return to it later if at all</td></tr><tr><td>Hyperarousal</td><td>Difficulty concentrating; startle responses</td><td>Control the environment; keep sessions short</td></tr><tr><td>Dissociation</td><td>The person appears flat or detached</td><td>Do not read absence of emotion as fabrication</td></tr><tr><td>Shame</td><td>Omission of details felt to be humiliating</td><td>Normalise; never express surprise or judgement</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"study-callout\"><strong>Note:</strong> The most consequential misconception in this field is that a distressed or inconsistent account is a false one. Research on traumatic memory shows the opposite: fragmentation, non-linear recall and later recovery of detail are characteristic of genuine traumatic experience. An interviewer who treats them as indicators of lying will discard true accounts and will retraumatise the person.</div><h4>3. Planning</h4><ol><li><strong>Know why you are interviewing.</strong> Identify the specific information needed and the legal framework it serves; an interview with no purpose harms the person for nothing.</li><li><strong>Check whether the person has already been interviewed.</strong> Repeated interviewing by successive organisations is a recognised harm; consider using an existing account.</li><li><strong>Choose the setting.</strong> Private, safe to reach and to leave, free from interruption, with the interviewee seated nearest the door.</li><li><strong>Choose the interviewer.</strong> Consider the interviewee's stated preference on the sex of the interviewer, particularly in cases involving sexual violence.</li><li><strong>Arrange interpretation properly.</strong> A professional interpreter, briefed in advance, never a family member, a community leader or a child.</li><li><strong>Prepare referral information</strong> for medical, psychosocial, legal and protection services before you begin.</li><li><strong>Assess security</strong> for the interviewee, for yourself and for the record.</li></ol><h4>4. Structure of the interview</h4><div class=\"table-wrap\"><table class=\"study-table\"><thead><tr><th>Phase</th><th>Purpose</th><th>Technique</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Introduction</td><td>Establish who you are and what will happen</td><td>Explain your organisation, the purpose, the limits of what you can do</td></tr><tr><td>Informed consent</td><td>Secure a real decision</td><td>Cover recording, use, publication, identification, storage, withdrawal</td></tr><tr><td>Rapport</td><td>Reduce anxiety and establish control</td><td>Neutral topics; explain the person may pause, decline or stop</td></tr><tr><td>Free recall</td><td>Obtain an uncontaminated account</td><td>One open invitation, then listen without interrupting</td></tr><tr><td>Probing</td><td>Fill gaps</td><td>Open questions first, funnelling to specific ones only as needed</td></tr><tr><td>Clarification</td><td>Resolve ambiguity</td><td>Summarise back and invite correction</td></tr><tr><td>Closure</td><td>Return the person to the present</td><td>Neutral topic, referrals, what happens next, contact details</td></tr></tbody></table></div><p>The free recall phase is where most interviews are lost. An interviewer who interrupts to clarify in the first minutes replaces the interviewee's memory structure with their own, and everything afterwards is shaped by the interviewer's assumptions.</p><div class=\"table-wrap\"><table class=\"study-table\"><thead><tr><th>Question type</th><th>Example</th><th>Use</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Open</td><td>Tell me what happened that morning</td><td>Primary; produces the most reliable material</td></tr><tr><td>Probing open</td><td>You mentioned a vehicle. Tell me about the vehicle</td><td>Secondary; expands without directing</td></tr><tr><td>Closed specific</td><td>What colour was the vehicle</td><td>Only after open questions are exhausted</td></tr><tr><td>Leading</td><td>The vehicle was green, wasn't it</td><td>Never; it contaminates the account</td></tr><tr><td>Multiple</td><td>Who was there, what were they wearing and when did they arrive</td><td>Never; confuses and produces partial answers</td></tr></tbody></table></div><h4>5. Informed consent and confidentiality</h4><ol><li><strong>Consent must be informed:</strong> the person must understand what will be recorded, how it will be used, who will have access, whether they will be identifiable, how long it will be kept, and what the realistic outcomes are.</li><li><strong>Consent must be voluntary:</strong> free from pressure, from any suggestion that assistance depends on it, and from the presence of anyone whose attendance constrains the person.</li><li><strong>Consent is revocable:</strong> the person may withdraw at any point, and the record must be structured so that withdrawal can be given effect.</li><li><strong>Confidentiality must be honest.</strong> Never promise absolute confidentiality you cannot deliver. Explain any circumstances in which you would have to share information, such as an immediate risk to a child.</li><li><strong>Children require additional safeguards:</strong> the consent of a parent or guardian where that is safe and appropriate, the child's own assent, an interviewer trained for the purpose, and adaptation to the child's age and maturity.</li></ol><div class=\"study-defn\"><strong>Informed consent:</strong> A voluntary and revocable agreement given with a genuine understanding of what will be recorded, how it will be used, who will see it and what the realistic consequences are.</div><h4>6. Common errors</h4><div class=\"table-wrap\"><table class=\"study-table\"><thead><tr><th>Error</th><th>Why it damages the evidence</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Interrupting free recall</td><td>Substitutes the interviewer's structure for the witness's memory</td></tr><tr><td>Leading questions</td><td>Introduces detail the person then adopts, contaminating the account irreversibly</td></tr><tr><td>Expressing shock, sympathy or judgement</td><td>Shapes what the person chooses to say next</td></tr><tr><td>Treating inconsistency as lying</td><td>Discards genuine traumatic accounts</td></tr><tr><td>Pressing for detail the person is avoiding</td><td>Retraumatises and produces unreliable material</td></tr><tr><td>Promising outcomes</td><td>Destroys trust when the outcome does not arrive, and taints later accounts</td></tr><tr><td>Interviewing in a group</td><td>Accounts converge; independent corroboration becomes impossible</td></tr><tr><td>Using an untrained or interested interpreter</td><td>Summarising, censoring and distortion, none of it visible in the record</td></tr><tr><td>Failing to record the interview's own details</td><td>Undermines provenance</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"study-callout\"><strong>Example:</strong> An interviewer asks: 'So the soldiers in green uniforms came at about 6am and burned the houses?' Three errors are compressed into one sentence. It is leading, supplying the uniform colour, the time and the act. It is multiple, so a single yes cannot be attributed to any element. And it invites confirmation rather than recall. The correct opening is: 'Tell me, in your own words, what happened.' Then, much later: 'You mentioned the men who came. Describe them.' The difference is not politeness; it is the difference between evidence and a contaminated statement.</div><h4>7. After the interview</h4><ul><li>Provide the referral information you prepared, and follow up if you undertook to.</li><li>Record the interview's own metadata: date, time, place, who was present, language, interpreter, duration, and the consent given.</li><li>Store securely, separating identifying information from the account where possible.</li><li>Do not re-interview without a clear new purpose.</li><li>Attend to your own exposure. Vicarious trauma is a documented occupational risk, and organisations that ignore it lose staff and produce worse work.</li></ul><h4>8. How the examiner&#x27;s question should be answered</h4><p>Lead with the point that trauma-informed practice and good evidence are the same thing, not competing goals, since that framing is what the question is testing. Give the interview structure as a sequence of phases and explain what each achieves. Rank the question types and state plainly that free recall must be uninterrupted. Treat consent as four requirements, informed, voluntary, revocable and honest about confidentiality. Finish with the errors, explaining for each why it damages the evidence rather than only why it is unkind.</p><h4>Summary</h4><ul><li>Trauma-informed interviewing produces better evidence, because the techniques that protect the person also protect the account.</li><li>Fragmented, non-linear and later-recovered recall is characteristic of genuine traumatic memory, not of lying.</li><li>The interview runs through introduction, consent, rapport, free recall, probing, clarification and closure.</li><li>Free recall must be uninterrupted; open questions come first and leading and multiple questions are never used.</li><li>Consent must be informed, voluntary and revocable, and confidentiality must never be promised beyond what can be delivered.</li><li>Interviewing in groups destroys independent corroboration, and untrained interpreters distort the record invisibly.</li></ul><h4>Key Terms</h4><div class=\"table-wrap\"><table class=\"study-table\"><thead><tr><th>Term</th><th>Meaning</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Trauma-informed interviewing</td><td>An approach accounting for the effects of trauma on memory and behaviour, which improves both safety and accuracy.</td></tr><tr><td>Free recall</td><td>The uninterrupted account given in the person&#x27;s own words and structure before any questioning.</td></tr><tr><td>Leading question</td><td>A question supplying the answer, which contaminates the account irreversibly.</td></tr><tr><td>Vicarious trauma</td><td>The occupational psychological harm arising from repeated exposure to others&#x27; traumatic accounts.</td></tr><tr><td>Assent</td><td>A child&#x27;s own agreement to participate, required alongside guardian consent where appropriate.</td></tr></tbody></table></div><h4>Review Questions</h4><h5>Multiple choice</h5><div class=\"study-mcq\"><p class=\"mcq-q\">A witness gives an account that is out of order and gains detail on retelling. What does this indicate?</p><ol type=\"A\"><li>Characteristics consistent with genuine traumatic memory</li><li>Deliberate fabrication</li><li>Coaching by another party</li><li>That the account should be discarded</li></ol><details class=\"mcq-a\"><summary>Show answer</summary><p><strong>Answer: A.</strong> Treating fragmentation and non-linear recall as indicators of lying discards true accounts and retraumatises the person.</p></details></div><div class=\"study-mcq\"><p class=\"mcq-q\">What must happen before any questioning begins?</p><ol type=\"A\"><li>An uninterrupted free recall account in the person&#x27;s own words</li><li>A list of closed questions</li><li>Corroboration from another witness</li><li>A written statement</li></ol><details class=\"mcq-a\"><summary>Show answer</summary><p><strong>Answer: A.</strong> Interrupting free recall substitutes the interviewer&#x27;s structure for the witness&#x27;s memory and shapes everything that follows.</p></details></div><div class=\"study-mcq\"><p class=\"mcq-q\">Why should an interpreter never be a family member or community leader?</p><ol type=\"A\"><li>They summarise, censor and distort in ways invisible in the record, and their presence constrains the interviewee</li><li>They cannot be paid</li><li>They are not fluent enough</li><li>It breaches data protection law</li></ol><details class=\"mcq-a\"><summary>Show answer</summary><p><strong>Answer: A.</strong> A professional interpreter briefed in advance is required, and a child must never be used.</p></details></div><div class=\"study-mcq\"><p class=\"mcq-q\">What is wrong with promising absolute confidentiality?</p><ol type=\"A\"><li>It cannot always be delivered, and a broken promise destroys trust and taints later accounts</li><li>It is prohibited by treaty</li><li>It slows the interview</li><li>It prevents publication</li></ol><details class=\"mcq-a\"><summary>Show answer</summary><p><strong>Answer: A.</strong> Any circumstances in which information would have to be shared must be explained before consent is taken.</p></details></div><h5>Short answer</h5><div class=\"study-sa\"><p class=\"sa-q\">Describe the seven phases of a well-structured interview.</p><details class=\"sa-a\"><summary>Model answer</summary><p>Introduction establishes who the interviewer is, the purpose, and honestly what the organisation can and cannot do. Informed consent covers recording, use, publication, identification, storage and withdrawal. Rapport reduces anxiety and establishes that the interviewee controls the pace and may pause, decline or stop. Free recall obtains an uncontaminated account through a single open invitation followed by uninterrupted listening. Probing fills gaps using open questions first and funnelling to specific ones only when open questions are exhausted. Clarification summarises the account back and invites correction. Closure returns the person to the present with a neutral topic, provides referral information, explains what happens next and gives contact details.</p></details></div><div class=\"study-sa\"><p class=\"sa-q\">Explain why trauma-informed practice improves the quality of evidence.</p><details class=\"sa-a\"><summary>Model answer</summary><p>Trauma affects how memory is encoded and retrieved: recall is fragmented and non-linear, sensory detail is vivid while chronology is weak, avoidance causes omission of the worst events, and shame suppresses humiliating details. An interviewer who does not understand this will interrupt to impose order, press on avoided material, read flat affect as fabrication and treat inconsistency as lying. Each of those responses damages the evidence as well as the person: interruption substitutes the interviewer&#x27;s structure for the witness&#x27;s memory, pressure produces unreliable material, and misreading trauma responses causes true accounts to be discarded. Conversely, allowing uninterrupted free recall, anchoring time to external events, controlling the environment and avoiding judgement produce fuller and more accurate accounts. Protection and accuracy point the same way.</p></details></div><h4>Class Activity</h4><ol><li>In pairs, conduct a practice interview on a neutral difficult experience. The observer records every question asked and classifies it as open, probing, closed, leading or multiple, then feeds back the proportions.</li><li>Write an interview plan for a real documentation task: purpose, setting, interviewer, interpreter arrangements, consent script, referral list and security assessment.</li></ol><h4>Assignment</h4><p>Write two pages on interviewing victims and witnesses safely. Explain how trauma affects memory and why trauma-informed practice produces better evidence, set out the planning steps and the seven phases of an interview, rank the question types and explain why free recall must be uninterrupted, set out the four requirements of consent and the additional safeguards for children, list the common errors explaining for each why it damages the evidence, and describe post-interview obligations including attention to vicarious trauma.</p><p class=\"study-print\"><strong>Printable:</strong> use your browser\u2019s Print \u2192 Save as PDF to keep an offline copy.</p></div>",
  "Report Writing and Advocacy": "<div class=\"study-note\"><div class=\"revision-banner\"><strong>Human Rights and International Humanitarian Law</strong><span>Module 12: Human Rights in Practice</span></div><h3 class=\"lesson-note-title\">LESSON NOTE: REPORT WRITING AND ADVOCACY</h3><div class=\"table-wrap\"><table class=\"study-table\"><thead><tr><th>Subject</th><th>Topic</th><th>Level</th><th>Lesson duration</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Human Rights and International Humanitarian Law</td><td>Report Writing and Advocacy</td><td>Beginner to Advanced</td><td>45 minutes</td></tr></tbody></table></div><h4>Learning objectives</h4><p>By the end of this lesson you should be able to:</p><ul><li>Structure a human rights report</li><li>Write findings that are evidenced, attributed and legally framed</li><li>Draft recommendations that can actually be implemented</li><li>Plan an advocacy strategy around a report</li></ul><h4>1. Introduction</h4><p>A report that no one reads changes nothing, and a report that is read but cannot be acted on changes little more. Reporting is not the end of documentation; it is the beginning of advocacy, and it should be designed backwards from the change it is meant to produce.</p><h4>2. Structure</h4><div class=\"table-wrap\"><table class=\"study-table\"><thead><tr><th>Section</th><th>Function</th><th>Common failure</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Executive summary</td><td>The whole argument in two pages for a reader who reads nothing else</td><td>Written last and treated as an afterthought</td></tr><tr><td>Methodology</td><td>How the facts were established, over what period, with what limitations</td><td>Omitted, which invites the response that the findings are unverified</td></tr><tr><td>Background</td><td>Only the context the reader needs to understand the findings</td><td>Expanded into a history that buries the argument</td></tr><tr><td>Legal framework</td><td>The specific provisions engaged</td><td>A list of ratified treaties with no application to the facts</td></tr><tr><td>Findings</td><td>What happened, evidenced and attributed</td><td>Narrative without attribution, or conclusions without evidence</td></tr><tr><td>Analysis</td><td>Application of the framework to the findings</td><td>Merged into findings, so fact and law become indistinguishable</td></tr><tr><td>Recommendations</td><td>What each named actor should do</td><td>Addressed to no one in particular and unmeasurable</td></tr><tr><td>Annexes</td><td>Supporting material</td><td>Used to hide weak evidence</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"study-callout\"><strong>Note:</strong> The methodology section is the one most often cut and the one most often attacked. State the period covered, the number and type of sources, how they were verified, the standard of proof applied, what access was sought and whether it was granted, and what the limitations are. A report that states its own limitations is harder to dismiss than one that claims completeness.</div><h4>3. Writing a finding</h4><p>A finding has four components. Missing any one gives the reader a reason to set it aside.</p><ol><li><strong>What happened.</strong> Specific: date, place, who was affected, what was done.</li><li><strong>How it is known.</strong> The evidence and its corroboration, without exposing sources.</li><li><strong>Who is responsible.</strong> Attribution to a named institution, unit or actor, stated at the level the evidence supports.</li><li><strong>Which rule it breaches.</strong> The specific provision, not the treaty in general.</li></ol><div class=\"study-callout\"><strong>Example:</strong> Weak: 'There are widespread reports of abuses by security forces in the region, and the human rights situation is deteriorating.' Nothing here can be verified, denied or acted on. Strong: 'Between 3 and 19 March, police at the district station detained at least 24 people without charge for periods of 11 to 40 days. The finding rests on interviews with 14 former detainees conducted separately, three of whom produced release documents, and on the station custody register obtained on 2 April, which records no charge for 22 of the 24. This breaches ICCPR Article 9(3), which requires that anyone arrested be brought promptly before a judge.' The second version states the standard of proof it meets, can be checked, and tells the reader precisely what rule was broken.</div><h4>4. Recommendations</h4><p>Most recommendations fail because they are addressed to no one and specify nothing. Test every recommendation against six criteria.</p><div class=\"table-wrap\"><table class=\"study-table\"><thead><tr><th>Criterion</th><th>Question</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Addressed</td><td>Is a specific actor named, with the power to act?</td></tr><tr><td>Specific</td><td>Does it say what to do, not what to achieve?</td></tr><tr><td>Measurable</td><td>Will it be evident whether it was done?</td></tr><tr><td>Achievable</td><td>Is it within the actor's authority and resources?</td></tr><tr><td>Time-bound</td><td>By when?</td></tr><tr><td>Sequenced</td><td>Does it depend on something else happening first?</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"table-wrap\"><table class=\"study-table\"><thead><tr><th>Weak</th><th>Strong</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>The government should respect human rights</td><td>The Ministry of Justice should, within six months, table amendments to the Criminal Procedure Code imposing a 48-hour maximum on detention before production to a court</td></tr><tr><td>Impunity should end</td><td>The Inspector General should refer the four cases identified in Annex B to the prosecuting authority within 30 days and publish the outcome</td></tr><tr><td>Conditions should improve</td><td>The Prisons Service should reduce occupancy at the central facility to its stated capacity of 400 by transferring remand prisoners whose cases have been dismissed</td></tr></tbody></table></div><p>Group recommendations by addressee rather than by theme. A ministry reading a report should be able to find, in one place, everything it is being asked to do.</p><h4>5. Advocacy strategy</h4><ol><li><strong>Define the change.</strong> Not 'raise awareness' but a specific decision by a specific decision-maker.</li><li><strong>Identify the decision-maker</strong> who can deliver it, and the people who influence them.</li><li><strong>Analyse power.</strong> What does the decision-maker want, what do they fear, and who has leverage over them?</li><li><strong>Choose the channels.</strong> Direct engagement, coalition, media, litigation, international mechanisms, or a sequence of these.</li><li><strong>Time it.</strong> To a budget cycle, a parliamentary session, an election, a treaty body review or a Universal Periodic Review slot.</li><li><strong>Prepare for the response.</strong> Anticipate denial, discrediting of sources, counter-accusation and delay, and decide in advance how to answer each.</li><li><strong>Measure.</strong> Track whether the decision was taken, not how many people attended the launch.</li></ol><div class=\"table-wrap\"><table class=\"study-table\"><thead><tr><th>Channel</th><th>Best for</th><th>Risk</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Private engagement</td><td>Technical change where the authority is willing</td><td>Indefinite delay with no external pressure</td></tr><tr><td>Public report and media</td><td>Creating pressure where engagement has failed</td><td>Hardens positions; may endanger sources</td></tr><tr><td>Coalition</td><td>Broadening legitimacy and sharing risk</td><td>Slower; lowest common denominator positions</td></tr><tr><td>Litigation</td><td>Establishing a binding obligation</td><td>Slow, costly, and a loss creates adverse precedent</td></tr><tr><td>International mechanisms</td><td>Where domestic routes are closed</td><td>Distant; invites the charge of foreign interference</td></tr><tr><td>Universal Periodic Review</td><td>Converting an external criticism into a state commitment</td><td>Fixed cycle, so timing is not in your control</td></tr></tbody></table></div><h4>6. Right of reply and accuracy</h4><ul><li>Put the substance of adverse findings to the authority or actor concerned before publication, with a reasonable deadline, and reflect the response in the report.</li><li>This is both an ethical obligation and a practical protection: an unanswered allegation is easier to dismiss than one the authority declined to answer.</li><li>Correct errors promptly and visibly. A single uncorrected error is used to discredit an entire report.</li><li>Do not overstate. Claiming more than the evidence supports invites a rebuttal that then obscures the findings that were sound.</li><li>Distinguish clearly between what is established, what is alleged and what is inferred.</li></ul><h4>7. How the examiner&#x27;s question should be answered</h4><p>Set out the structure with the function of each section, and single out methodology as the section that determines whether the report survives contact with its target. Give the four components of a finding and demonstrate them with a weak and a strong example, since the contrast is more persuasive than description. Test recommendations against the six criteria and give paired examples. On advocacy, insist that the objective be a decision by a named decision-maker rather than awareness, and note the right of reply as both ethics and protection.</p><h4>Summary</h4><ul><li>A report should be designed backwards from the change it is meant to produce.</li><li>The methodology section determines whether findings survive challenge and should state limitations openly.</li><li>A finding needs four components: what happened, how it is known, who is responsible, and which rule it breaches.</li><li>Recommendations must be addressed, specific, measurable, achievable, time-bound and sequenced, and grouped by addressee.</li><li>Advocacy begins with a specific decision by a named decision-maker, not with awareness.</li><li>Adverse findings should be put to the actor concerned before publication, which is both ethical and protective.</li></ul><h4>Key Terms</h4><div class=\"table-wrap\"><table class=\"study-table\"><thead><tr><th>Term</th><th>Meaning</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Methodology section</td><td>The part of a report stating how facts were established, the standard applied and the limitations.</td></tr><tr><td>Finding</td><td>A statement of what happened, how it is known, who is responsible and which rule was breached.</td></tr><tr><td>Right of reply</td><td>The opportunity given to an actor to respond to adverse findings before publication.</td></tr><tr><td>Advocacy objective</td><td>A specific decision by a specific decision-maker, as distinct from raising awareness.</td></tr><tr><td>Sequencing</td><td>Ordering recommendations so that those depending on others come later.</td></tr></tbody></table></div><h4>Review Questions</h4><h5>Multiple choice</h5><div class=\"study-mcq\"><p class=\"mcq-q\">Which section of a report is most often cut and most often attacked?</p><ol type=\"A\"><li>Methodology</li><li>Background</li><li>Annexes</li><li>Executive summary</li></ol><details class=\"mcq-a\"><summary>Show answer</summary><p><strong>Answer: A.</strong> Stating the period, sources, verification, standard of proof, access sought and limitations makes findings much harder to dismiss.</p></details></div><div class=\"study-mcq\"><p class=\"mcq-q\">What are the four components of a finding?</p><ol type=\"A\"><li>What happened, how it is known, who is responsible, and which rule it breaches</li><li>Date, place, victim and perpetrator</li><li>Allegation, denial, evidence and conclusion</li><li>Background, analysis, finding and recommendation</li></ol><details class=\"mcq-a\"><summary>Show answer</summary><p><strong>Answer: A.</strong> Omitting any one gives the reader a reason to set the finding aside.</p></details></div><div class=\"study-mcq\"><p class=\"mcq-q\">Which is a well-drafted recommendation?</p><ol type=\"A\"><li>The Ministry of Justice should within six months table amendments imposing a 48-hour maximum on pre-charge detention</li><li>The government should respect human rights</li><li>Impunity should end</li><li>Conditions in detention should improve</li></ol><details class=\"mcq-a\"><summary>Show answer</summary><p><strong>Answer: A.</strong> It names an actor with the power to act, says what to do rather than what to achieve, and is measurable and time-bound.</p></details></div><div class=\"study-mcq\"><p class=\"mcq-q\">Why put adverse findings to the authority before publication?</p><ol type=\"A\"><li>It is an ethical obligation and a protection, since an unanswered allegation is harder to dismiss than one the authority declined to answer</li><li>It is required by the Rome Statute</li><li>It guarantees a correction</li><li>It shortens the report</li></ol><details class=\"mcq-a\"><summary>Show answer</summary><p><strong>Answer: A.</strong> The response should then be reflected in the report itself.</p></details></div><h5>Short answer</h5><div class=\"study-sa\"><p class=\"sa-q\">Set out the six criteria for a good recommendation.</p><details class=\"sa-a\"><summary>Model answer</summary><p>It must be addressed to a specific actor who has the power to act, rather than to a government in general. It must be specific, saying what to do rather than what to achieve. It must be measurable, so that it is evident whether it was done. It must be achievable within the actor&#x27;s authority and resources, since an impossible recommendation invites dismissal of the whole report. It must be time-bound, stating by when. And it must be sequenced, so that recommendations depending on prior steps come in the right order. Recommendations should be grouped by addressee rather than by theme, so that each institution can find everything it is being asked to do in one place.</p></details></div><div class=\"study-sa\"><p class=\"sa-q\">Describe the steps in planning an advocacy strategy around a report.</p><details class=\"sa-a\"><summary>Model answer</summary><p>Begin by defining the change as a specific decision by a specific decision-maker, not as raising awareness. Identify who can actually deliver that decision and who influences them. Analyse power by asking what the decision-maker wants, what they fear and who has leverage over them. Choose the channels, whether private engagement, coalition, media, litigation, international mechanisms or a sequence of these, weighing what each is good for against its risks. Time the intervention to a budget cycle, parliamentary session, treaty body review or Universal Periodic Review slot. Prepare in advance for denial, discrediting of sources, counter-accusation and delay. Finally, measure success by whether the decision was taken, not by attendance at a launch event.</p></details></div><h4>Class Activity</h4><ol><li>Take a weak recommendation from any published report and rewrite it against the six criteria. Show both versions.</li><li>Draft the methodology section for a report on an issue in your community, covering period, sources, verification, standard of proof, access and limitations.</li></ol><h4>Assignment</h4><p>Write two pages on report writing and advocacy. Set out the structure of a report with the function of each section and explain why methodology is decisive, give the four components of a finding with a weak and a strong worked example, set out the six criteria for recommendations with paired examples, describe the steps in planning an advocacy strategy and compare at least four channels, and explain the right of reply as both an ethical and a practical requirement.</p><p class=\"study-print\"><strong>Printable:</strong> use your browser\u2019s Print \u2192 Save as PDF to keep an offline copy.</p></div>",
  "Transitional Justice": "<div class=\"study-note\"><div class=\"revision-banner\"><strong>Human Rights and International Humanitarian Law</strong><span>Module 12: Human Rights in Practice</span></div><h3 class=\"lesson-note-title\">LESSON NOTE: TRANSITIONAL JUSTICE</h3><div class=\"table-wrap\"><table class=\"study-table\"><thead><tr><th>Subject</th><th>Topic</th><th>Level</th><th>Lesson duration</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Human Rights and International Humanitarian Law</td><td>Transitional Justice</td><td>Beginner to Advanced</td><td>45 minutes</td></tr></tbody></table></div><h4>Learning objectives</h4><p>By the end of this lesson you should be able to:</p><ul><li>Define transitional justice and identify its four pillars</li><li>Describe truth commissions and their design choices</li><li>Explain the debate on amnesties and the peace versus justice question</li><li>Describe reparations and guarantees of non-repetition</li></ul><h4>1. Introduction</h4><p>When a conflict or an authoritarian period ends, a society confronts a set of questions that no single mechanism answers: what happened, who is responsible, what is owed to those harmed, and how to stop it recurring. Transitional justice is the field concerned with answering all four together.</p><h4>2. Definition and pillars</h4><div class=\"study-defn\"><strong>Transitional justice:</strong> The full range of processes and mechanisms associated with a society's attempts to come to terms with a legacy of large-scale past abuses, in order to secure accountability, serve justice and achieve reconciliation.</div><div class=\"table-wrap\"><table class=\"study-table\"><thead><tr><th>Pillar</th><th>Question it answers</th><th>Mechanisms</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Truth</td><td>What happened?</td><td>Truth commissions, inquiries, archives, forensic identification, missing persons work</td></tr><tr><td>Justice</td><td>Who is responsible?</td><td>Prosecutions, hybrid courts, vetting, traditional and community processes</td></tr><tr><td>Reparation</td><td>What is owed?</td><td>Compensation, restitution, rehabilitation, satisfaction, symbolic measures</td></tr><tr><td>Guarantees of non-repetition</td><td>How is it prevented?</td><td>Institutional reform, security sector reform, judicial reform, education, constitutional change</td></tr></tbody></table></div><p>The four are complementary, not alternative. This point matters because transitional justice is frequently reduced in public debate to a choice between prosecution and amnesty, which ignores two of the four pillars entirely. The UN's guidance and the Updated Set of Principles on impunity both treat the pillars as a single integrated obligation.</p><div class=\"study-defn\"><strong>Right to the truth:</strong> The right of victims, their families and society to know the truth about gross violations, the circumstances in which they occurred and the fate of the disappeared.</div><h4>3. Truth commissions</h4><div class=\"table-wrap\"><table class=\"study-table\"><thead><tr><th>Feature</th><th>Typical design choice</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Mandate period</td><td>The years of conflict or authoritarian rule, defined precisely</td></tr><tr><td>Scope of violations</td><td>Which violations are covered; whether economic crimes and corruption are included</td></tr><tr><td>Powers</td><td>Whether it can subpoena, search, or compel testimony</td></tr><tr><td>Naming</td><td>Whether individuals may be named, and with what due process protections</td></tr><tr><td>Public hearings</td><td>Whether hearings are public, which affects both catharsis and witness safety</td></tr><tr><td>Relationship to prosecution</td><td>Whether testimony is admissible in later criminal proceedings</td></tr><tr><td>Amnesty power</td><td>Whether the commission can recommend or grant amnesty</td></tr><tr><td>Composition</td><td>National, international or mixed; how commissioners are selected</td></tr><tr><td>Report</td><td>Whether publication is guaranteed, and who controls it</td></tr><tr><td>Follow-up</td><td>Whether an implementation body exists to pursue the recommendations</td></tr></tbody></table></div><ol><li><strong>South Africa (1995).</strong> The Truth and Reconciliation Commission had the power to grant individual amnesty conditional on full disclosure of politically motivated acts. It is the most studied example and the model most often misapplied elsewhere, because its amnesty was conditional and individualised, not blanket.</li><li><strong>Sierra Leone (2002).</strong> A truth commission operated alongside the Special Court, the first time a commission and an international tribunal ran in parallel. Their relationship required careful management over access to witnesses.</li><li><strong>Liberia (2005).</strong> The Truth and Reconciliation Commission reported in 2009, recommending prosecutions, a domestic war crimes court, reparations and lustration of named individuals. Implementation was limited for many years, and the establishment of a war crimes court remained under debate long afterwards. The Liberian experience is the central case for this course and is examined further in the final lesson of this module.</li><li><strong>Timor-Leste, Peru, Guatemala, Morocco, Kenya, Tunisia, Colombia and The Gambia</strong> provide the wider comparative set.</li></ol><div class=\"study-callout\"><strong>Note:</strong> A truth commission is not a court. It cannot convict, its standard of proof is lower, and its purpose includes acknowledgement and historical record as well as findings on individual conduct. Criticising a commission for failing to deliver criminal justice mistakes its function, but so does treating a commission as a substitute for prosecutions.</div><h4>4. Amnesties and the peace versus justice debate</h4><div class=\"table-wrap\"><table class=\"study-table\"><thead><tr><th>Type of amnesty</th><th>Description</th><th>Legal status</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Blanket</td><td>Covers all persons and all acts, without condition</td><td>Impermissible for gross violations; invalid under Inter-American jurisprudence and inconsistent with treaty duties to prosecute</td></tr><tr><td>Self-amnesty</td><td>Enacted by the outgoing regime for its own conduct</td><td>Impermissible; the paradigm case in Barrios Altos</td></tr><tr><td>Conditional</td><td>Granted individually on conditions such as full disclosure, cooperation or reparation</td><td>Contested; the South African model is the leading example</td></tr><tr><td>Limited</td><td>Excludes international crimes; covers lesser offences such as membership of an armed group</td><td>Generally accepted</td></tr></tbody></table></div><ol><li>The duty to prosecute grave breaches, torture and enforced disappearance arises from treaty, so an amnesty covering those conflicts with the state's obligations.</li><li>The Inter-American Court held in Barrios Altos that amnesties for gross violations are inadmissible, and in Gelman that approval by referendum does not cure this.</li><li>The UN will not endorse peace agreements that promise amnesty for genocide, crimes against humanity, war crimes or gross violations of human rights.</li><li>The counter-argument is practical: without an amnesty a party may not lay down arms, and continued conflict causes further violations.</li><li>The reconciling position generally adopted is that limited and conditional measures may be permissible for lesser offences, that international crimes cannot be amnestied, and that sequencing rather than exemption is the answer, with prosecutions focused on those most responsible and alternative measures for others.</li></ol><div class=\"study-defn\"><strong>Conditional amnesty:</strong> Relief from prosecution granted individually on conditions such as full disclosure, cooperation with a truth process, or contribution to reparations.</div><h4>5. Prosecution strategy</h4><ul><li>Prosecuting everyone is never possible; case selection is unavoidable and should be explicit rather than hidden.</li><li>The usual approach concentrates on those most responsible, meaning those who planned, ordered, financed or bore command responsibility, rather than the largest number of direct perpetrators.</li><li>Sequencing matters: early symbolic cases build capacity and public confidence, but a failed early case is costly.</li><li>Vetting, sometimes called lustration, removes those responsible from public office. It must be individualised, with due process and a right of appeal; collective purges based on former affiliation have repeatedly been found to violate rights.</li><li>Traditional and community mechanisms can process large numbers of lesser cases, provided they meet minimum fair trial standards and do not handle serious international crimes.</li></ul><h4>6. Reparations and non-repetition</h4><p>Reparation follows the five forms established in the Basic Principles and Guidelines on the Right to a Remedy and Reparation, adopted by the General Assembly in 2005.</p><div class=\"table-wrap\"><table class=\"study-table\"><thead><tr><th>Form</th><th>Content</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Restitution</td><td>Restoring the prior situation: liberty, identity, family life, citizenship, employment, property</td></tr><tr><td>Compensation</td><td>For economically assessable damage: physical and mental harm, lost opportunities, material damage, moral damage, costs of assistance</td></tr><tr><td>Rehabilitation</td><td>Medical and psychological care, and legal and social services</td></tr><tr><td>Satisfaction</td><td>Cessation, truth-seeking, search for the disappeared, official declaration or judicial decision restoring dignity, public apology, sanctions, commemoration</td></tr><tr><td>Guarantees of non-repetition</td><td>Civilian control of forces, independent judiciary, protection of legal and health professionals and human rights defenders, human rights education, codes of conduct, mechanisms to prevent conflict</td></tr></tbody></table></div><ol><li><strong>Individual and collective.</strong> Reparation may be delivered to individuals or to affected communities through services and infrastructure; collective programmes reach more people but risk being indistinguishable from ordinary development.</li><li><strong>Administrative programmes versus litigation.</strong> Administrative programmes reach far more victims than courts can, with lower evidentiary burdens, and are usually the realistic route.</li><li><strong>Registration.</strong> A victim register is the practical precondition of any programme and is itself contested, since the definition of victim determines who is included.</li><li><strong>Symbolic measures matter.</strong> Apologies, memorials and days of remembrance are frequently dismissed as cheap substitutes, but victims consistently rank official acknowledgement highly.</li><li><strong>Non-repetition is the pillar most often neglected</strong>, because institutional reform is slow, technical and politically costly, and produces no visible event.</li></ol><div class=\"study-callout\"><strong>Example:</strong> A state establishes a truth commission, prosecutes a handful of senior figures and pays a modest sum to registered victims, but leaves the security services, the judiciary and the vetting of officials untouched. Three pillars have been partially addressed and the fourth ignored. The predictable result is that the conditions producing the violations remain in place, and the transitional justice process is later judged to have failed even though it delivered truth, some justice and some reparation. This pattern is common enough to be the standard critique of the field.</div><h4>7. How the examiner&#x27;s question should be answered</h4><p>Set out the four pillars and insist they are complementary, since the commonest weakness is treating the field as a choice between prosecution and amnesty. On truth commissions, discuss design choices rather than describing one commission, and distinguish a commission from a court. On amnesties, classify the four types and give the legal status of each, citing Barrios Altos and Gelman. On reparations, give the five forms from the Basic Principles. Close on guarantees of non-repetition and note that it is the pillar most often neglected, which is the analytical point that lifts an answer.</p><h4>Summary</h4><ul><li>Transitional justice rests on four complementary pillars: truth, justice, reparation and guarantees of non-repetition.</li><li>A truth commission is not a court; it establishes record and acknowledgement to a lower standard and cannot convict.</li><li>Blanket and self-amnesties for gross violations are impermissible; limited and conditional measures for lesser offences may be acceptable.</li><li>Barrios Altos held amnesties for grave violations inadmissible and Gelman held a referendum cannot cure this.</li><li>Prosecution strategy concentrates on those most responsible, with vetting individualised and subject to due process.</li><li>Reparation takes five forms, and guarantees of non-repetition is the pillar most often neglected.</li></ul><h4>Key Terms</h4><div class=\"table-wrap\"><table class=\"study-table\"><thead><tr><th>Term</th><th>Meaning</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Transitional justice</td><td>The processes by which a society addresses a legacy of large-scale past abuses.</td></tr><tr><td>Truth commission</td><td>A temporary body establishing a record of past violations, without power to convict.</td></tr><tr><td>Blanket amnesty</td><td>An unconditional amnesty for all persons and acts, impermissible for gross violations.</td></tr><tr><td>Vetting</td><td>Individualised removal of those responsible from public office, requiring due process.</td></tr><tr><td>Guarantees of non-repetition</td><td>Institutional reforms designed to prevent recurrence.</td></tr></tbody></table></div><h4>Review Questions</h4><h5>Multiple choice</h5><div class=\"study-mcq\"><p class=\"mcq-q\">What are the four pillars of transitional justice?</p><ol type=\"A\"><li>Truth, justice, reparation and guarantees of non-repetition</li><li>Prosecution, amnesty, compensation and reconciliation</li><li>Investigation, trial, sentence and appeal</li><li>Peace, security, development and democracy</li></ol><details class=\"mcq-a\"><summary>Show answer</summary><p><strong>Answer: A.</strong> They are complementary rather than alternative, and reducing the field to a choice between prosecution and amnesty ignores two of them.</p></details></div><div class=\"study-mcq\"><p class=\"mcq-q\">What distinguishes the South African amnesty from a blanket amnesty?</p><ol type=\"A\"><li>It was individual and conditional on full disclosure of politically motivated acts</li><li>It covered only economic crimes</li><li>It was granted by a court</li><li>It applied only to the former government</li></ol><details class=\"mcq-a\"><summary>Show answer</summary><p><strong>Answer: A.</strong> The model is frequently misapplied elsewhere precisely because this conditionality is overlooked.</p></details></div><div class=\"study-mcq\"><p class=\"mcq-q\">Which pillar is most often neglected in practice?</p><ol type=\"A\"><li>Guarantees of non-repetition, because institutional reform is slow, technical and politically costly</li><li>Truth</li><li>Justice</li><li>Reparation</li></ol><details class=\"mcq-a\"><summary>Show answer</summary><p><strong>Answer: A.</strong> Leaving the security services and judiciary untouched means the conditions producing the violations remain in place.</p></details></div><div class=\"study-mcq\"><p class=\"mcq-q\">How many forms of reparation do the 2005 Basic Principles set out?</p><ol type=\"A\"><li>Five: restitution, compensation, rehabilitation, satisfaction and guarantees of non-repetition</li><li>Three</li><li>Two</li><li>Seven</li></ol><details class=\"mcq-a\"><summary>Show answer</summary><p><strong>Answer: A.</strong> Satisfaction includes truth-seeking, search for the disappeared, public apology and commemoration.</p></details></div><h5>Short answer</h5><div class=\"study-sa\"><p class=\"sa-q\">Classify the types of amnesty and state the legal position on each.</p><details class=\"sa-a\"><summary>Model answer</summary><p>A blanket amnesty covers all persons and acts without condition and is impermissible for gross violations, since it conflicts with treaty duties to prosecute grave breaches, torture and enforced disappearance. A self-amnesty, enacted by an outgoing regime for its own conduct, is the paradigm impermissible case and was struck down in Barrios Altos, with Gelman confirming that approval by referendum does not cure the incompatibility. A conditional amnesty is granted individually on conditions such as full disclosure, cooperation or contribution to reparation; the South African model is the leading example and its status remains contested. A limited amnesty excluding international crimes and covering lesser offences, such as mere membership of an armed group, is generally accepted. The reconciling position is that international crimes cannot be amnestied and that sequencing rather than exemption is the answer.</p></details></div><div class=\"study-sa\"><p class=\"sa-q\">Set out the five forms of reparation with an example of each.</p><details class=\"sa-a\"><summary>Model answer</summary><p>Restitution restores the prior situation, for example by reinstating employment, returning property or restoring citizenship. Compensation addresses economically assessable damage, including physical and mental harm, lost opportunities, material damage and the costs of legal or medical assistance. Rehabilitation provides medical and psychological care together with legal and social services. Satisfaction covers cessation of violations, truth-seeking, the search for the disappeared, an official declaration restoring dignity, public apology, sanctions against those responsible and commemoration. Guarantees of non-repetition require structural change such as civilian control of the armed forces, an independent judiciary, protection of human rights defenders, human rights education and codes of conduct for officials.</p></details></div><h4>Class Activity</h4><ol><li>Choose one truth commission and assess it against the ten design choices in this lesson. Identify the two choices you think most affected its outcome.</li><li>Draft a reparations proposal for a specific harmed group, specifying which of the five forms you would use, how victims would be registered, and how the programme would be funded.</li></ol><h4>Assignment</h4><p>Write two pages on transitional justice. Define the field and set out the four pillars, explaining why they are complementary, describe truth commissions through their design choices and distinguish a commission from a court, classify amnesties and state the legal position on each citing the leading cases, explain prosecution strategy including case selection and vetting, set out the five forms of reparation, and explain why guarantees of non-repetition is the pillar most often neglected.</p><p class=\"study-print\"><strong>Printable:</strong> use your browser\u2019s Print \u2192 Save as PDF to keep an offline copy.</p></div>",
  "Protecting Human Rights Defenders": "<div class=\"study-note\"><div class=\"revision-banner\"><strong>Human Rights and International Humanitarian Law</strong><span>Module 12: Human Rights in Practice</span></div><h3 class=\"lesson-note-title\">LESSON NOTE: PROTECTING HUMAN RIGHTS DEFENDERS</h3><div class=\"table-wrap\"><table class=\"study-table\"><thead><tr><th>Subject</th><th>Topic</th><th>Level</th><th>Lesson duration</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Human Rights and International Humanitarian Law</td><td>Protecting Human Rights Defenders</td><td>Beginner to Advanced</td><td>40 minutes</td></tr></tbody></table></div><h4>Learning objectives</h4><p>By the end of this lesson you should be able to:</p><ul><li>Define a human rights defender and describe the Declaration</li><li>Identify the main threats defenders face</li><li>Describe protection measures at individual, organisational and state level</li><li>Explain the specific risks facing women and environmental defenders</li></ul><h4>1. Introduction</h4><p>The people who document violations become targets of them. Protecting defenders is not a side issue in this field: without them there is no documentation, no litigation and no advocacy, and every mechanism described in this course depends on information they supply.</p><h4>2. Who is a defender</h4><div class=\"study-defn\"><strong>Human rights defender:</strong> Any person who, individually or with others, acts to promote or protect human rights peacefully, regardless of profession, qualification, payment or organisational affiliation.</div><ol><li><strong>The definition is functional, not professional.</strong> It covers lawyers, journalists, trade unionists, community leaders, students, faith leaders, health workers, whistleblowers and ordinary people acting on a single local issue.</li><li><strong>Peaceful action is the only qualifying condition.</strong> A person who uses or advocates violence is not a defender, whatever their cause.</li><li><strong>Correctness is not required.</strong> A defender need not be right about the law or the facts; the protection attaches to the activity, not to its accuracy.</li><li><strong>No registration or accreditation is required.</strong> States that limit the label to registered organisations are narrowing a definition that international law leaves open.</li></ol><h4>3. The Declaration on Human Rights Defenders</h4><p>Adopted by the General Assembly in 1998, formally the Declaration on the Right and Responsibility of Individuals, Groups and Organs of Society to Promote and Protect Universally Recognized Human Rights and Fundamental Freedoms.</p><ul><li>It is a declaration, so not binding as such, but the rights it articulates are drawn from binding instruments.</li><li>Article 1 states the right to promote and to strive for the protection and realisation of human rights.</li><li>Article 5 protects the rights to meet peaceably, to form organisations, and to communicate with non-governmental and intergovernmental bodies.</li><li>Article 6 protects the rights to know, seek, obtain, receive and hold information about all human rights, and to publish and disseminate views on them.</li><li>Article 9 provides for effective remedies and for the right to complain about the policies and actions of officials.</li><li>Article 12 requires states to protect defenders against violence, threats, retaliation, discrimination, pressure and any other arbitrary action arising from the legitimate exercise of these rights.</li><li>A Special Rapporteur on the situation of human rights defenders was established in 2000 and remains the principal international mechanism.</li></ul><h4>4. The threats</h4><div class=\"table-wrap\"><table class=\"study-table\"><thead><tr><th>Category</th><th>Forms</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Physical</td><td>Killing, enforced disappearance, torture, assault, sexual violence</td></tr><tr><td>Legal</td><td>Criminal charges for defamation, sedition, terrorism, public order or foreign agent offences; strategic litigation designed to exhaust resources</td></tr><tr><td>Administrative</td><td>Deregistration, refusal of permits, tax and audit pressure, restrictions on foreign funding, visa denial</td></tr><tr><td>Digital</td><td>Surveillance, spyware, device seizure, account compromise, doxxing, coordinated online harassment</td></tr><tr><td>Reputational</td><td>Smear campaigns branding defenders as foreign agents, terrorists, criminals or immoral</td></tr><tr><td>Economic</td><td>Loss of employment, exclusion from contracts, pressure on family members' livelihoods</td></tr><tr><td>Against family</td><td>Threats to and attacks on relatives, particularly children</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"study-callout\"><strong>Note:</strong> The trend most often identified in reporting is the shift from overt violence to legal and administrative pressure. Prosecuting a defender under a broadly drafted terrorism or foreign funding law achieves the same silencing effect as violence, while presenting internationally as ordinary law enforcement. Recognising that pattern, and analysing such laws for overbreadth and disproportionate effect, is the substantive skill in this area.</div><h4>5. Protection measures</h4><div class=\"table-wrap\"><table class=\"study-table\"><thead><tr><th>Level</th><th>Measures</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Individual</td><td>Risk assessment; varying routines; secure communications; device hygiene; trusted contacts and check-in protocols; documentation of threats; a relocation plan</td></tr><tr><td>Organisational</td><td>A security policy; incident reporting; data protection and encryption; legal support arrangements; insurance; psychosocial support; a duty of care to staff and to sources</td></tr><tr><td>National</td><td>Protection mechanisms and programmes; prompt investigation of threats; repeal of laws criminalising legitimate activity; a national human rights institution with a defender mandate; public acknowledgement of defenders' legitimacy by senior officials</td></tr><tr><td>Regional and international</td><td>Special Rapporteur communications; regional court precautionary and provisional measures; emergency grants; temporary relocation and shelter initiatives; trial observation; diplomatic accompaniment</td></tr></tbody></table></div><ol><li><strong>Risk equals threat multiplied by vulnerability, divided by capacity.</strong> This standard formula is useful because it shows three levers: reduce the threat, reduce vulnerability, or increase capacity. Most protection work operates on the second and third.</li><li><strong>Protection must be individualised.</strong> A generic package fails; measures must match the specific threat, the person's circumstances and their own assessment.</li><li><strong>Defenders' own agency is central.</strong> They know their context best, and protection imposed without their participation is often both resented and ineffective.</li><li><strong>Public visibility can protect or endanger.</strong> Raising a defender's profile deters some attackers and provokes others; the judgement belongs to the defender.</li><li><strong>Investigating threats is itself protection.</strong> Where threats are never investigated, escalation follows; a single prompt investigation changes the calculation of those making them.</li></ol><h4>6. Women defenders</h4><p>Women human rights defenders face the general risks and additional ones arising from gender.</p><ul><li>Attacks framed around sexuality, family role and respectability rather than the substance of their work.</li><li>Sexual violence and threats of it used as a specific method of intimidation.</li><li>Online harassment that is disproportionately gendered and sexualised.</li><li>Hostility from within their own communities and families, so that home is not a place of safety.</li><li>Particular exposure for those working on sexual and reproductive rights, gender-based violence and the rights of sexual minorities.</li><li>Care responsibilities that constrain relocation and make family members a point of leverage.</li></ul><p>The consequence for protection design is that measures must address the private sphere as well as the public one, must not assume the ability to relocate, and must include family. General Assembly and Human Rights Council resolutions on women human rights defenders recognise these distinct risks explicitly.</p><h4>7. Environmental and land defenders</h4><ul><li>Environmental, land and indigenous defenders are consistently identified in annual monitoring as the most frequently killed category of defender.</li><li>Their opponents are often private actors, mining, logging and agribusiness interests, sometimes with state acquiescence, which complicates both attribution and protection.</li><li>The threats typically arise in remote areas where state protection is weakest and impunity is highest.</li><li>Free, prior and informed consent processes, discussed in the indigenous rights lesson, are directly relevant, since disputes over consent are the usual trigger.</li><li>The Escazu Agreement in Latin America and the Caribbean is the first treaty to contain specific obligations on the protection of environmental defenders, and is the reference point for arguments that similar provisions should exist elsewhere.</li></ul><h4>8. How the examiner&#x27;s question should be answered</h4><p>Define a defender functionally and stress that the test is peaceful activity, not profession or accuracy. Set out the Declaration by article rather than describing it generally, and note that it is not binding while the underlying rights are. Categorise the threats and identify the shift from violence to legal and administrative pressure as the key trend. Give protection at four levels and use the risk formula to structure the analysis. Address women and environmental defenders separately, since a generic answer misses the specific risks that make protection design different for each.</p><h4>Summary</h4><ul><li>A human rights defender is anyone acting peacefully to promote or protect human rights, regardless of profession or affiliation.</li><li>The 1998 Declaration is not binding but articulates rights drawn from binding instruments, with Article 12 requiring state protection.</li><li>Threats are physical, legal, administrative, digital, reputational, economic and directed at family.</li><li>The main trend is a shift from overt violence to legal and administrative pressure that presents as ordinary law enforcement.</li><li>Risk is threat multiplied by vulnerability divided by capacity, which identifies the three levers protection can pull.</li><li>Women defenders face gendered attacks including in the private sphere, and environmental and land defenders are the most frequently killed category.</li></ul><h4>Key Terms</h4><div class=\"table-wrap\"><table class=\"study-table\"><thead><tr><th>Term</th><th>Meaning</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Human rights defender</td><td>Any person acting peacefully, alone or with others, to promote or protect human rights.</td></tr><tr><td>Declaration on Human Rights Defenders</td><td>The 1998 General Assembly declaration setting out defenders&#x27; rights and states&#x27; protection duties.</td></tr><tr><td>Judicial harassment</td><td>Use of criminal or civil proceedings to silence or exhaust a defender.</td></tr><tr><td>Risk formula</td><td>Risk equals threat multiplied by vulnerability, divided by capacity.</td></tr><tr><td>Escazu Agreement</td><td>The first treaty containing specific obligations on the protection of environmental defenders.</td></tr></tbody></table></div><h4>Review Questions</h4><h5>Multiple choice</h5><div class=\"study-mcq\"><p class=\"mcq-q\">Who qualifies as a human rights defender?</p><ol type=\"A\"><li>Anyone acting peacefully to promote or protect human rights, regardless of profession, payment or registration</li><li>Only staff of registered non-governmental organisations</li><li>Only lawyers and journalists</li><li>Only those accredited by a national institution</li></ol><details class=\"mcq-a\"><summary>Show answer</summary><p><strong>Answer: A.</strong> The definition is functional, and a defender need not be correct about the law or facts for the protection to attach.</p></details></div><div class=\"study-mcq\"><p class=\"mcq-q\">What is the main trend in threats against defenders?</p><ol type=\"A\"><li>A shift from overt violence to legal and administrative pressure that presents as ordinary law enforcement</li><li>A decline in all forms of threat</li><li>A shift from legal pressure to violence</li><li>An increase only in digital threats</li></ol><details class=\"mcq-a\"><summary>Show answer</summary><p><strong>Answer: A.</strong> Prosecuting a defender under a broad terrorism or foreign funding law achieves the same silencing effect while appearing legitimate internationally.</p></details></div><div class=\"study-mcq\"><p class=\"mcq-q\">What does the risk formula identify?</p><ol type=\"A\"><li>Three levers: reducing the threat, reducing vulnerability, or increasing capacity</li><li>The likelihood of prosecution</li><li>The cost of protection</li><li>The number of defenders at risk</li></ol><details class=\"mcq-a\"><summary>Show answer</summary><p><strong>Answer: A.</strong> Most protection work operates on vulnerability and capacity, since reducing the threat usually requires state action.</p></details></div><div class=\"study-mcq\"><p class=\"mcq-q\">Which category of defender is most frequently killed according to annual monitoring?</p><ol type=\"A\"><li>Environmental, land and indigenous defenders</li><li>Lawyers</li><li>Journalists</li><li>Trade unionists</li></ol><details class=\"mcq-a\"><summary>Show answer</summary><p><strong>Answer: A.</strong> Their opponents are often private commercial actors in remote areas where state protection is weakest and impunity highest.</p></details></div><h5>Short answer</h5><div class=\"study-sa\"><p class=\"sa-q\">Describe protection measures at four levels.</p><details class=\"sa-a\"><summary>Model answer</summary><p>At the individual level: risk assessment, varying routines, secure communications and device hygiene, trusted contacts with check-in protocols, systematic documentation of threats, and a relocation plan. At the organisational level: a written security policy, incident reporting, data protection and encryption, arrangements for legal support, insurance, psychosocial support, and a recognised duty of care to both staff and sources. At the national level: dedicated protection mechanisms, prompt investigation of threats, repeal of laws criminalising legitimate activity, a national human rights institution with a defender mandate, and public acknowledgement by senior officials that defenders are legitimate. At the regional and international level: special procedure communications, precautionary and provisional measures from regional bodies, emergency grants, temporary relocation initiatives, trial observation and diplomatic accompaniment.</p></details></div><div class=\"study-sa\"><p class=\"sa-q\">Explain the specific risks facing women human rights defenders.</p><details class=\"sa-a\"><summary>Model answer</summary><p>They face the general threats and additional ones arising from gender. Attacks are framed around their sexuality, family role and respectability rather than the substance of their work, which shifts the debate away from the violations they document. Sexual violence and threats of it are used as a specific method of intimidation, and online harassment against them is disproportionately gendered and sexualised. Hostility frequently comes from within their own communities and families, so the home is not a place of safety, which is a difference from most male defenders. Those working on sexual and reproductive rights, gender-based violence and the rights of sexual minorities are particularly exposed. Care responsibilities constrain relocation and make family members a point of leverage. Protection design must therefore reach the private sphere, must not assume mobility, and must include family members.</p></details></div><h4>Class Activity</h4><ol><li>Carry out a risk assessment for a defender working on a real local issue, using the risk formula and identifying one measure for each of the three levers.</li><li>Find one law in your country that could be used against defenders. Analyse it for overbreadth and write a paragraph on how it would be applied to silence legitimate activity.</li></ol><h4>Assignment</h4><p>Write one and a half pages on protecting human rights defenders. Define a defender and explain why the definition is functional, set out the main provisions of the 1998 Declaration by article, categorise the threats and identify the principal trend, describe protection measures at four levels using the risk formula, and explain the distinct risks facing women defenders and environmental and land defenders.</p><p class=\"study-print\"><strong>Printable:</strong> use your browser\u2019s Print \u2192 Save as PDF to keep an offline copy.</p></div>",
  "Business and Human Rights": "<div class=\"study-note\"><div class=\"revision-banner\"><strong>Human Rights and International Humanitarian Law</strong><span>Module 12: Human Rights in Practice</span></div><h3 class=\"lesson-note-title\">LESSON NOTE: BUSINESS AND HUMAN RIGHTS</h3><div class=\"table-wrap\"><table class=\"study-table\"><thead><tr><th>Subject</th><th>Topic</th><th>Level</th><th>Lesson duration</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Human Rights and International Humanitarian Law</td><td>Business and Human Rights</td><td>Beginner to Advanced</td><td>40 minutes</td></tr></tbody></table></div><h4>Learning objectives</h4><p>By the end of this lesson you should be able to:</p><ul><li>Describe the UN Guiding Principles and their three pillars</li><li>Explain human rights due diligence</li><li>Describe the access to remedy pillar and its barriers</li><li>Explain mandatory due diligence laws and the treaty process</li></ul><h4>1. Introduction</h4><p>Human rights law binds states. Yet the actors whose decisions most directly affect rights in many places are companies, and companies are not parties to human rights treaties. The framework covered in this lesson is the international community's answer to that gap, and its central move is to distinguish what states must do from what companies should do.</p><h4>2. The Guiding Principles</h4><div class=\"table-wrap\"><table class=\"study-table\"><thead><tr><th>Feature</th><th>Detail</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Full name</td><td>Guiding Principles on Business and Human Rights: Implementing the United Nations 'Protect, Respect and Remedy' Framework</td></tr><tr><td>Endorsed</td><td>Human Rights Council, 2011</td></tr><tr><td>Author</td><td>John Ruggie, Special Representative of the Secretary-General</td></tr><tr><td>Status</td><td>Not binding; a soft law framework, now the authoritative global reference</td></tr><tr><td>Structure</td><td>31 principles across three pillars</td></tr></tbody></table></div><p>Their significance is that they resolved a decade of deadlock. Earlier attempts to impose direct human rights obligations on companies, notably the draft Norms of 2003, failed because states and business both rejected them. The Guiding Principles succeeded by separating the state duty, which is legal, from the corporate responsibility, which is a standard of expected conduct.</p><div class=\"table-wrap\"><table class=\"study-table\"><thead><tr><th>Pillar</th><th>Holder</th><th>Nature</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Protect</td><td>The state</td><td>A legal duty to protect against human rights abuse by third parties, including business</td></tr><tr><td>Respect</td><td>The business enterprise</td><td>A responsibility to respect human rights, meaning to avoid infringing and to address adverse impacts</td></tr><tr><td>Remedy</td><td>Both</td><td>Access to effective remedy, through judicial and non-judicial mechanisms</td></tr></tbody></table></div><h4>3. Pillar one: the state duty to protect</h4><ul><li>States must protect against abuse by business enterprises through effective policies, legislation, regulation and adjudication.</li><li>They should enforce laws requiring business to respect human rights, and periodically assess their adequacy.</li><li>They should ensure that other laws, such as company and investment law, do not constrain business respect for human rights.</li><li>The state-business nexus principles apply heightened expectations where a state owns, controls or contracts with an enterprise, or where it provides substantial support such as export credit.</li><li>States should maintain policy coherence, so that trade and investment agreements do not undermine their own human rights obligations.</li><li>In conflict-affected areas, states should assist enterprises to identify and mitigate heightened risks, and deny support to those involved in gross abuses.</li></ul><h4>4. Pillar two: the corporate responsibility to respect</h4><p>This pillar has four components, and human rights due diligence is the operative one.</p><ol><li><strong>A policy commitment</strong> to respect human rights, approved at the most senior level, informed by expertise, publicly available and embedded through operating procedures.</li><li><strong>Human rights due diligence</strong> to identify, prevent, mitigate and account for how the enterprise addresses its impacts.</li><li><strong>Remediation</strong> of adverse impacts the enterprise has caused or contributed to.</li><li><strong>Scope:</strong> the responsibility covers impacts the enterprise causes, contributes to, or that are directly linked to its operations, products or services through its business relationships.</li></ol><div class=\"study-defn\"><strong>Human rights due diligence:</strong> An ongoing process to identify, prevent, mitigate and account for how an enterprise addresses its adverse human rights impacts.</div><div class=\"table-wrap\"><table class=\"study-table\"><thead><tr><th>Step</th><th>Content</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Assess</td><td>Identify actual and potential impacts, drawing on internal and independent expertise and meaningful consultation with potentially affected groups</td></tr><tr><td>Integrate</td><td>Assign responsibility at the appropriate level and act on the findings across relevant functions</td></tr><tr><td>Track</td><td>Monitor effectiveness using qualitative and quantitative indicators, including feedback from affected stakeholders</td></tr><tr><td>Communicate</td><td>Account externally, with sufficient information to evaluate the adequacy of the response, particularly where risks are severe</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"table-wrap\"><table class=\"study-table\"><thead><tr><th>Relationship to the harm</th><th>Expected response</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Cause</td><td>Cease the activity and provide remediation</td></tr><tr><td>Contribute</td><td>Cease or prevent the contribution, use leverage to mitigate any remaining impact, and remediate</td></tr><tr><td>Directly linked</td><td>Use leverage to seek to prevent or mitigate; increase leverage if insufficient; consider ending the relationship if that fails</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"study-callout\"><strong>Note:</strong> The cause, contribute, directly linked distinction is the most examinable content in this lesson. It determines whether an enterprise must remediate or merely use leverage. A company whose own operations pollute a water source has caused the harm and must remediate; a company buying from a supplier that uses forced labour is directly linked and must use leverage, escalating to ending the relationship if leverage fails.</div><h4>5. Pillar three: access to remedy</h4><div class=\"table-wrap\"><table class=\"study-table\"><thead><tr><th>Mechanism</th><th>Examples</th><th>Barriers</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>State-based judicial</td><td>Courts in the host state or the home state of the parent</td><td>Cost, corporate veil, forum non conveniens, limitation, evidence held abroad, legal aid</td></tr><tr><td>State-based non-judicial</td><td>National human rights institutions, labour inspectorates, ombudsmen, OECD National Contact Points</td><td>Limited powers; non-binding outcomes</td></tr><tr><td>Non-state</td><td>Operational-level grievance mechanisms, industry and multi-stakeholder initiatives</td><td>Perceived lack of independence; may be used to deflect litigation</td></tr></tbody></table></div><p>Principle 31 sets eight effectiveness criteria for non-judicial mechanisms: legitimate, accessible, predictable, equitable, transparent, rights-compatible, a source of continuous learning, and, for operational-level mechanisms, based on engagement and dialogue with affected stakeholders.</p><ol><li><strong>The corporate veil</strong> is the central legal obstacle. A parent company is a separate legal person from its subsidiary, so harm caused abroad by a subsidiary is generally not the parent's liability.</li><li><strong>Duty of care litigation</strong> has partially eroded this. Courts in several jurisdictions have accepted that a parent may owe a direct duty of care to those affected by a subsidiary's operations where it exercised sufficient control or held itself out as doing so.</li><li><strong>Forum non conveniens</strong> allows a home state court to decline jurisdiction on the ground that another forum is more appropriate, which can leave claimants with no effective route.</li><li><strong>Access to evidence</strong> is the practical barrier: the material is usually in the company's control and in another country.</li></ol><h4>6. Mandatory due diligence and the treaty process</h4><ul><li>Several states have moved from voluntary to mandatory approaches, requiring companies to conduct and report on human rights due diligence, with the European Union adopting a directive on corporate sustainability due diligence.</li><li>Sector-specific instruments exist on conflict minerals, timber and modern slavery reporting.</li><li>An open-ended intergovernmental working group has been negotiating a legally binding instrument on transnational corporations and human rights since 2014, with successive draft texts.</li><li>The negotiation is contested on scope, whether to cover all business or only transnational enterprises, on the extent of extraterritorial obligations, and on the relationship to the Guiding Principles.</li><li>The OECD Guidelines for Multinational Enterprises, updated to align with the Guiding Principles, provide a parallel route through National Contact Points, which can hear complaints and offer mediation.</li></ul><div class=\"study-callout\"><strong>Example:</strong> A mining company operating under a concession displaces a community without adequate consultation, and its security contractor injures protesters. Applying the framework: the state has failed its duty to protect by granting the concession without securing consultation and by not investigating the injuries. The company caused the displacement harm, so it must remediate, and it contributed to the security harm through its contracting and instructions, so it must cease the contribution, use leverage over the contractor and remediate. Its due diligence plainly failed at the assess stage, since meaningful consultation with potentially affected groups did not occur. Remedy routes include the host state courts, a parent company duty of care claim in the home state, an OECD National Contact Point complaint, and any operational-level grievance mechanism, each with the barriers set out above.</div><h4>7. How the examiner&#x27;s question should be answered</h4><p>Name the three pillars and attribute each to the right actor, since confusing the state's legal duty with the company's responsibility is the fundamental error. Set out due diligence as a four-step process and then apply the cause, contribute, directly linked distinction, which is where the marks are. On remedy, give the three types of mechanism with their barriers and identify the corporate veil as the central legal obstacle. Note that the Guiding Principles are not binding, and close with the shift towards mandatory due diligence and the treaty negotiation.</p><h4>Summary</h4><ul><li>The Guiding Principles, endorsed in 2011, rest on three pillars: the state duty to protect, the corporate responsibility to respect, and access to remedy.</li><li>They succeeded where earlier attempts failed by separating the state&#x27;s legal duty from the company&#x27;s standard of expected conduct.</li><li>Human rights due diligence has four steps: assess, integrate, track and communicate.</li><li>The response required depends on whether the enterprise caused, contributed to, or is directly linked to the harm.</li><li>The corporate veil is the central legal barrier to remedy, partially eroded by parent company duty of care litigation.</li><li>States are moving from voluntary to mandatory due diligence, and a binding treaty has been under negotiation since 2014.</li></ul><h4>Key Terms</h4><div class=\"table-wrap\"><table class=\"study-table\"><thead><tr><th>Term</th><th>Meaning</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Guiding Principles</td><td>The 2011 framework on business and human rights built on protect, respect and remedy.</td></tr><tr><td>Human rights due diligence</td><td>An ongoing process to identify, prevent, mitigate and account for adverse human rights impacts.</td></tr><tr><td>Directly linked</td><td>A relationship to harm through business relationships, requiring the use of leverage rather than remediation.</td></tr><tr><td>Corporate veil</td><td>The separate legal personality of a subsidiary, shielding a parent from liability for its acts.</td></tr><tr><td>National Contact Point</td><td>The OECD mechanism hearing complaints under the Guidelines for Multinational Enterprises.</td></tr></tbody></table></div><h4>Review Questions</h4><h5>Multiple choice</h5><div class=\"study-mcq\"><p class=\"mcq-q\">Which pillar imposes a legal duty rather than a standard of expected conduct?</p><ol type=\"A\"><li>The state duty to protect</li><li>The corporate responsibility to respect</li><li>Access to remedy</li><li>All three equally</li></ol><details class=\"mcq-a\"><summary>Show answer</summary><p><strong>Answer: A.</strong> Separating the state&#x27;s legal duty from the company&#x27;s responsibility is what allowed the Guiding Principles to succeed where the 2003 draft Norms failed.</p></details></div><div class=\"study-mcq\"><p class=\"mcq-q\">A company buys from a supplier that uses forced labour. What is its relationship to the harm?</p><ol type=\"A\"><li>Directly linked, so it must use leverage and consider ending the relationship if that fails</li><li>Cause, so it must remediate</li><li>Contribute, so it must remediate</li><li>No relationship arises</li></ol><details class=\"mcq-a\"><summary>Show answer</summary><p><strong>Answer: A.</strong> Causing harm requires remediation, contributing requires ceasing and remediating, and being directly linked requires the use of leverage.</p></details></div><div class=\"study-mcq\"><p class=\"mcq-q\">What are the four steps of human rights due diligence?</p><ol type=\"A\"><li>Assess, integrate, track and communicate</li><li>Plan, do, check, act</li><li>Identify, litigate, settle, report</li><li>Consult, contract, monitor, exit</li></ol><details class=\"mcq-a\"><summary>Show answer</summary><p><strong>Answer: A.</strong> Assessment must draw on independent expertise and meaningful consultation with potentially affected groups.</p></details></div><div class=\"study-mcq\"><p class=\"mcq-q\">What is the central legal barrier to remedy against a parent company?</p><ol type=\"A\"><li>The corporate veil, since a subsidiary is a separate legal person</li><li>Limitation periods</li><li>Lack of applicable law</li><li>Absence of a treaty</li></ol><details class=\"mcq-a\"><summary>Show answer</summary><p><strong>Answer: A.</strong> Duty of care litigation has partially eroded it where a parent exercised sufficient control or held itself out as doing so.</p></details></div><h5>Short answer</h5><div class=\"study-sa\"><p class=\"sa-q\">Explain the cause, contribute and directly linked distinction.</p><details class=\"sa-a\"><summary>Model answer</summary><p>Where an enterprise causes an adverse human rights impact through its own activities, it must cease the activity and provide remediation to those harmed. Where it contributes to an impact, whether jointly with another actor or by facilitating one, it must cease or prevent its contribution, use its leverage to mitigate any remaining impact, and remediate to the extent of its contribution. Where an impact is directly linked to its operations, products or services through a business relationship but it neither caused nor contributed to it, no remediation obligation arises; instead it must use its leverage to seek to prevent or mitigate the impact, seek to increase that leverage if it is insufficient, and consider ending the relationship if leverage cannot be exercised effectively. The distinction determines the entire scope of what a company is expected to do.</p></details></div><div class=\"study-sa\"><p class=\"sa-q\">Describe the barriers to remedy in business and human rights cases.</p><details class=\"sa-a\"><summary>Model answer</summary><p>Judicial remedies in the host state are often obstructed by cost, weak courts, corruption or a lack of legal aid. Claims in the home state of a parent company face the corporate veil, since a subsidiary is a separate legal person, and forum non conveniens, which allows a court to decline jurisdiction in favour of another forum that may in practice be unavailable. Limitation periods run while claimants are gathering evidence, and the evidence itself is usually held by the company in another country, so access to it is the decisive practical difficulty. State-based non-judicial mechanisms such as National Contact Points and national human rights institutions have limited powers and produce non-binding outcomes. Operational-level grievance mechanisms are perceived to lack independence and may be used to deflect litigation.</p></details></div><h4>Class Activity</h4><ol><li>Choose one company operating in your country. Assess publicly available information against the four steps of due diligence and identify the largest gap.</li><li>Take a real case of alleged corporate human rights harm. Classify the company&#x27;s relationship to the harm as cause, contribute or directly linked, and set out what the framework requires of it.</li></ol><h4>Assignment</h4><p>Write one and a half pages on business and human rights. Describe the Guiding Principles and explain why separating the state duty from the corporate responsibility allowed them to succeed, set out the three pillars with the content of each, explain human rights due diligence as a four-step process, analyse the cause, contribute and directly linked distinction with examples, describe the routes to remedy and their barriers including the corporate veil, and explain the shift towards mandatory due diligence and the treaty negotiation.</p><p class=\"study-print\"><strong>Printable:</strong> use your browser\u2019s Print \u2192 Save as PDF to keep an offline copy.</p></div>",
  "Human Rights in Liberia and West Africa": "<div class=\"study-note\"><div class=\"revision-banner\"><strong>Human Rights and International Humanitarian Law</strong><span>Module 12: Human Rights in Practice</span></div><h3 class=\"lesson-note-title\">LESSON NOTE: HUMAN RIGHTS IN LIBERIA AND WEST AFRICA</h3><div class=\"table-wrap\"><table class=\"study-table\"><thead><tr><th>Subject</th><th>Topic</th><th>Level</th><th>Lesson duration</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Human Rights and International Humanitarian Law</td><td>Human Rights in Liberia and West Africa</td><td>Beginner to Advanced</td><td>45 minutes</td></tr></tbody></table></div><h4>Learning objectives</h4><p>By the end of this lesson you should be able to:</p><ul><li>Describe Liberia&#x27;s international and regional human rights obligations</li><li>Explain the constitutional framework and the national institutions</li><li>Describe the transitional justice record and the accountability debate</li><li>Describe the ECOWAS Court and the regional human rights landscape</li></ul><h4>1. Introduction</h4><p>This final content lesson brings the whole course to the setting most of you will work in. Liberia is a state with extensive treaty obligations, a constitution containing a substantial bill of rights, a national human rights institution, a completed truth commission, and a long-running debate about accountability for the civil wars. Every module in this course bears on that situation.</p><h4>2. Liberia&#x27;s international obligations</h4><div class=\"table-wrap\"><table class=\"study-table\"><thead><tr><th>Instrument</th><th>Position</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>ICCPR and ICESCR</td><td>Party</td></tr><tr><td>ICERD</td><td>Party</td></tr><tr><td>CEDAW</td><td>Party</td></tr><tr><td>CRC and its Optional Protocols</td><td>Party</td></tr><tr><td>CAT</td><td>Party</td></tr><tr><td>CRPD</td><td>Party</td></tr><tr><td>Geneva Conventions of 1949</td><td>Party, as is every state</td></tr><tr><td>Additional Protocols I and II</td><td>Party</td></tr><tr><td>Rome Statute of the ICC</td><td>Party</td></tr><tr><td>1951 Refugee Convention and 1967 Protocol</td><td>Party</td></tr><tr><td>African Charter on Human and Peoples' Rights</td><td>Party</td></tr><tr><td>Maputo Protocol on the Rights of Women in Africa</td><td>Party</td></tr><tr><td>African Charter on the Rights and Welfare of the Child</td><td>Party</td></tr><tr><td>Kampala Convention on internally displaced persons</td><td>Party</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class=\"study-callout\"><strong>Note:</strong> The pattern to notice is broad ratification alongside uneven domestication. Liberia is a dualist system, so a ratified treaty does not become domestic law automatically; incorporating legislation is required. Applying the seven implementation steps from the ratification lesson to any of these treaties is the analytical exercise that makes this lesson useful rather than descriptive.</div><h4>3. The constitutional framework</h4><ul><li>The Constitution of 1986 contains a bill of rights in Chapter III covering equality before the law, due process, freedom of expression, assembly, association, religion, movement, property and protection from arbitrary arrest.</li><li>Article 11 states that all persons are born equally free and independent and have inalienable rights.</li><li>Article 20 provides that no person shall be deprived of life, liberty, security of the person, property, privilege or any other right except as the outcome of a hearing judgment consistent with due process.</li><li>Article 21 contains detailed criminal procedure protections including the prohibition of ex post facto laws and excessive bail, and the right to counsel.</li><li>Article 26 provides a remedy: any person whose rights are infringed may petition the courts.</li><li>Article 2 makes the Constitution the supreme and fundamental law, with any inconsistent law void.</li><li>Article 65 vests judicial power in the Supreme Court and subordinate courts, and the Supreme Court exercises constitutional review.</li></ul><h4>4. National institutions</h4><div class=\"table-wrap\"><table class=\"study-table\"><thead><tr><th>Institution</th><th>Function</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Independent National Commission on Human Rights</td><td>Established by the 2005 Act; monitoring, complaints, advice on legislation, implementation of TRC recommendations; accredited under the Paris Principles</td></tr><tr><td>Judiciary</td><td>Constitutional review, criminal and civil adjudication; challenges include capacity, case backlog, access outside Monrovia and the interaction with customary courts</td></tr><tr><td>Liberia National Police and the Police Support Unit</td><td>Law enforcement; use of force and detention standards are recurring concerns in reporting</td></tr><tr><td>Ministry of Justice</td><td>Prosecution, and coordination of treaty reporting</td></tr><tr><td>Legislature</td><td>Domestication of treaties, oversight and budget</td></tr><tr><td>Traditional and customary structures</td><td>Dispute resolution reaching much of the population, raising questions about consistency with due process and equality</td></tr></tbody></table></div><p>The dual legal system, statutory and customary, is a defining feature. Customary law reaches a large share of the population, particularly outside Monrovia, and questions of women's inheritance and property rights, marriage, and the compatibility of certain practices with constitutional and treaty guarantees arise directly from it. Any serious human rights work in Liberia has to engage with customary structures rather than treat them as external.</p><h4>5. The Truth and Reconciliation Commission</h4><ol><li>Established under an Act of 2005, following the Comprehensive Peace Agreement of 2003 that ended the second civil war.</li><li>Mandate covered gross human rights violations and violations of international humanitarian law between January 1979 and October 2003.</li><li>It took statements from many thousands of people, held public hearings including diaspora hearings, and reported in 2009.</li><li>Its recommendations included prosecutions of named individuals, the establishment of an extraordinary criminal tribunal, reparations, lustration barring named persons from public office for thirty years, and institutional reform.</li><li>Implementation was limited for a long period. The lustration recommendation was contested and the Supreme Court addressed due process objections to barring named individuals without trial.</li><li>The question of a war and economic crimes court remained a live political issue for many years after the report, with successive resolutions and debates in the legislature and sustained civil society campaigning.</li><li>Meanwhile a number of prosecutions of Liberian nationals for civil war era conduct proceeded in other countries under universal jurisdiction and immigration fraud provisions, which is a direct application of Module 11 to this context.</li></ol><div class=\"study-callout\"><strong>Note:</strong> Assess the Liberian record against the four pillars from the transitional justice lesson. Truth was substantially delivered through the Commission's work and report. Justice was largely deferred domestically, with such accountability as occurred taking place abroad. Reparation was recommended but slow to materialise at scale. Guarantees of non-repetition were partially pursued through security sector and institutional reform. That four-pillar assessment is the structure to use in any essay on this subject.</div><h4>6. The ECOWAS Court of Justice</h4><p>For West Africa this is the most important regional judicial body in practice, and its features were introduced in the regional systems module.</p><div class=\"table-wrap\"><table class=\"study-table\"><thead><tr><th>Feature</th><th>Detail</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Human rights jurisdiction</td><td>Conferred by the 2005 Supplementary Protocol</td></tr><tr><td>Applicable law</td><td>The African Charter and other international human rights instruments binding the respondent state</td></tr><tr><td>Access</td><td>Individuals may apply directly</td></tr><tr><td>Exhaustion</td><td>Not required, which is unusual and makes it far more accessible than the African Court</td></tr><tr><td>Article 34(6) declaration</td><td>Not applicable; no separate acceptance is needed</td></tr><tr><td>Judgments</td><td>Binding on member states</td></tr><tr><td>Enforcement</td><td>Depends on national compliance and remains the principal weakness</td></tr></tbody></table></div><p>Its jurisprudence includes decisions on slavery, the right to education, arbitrary detention, freedom of expression and the treatment of journalists. Because it requires no exhaustion and no special declaration, it is often the fastest route to a binding international judgment for a claimant in the region, and it should be the first mechanism considered in any West African case.</p><h4>7. Recurring regional issues</h4><ul><li><strong>Accountability for past conflicts</strong> in Liberia and Sierra Leone, with different models adopted in each and continuing debate about the Liberian approach.</li><li><strong>Unconstitutional changes of government</strong> in several states, engaging the ECOWAS Protocol on Democracy and Good Governance and the African Charter on Democracy, Elections and Governance.</li><li><strong>Freedom of expression and the press</strong>, including the decriminalisation of defamation, on which Liberia legislated, and the use of cybercrime and sedition provisions.</li><li><strong>Women's rights</strong>, including implementation of the Maputo Protocol, gender-based violence, inheritance and property rights, and female genital mutilation.</li><li><strong>Land and natural resources</strong>, including concessions, community consent, and the interaction with customary tenure.</li><li><strong>Detention conditions and pre-trial detention</strong>, with prolonged remand a persistent finding across the region.</li><li><strong>Economic and social rights</strong>, including health system capacity, education access and water and sanitation.</li><li><strong>Statelessness and citizenship</strong>, including restrictions based on race or descent, which raise ICERD questions.</li></ul><h4>8. How the examiner&#x27;s question should be answered</h4><p>Do not simply list treaties. State the ratification position, then apply the domestication analysis, since Liberia is dualist and ratification alone changes nothing. Set out the constitutional provisions by article. On transitional justice, use the four pillars as the assessment structure and note that accountability has largely occurred abroad through universal jurisdiction, which connects this lesson to Module 11. Identify the ECOWAS Court as the most accessible international route and explain precisely why: direct access and no exhaustion requirement. Finish with a specific, implementable recommendation rather than a general call for improvement.</p><h4>Summary</h4><ul><li>Liberia is party to the core UN human rights treaties, the Geneva Conventions and Protocols, the Rome Statute and the main African instruments.</li><li>It is a dualist system, so ratification alone gives a treaty no domestic effect without incorporating legislation.</li><li>The 1986 Constitution contains a bill of rights in Chapter III, with a remedy in Article 26 and supremacy in Article 2.</li><li>The Truth and Reconciliation Commission reported in 2009 recommending prosecutions, a tribunal, reparations and lustration.</li><li>Accountability for civil war era conduct has largely occurred abroad through universal jurisdiction prosecutions.</li><li>The ECOWAS Court allows direct individual access with no exhaustion requirement, making it the most accessible regional route.</li></ul><h4>Key Terms</h4><div class=\"table-wrap\"><table class=\"study-table\"><thead><tr><th>Term</th><th>Meaning</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Dualism</td><td>The system in which a ratified treaty has no domestic effect until incorporated by legislation.</td></tr><tr><td>Independent National Commission on Human Rights</td><td>Liberia&#x27;s national human rights institution, established by the 2005 Act.</td></tr><tr><td>Lustration</td><td>Barring named individuals from public office, requiring due process to be lawful.</td></tr><tr><td>ECOWAS Court of Justice</td><td>The West African regional court with human rights jurisdiction and direct individual access.</td></tr><tr><td>Dual legal system</td><td>The coexistence of statutory and customary law within one jurisdiction.</td></tr></tbody></table></div><h4>Review Questions</h4><h5>Multiple choice</h5><div class=\"study-mcq\"><p class=\"mcq-q\">Why does ratifying a treaty not by itself change Liberian domestic law?</p><ol type=\"A\"><li>Liberia is a dualist system, so incorporating legislation is required</li><li>Treaties are not binding on Liberia</li><li>The Constitution prohibits treaties</li><li>Only regional treaties apply</li></ol><details class=\"mcq-a\"><summary>Show answer</summary><p><strong>Answer: A.</strong> Applying the seven implementation steps of incorporation, harmonisation, criminalisation, institutions, training, remedies and reporting is the useful analysis.</p></details></div><div class=\"study-mcq\"><p class=\"mcq-q\">What did the Liberian Truth and Reconciliation Commission recommend?</p><ol type=\"A\"><li>Prosecutions, an extraordinary criminal tribunal, reparations, lustration and institutional reform</li><li>Only reparations</li><li>A general amnesty</li><li>No further action</li></ol><details class=\"mcq-a\"><summary>Show answer</summary><p><strong>Answer: A.</strong> Implementation was limited for a long period and the question of a war and economic crimes court remained politically live for many years.</p></details></div><div class=\"study-mcq\"><p class=\"mcq-q\">What makes the ECOWAS Court unusually accessible?</p><ol type=\"A\"><li>Direct individual access with no requirement to exhaust domestic remedies and no separate declaration</li><li>It sits in every member state</li><li>It awards automatic compensation</li><li>It has criminal jurisdiction</li></ol><details class=\"mcq-a\"><summary>Show answer</summary><p><strong>Answer: A.</strong> By contrast the African Court requires an Article 34(6) declaration that most states have not made.</p></details></div><div class=\"study-mcq\"><p class=\"mcq-q\">Assessed against the four transitional justice pillars, where has Liberia&#x27;s record been weakest domestically?</p><ol type=\"A\"><li>Justice, since accountability has largely occurred abroad rather than in national courts</li><li>Truth</li><li>Reparation</li><li>All four equally</li></ol><details class=\"mcq-a\"><summary>Show answer</summary><p><strong>Answer: A.</strong> The Commission substantially delivered truth, while prosecutions of Liberian nationals proceeded in other countries under universal jurisdiction.</p></details></div><h5>Short answer</h5><div class=\"study-sa\"><p class=\"sa-q\">Explain why broad ratification has not automatically improved rights protection in Liberia.</p><details class=\"sa-a\"><summary>Model answer</summary><p>Liberia is a dualist state, so a ratified treaty binds it internationally but has no domestic legal effect until incorporated by legislation. Ratification is therefore only the first of several steps. The treaty must be incorporated or its substance enacted; existing law must be harmonised by repealing or amending inconsistent provisions; where the treaty requires specific offences, such as torture under the Convention Against Torture, those offences must be created with adequate penalties; the institutions the treaty requires must be established or empowered; the training of police, prosecutors and judges must be revised; an accessible remedy must exist; and reporting obligations must be met. The useful question about any Liberian treaty commitment is therefore not whether it was ratified but which of those steps has actually been taken.</p></details></div><div class=\"study-sa\"><p class=\"sa-q\">Assess Liberia&#x27;s transitional justice record against the four pillars.</p><details class=\"sa-a\"><summary>Model answer</summary><p>On truth, the record is strongest: the Truth and Reconciliation Commission took statements from many thousands of people, held public hearings including in the diaspora, and produced a report in 2009 establishing a substantial historical record. On justice, the domestic record is weakest, since the recommended prosecutions and extraordinary criminal tribunal were not established for many years, and such accountability as occurred took place in foreign courts through universal jurisdiction and immigration proceedings against Liberian nationals. On reparation, a programme was recommended but was slow to materialise at scale. On guarantees of non-repetition, some progress was made through security sector and institutional reform, though this pillar is the one most often neglected everywhere. The overall pattern of truth delivered, justice deferred and reparation delayed is common to many transitions.</p></details></div><h4>Class Activity</h4><ol><li>Choose one treaty Liberia has ratified. Apply the seven implementation steps and write a one-page assessment of which have been completed.</li><li>Research one ECOWAS Court judgment against any member state. Summarise the facts, the finding and whether the state complied, and explain what the case shows about the Court&#x27;s effectiveness.</li></ol><h4>Assignment</h4><p>Write two pages on human rights in Liberia and West Africa. Set out the international and regional obligations and explain the effect of dualism on their domestic force, describe the constitutional framework by article and the national institutions including the dual legal system, assess the transitional justice record against the four pillars and explain the role universal jurisdiction has played, describe the ECOWAS Court and why it is the most accessible regional route, and identify the recurring regional issues, ending with one specific implementable recommendation.</p><p class=\"study-print\"><strong>Printable:</strong> use your browser\u2019s Print \u2192 Save as PDF to keep an offline copy.</p></div>"
  };
})();
